(disjointWith Organization InorganicMaterial)
(comment Infamous "Quantity used to describe a person, act, event or area which has an exceptionally bad reputation and is well-known for being dishonorable, scandalous, disgraceful.  This quantity is context dependent so its uses should be restricted to contexts where the reference class is understood.")
(comment Famous "The is the quantity corresponding to a high quantity of fame or awareness by an implicit reference group.  Since this quantity is context dependent care should be taken to only employ it in contexts where the reference group is understood.")
(disjointWith HouseholdAppliance DisposableProduct)
(disjointWith ComputerHardwareComponent GeographicalThing)
(disjointWith FoodOrDrink DisposableProduct)
(comment arg1GenlQuantity "(#$arg1GenlQuantity REL QUANT) means that anything given as the first argument to the #$Relation REL has to be a quantity that is subsumed by the quantity QUANT (so that (#$quantitySubsumes QUANT ARG-1) must hold for a prospective first argument ARG-1).")
(comment unchangedWRTType "(#$unchangedWRTType THING COLL EVENT) means that the thing THING was an instance of COL prior to participation in some event EVENT.  Furthermore, THING continues to be an instance of COL both
during EVENT and after EVENT.")
(comment SiblingDisjointAttributeType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$SiblingDisjointAttributeType is a collection of quantities.  If COL is such a collection, then nothing can have two instances of COL as quantities unless those two instances are related in a #$quantitySubsumes relationship to each other.  See also #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType.")
(comment arg2GenlQuantity "(#$arg2GenlQuantity REL QUANT) means that anything given as the second argument to the #$Relation REL has to be a quantity subsumed by the quantity QUANT (so that (#$quantitySubsumes QUANT ARG-2) must hold for a prospective second argument ARG-2).")
(comment arg3GenlQuantity "(#$arg3GenlQuantity REL QUANT) means that anything given as the third argument to the #$Relation REL has to be a quantity subsumed by the quantity QUANT (so that (#$quantitySubsumes QUANT ARG-3) must hold for a prospective third argument ARG-3).")
(comment arg4GenlQuantity "(#$arg4GenlQuantity REL QUANT) means that anything given as the forth argument to the #$Relation REL has to be a quantity subsumed by the quantity QUANT (so that (#$quantitySubsumes QUANT ARG-4) must hold for a prospective forth argument ARG-4).")
(comment arg5GenlQuantity "(#$arg5GenlQuantity REL QUANT) means that anything given as the fifth argument to the #$Relation REL has to be a quantity subsumed by the quantity QUANT (so that (#$quantitySubsumes QUANT ARG-5) must hold for a prospective fifth argument ARG-5).")
(comment arg6GenlQuantity "(#$arg6GenlQuantity REL QUANT) means that anything given as the sixth argument to the #$Relation REL has to be a quantity subsumed by the quantity QUANT (so that (#$quantitySubsumes QUANT ARG-6) must hold for a prospective sixth argument ARG-6).")
(comment argGenlQuantity "(#$argGenlQuantity REL N QUANT) means that anything given as the Nth argument to REL has to be a quantity subsumed by the quantity QUANT.")
(disjointWith Person DisposableProduct)
(disjointWith Person PhysicalDevice)
(comment SocialQuantityType "This is a collection of collections.  Each #$SocialQuantityType is itself a coherent collection of quantities that pertain to human behavior.  This definition is very broad, and embraces attributes of behavior (personal and social) one might read about in a sociology, psychology, cultural anthropology, economics, political science, or social philosophy course -- in short, in the (human) `behavioral' sciences.")
(disjointWith ComputerHardwareMemoryStorageUnit Mineral)
(disjointWith PersonalProduct IndustrialWaste)
(comment ResemblingTypeFn "'(#$ResemblingTypeFn COLLECTION)' denotes the collection of things that resemble instances of COLLECTION.  Since everything resembles itself, the collection returned will be a generalization of the collection used in argument 1.  The returned collection represents the quality of 'being like', or 'resembling' a typical instance of COLLECTION (in some unspecified way).  For example (#$isa MyCalculator (#$ResemblingTypeFn #$Computer)) says that my calculator is 'computer-like' is some respect.  In English, one usually says things like 'Y is X-like' in order to convey that Y is not an X, or that one is uncertain whether Y is an X. However, this is a matter of implicature and not strict inference, so in Cyc all instances of X are such that they are, indeed, X-like.")
(comment StateOfDiplomaticTension "This collection of quantities includes all  the degrees of diplomatic tension between members of #$GeopoliticalEntity and other types of international agents.  For instance, the state of diplomatic tension between the U.S. and Iran was very high in 1998, that is, in CycL: (#$holdsIn  (#$YearFn 1998) (#$statusWithAgent-Gradient #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Iran  (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$StateOfDiplomaticTension) )).")
(disjointWith TeaOrder Agent-Generic)
(disjointWith Side Card)
