(comment AspatialInformationStore "A specialization of #$AspatialThing, #$IntangibleIndividual, and #$InformationStore.  #$AspatialInformationStore is the collection of all information stores that have no spatial location.  Specializations of #$AspatialInformationStore include #$ConceptualWork, #$Microtheory, #$AbstractInformationStructure, and #$FieldOfStudy.  Although no instance of #$AspatialInformationStore has a spatial location, some instances can have multiple spatio-temporal \"embodiments\". For example, an instance of #$PropositionalInformationThing may be the content of several concrete documents, such as several instances of #$BookCopy (see #$containsInfoPropositional-IBT); and several distinct events, such as spoken utterances, may have a certain unique #$Proposition as their content (see #$containsInformation).")
(comment InformationStore "A specialization of #$PartiallyIntangibleIndividual.  Each instance of #$InformationStore is a tangible or intangible, concrete or abstract repository of information.  The information stored in an information store is stored there as a consequence of the actions of one or more agents.  Wholly intangible instances of #$InformationStore include instances of #$AspatialInformationStore and #$PropositionalInformationThing.  Some instances of #$InformationStore -- namely, instances of #$InformationBearingObject, such as copies of a newspaper at a newsstand -- are only partially intangible.")
(comment DevisedPracticeOrWork "A specialization of #$Artifact-Intangible, #$IntangibleExistingThing, and #$AspatialInformationStore.  Each instance of  #$DevisedPracticeOrWork is an abstract work which is the deliberate creation of one or more individuals working in concert.  Such works are instantiated either in #$Events (for instances of #$DevisedStructuredActivity (q.v.)) or in #$InformationBearingThings.  Positive examples include: #$MobyDickNovel (as opposed to any instance COPY of #$BookCopy such that (#$instantiationOfWork #$MobyDickNovel COPY), #$Chess (as opposed to games of chess -- see  (#$PlayingFn #$Chess)), and Beethoven's 9th Symphony (as opposed to any performance of this symphony or any copy of its score).  Negative examples include paintings (instances of #$InformationBearingObject), customs (not deliberate creations), natural languages (not deliberate creations), and codes (their uses, not instantiations, are IBTs).  For those works which have associated #$AbstractInformationStructures see #$ConceptualWork.")
(disjointWith MakingAPhoneCall AttackOnObject)
(disjointWith PreparingFoodOrDrink Flatulence)
(disjointWith QualitativeTimeOfDay InformationStore)
(disjointWith MountainDew Water)
(disjointWith ColaSoftDrink Water)
(disjointWith Flatulence Bombing)
(disjointWith LemonLimeSoftDrink Water)
(comment physicalSchemaSourceMap "(#$physicalSchemaSourceMap SCHEMA SOURCE) relates a physical schema (SCHEMA) to the knowledge source (SOURCE) where it is defined.  For example, the #$OFAC-main-PS physcial schema is defined in the #$OFAC-main-KS (#$physicalSchemaSourceMap #$OFAC-main-PS #$OFAC-main-KS).  A physical schema may have more that one source.")
(disjointWith Resting-Relaxing AttackOnObject)
(disjointWith HeatingProcess CoolingProcess)
(comment IterationOfTypeFn "(#$IterationOfTypeFn EVENT-TYPE) denotes the collection of all
events that consist of iterations of individual events of type
EVENT-TYPE. Thus e.g. (#$IterationOfTypeFn #$Tapping-Hitting) would be the collection
of all 'iterative tapping' events, in which an agent performs a tapping
action many times. Many verbs naturally denote iterative event types
(cf. also #$IterativeEventType).")
(comment IBTContentType "A collection of collections.  Instances of #$IBTContentType are specs of #$InformationBearingThing.  Each such instance (that is, each IBTContentType) is specified primarily by the type or organization of information contained by its instances.  For example, #$JazzMusic, #$ComputerProgramCopy, and #$Spreadsheet are all IBTContentTypes, since each is primarily distinguished from other kinds of IBTs by the nature or organization of the information contained.  Non-examples include #$CompactDisc-Generic, since compact discs are distinguished by their physical format, rather than their information content. (see #$IBTFormatType). IBTContentTypes whose instances contain not just similar but identical information (such as a single magazine issue, or #$CitizenKane-TheMovie), should be made specs of the more specific #$SpecifiedInformationBearingThingType.  IBTContentTypes whose instances are members of a series (for example, #$StarTrek-Classic) should be made instances of #$IBTSeriesType.")
(comment System "The collection of all systems -- complex objects that decompose into simpler components that, by virtue of the relations among them, form a recognizable, cohesive whole.  Notable specializations include #$PathSystem and #$FunctionalSystem.  The components of an instance of #$System can be identified using #$systemComponents.")
(comment FirstOrderCollection "An instance of #$CollectionType, and a specialization of #$FixedOrderCollection.  #$FirstOrderCollection is the collection of all specializations of #$Individual (q.v.)  Notable specializations of #$FirstOrderCollection include #$TemporalObjectType, #$ExistingStuffType, and #$ExistingObjectType.")
(comment responsibleFor "If (#$responsibleFor AGENT SIT) holds at time t, this means that, sometime prior to time t AGENT deliberately performed an action which was instrumental in bringing about SIT to the extent that, other things being equal, if the action had not been performed, SIT would not have come about.  This sense of 'responsibility' is stronger than causal responsibility--ie, it requires that AGENT play more than an unwitting causal role in bringing about SIT; however, it is probably weaker than full-blown moral responsibility, since even though AGENT intended to perform the action which brought about SIT, AGENT might not have intended to bring about SIT.  This is roughly the level of responsibility that applies, eg, to a case where somebody has killed a pedestrian by recklessly running a stop sign: the malefactor may not have intended to kill the pedestrian, but a deliberate decision of the malefactor's nevertheless was instrumental in causing the pedestrian's death.  It is a necessary condition for the stronger sense of responsibility aforementioned, and is probably a sufficient condition for the weaker sense of causal responsibility aforementioned.")
(comment IslamicHoliday "The subset of #$ReligiousHolidays specified as part of the religion #$Islam (or some branch thereof).")
(comment ThirdOrderCollection "An instance of #$FourthOrderCollection and a specialization of #$CollectionTypeType.  #$ThirdOrderCollection is the collection of all specializations of #$SecondOrderCollection (q.v.).  Notable instances of #$ThirdOrderCollection include #$SecondOrderCollection and #$BiologicalTaxonType.")
(comment May "A specialization of #$CalendarMonth. Each instance of #$May is the fifth month of a particular year in the #$GregorianCalendar (q.v.).")
(comment October "A specialization of #$CalendarMonth.  Each instance of #$October is the tenth month of a particular year in the #$GregorianCalendar (q.v.).")
(comment BiologicalOrder "A specialization of #$BiologicalTaxon.  Each instance of #$BiologicalOrder is an immediate subdivision of some instance of #$BiologicalClass or #$BiologicalSubclass (qq.v.).  Instances of #$BiologicalOrder include #$CarnivoreOrder, #$Monotreme, and #$Primate.")
(comment November "A specialization of #$CalendarMonth. Each instance of #$November is the eleventh month of a particular year in the #$GregorianCalendar (q.v.).")
(comment YomKippur "A solemn Jewish fast day; 10th of #$Tishri-JewishMonth; its observance is one of the requirements of the Mosaic Law.")
(comment ReligiousAgnostic "The collection of instances of #$Person who are uncertain as to whether or not there are #$Gods.")
(comment AnalyzingSomething "A specialization of #$ConsciousActivity and #$AtLeastPartiallyMentalEvent: the collection of all events that involve considering something (real or imaginary) in detail in order to understand features of it.  Examples include analyzing a sonnet, evidence or a spreadsheet. See also #$Evaluating, which is less a matter of pure ratiocination (but may involve, for instance, affective responses), and which also differs in being essentially directed towards an existing thing.")
(comment InformationAnalysis "The subcollection of #$Evaluating in which what is evaluated are instances of #$AspatialInformationStore.")
(disjointWith TopographicalFeature Stairway)
(comment Employee "A subcollection of #$PersonWithOccupation.  Each instance of #$Employee works directly for some agent (to which it is related via the predicate #$employees). This collection is disjoint with #$SelfEmployedWorker. See also #$HasPositionRelationship.")
(comment EatingEvent "A specialization of #$ConsumingFoodOrDrink.  Each instance of #$EatingEvent is an event which involves the consumption of a portion of food by one animal.   Each instance of #$EatingEvent is a meal or snack taken in its entirety; it is a series of individual #$EatingAMorsel (q.v.) events.")
(comment DrinkingEvent "A specialization of #$ConsumingFoodOrDrink.  In each instance of #$DrinkingEvent, one animal consumes a portion of some instance of #$Drink.  Each instance of #$DrinkingEvent typically consists of several instances of #$DrinkingASip (q.v.).  See also #$EatingEvent.")
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion Tower)
(disjointWith BodyOfWater GroundsOfOrganization)
(disjointWith TopographicalFeature PartOfAnHSC)
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion DisplayStructure)
(disjointWith BodyOfWater Facility-Construct)
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion Puddle)
(disjointWith BodyOfWater PathForWheeledVehicles)
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion Decoration)
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion CommercialOrganization)
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion MilitaryOrganization)
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion FinancialOrganization)
(comment LongPants "A specialization of #$Pants.  Each instance of #$LongPants is a pair of pants that covers the wearer's hips and legs.  This collection is disjoint with #$ShortPants (q.v.).")
(comment FourthOrderCollection "An instance of #$FixedOrderCollection and a specialization of #$CollectionTypeType.  #$FourthOrderCollection is the collection of all specializations of #$ThirdOrderCollection.  A notable instance of #$FourthOrderCollection is #$ThirdOrderCollection.")
(disjointWith StructuredKnowledgeSource BiologicalLivingObject)
(disjointWith DeadAnimal BiologicalLivingObject)
(comment SymbolicThing "A specialization of both #$InformationStore and #$PartiallyIntangibleIndividual.  Each instance of #$SymbolicThing symbolizes some thing or event or achievement, without describing it in any detail.  Examples include halos (#$Halo-Symbolic), #$NationalAnthems, school colors, and physical objects such as national flags which are instances of the (notable) subcollection #$SymbolicObject.  Note that instances of #$SymbolicThing can be #$InformationBearingThings or #$ConceptualWorks. They generally do not have propositional information (#$PropositionalInformationThing) contents -- rather, they are typically associated (by an informed interpreter) with particular entities.  To emotional interpreters, such as #$Persons, symbolic objects are often evocative of certain attitudes associated with the entities symbolized -- such as nationalism, respect, school spirit, hatred, reverence, etc. #$SymbolicThing differs from #$Signal in that #$Signals are one-time-only occurrences, whereas #$SymbolicThings are open to their characteristic interpretation any time they are encountered. See also #$signifiesTo, #$signifies.")
(comment UniqueAnatomicalPartType "A default pairwise disjoint collection of collections (see #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType) and a specialization of #$AnimalBodyPartType.  Instances are types of #$AnimalBodyRegion that occur uniquely in the organisms (#$Organism-Whole) that have them.  For example, #$Head-AnimalBodyPart is an instance of this collection. Contrast with #$SymmetricAnatomicalPartType. ")
(comment InternalOrgan "A specialization of #$InternalAnatomicalPart.  #$InternalOrgan is the collection of all organs (see the collection #$Organ) that are also instances of #$InternalAnatomicalPart.  Specializations of #$InternalOrgan include #$Liver, #$Stomach, #$Heart, and #$Brain.")
(disjointWith MilitaryVehicle (CollectionDifferenceFn Automobile MilitaryVehicle))
(comment equipmentTypeOfUnit "(#$equipmentTypeOfUnit ?UNIT ?EQUIP-TYPE)
indicates the type of equipment ?EQUIP-TYPE currently possessed by an instance of #$MilitaryOrganization ?UNIT.")
(comment FusionEvent "A specialization of #$PhysicalSynthesis, and an instance of #$CreationTypeByMethod. In each #$FusionEvent, two or more objects fuse together, yielding a newly-created thing. A notable specialization of this collection is #$BiologicalFusionEvent.")
(comment AnniversaryOfTheRevolution "An instance of #$AnnualHolidayType and a specialization of #$Holiday.  Each instance of #$AnniversaryOfTheRevolution falls on the seventeenth day of an instance of #$July.  #$AnniversaryOfTheRevolution commemorates the day in (#$YearFn 1968) that the Arab Baath Socialist Party came to power in #$Iraq.")
(comment IslamicRepublicDay "An instance of #$AnnualHolidayType.  Each instance of #$IslamicRepublicDay is a #$Holiday falling on the first day of #$April.  #$IslamicRepublicDay commemorates the founding, in (#$YearFn 1979), of the #$IslamicRepublic of #$Iran.")
(comment politenessOfWS "(#$politenessOfWS ASRTN POL) means that the word or phrase whose meaning is given by the #$CycLNLSemanticAssertion ASRTN is typically used in a register that has politeness level POL.")
(comment formalityOfWS "(#$formalityOfWS ASRTN FORM) means that the word or phrase whose meaning is given by the #$CycLNLSemanticAssertion ASRTN is typically used in a register that has formality level FORM.")
(disjointWith InorganicMaterial OrganicChemicalObject)
(disjointWith InorganicMaterial WoodedArea)
(disjointWith Lawn InorganicMaterial)
(comment NewYearsDay "Each instance of this collection is a #$Holiday celebrating the first day of a #$GregorianYear.")
(comment January "A specialization of #$CalendarMonth.  Each instance of #$January is the first month of a particular year in the #$GregorianCalendar (q.v.).")
(comment PredicateDenotingFunction "A specialization of #$IndividualDenotingFunction.  #$PredicateDenotingFunction is the collection of all functions (i.e., instances of #$Function-Denotational) that, when applied  to any appropriate (sequence of) arguments, return instances of #$Predicate.  Examples include #$Kappa and #$AssistantFn.")
(comment SecondOrderCollection "The collection of all specializations of #$FirstOrderCollection, that is, of all collections of (first-order) collections, and an instance of #$ThirdOrderCollection.  Instances of #$SecondOrderCollection are collections of #$FirstOrderCollections.  Any instance of any instance of any instance of #$SecondOrderCollection is an #$Individual.")
(comment granuleOfTemporalStuff "(#$granuleOfTemporalStuff STUFFTYPE OBJTYPE) means that the stuff-like collection STUFFTYPE has as its temporal granules (or granularity level) instances of the object-like collection OBJTYPE. If some collection is temporally stuff-like, that means that the instances of that collection can be divided temporally, and the temporal slices remaining will still be instances of that collection; e.g., a time slice of some instance of #$Person is still a person, and a temporal slice of a walking process is still a walking process (cf. #$TemporalStuffType). Such division cannot always go on indefinitely, however: eventually, division of something temporally stuff-like will result in the temporally object-like 'granules' out of which the stuff-like thing is composed. For instances, division of a walking process would eventually result in individual steps. At this level of division or below, the remaining temporal slices do NOT count as instances of the temporal stuff-type from which they were divided. This may seem counter-intuitive, but since the individual temporal granules of a temporal stuff typically do NOT have most of the properties that the groups made of the granules have (including the property of being temporally stuff-like),  we do not count the individual granules as instances of the collection of which they are granules. See also #$granuleOfSpatialStuff.")
(comment atomsOfElementStuff "This predicate relates the stuff-like instances of #$ElementStuffType (that is, the elements of the periodic table) to the collection of their atoms.  For instance, (#$atomsOfElementStuff #$Hydrogen (#$AtomFn #$Hydrogen)).")
(disjointWith ParamilitaryOrganization BranchOfMilitaryService)
(disjointWith Department BranchOfMilitaryService)
(comment GerundiveNoun "The collection of all gerunds, that is, deverbal nouns formed (in English) with the suffix '-ing', that have mass-noun agreement and usually denote a type of action.")
(disjointWith InorganicMaterial BiologicalWeapon)
(disjointWith AspatialInformationStore (GroupFn Event))
(disjointWith AgricultureOrganization BranchOfMilitaryService)
(comment AbstractInformationStructure "A specialization of #$AbstractStructure.  Each instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure is an abstract individual comprising abstract symbols and relations between them. Important specializations of this collection include #$CharacterString and #$Sentence. #$AbstractInformationStructure also includes abstract diagrams, graphs, and bit strings. The collection can be more precisely defined as follows: Each #$AbstractInformationStructure is such that each of its physical instantiations (see #$instantiationOfAIS) consists of instantiations of instances of #$AtomicSymbol-Abstract, arranged in a certain way. For example, the abstract sentence 'The pig flies' is an #$AbstractInformationStructure. Each written instantiation of it consists of an instantiation of the words (symbols) 'The', 'pig' and 'flies', written in that order. (If the #$AbstractInformationStructure 'The pig flies' were spoken, the same words would appear in the same order, i.e. 'The' first, etc., but the sequence would be determined by the arrangement of the spoken words in time, rather than space.) Likewise with abstract diagrams, graphs, etc. Each of these is such that its physical instantiations consist of arrangements of instantiations of instances of #$AtomicSymbol-Abstract. A hard copy of a wiring diagram consists of a group of concrete symbols representing various circuit components, in which these symbols are spatially arranged in a certain in way. The arrangement of the concrete symbols in an instantiation of an #$AbstractInformationStructure is not always a simple matter of arrangement in space or time. The sequence of symbols '0010010111011001' can be instantiated in written, spoken, or electronic forms. In the last case, the order of the symbols is determined by conventions concerning the electronic medium in which it is stored, rather than by any common criterion for precedence or subsequence in space or time.")
(comment keNeighborSuggestion "(#$keNeighborSuggestion TERM FORMULA) states an extremely weak suggestion that something like FORMULA might be true about TERM due to a similar formula being true of some neighboring concept in the ontology.")
(comment MassNoun "A #$SpeechPart and a specialization of #$MassNoun-Generic  (q.v.).  This is the collection of all ordinary mass nouns.  Mass nouns are typically used to refer to \"stufflike\" things, such as homogeneous substances or processes/conditions/concepts without subdivisions (e.g. instances, or instances of instances, of #$StuffType (q.v.)).  In English syntax, mass nouns are always singular in form, are preceded in indefinite descriptions with the word 'some' rather than 'a' or 'an', and cannot be preceded with a numeric determiner (e.g. 'three').  Examples of English mass nouns include 'water'and 'glamour'.  Cf #$CountNoun.")
(comment Adjective-Gradable "A #$SpeechPart and a specialization of #$OpenClassWord.  This is the collection of all adjectives.  An adjective is typically used as a noun modifier to denote a quality of the thing denoted by the noun phrase as a whole (as 'red' in 'the red shoes').  Many adjectives can be used either attributively (as in the foregoing example) or predicatively (as in 'the shoes are red').  Many adjectives have comparative and superlative forms (e.g. 'dark'/'darker'/'darkest').")
(comment LargeIncorporatedBusiness "A specialization of both #$LegalCorporation and #$Business.  Each instance of #$LargeIncorporatedBusiness is a relatively large (e.g., having at least several hundred employees) business corporation.  Specializations of #$LargeIncorporatedBusiness include #$ChainOrganization and #$MultinationalCorporation.")
(disjointWith LargeIncorporatedBusiness ServiceEstablishment)
(comment CountNoun "A #$SpeechPart and a specialization of #$CountNoun-Generic (q.v.).  This is the collection of all ordinary count nouns.  Count nouns are typically used to refer to \"objectlike\" things (e.g., to instances of instances of #$ObjectType).  In English syntax, count nouns (unlike mass nouns) can be pluralized, and count nouns are preceded in indefinite descriptions by the word 'a' or 'an' rather than the word 'some'.  The plural form of a count noun can be preceded by a numerical determiner (such as 'three').  Examples of English count nouns include 'mountain' and 'lemma'. Cf. #$MassNoun.")
(comment FixedWingAircraft "A specialization of #$AirTransportationDevice.  Each instance of #$FixedWingAircraft is an air transportation device that has a  fixed wing structure as its primary source of lift.  Notable specializations of #$FixedWingAircraft include #$Airplane and #$Glider.")
(comment ShippingOrganization "A specialization of #$TransportationCompany-Provider.  Each instance of #$ShippingOrganization is an organization whose main function is to ship objects for its customers.  A notable specialization of #$ShippingOrganization is #$PostalService.")
(disjointWith ShippingOrganization TaxiCompany)
(comment Td-HTMLTag "The #$CharacterString used in instances of #$HTMLSource to identify a data cell within a #$Table-HTMLTag.")
(comment interArgReln-Inferred "(#$interArgReln-Inferred RELN N M PRED) means that if RELN appears in the zeroth argument position of a #$CycLSentence in which ARG-N appears as the Nth argument and ARG-M appears as the Mth argument, it should be inferred that (PRED ARG-N ARG-M).")
(comment (FruitFn CoconutPalm) "The collection of individual fruits of the coconut palm; contain single large seeds which are coconuts.")
(disjointWith Speed ElectricalCurrentLevel)
(disjointWith Microtheory Proposition)
(comment ConceptualWork "A specialization of #$DevisedPracticeOrWork (q.v.).   Each instance of #$ConceptualWork is a partially abstract work (in the sense that each instance has a beginning in time, but lacks a location in space) which either has an associated #$AbstractInformationStructure (q.v.) or has a version with an associated #$AbstractInformationStructure.  Conceptual works or versions of conceptual works can be instantiated in instances of #$InformationBearingThing (q.v.); every such instantiation of a conceptual work will also be an instantiation of an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure.  Notable specializations of #$ConceptualWork include #$ComputerProgram-CW, #$VisualWork, and #$Book-CW.")
(disjointWith Proposition AtomicSymbol-Abstract)
(disjointWith Proposition GeneticInformationStructure)
(comment subInformation "(#$subInformation INFO SUBINFO) means that the #$AspatialInformationStore SUBINFO is somehow \"contained within\" the #$AspatialInformationStore INFO.  The containment may be direct, as in the case where INFO is a concatenation of instances of #$AspatialInformationStore, one of which is SUBINFO; or the containment may be indirect, as in the case where SUBINFO can be inferred from INFO. See also #$partOfIBT.")
(disjointWith TimeOfDayType MonthOfYearType)
(disjointWith MonthOfYearType WeeklyTemporalObjectType)
(comment Plant "The collection of all individual plants, an instance of #$OrganismClassificationType and a specialization of #$EukaryoticOrganism.  #$Plant is a member of the #$BiologicalKingdom and contains the primary subjects of #$Botany.  The instances of this collections are typically stationary, living, whole organisms; the cells of plants generally lack cholesterol and have cell walls that include substances of #$Cellulose.  Most, though not all, plants are capable of making sugars by #$Photosynthesis-Plant processes and have green parts.  Some example subsets of #$Plant are the collections #$RoseBush, #$SpruceTree, and #$Moss.")
(disjointWith Harbor Stream)
(disjointWith Sea Harbor)
(disjointWith Harbor Canal)
(comment GovernmentAgency "A specialization of #$GovernmentalOrganization.  Each instance of #$GovernmentAgency is a sub-organization (see the predicate #$subOrganizations) of an organization within the executive branch of some government.  Instances of #$GovernmentAgency include #$DefenseAdvancedResearchProjectsAgency and #$BureauOfAlcoholTobaccoAndFirearms.")
(disjointWith Event PostalCodeRegion)
(disjointWith Lake Harbor)
(disjointWith GovernmentMilitaryOrganization GovernmentAgency)
(comment Event-Localized "A specialization of #$Event.  #$Event-Localized is the collection of all events that occur at a specific location in space. Notable specializations of #$Event-Localized include #$PhysicalEvent and #$AnimalActivity.")
(comment NonPositiveInteger "A specialization of #$Integer.  #$NonPositiveInteger is the collection of all integers less than or equal to zero.")
(comment ArtifactTypeForUserTypeFn "(#$ArtifactTypeForUserTypeFn PRODUCT-TYPE USER-TYPE) specifies the collection of all instances of PRODUCT_TYPE which are designed for use by instances of USER-TYPE.  Thus (#$ArtifactTypeForUserTypeFn #$Pants #$AdultMaleHuman) would be the collection of all men's pants.")
(comment SubsumedByTypeFn "#$SubsumedByTypeFn takes two specs of #$TemporalThing and returns the collection of instances of the  first collection that are temporally subsumed by instances  of the second.  For example,   (#$SubsumedByTypeFn #$DrinkingEvent #$HavingAMeal)  is the collection of drinking events that occur  during the course of a meal.")
(comment tokensString "This predicate is a specialization of #$instantiationOfAIS that relates an instance of #$CharacterStringToken to the instance of #$CharacterString it tokens.  (#$tokensString TOKEN STRING) means that TOKEN is a physical token or representation or realization of the abstract STRING.  By definition, any character-string token #$tokensString exactly one character-string; conversely, a given character-string might be tokened by various distinct character-string tokens.  Character-string tokens, like instances of #$CharacterToken, come in the form of inscriptions, sounds, or electromagnetic encodings; and they often, but not always, consist in spatial or temporal series of character tokens.  See also #$tokensCharacter.")
(comment PrivatelyHeldOrganization "A specialization of #$CommercialOrganization.   #$PrivatelyHeldOrganization is the collection of all instances of #$Organization whose securities or other ownership interests are held by a (usually small) group of individuals or entities, and whose securities are forbidden by law to be traded among members of the general public.  A notable specialization of #$PrivatelyHeldOrganization is #$SoleProprietorship.")
(comment InternalCombustionPoweredDevice "A specialization of both #$FuelPoweredDevice and #$SelfPoweredDevice.  Each instance of #$InternalCombustionPoweredDevice is a device powered by an internal combustion engine.  Specializations of #$InternalCombustionPoweredDevice include #$Motorboat and #$RoadVehicle-InternalCombustionEngine.")
(disjointWith InternalCombustionPoweredDevice Engine)
(disjointWith Lake Canal)
(disjointWith Water RoadwayJunction)
(comment ModernMilitaryOrganization "A specialization of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Each instance of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization is a military organization of current (or recent) times that has modern equipment and methods.  Instances of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization generally make at least some use of high-tech military hardware.  Specializations of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization include #$AirForce, #$Navy, and #$Army-BranchOfService.")
(disjointWith AnimalPartRegionFunction PredicateDenotingFunction)
(disjointWith GeographicDirectionFunction PredicateDenotingFunction)
(disjointWith PredicateDenotingFunction DerivedWordFormingFunction)
(comment DogTypeByBreed-Pure "A collection of collections, a specialization of #$DogTypeByBreed. Each #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure is a collection of dogs belong to a single pure breed, recognized conventionally. To be recognized as a pure breed, a group of dogs must be sufficiently similar in genetics and in comformation to an accepted breed standard that a mating of any two dogs belonging to the breed produces offspring who also conform to that standard.  In general, popular acceptance of a group of dogs as a #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure also depends on the recognition of the breed by some organization that has dog breeding and standards as its focus, such as the #$AmericanKennelClub. Except in unusually isolated environments, this type of genetic uniformity does not occur naturally in domestic dogs. Most instances of #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure are the result of intentional design and action by some #$HomoSapiens.")
(disjointWith SomethingExisting Situation)
(disjointWith Microtheory Situation)
(comment OrganismClassificationType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$OrganismClassificationType is a specialization of #$Organism-Whole (q.v.), where membership in that sub-collection is determined by satisfaction of  some set of classificatory criteria accepted by some scientific community.  Instances of #$OrganismClassificationType may or may not correspond to naive categories of organisms, and, in addition, although they are scientific, they might not be officially accepted biological taxons at the standard taxonomic levels (#$Invertebrate, for example, is an #$OrganismClassificationType, but _not_ a #$BiologicalTaxon (q.v.)).  Note that #$OrganismClassificationType is _not_ an instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType, since there are scientific classifications along different dimensions.  Many specializations of #$OrganismClassificationType are, however, instances of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType.")
(disjointWith Situation PathSystem)
(disjointWith Situation FictionalCharacter)
(comment nearestNeighborInSet "This predicate relates an element of a set to its
\"nearest neighbor\" in the set.  That is, (#$nearestNeighborInSet P Q
SET) means that (i) P and Q are elements of SET, and (ii) P is the
closest element to Q in SET, with respect to some \"distance\" function. Note that in general, it does not follow that Q is also the closest element to P in SET.")
(disjointWith PharmacyProductType PhysiologicalConditionType)
(disjointWith Cave Mountain)
(comment InternetSearching "A specialization of #$ElectronicDataSearching.  Each instance of #$InternetSearching is an event in which a computer is used to search for information on the #$Internet.  The desired information may be sought by browsing, or by using search engines (see #$SearchEngine) or Information Retrieval systems, or by invoking other 'machine agents' to find the information.  The information sources are presumed to be distributed among computers on the #$Internet distant from the computer interface used by the searcher.")
(comment BlueCollarWorker "A subcollection of #$PersonWithOccupation and an instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Each instance of #$BlueCollarWorker is a person whose occupation is manual-labor intensive, sometimes demanding physical strength and, in some jobs, considerable skill, practical judgment, and work experience.  Types of blue-collar workers include miners, truck drivers, steelworkers, construction workers, agricultural workers, laborers, mechanics, garbage collectors, assembly line workers, janitorial workers, etc.  Their jobs may require some specialized training or apprenticeship but don't require a college degree. Cf. #$WhiteCollarWorker.")
(comment ElectronicDataSearching "A specialization of #$LookingForAThingOrTypeOfThing and #$ComputerActivity.  Each instance of #$ElectronicDataSearching is an event in which an agent uses a computer to find information that is stored electronically.  Typically, an agent uses a computer program that implements a #$SearchAlgorithm.  A positive example is an event in which a person enters a string into the text box at http://www.google.com and thereby initiates a process that searches over Google's index of words that occur in internet documents. Other positive examples include an event in which a Unix user greps through a filesystem, or an event in which a Windows user opens up a folder of files and visually examines its contents to see if a certain file is contained in the folder. Important specializations of #$ElectronicDataSearching include #$DatabaseSearching and #$InternetSearching.")
(comment SearchAlgorithm "The collection of all #$Algorithms which are used to search for information.")
(comment SortingAlgorithm "The collection of all #$Algorithms which are used to sort information.")
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType AminoAcidTypeByChemicalSpecies)
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType MolecularComponentType)
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType PolynucleotideComponentType)
(comment Ordering-CommunicationAct "A specialization of #$Requesting-CommunicationAct.  Each instance of #$Ordering-CommunicationAct is a communication act in which an agent orders one or more other agents to do something.  Specializations of #$Ordering-CommunicationAct include #$Ordering-MilitaryCommunicationAct and #$AssigningAgentToRole.")
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType RNAMoleculeType)
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType DNAMoleculeType)
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType NucleotideType)
(comment Set-Mathematical "A specialization of #$SetOrCollection (q.v.); the collection of mathematical sets.  An instance of #$Set-Mathematical can be any arbitrary set of #$Things.  A good way to explain this notion with respect to the Cyc ontology is to contrast #$Set-Mathematical with #$Collection (q.v.).  First, while the instances of a given collection all have some more-or-less significant (often \"natural\") property or properties in common, the elements (see #$elementOf) in a given set might have nothing in common (besides membership in that set).  Second, while it is in principle possible for two distinct collections to have exactly the same elements (with respect to a given context), this cannot happen in the case of sets, which are individuated strictly in terms of their extensions (see #$extent).  Third (and specifically regarding their expression in the CycL language), unlike with collections, rarely will it be desirable to create a new constant to denote a particular set.  Instead, a set will often be either (a) intensionally specified by a defining property via #$TheSetOf (q.v.), as in `(#$TheSetOf ?X (#$and (#$isa ?X #$Integer) (#$greaterThan ?X 42)))', or (b) extensionally specified by enumerating its elements via #$TheSet (q.v.), as in `(#$TheSet 3 4 5)'; see also #$ThePartition and #$TheCovering.")
(comment betweenOnPath "An instance of #$QuaternaryPredicate.  (#$betweenOnPath POINT1 POINT2 POINT3 PATH) means that POINT1, POINT2, and POINT3 are points on the path PATH (see the predicate #$onPath) and POINT1 is between POINT2 and POINT3.  Note that (#$betweenOnPath POINT1 POINT2 POINT3 PATH) implies nothing about the ordering of POINT2 and POINT3; it just implies that POINT1 is between them.")
(comment Pointer-GermanWireHaired "A collection of medium-sized dogs. Generally known as the German Wire-Haired Pointer.")
(comment Map "A specialization of #$VisualArtifact-CW.  Each instance of #$Map is a visual conceptual work that represents a region of physical space.  Instances of #$Map represent features such as the following: geographical areas (#$GeographicalRegion), topographical features (#$TopographicalFeature), political regions (#$GeopoliticalEntity), climate zones (#$ClimateCycleType), human artifacts (#$ConstructionArtifact), population densities (#$populationDensity).  Maps are generally intended to help an agent to orient itself in space or to understand some aspect(s) of a large spatial area.  Instances of #$Map are physically instantiated in map copies (see the collection #$MapCopy), which may be on paper, in computerized geographical information systems, or in other forms.")
(comment STIF "(#$STIF X) returns the `Short Time Interval Following' X. (#$STIF FOO) does not include the time point (#$EndFn FOO), but does include every time point in between (#$EndFn FOO) and (#$EndFn (#$STIF FOO)).  The function #$STIF is used to state axioms which assert propositions about the world just after some #$TemporalThing (i.e., after an event ends, after a tangible object ceases to exist, etc.).  Each of those propositions may or may not hold beyond the bounds of that short interval.  Consider the axiom `after swimming, the swimmer is wet'. The swimmer is only known to be wet for a short time interval immediately following the swimming event, and so we use #$STIF to specify that short time interval in which the assertion of wetness will hold.  Beyond that time, additional axioms would be required to decide whether the wetness would persist or not.  Also see: #$STIB.")
(disjointWith Mountain (MobFn Hill))
(comment TakingCareOfSomething "A specialization of #$HumanActivity.  Each instance of #$TakingCareOfSomething is a purposeful action in which an agent acts to maintain, preserve, or promote the health or good condition of someone or something (the thing taken care of is related to the action via the predicate #$objectTakenCareOf (q.v.)).  Notable specializations of #$TakingCareOfSomething include #$Exercising, #$MedicalTreatmentEvent, and #$TeethCleaning.")
(comment PersonTypeByGeoPoliticalAffiliation "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$PersonTypeByRegionalAffiliation.  An instance of this collection is a specialization of #$Person whose instances are people affiliated by citizenship or membership or other similar relation to an identifiable geo-political entity (country, region, etc.: cf. #$GeopoliticalEntity). This facet of human culture is slightly different from that which derives from mere residence in a place.  For that, see #$PersonTypeByGeographicResidence.")
(disjointWith Mountain Valley)
(comment branchesInto "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$branchesInto BIGPATH LITTLEPATH) means that the #$Path-Customary BIGPATH ends by branching into two or more (usually smaller) paths, one of which is the #$Path-Customary LITTLEPATH.  Contrast #$branchesInto with #$sideBranches, in which one smaller path joins a larger path somewhere along its length, but not at one of its termini.  Note that there may be a #$BranchingJunction (q.v.) where BIGPATH #$branchesInto LITTLEPATH and other paths.")
(comment cavityConnectedAlongPathSide "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate, and a specialization of #$spatiallyRelated.  (#$cavityConnectedAlongPathSide CAVITY PATH) means that there is a portal somewhere along the wall of the #$Path-Simple PATH which leads to the #$CavityOrContainer CAVITY.  Consequently, PATH is connected to CAVITY at that portal (so that (#$connectedAtContact CAVITY PATH) holds when CAVITY is tangible).  For example, any room opening along the side of a hallway would be related to that hallway by #$cavityConnectedAlongPathSide.")
(comment BranchingJunction "A specialization of #$JunctionOfPaths.  Each instance of #$BranchingJunction is a junction of paths in which at least one path adjoining the junction branches into (see the predicate #$branchesInto) two or more others, the latter paths being about equal in size to one another and no wider than the former path.")
(disjointWith CalendarCoveringType AccomplishmentType)
(disjointWith CalendarCoveringType DurativeEventType)
(comment CalendarCoveringType "A specialization of both #$TemporallyDisjointTemporalObjectType and #$TimeParameter (qq.v.), and a collection of collections of time intervals.  Each instance COVTYPE of #$CalendarCoveringType is a collection of #$TimeIntervals such that the temporally-ordered sequence of all of the instances of COVTYPE would completely cover all of time without any overlap.  Thus, #$CalendarHour is a #$CalendarCoveringType because all of time consists of a non-overlapping sequence of #$CalendarHours.  Similarly, #$CalendarWeek, #$CalendarDay, and #$GregorianMonth are calendar-covering types.  Conversely, #$Monday and #$December are _not_ calendar-covering types because time does not consist of an unbroken sequence of Mondays, or of Decembers.  Also note that a collection called (say) \"Week\" -- defined (unlike CalendarWeek) as the collection of _all_ seven-day-long periods of time (thus an instance of #$SameLengthTimeIntervalType) -- would _not_ be an instance of CalendarCoveringType since different Weeks of this sort overlap; e.g. the week beginning today, the week beginning yesterday, and the week beginning tomorrow all overlap.")
(comment BisexualHuman "An instance of #$HumanTypeBySexualOrientation and a specialization of #$HomoSapiens.  Each instance of #$BisexualHuman is a human who is sexually attracted to both males and females but does not, in general, have a strong preference for one over the other.  Note that #$BisexualHuman is disjoint with both #$HeterosexualHuman and #$HomosexualHuman.")
(comment AcademicSemester "A specialization of #$AcademicTerm. Each instance of this collection is an occurrence consisting of the complex of scheduled activities taking place at an educational institution over a roughly 4-month long period.  This is not a collection of pure time intervals: note that, for example, the 2003 spring semesters at Boston University and at the University of Texas, Austin are represented by distinct, though of course temporally overlapping, instances.")
(comment AcademicQuarter "A specialization of AcademicTerm. Each instance of this collection is an occurrence consisting of the complex of scheduled activities taking place at an educational institution over a roughly 3-month long period: one quarter of their AcademicYear. This is not a collection of pure time intervals: note that, for example, the 2003 spring quarters at Stanford University and at UCLA would be represented by distinct, though of course temporally overlapping, instances. ")
(comment AcademicTrimester "A specialization of #$AcademicTerm. Each instance of this collection is an occurrence consisting of the complex of scheduled activities taking place at an educational institution over a roughly 3-month long period.  This is not a collection of pure time intervals: note that, for example, the 2003 summer trimester at the University of Wellington and at Otago University would be represented by distinct, though most likely temporally overlapping, instances.")
(disjointWith AcademicTrimester AcademicYear)
(disjointWith AcademicSemester AcademicTrimester)
(comment SubPathBetweenFn "An instance of both #$TernaryFunction and #$PartialFunction.  When applied to a #$Path-Simple PATH and different points POINT1 and POINT2 on PATH, (#$SubPathBetweenFn PATH POINT1 POINT2) returns a unique subpath SUB of PATH that is between POINT1 and POINT2.  Note that this function may be undefined for instances of #$Path-Simple that are _not_ part of a path system (see the collection #$PathSystem), since only in the context of a path system is it guaranteed that a subpath exists between each pair of points on the path.")
(comment EvaluatableFunction "A specialization of both #$EvaluatableRelation and #$Function-Denotational (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$EvaluatableFunction is a function that is associated (via #$evaluationDefn) with a piece of HL (\"heuristic level\") code that computes the result of applying the function to arguments for which that function is defined.  An evaluation of this sort is carried out, for example, when the system is queried using an #$evaluate (q.v.) sentence.  As one might expect, most evaluatable functions are mathematical or syntactic in nature; for, unlike with functions generally, operations on numbers, sets, lists, and strings can in many cases be calculated algorithmically.  Examples include #$PlusFn and #$JoinListsFn.  The practical result of evaluating a term built from (a constant that denotes) an evaluatable function is another _term_ -- one that has the same denotatum as the former term, but that is syntactically simpler and constitutes a more straightforward way of referring to that denotatum.  For example, the term '(#$PlusFn 2 3 4)', when evaluated, results in the term '9'.  So if a query using the open sentence '(#$evaluate ?X (#$PlusFn 2 3 4))' is asked, the answer (or \"binding\" for the free variable '?X') returned will be the term `9'.  By way of contrast, consider the non-evaluatable function #$BorderBetweenFn and the following arbitrarily-chosen non-atomic term built with its CycL name: '(#$BorderBetweenFn #$France #$Germany)'.  As there is no general algorithm for finding simpler or \"standard\" terms for given geographical borders, it is not possible for the Cyc system to \"evaluate\" non-atomic terms like this one.")
(comment JointTypeBetweenFn "An instance of both #$CollectionDenotingFunction and #$BinaryFunction.  When applied to instances OBJ-TYPE1 and OBJ-TYPE2 of #$ExistingObjectType (where OBJ-TYPE1 and OBJ-TYPE2 are also specializations of #$PartiallyTangible), #$JointTypeBetweenFn returns the collection of all instances of #$Joint-Physical that connect an instance of OBJ-TYPE1 to an instance of OBJ-TYPE2.  For instance, (#$JointTypeBetweenFn (#$LeftObjectOfPairFn #$FourthMetaTarsal) (#$LeftObjectOfPairFn #$FifthMetaTarsal)) is the collection of all joints connecting a left fourth metatarsal to a left fifth metatarsal.")
(comment StructurallySpecificWork "The collection of all instances of #$ConceptualWork that are associated with a particular #$AbstractStructure.  For example, the original version of Alice In Wonderland, an instance of #$StructurallySpecificWork, contains a specific text, as well as a particular set of illustrations, which together comprise the #$AbstractStructure of that instance of #$StructurallySpecificWork.  See #$containsStructure-CW.")
(comment adjacentPathsAtJunction "An instance of #$BinaryPredicate.  (#$adjacentPathsAtJunction JUNCTION PATH) means that the #$JunctionOfPaths JUNCTION has PATH as one of the paths (instances of #$Path-Spatial) joining it.  #$adjacentPathsAtJunction would hold between a confluence of rivers (see the collection #$ConfluenceOfStreams) and a river joining that confluence, an intersection of streets (see the collection #$Intersection-Street) and a street joining that intersection, or a junction of pipes (see the collection #$JunctionOfPipes) and a pipe joining that junction.")
(comment facets-Strict "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections and a specialization of #$facets-NearStrict (q.v.).  (#$facets-Strict COL COLTYPE)  means that COLTYPE corresponds to one way of dividing COL up into  disjoint subcollections.  Specifically, the instances of COLTYPE are  all the same sort of subcollection of COL and are, moreover, all  disjoint with (see #$disjointWith) each other.  For example,  #$DogTypeByBreed strictly facets #$Dog, because the breeds of dog  constitute mutually disjoint subcollections of #$Dog.  Other possible  ways of strictly faceting #$Dog are by sex, by size, and by whether or  not they are domesticated.  Note that (#$facets-Strict COL COLTYPE)  does _not_ imply (#$covering COL COLTYPE), since the instances of  COLTYPE might not cover all of COL.  For example, some dogs belong to no breed.  Cf. #$facets-Covering and #$facets-Partition.")
(comment facets-Partition "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections and a specialization of #$facets-Strict, #$facets-Covering, #$facets-NearPartition, and  #$partitionedInto (qq.v.).  (#$facets-Partition COL COLTYPE) means  that COLTYPE corresponds to one way of dividing the entirety of COL  into disjoint subcollections.  Specifically, (i) the instances of  COLTYPE are all the same sort of subcollection of COL and (ii) each  instance of COL is an instance of exactly one instance of of COLTYPE.   For example,  (#$facets-Partition #$FixedArityFunction #$FixedArityFunctionTypeByArity) holds, as each fixed-arity function has exactly one arity (see #$arity).")
(disjointWith AcademicQuarter AcademicYear)
(disjointWith AcademicQuarter AcademicSemester)
(comment Database-Physical "A specialization of both #$InformationBearingObject and #$StructuredKnowledgeSource.  Each instance of #$Database-Physical is an IBO (either hardcopy or electronic) that stores many pieces of information, organized for easy scanning and access.  Typically, a database involves one or more formatted data record schemes, together with some functionality for searching and retrieving data.  Note that, as an IBO, an instance of #$Database-Physical is some particular, tangible copy of a database. To refer to the abstract \"content\" of a database, use #$Database-AbstractContent, which is a specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore. For computer programs which deal with databases, see #$DatabaseProgram.")
(comment FieldOfStudy "A specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore.  Each instance of #$FieldOfStudy is a particular area of study, with its own distinctive set of theories, hypotheses, and problems.  Instances of #$FieldOfStudy are typically the subject of teaching and/or research within instances of #$AcademicDepartment (q.v.), although one #$AcademicDepartment may cover several instances of #$FieldOfStudy.  Instances of #$FieldOfStudy include #$ArtHistory, #$Physics, and #$ArtificialIntelligence.")
(comment abstractInfoAbout "(#$abstractInfoAbout INFO TERM) means that the #$AspatialInformationStore INFO contains information about TERM.  The information does not *necessarily* have propositional content; a picture may convey abstract information concerning the way someone looks, but 'the way someone looks' need not be expressible propositionally.")
(comment intendedAudienceType "The predicate #$intendedAudienceType indicates the type of agents who are the intended recipients of certain information.  (#$intendedAudienceType AIS TYPE) means that agents who are instances of TYPE are in the intended audience of the particular #$AspatialInformationStore AIS.  Examples: the #$intendedAudienceType for the information in a copy of `AMA Journal' is the collection of American physicians; the #$intendedAudienceType of TV commercials for sugary packaged cereals are those members of #$HumanChild living in the broadcast area. See also #$intendedAudience, which relates #$InformationBearingThings to particular agents (not to agent types).")
(comment pitOfIBT "A binary predicate which relates instances of #$InformationBearingThing to instances of #$PropositionalInformationThing. (#$pitOfIBT IS INFO) means that the #$InformationStore IS contains all and only
the propositional information content of the  #$PropositionalInformationThing, INFO . This predicate is a specialization of #$containsInformation, which relates instances of #$InformationStore to instances of #$AspatialInformationStore.")
(comment usesCommConventionForInfo "A ternary predicate, each usage of which relates an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore, an instance of #$CommunicationConvention and an instance of #$InformationBearingThing. (#$usesCommConventionForInfo INFO CC IBT) means that the information-bearing thing IBT contains the information INFO, and INFO was encoded using the convention CC.  This predicate might be used, for instance, to assert that a particular #$GraveMarker conveys the information that a certain individual is buried beneath it in #$RunicWriting. INFO need not be propositional, however. See also the binary predicate #$usesCommunicationConvention and the ternary predicate  #$usesCommunicationConventionForWork (which relates an #$InformationStore, a #$CommunicationConvention and a #$ConceptualWork).")
(comment granuleOfStuff "This predicate relates instances of #$StuffType to the type of object whose instances act as the \"granules\" of instances of the collection.  (#$granuleOfStuff STUFFTYPE OBJECTTYPE) means that if STUFF is an instance of STUFFTYPE and STUFFPART is any self-connected subdivision of STUFF, then: (i) if STUFFPART is \"larger\" than an instance of OBJECTTYPE it must itself be an instance of STUFFTYPE, (ii) if STUFFPART is equal to or \"smaller\" than an instance of OBJECTTYPE it is not necessarily an instance of STUFFTYPE, and (iii) there is no other instance of #$ObjectType for which (i) and (ii) hold and such that its own instances are comparably \"smaller\" than those of OBJECTTYPE.  This applies to specs of #$PartiallyTangible, #$Situation, and #$AspatialInformationStore .  For instance, consider an instance of #$CharacterString.  If we take a substring (a connected piece) of the string (see #$substring) the result will be a new instance of #$CharacterString.  However, we could not take a single character in the string (#$Character-Abstract) and further divide it into new character strings.  Hence the granularity level for a character string is #$Character-Abstract.  Note that one will likely want to use specializations of this predicate for most temporal and/or spatial stuff types, i.e., #$granuleOfTemporalStuff or #$granuleOfSpatialStuff.  Also note that,  #$granuleOfStuff is not a specialization of #$disjointWith.  We would not, for instance, want to insist that instances of #$Character-Abstract cannot be instances of #$CharacterString.")
(comment ClassificationSystem "A specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore.  Each instance of #$ClassificationSystem is an agreed-upon or conventional classification system consisting of several #$ConventionalClassificationTypes (q.v.).  In such a system, a change or reclassification is possible by a decision of an authority, or by a changed social agreement, without changing the intrinsic natures of the actual objects in the classificatory categories.  #$ClassificationSystems include biological taxonomies, standard classifications, data dictionaries, thesauri, cultural taboo systems, military doctrinal systems, and calendar systems.")
(comment SymmetryMemberFunction "The class of functions which return one member of a symmetric relation.  This class consists of #$LeftObjectOfPairFn and #$RightObjectOfPairFn.")
(comment LinguisticObject "A specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore.  Each instance of #$LinguisticObject is an intangible object that is part of some language or other, in a broad sense of \"part\" whereby #$LinguisticObject's specializations include such diverse collections as #$Title, #$WordSense, #$SententialConstituent, and #$NonlexicalLinguisticObject.")
(comment pipesDirectlyConnected "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$pipesDirectlyConnected PIPE1 PIPE2) means that the #$Pipe-GenericConduit PIPE1 is directly connected to the #$Pipe-GenericConduit PIPE2.  (#$pipesDirectlyConnected PIPE1 PIPE2) implies either that (i) PIPE1 and PIPE2 are connected end-to-end (so that (#$endToEndConnected PIPE1 PIPE2) holds) or (ii) PIPE1 and PIPE2 meet at a junction (i.e., an instance of #$JunctionOfPipes).  In either case, fluid can flow between PIPE1 and PIPE2.")
(comment connectedTo "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$connectedTo OBJ1 OBJ2) means that OBJ1 and OBJ2 are (directly or indirectly) physically connected in a way that allows at most only limited types of relative motion between them.  A hinged connection, for example, allows limited rotational motion between OBJ1 and OBJ2.  In many cases, being #$connectedTo implicitly involves another object(s); e.g. a door is #$connectedTo a door frame by a hinge, which is itself #$connectedTo-Directly (q.v.) both the door and the frame.")
(comment SideBranchJunction "A specialization of #$ThreeWayJunctionOfPaths.  Each instance of #$SideBranchJunction is a junction where a larger #$Path-Customary is joined along its side by a substantially smaller #$Path-Customary that ends there (in such an arrangement, the larger path is related to the smaller path by #$sideBranches (q.v.)).  Although many instances of #$SideBranchJunction are T-junctions, the smaller path may join the larger path at any angle.  In systems of named paths, it is usual for the large #$Path-Customary to keep the same name on both sides of the #$SideBranchJunction.  See also the contrasting #$BranchingJunction.")
(comment publisherOfIBO "(#$publisherOfIBO IBO AGENT) means that the #$InformationBearingObject IBO was published by the #$IntelligentAgent AGENT.  Not all #$InformationBearingObjects have been published or are publishable.  To have been published, an #$InformationBearingObject must be one of a number of identical copies (physical or electronic) of some #$AspatialInformationStore, which copies have been distributed to the public or to an identifiable subset of the public consisting of more than a few members.")
(comment fileCopyContents "(#$fileCopyContents FILECOPY INFO) means that INFO is an abstract piece of information (an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore) contained in the #$ComputerFileCopy FILECOPY.")
(comment GeneticInformationStructure "A specialization of
#$AspatialInformationStore. Instances of #$GeneticInformationStructure are
the abstract structure of instances of #$GeneticInformation that are
carried in the genetic material of a BLO.  For example, instances of
#$Gene-GIS are the abstract structures instantiated by instances of
#$Gene-HereditaryUnit. While the genetic information [see
#$GeneticInformation] can be transferred, copied, inherited or expressed,
it is said to be encoded [#$structureEncodesGenInfo] in instances of
#$GeneticInformationStructure, which is abbreviated GIS. Instances of GIS
are themselves instantiated or realized [#$instantiationOfGIS] in a
chemical object, where the chemical structure conforms to the abstract
structure of the GIS. Each instances of #$GeneticInformationStructure is an
abstract configuration. Its structure is analyzable in terms of other
fundamental structures, see #$Nucleotide-GIS. Instances of #$Nucleotide-GIS
concatenated in lists form an instance of
#$NucleicAcidSequence-GIS. Instances of the latter are 'meaningful' since
they are the abstract structures that biochemical agents instantiate (see
#$Gene-GIS)  or the abstract structure of a smaller unit that composes the
biological agent (see #$Codon-GIS). For the corresponding family of
chemical instantiations of GISs, see #$ChemicalGeneticIBT. Note finally
that the instances of the collection #$Genome-GIS are lists encoding all of
the genetic information carried by one - actual or typical - individual in
its genetic material, they relate to instances of #$Genome-GenInfo  that
they encode via #$structureEncodesGenInfo.")
(comment fileContents "(#$fileContents FILE CONTENT) means that CONTENT is a bit of information contained in the computer file FILE.  Note that this constant represents a broader, more vague concept than that represented by #$subInfoStructures, insofar as the second argument is only restricted to being an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore.  The copy-level analog of this predicate is #$fileCopyContents.")
(comment CollectingInformation "The subcollection of #$Collecting whose instances are events in which instances of #$AspatialInformationStore are collected. #$CollectingInformation  differs from #$InformationGathering in that instances of the former involve more than just the transfer of information.  Instances of #$CollectingInformation may involve actitivites that are not themselves instances of #$InformationTransferEvent. For example, a military reconnaissance flight might be an instance, or subevent of an instance, of #$CollectingInformation, but it involves events - such as the motion of the aircraft - that are not information transfer events.  In addition,  instances of #$CollectingInformation involve the creation of an instance of #$InformationBearingObject, which need not be the case for instances of #$InformationGathering.")
(comment screwedTo "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$screwedTo OBJ1 OBJ2) means that at least one screw (i.e., instance of #$Screw-Connector) holds OBJ1 to OBJ2.  The screw goes through OBJ1, its threads grab onto OBJ2, but it does not go all the way through OBJ2.  Depending upon the type of screw and style of attachment, the head of the screw may be recessed into, flush with, or raised above the side of OBJ1.")
(comment remainsAtEventLocation "(remainsAtEventLocation EVENT OBJ) means that OBJ was located at the instance of #$EnduringThing-Localized at which EVENT occured during EVENT and remains in that location after EVENT has taken place without leaving that location.")
(comment Afternoon "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfDay. An instance of this collection is the daily celestial event in which the #$Sun moves from its `highest' position in the daily cycle and `sets' or becomes a #$MidnightSun, i.e from noon till #$Sunset or #$MidnightSun.  Each #$Afternoon is #$temporallyFinishedBy a #$Sunset or #$MidnightSun.")
(comment Evening "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfDay. An instance of this collection is a regularly recurring celestial event started by an instance of #$Dusk and #$temporallyCoterminal with the #$CalendarDay of which it is a part. ")
(disjointWith Midday Evening)
(disjointWith Afternoon Sunrise)
(comment Midday "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfDay. An instance of #$Midday is the daily recurring event during which the #$Sun is near its `highest' position in the daily cycle.  A #$Midday overlaps the start of an #$Afternoon, and a #$Morning overlaps the start of a #$Midday.")
(comment JunctionOfPaths "A specialization of #$ArrangementOfLikeObjects.  Each instance of #$JunctionOfPaths is an arrangement of three or more instances of #$Path-Customary that are all locally connected to each other.   Instances of #$JunctionOfPaths include arrangements of two or more paths that 'cross' at a junction (because four path segments are thereby joined), but not arrangements in which the end of one path simply joins the end of another path, and no others.  Specializations of #$JunctionOfPaths include #$Intersection-Street, #$JunctionOfPipes, and #$RailwayCrossing.")
(disjointWith Morning Evening)
(disjointWith Afternoon Morning)
(comment Morning "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfDay. An instance of this collection is a daily recurring event during which the sun apparently `rises' and `moves' to its `highest' position in the daily cycle, i.e. from a #$Sunrise to the ensuing noon. The definition is more complex in polar regions in which it is possible that the sun does not rise or set during a day. ")
(comment enclosure "'(#$enclosure ENCLOSING ENCLOSURE)' means that ENCLOSURE is the surrounding object in the #$EnclosingSomething ENCLOSING.")
(comment Astronaut "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Each instance of #$Astronaut is a person who participates in space travel and/or conducts scientific research in space.")
(disjointWith DaytimeHours Night)
(comment sideBranches "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$sideBranches MAINPATH SIDEPATH) means that the #$Path-Customary SIDEPATH joins the #$Path-Customary MAINPATH at some point other than one of MAINPATH's ends, and that SIDEPATH ends where it joins MAINPATH.  Contrast this predicate with #$branchesInto.")
(comment SpatialPathSystem "A specialization of #$EnduringThing-Localized and #$PathSystem (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$SpatialPathSystem is a systems of spatial paths (see #$Path-Spatial).  Specializations of #$SpatialPathSystem include #$TransportationPathSystem and #$ElectricPowerGrid.")
(comment Path-Spatial "A specialization of both #$Path-Generic and #$EnduringThing-Localized (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Path-Spatial is a path that has spatial extent, and that joins spatially located things.  So instances of #$Path-Spatial include roads, corridors, wires, blood vessels, and nerves; however, purely abstract paths, such as those in kinship diagrams and mathematics, are _not_ instances of #$Path-Spatial.  Instances of #$Path-Spatial may be pre-existing paths in a #$CustomarySystemOfLinks (q.v.) or they may be paths in some specially specified #$PathSystem (q.v.) consisting of spatial paths.")
(comment PartiallyTangible "A subcollection of #$EnduringThing-Localized and #$TemporalThing.  Each instance of #$PartiallyTangible has a tangible (i.e. material) part and a temporal extent (i.e. it exists in time).  It might or might not also have an intangible part.  For example, a particular copy of a book is made of matter, has temporal extent, and also has an intangible part: the information content of the text markings on its pages.")
(comment PipeEndToCavityJunction "A specialization of #$Portal.  Each instance of #$PipeEndToCavityJunction is a portal formed when an instance of #$Pipe-GenericConduit (q.v.) ends at a #$Cavity (q.v.) of larger diameter than the pipe, allowing flow or access between the pipe and the cavity.  The flange where a water pipe enters a water tank, and the junction of the esophagus and the stomach in an animal, are both examples of #$PipeEndToCavityJunction")
(comment ibtUsed "A binary predicate which relates instances of #$Event to instances of #$InformationBearingThing. (#$ibtUsed EVENT IBT) means that IBT is directly used in EVENT in some way. For instance, EVENT might be the reading of a pamphlet and IBT might be the pamphlet read, or EVENT may be the installation of a computer program and IBT be the #$ComputerProgramCopy installed. See also the predicate #$informationUsed, which relates instances of #$Event to instances of #$AspatialInformationStore.")
(comment father "An instance of #$FamilyRelationSlot and a specialization of #$coreRelatives.  (#$father CHILD FATHER) means that FATHER is a male animal (there might be more than one) who fills the role of father for CHILD.  In the case of humans, FATHER might be CHILD's biological father, adoptive father, step father, or foster father.  But note that being a person's #$biologicalFather (q.v.) is neither necessary nor sufficient for being his/her father in the present sense.  In the case of nonhuman animals, however, #$father holds between two given creatures if and only if #$biologicalFather does.")
(comment FatherFn "'(#$FatherFn ANIMAL)' denotes the biological father of ANIMAL.")
(comment JunctionOfPipes "A specialization of #$JunctionOfPaths.  Each instance of #$JunctionOfPipes is an arrangement of three or more non-overlapping lengths of pipe (i.e., instances of #$Pipe-GenericConduit (q.v.)) joined at their ends. Specializations of #$JunctionOfPipes include #$BronchialJunction and #$TracheobronchialJunction.")
(disjointWith JunctionOfPipes RoadwayJunction)
(comment sourceOfTerm "(#$sourceOfTerm TERM SOURCE) means that the #$CycLIndexedTerm TERM has its origin in #$AspatialInformationStore SOURCE (such as a #$PropositionalConceptualWork, a PIT or a WebPage). If TERM is a constant, it models some important concept used in SOURCE. If it is an assertion, then typically it is an assertion which is true in SOURCE (if source is a PIT), or true in the context of SOURCE (#$ContextOfPCWFn SOURCE), if SOURCE is a #$PropositionalConceptualWork.")
(comment physicalExtent "A #$PhysicalPartPredicate and specialization of #$physicalParts that relates an object with tangible and intangible parts (see #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject) to its greatest tangible  part.  (#$physicalExtent WHOLE PART) means that PART is the complete part of WHOLE that is #$PartiallyTangible and not itself a  #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject (and thus does not include  any intangible part of WHOLE).  For example, the #$physicalExtent  of a given copy of \"Moby Dick\" includes the cover, the pages, and  their markings; it excludes the #$AspatialInformationStore and  the #$ConceptualWork that are also in some sense present in that  copy of \"Moby Dick\".")
(comment InformationBearingObject "A specialization of both #$InformationBearingThing and #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject.  Each instance of #$InformationBearingObject (or \"IBO\") is a physical object that can be interpreted to yield information.  #$InformationBearingObject includes: (1) artifacts made solely for the purpose of conveying information (e.g., a newspaper, or a children's science video); (2) artifacts that convey information in addition to their intended function (e.g., Neolithic pottery); and (3) non-artifacts, such as a person's fingerprints or cellular DNA, which may be interpreted to yield information. See the comment on #$InformationBearingThing on the importance of distinguishing the many specializations of this collection from the many specializations of both #$AspatialInformationStore (information which may be instantiated in particular IBOs -- see #$instantiationOfAIT) and #$ConceptualWork (conceptual and/or artistic creations which may be instantiated in particular IBOs -- see #$instantiationOfWork). An important specialization of this collection (which naturally includes most of the things asserted so far in the Cyc #$KnowledgeBase to be instances of #$InformationBearingObject) is #$HumanAccessibleIBO -- objects which can be interpreted to yield information by #$Persons.  See also #$containsInformation.")
(comment FullUseRights "A collection of instances of #$Situation.  Each instance of this collection is a situation that consists of someone having full use rights to an object.  See the comments on #$ExclusiveUserRights and #$SituationTypeByUserRights for a fuller explanation of how this is cashed out.  This collection represents the concept of \"full use rights\" with respect to an object meaning that if the #$Agent-Generic has this right to the object, it does not place any restrictions on what the #$Agent-Generic may do with the object.  If there are restrictions on what the #$Agent-Generic may do with the object, they arise from other sources, such as formal and informal laws of behavior.  E.g., even if you have #$FullUseRights to your car, you can't violate traffic laws, kill people with it, blow it up in a parking lot, etc.")
(comment PrimaryRoleUseRights "A collection of instances of #$Situation. Each instance of this collection is a situation that consists of someone having primary role use rights to an object. See the comments on #$ExclusiveUserRights and #$SituationTypeByUserRights for a fuller explanation of how this is cashed out.  This collection represents the use right associated with agents being allowed to use an object in events where the object performs its #$primaryFunction.  A rental agreement typically gives the renter this kind of right to use of an object (such as a car, residence, videotape, etc.)  Just because you rent a car, though, does not give you the right to sell it, burn it up, or repaint it.")
(comment proximityConstraints "(#$proximityConstraints SIT ROLE1 ROLE2 RANGE) means that #$Distance-Absolute RANGE is the range of distance that object OBJ can be from the location in which something plays ROLE2 in #$Situation SIT in order for OBJ to play the role ROLE1 in SIT. Cf. #$proximityConstraints-Type.")
(comment in-ContFullOf "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-ContFullOf CONTAINED CONTAINER) means that CONTAINED is located in CONTAINER, and, moreover, that CONTAINED fills CONTAINER.  That CONTAINED fills CONTAINER implies that the inside surface of CONTAINER touches CONTAINED at virtually all points of that surface.  In order to fill CONTAINER, CONTAINED must either be something that can easily conform itself to the shape of CONTAINER, or something with a relatively rigid shape that happens to fit CONTAINER.")
(comment in-Held "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-Held OBJ HOLDER) means that the #$SolidTangibleThing OBJ is being held or restrained by the #$HolderGripper HOLDER, which is touching and applying pressure to OBJ.  HOLDER may be either a device (see #$deviceUsed) or an anatomical part (see #$anatomicalPartTypeAffected) used in an instance of #$HoldingAnObject; in either case, OBJ would be the #$objectActedOn (q.v.).  For example, #$in-Held would be used to relate salad held by tongs, a bread crumb in an ant's pincers, a bird in a hand, or a long two-by-four in a vise.")
(comment DaytimeHours "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfDay. #$DaytimeHours is the collection of regularly occurring celestial events during which the #$Sun is above the horizon.  This collection is the temporal complement of #$Night -- every instance of #$DaytimeHours is #$contiguousAfter one instance of #$Night and vice versa (except for days in which the #$Sun neither rises nor sets).")
(comment CalendarMinute "An instance of #$CalendarCoveringType, and a specialization of #$Date.  Each instance of #$CalendarMinute is a minute on a particular calendar. For example, the first minute of the year 2000 is an instance of #$CalendarMinute, representable as (#$MinuteFn 1 (#$HourFn 0 (#$DayFn 1 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 2000))))).")
(comment SubLSymbol "The collection of all #$SubLAtoms that are also \"symbols\" in the SubL language.  That is, each instance of #$SubLSymbol satisfies the #$defnIff SYMBOLP.  Note that `symbol' has a very specific, technical meaning in SubL.  Save for those that are variables (see #$SubLVariable), #$SubLSymbols are rarely used in CycL assertions, except within those built with certain #$CycInferenceDescriptorPredicates like #$defnIff. Examples of SubL symbols include the symbols 'GENLS' and 'CYC-SYSTEM-NON-VARIABLE-SYMBOL-P'. Note also that #$SubLSymbol is a \"quoted-collection\" (see #$quotedCollection).")
(comment in-Permeates "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-Permeates LIQUID SOLID) means that the #$LiquidTangibleThing LIQUID is diffused throughout the #$SolidTangibleThing SOLID.  Note that LIQUID can be separated from SOLID by physical (in contrast to chemical) means.  For example, #$in-Permeates would be used to relate the water in a wet sponge to the sponge itself.")
(disjointWith CalendarMinute CalendarWeekend)
(comment eventParticipants "This predicate provides a way to compress indefinitely many #$ActorSlots used in the description of an #$Event into a single predicate. Thus (#$eventParticipants EVENT EVTYPE LIST01 LIST02) is true iff EVENT is an instance of #$Situation and EVTYPE and, for every N (0 < N <= (#$LengthOfListFn LIST01)), the N-th element of LIST02 is a #$Thing playing in EVENT the role characterized by the N-th element of LIST01. Note that #$eventParticipants does not include specification of the event or script type of EVENT.")
(comment interArgFormat1-1 "An instance of #$InterArgFormatPredicate (q.v.). (#$interArgFormat1-1 PRED COL FORMAT) means that FORMAT is a #$Format (q.v.) requirement for the first argument-place of PRED when PRED's first argument is an instance of COL.  That is, with respect to a sentence of the form (PRED ARG1 ... ARGN) in which ARG1 is an instance of COL, PRED's arg1 position (i.e. the position occupied by the metavariable `ARG1' in the preceding sentence-form) is subject to FORMAT.  See #$Format and its instances for further explanation.  Note that #$interArgFormat1-1 is one of several \"degenerate-case\" inter-argument format predicates, in which the specified condition and the corresponding format constraint both have to do with the _same_ argument-place of a given predicate.")
(comment CalendarWeekend "A specialization of #$Date and an instance of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-DisjointWithGaps. Instances of this collection are #$Dates that are composed exactly of a #$Saturday and the immediately following #$Sunday.  Note that this collection of time intervals is not a #$CalendarCoveringType : although the instances of #$CalendarWeekend are mutually disjoint, the union of all its instances does not cover all of time).")
(comment correspondingCycCollection "(#$correspondingCycCollection SIT-PRED SIT-COLLECTION) means that there is a mapping from the #$SituationPredicate, SIT-PRED, to some specialization of the Cyc #$Collection, #$Situation. More specifically, if (#$correspondingCycCollection SIT-PRED SIT-COLLECTION) holds, then an assertion of the form (SIT-PRED . TERMS), if made in the context of a #$SituationSpecificationMicrotheory, entails the existence of an instance of of SIT-COLLECTION. (Please see #$SituationPredicate, #$EventPredicate and #$ActionPredicate for more information. If (SIT-PRED.TERMS) is asserted in a #$PlanSpecificationMicrotheory, it entails that some agents intends that there be an instance of SIT-COLLECTION.")
(disjointWith CalendarWeek CalendarWeekend)
(comment Poisoning "A specialization of #$AilmentCondition (q.v.).  This is the collection of _conditions_ resulting from the intake of a substance by an organism (where the kind and quantity of the substance is toxic to the organism in question).  Note that none of the instances of this collection include the actual intake of the substance.  For a collection with instances that do include these, see #$Poisoning-Script.")
(comment Hijacking "A specialization of #$Interfering. Each instance of #$Hijacking is a hijacking event. For each such event, there is exactly one associated #$TransportationEvent that plays the role of #$tripHijacked in the #$Hijacking. There is also exactly one #$SolidTangibleThing that plays the role of #$transporterHijacked in the #$Hijacking, and that #$SolidTangibleThing is one of the things that plays the role of #$transporter in the Hijacking's associated #$TransportationEvent. In addition, there are one or more #$SocialBeings that play the role of #$hijackerPerpetrator in the #$Hijacking. Each #$Hijacking has two subevents, one in which a #$hijackerPerpetrator of the #$Hijacking wrests control of the #$transporterHijacked from the heretofore-controlling-agent, and a subsequent one in which a #$hijackerPerpetrator of the #$Hijacking controls the #$transporterHijacked.")
(comment DailyMorningRoutine "The complex sequence of actions one performs on a typical working day morning in order to get ready to leave the house.")
(comment BrushingOnesTeeth "This is the collection of all complex actions the focal point of which is brushing one's own teeth. Each instance begins with the preparations for brushing one's teeth--looking for the tools--and continues through the physical act of brushing, ending only after the cleaning up--putting away the tools--is finished. For the simple physical action of brushing one's teeth, see #$TeethCleaning.")
(comment TransportInvolvingADriver "The collection of all events in which some driver (cf. #$driverActor) takes control of a #$TransportationDevice (such as a car, a bicycle or a train) and drives it somewhere.  Borderline exemplars include soap box derby car transportation or a cable car transportation  (although these are slightly atypical in that the #$transporters involved are not self-powered devices, they do meet the requirement that there are #$driverActors involved in their instances).")
(comment GoingOutToEat "This is the collection of complex events of going out to eat at a restaurant, which begin with travelling to get to the restaurant, and end when the restaurant is left.  This is the most general specialization of #$Situation for goings out to eat.  A.K.A.: 'The Restaurant Script'.")
(comment CellularProcessType "The collection of specs of #$BiologicalProcess-CellularLevel.")
(comment in-Snugly "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-Snugly INNER OUTER) means that INNER is in (see #$in-ContGeneric) OUTER, that INNER touches (see #$touches) OUTER, and that frictional forces must be overcome in order to remove INNER from OUTER.")
(comment sitEnablesAgentTo "#$sitEnablesAgentTo is a generalization of all enables-* predicates for which the #$arg1Isa #$Situation, with the exception of #$enables-ImpartsSkills. Motivation: occasionally one needs to label a broad class of events as generically `enabling', i.e., as events that enable an agent to do something in one or the other sense of enabling. This predicate should be used sparingly: When describing the enabling relation between a specific event or type of event, an agent and a specific action or type of action, one should use the specific enables-* predicate appropriate to that circumstance.")
(comment maximalConstraint-Genls "(#$maximalConstraint-Genls PRED N COL) says that the known maximal constraint for the Nth argument place of PRED is a spec of COL.  Here, 'maximal constraint' means that typically, the best or most sensible collection, any spec of which could fill the Nth argument place, is COL.  The maximal #$genls constraint for a predicate need not always be determined by its #$argGenl assertions, though sometimes it will be (when it its not, the maximal #$genls constraint is determined by the predicates #$typicalArgIsa assertions).  An example of a predicate for which the #$argGenls assertions do not determine a maximal #$genls constraint could be, #$primaryFunction, whose #$arg2Genl is #$Situation.  This isn't maximal, since much, much more often than not, the collection we should use with #$primaryFunction will be a spec of the more specific collection, #$Event.")
(comment PreparednessFn "#$PreparednessFn takes as argument any spec SITTYPE of #$Situation and returns the collection of all instances of #$PreparingForUndesirableSituation that are preparings for SITTYPE. For example, (#$PreparednessFn #$DisasterEvent) denotes the collection of all preparations for natural disasters. See also #$PreparingForUndesirableSituation.")
(comment PreparingForUndesirableSituation "The collection of #$PurposefulActions in which an agent prepares for a possible situation (#$Situation) that is abnormal, undesirable, and forseeable from the point of view of the agent. The action can be entirely mental, as in #$PlanningToDoSomething, or can involve #$CompositePhysicalAndMentalEvents, such as buying extra food as a preparation for a possible supermarket shortage. To relate #$PreparingForUndesirableSituations to the #$Situations that they prepare for, use the function #$PreparednessFn. See also #$PreventFromHappening and #$PreventionFn.")
(comment DangerousSituation "A specialization of #$Situation. In each instance of #$DangerousSituation there is a significant risk of death, injury, or property damage. Instances of #$DangerousSituation which actually result in large numbers of deaths or injuries or large amounts of property damage, are instances of #$DisasterEvent.  If a #$DangerousSituation naturally occurs, it is an instance of #$NaturalHazard.")
(comment SituationTypeTernaryPredicate "A specialization of #$TernaryPredicate.  Each instance of #$SituationTypeTernaryPredicate is a ternary predicate that takes an instance of #$SomethingExisting as its first argument, a subcollection of #$Situation as its second argument, and an instance of #$Role as its third argument.  Many of these predicate are used to express some version of capability; others may be used to delineate options or potential duties of an agent.")
(comment MentalSituation "A specialization of #$Situation. Each instance of #$MentalSituation is a mental situation such as a person seeing the color red, or believing that his enemies control the CIA, or desiring that Fidel Castro shave his beard. An important subcollection of this collection is #$AtLeastPartiallyMentalEvent whice is the collection of mental situations that are events, such as thinking about something or tasting something. Other instances of #$MentalSituation are more \"long-standing\" and stable, and hence also instances of #$StaticSituation, e.g., believing something, or having some goal. Cf. #$MentalSituationFn.")
(comment instrumentalRole "This predicate is a generalization of #$instrument-Generic that relates an event (or other temporal situation) to an  individual that plays an instrumental role in it (or in bringing  it about).  (#$instrumentalRole SIT INSTR) means that INSTR is used (in a broad sense of `used') by the \"doer\" DOER of SIT in order to \"do\" (or bring about) SIT.  DOER will usually (but  not necessarily) be an #$Agent-Generic.  If SIT is an #$Event,  DOER is s/he who (or that which) fills the #$doneBy actor slot  with respect to SIT.  If SIT is a #$StaticSituation, DOER is  somebody who (or something that) is both a primary  #$situationConstituents of SIT and has played a salient causal  role in bringing SIT about (see #$causalActors).  Consider a  situation of George W. Bush napping on his cot; whether this is  construed as an event or as a static situation, Bush would be its  \"doer\" in the present sense and the cot would be playing an  #$instrumentalRole.  If SIT is an event and INSTR is partially  tangible, use #$instrument-Generic or a specialization thereof.   But if SIT is a static situation or INSTR is an intangible  individual (such as a #$CommunicationConvention or a #$Letter-CW),  #$instrumentalRole itself might need to be used.  For a type-level  version of this predicate, see #$instrumentalRole-Type.")
(comment PhysiologicalCondition "A specialization of #$SystemCondition.  Each instance of #$PhysiologicalCondition involves an #$Organism-Whole being in some physiological state that is not  typical for that organism's species, modulo considerations about the organism's sex or (in some cases) subspecies.  Thus #$Menstruating, though not typical among all persons, is part of being a healthy adult female, and so is not a specialization of #$PhysiologicalCondition (though it is a type of #$PhysiologicalProcess); #$Pregnancy, on the other hand, is a specialization of #$PhysiologicalCondition.  Note that some but not all
#$PhysiologicalConditions are events; but they are all #$Situations.
Thus each physiological condition has a temporal start and end, though in some cases (e.g. dwarfism) these will be the same as the start and end of the life of the organism.")
(comment StaticSituation "A specialization of #$Situation.  Each instance of #$StaticSituation is a state of affairs between two or more things, persisting statically over some time interval. Instances of #$StaticSituation always have a temporal extent, and they usually have a tangible and spatial extent.   As an example, consider the situation of Bill Clinton sitting in his easy chair on the evening of 7/4/96.   There are participant objects such as Bill and the chair, there are relationships such as the seat of the chair supporting his bottom and his weight being off his feet, etc.   In any #$StaticSituation, for the participants in that situation, there is some significant or focal relationship between them which does not change.  In the most typical cases, there is no important change whatsoever, e.g. someone sitting would be such a #$StaticSituation. But some things represented as #$StaticSituations can alternatively be represented as #$Events.  For example, a situation in which geese were flying in a flock would be static (the flock-like spatial relationship between the  geese would be retained) but it would also be dynamic in that the geese were moving, so either representation could be chosen depending on the context.")
(comment actorCapacity "The predicate #$actorCapacity is used to indicate the capacity in which some entity participates in a particular event or situation.  (#$actorCapacity ENTITY SIT CAP) means that ENTITY has an (unspecified) role in SIT with the #$CapacityPredicate CAP.  An important difference between the #$CapacityPredicates and this predicate is that the #$CapacityPredicates are type level with respect to the event -- that is, they take an event type as their third argument while #$actorCapacity takes an instance of #$Situation as its second argument.  An important use of #$actorCapacity is with the capacity predicates #$actsInCapacity-MainFunction or #$actsInCapacity-IntendedFunction to state that a device is serving its #$primaryFunction or intended function (i.e., #$intendedBehaviorCapable) in a certain situation.")
(comment appropriateEmotion "(#$appropriateEmotion SIT ROLE QUANT) means that in the #$Situation SIT, an intelligent agent filling the #$Role ROLE would be expected to feel the quantity of emotion described by QUANT .  If such an agent did not feel that emotion, s/he would generally be considered rude or strange. E.g., (#$HighAmountFn #$Grief) is an #$appropriateEmotion for someone in the audience at a funeral (but not for the workers, the deceased, etc.)")
(comment CycKB "A specialization of #$KnowledgeBase.  #$CycKB is the collection of all Cyc knowledge bases.  Each instance of #$CycKB is a formalized representation of a vast quantity of fundamental human knowledge: facts, rules of thumb, and heuristics for reasoning about the objects and events of everyday life.  Each copy of #$CycProgram (i.e., each instance of #$CycTheCollection) contains the information represented in exactly one instance of #$CycKB. The Cyc KB (like the #$CycProgram) changes frequently.  Also, #$CycKBs can be specialized for different users and uses.")
(comment evaluationDefn "A #$CycInferenceDescriptorPredicate and #$InferenceRelatedBookkeepingPredicate that is used to relate an Cyc-evaluatable function or predicate to the name of the piece of code that is used to evaluate it.  (#$evaluationDefn RELN NAME) means that the #$SubLSymbol NAME is the name of a piece of Heuristic Level (SubL) code in the Cyc system that is used to compute the value of closed CycL formulas built from (the CycL name of) the #$EvaluatableRelation RELN.  For example, the sentence '(#$evaluationDefn #$MinusFn CYC-MINUS)' is asserted in the Knowledge Base.'")
(comment hangsAround "An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$hangsAround LOOP OBJ) means that the at least somewhat flexible #$SolidTangibleThing LOOP is looped around a piece of the #$SolidTangibleThing OBJ.  LOOP is supported by gripping friction or by LOOP's limited ability to elongate as it moves down on OBJ.  See also the generalization #$hangsFrom.")
(comment InterAgentSituation "The class of static situations that exist as the result of some relationship(s) between some plurality of agents.  For instance, world peace in which all countries in the world are at peace with one another.  See #$InterAgentSituation-TwoAgent for a specialization involving only two agents.")
(comment JournalSeries "A periodical dealing especially with areas of current interest.")
(comment programImplementsLanguage "#$programImplementsLanguage is a binary predicate that relates a computer program to a computer language.  (#$programImplementsLanguage PROG-IMP LANG) means that PROG-IMP, an instance of #$ProgrammingLanguageImplementation, implements programs written in LANG, an instance of #$ComputerLanguage.")
(comment programSourceCodeWrittenIn "(#$programSourceCodeWrittenIn PROG LANG) means that a #$ComputerCode-Source for PROG has been written in LANG.")
(comment HumanTypeByHairColor "A collection of collections.  Each instance is a collection of #$HomoSapiens grouped by the color of the hair on their head.   This is commonly used in personal descriptions.  In addition there are common  beliefs about the personality traits of people of particular hair colors.")
(comment relationHasAnArgConstrainedToType "A #$BinaryPredicate relating a #$Relation to a #$Collection.  (#$relationHasAnArgConstrainedToType REL COL) means that at least one of the arguments of REL is required to be an instance of COL, i.e. for some N, (#$argIsa REL N COL).")
(comment OrganizationTypeByIndustry "A collection of collections.  Each instance 
of #$OrganizationTypeByIndustry is a set distinguished by the nature of the industry engaged in by the set members.  Examples of instances of #$OrganizationTypeByIndustry include #$EntertainmentOrRecreationOrganization, #$HealthcareOrganization, and #$InsuranceOrganization.  Because this collection represents a generic faceting of #$Organizations, it is common for many instances of #$Organization to be members of more that one collection that is an instance of #$OrganizationTypeByIndustry.  For example, the collection #$MortgageLender is a specialization of both #$FinancialOrganization and #$RealEstateOrganization.")
(comment InterAnimalRelationPredicate "An instance of #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$InterExistingObjectPredicate.  Each instance of #$InterAnimalRelationPredicate is a certain relationship between two #$Animals.  Instances of this collection include #$mate, #$friends, and #$boss.")
(comment FamilyRelationSlot "A specialization of #$InterAnimalRelationPredicate.  Each instance of #$FamilyRelationSlot is a binary relation between certain members of (at least) human families.  Instances of this collection include #$spouse, #$siblings, #$grandmothers, #$grandfathers, #$relatives, and #$cohabitingFamilyMembers.")
(comment subInfoStructures "This predicate relates abstract information structures to one another.  (#$subInfoStructures AIS SUBAIS) means that whatever instantiates AIS also instantiates SUBAIS (see #$instantiationOfAIS). For example, whatever instantiates the #$TextString \"The cat is on the mat\" also instantiates the #$TextString \"on the mat\", so the former bears the relation #$subInfoStructures to the latter. If the AIS DIAGRAM is a wiring diagram, and SUBDIAGRAM is a smaller wiring diagram that is a part of DIAGRAM, then it would be true that (#$subInfoStructures DIAGRAM SUBDIAGRAM). Note that the relation #$subInfoStructures does not always mirror one of spatial containment -- whatever instantiates a capital letter A also instantiates an uncased letter A, so (#$subInfoStructures #$LatinCapitalLetterA #$A-TheLetter) is true, even though we do not think of an uncased A as part of an upper case A, in any remotely spatial sense of \"part\".")
(comment eMailTimeStamp "A predicate for relating an email message to the character string that appears in its \"Date:\" header. (#$eMailSubjectHeadingText MSG DATE) means that the #$EMailMessage, MSG, has DATE, an instance of #$CharacterString, as its time stamp.  An archetypal email timestamp is \"Tue, 23 Oct 2001 15:33:34 -0500\".")
(comment containsInformation "A binary predicate that relates instances of #$InformationStore to instances of #$AspatialInformationStore. (#$containsInformation IS INFO) means that INFO is part of the information content of the #$InformationStore, IS. See also #$containsInfoPropositional-IBT. For assertions that a given IBT contains information about a given #$Thing, see #$containsInformationAbout and its specializations.")
(comment containsStructure-CW "(#$containsStructure-CW WORK STRUC) means that the #$StructurallySpecificWork WORK contains STRUC, an instance of either #$GraphicalStructure or #$TextString.  STRUC may correspond to all or part of WORK. For example, the instance of #$StructurallySpecificWork represented by a first edition of Alice In Wonderland contains both the #$AbstractVisualStructures associated with the illustrations contained in that work and the #$TextString corresponding to the text.")
(comment covers-Hairlike "An instance of #$CoveragePredicate.  (#$covers-Hairlike MOB OBJECT) means that members (see the predicate #$groupMembers) of the #$Mob MOB are embedded close together in the #$PartiallyTangible OBJECT, and cover some portion of its surface.  For example, #$covers-Hairlike would hold between (a portion of) the hair on an animal's body, and the body parts it covers, as well as between a clump of grass and the soil in which it grows.")
(comment ExampleFn "This funtion is meant to be used only in #$examplePredSentences and #$exampleNATs assertions.  (ExampleFn #$AdultFemaleHuman) means \"some woman\", and could be used to exemplify what's an acceptable arg2 for #$mother.")
(comment subSituationTypeGenls "(#$subSituationTypeGenls SIT-TYPE1 SIT-TYPE2) means that some proper sub-situation type of SIT-TYPE1 (a specialization of #$Situation) stands in the #$genls relation to SIT-TYPE2.  For example, PhosphodiesterBonding is includes as sub-situation types several kinds of hydrolysis.  The latter can be accomplished with various kinds of molecules playing particular roles.  In the case of PhosphodiesterBonding, these roles are played by particular kinds of molecules. Hence those sub-situation types stand in the #$genls relation to hydrolysis, while PhosphodiesterBonding as a whole does not.")
(comment someTypePlaysRoleInSituationType "(#$someTypePlaysRoleInSituationType SIT-TYPE COL ROLE) means that a situation of type SIT-TYPE always involves  (as a result of some type of requirement) an instance of COL playing ROLE.  #$someTypePlaysRoleInSituationType is a variant of  the predicate #$requiresForRole with the first argument a subclass of, rather than an instance of, #$Situation. A salient assertion on  #$someTypePlaysRoleInSituationType allows Cyc to infer the instance-level version from the type-level version.  See also the more general predicate #$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSituationType.")
(comment UnaryIntersectionFunction "A #$FunctionType that is a specialization of both #$GenlsPreservingSubcollectionDenotingFunction and #$UnaryFunction (qq.v.). A #$UnaryIntersectionFunction takes a collection as its argument and always returns a subcollection of a given collection-as-argument as its corresponding value.  This value is in effect the \"intersection\" (see #$collectionIntersection) of the collection that is its argument with a collection specified in the function's definition (via #$intersectionType).  For example, where ANIMALTYPE is a type of #$Animal, (#$JuvenileFn ANIMALTYPE) is the collection of juvenile animals of ANIMALTYPE (i.e. the \"intersection\" of ANIMALTYPE and #$JuvenileAnimal).  Other instances of #$UnaryIntersectionFunction are #$FemaleFn, #$PureFn, and #$SolidFn.")
(comment covers-Skinlike "An instance of #$CoveragePredicate.  (#$covers-Skinlike SKIN OBJECT) means that SKIN forms all or part of OBJECT's outer surface, shell, or skin.  (#$covers-Skinlike SKIN OBJECT) does _not_ imply that SKIN is a part (see #$physicalDecompositions) of OBJECT, nor does it imply that SKIN is _not_ part of OBJECT.")
(comment supportedBy "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$supportedBy OBJECT SUPPORT) means that SUPPORT is at least partially responsible for holding OBJECT up and maintaining its vertical position.  If SUPPORT were to be removed, then (everything else being equal) either OBJECT would fall or at least part of
OBJECT would move downward.  See also #$supportedObject, #$supportingObject,
and #$SupportingSomething.")
(comment queryAllRolesInSituationType "A #$QueryDedicatedPredicate used for querying for all the roles that a given actortype plays w.r.t. a situation type or w.r.t. any sub-situation type of that situation type.")
(comment SituationTypePredicate "A collection of #$Predicates each intance of which takes an subcollection of #$Situation as its second argument.  For details about the uses of such predicates, see the specializations  #$SituationTypeSlot and #$SituationTypeTernaryPredicate.")
(comment SubCollectionsAreInstancesOfFn "(#$SubCollectionsAreInstancesOfFn CT) denotes the #$Collection, COL, of instances of the instances of CT, a #$CollectionType. See the predicate #$allAndOnlySubCollectionsAreInstancesOf for more details. For a more general and set theoretic notion of union, see #$collectionUnion and #$CollectionUnionFn, together with #$SetOfTypeFn.")
(comment behind-Generally "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate. (#$behind-Generally AFT FORE) means that AFT is behind FORE.  More precisely, a line originating at the center of FORE projecting away from the front side of FORE and parallel to the intrinsic front-to-back axis of FORE forms an angle of less than 45 degrees with a line intersecting both AFT and FORE.  Note that AFT may be intangible (e.g., a shadow), although FORE must be tangible.")
(comment TemporalPartPredicate "A specialization of both #$PartPredicate and #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (qq.v.).  This is a collection of binary predicates that are used to relate instances of #$TemporalThing (q.v.) to their temporal parts or components.  Each instance of #$TemporalPartPredicate is a proper specialization (via #$genls or #$genlInverse) of #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.).  Examples include #$startingPoint and #$timeSlices.")
(comment behind-Directly "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$behind-Directly AFT FORE) means that AFT is directly behind tangible object FORE.  More precisely, it implies both (#$behind-Generally AFT FORE) and that there is at least one line parallel to the backward pointing axis of FORE that intersects both AFT and FORE.  Note that FORE must have a back side.  AFT may be intangible, such as an image projected behind an actor on a stage.")
(comment typesHaveRelationInSituationType-Existential "(#$typesHaveRelationInSituationType-Existential SIT-TYPE TYPE1 TYPE2 BINREL) means that in any instance of SIT-TYPE, there are instances of TYPE1 and TYPE2 such that it will hold throughout the instance (see #$holdsIn) that the instance of TYPE1 has the relation BINREL to the instance of TYPE2.")
(comment inFrontOf-Directly "An instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  (#$inFrontOf-Directly FORE AFT) means that the #$SpatialThing-Localized FORE is directly in front of the #$PartiallyTangible AFT.  More precisely, (#$inFrontOf-Directly FORE AFT) implies both (#$inFrontOf-Generally FORE AFT), and that there is at least one line parallel to the forward pointing axis of AFT that intersects both FORE and AFT.  Note that FORE may be an intangible thing, such as an image projected onto a screen.")
(comment SubSitTypeWithTypeInRoleFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction. '(#$SubSitTypeWithTypeInRoleFn SIT-TYPE SUBSITTYPE OBJECT-TYPE ROLE)' is the sub-situation type SIT-TYPE of type SUBSITTYPE (a specialization of #$Situation) in instances of which an object of type OBJECT-TYPE (an instance of #$Collection) plays ROLE (an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate).")
(comment PrimitiveTemporalPredicate "A specialization of #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate (q.v.) whose instances relate individual time points only.  Thus, each instance of  #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicate has both of its argument-places constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$TimePoint.  Instances include #$after and #$simultaneousWith (and those are in fact the only two primitive temporal predicates reified in the Cyc knowledge base).  Compare #$ComplexTemporalPredicate.")
(comment ComplexTemporalPredicate "A specialization of #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate (q.v.) whose instances relate temporal things other than (but possibly including) individual time points.  Thus, each instance of #$ComplexTemporalPredicate has  both its argument-places constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$TemporalThing or some specialization thereof (and at least one of these constraints must be broader than #$TimePoint).  A complex  temporal predicate might be used to relate events, tangible objects, proper time intervals, and so forth.  Instances include  #$temporallyIntersects and #$startsAfterStartingOf.  Compare #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicate.")
(comment subSituationTypeList "'(#$subSituationTypeList SIT-TYPE LIST)' means that LIST is a #$List of subsituation types in SIT-TYPE such that each member in
LIST #$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType the earlier members in LIST.")
(comment pathBetweenInSystem "(#$pathBetweenInSystem PATH X Y SYS) means that PATH is a path in the system SYS, and X and Y are the end points (in the #$PathSystem) of PATH.  If PATH is a \"link\" of the system (see #$linkInSystem), (#$pathBetweenInSystem PATH X Y SYS) iff (#$linkBetweenInSystem PATH X Y SYS).  Note that there can be multiple paths in SYS between the same two points in SYS (which may or may not be \"nodes\" in SYS; see #$nodeInSystem).   See also #$pathBetween when no #$PathSystem is specified.")
(comment sitTypeHasSubSitTypeWithRolePlayerOfType "(#$sitTypeHasSubSitTypeWithRolePlayerOfType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE OBJ-TYPE ROLE) means that there is at least one subsituation of type SUBSIT-TYPE in any instance of SIT-TYPE that has an object of type OBJ-TYPE playing the role ROLE.  There may be other subsituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE in the instance of SIT-TYPE which do not have an object of type OBJ-TYPE playing that role.  See #$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSituationType for the meaning that every subsituation of type SUBSIT-TYPE in SIT-TYPE has an instance of OBJ-TYPE playing role ROLE, but not requiring any such subsituation to exist.")
(comment Jumping "The collection of #$MovementProcesses in which an #$Animal agent springs vertically or horizontally into the air by rapidly extending its legs.  Distinct from #$FallingProcesses.")
(comment ailmentTypeAfflictsPartType "(#$ailmentTypeAfflictsPartType AILMENT-TYPE PART-TYPE) indicates  that all ailments that are elements of AILMENT-TYPE afflict some organism part which is an element of PART-TYPE.  For example,  (#$ailmentTypeAfflictsPartType #$HeartAttack #$Heart).")
(comment HowlingSound "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$AnimalSound.  Each instance of #$HowlingSound is either a loud, prolonged, mournful cry emitted by a #$CanineAnimal, or an imitation of such a sound (e.g. the sound made by a human who is howling like a #$Wolf).")
(comment OinkingSound "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$AnimalSound.  Each instance of #$OinkingSound is either an \"oink\"-like sound emitted by a #$Pig, or an imitation of such a sound (e.g. the sound made by a human who is oinking like a pig).")
(comment QuackingSound "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$AnimalSound.  Each instance of #$QuackingSound is either a \"quack\"-like sound emitted by a #$Duck, or an imitation of such a sound (e.g. the sound made by a human who is quacking like a duck).")
(comment MooingSound "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$AnimalSound.  Each instance of #$MooingSound is either a \"moo\"-like sound emitted by a #$Cow, or an imitation of such a sound (e.g. the sound made by a human who is mooing like a cow).")
(comment HomosexualMaleHuman "The collection of male humans whose sexual preference is predominantly for other males.")
(comment HomosexualFemaleHuman "The collection of female humans whose sexual preference is predominantly for other females.")
(comment inheritTypeOfRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType-Unique "This predicate is meant to state a very strong condition that applies to a number of specializations of #$Situation: namely, that if a particular type of thing is referenced as playing some role in all instances of a situation type, and the same type is referenced as playing some other role or roles in particular sub-situation types of the situation type, then an instance of the type that plays the specified role in a particular instance of the situation type overall plays all of the other roles specified for that type in the situations that instantiate the particular sub-situation types.  More specifically, if it is known that (#$inheritTypeOfRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType-Unique SITTYPE THINGTYPE), (#$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSituationType SITTYPE THINGTYPE ROLE), (#$properSubSituationTypes SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE), and (#$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSituationType SITTYPE THINGTYPE SUBSITTYPE OTHERROLE), then, for any SIT such that (#$isa SIT SITTYPE) and any instance of THINGTYPE, THING, such that (ROLE SIT THING), and any instance of SUBSITTYPE, SUBSIT, such that (#$properSubSituations SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE), (OTHERROLE SUBSITTYPE THING).")
(comment SolarPoweredRoadVehicle "The collection of road vehicles that use energy from the sun for propulsion.")
(comment OrganizationTypeByProfitMotive "A collection of two disjoint collections, #$CommercialOrganization and #$NonProfitOrganization.  Each instance of #$OrganizationTypeByProfitMotive is a set whose elements are distinguished by either the fact that their activities are motivated by the desire to make a profit for their owners or by the fact that no such profit motive exists (or that any profit motive is secondary to a primary non-profit motive).  For example, #$SierraClub is an instance of #$NonProfitOrganization.  Although #$SierraClub may engage in activities designed to generate a profit (e.g., selling #$SierraClub products), those activities are engaged in only for the purpose of furthering the primary goal of #$SierraClub (which does not involve making money for the organization's owners (it has none) or members).")
(comment BarkingSound "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$AnimalSound.  Each instance of #$BarkingSound is either a harsh, \"woof\"-like sound emitted by a #$Dog (usually as an expression of excitement), or an imitation of such a sound (e.g. the sound made by a human who is barking like a dog).")
(comment PurringSound "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$AnimalSound.  Each instance of #$PurringSound is either a gentle, \"rolling\" sound emitted by a #$Cat (usually as an expression of happiness or pleasure), or an imitation of such a sound (e.g. the sound made by a human who is purring like a cat).")
(disjointWith BarkingSound PurringSound)
(comment Fermenting-Cellular "Anaerobic degredation of glucose (dextrose) and other simple sugars in the living cell.  Two main kinds: (1) in microorganisms and animal cells, production of lactic acid as end product; (2) in some yeast and some bacteria, production of ethyl alcohol and carbon dioxide.")
(comment NthSubSituationTypeFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  #$NthSubSituationTypeFn, when applied to a specialization SITTYPE of #$Situation, and a #$PositiveInteger N, returns the Nth sub-situation type in the quasi-temporal ordering of SITTYPE.")
(disjointWith HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject Organization)
(comment AttachingAnAmmoBelt "The collection of all events in which one
   loads an #$AutomaticFirearm-BeltFed.")
(comment LabellingAProduct "A collection of events.  In each #$LabellingAProduct, a label is attached to a product, typically in order to promote the sale of that product.  The label may be fastened by any means of connection, including glue, staples, and sewing.")
(disjointWith ShavingBody ShavingSomething)
(comment AppropriatingSomething "A specialization of #$TakingSomething.  In each instance of #$AppropriatingSomething, an agent takes something that no one else has user rights over, such as air for breathing, or some object which at that time belongs to no one (for example, a dime lying in the street).")
(disjointWith Watercraft HumanOccupationConstructObject)
(disjointWith CycLOpenSentence CycLOpenDenotationalTerm)
(disjointWith BoardGame-EquipmentSet PlayingCard)
(comment genlsAtLeast "Intended for use in the representation of underspecified plural noun phrases, as in the well-worn example `The sailors danced with the waitresses'. (#$genlsAtLeast NUM SUBCOLL COLL) means that SUBCOLL is a subcollection of COLL consisting of at least NUM elements. In the example above, `the sailors' can be rendered as (#$thereExists ?SUBCOLL (#$genlsAtLeast 2 ?SUBCOLL #$Sailor)), and the same form can be applied to `the waitresses'.")
(comment genlsExactly "Intended for use in the representation of underspecified plural noun phrases, as in the well-worn example `7 sailors danced with the waitresses'. (#$genlsExactly NUM SUBCOLL COLL) means essentially that SUBCOLL is a subcollection of COLL consisting of exactly NUM elements. However, the full definition of #$genlsExactly has additional contextual constraints, for, by itself, the previous statement would be logically equivalent to (#$genlsAtLeast NUM SUBCOLL COLL). In the example above, the NP `7 sailors' can be rendered as (#$thereExists ?SUBCOLL (#$genlsExactly 7 ?SUBCOLL #$Sailor)).")
(disjointWith PortableObject HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject)
(disjointWith NavalMine LandMine)
(disjointWith CrossingAnM4T6 CrossingARibbonBridge)
(comment schemaObjectID "(#$schemaObjectID SCHEMA OBJECT ID) means that OBJECT _is_ the object defined by SCHEMA with the unique id, ID.  OBJECT is typically referred to functionally using #$SchemaObjectFn.")
(comment queryForSubSituationTypeOrder "A #$QueryDedicatedPredicate used for querying about the quasi-temporal relations obtaining between sub-situation types.")
(comment Coelomate "A specialization of #$MulticellularOrganism and #$Animal. This is the collection of (individual) organisms that  have three well-defined derm layers, an ectoderm, endoderm, and mesoderm, and posses a coelem. A coelem is a fluid-filled body cavity between the outer body wall and the gut. Members of the phylum Annelida (segmented worms), for example, are #$Coelomates.")
(disjointWith Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge Corner-TwoEdge)
(disjointWith FirstMortgage SecondMortgage)
(disjointWith FirstMortgage ThirdMortgage)
(disjointWith Broadcasting CableTVTransmitting)
(disjointWith SecondMortgage ThirdMortgage)
(disjointWith CrossingATemporaryBridge CrossingAnExistingBridge)
(disjointWith CycLSentence CycLNonAtomicTerm)
(disjointWith CycLGenericRelationFormula CycLNonAtomicTerm)
(disjointWith CycLSentence CycLGenericRelationFormula)
(disjointWith DeoxygenatedBlood OxygenatedBlood)
(comment TaxonomicSlotForPredicates "A specialization of both #$TaxonomicSlot and #$TaxonomicPredicateForPredicates. Each instance of #$TaxonomicSlotForPredicates is a binary predicate used to help define the taxonomy of #$Predicates by specifying relations between various predicates; hence its argument-places are both constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$Predicate or some broad specialization thereof (such as #$BinaryPredicate).  Examples include #$genlPreds, #$genlInverse, and #$negationPreds.")
(comment TaxonomicSlot "A subcollection of #$TaxonomicPredicate.  Each instance of #$TaxonomicSlot is a binary predicate used to help specify the position of a thing within one of the major taxonomies or hierarchies in the Cyc ontology.  For example, #$genls orders the instances of #$Collection into a hierarchy.  Other examples of #$TaxonomicSlots are #$isa, #$partitionedInto, #$coExtensional, and #$disjointWith.")
(comment ControllingATransportationDevice "A collection of events; a subset of #$HandlingADevice.  Every instance of #$ControllingATransportationDevice represents some action of an #$Agent-Generic in controlling an object which plays the role of #$transporter (q.v.) in some instance of #$TransportationEvent.  The transporter could be an automobile, an airplane, a bicycle, a wheelchair, etc.  Instances of #$ControllingATransportationDevice include the individual actions which are involved in controlling the direction and speed of a #$transporter, e.g., such actions as pressing buttons, moving levers, and depressing pedals.  Elements of #$ControllingATransportationDevice are not the gross actual movement, trajectory etc. of the transporter, and thus not elements of #$TransportationEvent.  Of course a successful #$ControllingATransportationDevice event will generally mean that there was some successful #$TransportationEvent too!  I.e., if you successfully control the direction and speed of a car for a while, then presumably there was a transportation event in which that car transported you somewhere.  And the various sub-events of the #$ControllingATransportationDevice event can also be considered sub-events of that #$TransportationEvent --- in fact, the entire #$ControllingATransportationDevice event can be considered a sub-event of that #$TransportationEvent.")
(comment inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType "(#$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType  SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 ROLE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2 ROLE2 TYPE) means that if SIT-TYPE is such that (#$properSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1) and (#$properSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE2), and (#$isa SIT SIT-TYPE), and (#$isa SUB1 SUBSIT-TYPE1) and (#$isa SUB2 SUBSIT-TYPE2) and (#$subSituations SIT SUB1) and (#$subSituations SIT SUB2), then for any INST such that (#$isa INST TYPE) and such that (ROLE1 SUB1 INST), (ROLE2 SUB2 INST).  The vernacular sense of the predicate is that, in a situtation type featuring SUBSIT-TYPE1 and SUBSIT-TYPE2 as #$properSubSituationTypes, whatever instance of TYPE plays ROLE1 in the sub-situation that is an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE1 in an instance of the situation type also plays the role ROLE2 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE2.")
(disjointWith CycForwardModule CycTransformationModule)
(disjointWith CycForwardModule CycRemovalModule)
(disjointWith CycTransformationModule CycRemovalModule)
(comment SubSituationTypeDenotingFunction "An specialization of #$SubcollectionDenotingFunction.   For any instance FN of #$SubSituationTypeDenotingFunction, FN returns a particular subsituation type.")
(disjointWith GeographicalRegion IndividualAgent)
(comment SituationTypeRelation "This is the collection of all predicates and functions which are primarily used for representing the structure of complex situation types. This collection is mainly useful as a way of bundling together this vocabulary.")
(comment surfaceParts "An instance of #$PhysicalPartPredicate.  (#$surfaceParts BIG LITTLE) means that LITTLE is an external physical part (see #$externalParts) of a surface of BIG, or that LITTLE is a physical part of BIG itself, and a surface of LITTLE is part of a surface of BIG.  LITTLE is also something which 'does not go all the way through' BIG, in the sense that there is no line segment spatially subsumed by LITTLE that goes from a point at the surface of one side of BIG to a point of the surface on the opposite side of BIG. Positive examples: the skin of an orange is a #$surfaceParts of the orange, a window of a house is a #$surfaceParts of that house.  Borderline positive examples: the inside surface of a coffee cup is one of the #$surfaceParts of the cup; the inside surface of a beer-can in some contexts is a #$surfaceParts of the can.  Negative examples:  the brain is not a #$surfaceParts of a person.  A person's head is also a negative example of a #$surfaceParts.  There is a line that can go, for example, from the front side to the back side of that person.")
(disjointWith PersonTypeByActivity OrganizationTypeByActivityType)
(disjointWith OutdoorSportsEvent OnlineGameService)
(comment focalProperSubsituationTypes "(#$focalProperSubsituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUB-SIT-TYPE) means that the most important subsituation any instance of SIT-TYPE (a specialization of #$Situation) is SUB-SIT-TYPE.  Cf. #$properSubSituationTypes.")
(comment inheritRolePlayersOfType-SingleRole-SubSitTypeToSubSitType "This instance of #$ELRelation is an abbreviated version of #$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType (q.v.); (#$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SingleRole-SubSitTypeToSubSitType SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE1 ROLE SUBSITTYPE2 TYPE) is shorthand for (#$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType    SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE1 ROLE SUBSITTYPE2 ROLE TYPE).")
(disjointWith SnowSkiing AquaticBodyMovement)
(comment onlyTypePlaysRoleInSituationType "(#$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSituationType SITTYPE ACTORTYPE ROLE) means that in any instance of SITTYPE, whatever plays the role ROLE is expected to be an instance of ACTORTYPE.")
(disjointWith Sensor MultiIndividualAgent)
(disjointWith AutomobileTypeByBrand RoadVehicleTypeByModel)
(comment canonicalRolesList "(#$canonicalRolesList EVENTTYPE LIST) means that in normal instances of EVENTTYPE, it is usual to find the roles listed in LIST filled by actors suiting the argument constraints of the roles.")
(disjointWith Trailer MountableTransporter)
(disjointWith GeneralAutoMarketCategory Organism-Whole)
(disjointWith Dancing TranslationAlongInternalLivingStructure)
(comment coaSupportingTask "(#$coaSupportingTask SUPPORTING SUPPORTED) means that SUPPORTED requires the completion of SUPPORTING.  SUPPORTING and SUPPORTED are not assigned to the same agent(s), or to different tiers of some organizational hierarchy.  Contrast this with #$coaSubTasks.")
(comment followingIntervalType-Cumulated "(#$followingIntervalType-Cumulated Type-A Type-B TYPE n) means that there are n-1 instances Type-1, ..., Type-(n-1) of TYPE, none of which is the same as Type-A or Type-B (or any among themselves), such that (#$succeedingIntervalType Type-A Type-1), (#$succeedingIntervalType Type-1 Type-2), ..., (#$succeedingIntervalType Type-(n-2) Type-(n-1)), (#$succeedingIntervalType Type-(n-1) Type-B).  (See comment for #$succeedingIntervalType.)  For example, (#$succeedingIntervalType #$Monday #$Tuesday) and (#$succeedingIntervalType #$Tuesday #$Wednesday) are true, and all of #$Monday, #$Tuesday and #$Wednesday are different instances of #$DayOfWeekType, and therefore (#$followingIntervalType-Cumulated #$Monday #$Wednesday #$DayOfWeekType 2) is true.  Note that for each suitable arg3 to #$followingIntervalType-Cumulated, there is a maximal integer for its arg4.  For example, if the arg3 is #$DayOfWeekType, the maximal integer for the arg4 is 7, and if the arg3 is #$MonthOfYearType, the maximal integer for the arg4 is 12, etc.  Note also that this predicate is functional in its arg4 place.")
(comment positiveVestedInterest "(#$positiveVestedInterest AGENT THING) means that the #$Agent-Generic AGENT has a positive interest in the #$TemporalThing THING.  If THING is an instance of #$SomethingExisting, then (#$positiveVestedInterest AGENT THING) will generally imply that AGENT has a positive interest in the preservation or flourishing of THING, and that AGENT will be adversely affected if THING is not preserved, or does not flourish.  If THING is an instance of #$Event, then (#$positiveVestedInterest AGENT THING) will generally imply that AGENT stands to benefit from the outcome of THING.  Note that the restriction of THING to #$TemporalThing means that it would be incorrect to use this predicate to say (e.g.) that Douglas Adams has a #$positiveVestedInterest in the number 42.  Rather, what he has a vested interest in is public popularity and faddism focusing on that number.  A fad is a #$TemporalThing, but the number 42 isn't.")
(comment Starvation "A collection of events.  Each instance of #$Starvation is an event in which
an #$Animal is so hungry as to be #$Starving.  If prolonged, it results in death.  NB: This is a #$SingleDoerAction (q.v.), so it doesn't cover cases wherein one creature withholds food from another.")
(comment Ambulation "A specialization of #$Locomotion-SolidSurface: the  collection of all locomotion events on solid surfaces in which an #$Animal moves by lifting its feet, moving them through the air, and setting them down in a new location. Includes walking (cf. #$Walking-Generic), #$Running, skipping, trotting, etc.")
(comment UrineTest "The collection of events in which urine is tested to determine the presence and amount of certain substances.")
(comment Growling "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$MakingAnOralSound.  Each instance of #$Growling is an event in which a #$GrowlingSound is produced.")
(comment LatePartFn "(#$LatePartFn TIME-INTERVAL) denotes the #$TimeInterval
expressed by the English phrase 'late TIME-INTERVAL' -- e.g., 'the
late 1980s.' (#$LatePartFn TIME-INTERVAL) thus denotes an interval 
that begins roughly 2/3 of the way through
TIME-INTERVAL, and is #$temporallyCoterminal with it.  Cf., #$latePartOfInterval.")
(comment EarlyPartFn "(#$EarlyPartFn TIME-INTERVAL) denotes the #$TimeInterval
expressed by the English phrase 'early TIME-INTERVAL' -- e.g., 'the
early 1990s.' (#$EarlyPartFn TIME-INTERVAL) thus denotes an interval that
begins at the same time as TIME-INTERVAL (see #$temporallyCooriginating) and 
ends approximately 1/3 of the way through it.  Cf. #$earlyPartOfInterval.")
(comment TomographyScan "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$XRayTest.  Each instance of #$TomographyScan is a medical test in which #$XRays are used to obtain a three-dimensional image of organs and other features inside a human body.  Tomography scans are also known as \"CAT\" or \"CT\" scans (the \"C\" is for \"computerized\").")
(comment inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric "This predicate relates a given unary, quantitative function (see #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars) to another that is an \"inverse\" of it, in the sense that whenever the latter function maps a thing ARG to some thing VALUE, the former function maps VALUE to ARG.   (#$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric FUNC INV) means that, for any ARG in the domain of INV (see #$inDomainOf), (FUNC (INV ARG)) is ARG.  For example, the #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric of the natural logarithm function (#$LogFn) is the exponential function (#$ExpFn) since (#$LogFn (#$ExpFn ARG)) is ARG for all ARG in the domain of #$ExpFn.  Note that this predicate is usually used to relate a quantitative function to what is considered its standard inverse, e.g. the cosine function to the arc cosine function.  Both arguments to #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric are instances of #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars and #$UnaryFunction. Note also that this function is not symmetric. For a more general and symmetric predicate that can be used to relate any one-to-one function (see #$OneToOneFunction) to its inverse, regardless of whether or not either is a quantitative function, see #$inverseFunctions.")
(comment allInstancesSubOrgs "(#$allInstancesSubOrgs SUBORGTYPE ORG) means that every instance of SUBORGTYPE is a #$subOrganizations of ORG.")
(comment CertificateOfDeposit "The collection of receipts for bank (or other financial institution) deposits in certificate form.  #$CertificateOfDeposits bear interest and are payable either on a specific date or after the passage of a specific amount of time.")
(comment DomesticatedAnimalType "The collection of all types of animals that are commonly domesticated by humans. Instances include #$Dog, #$Goldfish, #$Cattle, etc. Being an instance of #$DomesticatedAnimalType does not imply that all of a collection's instances are instances of #$DomesticatedAnimal - so there can be #$Dogs, #$Goldfish, etc., that are wild.")
(comment allAndOnlySubCollectionsAreInstancesOf "(#$allAndOnlySubCollectionsAreInstancesOf COL COL-TYPE) 
means that COL is the #$Collection of all instances of instances of COL-TYPE, 
an instance of #$CollectionType, and that COL-TYPE is the collection of all specializations of COL. See also #$SubCollectionsAreInstancesOfFn.")
(comment Pretzel "This is the collection of pretzels. A pretzel is a wheat flour based
baked good which is in the shape of a narrow cylinder, often twisted
into the familiar pretzel shape.")
(comment MonthsDuration "A #$UnitOfTime (q.v.) function that takes one or two real numbers as arguments and returns, as its value, a comparable #$Time-Quantity measured in months.  More precisely, an expression of the form (#$MonthsDuration NUM) denotes the (point-value) #$Time-Quantity of being exactly NUM months in duration, and an expression of the form (#$MonthsDuration MIN MAX) denotes the (properly interval-like) #$Time-Quantity of being at least MIN and at most MAX months in duration.")
(comment ExperimentalMicrotheory "The collection of microtheories which are used for experimental, testing, provisional, or other temporary purposes.")
(comment DaysDuration "A #$UnitOfTime (q.v.) function that takes one or two real numbers as arguments and returns, as its value, a comparable #$Time-Quantity measured in days.  More precisely, an expression of the form (#$DaysDuration NUM) denotes the (\"point-value\") #$Time-Quantity of being exactly NUM days in duration, and an expression of the form (#$DaysDuration MIN MAX) denotes the (properly interval-like) #$Time-Quantity of being at least MIN days and at most MAX days in duration.")
(comment YearsDuration "A #$UnitOfTime (q.v.) function that takes one or two real numbers as arguments and returns, as its value, a comparable #$Time-Quantity measured in years. More precisely, an expression of the form (#$YearsDuration NUM) denotes the (\"point-value\") #$Time-Quantity of being exactly NUM years in duration, and an expression of the form (#$YearsDuration MIN MAX) denotes the (properly interval-like) #$Time-Quantity of being at least MIN years and at most MAX years in duration.")
(comment SecondsDuration "A #$UnitOfTime function that takes one or two real numbers as arguments and returns, as its value, a comparable #$Time-Quantity measured in seconds.  More precisely, an expression of the form (#$SecondsDuration NUM) denotes the (point-value) #$Time-Quantity of being exactly NUM seconds in duration, and an expression of the form (#$SecondsDuration MIN MAX) denotes the (properly interval-like) #$Time-Quantity of being at least MIN and at most MAX seconds in duration.")
(comment resultDisjointWith "This predicate is used to specify that a certain collection is disjoint with any value returned by a particular collection-denoting function. (#$resultDisjointWith CDF COL) means that when the #$CollectionDenotingFunction CDF is applied to any sequence of arguments ARG1, ..., ARGn for which it is defined, the result will be disjoint with COL, i.e. (#$disjointWith (CDF ARG1...ARGn) COL).  See also #$resultGenl and #$resultNotIsa.")
(comment nativeLanguage "(#$nativeLanguage PERSON LANG) means that the #$Person PERSON is fluent in the #$NaturalLanguage LANG, and that LANG was (one of) the first language(s) spoken by PERSON.  The #$arg2Format for this predicate is #$SetTheFormat, to cover cases in which a person has two or more first languages (as might be the case when a child is raised in a bilingual household).")
(disjointWith BiologicalDevelopmentEvent Dying)
(comment BarrierSituation "A collection of individual situations. Each #$BarrierSituation consists of an arrangement of a #$Path-Customary and some other #$PartiallyTangible thing or things, such that movement along the path is blocked. If this blocking is accomplished by active physical contact, as in a #$Dam blocking the flow of water, the situation is an instance of #$BarrierSituation-PhysicalForce. If this blocking is accomplished by the likelihood of such contact, as in a #$Fence keeping #$Cows in a field, the situation is an instance of #$BarrierSituation-Deterrent. Note that these two specializations are #$not #$disjointWith each other.")
(disjointWith SawingSomething SlicingSomething)
(comment BiometricAuthenticationEvent "a specialization of #$AuthenticationEvent is a collection of events. Each instance of #$BiometricAuthenticationEvent is an event in which a #$BiometricAuthenticationDevice is used to determine whether or not a person has the right to access some individual.")
(disjointWith SawingSomething ChoppingSomething)
(comment Buttocks "An instance of #$UniqueAnatomicalPartType and a specialization of #$ExternalAnatomicalPart.  Each instance of #$Buttocks is a fleshy, bisected protuberance located just below the lower back of an animal.")
(comment Sauerkraut "A specialization of #$PickledFood and #$Cabbage-Foodstuff.  Each instance of #$Sauerkraut is some finely cut cabbage   that has been allowed to ferment in a brine made of its own juice with salt.")
(disjointWith SawingSomething StabbingSomething)
(comment ComputerInformationStorageMedia "#$ComputerInformationStorageMedia is the subset of #$InformationStorageMedia whose instances are all and only those entities on which instances of #$ComputerFileCopy may be stored.")
(disjointWith PhysiologicalFunction Dying)
(disjointWith DrillingSomething SawingSomething)
(comment CatchingWithHands "A specialization of #$ReceivingAnObject and #$CoordinatedBodyMovement.  Each instance of #$CatchingWithHands is an event in which an animal catches something (such as a ball) that is coming towards it with its hand(s).")
(comment ChangeOfResidence "The most general change of residence; includes moving to college, moving next door, anything which changes the residence of an actor.  Not directly a transportation event, but the transportation of person and belongings is implied.")
(comment MovieShowing "The collection of all #$InformationBearingWavePropagations which are sequences of images and (usually) sounds produced when a recorded instantiation of a #$Movie-CW is played for an audience with a #$MediaPlayingDevice such as a #$MovieProjector or a #$DVDPlayer.")
(comment movieTitleString "(#$movieTitleString MOVIE TITLE) relates a MOVIE to a string which is its TITLE.  For example, the 1977 movie <i>Star Wars</i> has the string \"Star Wars\" as its title.")
(comment Screaming "An instance of #$ControllableAutonomousActionType and a specialization of #$MakingAnOralSound.  Each instance of #$Screaming is an event in which an agent makes a loud, piercing oral sound, often as an expression of #$Fear or #$Anger.  The sound made in an instance of #$Screaming (i.e. the scream) may or may not be articulate.")
(comment ServingFoodOrDrink "A specialization of both #$GivingSomething and #$ServiceEvent.  Each instance of #$ServingFoodOrDrink is an event in which an item of food or drink is served.  Each such event involves the server picking up the food or drink from a preparation or pickup area, carrying it (cf. the scene #$CarryingWhileLocomoting) and ends when the food or drink is deposited at a location where those being served may eat or drink it (cf. the scene #$PuttingSomethingSomewhere).  Such events are typically part of the job of any restaurant server who waits on tables (cf. #$WaitPerson), and they may also be performed by a host or servant at a dinner party or banquet, or by a family member at a home meal.  Note that events in which a \"serving\" of food is placed from a common dish onto a diner's plate are not instances of #$ServingFoodOrDrink.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork "A specialization of #$ConceptualWork. Each instance of #$PropositionalConceptualWork is an abstract work constituted at least in part by propositional information.  Propositional information is information which has a truth value in some context (see #$Proposition).  Typically, instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork will be works whose instantiations are composed of (or merely contain) one or more spoken or written sentences. Positive examples of #$PropositionalConceptualWork include any instances of #$ProseLiteraryWork-CW (including instances of #$AcademicEssay and #$ExpositoryArticle).  A positive example that is <i>not</i> a literary work is any instance of #$MusicalComposition that has lyrics that express propositions; another positive example is a mostly graphical advertisement that also includes text that expresses propositions.")
(comment xzCrossSectionShape "The cross section shape in the xz plane. It is not really the xz plane in the absolute sense but in the intrinsic axes sense.")
(disjointWith SawingSomething Piercing)
(comment directorsFavoriteActors "(#$directorsFavoriteActors DIRECTOR ACTOR) means that ACTOR is one of DIRECTOR's favorite actors to work with, as indicated by the latter's having used the former in several different movies.  For example, #$JohnWayne was one of the favorite actors of the director John Ford.")
(disjointWith PoliticalElectionCampaign Election)
(comment hypothesizes "(#$hypothesizes AGENT PROP) means that AGENT suspends disbelief to consider the ramification of PROP being true.  AGENT need not believe [#$beliefs] that PROP is true, but may think it likely [#$thinksProbable], be totally unsure, or even believe PROP to be false [i.e. believe (#$not PROP)].")
(comment cotemporalSubEvents "The predicate #$cotemporalSubEvents is used to relate an event to some sub-event which has the same duration as the whole event, but doesn't necessarily include everything that happens in the whole event.  (#$cotemporalSubEvents WHOLE PART) means that WHOLE and PART are cotemporal events (i.e., they have the same exact duration), and PART is a component of WHOLE.  For example, a particular instance of #$RainStormAsEvent may have distinguishable #$cotemporalSubEvents for (1) raining and (2) wind blowing, and an instance of swimming #$Backstroke has separable events for (1) kicking and (2) arm motion throughout the swimming.  See also #$cotemporal, #$subEvents.")
(comment transferRouter-Generic "This predicate can be used to indicate whatever acts as a router in an instance of #$GeneralizedTransfer and therefore plays an active and crucial role in it. (#$transferRouter-Generic TRANS THING) means that THING acts as a router in the #$GeneralizedTransfer TRANS.  For example, suppose a trip TRANS via airplane taking a person PER from New York to Austin makes a stop in #$CityOfOmahaNE, where PER changes flights.  If 'CityOfOmahaNEAirport-Org' is the instance of #$AirportOrganization running the airport located in the #$CityOfOmahaNE, then we can (probably) say that 'CityOfOmahaNEAirport-Org' is a router in the #$GeneralizedTransfer            TRANS of PER and represent this by (#$transferRouter-Generic TRANS 'CityOfOmahaNBAirport-Org').  See also #$transferRelay-Generic.")
(comment Campaign "The collection of all extended operations with a focused purpose undertaken by a group of people.  Types of campaigns include medical campaigns, #$PropagandaCampaigns, and #$PoliticalElectionCampaigns.")
(comment Proposition "A specialization of #$Information-Content (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Proposition is an abstract propositional thing that has some truth value in some context or \"world\".  A proposition is assumed to be representable (at least in principle) by a sentence in some formal or natural language.  But it should <i>not</i> be assumed that propositions are themselves intrinsically linguistic items in the way that sentences or formulae are.  Indeed, propositions are often viewed as extra-linguistic, intensional entities that (while not sentences themselves) are represented or expressed by meaningful sentences (or, on some versions of this view, by concrete tokens of sentences).  On such a view it is possible for distinct sentences (either from the same language or from different languages) to express the very same proposition; e.g. 'Snow is white.', 'White is the color of snow.', 'Schnee ist weiss.' (in German), and (arguably) '(#$relationAllInstance #$objectHasColor #$SnowMob #$WhiteColor)' (in CycL).  Similarly, it is sometimes the case that a single sentence -- when used in different contexts -- will express distinct propositions; e.g. 'I am hungry.' said by you and said by me.  Most formal languages (such as a first-order predicate calculus) and natural languages (such as English) include the resources for composing expressions that represent propositions from component expressions (that might or might not themselves represent other propositions).")
(comment RainStormAsObject "Heavy rain and lots of wind.  See also #$RainStormAsEvent.")
(comment IntelAttack "A subset of (#$AttemptingFn #$StealingInformation),  #$IntelAttack is the collection of attacks perpetrated with  the ambition of retrieving mission-critical enemy data. In this sort of attack, perpetrators first determine what information to target, then determine the location of theinformation, and, finally, obtain the information from the enemy.  The information targeted can be protected by any #$levelOfSecurity. Because instances of #$IntelAttack are not all done in secret, #$IntelAttack is not a specialization of #$IntelligenceGathering (q.v.).")
(comment encounteredObject "(#$encounteredObject ENCOUNTER OBJECT) means that the object encountered in the #$EncounteringSomething ENCOUNTER is OBJECT.")
(comment CycLNonAtomicTerm "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of both #$CycLFormula 
and #$CycLRepresentedTerm (qq.v.).  #$CycLNonAtomicTerm is the 
collection of non-atomic denotational terms in the CycL language.  
A CycL term is _non-atomic_ if it is constructible from other CycL 
terms via the syntax of CycL.  A CycL term is said to be 
\"denotational\" if it is the type of term that can have a 
denotatum (or assigned value; see #$CycLDenotationalTerm).  
#$CycLNonAtomicTerm thus includes all CycL denotational terms 
except constants and variables.  A CycL non-atomic term (or \"NAT\") 
consists of a CycL expression denoting a #$Function-Denotational 
followed by one or more CycL terms, with the entire sequence 
enclosed in parentheses.  The NAT itself denotes the value (if any) 
of this function for the denotations of the other terms taken as 
arguments.  (If there is no such value then the NAT has no denotatum;
see #$undefined.)  NATs are also known as \"denotational formulas\", 
in contrast to \"logical formulas\" (i.e. sentences).  Currently, 
there are two main types of NAT: (i) #$HLNonAtomicReifiedTerms (or 
\"NART\"s), which are a type of #$HLReifiedDenotationalTerm and are 
implemented with data structures that have indexing that enables all 
uses of the NAT to be retrieved, and (ii) #$ELNonAtomicTerms (or 
\"NAUT\"s), which have no such indexing and remain in the 
form of an EL expression in the assertions in which
they occur.")
(comment costarsIn "(#$costarsIn ACTOR1 ACTOR2 MOVIE) means that actors ACTOR1 and ACTOR2 both appear as actors in the same film MOVIE.  For example (#$costarsIn #$LeonardoDicaprio #$KathyBates #$Titanic-TheMovie) means that both LeonardoDicaprio and  KathyBates appear in 'Titanic', the movie.  Note that, although the name  might suggest otherwise, the two actors need not have the same billing or  status in the movie. See also #$costars.")
(comment AnimalTraining "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType #$and a specialization of #$Training.  Each instance of #$AnimalTraining is an action in which an agent trains some #$NonHumanAnimal to behave in a certain way.")
(comment Verification "A specialization of #$PurposefulAction.  Each instance of #$Verification is an event in which some intelligent agent either confirms or disconfirms the truth of some proposition.")
(comment HurricaneAsObject "A cyclone with high-speed winds and thunderstorms. See also #$HurricaneAsEvent.")
(comment hasAward "(#$hasAward THING AWARD) means that THING (a person, book, building, etc.) has the AWARD.")
(comment SolvingAProblem "A specialization of #$AnalyzingSomething and #$PurposefulAction.  Each instance of #$SolvingAProblem is an event in which an agent uses mental activity to devise a (correct or incorrect) solution to a problem.  Important specializations of #$SolvingAProblem include #$Diagnosing and #$ResolvingAConflict. See also #$problemToSolve, #$ProblemSolvingSituation.")
(comment FileServerProgram "#$FileServerProgram is the collection of programs that allow networked computers to act as file servers by enabling them to share remote disk drives so they appear to be local.")
(disjointWith (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet Octopus MaleHuman FemaleHuman)) (CollectionDifferenceFn Thing (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet Octopus MaleHuman FemaleHuman))))
(disjointWith CreditSale SaleByCreditCard)
(comment BreakingAPhysicalDevice "A collection of events.  In a #$BreakingAPhysicalDevice event, some #$PhysicalDevice becomes no longer #$behaviorCapable of performing its #$primaryFunction.  Such breaking events are #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvents, and the device is the #$objectOfStateChange in the event.")
(comment IterativeEventType "The class of all event types whose instances typically consist of the repetition of some basic 'iterated event unit'. E.g., Dripping, Bouncing, TickingOfClock.")
(comment Series "A specialization of #$Group (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Series is a complex temporal thing in which two or more other things are ordered in a linear fashion.  Examples include a line of people at a ticket booth (an instance of the specialization #$PhysicalSeries) and a series of pitches in a baseball game (an instance of the specialization #$EventSeries).  In each instance of #$Series, there is some relation by which its members are ordered (see the related predicate #$seriesOrderedBy); this relation often varies from series to series.")
(comment WorkingEvent "Activities which require a certain amount of physical or mental exertion, and which are not done purely for recreation, but rather as part of an occupation (to make a living), or to contribute to some goal, or out of some other obligation or necessity. Positive examples include a student doing homework for school, a homeowner mowing her lawn, a teacher teaching class, etc.")
(comment structuredKnowledgeSourceName "This predicate relates a structured knowlege source to its name, or string identifier.")
(comment Buying "A specialization of #$ExchangeOfUserRights and #$BuyingActivity.  Each instance of #$Buying is an event in which an agent (the #$buyer) voluntarily pays (or agrees to pay later) for goods or services provided by another agent (the #$seller).  A buying event might include subevents of negotiating, paying, and the transferring of the purchased thing to the buyer.  Examples of #$Buying include: purchasing fighter jets for the Navy; buying a Pepsi from a vending machine; ordering dinner at a restaurant; acquiring a life insurance policy for oneself; mail-ordering clothes from Sears; shopping at a farmers' market.  #$Buying is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$MonetaryExchangeOfUserRights, since there need not be any money exchanged in the actual #$Buying event; consider, e.g., a #$CreditSale.")
(disjointWith Bartering MoneyTransfer)
(disjointWith (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet GasField-Developed OilField-Developed)) (CollectionDifferenceFn ExcavationSite (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet GasField-Developed OilField-Developed))))
(comment StructuredSportsEvent "Sports where scores are kept, specific actions can score a point or be called a foul, where fouls can result in penalties, not just disqualification.  Things that we tend to call games.")
(disjointWith FurniturePiece Tower)
(disjointWith MakingAnAgreement AttackOnObject)
(comment flashPoint "(#$flashPoint STUFF TEMP)  means that TEMP is the lowest temperature at which a substance can form an ignitable mixture in air near its surface. The lower the #$flashPoint, the easier it is to ignite the material.")
(comment EnglishWord "A subcollection of #$LexicalWord. Each instance of #$EnglishWord is a lexical word of the English language.  Note that different inflectional forms of a given word do not count as different lexical words; for example, #$Eat-TheWord encompasses the strings 'eat', 'eats', 'eating', and 'ate'.")
(comment agentPopularity "The popularity of an agent.")
(comment MovingAComputerFile-CopyingAndDeleting "A specialization of #$FileOperation and #$GeneralizedTransfer. In each instance of #$MovingAComputerFile-CopyingAndDeleting, a computer reads a file, creates a new file with the same contents in a different physical location (see #$CopyingAComputerFile) and then deletes the original file (see #$DeletingAFile).  For events in which a computer file is only <i>metaphorically</I> moved, see #$MovingAComputerFile-Metaphorically.")
(comment Conference "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$Convention-Meeting.  Each instance of #$Conference is an #$Event-Organized in which members sharing a certain professional interest (e.g. #$RealEstate, #$Physics, #$ArtificialIntelligence) gather to share their views on that interest.")
(disjointWith StrictlyMentalEvent ViolentAction)
(comment SupportingOnesWeight "Supporting one's weight against gravity. Intended to mean 'grounded' in some way, so that a person hang gliding is not performing #$SupportingOnesWeight .")
(comment IntegerExtent "A specialization of #$IntervalOnNumberLine.  Each instance of #$IntegerExtent is an interval that includes a contiguous range of integers (see #$Integer) and which includes nothing which is not an integer.")
(comment cooperatingParticipants "This predicate can be used to indicate that a particular agent participates in a particular cooperation event in order to further the goals of that event.  (#$cooperatingParticipants COOP AGENT) means that AGENT participates in COOP in such a way as to further the goal(s) of   COOP, and does so at least in part for that very reason.  (Note that AGENT might also have other reasons for participating in COOP.)  AGENT is both a #$socialParticipants and a #$deliberateActors in COOP, and there is at least one other AGENT2 such that  (#$partnersInCooperation AGENT AGENT2 COOP) holds.  Example-positive: the #$UnitedStatesArmy cooperating with the Red Cross in an instance of #$ReliefOperation.  (But note that the individual soldiers involved in the operation might not be #$cooperatingParticipants.)   Example-negative: a construction worker helping to build a post office, not so that there will be a post office in that area, but in order to earn  money to feed her family.")
(comment partnersInCooperation "(#$partnersInCooperation AGT1 AGT2 COOP) means that AGT1 and AGT2 are #$IntelligentAgents who cooperate with each other in the #$CooperationEvent COOP.  Note that there could be more than two agents who cooperate in COOP, but there are at least two. Accordingly, AGT1 and AGT2 must be distinct agents when (#$partnersInCooperation AGT1 AGT2 COOP) holds.  See also #$cooperatingParticipants.")
(comment OpeningSomething "The collection of events in which some agent opens some physical object using tools or a physical or anatomical part.  Not all of the entries on the #$objectOfStateChange slot of a particular #$OpeningSomething event necessarily have portals or portal coverings (think, for example, of opening a coconut or a watermelon).  So at this most general level, #$OpeningSomething just means gaining access to the 'inside' of some object through an 'outer' layer or portion.  However, see the English of #$OpeningAContainerArtifact.")
(comment Murder "An instance of #$PunctualEventType and a specialization of both #$KillingPerson and #$CriminalAct.  Each instance of #$Murder is an event in which someone is illegally killed.  The key features that make a #$KillingByOrganism a #$Murder are (a) the organism killed is a #$Person; and (b) the killing violates some law.")
(comment MakingAThreat "A collection of all acts of making a threat. When an agent makes a threat, that agent conveys to a #$recipientOfInfo that he wishes to make the content of the threat true.")
(comment CreationEvent "A specialization of #$CreationOrDestructionEvent.  In each instance of #$CreationEvent, at least one instance of #$SomethingExisting (q.v.) is brought into existence (see #$outputsCreated).")
(comment CreationOrDestructionEvent "A specialization of #$Event-Localized.  During each instance of #$CreationOrDestructionEvent, one or more instances of #$SomethingExisting (q.v.) come into or go out of existence.  Specializations of this collection include #$CreationEvent, #$DestructionEvent, #$Manufacturing, #$MakingSomething, and #$KillingByOrganism.")
(comment directQuote "(#$directQuote AGENT STRING) means that, at some time, AGENT actually wrote or said the sequence of words represented by STRING.  See also #$indirectQuote.")
(comment BelieverFn "(#$BelieverFn SYSTEM) denotes the collection of all intelligent agents who believe the propositions that compose SYSTEM.")
(comment indirectQuote "(#$indirectQuote AGENT PROP) means that, at some time, AGENT wrote or said something that had PROP as part of its propositional information content.  See also #$directQuote.")
(comment MakingAPlan "An instance of #$HumanCapabilityType and a specialization of #$Planning and #$CreatingAnArtifact.  Each instance of #$MakingAPlan is an event in which an #$IntelligentAgent generates a #$Plan (q.v.).  Note that instances of #$MakingAPlan do not include (for example, as sub-events) the actual implementation of any plan; an actual implementation of a plan will be an activity that follows some instance of this collection.")
(disjointWith CommunicationAct-Single DistributionEvent)
(comment Criminal "The collection of all individuals who have performed an instance of #$CriminalAct.")
(comment Objecting-CommunicationAct "A specialization of #$CommunicationAct-Single.  In each instance of #$Objecting-CommunicationAct, some #$Agent-Generic objects to something.  An objection may be communicated to the agent(s) that the objector believes responsible for the objectionable state of affairs, or it may be raised with a third party or parties.")
(comment ShapingSomething "A specialization of #$MaterialHandling (q.v.). Each instance of #$ShapingSomething is an event in which something (usually more or less \"plastic\" in nature) is given a definite shape, or has its shape changed, through the purposeful actions of an agent that cause a #$ShapeChangeEvent (q.v.).  Specializations include #$Carving, #$Sewing, and #$SpreadingOntoSurface.  In many (but not  all) cases, an instance of #$ShapingSomething is also a #$PhysicalCreationEvent  (see e.g. #$MakingPottery).  Normally, a #$ShapingSomething concerns the basic,  overall shape of the object of change (see #$objectOfStateChange).  Thus, a  #$PolishingSomething event is not a #$ShapingSomething, as it doesn't affect  the basic shape of the object being polished.")
(comment RemovingSomething "A specialization of #$PurposefulAction.  Each instance of #$RemovingSomething is an event in which an agent deliberately removes one object from another object.  The removed object is related to the instance of #$RemovingSomething via the predicate #$objectRemoved, while the object to which it belonged prior to its removal is related via the predicate #$objectRemaining.  In different types of removals, either the #$objectRemoved or the #$objectRemaining may be destroyed, or both may survive. Specializations of #$RemovingSomething include #$Ablation, #$RemovingSomething-Translational, and #$CuttingOffAPiece.  The difference between this collection and  #$SeparationEvent is that instances of #$RemovingSomething are purposeful actions, while instances of #$SeparationEvent need not be purposeful.")
(comment ProjectilePropelling "A specialization of #$Propelling.  Each instance of #$ProjectilePropelling is an event in which an object is propelled, and in which no forces other than gravity (and possibly friction) are acting in the motion resulting from the initial propulsion force.")
(comment Lying "A specialization of #$Informing, (#$AttemptingFn #$Lying), and #$Pretending.  Each instance of #$Lying is an event in which the performer is not telling the truth, believes that what he is saying is not true, and intends to be saying what he says to get his audience to believe what he says.")
(comment ReceivingPayment "A collection of events; a subset of #$MoneyTransfer.  In an instance of #$ReceivingPayment, an #$Agent-Generic (or representative thereof) receives money that another agent owes as a debt to the first agent.  See #$Paying.")
(comment WeddingEvent-Generic "The class of all events associated with marriage.  Subsets include #$WeddingCeremony and #$WeddingReception.")
(comment SteeringADeviceByHand "An instance of #$CumulativeEventType and a specialization of #$GuidingAMovingObject and #$HandlingADevice.  Each instance of #$SteeringADeviceByHand is an event in which an agent manually controls the course of a #$TransportationDevice in a #$TransportationEvent.  See also #$SteeringFn.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Graphical "An instance of #$ComputerProgramTypeByFunction and a specialization of #$FileReadingProgram-Visual.  Each instance of #$ComputerGameProgram-Graphical is a #$ComputerGameProgram which implements some kind of graphical representation of what is going on in the game.")
(comment StoringSomething "A specialization of #$LocatingInIntendedPosition and #$TakingCareOfSomething.  Each instance of #$StoringSomething is an event in which an agent puts some #$PartiallyTangible in a safe place for later use.")
(comment Bottle "cylindrical container for holding stuff, usually liquid, with a single portal on the very top")
(comment Supervising "A specialization of #$InfluencingAnAgent.  Each instance of #$Supervising is an event in which an agent (the supervisor) directs and oversees the performance of some #$PurposefulAction which is performed by some other agent/s (the supervisee/s).  In any instance of #$Supervising there is just one action being supervised; however, that action may have multiple performers.")
(comment PilotingAShip "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$GuidingAMovingObject.  Each instance of #$PilotingAShip is an event in which some agent (the #$driverActor in an instance of (#$TransportViaFn #$Ship), often a #$ShipCaptain) exercises primary control of the path of a #$Ship through water.")
(comment ExchangeOfOpinion "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$Conversation.  Each instance of #$ExchangeOfOpinion is a #$Conversation in which two or more people deliberately make known to each other some of their #$beliefs in a serious discussion.")
(comment Experimenting "A specialization of #$CheckingWhetherConditionObtains.  Each instance of #$Experimenting is an event in which one performs tests to determine whether an hypothesis is true.  The tests in question need not be empirical in nature (cf. #$ThoughtExperimenting).  For the common case in which the tests in question <i>are</i> empirical, see #$Experimenting-Empirical.")
(comment playsRoleWithFrequency "(#$playsRoleWithFrequency INSTANCE SITTYPE ROLE FREQ) means that INSTANCE plays ROLE in instances of SITTYPE with the frequency FREQ.  Note that FREQ can be a numeric value, or it can be a coarse-grained measure such as  #$Often or #$Rarely. For a version of this predicate which is type-level with respect to the first argument, see #$frequencyOfActionType.  To state more concisely that an object 'regularly' plays ROLE in instances of SIT-TYPE, see #$playsRoleInActionTypeRegularly.")
(comment webSiteURL "A TemporallyQualifiableRelation that realtes a web site to its URL. (#$webSiteURL WS PATH) means that PATH is the URL (#$UniformResourceLocator) for the gateway page -- the main introductory page -- of the #$WorldWideWebSite WS. URLs may change over the course of a web site's lifetime, so the assertion should always receive appropriate temporal qualification.")
(comment ThrowingAnObject "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$ProjectilePropelling.  Each instance of #$ThrowingAnObject is a #$VoluntaryBodyMovement in which someone sends something flying through the air by quickly moving or straightening his #$Arm, and, in the process, releasing the object from his grasp.")
(comment Bombing "An instance of #$AttackTypeByWeaponType and a specialization of #$AttackOnTangible.  Each instance of this of this collection is an event in which at least one bomb (see #$Bomb) is dropped or placed in a region and detonated.  Each instance of #$Bombing has at least one instance of #$Explosion as a sub event.  If no explosion occurs, the event is not an instance of #$Bombing.")
(comment FindingAPhysicalObject "The collection of events in which a physical object is found (located, discovered, etc.).")
(comment decreasesImmediately "(#$decreasesImmediately PERIOD THING SLOT) means that the value for the predicate SLOT applied to THING (in the first argument position) is lower immediately after (#$STIF q.v.) PERIOD than it was immediately before (#$STIB q.v.) PERIOD.  PERIOD may be any instance of #$TemporalThing, i.e., anything that has a time period associated with it, whether an event, an entity, a life stage, etc. No causal connection is implied between the #$TemporalThing PERIOD and the decrease.  See #$NoteAboutQuantityChangePredicates for a map of related predicates.")
(comment DisasterEvent "A specialization of #$Emergency.  Each instance of #$DisasterEvent is a #$PhysicalDestructionEvent in which at least some of the objects destroyed or harmed are considered to be of significant value.  (We use 'value' in its most general sense, to include monetary value, social value, moral value, etc.)  Obviously, this is a very subjective and norm-laden concept; however, there are many things which <i>clearly</i> qualify as #$DisasterEvents, e.g. #$TitanicSinking, #$BombingOfHiroshima, and #$TerroristAttack-September-11-2001-NYC-PENN-WDC.  Two important specializations are #$NaturalDisaster and #$ManMadeDisaster.")
(comment Crucifying "An instance of #$DurativeEventType and a specialization of #$Execution-Judicial and #$Torture.  Each instance of #$Crucifying is an event in which an agent executes a human by nailing that human to a #$Cross-TortureDevice.")
(comment EntertainmentEvent "A specialization of #$CulturalEvent and #$ServiceEvent. Each instance of this collection is an activity performed by one or more agents primarily to amuse, entertain or otherwise divert others. Every instance of #$EntertainmentEvent necessarily has among its subevents both an instance of (#$PerformingFn #$EntertainmentEvent) and an instance of (#$AttendingFn #$EntertainmentEvent). It is thus distinguished from #$RecreationalActivity: a #$RecreationalActivity need not involve any entertainers, whereas an instance of #$EntertainmentEvent will always involve both an entertainer, and an entertainee. Note: This collection is more general than #$EntertainmentPerformance. For instance, #$JokeTelling is a specialization of #$EntertainmentEvent, since it involves both an entertainer and an entertainee, Such an event, however, can occur under informal circumstances, and need not be a formal performance. Thus #$JokeTelling is not a spec of #$EntertainmentPerformance.  NB: one need not be an #$Entertainer to play the role of entertainer in an #$EntertainmentEvent.")
(comment CycLConstant "The collection of all constants in the CycL language. #$CycLConstant has as instances all #$CycLAtomicTerms other than #$CycLVariables and #$SubLAtomicTerms (qq.v.).  Orthographically, CycL constants are those atomic terms (i.e. terms not constructable from other terms via CycL syntax) that are prefixed by '#$' in their printed (as opposed to their HTML-displayed) representations.  For example, '#$Dog' and '#$isa' are CycL constants, while other CycL terms like '?X', '42', and  '(#$GovernmentFn #$France)' are not.")
(disjointWith Execution-Judicial Murder)
(disjointWith Euthanasia Execution-Judicial)
(comment Conservatism "An instance of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem.  Agents with this ideology place an emphasis on the value of stability and tradition in politics, and are generally skeptical of proposals to radically reshape society via government policies.  In the United States, #$Conservatism is associated with laissez faire economics, opposition to abortion, and other particular policies, but none of these is essential to the ideology itself.")
(comment Execution-Judicial "A specialization of (#$KillingFn #$HomoSapiens) and #$Punishing. Each element of #$Execution-Judicial is a  judicially sanctioned action of putting a person to death as a punishment for some crime. Thus, it excludes Mafia 'hits' and euthanasia, even if the euthanasia is sanctioned by some part of the judicial system.")
(comment ArrangingObjects "A specialization of #$LocatingInIntendedPosition.  Each instance of #$ArrangingObjects is an event in which an agent places physical objects in a desired #$Configuration.")
(comment PerformanceDegradation-Computer "A specialization of #$ComputerActivity.  This is the collection of all events in which the performance of a computer is noticeably degraded.  A notable specialization is #$ComputerCrashing (q.v.).")
(comment Marxism "An instance of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem.  Agents with this ideology hold, following #$KarlMarx, that in a capitalist economy the working class (i.e. the proletariat) is exploited by those who control the means of production (i.e. the bourgeoisie).  Adherents of #$Marxism are often (but not necessarily) adherents of #$Communism (q.v.).")
(comment DeliveringSomeoneSomething "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$DeliveryServiceEvent and #$TransportationEvent.  Each instance of #$DeliveringSomeoneSomething is an event in which one agent brings some #$PartiallyTangible thing to another agent as a service.  The object delivered must be transported over a significant distance; thus we do not consider (e.g.) a waiter bringing food to a diner's table to be an instance of #$DeliveringSomeoneSomething.")
(comment AFewSecondsDuration "An instance of both #$Time-Quantity and #$OrderOfMagnitudeInterval (qq.v.) that is the interval of between 2 and 30 seconds duration.")
(comment Liberalism-SocialDemocratic "An instance of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem.  Agents with this ideology advocate signficant government intervention in capitalist economies to ameliorate inequality of material resources among citizens.  #$Liberalism-SocialDemocratic should not be confused with #$Liberalism-Classical (q.v.).")
(comment stockTickerSymbol "(#$stockTickerSymbol COMPANY SYMBOL) means that SYMBOL is the string used to identify COMPANY on some #$StockExchange.")
(comment MonetaryExchangeOfUserRights "A specialization of both #$ExchangeOfUserRights and #$MoneyTransaction.  Each instance of #$MonetaryExchangeOfUserRights is an event in which two instances of #$SocialBeing (who are related to the event via specializations of the predicate #$exchangers) perform two reciprocal instances of #$TransferringPossession, one of which is an instance of #$MoneyTransfer.  The agent who performs the instance of #$MoneyTransfer (this agent is related to the event via the predicate #$buyer) gives some money to the other agent (who is related to the event via the predicate #$seller) in order to gain some user rights over an object possessed by the #$seller (this object is related to the event via the predicate #$objectPaidFor).  The #$seller relinquishes some user rights over the #$objectPaidFor to the #$buyer, in exchange for the #$buyer's money (which is related to the event via the predicate #$objectTendered).  Notable specializations of #$MonetaryExchangeOfUserRights include #$Renting and #$Buying.")
(comment ArrangingObjects "A specialization of #$LocatingInIntendedPosition and #$ConfiguringEvent.  Each instance of #$ArrangingObjects is an event in which an agent deliberately places physical objects in a desired #$Configuration.")
(comment obligationFulfilled "(#$obligationFulfilled ACT OB) means that the #$Action ACT fulfills the #$Obligation OB. Note that this is stronger than an action merely being in conformity with some obligation, but is supposed to indicate that an action somehow positively fulfills an obligation. For instance, paying the minimum balance owed on my #$CreditCard would fulfill my financial obligation to the credit card company, but it would not fulfill my obligation to honor my father and mother, although it is not in conflict with that obligation.")
(comment Feminism "An instance of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem.  Agents with this ideology advocate political, social, and economic equality for women.")
(comment Transaction "A specialization of #$CooperationEvent.  Each instance of #$Transaction is an event in which goods, services, or funds are willingly exchanged or transferred.  Specializations include #$Investing, #$MoneyTransfer, and #$Bartering.")
(comment decreasesSlot "(#$decreasesSlot EVENT PRED) means that the #$objectOfStateChange in EVENT has a lower value for PRED after EVENT than before EVENT.")
(comment PoliticalBeliefSystem "The class of #$BeliefSystems that are political ideologies.  Sample elements of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem include #$RepublicanPartyIdeology, #$RightistIdeology, #$Pacifism, etc.")
(comment IntermediateVocabFunction "The collection of instances of #$Function-Denotational  that contribute to NL parsing, often by serving  to form an intermediate layer of CycL between  the input to be parsed and the final, terse CycL  to be used in inferencing.")
(comment formerName-Date "(#$formerName-Date THING DATE OLDNAME) means that before the date DATE, the thing THING was named OLDNAME.  The name after that date changed or it took on an additional name.  If the date of name change is unknown, see also #$formerName.")
(comment GivingAnAward "A specialization of #$GivingSomething.  In each instance of #$GivingAnAward, an agent bestows some award on another agent, typically in recognition of some accomplishment or beneficial act done by the agent receiving the award.  See also #$AwardPractice.")
(comment IntermediateVocabTerm "The collection of Cyc terms that are important  for NL parsing by contributing to an intermediate  vocabulary between the English to be parsed, and  the terse CycL that will be used in inferencing.")
(comment Fascism "An instance of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem.  Agents with this ideology hold, following #$BenitoMussolini, that the state, being more important than the individual, should exert near-total control over the activities of its citizens.")
(comment MakingAPromise "A specialization of #$Informing and #$ObligationCreatingEvent.  Each instance of #$MakingAPromise is an event in which someone promises to do something, thereby creating an obligation to do that thing.")
(comment AFewMinutesDuration "An instance of both #$Time-Quantity and #$OrderOfMagnitudeInterval (qq.v.) that is the interval of between 2 and 10 minutes duration.")
(comment Liberal-SocialDemocrat "An instance of #$PersonTypeByBeliefSystem.  Each instance of #$Liberal-SocialDemocrat is an agent who believes in #$Liberalism-SocialDemocratic (q.v.).")
(comment MakingACrashingSound "A specialization of #$EmittingSound.  Each instance of #$MakingACrashingSound is an event in which a 'crash'-type sound is produced.")
(comment AFewHoursDuration "An instance of both #$Time-Quantity and #$OrderOfMagnitudeInterval (qq.v.) that is the interval of between 2 and 10 hours duration.")
(comment ExposureEvent "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType.  Each instance of #$ExposureEvent is an #$Event the central focus of which is something's being exposed to something else for some period of time.  (The notion of X's being exposed to Y is difficult to define precisely, but very roughly it means that Y has access to, or can reach, X.)  A notable specialization is #$ExposureToSubstance.  For the action that initiates an #$ExposureEvent, see #$ExposingSomething.")
(disjointWith Stealing BorrowingSomething)
(comment AFewDecadesDuration "An instance of both #$Time-Quantity and #$OrderOfMagnitudeInterval (qq.v.) that is the interval of between 2 and 10 decades duration.")
(comment ExposureToSubstance "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$ExposureEvent.  Each instance of #$ExposureToSubstance is an event in which some #$PartiallyTangible is exposed to some other #$PartiallyTangible for some period of time.  For cases in which the thing or stuff to which the object is exposed is not (necessarily) an instance of #$PartiallyTangible (e.g. exposure to #$Light), use the more general #$ExposureEvent.")
(comment ComputerCrashing "An instance of #$ComputerActivityType and a specialization of #$ChangingDeviceState and #$PerformanceDegradation-Computer.  Each instance of #$ComputerCrashing is an event in which a #$Computer, often unexpectedly, loses its ability to function properly.  #$ComputerCrashing does not include events in which a computer shuts down by following a program provided for the purpose of carefully shutting it down.  A user may, however, intentionally crash a computer system.  Often an instance of #$ComputerCrashing will have multiple instances of #$ComputerProcessCrashing as #$subEvents.")
(comment Acknowledging-CommunicationAct "A specialization of #$CommunicationAct-Single.  Each instance of this collection is an action that conveys information to the effect that the performing agent has received some prior #$CommunicationAct-Single (which was performed by another agent).  An example is Joan saying, `I hear you' in response to Bette's exclaiming, `What a dump!'  Note that acknowledging can be implicit; for example, one can acknowledge a question by answering it.")
(comment Accusing "A specialization of #$AttributingResponsibility and #$HostileSocialAction.  Each instance of #$Accusing is an event in which an agent declares that another agent (the #$accused) is responsible for some inappropriate or undesirable state of affairs.")
(comment CableTVTransmitting "A collection of #$Events; a subset of #$TVTransmitting.  In every #$CableTVTransmitting event, an intangible #$InformationBearingThing (usually a #$MovingImageWithSound) is transmitted to a #$TelevisionSet via #$CoaxialCable.  See also #$TVBroadcasting and #$TVMediaProduct.")
(comment MakingAPrediction "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$StatingSomething.  Each instance of #$MakingAPrediction is a #$CommunicationAct-Single in which an agent makes an assertion about what will be the case at some point in the future.")
(comment contemporaryInArg "(#$contemporaryInArg PRED N) means that the #$Relation PRED is such that whenever a formula (PRED ... ARGN ...), with ARGN a #$TemporalThing, is true at a moment in time, it will be the case that the moment belongs to the temporal extent of ARGN (i.e., that ARGN temporally subsumes the moment).  For example, it's the case that (#$contemporaryInArg #$awareOf 1).  So from the assertion (#$holdsIn (#$YearFn 1992) (#$awareOf Fred #$GeorgeWashington)), we can conclude (given that Fred is a #$TemporalThing) that Fred was alive throughout (temporally subsumes) 1992.  But it's not the case that (#$contemporaryInArg #$awareOf 2).  And indeed we wouldn't want to conclude that #$GeorgeWashington was alive throughout 1992.  In general (with the qualifications indicated below), an assertion (#$holdsIn TIME (PRED ... ARGN ...)), with PRED such that (#$contemporaryInArg PRED N) and ARGN a #$TemporalThing, licenses the conclusion (#$temporallySubsumes ARGN TIME).  And an assertion (#$holdsSometimeDuring TIME (PRED ... ARGN ...)) licenses the conclusion (#$temporallyIntersects ARGN TIME).  Although what constitutes a moment can vary with context, for most microtheories explicit considerations of temporal granularity (in this sense) don't come into play.  In particular, in the case of most microtheories, one doesn't have to worry about the possibility of gaps in the fabric of time between moments.  (Such gaps would undermine the conclusion above about temporal subsumption.)  Another qualification is that ARGN is not a #$IndexicalFirstOrderTerm; in practice, it almost never is.  In order for a predicate PRED to be contemporary in its Nth argument-place, the arity of PRED must be greater than or equal to N, and the argument-type of the Nth argument-place of PRED must be non-disjoint with #$TemporalThing (or, more generally, the intersection of the argument-types of the Nth argument-place of PRED must be non-disjoint with #$TemporalThing).  This is to bar cases in which a predicate would otherwise trivially qualify as contemporary in its Nth argument-place.  Note that #$CoexistingObjectsPredicates are contemporary in both their first and second argument-places, and #$CotemporalPredicates are contemporary in at least one argument-place.")
(comment SpareRib-MeatCut "The collection of spare ribs. Spare ribs are a meat cut from the #$RibCage section of a #$Pig. These are often barbecued and served with sauce, and are very popular in the Southern United States.")
(comment EngagementEvent "The collection of engagements of #$Persons to get married. An engagement lasts from the time two #$Persons agree to marry one another until either (1) they get married or (2) they break off the engagement.  The #$firstSubEvents is a #$EngagementEventPart-BecomingEngaged.")
(comment Synthesis-Generic "A specialization of #$Action and #$CreationEvent.  Each instance of #$Synthesis-Generic is an event in which an #$Agent-Generic synthesizes (combines, puts together) two or more instances of #$SomethingExisting (e.g. #$Paints or #$LinguisticObjects) to create something new.")
(comment LockingSomething "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$ChangingDeviceState.  Each instance of #$LockingSomething is an action which changes the state of some lockable device from #$Device-Unlocked to #$Device-Locked.")
(disjointWith Rubbing FrictionlessMotion)
(disjointWith Rubbing Sliding-LowFriction)
(comment BarleyPlant "A specialization of #$CerealPlant.  Each instance of #$BarleyPlant is a barley plant.")
(comment GreetingSomeone "The collection of actions performed by one #$Agent-Generic to greet another.  Includes verbal and physical greetings.")
(comment LocatingInIntendedPosition "A collection of events, and a subcollection of
#$PuttingSomethingSomewhere.  In each instance of
#$LocatingInIntendedPosition, an #$Agent-Generic guides an #$objectMoving to
an intended #$toLocation; that is, the #$toLocation corresponds to the
#$target of the motion.  Examples include: placing a book in the
correct place on the library shelves, hanging a picture on the wall,
and screwing the top on a bottle.  Non-examples are: absent-mindedly
leaving a book on the floor (guided motion, but without a particular
target), and throwing a basketball through the hoop (not guided
motion, though the target is reached).")
(comment EmittingAnObject "A specialization of #$Emission.  Each instance of #$EmittingAnObject is an emission event in which some partially tangible object (related to the emission event via #$objectEmitted) is emitted from something else (the emitter, related to the emission event via #$emitter).  The emitted object goes from a place inside of the emitter to some place that is not within the emitter, and the emitter plays an active role in the emission. Notable specializations of #$EmittingAnObject include #$Bleeding-TheAction, #$PrecipitationProcess, and #$SecretionEvent.")
(comment StillCamera "An instance of #$ArtifactTypeByGenericCategory.  Each instance of #$StillCamera is a #$Camera that records still #$VisualImages one at a time (rather than many in quick succession to create a moving image, as with a #$VideoCamera).  Specializations include #$StillCamera-Digital and #$StillCamera-Film.")
(comment CokeFuel "the carbon-rich residue of bituminous coal after volatile components have been distilled out.  Typically (always?) a solid.  #$CokeFuel is commonly used in steel-making and as a fuel.")
(comment Camera "A specialization of #$ArtifactTypeByGenericCategory.  Each instance of #$Camera is a #$VisualInformationRecordingDevice that focuses a #$VisualImage through a #$Lens-Artificial and records it on some kind of storage medium, e.g. #$PhotographicFilm (see #$FilmCamera) or #$ComputerMemory (see #$DigitalCamera).  A #$Camera may record #$StillImages one at a time (see #$StillCamera) or in rapid succession to create a #$MovingImage (see #$VideoCamera).")
(comment VideoCamera "An instance of #$ArtifactTypeByFunction.  Each instance of this collection is a #$Camera designed to record moving images onto a storage medium for later viewing.")
(comment SublimatingToSolid "A collection of events.  In each instance of #$SublimatingToSolid, a gas is cooled to (or its pressure lowered to) the point where the gas molecules solidify into a solid.  This is the reverse process of #$Sublimating to a gas.")
(comment Sentencing-Legal "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$LegalProceeding and #$ImposingSanctions.  Each instance of #$Sentencing-Legal is an event in which someone convicted of a crime is assigned punishment (often by a #$Judge).")
(comment Hunting "An instance of #$SkilledActivityType.  Each instance of #$Hunting is a primarily land-based #$OutdoorActivity in which an #$Animal (or #$Group thereof) searches for other (typically weaker) #$Animals to kill for food and/or sport.  When #$Hunting is performed by humans, #$ProjectileWeapons are usually used.")
(comment RevealingTrueInformation "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType.  Each instance of #$RevealingTrueInformation is an instance of #$RevealingInformation in which all of the information revealed is true.")
(comment ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-MissionCritical "A subset of  #$IntelAttack and  #$ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-Information, #$ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-MissionCritical is the collection of  intel attacks perpetrated on instances of  #$ComputationalSystem with the  ambition of  retrieving instances of #$InformationDesignation-MissionCritical. Note that   #$ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-MissionCritical differs from     #$ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-General in the requirement that the information desired be mission critical.")
(comment Pretending "A specialization of #$HumanActivity and #$PurposefulPhysicalAction.  Each instance of #$Pretending is an action in which a person pretends that something is true.")
(comment Terrier-FunctionalGroup "A collection of #$Dogs. Instances of #$Terrier-FunctionalGroup are dogs whose breed background includes some #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure developed for the hunting and killing of small-to-medium-sized animals. The name 'Terrier' is derived from the Latin 'terra,' or 'ground'. Most terrier types were originally developed in the #$UnitedKingdomOfGreatBritainAndNorthernIreland.")
(comment Hepatitis "A collection of diseases. Any instance of #$Hepatitis is an inflammation of the liver. Main specializations are #$ViralHepatitis, a collection of viral diseases due to various kinds of viruses (see #$Hepatitis-VirusType), alcoholic hepatitis, resulting from alcoholism, instances of which are often precursors of cirrhosis of the liver, and toxic hepatitis, resulting from consumption of various substances or drugs.")
(comment TerroristAct "A specialization of #$HostileSocialAction.  Each instance of #$TerroristAct is an action which is performed with a political objective, and which is intended to be coercive through inducing terror (typically through the threat of future attacks).  Historically, the perpetrators of terrorist acts have not considered military targets to be uniformly more legitimate than civilian targets.  An important specialization is #$TerroristAttack.")
(comment Massacre "A collection of complex, violent events.  In each instance of #$Massacre, many people (or animals) are killed.  The number of #$victims may range from tens to thousands (or more).  In English, `massacre' has the connotation that the victims are significantly overpowered by the #$perpetrators and that the slaughter occurs without resistance or without a fair fight.")
(comment LodgingInPlace "A specialization of #$LocatingInIntendedPosition.  Each instance of #$LodgingInPlace is an event in which an #$Agent-Generic puts something in a position where it will be held relatively firmly in place.")
(comment TakingAPhotograph-FilmCamera "A specialization of #$TakingAPhotograph.  Each instance of #$TakingAPhotograph-FilmCamera is an event in which a single still #$VisualImage is recorded using a #$StillCamera-Film.")
(comment Ritual "A specialization of #$CulturalActivity and #$SocialOccurrence.  Each instance of #$Ritual is a highly stylized or formalized action (or series of actions) performed by a person or group of persons.  Instances of #$Ritual are usually performed with some solemnity.  Notable specializations of #$Ritual include #$Ceremony, #$Funeral, and #$Parade.")
(comment MakingAReservation "A collection of events.  In each #$MakingAReservation  event, something (e.g., a room, table, car) is reserved for future use by an agent.")
(comment politicalPartyMembers "(#$politicalPartyMembers PARTY PERSON)   means that PERSON is a registered member of PARTY.  For example, (#$politicalPartyMembers #$DemocraticParty-US #$BillClinton) holds during most of #$BillClinton's life.")
(comment HailProcess "A specialization of #$PrecipitationProcess.  Each instance of #$HailProcess is an event in which #$HailStones fall to the earth from clouds.")
(comment GrantingPermission "A specialization of #$MakingAnAgreement.  Each instance of #$GrantingPermission is an event in which one #$SocialBeing grants some #$Permission to another.")
(comment University "A specialization of #$DegreeGrantingHigherEducationInstitution, #$EducationalOrganization, #$ResearchOrganization, and #$GeographicalAgent. Instances of #$University are educational organizations (whose official names usually, but not always, include the word 'University') at which university-level teaching and research takes place. Some universities comprise multiple \"colleges\" and professional schools.  Note that this collection includes universities that lie within a more or less local and spatially contiguous campus as well as those that consist of a system of such campuses.  Thus both #$UniversityOfTexasAtAustin and its encompassing system (see #$subOrganizations) #$UniversityOfTexas are instances of #$University.")
(comment Cracking "A collection of change events.  In each #$Cracking, something becomes cracked.  I.e., two or more areas of the thing are separated from one another (though perhaps not divided wholly into parts).  In order to undergo a #$Cracking, the #$objectOfStateChange must be a #$SolidTangibleThing.")
(comment SoftwareVendor "A collection of businesses. Each #$SoftwareVendor is a firm  that produces computer software for clients or for retail sale.")
(comment IssuingALicense "A specialization of #$GrantingPermission.  Each instance of #$IssuingALicense is an event in which some #$License-LegalAgreement is issued/granted to someone.")
(comment Epilepsy "An instance of #$PhysiologicalConditionType and a specialization of #$NeurologicalDisease.  Each instance of #$Epilepsy is a #$ChronicCondition characterized by recurrent seizures (see #$Seizure-AilmentCondition).")
(comment Louse "The collection of insects which can give a person a medical condition known as 'head lice' (see #$HeadLice-Condition).  Having such an condition is usually associated with poor hygiene.")
(comment PetCare "A specialization of #$TakingCareOfSomething.  Each instance of this collection is an event in which some agent engages in actions intended to maintain or promote the good health of a pet, presumably one's own pet.  A notable specialization of this collection is #$WalkingAPet.  Positive examples of this collection include vet visits and instances of #$FeedingAnAnimal in which the animal is a pet.  Negative examples of this collection include instances of #$FeedingAnAnimal in which the animal fed is not a pet; consider a farmer feeding livestock or a person feeding ducks at a pond.  A borderline case might be those instances of events of taking care of another person's pet.")
(comment agentStoresProductTypeAtFacility "(#$agentStoresProductTypeAtFacility AGT PRODTYPE FACILITY)  means that AGT uses FACILITY as a place to   store PRODTYPE.   For example, (#$agentStoresProductTypeAtFacility  #$BMGMusicService   #$CompactDisc-MusicalDisk BMGWarehouse001) says  that BMG stores CD's BMGWarehouse001.")
(comment FluidFiltrationProcess-Artificial "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$FluidFiltrationProcess.  Each instance of #$FluidFiltrationProcess-Artificial is an event in which a #$FluidTangibleThing (e.g. #$Air or (#$LiquidFn #$Water)) passes through a #$PhysicalFilter.  During this process, any particles in the fluid that are too large to pass through the filter's pores are removed by the filter; the fluid is thereby purified.")
(comment productTypeStoredAtFacility "(#$productTypeStoredAtFacility PRODTYPE FACILITY)  means that PRODTYPE is stored at FACILITY.   For example, (#$productTypeStoredAtFacility   #$Tank-Vehicle StorageFacility001) says that    tanks are stored at StorageFacility001.")
(comment DateDenotingFunction "A specialization of #$IndividualDenotingFunction.  A function FUNC is an instance of #$DateDenotingFunction if and only if (#$resultIsa FUNC COL) holds for some specialization COL of #$Date.")
(comment Deceiving "A specialization of #$Pretending and #$InfluencingAnAgent: #$Deceiving is the collection of events in which an #$IntelligentAgent deliberately engages in activities which both are intended to, and in fact do, induce false beliefs in another agent. Note that if either the information conveyed by the would-be deceiver is in fact true (although the would-be deceiver thinks it's false), or the would-be victim of the deception does not, as a result of the would-be deceiver's actions, acquire the false beliefs as intended, then the act is not an instance of #$Deceiving, but is an instance of (#$AttemptingFn #$Deceiving). (Also see #$Lying.) Notable specializations of this collection include #$Cheating and #$Fraud.")
(comment Dysphagia "An instance of #$PhysiologicalConditionType.  Each instance of #$Dysphagia is an #$AilmentCondition characterized by difficulty in #$Swallowing and is caused by lesions or mechanical obstructions of the upper digestive tract or by disturbances in the nervous or muscular control of swallowing.")
(comment Gametophyte "#$Gametophytes are the haploid, gamete-producing life stage in the plant reproductive life cycle.  In plants, #$Meiosis produces spores [#$Spore-PlantLifeStage], which develop into #$Gametophytes.  #$Gametophytes produce #$Gametes, which fuse to form a #$Zygote, which develops into a #$Sporophyte, which produces spores by meiosis, etc.")
(comment Appendicitis "A collection of #$AilmentConditions in which the #$Appendix-OrganPart becomes infected. If the #$Appendix-OrganPart is not removed promptly, toxins are released into the bloodstream and can lead to death.")
(comment ShakingHands "An instance of #$TemporalStuffType and a specialization of #$NonVerbalCommunicating.  Each instance of #$ShakingHands is a #$TouchingEvent in which two #$SocialBeings link #$Hands and shake them briefly, usually to express #$Friendliness and #$GoodWill, and often to acknowledge the making of an agreement (see #$MakingAnAgreement).  By convention, this action is typically performed with the right hand (see (#$RightObjectOfPairFn #$Hand)).")
(comment organismTypeCanLethallyInfect "(#$organismTypeCanLethallyInfect ORGTYPE HOSTTYPE) means that under ordinary circumstances, instances of ORGTYPE are able to play the role of #$infectionCausedByOrganism in instances of #$Infection which culminate in the death of an instance of HOSTTYPE.")
(comment SnowMob "An instance of #$ExistingObjectType.  #$SnowMob is a #$rewriteOf (#$MobFn #$Snowflake).")
(disjointWith TupleOfIntervals GraphicalStructure)
(comment CityInCountryFn "A one-place function that takes an instance COUNTRY of  #$Country and returns the collection of all cities in COUNTRY.  More formally, (#$CityInCountryFn COUNTRY) denotes the collection of all cities in the instance COUNTRY of #$Country.  For example,  (#$CityInCountryFn #$France) denotes the collection of all cities in #$France.")
(comment ConveningAMeeting "The collection of events in which one sender of info issues a notification, invitation, or command to one or more recipients that results in these recipients or their representatives coming together in a #$Meeting-SocialGathering.")
(comment Listeriosis "An #$InfectiousDiseaseType, usually only affecting those instances of #$HomoSapiens with weakened immune systems and especially pregnant women. A serious case has a mortality risk of about 20%.")
(comment Chilling "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType, and a specialization of #$CoolingProcess.  Each instance of #$Chilling is a cooling process in which a substance is cooled to a #$Cold temperature.  For example, the preparation of Jello involves chilling it; so each time someone makes a batch of Jello, that is an instance of the collection #$Chilling.")
(comment Inventor "Each #$Inventor is a person who has invented something.")
(comment Eavesdropping "An instance of #$CumulativeEventType and a specialization of (#$SurreptitiouslyFn #$ListeningDeliberately).  Each instance of #$Eavesdropping is an event in which a #$PerceptualAgent secretly listens to a #$Conversation in which he is not an active participant.")
(comment Howling "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$MakingAnOralSound and #$AnimalActivity.  Each instance of #$Howling is an event in which a #$CanineAnimal emits a #$HowlingSound (q.v.).")
(comment LevelOfChange "A collection of attributes.  Every instance of #$LevelOfChange expresses a direction of quantity change: #$PositiveChange for increases, #$NegativeChange for decreases, and #$NoChange for no change.  It is used to describe changes in #$QuantitySlot values, and to describe the direction of dependencies between #$QuantitySlots.  See #$changesQuantityInDirection and #$dependsOn-Pairwise.")
(comment interArgCondIsa1-2 "(#$interArgCondIsa1-2 PRED INDEP-COL COND-COL DEP-COL) is shorthand for the class of rules that state the following: For every instance INDEP-INST of INDEP-COL, if there is some instance COND-INST of COND-COL such that (PRED INDEP-INST COND-INST) holds, COND-INST will be an element of DEP-COL. For example,(#$interArgCondIsa1-2 #$anatomicalParts #$Vertebrate #$Head-AnimalBodyPart #$Head-Vertebrate) -- if a #$Vertebrate has a head as an #$anatomicalParts, that head is a #$Head-Vertebrate.")
(comment primaryResidence "The predicate #$primaryResidence relates an instance of #$Person to an instance of #$HumanResidence. (#$primaryResidence PERSON RESIDENCE) means that RESIDENCE is the permanent primary of PERSON. This usually means that RESIDENCE is the home at which PERSON spends most of his/her time, as opposed to a vacation home.  While a person may have several residences, only one can be that person's primary residence. A single dwelling, however, can serve as a primary residence for many people, for instance all members of a family.")
(comment RadioWave "A specialization of #$ElectromagneticRadiation. Each instance of #$RadioWave is an instance of electromagnetic radiation having a wavelength in the range from approximately 1 centimeter (1x10<sup>8</sup> #$Angstrom) to 3,000,000 meters (3x10<sup>18</sup> #$Angstrom), and a frequency of approximately 10<sup>8</sup> #$Hertz to 10<sup>2</sup> #$Hertz.")
(comment BankVault "A room or sub-region of a room in which valuables (see #$Valuable-PersonalPossession) are kept and locked up. Can be found in banks, hotels, etc.")
(comment HumanActivity "A specialization of #$AnimalActivity.   Each instance of #$HumanActivity is a spatially-localized action  each of whose \"doers\" (see #$doneBy) is either a #$HomoSapiens or a  group of humans (i.e. a #$Group all of whose members are #$HomoSapiens or groups of humans).")
(comment RejectingDiplomaticAssistance "The collection of events where a state officially rejects another state's request for diplomatic assistance.")
(comment ConvertibleCar "An instance of #$AutomobileTypeByBodyStyle.  Each instance of #$ConvertibleCar is an #$Automobile whose roof can be folded back or removed.")
(comment Kelvin "The unit of measure for thermodynamic temperature. Mainly used in science, this unit is the base for the Celsius temperature scale (#$DegreeCelsius). See also #$DegreeFahrenheit and #$UnitOfTemperature.")
(comment Lever "An #$ExistingObjectType.  Each instance of #$Lever is a #$PhysicalDevice consisting of a #$Shaft which pivots on a #$Fulcrum.  #$Levers are used to transfer energy.  When force is applied to one end of a #$Lever in a particular direction, the other end moves in the opposite direction.")
(comment SONAR-Device "The collection of devices used to navigate and locate objects in water.  Instances consist of a transmitter which emits high-frequency sound waves through water and a receiver which measures the returning sound waves reflected from objects in the path of the sound waves.")
(comment Flask-LabGlassware "piece of lab glassware with a narrow throat and portal at the top, and wider cavity below.")
(comment RadarDevice "A specialization of #$ElectroMagneticRadiationSensor and #$DistanceMeasuringDevice.  Each instance of #$RadarDevice is a device used to detect and determine the location of #$PartiallyTangible objects such as airplanes and storms.  #$RadarDevices work by transmitting strong #$RadioWaves and sensing the \"echoes\" that come back when these waves reflect off of objects.")
(comment PlumbingSystem-Building "The entire plumbing system that runs throughout a building or other construct.")
(comment CruiseShip "A #$VehicleTypeByIntendedUse.  Each instance of #$CruiseShip is an #$OceanLiner used primarily for leisure-oriented voyages with many luxury services (see #$Cruise-Vacation).")
(comment NumericInterval "A partition of #$ScalarInterval (q.v.).  #$NumericInterval comprises the \"purely numeric\" scalars, and is thus disjoint with #$Quantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$NumericInterval is either a number of some sort (see #$Number-General) or a proper interval on the real number line (see #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine).")
(comment VehicleTowingSomething "Transportation where one vehicle is pulling something behind it.")
(comment Oiler "The collection of #$ModernNavalShips intended to be used for replenishment of petroleum products to other ships; a subcollection of #$OilTanker.")
(comment WeatherSatellite "The collection of all artificial satellites (#$Satellite-Device) which are designed for collecting data on the weather of the #$AstronomicalBody which they orbit (#$orbits).")
(comment MusicalPerformance "A specialization of both #$LiveEntertainment and #$InformationBearingPerformance.  Each instance of #$MusicalPerformance is an #$EntertainmentPerformance (q.v.) in which one or more agents produce music, either by singing or playing musical instruments, for a (public or private) audience. Specializations of #$MusicalPerformance include #$MusicalDrama, #$ClassicalMusicalPerformance, and #$JazzMusicalPerformance.")
(comment TransactionsPerSecond "A #$UnitOfCPUCapacity (q.v.) function. #$TransactionsPerSecond, commonly abbreviated \"TPS\", is the most often-used unit for measuring the size of a transaction processing workload.")
(comment ZeroVector "#$ZeroVector is the vector with zero magnitude.  It is the vector equivalent to the scalar point value '0'")
(comment PersonalDigitalAssistant "The collection of handheld computer-like devices that store personal information and perform some other functions that computer perform, such as connecting one to the internet.")
(comment MortarLauncher-Medium "The collection of medium-sized #$MortarLaunchers that fire 81mm cartridges. The #$MortarLauncher-Medium could be stripped down and carried by three men.")
(comment Portal-Underspecified "The collection of locations, tangible or otherwise, which are typically conceptualized by human beings for purposes of common-sense reasoning as Portals. This collection thus includes tangible Portals such as #$Gate, #$WindowPortal and #$DoorInABuilding, as well as metaphoric portal. For instance, intermediate goal states are frequently conceptualized as abstract portals, as in 'He went through Bob to meet the vice president' and 'He went through hell to get his kids back'.")
(comment Windsurfer-Person "The collection of instances of #$Athlete who ride #$Sailboards with some regularity.")
(comment EroticDramaMovie "A type of drama with an emphasis  on eroticism and where a sexual relationship plays an important  role in the plot. Often these frank films contain elements of  graphic nudity, sexual intercourse, and fetishism, yet these acts  rarely are portrayed gratuitously or for simple voyeuristic  pleasure. Often, the attention is drawn to issues of pleasure,  companionship, and emotional and physical necessity. Unlike  exploitation or #$PornographicMovies, erotic dramas offer more elaborate  dramatic structure, deeper psychological character motivations  and display a higher artistic level; some of them have become  acknowledged classics.")
(comment Boundary-Underspecified "The collection of all semantically underspecified boundaries whether spatial (and literal) like a #$Border, or metaphoric, like the boundaries of knowledge in a #$FieldOfStudy.")
(comment MortarLauncher-Light "The collection of smaller #$MortarLaunchers that are small enough to be carried and used by one man.  They usually fire 60mm cartridges.")
(comment Portal "A specialization of both #$Path-Spatial and #$Cavity.   Each instance of #$Portal is an opening -- in a  surface, through a tube, or whatever -- with or without a  covering (although any covering is not itself typically part  of the portal).  Specializations include #$Doorway, #$Valve,  and #$BodilyOrifice.")
(comment BallisticMissile-SS1 "A kind of #$BallisticMissile.")
(comment MortarLauncher-Heavy "The collection of large #$MortarLaunchers that fire 120mm cartridges and  larger cartridges.  These cannot be carried, but are usually mounted on  vehicles or remain in a stationary position.")
(comment Torpedo-MK-46 "A type of #$Torpedo with #$ActiveSonar-Device and #$PassiveSonar-Device to provide guidance. The #$Torpedo-MK-46 was designed specifically for attacking high performance #$Submarines and is the preferred  #$Warhead for #$AntiSubmarineRockets.")
(comment MakingACriminalCharge "A specialization of #$Accusing and #$LegalProceeding (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$MakingACriminalCharge is an action of making a #$CriminalCharge (q.v.).  In most western legal systems, this is done by a court, to a legal agent.")
(comment CopyingAComputerFile "A specialization of #$AccessingAFile-ReadOnly, #$CreatingAFile and #$IBTCopying. In each instance of this collection a computer reads a file and creates a new file with the same contents. Immediately after the event, the original file and the new file will contain exactly the same information. The two files may be stored on the same or different storage media. Notable specializations of this collection include #$UploadingAFile and #$DownloadingAFile. See also #$MovingAComputerFile-CopyingAndDeleting.")
(comment CriminalCharge "A specialization of #$Allegation (q.v.)  Each instance of #$CriminalCharge is a formal accusation -- the information content, <i>not</i> the act of #$MakingACriminalCharge (cf.) -- made by a court against a legal agent.")
(comment ArgConstraintPredicate "The collection of #$MetaRelations whose instances are used to put semantic well-formedness or entry-format constraints on one or more argument-places of a given #$Relation.  Such a constraint might concern an argument's <i>type</i>, an argument-place's #$Format (q.v.), a conditional relationship between two argument(-place)s' types or formats, or some other sort of relationship between two arguments.  For a fuller account, see the various specializations of #$ArgConstraintPredicate.")
(comment FastFoodRestaurant-Organization "Restaurants that serve fast food only, such as individual instances of McDonald's, Taco Bell, etc.  Not necessarily an outlet of a franchise, but is by default.")
(comment TimeTravel "A specialization of #$GeneralizedTransfer.  Each instance of #$TimeTravel is an #$Event in which an agent \"travels through time\", i.e. somehow travels into the past or into the future (at a rate significantly faster than we ordinarily \"travel\" into the future).  There is no widespread agreement on whether time travel is possible; if it is impossible, then #$TimeTravel has no instances.")
(comment TaiChiActivity "The collection of all activities consisting of a #$Person practicing, performing, or using Tai Chi, which is both a 'soft-style' Chinese martial art and a form of exercise. Tai Chi is derived from Taoism, a Chinese philosophy concerned with the relationship between the passive and active aspects of reality, called 'yin' and 'yang'. Its practitioners sought tranquility of mind and improvement of temperament. As a martial art, Tai Chi can be traced to the Tong Dynasty (618 AD to 907 AD).")
(comment AthleticActivity "A specialization of both #$PurposefulPhysicalAction and #$HumanActivity. Each instance of #$AthleticActivity is a purposeful, physical human activity  that is related to the playing of some sport, involves physical exertion, and  tends to require strength and stamina.  This collection is not limited  to competitive sports events, but also includes non-competitive sports  activities and exercising (e.g. spelunking, going for a jog, or shooting  a few hoops).")
(comment lienHolder "(#$lienHolder LIEN AGENT) holds just in case AGENT is the lien holder in LIEN.")
(comment spacecraftDestinationType "(#$spacecraftDestinationType CRAFT OBJ-TYPE) means that the destination of #$Spacecraft CRAFT is an instance of the subcollection of #$AstronomicalObject OBJ-TYPE. Typically, a successful mission using CRAFT involves CRAFT either landing, orbiting, or flying by OBJ-TYPE. For example, (#$spacecraftDestinationType #$NearEarthAsteroidRendezvous-Probe #$Asteroid) means that an instance of #$Asteroid is the destination of the space probe #$NearEarthAsteroidRendezvous-Probe. Use #$spacecraftDestination when the destination is an instance of #$AstronomicalObject.")
(comment HardcoreFn "A #$CollectionDenotingFunction which takes as an argument a subset of #$SexualEntertainmentThing and returns the collection of instances of that product which one might call hardcore.  Any #$PornographicMaterial which is dubbed hardcore involves explicit sexual acts and complete nudity.  For instance, (#$HardcoreFn #$PornographicMovie) would return the collection of all pornographic movies which show explicit sexual acts.  An instance of this collection is #$DeepThroat-TheMovie.  See also #$SoftcoreFn.")
(comment actsInCapacity-MainFunction "(#$actsInCapacity-MainFunction AGT ROLE   EVT-TYPE) holds just in case AGT performs ROLE in events of type EVT-TYPE as its main function (rather than, say, as a hobby).  #$actsInCapacity-MainFunction can be used to indicate the function that an organization was created to do; e.g., the main function of the #$USBorderPatrol is #$Patrolling the U.S. border.  See #$CapacityPredicate.")
(comment SoftcoreFn "A #$CollectionDenotingFunction which takes as an argument a subset of #$SexualEntertainmentThing and returns the collection of instances of that product which one might call softcore.  Any #$PornographicMaterial which is dubbed softcore usually does NOT involve explicit sexual acts, but is still considered pornographic because it does contain nudity and implied sexual messages.  For instance, (#$SoftcoreFn #$PornographicMagazineSeries) would return the collection of all pornographic magazines which contain nudity or partial nudity, but no explicit sex.  An instance of this collection is #$Playboy-TheMagazine.  See also #$HardcoreFn.")
(comment DragonStyleKungFuActivity "The collection of all activities consisting  of a #$Person practicing, performing, or using  #$DragonStyleKungFu-CW, a Chinese martial art.")
(disjointWith RegionType QuantityType)
(comment Extruding "A collection of events.  In each #$Extruding event, the shape of something is changed by forcing it through a die.  The #$objectActedOn in an #$Extruding must be in a #$Liquid-StateOfMatter, which also includes plastic/putty-like solids.  Unless the die changes shape during the #$Extruding, the output is a generalized cylinder, whose cross-section is the shape of the hole(s) in the die, and whose length is usually much larger than its diameter.  Sometimes the output remains in more or less #$Liquid-StateOfMatter, and so quickly loses this shape, such as happens when a soft-ice-cream dispenser extrudes a ribbon of ice cream.  See also #$Molding.")
(comment ChainOrganization "A specialization of #$LargeIncorporatedBusiness.  Each instance of #$ChainOrganization is a parent business which comes into contact with its customers or clients primarily through its geographically dispersed sub-organizations, which typically are (mostly) homogeneous in product line, style of physical quarters, and local organizational structure.  Examples include the parent organizations of McDonald's and Starbucks.")
(comment PermittingEntrance "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$GrantingPermission.  Each instance of #$PermittingEntrance is an event in which someone is granted permission to enter a particular area.")
(comment culturePrescribes-Categorical "(#$culturePrescribes-Categorical CULTURE ACTTYPE ROLE)    means that within CULTURE it is considered good or proper or    right to play ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE on a regular basis,   and bad or improper or wrong not to do so.  For example, some   cultures consider it proper to make an offering to a deity each day.     This does not entail, however, that CULTURE has a (hypothetical)    prescription for playing ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE with respect    to every possible condition; i.e. there might exist exceptional    situations in which playing ROLE in an ACTTYPE is not prescribed    (and might even be proscribed) by CULTURE.  One might be temporarily   exempted from the daily religious offering duty, for example, while    engaged in a protracted military battle.  This predicate applies    equally to moral rules, rules of conduct or etiquette, and customs.     See also #$culturePrescribes-Hypothetical,    #$cultureProscribes-Categorical, and #$cultureProscribes-Hypothetical.")
(comment Trapping "A specialization of #$CapturingAnAnimal and #$UsingADevice.  Each instance of #$Trapping is an event in which an #$Animal is captured using a #$TrapDevice (q.v.).  The animal may or may not be killed in the event.")
(comment County-England "The collection of traditional counties in England.")
(comment CapabilityPredicate-InstanceLevel "This is the subcollection of those #$CapabilityPredicates whose first argument #$isa #$Individual.")
(comment CapturingAnAnimal "A specialization of #$PurposefulPhysicalAction.  In each instance of #$CapturingAnAnimal, an agent takes physical control of a (human or non-human) animal.  Notable specializations of #$CapturingAnAnimal include #$Trapping and #$ArrestingSomeone.")
(disjointWith UnorderedQuantityType TotallyOrderedQuantityType)
(comment KidnappingSomeone "The collection of all instances of #$AbductingSomeone where an individual is illegally taken captive and held against his or her will.  Often, but not always, this is done with the intent of holding the kidnappee for ransom.")
(comment ShoppingMallBuilding "A specialization of #$Building and #$ModernShelterConstruction.  Each instance of #$ShoppingMallBuilding is a building that houses a number of retail store spaces.  Important specializatins are #$StripMall and #$IndoorShoppingMall (qq.v.).")
(comment ScalarPointValue "A specialization of #$ScalarInterval (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ScalarPointValue is a specific number or quantity, as opposed to a range of numbers or quantities (cf. #$ScalarProperInterval).  Instances of #$ScalarPointValue include all reals numbers and other #$Number-Generals (q.v.), as well as specific instances of #$Quantity such as (#$Meter 3) and (#$SecondsDuration 10).")
(comment BusinessMeeting "A specialization of #$Meeting-SocialGathering and #$BusinessEvent. Instances of #$BusinessMeeting are meetings sponsored by a business for the goals of the business, where participants are in some way affiliated with the business.")
(comment OrganismPartTypeFn "(#$OrganismPartTypeFn ORG-TYPE ORG-PART-TYPE) denotes the collection of all parts of the type ORG-PART-TYPE (e.g., #$Elbow, #$Leaf, #$CirculatorySystem) that belong to an organism of the type ORG-TYPE (e.g., #$Person, #$OakTree, #$Invertebrate).  For example, (#$OrganismPartTypeFn #$Person #$Heart) yields the collection of all human hearts. Use the function #$UniqueBodyPartFn to refer to an individual body part of a specific animal.")
(comment CeramicArtifact "A specialization of #$PartiallyTangibleProduct. Each instance of #$CeramicArtifact is a particular object -- such as a vessel, dish or #$Sculpture -- that is made from #$Ceramic (q.v.). It might also be an instance of #$ArtObject (q.v).")
(comment TerroristAttack "A specialization of #$AttackOnTangible and #$TerroristAttackOnObject (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TerroristAttack is an event in which terrorists act with intention of damaging or destroying some structures or people in order to achieve their political objectives.  Bombing a building and destroying an oil refinery are exemplars.  Taking hostages is not an exemplar of #$TerroristAttack but it is an exemplar of the more general term #$TerroristAct (q.v.).")
(comment ConspiringToDoSomething "The collection of events in which two or more #$IntelligentAgent plan together to carry out some illegal or nefarious action, usually secretly.")
(comment GuinnessBeer "A brand of #$StoutBeer popular in #$IrishRepublic and Britain (i.e., #$UnitedKingdomOfGreatBritainAndNorthernIreland). The most popular such beer in the world.")
(comment GovernmentOwnedCompany "A federal, state or city government organization formed with the purpose of providing services or products that may not be available from a private company.  Examples include the FDIC and local utility companies.")
(comment Astrology "The collection of belief systems that assume the influence of instances of #$AstronomicalBody on human affairs and personality.")
(comment StorageOrganization "A collection of organizations.  An element of #$StorageOrganization is an organization that has as a main function the storage of goods.  A storage organization may also be (but is not necessarily) a #$DistributionOrganization.  It may or may not be among the #$subOrganizations of a larger organization, either a #$Business or a government organization.  Example: a #$FederalSupplyDepot engaged in, e.g., moving and storage of goods.")
(comment PotteryArtifact "Instances of #$PotteryArtifact are various sorts of vessels made of clay and baked in a kiln.  This includes dishes, pots for holding plants, etc. Pottery is to be distinguished from the more general #$CeramicArtifact in that pottery doesn't include (to mention a few) chinaware, toilet bowls, or ceramic parts used in industry.")
(comment AcidicSolution-Liquid "A specialization of both #$LiquidSolution and #$ChemicallyAcidicSubstance. Each instance of #$AcidicSolution-Liquid has a pH less than 7.")
(comment mainIngredientsSet "(#$mainIngredientsSet FOOD SET) means that the food or drink, FOOD, has among its main ingredients the types of #$EdibleStuff in SET.  'Main' ingredients need not be those that make up the bulk of FOOD, or even are larger than other ingredients in FOOD; rather, main ingredients include those that make the food distinctive.  For example, anchovies are among the main ingredients in an anchovy pizza, even though they are only a small part of it.")
(comment GoingOnADate "A specialization of #$SociabilityBasedAction, #$CulturalActivity and #$MultipleDoerAction. Each instance of this collection is an outing embarked on by two people (usually in the evening) in the hope of further romantic entanglement. See also the specialization, #$GoingOnAFirstDate.")
(comment InformationBearingWavePropagation "A specialization of both #$InformationBearingThing and #$WavePropagation.  Each instance of #$InformationBearingWavePropagation is a wave propagation event that carries information for interpreters who understand its conventions.  Notable specializations of #$InformationBearingWavePropagation include #$Music, #$VisualImage, and #$SpokenUtterance-IBT.")
(comment Testicle "The collection of all testes.  A #$Testicle (testis) is a #$SpermCell producing #$Organ appearing in male and hermaphroditic animals, both #$Vertebrate and #$Invertebrate.  In most #$Vertebrates these occur in pairs, and in some #$Mammals (notably #$MaleHumans) are held in a pouch (#$Sac-Organic) of #$Skin (the #$Scrotum) which hangs from the #$Penis.")
(comment SociabilityBasedAction "A specialization of #$SocialOccurrence.  Each instance of #$SociabilityBasedAction is an action done mostly for the sake of sociability, in which considerations of socially acceptable interactions with others are important.")
(comment interArgReln2-1 "A binary #$InterArgRelnPredicate (q.v.).  (#$interArgReln2-1 RELN BINPRED) means that a formula built from RELN (i.e. a \"GAF\" or \"NAT\"; see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence and  #$CycLClosedNonAtomicTerm) is semantically well-formed only if BINPRED holds between the second and first arguments to RELN (in that order) in the GAF/NAT.  That is, a formula (RELN ARG1 ARG2 ..) is semantically well-formed only if (BINPRED ARG2 ARG1) holds.  For example, (#$interArgReln2-1 #$streetIntersectionOf #$subPaths) means that the arg2 and arg1 of a #$streetIntersectionOf assertion must be related by #$subPaths.  For further explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations. See also the more general quaternary predicate #$interArgReln, which has two argument-places for specifying the pair of RELN's arguments that must be related by BINPRED.")
(comment Locomotion-LiquidSurface "The collection of all instances of #$LocomotionProcess in which  the locomotor is #$in-Floating a #$LiquidTangibleThing, including both the movement of an Olympic swimmer in a pool and the movement of a boat on a body of water.")
(comment Waiting "A specialization of #$ConsciousActivity.  Each instance of #$Waiting is an event in which an agent remains in a state of expectation, anticipating the occurence of some future event.")
(comment MilitaryIntelligence "The collection of intelligence resulting
from an instance of #$MilitaryIntelligenceProcess.")
(comment Communicating "A specialization of #$PurposefulAction and characterized by one or more #$InformationTransferEvent sub-events.  Each instance of #$Communicating is an event in which the transfer of information between or among agents is a focal action; communicating is the main purpose and/or goal in the event.  That may be contrasted with events which involve communication but wherein the focus is different, e.g., playing cards (wherein the progressive actions -- and winning -- of the game are focal).  Since #$Communicating is a specialization of #$PurposefulAction, each #$Communicating event must be intentional on the part of the #$communicatorOfInfo; it may or may not be intentional on the part of the agent playing the #$infoCommunicatedTo role.  Hence, a speaker on a soapbox haranguing an indifferent crowd is performing an instance of #$Communicating.  In contrast, Juliet soliloquizing on her balcony, unaware that Romeo is listening to her, is not #$Communicating; rather Romeo (and Juliet, unaware) are engaged in an #$Eavesdropping. Communicating may be either a one-way or a two-way transfer of information (cf. #$CommunicationAct-Single, #$MultiDirectionalCommunication).  Every event belonging to #$Communicating contains at least one transfer of information between at least two agents who participate in the event.  (Note that the latter requirement excludes reading and writing from #$Communicating, when those events are just the private accessing or generating of information.)  Communicating may be specialized in various ways, such as, by the method or medium used (e.g., #$AudioCommunicating, #$NonVerbalCommunicating, #$FaceToFacePresenceCommunicating); by the type of information involved (e.g., #$MakingAnAgreement); by the purpose of the communication (e.g., #$Teaching, #$Negotiating); by the agents involved (e.g., #$IntraOrganizationCommunication, #$StageProduction).  Examples of #$Communicating include a symphony performance, an email message, a telephone call, a speech, a handshake, issuing a traffic ticket -- all of which normally, and focally, involve communication between two or more agents.")
(comment Pimp "An instance of #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Each instance of #$Pimp is a person whose job is to solicit customers for prostitutes (see the collection #$Prostitute) or for a brothel (see the collection #$Brothel), in return for a share of the earnings from those customers.  Instances of #$Pimp are usually #$MaleHumans.")
(comment TeamSport "This is the collection of team sports. A team sport is one that is usually engaged in by teams rather than individuals. Obvious examples would include such sports as football and basketball, which cannot be played except by teams. A less obvious example would be gymnastics, wrestling, and swimming. In these sports, athletes can compete on an individual basis, without being part of a team. Indeed, there is typically not group-versus-group competition in the way there is in football. The group aspect comes in in virtue of  cumulative scores from multiple events in which individual members from the teams perform.")
(comment IntelligenceReport "A specialization of #$WrittenReportOnSituation.  Each instance is characterized by being supported by some form of #$IntelligenceGathering.")
(comment Mutant "The collection of organisms (people, plants, etc.) that have mutated away from their natural state, either through some genetic disorder or through some exposure to #$RadioactiveStuff. #$Mutants have a valid scientific status (especially with regard to plants and genetic  experimentation), but the concept is often stretched into the realms of the fantastic in contexts such as that of #$Superhero (e.g., see #$Spiderman).")
(comment driverActor "(#$driverActor DRIVE DRIVER) means that the #$Person DRIVER controls (see #$ControllingATransportationDevice) the transporter (related to DRIVE by the predicate #$transporter (q.v.)) in the #$TransportInvolvingADriver DRIVE.")
(comment PhysicallyVulnerable "The attribute had by those things that are of vulnerable to physical threats.  Being  #$PhysicallyVulnerable is to have a weakness in physical structure (as opposed to, say, 'cyber' structure--see #$SystemVulnerableToAttackByComputerOperation ), such that damage could result from exploiting the weakness.")
(comment Percent "A variable-arity #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars (q.v.).  When applied to a number or other #$NumericInterval (pair of numeric-intervals), #$Percent returns the instance of #$IntervalOnNumberLine that is its argument (the interval between its two arguments) divided by 100.  For example (#$Percent 110) is 1.1, and (#$Percent 10 20) is (#$Unity 0.1 0.2): the interval between 0.1 and 0.2 inclusive.")
(comment Hippie "This collection includes the members of the Western cultural group which represented a anti-establishment sub-culture advocating e.g. peace. This group was at their hey-day during the late 1960s and early 1970s.")
(comment TaxiDriver "A specialization of #$Driver-TransportationProfessional.  Each instance of #$TaxiDriver is a person who drives a taxi as an occupation.")
(comment Gun-MuzzleLoading "The collection of #$Guns that are loaded at the #$Muzzle-GunPart  instead of the breech.  Muskets and older firearms were mostly loaded from the muzzle.  Artillery, such as #$MortarLaunchers, are also in this class.")
(comment MultiDirectionalCommunication "A collection of complex information transfer events. Each instance of #$MultiDirectionalCommunication is an event in which more than one agent plays the #$communicatorOfInfo role. For example, a conversation or a debate, as opposed to a speech or lecture. The predicate #$infoContributed is used to correlate each sending agent with the information s/he transmits in such an event. For communication acts having only one sender, see #$CommunicationAct-Single.")
(comment lessThanOrEqualTo "A #$NumericComparisonPredicate that is a generalization of the  mathematical less-than-or-equal-to ( <= ) relation to  #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative  intervals as well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).   (#$lessThanOrEqualTo VALUE1 VALUE2) means that VALUE1 is  less than or equal to VALUE2 with respect to some scale that  they are both on.  More precisely, there is some  #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType SCALE that both VALUE1 and  VALUE2 are instances of and either (i) the maximum  (see #$maxQuantValue) of VALUE1 is less than or equal to the  minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of VALUE2, (ii)  (#$followingValueOnScale VALUE1 VALUE2 SCALE) holds, or (iii) (#$equals VALUE1 VALUE2) holds.  Note that  #$lessThanOrEqualTo is an #$ELRelation (q.v.), and the above  sentence would actually canonicalize to  (#$greaterThanOrEqualTo VALUE2 VALUE1).")
(comment Emergency "A collection of #$DangerousSituations (hazardous #$Situations) in which death, significant physical injury, significant physical distruction, or significant financial (or other) disaster shall occur <i>unless</i> action is quickly taken to prevent (or significantly lessen) the pending injury or destruction.")
(comment HomosexualBehavior "The collection of all instances of #$SexualBehavior by two or more participants of the same sex.  Note that this refers only to the sexual behavior itself, not to any other behaviors that may happen to be exhibited by #$HomosexualHumans.")
(comment performsInstancesAsPartOfJob "An instance of #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate (q.v.) that  relates a given type of event to people who perform (see #$performedBy) events of that type as part of their job.  (#$performsInstancesAsPartOfJob EVENTTYPE PERSON) means that PERSON  performs instances of EVENTYPE as a significant part of his or her job. For example, #$KeithRichards performs instances of #$WritingAMusicalComposition as part of his work, and a #$SecurityGuard performs instances of  #$ProtectingSomething.  (The latter example is expressed by the  generalization '(#$relationInstanceAll #$performsInstancesAsPartOfJob #$ProtectingSomething #$SecurityGuard)'.)")
(comment Cafe-Place "An instance of #$ExistingObjectType and a specialization of #$ModernShelterConstruction. Each instance of #$Cafe-Place is a place where people can buy cups of coffee, as well as pastries of some sort. See also the related constant #$Cafe-Organization.")
(comment caloricContentOfFood "(#$caloricContentOfFood EDIBLESTUFF MASS CAL) means that MASS of EDIBLESTUFF, if burned, releases CAL of energy. CAL will generally be stated using the unit function #$Kilocalorie. For example, (#$caloricContent PeachPie001 (#$Gram 1) (#$Kilocalorie 7)) means that PeachPie001 has 7 Calories per gram. Unlike #$caloricContent, #$caloricContentOfFood allows qualitative values like (#$HighAmountFn #$EnergyQuantity) in addition to quantitative values like (#$Kilocalorie 400). A qualitative value indicates the substance's caloric content relative to some relevant specialization of #$EdibleStuff, but usually the collection #$EdibleStuff itself.")
(comment UnitOfComputerResourceCapacity "A specialization of #$UnitOfMeasure.  Each instance of this collection is a unit which may be used to measure the various capacities that a computer can have, such as memory, CPU and disk capacities. Instances of this collection include #$Baud and #$Byte. Specializations of this collection include #$UnitOfBandwidth and #$UnitOfCPUSpeed.")
(comment IceCream "A specialization of #$DairyProduct and #$DessertFood.  Each instance of #$IceCream is some ice cream.")
(comment Supermodel "The collection of particularly famous and high-priced #$FashionModels. Like other celebrities, #$FashionModels have fans (cf. #$Fan-Supporter) and websites, and are the subject of tabloid newspaper articles.")
(comment Stealing-Physical "A specialization of #$Stealing.  In an instance of #$Stealing-Physical, the perpetrator is physically at the location with the stolen goods when committing the crime.  The perpetrator physically removes the goods.  The owner of the goods may or may not be present.  Thus, this collection includes all burglaries and stealing of cars from parking lots, as well as robberies and carjackings.  In contrast, instances of #$Stealing-Embezzlement need not be instances of #$Stealing-Physical.")
(comment PyrotechnicDevice "A specialization of #$Device-OneTimeUse and #$ExplosiveDevice.  Each instance of #$PyrotechnicDevice is a manufactured item that contains a portion of #$PyrotechnicMaterial and whose purpose is to use combustion processes to propell objects, create visual displays, or create sonic effects. The term has been used more broadly so as to include military ordnance, but the present sense means to exclude weapons. Any sort of fireworks count as pyrotechnic devices in the present sense, as do stationary and airborne signal flares, including those intended for military use.")
(comment groupMembers "This predicate relates a group (see #$Group) to its individual members.  (#$groupMembers GROUP MEMBER) means that MEMBER is a member of GROUP.  For example, #$PyramidOfCheops is a group-member of the #$SevenWondersOfTheAncientWorld, and #$BillClinton is a group-member of the #$DemocraticParty-US.")
(comment partOfSpeech "(#$partOfSpeech LEXITEM POS STRING) means that the
POS part-of-speech form for the lexical item LEXITEM is the string STRING")
(comment BeliefSystem "A specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore.  Each instance of #$BeliefSystem is an ideology (systems of belief) in terms of which an agent characterizes (i.e., makes sense of) the world.  Instances of #$BeliefSystem include: #$Vegetarianism, #$GermanNaziIdeology, #$RepublicanPartyIdeology, #$Communism, #$Pacifism, #$Atheism, etc. See also #$ClassificationSystem.")
(comment Vegan "The collection of #$Person that subscribe to #$Veganism.")
(comment Bible "The primary #$SacredText of #$Christianity. For printed versions, see #$Bible-BookCopy.")
(comment OfferingForSale "The collection of OfferingForSale events includes events in which an agent offers one or more things for sale to one or more other agents.")
(comment Foreskin "The collection of all foreskins (or 'prepuces').  Each member of this collection is a part of a #$Penis which covers the #$GlansPenis (when the organ is flaccid).  The #$Foreskin is often surgically removed in an operation called a #$Circumcision for either ritual or claimed medical reasons.")
(comment Pathname "#$Pathname is the collection of computer filesystem pathnames.  Pathnames are intended to identify directories or files on a given filesystem.  For example, 'file:/cyc/ke/' is a pathname which denotes a directory on the #$Cycorp unix filesystem.  'file:/cyc/ke/temp.txt' is a pathname which denotes a file on the Cycorp unix filesystem.  Similarly, 'file:/cyc/foobar/' is a syntactically legal pathname.  However, because 'file:/cyc/foobar/' is not a directory on the #$Cycorp unix filesystem (well, at least it currently (Jan 2000) isn't), it is not a semantically valid pathname.  The file: is required at the beginning of the name because the browser currently (Sept 2000) will only hotlink to a URL if http: or file: is prepended.  We desire hotlinks for all the uses of #$Pathname in the system currently.  If the universality of this desire lessens, perhaps someday we will want 'HotlinkedPathname' and 'NotHotlinkedPathname'.  #$salientCycorpPathname and relata should have hotlinked pathnames.")
(comment PostPunkMusic "A specialization of #$RockMusic whose heyday was the late 1970s to mid 1980s.  A spin-off of early #$PunkMusic; less raw and openly aggressive.  Often somber in sound and song lyrics, and avowedly underground in nature.  Influential practitioners of this style include Joy Division, Public Image Ltd., Wire, and Echo and the Bunnymen.")
(comment IntegerTypeByRange "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$IntegerTypeByRange is a collection of integers in a particular range from a minimal element to a maximal element.  For example, (#$IntegerFromToFn 1 10), the collection of integers from 1 to 10 would be a member, since it has a minimal element, a maximal element, and all integers in between are instances of the collection.  See also #$integerBetween.")
(comment orgTypeTypicallyHasMemberTypes "(#$orgTypeTypicallyHasMemberTypes ?ORGTYPE ?AGENTTYPE) means that instances of the kind of #$Organization ?ORGTYPE typically have instances of ?AGENTTYPE as members or employees.  Given an organization of that type, in most contexts a member would naturally be expected to be of the member type.  Thus (#$orgTypeTypicallyHasMemberTypes #$LawFirm #$Lawyer), even though most law firms have janitors, receptionists, etc., and such personnel may outnumber the lawyers.  This claim is stronger than (#$relationAllExists #$groupMembers #$LawFirm #$Lawyer) since it says not just that there exists a lawyer in each firm, but that it is typical and expected for firm members to be lawyers.  See also #$memberTypes, which applies to particular organizations, and #$groupMemberType.")
(comment totalDebt "(#$totalDebt AGENT MONEY) identifies MONEY as the total debt that the instance of #$LegalAgent AGENT owes. ")
(comment Agent-Underspecified "The collection of underspecified individuals-- tangible or
otherwise -- which are typically conceptualized by human beings for
purposes of common-sense reasoning as contributing (or complicitous)
agents. This collection thus includes sentient Agents such as
#$BillGates, instrumental 'agents' such as #$Knifes and #$Guns, and
intangible 'agents' such as #$Excitement (as in 'Cassius stabbed
Caesar with great excitement').")
(comment subCultures "(#$subCultures SUPERCULT SUBCULT) means that SUBCULT    is a specific form of SUPERCULT.  SUBCULT shares a significant    portion of the beliefs, practices, and artifacts associated    with SUPERCULT, but (if SUPERCULT and SUBCULT are distinct)    is associated with some additional beliefs, practices, and    artifacts that are not part of SUPERCULT.  This predicate is   intended to apply both to cases in which one culture is a    specific version of another (e.g. Orthodox Jewish culture to   Jewish culture in general; see #$subCultures-Friendly) and to    cases in which one culture    is in some way reacting against the surrounding culture (e.g.    the '60s counterculture to the Establishment; see #$subCultures-Unfriendly).     (See also    #$cultureBeliefs, #$culturePractices, #$cultureSpecialPractices   and #$cultureArtifacts.)")
(disjointWith SententialConstituent Tuple)
(comment Concatenate "just stick em together.")
(comment CultureDefiningMicrotheory "A specialization of #$CultureSpecificMicrotheory, each    instance of which contains assertions describing the beliefs,    practices, and artifacts that characterize some particular instance   of #$Culture-Practice.  See also #$mtDefinesCulture, #$CultureBeliefsMicrotheory, #$cultureBeliefs,    #$culturePractices, #$cultureSpecialPractices, and    #$cultureArtifacts.")
(comment CultureSpecificMicrotheory "A collection of microtheories, each instance of which   contains only assertions that reflect, and are considered true    from, the perspective of a particular #$Culture-Practice (and its subcultures,    if any).   Beyond this, the type of relevance or pertinence of   the microtheory to the culture will depend on other dimension    attributes for the microtheory, including modality.  See also    #$mtOfCulture.  For a specialization of this collection whose    instances are microtheories in which the characteristic beliefs,    practices, and artifacts of particular cultures are precisely specified,    see #$CultureDefiningMicrotheory.  See also #$CultureBeliefsMicrotheory.")
(comment Goodness-Moral "A linear-ordered specialization of #$EvaluativeQuantity  whose instances reflect assessments of the relative moral worth of a particular thing.  Such assessments are usually made in relation to some (often implicit) standard held by the assessor.  Particular instances of this collection can be obtained via the various #$GenericValueFunctions.")
(comment AddingToSystemOfDevicesFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction used to denote the collection of           #$PurposefulActions in which some instance of a type of #$PhysicalDevice           is added to some instance of #$SystemOfDevices. (#$AddingToSystemOfDevicesFn SYS DEVTYPE) denotes the collection of #$Actions in which some instance of DEVTYPE is added to SYS so that it can work together with the other members of SYS.  Note that the #$resultGenls of this function includes #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent. What kind of #$Actions this subcollection contains depends of course on what the type of #$SystemOfDevices SYS is. If, for instance, SYS consists of instances of #$RadioTransmitter and #$RadioReceiver, then the #$Actions that consist in adding to SYS may involve no more than placing an instance of #$RadioReceiver in an appropriate location and seeing to it that it is in working condition and can receive a proper range of frequencies. If SYS is an instance of #$TelephoneSystem, then the #$Actions that consist in adding to SYS will likely be much more involved: all the actions that go into hooking up a telephone.")
(comment CultureBeliefsMicrotheory "A specialization of both #$CultureSpecificMicrotheory   and #$BeliefSystemMicrotheory, each instance of which contains   assertions having the propositional content of the characteristic    beliefs of some particular instance of #$Culture-Practice.  See    also #$cultureBeliefs, #$mtCultureBeliefs and    #$CultureDefiningMicrotheory")
(comment DisposableProduct "An instance of #$ProductType and a specialization of #$Artifact.  Each instance of #$DisposableProduct is an inexpensive, portable #$Product designed to be used for a short time, and then thrown away (or recycled).  Examples: an unrefillable plastic #$CigaretteLighter, a paper #$DinnerPlate, a #$DisposableCamera.  Non-examples: a refillable silver #$CigaretteLighter, a ceramic #$DinnerPlate, an expensive #$DigitalCamera.")
(comment SpecsFn "(#$SpecsFn COL) denotes the collection of all subcollections of COL.")
(comment Rigidity "A specialization of #$TangiblePhysicalQuantity.  Each instance of #$Rigidity is a property of rigidity belonging to physical objects.  See also the predicate #$rigidityOfObject, which relates a physical object to its #$Rigidity.")
(comment Paper "A specialization of #$SolidTangibleArtifact.  Each instance of #$Paper is a portion of paper, considered as a 'stuff' out of which things can be made.  Specializations of #$Paper include #$Cardboard, and #$SheetOfPaper.")
(comment PhysicalField "The collection of all #$CycL representations of physical fields. See also #$LogicalField.")
(comment SchemaType-LogicalOrPhysical "This type-level collection partitions
#$Schema-LogicalOrPhysical into the collections #$LogicalSchema
and #$PhysicalSchema.")
(disjointWith RecruitingActivity-Sports RecruitingActivity-Military)
(comment IfThenElseStatementFn "The famous if/then/else statement of countless programming languages.")
(comment hasSecretaries "(#$hasSecretaries AGENT SECRETARY) means that SECRETARY does secretarial work for AGENT, say a person or a department, etc.")
(disjointWith StorageConstruct WindowPortal)
(comment firstProperSubEventTypes "(#$firstProperSubEventTypes TYPE1 TYPE2) says (#$relationAllExists #$firstSubEvents TYPE1 TYPE2).")
(comment ProtoIndoEuropeanLanguage "A #$DeadLanguage from which all #$Indo-EuropeanLanguageFamily languages are descended.")
(comment ThaiLanguage "A member of the #$ThaiLanguageFamily.")
(comment StandardGeneralizedMarkupLanguage "SGML ('Standard Generalized Markup Language') is a generic #$MarkupLanguage for representing documents. It is an International Standard that describes the relationship between a document's content and its structure.")
(comment Zoroastrianism "A religion originating in Persia around
1400 B.C. It was adapted from an earlier #$PolytheisticReligion by
Zoroaster (or Zarathushtra). During its tenure as a widespread
religion, it had two deities: Ahura Mazda and Angra Mainyu (or
Ahriman). Surviving communities practitioners consider
#$Zoroastrianism to be a #$MonotheisticReligion.")
(comment BusinessTypeByActivityType "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$BusinessTypeByActivityType is a category of business characterized by the performance of some main business activity, such as the selling of flowers or the breeding and selling of animals.")
(comment Varuna "A Kuiper belt object discovered in 2000. It is 1,000 kilometers in diameter.")
(comment BaptistReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment RomanovaConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment VulcanFictionalLanguage "A #$FictionalLanguage.")
(comment InuktitutLanguage "A member of the #$InuitLanguageFamily.")
(comment Prograph-ProgrammingLanguage "An entirely graphical #$VisualProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment Deconstructionism "A school of late-Modern Continental philosophy and literary criticism founded by Jacques Derrida.")
(comment WabashRiver "A #$River in the #$Midwest-USRegion.")
(comment CopticChristianity "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment VorlinConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment OnondagaLanguage "A member of the #$IroquoianLanguageFamily.")
(comment ChauchilaLanguage "A member of the #$YokutLanguageFamily.")
(comment EpicureanSchoolBeliefs "A school of ancient Greek philosophy founded by #$Epicurus.")
(comment WhiteRiverOfIndiana "A #$River in #$Indiana-State.")
(comment GreekOrthodoxReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment AinuLanguage "A #$NaturalLanguage and #$LanguageIsolate spoken in #$Japan.")
(comment WyandotLanguage "A member of the #$IroquoianLanguageFamily.")
(comment SanBlasLanguage "A member of the #$ChibchanLanguageFamily.")
(comment AsteroidBelt "The region of the #$SolarSystem between
#$PlanetMars and #$PlanetJupiter, which is occupied by millions of
#$Asteroids. ")
(comment Atheism "The belief that #$Gods or
other #$SupernaturalBeings do not exist.")
(comment Phenomenology "A school of Continental philosophy founded by #$EdmundHusserl at the end of the 19th century.")
(comment JeffersonRiver "A #$River in #$Montana-State.")
(comment MahayanaBuddhism "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment CastilianLanguage "A member of the #$RomanceLanguageFamily")
(comment LatinLanguage "A member of the #$ItalicLanguageFamily.")
(comment InverseFunctionFn "A #$FunctionDenotingFunction that maps a one-to-one  unary function to its inverse.  (#$InverseFunctionFn FUNCTION)  is the one-to-one function INVERSE that is the inverse (see #$inverseFunctions) of FUNCTION.  Thus, for any things X and  Y, FUNCTION maps X to Y if and only if INVERSE maps Y to X.  For example, in a context involving only monogamous  heterosexual relationships, the inverse of the \"WifeFn\"  would be the \"HusbandFn\".  Note that the condition  stated above is necessary but _not_ sufficient for  one function's being the inverse of another.  This is  because #$Function-Denotationals are understood as being \"functions-in-intension\", making it theoretically possible  for numerically distinct (but extensionally equivalent)  functions to satisfy the above condition with respec to a  given function.  Note also that #$InverseFunctionFn is  \"symmetric\" in that if (#$InverseFunctionFn FUNCTION)  = INVERSE then (#$InverseFunctionFn INVERSE) = FUNCTION;  e.g. in the above example  (#$InverseFunctionFn (#$InverseFunctionFn WifeFn)) = WifeFn. Cf. #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric.")
(comment SwatowDialect "A member of the #$ChineseLanguageFamily.")
(comment GrandCanyon-Place "A #$Canyon located in #$Arizona-State
on the #$ColoradoRiverOfArizona. It is the second-largest canyon in
the world.")
(comment ALGOL "The first AbstractProgrammingLanguag language to be described in #$BackusNaurForm.")
(comment MoldauRiver "A #$River in the #$CzechRepublic.")
(comment MennoniteReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment GanLanguage "A member of the #$Niger-CongoLanguageFamily.")
(comment DiLingoConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment TzeltalLanguage "A member of the #$MayanLanguageFamily.")
(comment XiangLanguage "A member of the #$ChineseLanguageFamily.")
(comment DeathValley "A #$Valley in the #$MojaveDesert.")
(comment Ada-ProgrammingLanguage "Ada is a modern high-level programming language named in honor of the first programmer in history: Lady Ada Lovelace. Ada was designed Jean Ichbiah's team at CII Honeywell in 1979. It was made mandatory for Department ofDefense software projects by the Pentagon.")
(comment LeftistIdeology "A collection of ideologies that place an
emphasis on the value of progress and equality in politics, and are
generally in favor of proposals to reshape society via government
policies. In the United States, a #$LeftistIdeology is associated with
minority rights, downward distribution of wealth, cultural pluralism,
and other particular policies, but none of these is essential to the
ideology itself.")
(comment Pentecostalism "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment HawaiianPidginLanguage "A #$PidginLanguage spoken in the #$HawaiianIslands.")
(comment EssentialWorldEnglish "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment TzotzilLanguage "A member of the #$MayanLanguageFamily.")
(comment UdmurtLanguage "A member of the #$FinnoUgricLanguageFamily.")
(comment CaribbeanSea "The region of the #$AtlanticOcean bounded by
CentralAmerica, the #$ContinentOfSouthAmerica, and the#4
WestIndies-Archipelago.")
(comment CommonLisp "A dialect of #$Lisp. This language originated as an attempt to forge a core common dialect for #$Interlisp and successor implementations of #$MacLisp.")
(comment Marxism-Leninism "The variant of
#$Communism developed by #$VladimirLenin.")
(comment OuluRiver "A #$River in #$Finland.")
(comment PresbyterianReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment TurkicLanguageFamily "A subfamily of the #$AltaicLanguageFamily that includes Turkish, Uzbek, and Azerbaijani.")
(comment EurishConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment SesothoLanguage "A member of the #$Niger-CongoLanguageFamily.")
(comment VepsLanguage "A member of the #$FinnoUgricLanguageFamily.")
(comment KaraSea "The region of the #$ArcticOcean off northern
#$Siberia-Region, bound by the Kara Strait and the Severnaya Zemlya
Islands and the Northern Land Archipelago.")
(comment LDL "An instance of #$AbstractProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment Trotskyism "The variant of
#$Communism developed by #$Trotsky. This variant was deemed unorthodox
in the #$UnionOfSovietSocialistRepublics by #$JosefStalin.")
(comment ConstructingABuilding "A collection of events.  In each #$ConstructingABuilding event, a new #$Building is made.")
(comment ProtestantReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment TheravadaBuddhism "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Buddhism.")
(comment EurolangConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment MannanLanguage "A member of the #$SouthDravidianLanguageFamily.")
(comment VoticLanguage "A member of the #$FinnoUgricLanguageFamily.")
(comment UnitedStatesCapitolBuilding "The #$GovernmentalBuilding
that is the seat of the #$USCongress")
(comment LOGO-ProgrammingLanguage "A #$Lisp -like language designed for teaching programming.")
(comment Hinduism "Hinduism is a #$PolytheisticReligion that
originated in the Vedic culture around 2000 B.C. It incorporates many
belief systems, deities, practices, and scriptures.  It is the third
largest religion with approximately 1 billion followers worldwide,
most of whom live in the #$IndianSubcontinent.")
(comment HurricaneAndrew "A #$HurricaneAsObject that occured in south Florida on August 24, 1992.")
(comment RussianOrthodoxReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment AlawiteIslam "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Islam.")
(comment GlosaConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment MinbeiLanguage "A member of the #$ChineseLanguageFamily.")
(comment OldEnglishLanguage "A member of the #$GermanicLanguageFamily.")
(comment MacLisp "A dialect of #$Lisp developed at MIT in 1966. #$MacLisp was once one of two main branches of #$Lisp (the other being #$Interlisp). CommonLisp was developed in an attempt to combine the best features of the two branches.")
(comment MayanReligion "A #$PolytheisticReligion indigenous to the ancient Maya culture.")
(comment HurricaneGeorges "A #$HurricaneAsObject that occured on the coast of #the $GulfOfMexico on September 28, 1998.")
(comment SeventhDayAdventistReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment IbadhiIslam "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Islam.")
(comment IdoConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment MoheganLanguage "A member of the #$AlgonquianLanguageFamily.")
(comment WalloonLanguage "A member of the #$RomanceLanguageFamily.")
(comment CycConstraintLanguage "A #$DeclarativeProgrammingLanguage for use in Cyc.")
(comment Shintoism "A #$PolytheisticReligion indigenous to
#$Japan.")
(comment CongoRiver "A #$River in the #$ContinentOfAfrica, also known as the Zaire River.")
(comment Holdings "A collection of groups of objects, tangible or intangible, which have some special financial, scientific, cultural, or educational value.  An instance of #$Holdings is a group of all the objects kept and usually maintained by some agent for their value, or to preserve the objects, or to make them available for public display or use (as in a museum's `holdings' or a library's `holdings').  The group-members (see the predicate #$groupMembers) in a group of holdings may or may not be all of a similar type (e.g., books, paintings).  An agent's holdings may or may not all be kept in the same location.  See #$holding for additional details.")
(comment AlbanianOrthodoxReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment LinguaFrancaNovaConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment CapitolHill "A small hill in #$CityOfWashingtonDC that is the location of the #$UnitedStatesCapitolBuilding")
(comment PapiamentuCreoleLanguage "A #$CreoleLanguage.")
(comment OkanoganLanguage "A member of the #$SalishanLanguageFamily.")
(comment PlannerLanguage "A #$DeclarativeProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment Taoism "A set #$ReligiousBeliefs - metaphysical precepts
and related ideas about ethics - indigenous to
#$China-Historical. Though deities have been part of #$Taoism at
certain times, it is not a #$Religion in the usual western sense.
However, it contains elements of mysticism that distinguish it from
#$PhilosophyBeliefs.")
(comment HurricaneHugo "A #$HurricaneAsObject that occured in #$SouthCarolina-State on September 10-22, 1989.")
(comment FenRiver "A #$River in #$China-PeoplesRepublic.")
(comment AnglicanReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment NeoConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment TokPisinCreoleLanguage "A #$CreoleLanguage.")
(comment TaosLanguage "A member of the #$TanoanLanguageFamily.")
(comment Prolog-ComputerLanguage "A #$DeclarativeProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment Vodou "A #$PolytheisticReligion indigenous to
#$Hispaniola-Island, originating among the Fon-Ewe peoples of West
Africa. It is centered in #$Haiti, though variants are practiced in
#$Brazil, #$Cuba, and the #$DominicanRepublic. It is similar to other
religions that evolved among transplanted Africans in the
Americas. Colloqially known as 'Voodoo' in the United States.")
(comment ArcticOcean "The #$ArcticOcean is a body of salt water in the #$NorthernHemisphere-Region, and it borders on the #$ContinentOfNorthAmerica and the #$ContinentOfEurasia. The #$NorthPoleOfEarth is located in this ocean.")
(comment HuaiRiver "A #$River in #$China-PeoplesRepublic.")
(comment ArmenianOrthodoxReligion "A #$ReligiousDenomination of #$Christianity.")
(comment NovialConstructedLanguage "A #$InternationalAuxiliaryLanguage.")
(comment VehicleAccident "A specialization of #$CollidingWithAnObstacle and #$Accident.  Each instance of #$VehicleAccident is an accident in which a vehicle collides with an obstacle.")
(comment ResigningFromEmployment "Voluntarily leaving your job.")
(comment helpfulProposition "The predicate #$helpfulProposition is used to state a condition that, if true, would (normally) increase an agent's chances of success in a particular effort.  (#$helpfulProposition ENDEAVOR PROP DEGREE) means that if the #$ELSentence-Assertible PROP is true, then AGENT (who is referred to in PROP) will (normally) be helped in achieving the #$PurposefulAction ENDEAVOR.  DEGREE is a #$GenericValuePredicate giving an estimate of how helpful such an occurrence would be to the success of ENDEAVOR.   For example, in a basketball game, it's normally helpful if one or more of the team's players is alot taller than the average adult.  Thus, for any particular game, a #$veryHighAmountOf advantage is indicated if the following #$helpfulProposition (expressed in #$CycL) is true: (#$heightOfObject PLAYER-X (#$RelativeGenericValueFn #$heightOfObject #$BasketballPlayer #$veryHighAmountOf)). (Rohrbaugh, 3/1998)")
(disjointWith Prisoner-Legal (CollectionDifferenceFn Person Prisoner-Legal))
(comment AirplaneCrash-Accidental "A specialization of #$AirplaneCrash and #$VehicleAccident (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$AirplaneCrash-Accidental is an event in which an airplane crashes accidentally.")
(comment CommunicationAct-Single "A collection of information transfer events; a specialization of #$Communicating.  Each instance of #$CommunicationAct-Single is a single-source transmission of information from <i>one</i> sender (see the predicate #$senderOfInfo) to one or more recipients (see the predicate #$recipientOfInfo).  Every instance of #$CommunicationAct-Single has a transmission sub-event (which is an instance of #$IBTGeneration) and one or more reception sub-events (which are instances of #$AccessingAnIBT).  An instance of #$CommunicationAct-Single starts when its transmission sub-event starts and ends when its accessing sub-event(s) end.")
(comment Reporting "A specialization of #$Informing.  In each instance of #$Reporting, some agent imparts collected information to some other agent(s).")
(comment Horsepower "An instance of #$UnitOfPower.  When applied to a number or pair of numbers, #$Horsepower returns an instance of #$Power as its value.  For example, (#$Horsepower 300) is a power of 300 horsepower.  Note that 1 horsepower is equivalent to 746 watts.")
(comment PhysicalAmountSlot "A specialization of both #$MeasurableQuantitySlot and #$PhysicalFeatureBinaryPredicate (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PhysicalAmountSlot is a binary predicate used to relate a spatial thing (of some kind or other) to one of its numerically-quantifiable physical attributes (such as its length, mass, or volume).  Examples include #$lengthOfObject, #$massOfObject, and #$bodyTemperature.")
(comment distributionMedium "(#$distributionMedium CW MEDIUM) means that copies (instantiations) of the conceptual work CW are (legally) available in format MEDIUM. For example, (#$distributionMedium (#$SoftwareVersionFn #$MSWindows9x \"98\") #$CD-ROM) means that copies of the Windows 98 software are available on CD-ROM.")
(comment hasMentalQuantity "The predicate #$hasMentalQuantity is used to indicate some mental state or mental capacity of a particular agent that is of a quantitative nature.  (#$hasMentalQuantity SENTIENT MENTAL-QUANTITY) means that the sentient being SENTIENT is characterized by the #$MentalQuantity MENTAL-QUANTITY.  For example, (#$hasMentalQuantity #$BobbyFischer #$HighIntelligence).")
(comment perceives "(#$perceives AGENT OBJECT) means that AGENT is perceiving OBJECT.  That is, AGENT is consciously aware of the presence of OBJECT via the functioning of at least one of her/his/its senses (e.g. sight, hearing, smell, etc).  Precisely which tangible features of OBJECT AGENT apprehends depends in part on the sensory modality used (see the specializations of #$perceives).  For example, if (#$sees AGENT OBJECT) then AGENT is aware of certain visible features of OBJECT (such as color or shape), whereas if (#$smells AGENT OBJECT) then AGENT apprehends certain aromatic features of OBJECT (such as its wearing L'air Du Temps perfume).  Only tangible objects and situations involving such objects (see #$situationConstituents) -- #$PartiallyTangibles, #$PhysicalEvents, and #$Configurations (which are #$StaticSituations involving partially tangibles) -- can be perceived.")
(comment evokes "(#$evokes OBJ QUANT) means that as a consequence of perceiving OBJ, a typical sane intelligent perceiving agent would experience a quantity of #$SensoryQuantity QUANT.  E.g., in some contexts, (#$evokes #$StatueOfLiberty (#$HighAmountFn #$Pride)).")
(comment Cohesiveness "A specialization of #$TangiblePhysicalQuantity.  Each instance of #$Cohesiveness is a specific capacity of a physical object to cohere.  Instances of #$Cohesiveness include #$RigidlyCohesive, #$LiquidCohesiveness, and #$HumanlySeparable.  Cohesiveness of objects is indicated with the predicate #$cohesivenessOfObject.")
(comment Fragility "The collection of scalars representing different degrees of fragility.  This collection is a specialization of #$DispositionalQuantity.  Each instance of #$Fragility is a specific degree of fragility usually denoted by the generic value functions as in (#$HighAmountFn #$Fragility).")
(comment credibilityToAgent "(#$credibilityToAgent INFOTR AGT AMOUNT) means that the #$InformationTransferEvent INFOTR has a credibility, or believability, to the #$Agent-Generic AGT, of amount AMOUNT. So, for instance, (#$credibilityToAgent ReadingUSAToday001 Sue (#$VeryLowToLowAmountFn #$Credibility)) means that Sue finds what she is reading in #$USAToday-TheNewspaper in that particular instance fairly unconvincing, and (#$credibilityToAgent Threatening101 Bill (#$HighAmountFn #$Credibility)) means that Bill finds that particular #$MakingAThreat quite credible.  Note: to say that some agent is credible, write rules stating that any #$Communicating in which that agent is the #$senderOfInfo has a high credibility for whomever receives it. Similarly, to say that some #$InformationBearingThing is credible, say that any #$InformationTransferEvents in which it is the #$informationOrigin has a high credibility for the #$recipientOfInfo.")
(comment Stability "A collection of attributes that are scalar indices of degree of stability of an object.  If an object has low degree of this attribute it suggests that it is very susceptible to being significantly altered, destroyed or damaged.  For instance, a snowball in a warm room has (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Stability).")
(comment Neurophysiologist " A neurophysiologist is a doctor who specializes in the study of the chemical and physical processes of the nervous system.")
(disjointWith OutdoorFurnitureMarketCategory HumanOccupationConstructObject)
(comment Accuracy "A collection of attributes. Each element of #$Accuracy is an attribute describing how accurate a thing or process is. For example, these attributes could apply to an #$InformationBearingThing, which can be described as a more or less accurate copy of something else. They can apply to #$InformationTransferEvents, in which information can be more or less accurately transferred. They can also apply to events in which there is some kind of target which can be hit or missed to different degrees, e.g., passing a football.")
(comment Landmark-Underspecified "The collection of regions/areas, tangible or otherwise, which are typically conceptualized by human beings for purposes of common-sense reasoning as spatial landmarks. This collection thus includes tangible #$GeographicalRegions such as #$CityOfDublinIreland, as well as temporal landmarks such as dates and times (e.g., 9 o'clock) and metaphoric landmarks such as ages and scalar values (e.g., 'at 43, Bill Gates is the richest man in the world'). In lexico-semantic terms, #$Landmark-Underspecified is the semantic category specified for by prepositions such as 'at' : while one can say 'on my mind' and 'in my mind', one cannot say 'at my mind'. The #$Mind can be conceptualized as a #$Container-Underspecified and as a #$SurfaceRegion-Underspecified (on which events and things can 'make impressions') but not as a #$Landmark-Underspecified.")
(comment ResearchFacility "A specialization of #$Facility-Construct (q.v.). Each instance of #$ResearchFacility is building or a complex of buildings or other type of construction used to house #$Research activites performed by #$ResearchOrganizations or #$Researchers.")
(comment NBCWeaponResearchFacility "The collection of instances of  #$ResearchFacility  (buildings or other type of construction)  used to perform #$Research  on kinds of #$WeaponOfMassDestruction.")
(comment insMarketedAs "The predicate #$insMarketedAs relates a type of product to a category in which it is marketed.  (#$insMarketedAs PRODTYPE MKTCAT) means that (at least some) elements of the #$ProductType PRODTYPE are typically marketed to potential consumers as belonging to the #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory MKTCAT.  For example, instances of #$FishingPole are marketed in the #$SportingGoodsMarketCategory; instances of #$SparkPlug are marketed in the #$StandardAutoMarketCategory; and instances of installing a computer network are marketed in the #$ComputerHardwareServiceMC.")
(comment hasAccuracyLevel "(#$hasAccuracyLevel EVT ACCU) says that the 
#$Event EVT has the #$Accuracy ACCU.")
(comment armedWithWeaponType "(#$armedWithWeaponType AGENT WEAPON-TYPE) holds just in case AGENT either is or was armed with instances of WEAPON-TYPE.  This will hold of any agent and weapon type if instances of the weapon type were used in some attack that was performed by the agent.  This predicate's holding of its arguments does not strictly entail that the agent currently has access to instances of the weapon type, but it does give one an idea of a group's possible firepower.")
(comment hasPhysicalPartCount "(#$hasPhysicalPartCount OBJ PART-TYPE INT) means that INT is the number of parts of type PART-TYPE that object OBJ has.")
(comment interArgIsa4-2 "A ternary instance of #$InterArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).   (#$interArgIsa4-2 RELN INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL DEPENDENT-ARG-COL) means that,  when an instance of INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL appears as the fourth argument to RELN,  a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness is that the second argument  must be an instance of DEPENDENT-ARG-COL.  That is, if INST is an instance of INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL, then  (RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 INST ... ARGN) is semantically well-formed only  if ARG2 is an instance of DEPENDENT-ARG-COL.  For an explanation of semantic  well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations.")
(disjointWith OrganicMaterial PrisonCell)
(comment interArgGenlIsa2-1 "(#$interArgGenlIsa2-1 REL VAL COL) means that whenever a specialization of VAL is in the second argument place of REL, an instance of COL must be in the first place.")
(comment lastProperSubEventTypes "(#$lastProperSubEventTypes TYPE1 TYPE2) says (#$relationAllExists #$lastSubEvents TYPE1 TYPE2).")
(comment agentPosesThreat "(#$agentPosesThreat THREAT-AGT AGT) means that THREAT-AGT is disposed to and is capable of acting in a way which threatens a vital interest of AGT.  The class of 'vital' interests in this sense includes (for instances of #$Person) physical and financial well-being, (for instances of #$Country) goals which fall under #$VitalNationalInterest, (for other instances of #$Organization) the ability to fulfill AGT's main function.")
(comment Thing "#$Thing is the \"universal collection\": the collection which, by definition, contains everything there is.  Every thing in the Cyc ontology -- every #$Individual (of any kind), every #$Set-Mathematical, and every #$Collection -- is an instance of (see #$isa) #$Thing.  Similarly, every collection is a subcollection of (see #$genls) #$Thing.  Trivially, #$Thing is both an instance of and a subcollection of itself, and is not a subcollection of any other collection.  (Note that the above reference to \"every thing in the Cyc ontology\" is <i>not</i> meant to be limited to things actually <i>reified</i> in the Cyc system, but includes (e.g.) every instance -- reified or not, known or not -- of every collection recognized by Cyc.)")
(comment Individual "#$Individual is the collection of all individuals:  things that are <i>not</i> sets or collections.  Individuals might be concrete or abstract, and include (among other things) physical objects, events, numbers,  relations, and groups.  An instance of #$Individual might have parts or  structure (including discontinuous parts); but <i>no</i> individual has elements  or subsets (see #$elementOf and #$subsetOf). Thus, an individual that has parts (e.g. #$physicalParts or #$groupMembers) is <i>not</i> the same thing as either the set or the collection containing those same parts.  For example, your car is an individual, but the collection of all the parts of your car is not an individual but an instance of #$Collection.  This collection (unlike the car itself) is abstract: it doesn't have a location, mass, or a top speed; but it does have instances, subcollections, and supercollections.  In partial contrast, the #$Group (q.v.) of parts of your car (while also not the same thing as the car itself) <i>is</i> an individual that has location and mass.  Another example: A given company, the group consisting of all the company's employees, the collection of those employees, and the set of those employees are four distinct things, and only the first two are individuals.")
(comment derivativesOfFunction "(#$derivativesOfFunction FN1 FN2) means that FN2 is the derivative of FN1.  This is a #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate and not asymetric because d e^x dx is e^x.")
(comment SoybeanPlant "Individual soybean plants.")
(comment equipmentOfUnit-TypeCount "(#$equipmentOfUnit-TypeCount UNIT EQUIP-TYPE NUM) means that the agent UNIT possesses NUM instances of the equipment type EQUIP-TYPE.  Nothing is implied regarding the condition of that equipment.  See also the more specific #$equipmentOfUnit-Operational, #$equipmentOfUnit-Damaged, and #$equipmentOfUnit-Destroyed.")
(comment StabbingSomeone "The collection of all instances of #$StabbingSomething where the #$objectActedOn (and by extension the #$maleficiary -- see #$genlPredsWRTTypes) is a #$Person.  This is currently specified in terms of semantic constraints via #$interArgIsa. I.e., the #$objectActedOn or #$maleficiary of a #$StabbingSomeone *must* be known to be an instance of #$Person.  We don't presuppose that the agent doing the stabbing will always be a #$Person -- sometimes a group can be said to be the perpetrator of a stabbing event.  The stabbing itself is a #$PurposefulAction.  We assume the #$deviceUsed will be a #$Knife.")
(comment CityOfCalcuttaIndia "The official name of #$CityOfCalcuttaIndia was changed from 'Calcutta' to 'Kolkata' in 2001.")
(comment commonLanguage "The Cyc predicate #$commonLanguage relates a human cultural group to their common language.  (#$commonLanguage GROUP LANG) means that members of GROUP are fluent in LANG.  Cultural groups include national groups, subcultures, even groups defined by their shared language.  A single group may have more than one #$commonLanguage.  Some examples: (#$commonLanguage #$DominicaPerson #$FrenchCreoleLanguage); (#$commonLanguage #$LiechtensteinPerson #$GermanLanguage); (#$commonLanguage #$WelshPerson #$EnglishLanguage); (#$commonLanguage #$WelshPerson #$WelshLanguage); (#$commonLanguage #$HaitianPerson #$HaitianCreoleLanguage); (#$commonLanguage #$Texan #$EnglishLanguage).")
(comment UniversalVocabularyMt "This is the microtheory which contains the 'definitional' assertions about everything in Cyc's universe of discourse.  Definitional assertions about a concept are those which are intrinsic to the concept's nature and cannot be violated in any context.")
(comment FixedArityRelation "A specialization of #$Relation.  A #$Relation is an instance  of #$FixedArityRelation just in case it takes a fixed number  of arguments.  Most relations reified in the Cyc ontology are  of fixed arity.  For example, #$likesAsFriend always takes two  arguments and #$pointOfContact three.  This collection is  disjoint with #$VariableArityRelation.")
(comment doorHingedTo "(#$doorHingedTo DOOR FRAME) means that DOOR is connected to FRAME vis a vis a hinge.  If no hinge is involved and at least one of the objects is flexible see #$flapHingedTo.")
(comment CollectionTypeType "A higher-order collection and an instance of #$VariedOrderCollection. Instances of #$CollectionTypeType are types of collection types, that is, collections whose instances are collections of (not necessarily first-order) collections. Thus any instance of #$FixedOrderCollection (q.v.) of order greater than or equal to 3 is a specialization of #$CollectionTypeType.")
(comment BusinessAdvisor "A collection of persons; a specialization of #$BusinessPerson.  Each instance of #$BusinessAdvisor provides advice to individuals or businesses about specific areas of interest.  #$BusinessAdvisors are very similar to #$Consultants, but differ from them in that #$BusinessAdvisors will frequently work with individuals rather than organizations.  Additionally, business advisors are generally more focused on the client's interests, while a consultant may just be asked to give their views on a certain topic, without knowing many details of the client's relationship to the topic.  Finally, an advisor (a non-professional advisor) need not be an expert in the field in which they are giving advice--it merely matters that the advisor be a trusted individual.  For example, a person's friend might be an advisor on financial affairs without knowing very much about finances, but a consultant would be expected to have significant knowledge of financial affairs.")
(comment Disapproving-CommunicationAct "A specialization of #$Informing. In each instance of #$Disapproving-CommunicationAct, an #$IntelligentAgent communicates disapproval of something.  Instances of #$Disapproving-CommunicationAct are usually accompanied by feelings of #$Disapproval. For situations in which what the agent disapproves of can be expressed in propositional form, see #$disapprovedStatement. It is also worth noting that instances of #$Disapproving-CommunicationAct differ from instances of #$Rejecting-CommunicationAct, in that the former are expressed judgments that a certain action or opinion is inappropriate, morally wrong, etc., while the latter involve the denial of the propositional content of some communication.")
(comment infectionAffectsPart "(#$infectionAffectsPart INFECTION PART) means that the #$AnimalBodyPart of the host  affected by the #$Infection INFECTION is PART.")
(comment Ox "Oxen are castrated male #$Cattle-Domestic (i.e. bulls) that have been trained for hauling loads, as opposed to Steers, the castrated male #$Cattle-Domestic which are raised for slaughter.")
(comment RatifyingAnInternationalAgreement "A specialization of both #$RatifyingSomething and #$DiplomaticAction (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all acts of ratifying an #$InternationalAgreement.  Instances of #$RatifyingAnInternationalAgreement are typically performed by a   #$RegionalGovernment or by the #$governingBody of a #$PoliticalOrganization.  A #$GeopoliticalEntity's being one of the #$signatories with respect to an   international agreement is normally a necessary condition for ratification by the entity's government, while ratification by its government is itself normally both necessary and sufficient for a geopolitical entity's being one of the #$agreeingAgents to the agreement.  (Similar relationships hold with respect to political organizations whose governing bodies ratify international agreements.)")
(comment policyCoversObjects "(#$policyCoversObjects INSURANCEPOLICY INSUREDTHING) means that INSUREDTHING is insured by INSURANCEPOLICY.")
(comment Ruminant "A group of mammals which bring previously  swallowed food back from their stomachs to re-chew into a finer and  more digestible consistancy.")
(comment FormalCOC "A specialization of #$CodeOfConduct.  Each instance of #$FormalCOC is a code of conduct imposed by some organization.   Formal codes of conduct typically are explicitly stated and publicly promulgated among the group subject to them, and they are usually associated with prescribed methods of enforcement and punishment of violators.  Thus, the collection #$FormalCOC includes the laws of any legal jurisdiction, the rules of deportment imposed by educational institutions, and the practices of some strict religious sects.   In contrast, informal codes of conduct are norms that are not prescribed or enforced by formal means; for example, etiquette (#$MannersCodeOfConduct) and ethics (#$EthicsCodeOfConduct).")
(comment PersonTypeBySocialClass "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$PersonTypeBySocialClass is a collection of people who share a social class, as determined by their general lifestyle, quality of life, and relative access to the (material and security oriented) desiderata of life.  It may depend on a cultural non-material system of social rank, based on birth, office, manners, vocal accent or other factors.  Instances of this collection include #$LowerMiddleClass, #$UpperClass, etc.")
(comment CreditAccount "The class of accounts in which the  balance indicates the amount the holder of the account owes,  such as a mortgage or #$CreditCardAccount. If I have a balance of $500 on my Citibank Visa account, that means I owe Citibank  $500. See #$BankAccount for an example of an account in which the balance indicates the amount the holder owns. Note: it is possible to have a 'negative' balance on a credit account, such as if one pays more than one owes to Citibank. This 'negative' balance would then indicate  the amount owed to the holder of the account.")
(comment AgentPredicate "A collection of predicates that are used to ascribe characteristics or properties (taken in a very broad sense) to individual agents.  As such, each instance of #$AgentPredicate has at least one argument place (often including the first) constrained (see #$argIsa) to instances of #$Agent-Generic or some subcollection thereof. Examples include #$likesAsFriend and #$beliefs.")
(comment ObjectPredicate "A collection of predicates that are used to ascribe characteristics or properties (taken in a very broad sense) to individual objects.  As such, each instance of #$ObjectPredicate has at least one argument place (often including the first) constrained (see #$argIsa) to instances of #$Individual or some subcollection thereof. Examples include #$likesAsFriend, #$connectedTo, and #$arg3Isa.  See also #$SetOrCollectionPredicate.")
(comment Punishment "A specialization of #$NormativeSpecification. Each  describes the 'content' of some element of #$Punishing.")
(comment InfoconLevelMicrotheory "#$InfoconLevelMicrotheory is the subcollection of #$Microtheory containing instances of infocon and infocon criterion microtheories.  Criterion microtheories are spec Mts of the infocon level microtheories and are used to group multiple axioms that together correspond to one evaluation condition for an infocon level.")
(disjointWith Champagne RedWine)
(comment failsCriterion "(#$failsCriterion PROP CRIT DESCRIP) means that something is provable in the #$SupposedToBeMicrotheory instance CRIT that is not provable in the 'real world' description DESCRIP.  'Not provable' of course means unprovable to some set limit on the inference engine's checking.")
(comment toFacilitateInPlan-Prop "(#$toFacilitateInPlan-Prop PLAN PROP) means that the success of the plan represented by the #$SupposedToBeMicrotheory PLAN is contingent upon PROP becoming true in the course of the plan.")
(comment SmallArm-Weapon "A specialization of #$ConventionalWeapon and #$PortableObject.  Each instance of #$SmallArm-Weapon is a weapon that can be carried and used by one person.  Specializations include #$Gun-Portable, #$HandGrenade, #$Sidearm and #$CloseQuarterCombatWeapon.")
(comment Strategy "A specialization of
#$PropositionalConceptualWork and #$NormativeSpecification.  Each
instance of this collection is the abstract work that specifies a
method of accomplishing something.")
(comment executionOfPlan "A #$MicrotheoryPredicate that relates a given instance of  #$PlanSpecificationMicrotheory to any #$PurposefulAction that  is a carrying-out of the plan in question.   (#$executionOfPlan PLANSPEC ACTION) means that ACTION is a complex event that figures as (a complete) execution of the #$Plan (q.v.)  represented by PLANSPEC.  ACTION might be reified in the PLANSPEC  microtheory itself or in some more general microtheory to which  PLANSPEC has access (via #$genlMt).")
(comment ProblemSolvingStrategy "A specialization of #$Strategy.
Each instance of this collection is a heuristic or method to solve
some problem.")
(comment GivingSomething "A specialization of #$TransferringPossession and #$AnimalActivity.  In each instance of #$GivingSomething, one #$Agent-PartiallyTangible gives a tangible thing to another #$Agent-PartiallyTangible.  Every event that is an instance of #$GivingSomething  can also be thought of as a receiving event.  Note: The common case in which one #$Agent-PartiallyTangible gives ownership of an object to another #$Agent-PartiallyTangible is represented by the specialization #$GiftGiving.")
(comment agentObligationInSituation "(#$agentObligationInSituation AGENT REQUIREMENT SITUATION) means that AGENT is required to fulfill REQUIREMENT due to its involvement in SITUATION.  Note that REQUIREMENT is an element of #$Obligation. If it can not be proven that agent fulfills REQUIREMENT (see #$agentFulfillsObligation) then (#$agentViolatesObligation AGENT REQUIREMENT) is concluded.  In order to see the content of REQUIREMENT, one should look at assertions on #$ist-Obligation for the microtheory REQUIREMENT.")
(comment MissionSpecificationMicrotheory "A specialization of #$PlanSpecificationMicrotheory such that the means and ends detailed constitute the mission of a #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  'Mission' is used here in a loose sense that accomodates both the long-term and near-term missions of a unit, and also short-term courses-of-action that might be prepared in a combat theater environment (see #$COASpecificationMicrotheory).  The collection is a specialization of both #$Plan and #$Obligation, since it is believed that a 'mission' presupposes an obligation on the part of someone to carry it out.")
(comment UnitOfVolume "A collection of functions and an instance of #$InterconvertibleUnitType. Each function in this collection is used to denote instances of #$Volume. For example, (#$Liter 5) denotes 5 liters and (#$CubicFoot 22) denotes 22 cubic feet.")
(comment PlanSpecificationMicrotheory "A specialization of #$Microtheory.  Each instance of #$PlanSpecificationMicrotheory is a microtheory whose contents are propositions that are the content of a #$Plan (q.v.).")
(comment timeConstraintOfPlan "(#$timeConstraintOfPlan PLAN DURATION) means that under normal conditions an #$executionOfPlan of PLAN must be completed within the measure of #$Time-Quantity in order for it to count as #$successfulForAgents for the performer.")
(comment Formulary "A #$Formulary is a policy of an HMO (#$HealthMaintenanceOrganization) or other health care provider [such as a hospital] listing the prescription drugs which it agrees  to pay for if one of its members needs it.  Each #$Formulary is  the policy of some #$MedicalCareOrganization, but not all MCOs have a #$Formulary.")
(comment LegalSystemType "A #$Collection of legal systems. Each instance of #$LegalSystemType contains the principles concerning legal procedure and legal structure embodied in a particular kind of legal system.")
(comment InfoconCriterionMicrotheory "#$InfoconCriterionMicrotheory is the subcollection of #$Microtheory whose instances contain GAFs that specify the criteria for going to a specific Infocon level.")
(disjointWith ScalarInterval VectorInterval)
(comment EquipmentTypeForTreatmentOfAilmentTypeFn "This #$SubTopicFunction was created to generate intelligent subtopic followups for specs of #$AilmentCondition, replacing the awkward and misleading-sounding followups that had been generated by #$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn. (#$EquipmentTypeForTreatmentOfAilmentTypeFn AILMENTTYPE) is the collection of all specializations of #$PhysicalDevice whose instances are used in the treatment of AILMENTTYPE. As such, the collection is #$coExtensional with (#$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn (#$TreatmentFn AILMENTTYPE)).")
(comment GovernmentCOC "A collection of codes of conduct; a subcollection of #$FormalCOC (the collection of formal codes of conduct).  Each instance of #$GovernmentCOC is a formal regulation of behavior imposed upon agents -- including citizens, tourists, businesses, government entities, etc., in so far as they are subject to it -- who are located within the legal jurisdiction where that code of conduct holds.  #$GovernmentCOC includes both individual laws and whole legal codes.  Some examples of #$GovernmentCOC include: the Napoleonic Code, the United States Constitution and all Federal, State, and local laws of the United States, and the particular laws against stealing found in most, if not all, countries.  Enforcement of a particular instance of #$GovernmentCOC is authorized by the government associated with that code and carried out by its agents within their jurisdiction(s).  See also #$LegalCode and #$Law.")
(comment toEnsureInPlan-SitType "(#$toEnsureInPlan-SitType PLAN SITTYPE) means that the success of the plan represented by the #$SupposedToBeMicrotheory PLAN is contingent upon a situation of type SITTYPE 'happening' or becoming true.  More colloquially, a situation of type SITTYPE is 'supposed to happen' in the course of PLAN.")
(comment UltravioletLight "A collection of events; a subset of #$ElectromagneticRadiation.  Each element of #$UltravioletLight is an instance of electromagnetic radiation having a wavelength in the range from approximately 1X10^-7 centimeters (10 #$Angstrom) to 4X10^-5 centimeters (4000 #$Angstrom), and a frequency  of approximately 7.5X10^14 #$Hertz to 3X10^17 #$Hertz.")
(comment InfraredRadiation "A collection of events; a subset of #$ElectromagneticRadiation.  Each element of #$InfraredRadiation is an instance of electromagnetic radiation having a wavelength in the range from approximately 7X10^5 centimeters (7000 #$Angstrom) to .01 centimeters (1X10^6 #$Angstrom), and a frequency  of approximately 3X10^12 #$Hertz to 4.3X10^14 #$Hertz.")
(disjointWith UltravioletLight InfraredRadiation)
(disjointWith VisibleLight UltravioletLight)
(comment TransportationOfWeaponToTarget "#$TransportationOfWeaponToTarget is the collection of events in which a weapon is transported to a location prior to use by some #$transporter.  For example, the delivery of an #$AerialBomb involves being flown to a place above the target and then dropping.  It is a subcollection of #$DeliveryOfWeaponToTarget.")
(comment visuallyAcquaintedWith "The predicate which specifies that person X can recognize person Y's physical appearance.  (#$visuallyAcquaintedWith X Y) does not imply (#$visuallyAcquaintedWith Y X ); that is, the fact that X can recognize Y does not allow us to conclude that Y can recognize X.  X may simply have seen Y on TV, etc.")
(comment friends "(#$friends X Y) means X and Y are friends; typically they have a close relationship of reciprocal care, concern, respect, enjoyment, and mutual regard based on emotional (and/or ideological and intellectual) compatibility, shared interests, etc. Note:  In the cases of assertions of two non-human animals being friends, there may be some anthropomorphism going on.")
(comment cohabitants "A predicate that relates animals that live with each other.  (#$cohabitants ANIMAL1 ANIMAL2) means that ANIMAL1 and ANIMAL2 live  together in the same house, apartment unit, nest, hive, den, or other dwelling. Cohabitants are usually but not always of the same species.  My pet cats are  cohabitants of mine, but the ants in my kitchen are not.")
(comment VectorInterval "A specialization of #$TupleOfIntervals.  Each instance of #$VectorInterval is an n-tuple of intervals (where n > 1), one of which is a direction.  Like the instances of #$ScalarInterval, the intervals in an instance of #$VectorInterval may be point-valued or cover a range of values.  The minimal interval (i.e., point-valued) type of vector interval is exemplified by a vector such as `10 meters due east'.   Vectors may also cover a range of values; e.g., `at least 10 feet away and in a horizontal direction'; `between ten to twelve miles NNW'.")
(comment nephews "(#$nephews PERSON NEPHEW) means that NEPHEW is the nephew of PERSON.")
(comment OnceAGeneration "Used to qualify types of events that typically happen once roughly every thirty years.")
(comment awareOfExistenceOf "(#$awareOfExistenceOf X Y) means that X is aware of Y's existence. This predicate is not symmetric: the fact that X knows about Y's existence does not allow us to conclude that Y knows about X's existence.")
(comment DeregulatingASectorOrIndustry "A specialization of both #$LegalProceeding and #$EconomicAction.  Each instance of #$DeregulatingASectorOrIndustry is an event in which a government removes some of the regulations governing a certain industry or economic sector.  The government's purpose in deregulating an industry or sector is usually to make that industry or sector more efficient.")
(comment Irrigation "A specialization of #$DirectedTranslation and #$ChannelFlow.  Each instance of #$Irrigation is the supplying of water to some piece of land by artificial means, usually for the benefit of crops growing on the land.")
(comment ResidentFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction. #$ResidentFn, when applied to an instance of #$GeographicalRegion REGION, returns the collection of all people who (at some specified or implied time) reside in REGION.  See also #$CitizenFn.")
(comment BridgeFinancingAgreement "A specialization of #$AuthorizedAgreement and #$ShortTermLoan.  Each #$BridgeFinancingAgreement results from an instance of #$BridgeFinancing, in which a #$borrower obtains a short-term loan, enabling the #$borrower to start or continue business operations before obtaining a long-term loan.")
(comment Library-Organization "A specialization of #$ServiceOrganization.  Each instance of this collection is an organization which purchases, collects, maintains, and often lends out a collection of #$InformationBearingThings such as books, magazines, videos, audio cassettes, CDs, CD-ROMs, DVDs, etc. (see #$LibraryHoldings) for perusal by its members, or by the general public.  Instances of this collection may be organizations in their own right, or #$subOrganizations of some larger entity (such as a University). For the latter case, see the more specialized collection #$Library-Department.")
(comment ElectricalCurrentLevel "A specialization of #$Rate and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ElectricalCurrentLevel is a rate of electrical flow, given in terms of the amount of charge (see the collection #$ElectricalCharge) per unit time.  The standard unit of measure (see the collection #$UnitOfMeasure) for #$ElectricalCurrentLevel is the ampere (see #$Ampere).")
(disjointWith InterArgRelnPredicate InterArgConstraintPredicate)
(comment InterArgRelnPredicate "The collection of all constraining predicates that are used to state that a particular relation must hold between two arguments of a relation of #$arity 2 or higher in terms employing that predicate.")
(comment UnitOfMonetaryRate "The collection of functions which return elements of #$MonetaryRate (i.e. transfer of money per unit of time).  E.g., (#$DollarsPerHour 10) denotes a #$MonetaryRate of $10 per hour.")
(comment Speed "A specialization of #$Rate and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Speed is a rate of change in position (e.g., of a spatially localized thing, such as a physical object or a wave front).  Instances of #$Speed include (#$MilesPerHour 65) and (#$Mach-UnitOfSpeed 2).")
(comment Power "A specialization #$Rate and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Power is an amount of energy per some unit of time.")
(comment CPUSpeed "A specialization of #$ComputerQuantity, #$Rate, and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.). Each instance of this collection is some particular measure of computer CPU (central processing unit) power -- usually understood in some way in terms of speed in processing instructions. Instances of this collection are often denoted using functions such as #$MIPS, which fall into the collection #$UnitOfCPUSpeed.")
(comment AngularAccelerationRate "A specialization of #$Rate and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$AngularAccelerationRate is a rate at which the #$RateOfRotation (q.v.) of some object changes.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn ThirdRib) Tuberosity) "<p>")
(comment VariedOrderCollection "A #$CollectionType and the collection of all and only those #$Collections some of whose instances (or instances of instances, or instances of instances of instances, etc.) are of different \"orders\", where order is understood as follows.  Let #$Individuals be \"zeroth-order\" things, collections of (only) #$Individuals \"first-order\", ..., and collections of (only) Nth-order things \"(N+1)th-order\".  A more precise, recursive definition, then, is: a collection COL is an instance of #$VariedOrderCollection if and only if COL has instances INST1 and INST2 such that either (i) INST1 and INST2 are of two different fixed orders N1 and N2, (ii) INST1 is of the fixed order N1 and INST2 is a varied-order collection, or (iii) INST1 and INST2 are both varied-order collections.
<p>
For example, #$AbstractThing is a varied-order collection since it contains both (zeroth-order) abstract individuals and (higher-order) collections.  Other instances of #$VariedOrderCollection include #$Intangible, #$Collection, #$CollectionType, and #$Thing.  Note that (by clauses ii and iii) any generalization of a varied-order collection is varied-order; and (by clause iii) any (non-empty) specialization of #$VariedOrderCollection is varied-order.
<p>
Note that #$FixedOrderCollection (q.v.) and #$VariedOrderCollection do _not_ constitute a partition of #$Collection: since the notion of order described above is not applicable to sets, #$Set-Mathematical (e.g.) and its specializations are neither fixed-order nor varied-order collections.")
(comment frequencyOfActionType "A predicate for stating the frequency with which typical instances of some type of #$TemporalThing play certain roles in certain types of event. Specifically, <code>(#$frequencyOfActionType ACTTYPE ACTORTYPE ROLE FREQUENCY)</code> indicates that typical instances of <code>ACTORTYPE</code> (where A<code>CTORRTYPE</code> is a specialization of #$TemporalThing) play the role <code>ROLE</code> in instances of <code>ACTTYPE</code> (where <code>ACTTYPE</code> is a specialization of #$Event) with the frequency <code>FREQUENCY</code>. For example, #$frequencyOfActionType can be used to express the fact that typical vertebrates are virtually always breathing: (#$frequencyOfActionType #$Breathing #$Vertebrate #$bodilyDoer #$Continuously).  This predicate should _not_ be used for action types that instances of <code>ACTORTYPE</code> do not typically perform role <code>ROLE</code> in, even if those instances of <code>ACTORTYPE</code> that do, do so with frequency <code>FREQUENCY</code>. For those kinds of assertions, use
#$regularFrequencyOfActionType.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn SeventhRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$TotallyOrderedCollection and #$FirstOrderCollection (qq.v.). Each #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType is a totally-ordered collection all of whose instances are #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.)
<p>
Specializations of this collection include #$MeasurableScalarIntervalType and #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn NinthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment objectOfControlTransfer "<code>(#$objectOfControlTransfer SIT OBJ)</code> means that physical control of <code>OBJ</code> passes from one controlling agent to another in <code>SIT</code>.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn SeventhRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment CycLGAFAssertion "A #$CycLExpressionType and a specialization of both #$CycLAtomicAssertion and #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence (qq.v.).  This is the collection of all and only the closed atomic CycL sentences that have been asserted to the Knowledge Base or deduced there by the inference engine.  GAF assertions typically express particular facts (as opposed to general rules) about the world.  The acronym `GAF' stands for \"ground atomic formula\"; see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence.
<p>
At the epistemological level (see #$ELExpression), a GAF assertion contains no free variables and is thus (at least) \"ground\".  At the heuristic level (see #$HLExpression), however, the #$HLAssertion(s) representing the GAF assertion -- the sentence(s) that are actually stored in the KB -- contain no variables whatsoever and are thus \"_fully_ ground\" (see #$CycLFullyGroundAtomicSentence).  For example, if the ground atomic sentence
<p>
(#$isa (#$TheSetOf ?X (#$objectHasColor ?X #$GreenColor)) #$Thing)
<p>
were asserted it would be a GAF assertion, and it would be represented in the KB by some fully ground atomic sentence(s) containing no variables at all.")
(comment RegularlyRepeatedEventType "A specialization of #$RepeatedEventType (q.v.).  Each instance of #$RegularlyRepeatedEventType is a collection of events whose instances typically occur along with other instances of the same event-type at more or less regular intervals.  These regular occurrences might be the result of natural phenomena (e.g. the sunrise), human convention (e.g. the execution of a dance step within a given dancing event), or a combination of both (e.g. the annual celebration of Oktoberfest).
<p>
Other examples of #$RegularlyRepeatedEventType are #$OlympicGames, #$AcademicTerm, and #$TakingABreath.
<p>
See also #$IterativeEvent and #$IterativeEventType.")
(comment endsAfterStartingOf "<code>(#$endsAfterStartingOf ENDER STARTER)</code> means that <code>ENDER</code> ceases to exist or occur after <code>STARTER</code> begins to exist or occur. That is, the #$endingPoint of <code>ENDER</code> is later than the #$startingPoint of <code>STARTER</code>.  Equivalently: <code>(#$after (#$EndFn ENDER) (#$StartFn STARTER))</code>.  This implies nothing about whether, or by how much, the temporal extents of <code>ENDER</code> and <code>STARTER</code> overlap.")
(comment MicrotheoryPredicate "A specialization of #$IntangibleObjectPredicate whose instances are used to describe the properties and relationships of microtheories.  As such, each instance of #$MicrotheoryPredicate has at least one of its argument-places constrained (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$Microtheory or some specialization thereof.  Instances of #$MicrotheoryPredicate include #$domainAssumptions, #$mtTime, #$genlMt, #$adheresToCodeOfConduct, #$ist-Agreement, and #$ist-Obligation.
<p>
For predicates that relates microtheories to each other, see the specialization #$MicrotheoryRelatingPredicate.")
(comment programCopies "A predicate that relates instances of #$ComputerProgram-CW to instances of #$ComputerProgramCopy.  (#$programCopies PROG COPY) means that COPY is a physical instantiation of PROG.  For example: (#$programCopies #$Emacs-TheProgram ACopyOfEmacsOnMyHardDrive).
<p>
A #$ComputerProgramCopy might not have the exact same #$ComputerCode as other #$programCopies of the same #$ComputerProgram-CW, and it might not even have the exact same algorithms, but the functionality must be about the same.
<p>
For example, if (#$SoftwareVersionFn #$Emacs-TheProgram \"20.4\") were written in C, and one compiled one copy of it on a Solaris machine and another copy on an HP-UX machine, the compiled versions would be different but they would still be #$programCopies of the same #$ComputerProgram-CW, (#$SoftwareVersionFn #$Emacs-TheProgram \"20.4\"), and also #$programCopies of #$Emacs-TheProgram.")
(comment threatAntecedent "The form of a #$Threat is: 'If you (the #$threatenedAgent) do <i>X</i>, I (the #$threateningAgent) will do <i>Y</i>'.  The antecedent of this conditional (i.e. 'the threatened agent does <i>X</i>') is the #$threatAntecedent in an instance of #$MakingAThreat.  Thus <code>(#$threatAntecedent THREATENING SENT)</code> means that the #$ELSentence-Assertible <code>SENT</code> is the antecedent of the #$Threat made in the #$MakingAThreat <code>THREATENING</code>.  See also #$threatConsequent.")
(comment VeryLowAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$VeryLowAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>very low amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Happiness) is a very low amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$NoAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$LowAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$veryLowAmountOf.")
(comment RepeatedEventType "A specialization of #$FirstOrderCollection (q.v.) whose instances are collections of #$Events.  Each instance of #$RepeatedEventType is a collection of events whose instances tend to come in series or to occur at more or less regular intervals.  These series or recurrences might be the result of natural phenomena (e.g. the spoutings of Old Faithful), human convention (e.g. the occurrence of #$BaseballInnings within a given game), or a combination of both (e.g. the annual celebration of Oktoberfest).
<p>
An important specialization of this collection is #$RegularlyRepeatedEventType.
<p>
See also #$IterativeEvent and #$IterativeEventType.")
(comment onlyTypePlaysRoleInSubSituationType "<code>(#$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSubSituationType SIT-TYPE ROLE-PLAYER-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that in an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, whatever plays the role <code>ROLE</code> in the sub-situation that is the instantiation of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE</code> must be an instance of <code>ROLE-PLAYER-TYPE</code>. Cf. the stronger predicate #$someAndOnlyTypePlaysRoleInSubSituationType, which requires that in every instance of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE</code>, some instance of <code>ROLE-PLAYER-TYPE</code> plays <code>ROLE</code>.")
(comment abnormal "A binary #$MetaKnowledgePredicate that is used to state exceptions to a given general #$CycLAssertion.  Typically, an #$abnormal assertion will be the result of the #$CycCanonicalizer having \"transformed\" a sentence asserted at the EL level that was built from an #$ExceptionPredicate (i.e. #$exceptWhen or #$exceptFor).  While perhaps less common-sensically intuitive than #$exceptWhen or #$exceptFor, #$abnormal facilitates an efficient and uniform handling of exceptions.
<p>
Here, in more detail, is how #$abnormal is used.  Every default rule in our system, schematically:
<p>
P[X1 ... Xn] -> Q[X1 ... Xn]
<p>
(with X1, ..., Xn free in both P and Q) is implicitly treated as
<p>
(not (\"abnormal-for-this-rule\" X1 ... Xn)) & (P[X1 ... Xn] -> Q[X1 ... Xn]) .
<p>
Thus rules without exceptions need never incur the overhead of default reasoning.  Since a different \"abnormality\" relation would otherwise be needed for every default rule in the system, we use instead a single predicate -- #$abnormal -- which takes the rule in question as an argument.  And the other argument to #$abnormal is a #$List of variables (or of a single closed term) over which abnormality is computed; this allows #$abnormal to be binary rather than variable-arity.  Hence a sentence of the form
<p>
(#$exceptWhen EXCEPTION RULE) ,
<p>
in which each of the variables X1, ..., Xn occurs free in both EXCEPTION and RULE, gets canonicalized to an #$implies rule that concludes to an #$abnormal sentence:
<p>
<pre>
(#$implies
  EXCEPTION
  (#$abnormal (#$TheList X1 .... Xn) RULE)) .
</pre>
<p>
And a sentence of the form
<p>
(#$exceptFor TERM RULE) ,
<p>
in which exactly one variable X occurs free in RULE, gets canonicalized to a sentence of the form
<p>
(#$abnormal (#$TheList TERM) RULE) .
<p>
See also #$pragmaticallyNormal.")
(comment quantitiesShareScale "A ternary predicate that relates two instances of #$Quantity to a linear ordering (see #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType) that they both occur in.  <code>(#$quantitiesShareScale QUANT1 QUANT2 SCALE)</code> means that <code>QUANT1</code> and <code>QUANT2</code> are two distinct instances of the totally-ordered quantity-type <code>SCALE</code> (see #$colinearQuantities).  This implies that either <code>(#$followingValueOnScale QUANT1 QUANT2 SCALE)</code> or <code>(#$followingValueOnScale QUANT2 QUANT1 SCALE)</code> holds.
<p>
<p>
Where <code>QUANT1</code> and <code>QUANT2</code> are both #$Quantity[ies], <code>(#$followingValueOnScale QUANT1 QUANT2 SCALE)</code> also implies <code>(#$quantitiesShareScale QUANT1 QUANT2 SCALE)</code>.  We can think of the latter predicate as a version of the former that applies only to quantities and is unconcerned about their ordering with respect to <code>SCALE</code>.")
(comment relationAllExistsMax "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  (#$relationAllExistsMax BINPRED COL1 COL2 N) means that for any instance INST of COL1 there are at most N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL2 such that (BINPRED INST INST(i)) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$implies
(#$isa ?INST COL1)
(#$thereExistAtMost N ?INST-I
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST-I COL2)
(BINPRED ?INST INST-I)))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationAllExistsMax #$anatomicalParts #$Dog #$Leg 4)' means that all dogs have at most four legs.
<p>
Cf. #$relationExistsMaxAll; also see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment outputs "This predicate relates a particular creation or destruction event to any of its \"outputs\".  <code>(#$outputs EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is an output of <code>EVENT</code>.  That is, <code>OBJECT</code> is either created/produced as a result of <code>EVENT</code> (see the specializations #$outputsCreated, #$products, #$byProducts) or something left after another thing was destroyed in <code>EVENT</code>  (see the specialization #$outputsRemaining). For example, silicon chips are both #$outputsCreated in and #$products of a chip manufacturing process, while a hunk of scrap metal is an #$outputsRemaining after a car is put through a crusher.  When appropriate, instead of using #$outputs, use one of its specializations (of which those mentioned above are the nearest).")
(comment UnitProductFn "A binary #$UnitOfMeasureDenotingFunction that takes two #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) functions and returns the unit-of-measure function whose value for any given argument is the multiplicative \"product\" of the respective values yielded by the first two units, when one unit is applied to that same argument and the other unit is applied to 1.  That is, <code>(#$UnitProductFn UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> is the function <code>PRODUCT-FN</code> such that, for any number or other #$NumericInterval <code>NUM</code>, <code>(PRODUCT-FN NUM)</code> = <code>(#$TimesFn (UNIT1 NUM) (UNIT2 1))</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$UnitProductFn #$Meter #$Meter) is #$SquareMeter; and (e.g.) (#$SquareMeter 5) is equal to (#$TimesFn (#$Meter 5) (#$Meter 1)). Also, (#$UnitProductFn  #$Kilowatt #$HoursDuration) is #$KilowattHour.
<p>
See also #$PerFn.")
(comment AccomplishmentType "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$AccomplishmentType is a type of events that include, by their nature, a 'culmination' or completion point, conceptually distinct from their mere temporal ending. Moreover, each instance of an instance of #$AccomplishmentType is 'quantized' in the following sense: if <code>EVENT-1</code> and <code>EVENT-2</code> are instances of the same instance of #$AccomplishmentType, then <code>EVENT-2</code> cannot be a proper sub-event of <code>EVENT-1</code>, nor can <code>EVENT-1</code> be a proper sub-event of <code>EVENT-2</code>.")
(comment noAmountOf "The instance of #$GenericValuePredicate (q.v.) that corresponds to (see #$correspondingGenericValuePredicate) the generic-value function #$NoAmountFn.  <code>(#$lowAmountOf QUANTTYPE QUANTITY)</code> holds just in case <code>QUANTITY</code> is the #$GenericQuantity that corresponds to roughly <i>no amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$noAmountOf #$Happiness (#$NoAmountFn #$Happiness))</code> holds.  And <code>(#$levelOfCompatibility OLDTHING NEWTHING #$noAmountOf)</code> holds just in case <code>OLDTHING</code> and <code>NEWTHING</code> are not compatible at all.
<p>
See also #$NoteAboutTheDualRolesOfGenericValuePredicates.")
(comment ExponentFn "A #$BinaryFunction which returns the result of raising a specified number to a specified exponent. (#$ExponentFn BASE EXP) returns BASE^EXP, i.e. BASE to the power EXP, where BASE and EXP are both instances of #$ComplexNumber. It returns instances of #$ComplexNumber.
<p>
Note that if one uses this function to determine the Nth root of numbers where N is even, i.e. by evaluating (#$ExponentFn BASE (#$QuotientFn 1 N)),  this function returns only the positive real, X, such that X^N = BASE (whereas a negative root may also exist).   Also note that the function is often undefined when the first argument is negative.
<p>
See also #$ExpFn, a unary function which returns the result of raising #$E-LogarithmBase to a specified exponent.  And see #$ScalarExponentFn, an extension of #$ExponentFn to all (measurable) #$ScalarIntervals.")
(comment arg2Isa "A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg2Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the second argument to RELN must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2</code> ...) is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$isa ARG2 COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a similar but more flexible ternary  predicate.")
(comment objectEmitted "This predicate is used in connection with particular instances of #$EmittingAnObject, to identify the object that 'comes out' during the event.  <code>(#$objectEmitted EMIT OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is emitted from the #$emitter during the emission event <code>EMIT</code>.  The #$objectEmitted is also an #$objectMoving in <code>EMIT</code>.  For example, in a particular human #$BirthEvent, some particular #$HumanInfant is an #$objectEmitted.")
(comment temporallySubsumes-TypeType "A #$TemporalPredicate and a specialization of #$temporallyIntersects-TypeType (q.v.) that is a type-level correlate of #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.).  This predicate is used to state that things of one given type always temporally subsume things of another given type.  (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType LONGTYPE SHORTTYPE) means that for every instance LONGINST of LONGTYPE, there is an instance SHORTINST of SHORTTYPE such that (#$temporallySubsumes LONGINST SHORTINST).
<p>
For example, (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType #$CalendarQuarter #$CalendarMonth) means that every calendar quarter subsumes at least one entire calendar month.
<p>
See also #$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType; and note that (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType SHORTTYPE LONGTYPE) is neither necessary nor sufficient for (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType LONGTYPE SHORTTYPE).")
(comment denotation "A quaternary #$NLSemanticPredicate that relates natural language words to CycL terms to which they correspond in a certain way.  Some words have multiple such denotations due to the fact that they possess multiple #$WordSenses (q.v.), which word-senses might or might not belong to the same parts of speech (i.e. nouns, verbs, adjectives etc.); thus #$denotation also has argument-places for specifying both a #$SpeechPart (q.v.) and a particular word-sense (as represented by an integer understood in relation to some standardized word-sense numbering scheme).
<p>
(#$denotation WORD SPEECH-PART N TERM) means that the CycL correlate (or \"denotation\") of WORD, with respect to SPEECH-PART and the corresponding word-sense numbered N, is TERM.  Examples: (1) #$Place-TheWord, an instance of #$EnglishWord, has the denotation #$PuttingSomethingSomewhere when used as a #$Verb with its word-sense 0, and the denotation #$Place when used as a #$CountNoun with that same word-sense; (2) #$Ring-TheWord currently has four denotation assertions in the Knowledge Base: #$EmittingSound (as a #$Verb with its word-sense 1); #$AudibleSound (as a #$CountNoun with its word-sense 1); #$RingShapedObject (as a #$CountNoun with its word-sense 0); and #$Ring-Jewelry (as a #$CountNoun with its word-sense 2).  An example of a #$denotation sentence that has a NAT (see #$CycLNonAtomicTerm) rather than a constant in its fourth argument-position is
<p>
    `(#$denotation #$Density-TheWord #$Adjective 0 (#$IntervalMinFn #$LowAmountFn #$Density))'.
<p>
Note that #$denotation is _not_ to be confused with #$denotes (q.v.), which relates denoting CycL terms to things \"out there in the world\" (i.e. in the intended model of the CycL language).")
(comment motionPathway-Complete "This predicate can be used to indicate that the trajectory or trajectories travelled by one or more objects during a movement event follow some spatial path throughout the entire movement. More formally, if <code>MOVE</code> is an instance of #$Translation-SingleTrajectory, <code>(#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH)</code> means that the instance  <code>PATH</code> of #$Path-Spatial contains the entire instance of #$Trajectory along which the objects moving (see #$objectMoving) travel in <code>MOVE</code>.  Note that there can be at most one #$motionPathway-Complete in this case.  If <code>MOVE</code> is an instance of #$Translation-MultiTrajectory, <code>(#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH)</code> means that the instance <code>PATH</code> of #$Path-Spatial contains the entire instance of #$Trajectory along which at least one of the objects moving in <code>MOVE</code> travels.  In this case, more than one #$motionPathway-Complete may exist.  (Note that the instance of #$Traversal determined by the #$trajectory-Complete need not occupy the whole length of <code>PATH</code>.) To explain this predicate somewhat differently, any #$motionPathway-Complete of an instance of #$Movement-TranslationEvent connects the starting and stopping points (the #$fromLocation and #$toLocation) of the whole trajectory of at least one object in the movement event (See also #$pathConnects).   Note that <code>PATH</code> may be a marked or unmarked instance of #$Path-Spatial.  See also #$trajectoryPassesThrough and #$motionPathway-Partial. You can use #$trajectoryPassesThrough to state that an object passes through a particular location on the object's trajectory.")
(comment AsymmetricBinaryPredicate "An instance of #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and a specialization of #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate (q.v.).  A binary predicate <code>BINPRED</code> is an instance of #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate if and only if (i) for any <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>, <code>(BINPRED THING1 THING2)</code> implies <code>(#$not (BINPRED THING2 THING1))</code> and (ii) the single-argument argument type (i.e. #$argIsa and #$argGenl) constraints on <code>BINPRED</code>'s two respective argument-places are \"co-satisfiable\" (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement).
<p>
Examples of asymmetric binary predicates include #$northOf, #$children, #$greaterThan, and #$overrides.  Cf. #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.")
(comment postEvents "A #$BinaryRolePredicate (q.v.) that relates one event to another that follows  it.  <code>(#$postEvents BEFORE AFTER)</code> implies two things:
<p>
(1) <code>BEFORE</code> occurs strictly before <code>AFTER</code>: <code>AFTER</code> starts after <code>BEFORE</code> has ended (see the generalization of #$postEvents, #$startsAfterEndingOf).
<p>
(2) <code>BEFORE</code> and <code>AFTER</code> are significantly <i>relevant</i> to each other.  This required  \"relevance\" of the two arguments to each other is difficult to formalize but  often intuitively clearcut.  Sometimes <code>AFTER</code> is caused by <code>BEFORE</code>.  For  example, we might say (#$postEvents TakingAShower001 ExitingShower001), but  would not say (#$postEvents BolshevikRelvolution DeathOfJohnLennon) since these two events are not at all relevant to each other.")
(comment directingAgent "An instance of #$ActorSlot (q.v.) that is a  specialization of #$deliberateActors.  <code>(#$directingAgent EVENT DIRECTOR)</code> means that <code>DIRECTOR</code> deliberately controls or directs <code>EVENT</code>, typically by controlling other participants in <code>EVENT</code> (see #$controls).  <code>AGENT</code> might or might not perform <code>EVENT</code> directly.  If an agent other than <code>DIRECTOR</code> performs (see  #$performedBy) <code>EVENT</code>, then that performer's actions in <code>EVENT</code> are controlled by at least one of <code>EVENT</code>'s directing agents.  (In many cases, of course, <code>EVENT</code> will have only one director.)  Non-performing directors typically control the actual performers by communicating instructions (or orders,  commands, etc.) to them.  For example,  (#$directingAgent PSOBicentennialConcertOfBeethovensNinth WilliamSteinberg)  holds, because Steinberg was the conductor of that concert; he directed  the many other musicians who performed in that event.  Other examples are  (#$directingAgent MassacreAtMyLai LtWilliamCalley) and  (#$directingAgent TateLaBiancaMurders CharlesManson).")
(comment interArgIsaGenl "<code>(#$interArgIsaGenl RELN INDEPENDENT-ARGNUM INDEPENDENT-COL DEPENDENT-ARGNUM DEPENDENT-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-COL</code> appears as the <code>INDEPENDENT-ARGNUM</code><sup>th</sup> argument to <code>RELN</code>, the corresponding <code>DEPENDENT-ARGNUM</code><sup>th</sup> argument must be a subcollection of <code>DEPENDENT-COL</code>, or the result is not semantically well-formed.")
(comment thereExistAtMost "An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) and a specialized (albeit higher arity) version of #$thereExists (q.v.).  Compared to the latter, standard existential quantifier, #$thereExistAtLeast has an extra argument-place for specifying how many things (at the most) satisfy a given condition (specified by an #$ELSentence-Assertible, in which the also-specified #$ELVariable will typically occur free).  A closed sentence of the form (#$thereExistAtMost NUM VAR SENT) means that there are at most NUM distinct things that satisfy SENT, i.e. that render SENT true when taken as the value of VAR.  For example, the sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$thereExistAtMost 7 ?X (#$isa ?X #$Sea))
</pre>
<p>
means that there are at most seven seas.
<p>
In the degenerate case where VAR does not appear free in SENT, then (#$thereExistAtMost NUM VAR SENT) is equivalent to SENT.
<p>
See also #$thereExistAtLeast, #$thereExistExactly.")
(comment geographicalSubRegions "<code>(#$geographicalSubRegions SUPER SUB)</code> means that <code>SUPER</code> and <code>SUB</code> are both elements of #$GeographicalRegion, and the area <code>SUB</code> lies wholly within the region <code>SUPER</code> (see #$inRegion).  <code>SUPER</code> may or may not completely surround <code>SUB</code>, as they may share an outer boundary (e.g., the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and  #$Texas-State in the #$WorldGeographyDualistMt).   Note that, if either argument to #$geographicalSubRegions is an instance of #$GeopoliticalEntity, the assertion must be made in #$WorldGeographyDualistMt or a more specialized microtheory.   To relate two instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity, use the predicate #$geopoliticalSubdivision, if it applies.  See also #$surroundsHorizontally and #$bordersOn.")
(comment arg6Genl "A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg6Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the sixth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5 ARG6 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG6 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment requiresMemory "
(#$requiresMemory PROGRAM RAM) means that PROGRAM
  is an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW and that PROGRAM will only run on
  a computer that has #$ComputerMemoryCapacity RAM.")
(comment ist-Asserted "A #$MicrotheoryDesignatingPredicate that is used to relate CycL sentences to microtheories in which they are explicitly asserted. (#$ist-Asserted MT SENT) states that there is a #$CycLAssertion for SENT in the #$Microtheory MT.  This predicate is therefore a more specialized form of #$ist (q.v.) with the additional requirement that there be an actual assertion for SENT in MT.
<p>
Note that the above is also more precise than (#$ist MT (#$assertedSentence SENT)), which states that there is an assertion for SENT in some microtheory visible from (see #$genlMt) MT.")
(comment integerRange "A binary predicate that relates an #$IntegerTypeByRange (q.v.) to the #$IntegerExtent (q.v.) that corresponds to the range of the former.  <code>(#$integerRange INTEGER-TYPE EXTENT)</code> means that <code>EXTENT</code> spans exactly the range of the integers in the collection <code>INTEGER-TYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$integerRange (#$IntegerFromToFn 1 10) (#$Unity 1 10)) holds.")
(comment relationExtension "A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a given instance of #$Relation to its set-theoretic extension: the set containing all of and only the sequences of things among which the relation holds. (#$relationExtension RELATION EXTENT) means that the extension of RELATION is EXTENT.  More precisely (and following standard mathematical practice): EXTENT is the set of instances SEQ(i) of #$List such that RELATION holds among the members of SEQ(i) (taken in the same order as they appear in SEQ(i)).  If RELATION is a #$FixedArityRelation with #$relationalArity (q.v.) N then each of the SEQ(i) will have length N.  If RELATION is a #$VariableArityRelation (q.v.) then there will be no particular length shared by each of the SEQ(i).
<p>
Note also that since #$Relations are \"intensionally defined\" it follows that (i) a given relation can have different extensions with respect to different contexts (see #$Microtheory) and (ii) two distinct relations can have the same extension with respect to a given context.
<p>
See also #$relationHoldsAmong, #$relationDomain, and #$relationRange.")
(comment decreasesCausally "<code>(#$decreasesCausally EVENT THING SLOT)</code> means that <code>EVENT</code> causes the value of the predicate <code>SLOT</code> applied to <code>THING</code> (in the first argument position) to decrease over some time period after the start of <code>EVENT</code>.  This is defined in terms of #$decreasesCausallyOverPeriod (see also).  Compared with its value immediately before <code>EVENT</code>, the value of <code>SLOT</code> applied to <code>THING</code> is guaranteed to be higher immediately following or some time after <code>EVENT</code>.  This net decrease need not be monotonic.  See #$NoteAboutQuantityChangePredicates for a map of related predicates.")
(comment Slide-Photograph "The collection of all instances of #$Photograph that are designed, not to be viewed directly, but for light to be projected through them onto a surface generating a #$StillImage to be viewed.
<p>
Note that a given #$Slide-Photograph might or might not be an instantiation (see #$instantiationOfWork) of some #$Slide-CW (q.v.), depending on whether or not the former is used in the performance of a #$Slideshow-CW (q.v.).")
(comment informationDestination "A specialization of both #$informationTerminal and #$to-Generic that is used to  indicate where information is transferred in a given #$InformationTransferEvent.   <code>(#$informationDestination TRANSFER DESTINATION)</code> means that in <code>TRANSFER</code>, the  information being transferred is sent to, given to, or impressed upon <code>DESTINATION</code>. <code>DESTINATION</code> is typically either an #$InformationBearingThing (at least upon  the completion of <code>TRANSFER</code>, if not before) or an agent; but note that  in the latter case one would normally use the more specific predicate  #$recipientOfInfo.")
(comment spatiallyRelated "A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate (q.v.) and an #$EquivalenceRelation.  This is indeed the most general of all #$SpatialPredicates (q.v.), in that it is by design a generalization (via either #$genlPreds or #$genlPredsWRTArgs) of every spatial predicate.  <code>(#$spatiallyRelated THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> are somehow spatially related to each other.  This implies that the two things are embedded within the same surrounding space, but does not specify any more particular spatial relation between them.  Thus, any two spatial objects located in the empirically-observable universe (see #$SpatialThing-Localized) are #$spatiallyRelated to each other.  Conversely, two spatial things that are embedded in different spaces (e.g. an object in the physical universe and a geometric figure in an abstract space defined in the context of a geometry problem) are not spatially related.")
(comment ingredients "The predicate #$ingredients is used to indicate a particular input used to make a particular artifact.  (#$ingredients ART INGR) means that the #$PartiallyTangible thing INGR is one of the #$inputs in the creation of the #$Artifact ART, and INGR (or matter derived from it) is one of ART's #$physicalParts.
<p>
This is a general predicate that usually can be stated in a more specific manner.  For example: #$ingredients-Separable applies only to those inputs which retain their identity in the creation process and which are incorporated into the resulting #$Artifact; #$ingredients-Constituent applies to ingredients that permiate the resulting #$Artifact; and #$ingredients-Destroyed refer to ingredients that have been transformed in the creation process.")
(comment thereExistAtLeast "An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) and a specialized (albeit higher arity) version of #$thereExists (q.v.).  Compared to the latter, standard existential quantifier, #$thereExistAtLeast has an extra argument-place for specifying how many things (at the least) satisfy a given condition (specified by an #$ELSentence-Assertible, in which the also-specified #$ELVariable will typically occur free).  A closed sentence of the form (#$thereExistAtLeast NUM VAR SENT) means that there are at least NUM distinct things that satisfy SENT, i.e. that render SENT true when taken as the value of VAR.  For example, the sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$thereExistAtLeast 7 ?X (#$isa ?X #$Sea))
</pre>
<p>
means that there are at least seven seas.
<p>
In the degenerate case where VAR does not appear free in SENT, then (#$thereExistAtLeast NUM VAR SENT) is equivalent to SENT.
<p>
See also #$thereExistAtMost, #$thereExistExactly.")
(comment EquivalenceRelation "A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature that is the \"intersection\"  (see #$collectionIntersection) of #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate, #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate, and #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate (qq.v.).   A binary predicate is an instance of #$EquivalenceRelation if and only  if it is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.
<p>
An equivalence relation <code>EQUIV</code> picks out one or more (pairwise disjoint and mutually  exhaustive) \"equivalence classes\" within its domain <code>DOM</code> (i.e. the class of all  things that stand in the <code>EQUIV</code> relation), where each member of a given  equivalence class is related by <code>EQUIV</code> to every other member of that class, and is  related by <code>EQUIV</code> to nothing else.
<p>
A simple example of an equivalence relation  is \"the-same-color-as\", applied to the squares on a particular chess board.  This relation partitions (see #$partitionedInto) the squares into two equivalence  classes: the class of white squares and the class of black squares.  Other examples of equivalence relations include #$equals, #$temporalBoundsIdentical and #$levelWith.")
(comment threatConsequent "The form of a #$Threat is: 'If you (the #$threatenedAgent) do <i>X</i>, I (the #$threateningAgent) will do <i>Y</i>'.  The consequent of this conditional (i.e. 'the threatening agent does <i>Y</i>') is the #$threatConsequent in an instance of #$MakingAThreat.  Thus <code>(#$threatConsequent THREATENING SENT)</code> means that the #$ELSentence-Assertible <code>SENT</code> is the consequent of the #$Threat made in the #$MakingAThreat <code>THREATENING</code>.  See also #$threatAntecedent.")
(comment SubcollectionDenotingFunction "A specialization of #$CollectionRelatingFunction (q.v.).  This is the type of function that always returns a subcollection of one of its arguments.  A function FUNC is an instance of #$SubcollectionDenotingFunction if and only if it has an argument-place (say the Nth) such that (#$resultGenlArg FUNC N) holds.
<p>
For example, #$PartTypeFn always returns a subcollection of its second argument.  Thus (#$PartTypeFn #$Cat #$Paw), the collection of cat paws, is a subcollection of #$Paw.
<p>
An important specialization of #$SubcollectionDenotingFunction is #$GenlsPreservingSubcollectionDenotingFunction (qq.v.).")
(comment surroundsCompletely "An instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$surroundsCompletely OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> means that <code>OUTSIDE</code> completely surrounds <code>INSIDE</code>.  To be more precise, all rays with origins at <code>INSIDE</code> pass through <code>OUTSIDE</code> by default (with some exceptions; for example, a ray with an origin at <code>INSIDE</code> might pass through a small hole in <code>OUTSIDE)</code>. Note that <code>(#$surroundsCompletely OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> implies that <code>OUTSIDE</code> and <code>INSIDE</code> are spatially disjoint (see the predicate #$spatiallyDisjoint).  For example, #$surroundsCompletely would hold between a a candy bar wrapper and a candy bar, or between a body of water and a fish in that body of water.")
(comment physicallyContains "(#$physicallyContains CONT OBJ) means that CONT physically contains (i.e., includes or at least partially surrounds) the object OBJ.  #$physicallyContains is agnostic as to whether or not OBJ is a part of CONT and as to whether OBJ is fully inside of CONT.  (#$physicallyContains CONT OBJ) it is true that whenever CONT moves, OBJ moves. (A container-predicate whose GAFs do not imply co-movement is #$spatiallyContains (q.v.).)  Note that a more specific predicate than #$physicallyContains is almost always applicable -- e.g., #$physicalDecompositions (q.v.) or a more restrictive predicate should be used if OBJ is definitely part of CONT, and #$surrounds-3D (q.v.) or a more restrictive predicate should be used when partonomy is to be ruled out.
<p>
For example, although #$physicallyContains expresses the relationship between an instance of #$Brain and the head that it is in, #$internalParts (q.v.) is more precise.  #$in-ContClosed (q.v.) is more appropriate to specify the relationship of a #$Brain in a #$Skull.  The body of a pregnant woman #$physicallyContains a #$Placenta.  Since a portion of the placenta is a part of the woman's body, while other portions of it are part of the #$Fetus's body, no more restrictive predicate applies.")
(comment transportees "The predicate #$transportees relates a translational motion event to the object(s) transported in that event. <code>(#$transportees MOVE OBJ)</code> means that some conveyance (see the predicate #$conveyor-Generic) transports <code>OBJ</code> in <code>MOVE</code>.  The conveyance in question can either move along with <code>OBJ</code> in <code>MOVE</code> or remain stationary during <code>MOVE</code>.  The conveyance in question is separate from <code>OBJ</code> (that is, <code>OBJ</code> is not a part of the conveyance).")
(comment requiresOperatingSystem "(#$requiresOperatingSystem PROGRAM OS) means
  that copies of the program PROGRAM will only run on the operating system OS.")
(comment subWorks " <code>(#$subWorks WORK SUBWORK)</code> means that <code>SUBWORK</code> is a conceptual work that is somehow included in the \"larger\" conceptual work, <code>WORK</code>.  Examples include a movement as a #$subWorks of a symphony, a #$PeriodicalIssue as a #$subWorks of a #$PeriodicalSeries, a #$Chapter-PCW as a #$subWorks of a #$Novel-CW (see the more specialized predicate #$chapters), or an #$OperatingSystemKernel as a #$subWorks of an #$OperatingSystem (see the more specialized predicate #$containsPrograms).")
(comment birthPlace "<code>(#$birthPlace ANIMAL PLACE)</code> means that <code>ANIMAL</code> was born in the location <code>PLACE</code>.  <code>PLACE</code> may be any instance of #$Place, such as a building or a geographical location.")
(comment transferredObject "The predicate #$transferredObject is used to identify the object whose external relationships change in some event considered generically as a \"transfer\".  <code>(#$transferredObject TRANSFER OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is being moved, transferred, or exchanged in the #$GeneralizedTransfer event <code>TRANSFER</code>.  Note that in general, and if possible, it is preferable to use a more specialized instance of #$ActorSlot than #$transferredObject (e.g., #$objectMoving for physical transfer of an object, or #$objectOfPossessionTransfer for an object that changes possession but not necessarily location) in representing ground facts about transfers.")
(comment (LeftObjectOfPairFn (PartTypeFn SecondThoracicVertebra VertebralTransverseProcess)) "<p>")
(comment fanOutArg "<code>(#$fanOutArg PRED N)</code> means that transitively-related assertions using <code>PRED</code> usually \"fan out\" in the direction of argument position <code>N</code>.  For example, (#$fanOutArg #$geographicalSubRegions 2) means that usually a geographical region has more direct sub-regions than super-regions, so for any region <code>REG</code> there will likely be more assertions of the form <code>(#$geographicalSubRegions REG SUB)</code> than there are assertions of the form <code>(#$geographicalSubRegions SUPER REG)</code>.")
(comment and "A #$LogicalConnective that represents conjunction in #$CycL. It is a #$VariableArityRelation, taking an arbitrary number of #$ELSentence-Assertibles as arguments.  <code>(#$and P Q ... Z)</code> is true if and only if all of the sentences <code>P</code>, <code>Q</code>, ..., and <code>Z</code> are true.")
(comment endingDate "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that is used to  specify the date (see #$Date) on which a given temporal thing ends or ceases to exist.  <code>(#$endingDate TEMP-THING DATE)</code> means that <code>TEMP-THING</code> ends (if <code>TEMP-THING</code> is an event or other  #$Situation) or ceases to exist (if <code>TEMP-THING</code> is a physical object or other #$SomethingExisting) sometime during <code>DATE</code>.  That is, <code>(#$temporallySubsumes DATE (#$EndFn TEMP-THING))</code> holds.  Note that <code>DATE</code>, though defined in terms of a calendar, need not be a particular day or year (e.g. it might be a particular minute or month or decade).  See also #$endingPoint and  #$startingDate.")
(comment different "A variable-arity predicate (see #$VariableArityRelation) that is used to state the non-identity of two or more things. <code>(#$different THING1..THINGn)</code> means that for any <code>THING</code>i and <code>THING</code>j (where 0 <= i <= n, 0 <= j <= n, and i =/ j), <code>THING</code>i is not identical with <code>THING</code>j.  That is, each of <code>THING</code>1, ..., <code>THING</code>n is distinct from all of the others.  Cf. #$equals.")
(comment resultIsaArg "A #$MetaRelation predicate.  <code>(#$resultIsaArg FUNC N)</code>  means that the value returned by <code>FUNC</code> for any given sequence of arguments  is an instance of the <code>N</code>th argument in that sequence.  That is, if  <code>(FUNC ARG1 ... ARGN ...) = VALUE</code>, then <code>(#$isa VALUE ARGN)</code> holds.  Obviously, <code>ARGN</code> must be a collection; so <code>FUNC</code>'s <code>N</code>th argument-place is likely to be constrained (via #$argIsa) to be an instance of #$Collection or some specialization thereof.
<p>
For example, (#$resultIsaArg #$The 1) entails that (#$The #$Dog) is an instance of #$Dog.
<p>
See also #$resultGenlArg and #$resultIsa.")
(comment Weapon "A subcollection of #$Artifact, instances of which are primarily designed as instruments for disabling, physically harming, or destroying animals (often humans), buildings, or other #$ConstructionArtifacts.  Subcollections of #$Weapon include #$LethalWeapon, #$NonlethalWeapon, #$Gun-Portable, #$Bomb, and #$Napalm.  Also included are various types of military hardware used to kill, destroy, or immobilize.  Note that in the case of weapons which are 'fired' or otherwise distributed, both the launcher and the unit of ammunition are considered #$Weapons in this sense.  E.g. a bow, an arrow, a missile launcher, an ICBM, a weapons-grade sprayer, a cloud of mustard gas, a gun, and a bullet are all #$Weapons.
<p>
Non-artifacts and artifacts designed for some other purpose (as a found rock or brick) may be \"used as\" weapons, but are not instances of the collection, #$Weapon.")
(comment RegularExpression "A collection of character strings used in text-processing.  #$RegularExpression is the collection of all 'regular expressions' or 'regex'es: character strings that are templates used by matching programs to indicate a match or partial match between the 'regular expression template' and a target string of characters.   See (#$regexStringMatch REGEX STRING), which  means that the 'regular expression template' (or 'regex') REGEX matches some substring within STRING, and see also #$regexFileMatch.  A regular expression allows for 'wildcard characters' and other partial-match features in addition to exact matching.  The rules for what characters to use in the regex, and what constitutes a valid match, depend on the particular regular expression matcher being used.  (Regular expressions are used for matching in many programming languages.)  Some regex features are common to all such programs, including these: . [...] [^...] char ? * + ^ $ \\< \\> | (...) \\1,\\2,\\3,...  Some other features vary.  This collection assumes that the PERL specification is used to define what a regular expression is.
<p>
Briefly: A period in the regex matches 'any character' in the target string.  Square brackets match a class of characters, those listed, possibly with ranges like A-Z or a-z or 1-8.  Square brackets with a caret-character as the first included character match the class of characters other than those listed between the brackets.   Backslash-character means the folowing special character is directly matched and is not treated as a special character.  Caret matches the position of the start-of-line.  Dollar-sign matches the position at the end-of-line.  \\< matches any start of word.  \\> matches any end-of-word.  Parentheses group whatever they enclose.  A character or group followed by a question-mark is optional; it matches zero or one occurence of that group or charater.  A character or group followed by an asterisk matches any number of repetitions that group or character, including zero.  A character or group followed by a plus-sign matches one or more repetitions of that group or character.  A vertical bar | separates alternative matches -- either may succeed.  \\1,\\2,\\3, etc. are backreference variables to which each matched group is assigned in order, and they will match later occurrences of the assigned group.  For further details, see documentation for regular expressions or for PERL.")
(comment Determiner-Numeric "The collection of all #$Postdeterminers that represent numeric values.  These are generally cardinal numbers (e.g. my <i>three</i> children).  Thus, all #$Number-SPs are also #$Determiner-Numerics.")
(comment movieDirector "A binary predicate that relates a given #$Movie-CW to the agent(s) who directed it.  <code>(#$movieDirector MOVIE DIRECTOR)</code> means that <code>DIRECTOR</code> directed <code>MOVIE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$movieDirector #$CitizenKane-TheMovie #$OrsonWelles)</code> holds.")
(comment obligatedAgents "A #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate and specialization of #$normativelyBoundAgents that relates #$Obligations (q.v.) to the agents who are subject to them. <code>(#$obligatedAgents OBLIGATION AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is subject to the terms of <code>OBLIGATION</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$obligatedAgents #$ChemicalWeaponsConvention #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)</code> means that the US is subject to the terms of the #$ChemicalWeaponsConvention, and <code>(#$obligatedAgents #$TheFirstCommandment Joe)</code> means that Joe has an obligation to follow the first commandment.")
(comment TangibleStuffCompositionType "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$ExistingStuffType.  Instances are subcollections of #$PartiallyTangible whose membership is based only on the physical and/or chemical composition of their instances. #$TangibleStuffCompositionType does not have as instances collections whose instances are determined _solely_ by the physical state they are in -- for that, see #$TangibleStuffStateType.  However, many substances that chemically break down before melting (e.g. #$FattyTissue) _are_ included in this collection, even though they exist solely in one physical state.
<p>
The collection #$Water is an instance of #$TangibleStuffCompositionType, as instances of #$Water are all pieces of substance with the chemical composition H20.  On the other hand , the collection of all pieces of ice (i.e. (#$SolidFn #$Water)) is not a #$TangibleStuffCompositionType, because membership in that collection depends on the substance's composition _and_ on its physical state.
#$TalcumPowder is not an instance of this collection because a fused mass of the substance would no longer be powder, but would still be of the same  #$TangibleStuffCompositionType.
<p>
Further instances of #$TangibleStuffCompositionType are #$Nylon, #$GasolineFuel, #$FattyTissue, #$Nitrogen, and #$Glass. An important specialization of this collection is #$ChemicalSubstanceType -- those substance types defined solely by their chemical formulae.")
(comment relationExistsMaxAll "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to two collections.  <code>(#$relationExistsMaxAll BINPRED COL1 COL2 N)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL2</code>, there are at most <code>N</code> instances of <code>(INST(1), ..., INST(N))</code> of <code>COL1</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST(i) INST)</code> holds (for 1 <= <code>i</code> <= <code>N</code>).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa INST COL2)
    (#$thereExistAtMost N ?INST-I
      (#$and
        (#$isa ?INST-I COL1)
        (BINPRED ?INST-I INST)))).
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$relationExistsMaxAll #$physicalParts #$Hand #$Finger 1)</code> means that every finger is part of at most one hand.  Cf. #$relationAllExistsMax, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment objectMoving "The predicate #$objectMoving relates movement events to the object or objects moving in those events.  <code>(#$objectMoving MOVE OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is in motion at some point during the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> and is either the primary object moving (see the predicate #$primaryObjectMoving) in <code>MOVE</code> or the primary object moving in some sub-event (see the predicate #$subEvents) of <code>MOVE</code>.")
(comment temporallyDisjointActionTypes "A specialization of #$disjointWith that holds between certain types of #$Events.  <code>(#$temporallyDisjointActionTypes EVENTTYPE1 EVENTTYPE2)</code> means that it is not possible for something to be doing (see #$doneBy) an event of <code>EVENTTYPE1</code> at the same time that it is doing an event of <code>EVENTTYPE2</code>.
<p>
Note that if <code>EVENTTYPE1</code> and <code>EVENTTYPE2</code> are both specializations of #$PhysicalEvent (as will typically be the case in practice), the phrase \"not possible\" in the above should be understood to mean \"not <i>physically</i> possible\".
<p>
For example, <code>(#$temporallyDisjointActionTypes #$Exhaling #$Inhaling)</code> means that no animal can inhale and exhale at the same time.")
(comment GroupingObjects "A specialization of #$Collecting, #$CreationEvent and #$PurposefulAction. Each instance of this collection is an event in which two or more #$PartiallyTangibles are grouped together (possibly in accordance with some general convention) in such a way as to constitute a #$Group that did not exist before.
<p>
Such an act of grouping (unlike an instance of the superficially similar #$PhysicalCollectionEvent) requires an intention to do so on the part of the agent who performs the act, and often (but not always) also involves some physical act of bringing the objects into proximity with each other.  Examples include putting flowers together to make a bouquet and assembling a sports team.  See also #$GroupingThingsMentally.")
(comment typePromotesRisk "<code>(#$typePromotesRisk SIT-TYPE1 ROLE1 SIT-TYPE2 ROLE2)</code> means that if an object <code>OBJ</code> plays <code>ROLE1</code> in an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE1</code>, then <code>OBJ</code> is at risk for playing the role <code>ROLE2</code> in an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE2</code>. For instance, '(#$typePromotesRisk #$Smoking #$doneBy (#$CancerFn #$Lung) #$bodilyDoer)' means that smoking promotes the risk of getting lung cancer. See also #$typeImmediateRisk and #$promotesRisk.")
(comment resultantStateObtains "A 3-place relation between an event type, an actor slot and an actor slot filler. <code>(#$resultantStateObtains TYPE SLOT FILLER)</code> means that <code>FILLER</code> is in a state resulting from playing the role <code>SLOT</code> in an instance of <code>TYPE</code>. It presupposes, in other words, that an occurrence of <code>TYPE</code> has happened. A relevant use of this predicate is in linking perfect-tense assertions in instances of #$AspectTenseMicrotheory to their associated states, i.e., the resultative states of events of the given type. Note that this is a type-level predicate, unlike #$postSituations, which relates an particular event or situation to a particular individual regardless of role played.")
(comment ObjectIDFn "(#$ObjectIDFn ?OBJ) returns the collection of all instances of #$UniqueID which
  refer to the individual ?OBJ.")
(comment pretendsThat "A #$BinaryRolePredicate that relates an instance of #$Pretending to an #$ELSentence-Assertible.  <code>(#$pretendsThat PRETENDING SENT)</code> means that the performers of <code>PRETENDING</code> are pretending that <code>SENT</code> is true.  <code>(#$pretendsThat PRETENDING SENT)</code> does not entail that <code>SENT</code> is false; however, it does entail that the performers of <code>PRETENDING</code> <i>believe</i> <code>SENT</code> to be false.")
(comment FunctionToArg "A binary #$FunctionDenotingFunction (q.v.) that takes as argument a #$FunctionalPredicate (and an integer indicating an argument-place in which that predicate is functional) and returns the corresponding function (whose value is always equal to the predicate's corresponding argument in the indicated place).
<p>
More precisely: if <code>PRED</code> is an <code>N</code>-ary functional predicate that is functional in its <code>M</code>th argument (see #$functionalInArgs), then <code>(#$FunctionToArg M PRED)</code> is the (<code>N</code>-1)-ary function <code>FUNC-TO-ARGM</code> such that <code>(PRED ARG(1) ... ARG(N))</code> holds if and only if <code>(FUNC-TO-ARGM ARG(1) ... ARG(M-1) ARG(M+1) ... ARG(N)) = ARG(M)</code>.  If <code>PRED</code> is not functional in its <code>M</code>th argument, then <code>(#$FunctionToArg PRED M)</code> is #$undefined.
<p>
For example, (#$FunctionToArg 2 #$age) is the function that returns the age of a given person.  Thus ((#$FunctionToArg 2 #$age) #$GeorgeWBush) = (#$YearsDuration 50) just in case (#$age #$GeorgeWBush (#$YearsDuration 50)) is true.
<p>
See also #$Lambda and #$Kappa.")
(comment BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate "A specialization of both #$TemporalPredicate and #$BinaryPredicate.  Each instance of #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate is a (direct or indirect) specialization of #$temporallyRelated (q.v.) that is used to say something about the relative positions of two #$TemporalThings in time.
<p>
#$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate is partitioned into #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicate and #$ComplexTemporalPredicate.  #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicates (such as #$after) relate #$TimePoints only, and #$ComplexTemporalPredicates (such as  #$postEvents and #$startingDate) also relate more complicated temporal objects such as events, tangible objects, and proper time intervals.
<p>
Note that _not_all_ binary #$TemporalPredicates are #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicates; e.g. those that relate time-quantities (see #$Time-Quantity) are not. Note also that _not_all_ specializations of #$temporallyRelated are #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicates, or even #$TemporalPredicates; e.g. while (#$relatives ORGANISM1 ORGANISM2) implies that ORGANISM1 and ORGANISM2 are somehow temporally related, it says nothing about their relative positions in time.")
(comment skillCapableOf "This predicate can be used to assert that a given animal agent has the ability to play a certain role in a certain type of #$SkilledActivityType (q.v.).  <code>(#$skillCapableOf ANIMAL ACTTYPE ROLE)</code> means that <code>ANIMAL</code> has the necessary skills to act in ROLE in normal instances of <code>ACTTYPE</code>.  For example, that #$Gumby can swim is expressed by (#$skillCapableOf #$Gumby #$Swimming-Generic #$performedBy).  #$skillCapableOf entails that <code>ANIMAL</code> satisfies any anatomical or intellectual prerequisites for possessing or exercising the skill; but it is neutral as to whether <code>ANIMAL</code> has the necessary resources of time, money, opportunity, etc., to use the skill in any particular instance of <code>ACTTYPE</code>.")
(comment ExpandSubLFn "<code>(#$ExpandSubLFn PARAMETERS SUBL-TEMPLATE)</code> denotes the SubL expression resulting from expanding the SubL template <code>SUBL-TEMPLATE</code> once all <code>PARAMETERS</code> are known.  For example, <code>(#$ExpandSubLFn (?X) (+ 1 ?X))</code> will denote the SubL expression <code>(+ 1 2)</code> when the parameter <code>?X</code> is bound to 2.  See also #$EvaluateSubLFn, #$trueSubL and #$performSubL.")
(comment scalarBounds "A #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate and a specialization of #$quantitySubsumes (q.v.) that relates a given #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) to either of the #$ScalarPointValues that are its boundaries or limits.  <code>(#$scalarBounds SCALAR BOUND)</code> means that <code>BOUND</code> is either the upper bound (see #$maxQuantValue) or the lower bound (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>SCALAR</code>.
<p>
For example, both of the following hold:
<pre>
  (#$scalarBounds (#$Mile 5 10) (#$Mile 5))

  (#$scalarBounds (#$Mile 5 10) (#$Mile 10)).
</pre>")
(comment RegistryKeyTemplateFn "A variable-arity, reifiable function which  allows rule-writing using only partially instantiated MS Windows Registry Key (cf. #$RegistryKey) pathnames (cf. #$Pathname), into which variable strings may be substituted (generating full pathnames during inference).
<p>
For instance, the result of using the function as follows: (#$RegistryKeyTemplateFn \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Internet Settings~s \"101\") is the pathname: \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Internet Settings\\101\".")
(comment BusinessEvent "A specialization of #$SocialOccurrence and #$PurposefulAction (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$BusinessEvent is a purposeful activity that is closely associated with, and either helps or hinders, the business purpose of some agent.  Examples include the development of General Motors' business plan for 2003, an individual sales call on a particular customer, printing the 2003 Yellow Pages directory for the Los Angeles area, selling a newspaper, and designing and manufacturing a new memory chip.
<p>
Though there is a great deal of overlap between this collection and #$CommercialActivity, they are not the same. Firstly, #$BusinessEvent does not include any purely social or recreational events (whereas a weekend swap-meet, for example, would be an instance of #$CommercialActivity). Secondly, not all activities carried out in pursuit of business interests involve buying or selling (consider head-hunting, for example).")
(comment sellingPerformer "<code>(#$sellingPerformer COM AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> actively pursues making a sale in <code>COM</code>.  Note that <code>AGENT</code> may be either the seller of the goods in <code>COM</code> (see the predicate #$seller), or an agent representing the seller of the goods (see the predicate #$sellingAgent).")
(comment PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType is both (i) a specialization of #$Quantity whose instances are totally ordered by some relation (see #$TotallyOrderedCollection) and (ii) a type of quantity that is not derived (via e.g. #$ProductFn or #$QuotientFn) from other quantities.  Instances of #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType include #$Time-Quantity, #$Mass, and #$Happiness.
<p>
See also the specialization #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType, whose instances are  specializations of #$OneDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).")
(comment TotallyOrderedCollectionType "A #$CollectionTypeType and the collection-intersection of #$TotallyOrderedCollection and #$CollectionType (qq.v.).  Thus, something is an instance of #$TotallyOrderedCollectionType if and only if it is a totally-ordered collection of collections.
<p>
An example of a totally-ordered collection-type is #$DegreeOfCloudiness.")
(comment (LeftObjectOfPairFn (PartTypeFn ThirdThoracicVertebra VertebralTransverseProcess)) "<p>")
(comment relationAllAll "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relation to two collections. <code>(#$relationAllAll BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code> and any instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code>, <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds. This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
     (#$implies
          (#$and
               (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)
               (#$isa ?INST2 COL2))
          (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)) .
</pre>
<p>
For example '(#$relationAllAll #$loves #$Person #$DomesticCat)' means that all people love all cats.  See also the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment populationDensity "A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot and specialization of #$hasNonAspectualQuantity (q.v.) that relates a given #$GeographicalRegion to a #$DensityOfPopulation (q.v.).  <code>(#$populationDensity REGION DENSITY)</code> means that <code>REGION</code> has a population-density <code>DENSITY</code>.
<p>
For example,
<pre>
  (#$populationDensity
    Cucamonga
    (#$LowAmountFn #$DensityOfPopulation))</code>
</pre>
<p>
means that the population density of Cucamonga is low.
<p>
See also #$populationOfRegion, #$populationGrowthRate, and #$populationRange.")
(comment UnitOfMeasure "A specialization of #$ScalarDenotingFunction (q.v.).  Each instance of #$UnitOfMeasure is a function that takes one or two numbers or other #$NumericIntervals as arguments, and returns as value a #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.), such as a #$Distance or a #$Speed or a #$Volume.  If a unit of measure is applied to one number (see #$Number-General) the result is a precise quantity that is a #$ScalarPointValue; if applied to two (different) numbers -- or to one (or two) #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine(s) -- the result is a closed-interval quantity that is a #$ScalarProperInterval.  For example, (#$Meter 5) is the distance five meters and (#$Meter 5 10) is the distance \"five to ten meters (inclusive)\".
<p>
(A partial exception to the above is the unit-of-measure #$Unity (q.v.), which always returns a #$NumericInterval rather than a #$MeasurableQuantity.)
<p>
Specializations of #$UnitOfMeasure grouped by what they measure include #$UnitOfTime, #$UnitOfSpeed, and #$UnitOfVolume.  Other specializations are #$OneDimensionalUnitOfMeasure, #$MultiDimensionalUnitOfMeasure, #$UnitOfMeasureWithPrefix and #$UnitOfMeasureNoPrefix.")
(comment inverseFunctions "A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates unary one-to-one  functions.  <code>(#$inverseFunctions FUNC1 FUNC2)</code> means that <code>FUNC1</code> and <code>FUNC2</code> are the inverses of each other.  Thus, for  any things <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code>, <code>FUNC1</code> maps <code>X</code> to <code>Y</code> if and only if <code>FUNC2</code> maps <code>Y</code> to <code>X</code>. Note that the foregoing condition is necessary  but <i>not</i> sufficient for one function's being the inverse of  another.  This is because #$Function-Denotationals are  understood as being \"functions-in-intension\", making it  theoretically possible for numerically distinct (but  extensionally equivalent) functions to satisfy the above  condition with respect to a given function.  For example,  in a context restricted to monogamous heterosexual married  people, the inverse of a \"HusbandFn\" would presumably be  the \"WifeFn\" and <i>not</i> the \"BestFriendOfThisManFn\",  even if the latter two functions happened to be coextensional  in that context.  See also the corresponding function  #$InverseFunctionFn.  To relate a mathematical function  (i.e. a #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars) to its inverse,  see #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric.")
(comment SandwichFn "<code>(#$SandwichFn SET)</code> denotes the collection of #$Sandwiches that have the food-types in <code>SET</code> as their main ingredients.")
(comment SlightlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$SlightlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>slight</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$SlightlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are slightly hilly: those that are slight in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment HighAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.   In a given context, <code>(#$HighAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>high amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness) is a high amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$highAmountOf.")
(comment MacroRelation "A specialization of #$Relation whose instances are used to form shorthand expressions for more complex formulas.  Each instance of #$MacroRelation is correlated -- via an #$expansion assertion -- with a certain template for a class of (possibly quantified) CycL formulas.  A closed formula (i.e. a closed \"GAF\" or \"NAT\"; see #$CycLClosedFormula) built from a given macro-relation is treated by the Cyc system as semantically equivalent to the (usually more complex) corresponding formula fitting the template.  Thus suppose the macro-relation MACROREL had an expansion assertion of the form:
<p>
(#$expansion MACROREL
COMPLEX-RELATIONAL-STRUCTURE :ARG1 ... :ARGN) .
<p>
Then a closed formula of the form
<p>
(MACROREL TERM(1) ... TERM(N))
<p>
would be treated by the system as shorthand for the more complex formula
<p>
(COMPLEX-RELATIONAL-STRUCTURE TERM(1) ... TERM(N)) .
<p>
Specializations of #$MacroRelation include #$RuleMacroPredicate, #$ELRelation, and #$TermMacroFunction (qq.v.).  Instances of #$MacroRelation that are not in any of the latter three collections include #$implies, #$argFormat, and #$quotedIsa.")
(comment simultaneousWith "A #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicate (q.v.) that is a specialization of both #$cotemporal (q.v.) and #$equals. The predicate #$simultaneousWith is just the cotemporal relation restricted to #$TimePoints (q.v.), with respect to which (as with #$TimeIntervals generally) cotemporality is both a necessary and a sufficient condition for identity.  (#$simultaneousWith T1 T2) means that T1 and T2 both extend over (and thus _are_) the same single point in time.
<p>
Note that individual #$TimePoints are seldom mentioned in axioms in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Most time-related axioms use #$ComplexTemporalPredicates (cf.), such as #$cotemporal, which can hold between #$TemporalThings of any sort.")
(comment BiologicalProductionEvent "A specialization of both #$PhysicalCreationEvent and #$BiologicalEvent.  In each instance of #$BiologicalProductionEvent, raw materials are combined to produce a product, and at least one instance of #$BiologicalLivingObject (q.v.) is the producer.  Specializations of #$BiologicalProductionEvent include #$SecretionEvent and #$BiologicalReproductionEvent.
<p>
Note that the distinction between this collection and #$BiologicalDevelopmentEvent is not always sharp, but instances of #$BiologicalProductionEvent are distinguished insofar as the event in question produces a reasonably distinct entity from start to finish. Thus, #$FlowerDevelopmentEvent is an instance of #$BiologicalDevelopmentEvent although some case could be made for it being an instance of #$BiologicalProductionEvent.")
(comment interArgResultIsa "A quaternary #$MetaRelation predicate for stating that a certain kind of complex, partly #$isa -based relationship holds between a given argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned.
<p>
<code>(#$interArgResultIsa FUNC N INDEP-ARG-COL DEP-ARG-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEP-ARG-COL</code> appears as the <code>N</code>th argument to <code>FUNC</code>, then (regardless of what the other arguments to <code>FUNC</code> might be) the value (if any) of that function for those arguments is an instance of <code>DEP-ARG-COL</code>.  That is: if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEP-ARG-COL</code> and <code>(FUNC ARG1 ... ARGN-1 INST ...) = VALUE</code>, then <code>VALUE</code> is an instance of <code>DEP-ARG-COL</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$interArgResultIsa #$RoundUpFn 1 #$RealNumber #$Integer)</code> means that the rounding-up function #$RoundUpFn returns an integer whenever it is given a real number as its first (and only) argument.
<p>
Note that, unlike (e.g.) the #$InterArgTypePredicates, #$interArgResultIsa <i>cannot</i> be used to put semantic well-formedness constraints on functions.  See also the similar but less flexible ternary #$interArg1ResultIsa et al.")
(comment subsetOf "This predicate relates a set or collection <code>SUB</code> to a set or collection <code>SUPER</code> whenever the extent (see #$extent) of <code>SUB</code> is a  subset of the extent of <code>SUPER</code>.  That is, <code>(#$subsetOf SUB SUPER)</code> means that  every element of (see #$elementOf) <code>SUB</code> is an element of <code>SUPER</code>.  #$subsetOf is thus a generalization both of the subset relation in set theory and of #$genls (q.v.); and (unlike either of those other relations) #$subsetOf can hold between a set and a collection, or between a collection and a set.")
(comment BiologicalSubspecies "A specialization of #$BiologicalTaxon whose instances are more restrictive
than those of #$BiologicalSpecies.  Each instance of #$BiologicalSubspecies
is a specialization of some instance of #$BiologicalSpecies.  Members of
different subspecies of the same species can produce fertile offspring by
interbreeding; but such offspring are not members of either of the parental
subspecies, although they are members of the common species.  All instances
of a given biological subspecies have significant traits or collections of
traits in common that are not shared by all other members of the subsuming
species.
<p>
A #$BiologicalSubspecies is formed by inbreeding of a restricted group of
members of the same species.  This can happen naturally through geographic
isolation or intentionally through controlled reproduction to create, for
example, dog breeds or crop strains.")
(comment GenericValueFunction "A specialization of #$ScalarDenotingFunction whose instances map #$TotallyOrderedQuantityTypes to certain #$GenericQuantity[ies] (qq.v.) of those types.  Each #$GenericValueFunction is a unary function which, when applied to a totally-ordered quantity-type <code>QUANTTYPE</code>, returns some \"generic amount\" or roughly-specified range (e.g. <i>high, medium, low</i>) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, #$HighAmountFn is a generic-value function, and (#$HighAmountFn #$Elasticity) is a the generic-quantity \"high amount of elasticity\" (with respect to some contextually-determined, conventional elasticity scale).
<p>
Other instances of #$GenericValueFunction are #$LowAmountFn, #$MediumAmountFn, and #$MediumToVeryHighAmountFn.
<p>
See also #$RelativeGenericValueFn and #$NoteAboutGivingGenericValueFunctionsNumericValues.  And cf. #$GenericValueTypeFunction.")
(comment endingPoint "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.) and specialization of both #$temporallyCoterminal and #$temporallySubsumes that relates a #$TemporalThing to the #$TimePoint at which it ceases to exist (if it is a #$SomethingExisting), happen (if an #$Event), or obtain (if a #$StaticSituation). <code>(#$endingPoint TEMP T)</code> means that <code>THING</code> ends at <code>T</code>, the latest moment of its #$temporalExtent (q.v.).
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$EndFn; and see #$startingPoint.")
(comment conveyor-Generic "<code>(#$conveyor-Generic MOVE OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> enables or facilitates the conveyance of what is transported (the animals, goods, people, etc. that are the #$transportees) in the #$Conveying-Generic <code>MOVE</code>.  In the case where <code>OBJ</code> itself is an #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code>, use the more specific predicate #$transporter.  If <code>OBJ</code> is known not to be an #$objectMoving, use the more specific predicate #$conveyor-Stationary.")
(comment TupleOfIntervals "A specialization of #$Tuple (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TupleOfIntervals is a tuple whose members (see #$memberOfTuple) are all #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.).
<p>
Notable specializations of #$TupleOfIntervals include #$ScalarInterval itself (whose instances are one-tuples), #$VectorInterval (whose instances are two-tuples of scalars), and #$BloodPressureReading.")
(comment rewriteOf "A specialization of #$equals and a #$CycLReformulatorConstant that relates pairs of #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerms.   #$rewriteOf is, in a sense, a \"directional\" version of #$equals.  <code>(#$rewriteOf FAVORED-TERM DISFAVORED-TERM)</code> means that (1) <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> and <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> denote the same thing, and (2) <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> is deemed worthy of being privileged over <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> in the following senses: assertions made on <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> will be propagated to <code>FAVORED-TERM</code>, and whenever possible attempts to reason about  <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> will be transformed into attempts to reason about <code>FAVORED-TERM</code>.  The propagated assertions will differ from the original assertions only in that the propagated assertions will be the result of substituting <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> for <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> wherever <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> occurs in the original assertions.  Note that substitution does not happen in the opposite direction.  That is, assertions containing <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> will not propagate to <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code>.
<p>
One common use of #$rewriteOf is that in which <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> is a syntactically simpler term than <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code>.  For example, <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> may contain fewer nested terms that denote #$Function-Denotationals than does <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code>.  (#$rewriteOf #$UnitedStatesNavy (#$NavyFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)) is a good example of this common sort of use.
<p>
Note that #$rewriteOf expresses a strictly conventional notion of \"directional\" equality. Pragmatic considerations, not ontology, will dictate which term is chosen to occur as the privileged (left-hand side) term in a #$rewriteOf assertion.  Thus, in the example the fact that simpler terms are generally easier to use (remember, manipulate, etc.) dictates that #$UnitedStatesNavy should be a rewrite of (#$NavyFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica), and not vice versa.")
(comment MediumAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.   In a given context, <code>(#$MediumAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>medium amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness) is a medium amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$LowAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$mediumAmountOf.")
(comment cultureBeliefs "This predicate relates cultures to the beliefs   that are characteristic of and important to their participants.  <code>(#$cultureBeliefs CULTURE PROP)</code> means that participants in    <code>CULTURE</code> (but not participants in all other cultures) tend    to believe that <code>PROP</code> is true and would (at least upon   reflection) consider a belief in <code>PROP</code> to be important   to or distinctive of <code>CULTURE</code>.  Arguably, every human culture   has an associated set of beliefs that--taken as a whole--   serves to distinguish it, more or less, from all other    cultures.  This set might include religious beliefs,   socio-political beliefs, beliefs about the causes of    certain things, views on morality, views on how best to   accomplish certain tasks, etc.  (In using this predicate    it is important to avoid unjustified stereotypes of the    culture in question and to refrain from attributing to the    culture beliefs that do not accurately characterize it.)")
(comment EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn "A #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$FirstOrderCollection and returns a #$SecondOrderCollection.  More specifically, #$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn takes a specialization of #$Event and returns a collection of specializations of #$PhysicalDevice.  <code>(#$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn EVENT-TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of all device types <code>DEVICE-TYPE</code> such that in each instance of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code> some instance of <code>DEVICE-TYPE</code> is used.  For example, #$Microphone is an instance of (#$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn #$KaraokeEvent), but #$Bug-InformationGatheringDevice is not.")
(comment DerivedMeasurableQuantityType "A collection of collections and a specialization of both #$MeasurableScalarIntervalType and #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType is a totally-ordered collection of instances of #$MultiDimensionalQuantity: measurable quantities that are derived from some more basic quantities (via e.g. #$ProductFn or #$QuotientFn).   Examples include #$Volume, #$Area, and #$Acceleration.
<p>
Cf. #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType.")
(comment SingleDoerAction "A specialization of #$Action.  Each instance of #$SingleDoerAction is an action which can have only one \"doer\" (see the predicate #$doneBy).  Such actions may be intentional, but they need not be.  Most bodily functions (e.g., the instances of #$Heartbeating or #$Bleeding-TheAction) belong to specializations of #$SingleDoerAction, because the only doer of a normal bodily function is the body of a single agent.  In order for a type of action (i.e., a specialization of #$Action) to be a specialization of #$SingleDoerAction, it must be inconsistent to assert, for any instance <code>ACT</code> of that type of action, both <code>(#$doneBy ACT X)</code> and <code>(#$doneBy ACT Y)</code>, where <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> are different.  Notable specializations of #$SingleDoerAction include #$Sleeping, #$Dying, #$AnimalWalkingProcess, and #$WritingByHand.")
(comment pointQuantValue "A #$NumericComparisonPredicate and a specialization of #$numericallyEquals, #$minQuantValue, and #$maxQuantValue (qq.v.).  #$pointQuantValue is essentially the relation #$numericallyEquals restricted to scalar point-values, with the additional proviso that its first argument must be given using a reifiable CycL term.  Thus a sentence of the form
<p>
    <code>(#$pointQuantValue REIFIABLE-TERM OTHER-TERM)</code>
<p>
is true if and only if <code>REIFIABLE-TERM</code> is a #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm (q.v.), and <code>REIFIABLE-TERM</code> and <code>OTHER-TERM</code> denote the same #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.).
<p>
This predicate is primarily used to state the equivalence of a reified scalar-denoting term (e.g. '#$Zero', '#$AvogadrosNumber', '#$SpeedOfLight') with an appropriate unreifiable term.  For example, both of the following sentences hold:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$pointQuantValue #$Zero 0)
</pre>
<p>
<pre>
  (#$pointQuantValue
    #$SpeedOfLight
    (#$PerFn (#$Mile 186000) (#$HoursDuration 1))'  .
</pre>")
(comment IncarnationPhysicalFn "A function which returns the set of individuals that collectively comprise the physical incarnations of the stated divine being. Thus, in the #$ChristianityMt, #$JesusOfNazareth is the physical incarnation of both #$JesusChrist (the divine being representing the son of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob) and the physical incarnation of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob itself. Likewise, the resurrected Christ who appeared to the apostles is also a physical incarnation of #$JesusChrist. This introduces issues of the holy Trinity that should be investigated further in Cyc.
<p>
Religions which believe in reincarnation may also be axiomatized with this function, if one considers sentient animals as physical incarnations of a non-physical 'soul', where soul denotes a #$DivineBeing. Since some religions believe that people can be reincarnated as \"lower\" animals, the result of #$IncarnationPhysicalFn must be specified (hence it is a binary function).  Thus, we can tell Cyc that B is a reincarnation of A if there exists some divine-being (call it a soul) such that A and B are both physical incarnations of this being, and B is born after A dies (see #$Reincarnation)")
(comment startingPoint "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.) and specialization of both #$temporallyCooriginating and #$temporallySubsumes that relates a #$TemporalThing to the #$TimePoint at which it starts to exist (if it is a #$SomethingExisting), happen (if an #$Event), or obtain (if a #$StaticSituation). <code>(#$startingPoint TEMP T)</code> means that <code>THING</code> begins at <code>T</code>, the earliest moment of its #$temporalExtent (q.v.).
<p>
See also the corresponding function #$StartFn; and see #$endingPoint.")
(comment spatiallySubsumes "A #$SpatialPredicate that is a specialization of both #$spatiallyIncludes and #$spatiallyIntersects (qq.v.).  <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes SUPER SUB)</code> means that the spatial extent of <code>SUB</code> lies within the spatial extent of <code>SUPER</code>.  For example, a model ship in a bottle is <i>not</i> spatially subsumed by the bottle, but the lip of the bottle <i>is</i> spatially subsumed by the bottle.  (The bottle #$spatiallyContains the ship.)  Note that <code>SUB</code> might or might not be a part of <code>SUPER</code>.  For example, a shadow cast on a wall is spatially subsumed by the surface of the wall, but is not a part of it.  But note also that <code>SUPER</code> does have a part that is at least spatially coextensive (see #$cospatial) with <code>SUB</code>.  Specializations of #$spatiallySubsumes include #$physicalDecompositions and #$geographicalSubRegions.")
(comment granuleOfSpatialStuff "(#$granuleOfSpatialStuff STUFFTYPE OBJTYPE) means  that the stuff-like collection STUFFTYPE has as its spatial granules (or granularity level) instances of the object-like collection OBJTYPE. If some collection is spatially stuff-like, that means that the instances of that collection can be divided spatially, and the resulting physical portions will themselves be instances of that collection; e.g., a physical portion of some instance of #$SandMob is still sand (cf. #$ExistingStuffType). Such divisibility cannot go on indefinitely, however: eventually, division of something spatially stuff-like will result in the object-like 'granules' out of which the stuff-like thing is composed. For instances, division of sand would eventually result in individual grains of sand, division of water would eventually get down to individual molecules of water, etc.
<p>
_Below_ this level of division, the remaining physical portions do _not_ count as instances of the stuff-type from which they were divided. _At_ this level of division, things may go one way or another: a single molecule of water is #$Water (at least if we stick by the necessary identity of water and H2O), whereas a single grain of sand is not plausibly sand.")
(comment supervisedAction "An instance of #$BinaryPredicate and a specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$temporallySubsumes.  <code>(#$supervisedAction SUPERVISING ACTION)</code> means that <code>ACTION</code> is the #$PurposefulAction being supervised in the #$Supervising <code>SUPERVISING</code>.")
(comment commonGenlPreds "A #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a given #$PredicateType to any #$Predicate that is a generalization (see #$genlPreds) of all instances of that type.  <code>(#$commonGenlPreds PREDTYPE PRED)</code> means that, for any instance <code>PREDINST</code> of <code>PREDTYPE</code>, <code>(#$genlPreds PREDINST PRED)</code> holds.  Thus the #$commonGenlPreds sentence is equivalent to <code>(#$relationAllInstance #$genlPreds PREDTYPE PRED)</code>.
<p>
Note that #$commonGenlPreds is not to be confused with #$typedGenlPreds (q.v.).")
(comment SexualReproductionEvent "A specialization of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent.  In each instance of #$SexualReproductionEvent, two #$parentActors produce one or more offspring.  Sexual activity which does not result in offspring is not included in this concept.  On the other hand, if offspring begin to develop, but never finish (e.g., it just hasn't finished yet, or the mother dies, or the offspring is aborted, or the offspring doesn't survive to independence, etc.), then that is still considered an instance of #$SexualReproductionEvent but an incomplete one.
<p>
The example of #$SexualReproductionEvent most familiar to most people is when two mammals, one male and one female, mate; sperm from the male combines with one or more eggs inside the female and the resulting offspring develop(s), inside the female parent and dependent upon her for nutrition, until it/they is/are born.  However, #$SexualReproductionEvent is more general than that, and would include these examples: Two birds mate; the female lays one or more eggs; both parents incubate the eggs until they hatch.  Two fish engage in courtship; the female expells her eggs; the male expells his sperm on top of them, and they swim away leaving many fertilized eggs to develop into juvenile fish.  One oak tree's flowers give off airborne pollen; some grains land on the stigma of another tree's flower and fertilize the ovary inside, which then develops into an acorn which is shed by the female parent at the end of summer.")
(comment genls "A instance of #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections (q.v.) and  a specialization of #$generalizations.  #$genls relates a given collection  (see #$Collection) to those collections that subsume it.  More precisely, <code>(#$genls SUBCOL SUPERCOL)</code> means that <code>SUPERCOL</code> is a supercollection of <code>SUBCOL</code>:  anything that is an instance of (see #$isa) <code>SUBCOL</code> is also an instance of <code>SUPERCOL</code>.  For example, <code>(#$genls #$Dog #$Mammal)</code> holds.  #$genls is one of the most commonly-occurring predicates in the Cyc Knowledge Base, and one of  the relations most fundamental to the Cyc ontology.  See also #$subsetOf.")
(comment exceedsCriterion "<code>(#$exceedsCriterion PROP DESCRIP CRIT)</code> means that <code>PROP</code> is provable in the 'real world' description microtheory <code>DESCRIP</code>, but not in the #$Microtheory instance <code>CRIT</code>.  'Not provable' of course means unprovable to some set limit on the inference engine's checking.")
(comment greaterThanOrEqualTo "A #$NumericComparisonPredicate (q.v.) that is a generalization of the mathematical greater-than-or equal-to <code>(>=)</code> relation to #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see #$NumericInterval and #$Quantity) as well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  <code>(#$greaterThanOrEqualTo VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> means that <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than or equal to <code>VALUE2</code> with respect to some scale that they are both on.  More precisely, there is some #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType <code>SCALE</code> that both <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> are instances of and either (i) <code>SCALE</code> is a specialization of #$NumericInterval (e.g. #$RealNumber) and the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than or equal to the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE2</code>, (ii) <code>(#$followingValueOnScale VALUE2 VALUE1 SCALE)</code> holds, or (iii) <code>(#$equals VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> holds.
<p>
See also #$greaterThan and #$lessThanOrEqualTo.")
(comment PlusFn "A variable-arity #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is an extension of the arithmetical operation of addition to measurable #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) generally.  Where  <code>ADDEND1</code>, ..., and <code>ADDENDn</code> are pairwise #$numericallyComparable (q.v.), <code>(#$PlusFn ADDEND1 ... ADDENDn)</code> is the arithmetic sum of <code>ADDEND1</code>, ..., and <code>ADDENDn</code>.  For example, (#$PlusFn 2 3 4) is 9, and (#$PlusFn (#$Meter 1.5) (#$Meter 0.7)) is (#$Meter 2.2).
<p>
Note that the term <code>(#$PlusFn ADDEND1 ... ADDENDn)</code> is #$undefined if there are <code>ADDENDi</code> and <code>ADDENDj</code> (1 <code><= i,j <= n)</code> that are not comparable.  For example, <code>(#$PlusFn (#$MinutesDuration 1) (#$Meter 3))</code> is undefined, since (#$MinutesDuration 1) is a #$Time-Quantity and (#$Meter 3) is a #$Distance, and time-quantities are not comparable with distances.
<p>
For subtraction, see #$DifferenceFn.")
(comment startingDate "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that is used to  specify the date (see #$Date) on which a given temporal thing starts to  happen or begins to exist.  <code>(#$startingDate TEMP-THING DATE)</code> means that <code>TEMP-THING</code> starts (if <code>TEMP-THING</code> is an event or other  #$Situation) or begins to exist (if <code>TEMP-THING</code> is a physical object or other #$SomethingExisting) sometime during <code>DATE</code>.  That is, <code>(#$temporallySubsumes DATE (#$StartFn TEMP-THING))</code> holds.  Note that <code>DATE</code>, though defined in terms of a calendar, need not be a particular day or year (e.g. it might be a particular minute or month or decade).  See also #$startingPoint and  #$endingDate.")
(comment Determiner-Indefinite "The class of indefinite determiners, such as <i>a</i>, <i>an</i>.")
(comment likesAsFriend "An instance of both #$PersonalAssociationPredicate and #$LikesPredicate that relates a given agent to another agent s/he feels friendliness toward.  <code>(#$likesAsFriend AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT1</code> enjoys interacting socially with <code>AGENT2</code>.  See also #$Friendliness.  Note that this  does not preclude <code>AGENT1</code>'s \"liking\", or being fond of, <code>AGENT2</code> in other senses (e.g. a romantic sense) as well.")
(comment compositionOfFunctions "A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates two functions to the function that is their composition.  (#$compositionOfFunctions COMP FUNC1 FUNC2) means that COMP is the composition of FUNC1 and FUNC2.  That is, for any element DOM-EL #$inDomainOf FUNC1, the value of COMP applied to DOM-EL is the same as the result obtained by applying FUNC1 to DOM-EL, and then applying FUNC2 to that result.  Symbolically, (COMP DOM-EL) = (FUNC2 (FUNC1 DOM-EL)).
<p>
For example, a function called \"MaternalGrandfatherFn\" could be construed as the composition of #$MotherFn with #$FatherFn: A is the maternal grandfather of B if and only if A is the father of the mother of B.
<p>
Note that in the general description above, the domain of COMP is identical to the domain of FUNC1, and the range (see #$RangeOfFunctionFn) of COMP is a (proper or otherwise) subset of the range of FUNC2.  (Strictly speaking, the range of COMP is the set of elements of the range of FUNC2 that are the values for some element or other of the range of FUNC1 taken as arguments for FUNC2.)  This means of course that COMP and FUNC1 must have the same #$arity (or both must be #$VariableArityFunctions); also the #$arity of FUNC2 must be 1.
<p>
See also the corresponding #$FunctionCompositionFn.")
(comment endTogetherInSituationType "(#$endTogetherInSituationType <code>SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> end at the same time.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$endTogetherInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment thereExistRange "An instance of #$ExistentialQuantifier-Bounded (q.v.) that can be used to make rough or inexact statements about the number of things that satisfy a given criterion.  <code>(#$thereExistRange RANGE VAR SENT)</code> is true if and only if the number of things in the universe of discourse that satisfy <code>SENT</code> with respect to <code>VAR</code> falls within <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange).  (A given <code>THING</code> \"satisfies <code>SENT</code> with respect to <code>VAR</code>\" just in case any sentence obtained by substituting a CycL name of <code>THING</code> for each free occurrence of <code>VAR</code> within <code>SENT</code> is true.)  For example, the sentence '(#$thereExistRange #$Billions-Quant ?X (#$isa ?X #$Star))' means that there are billions of stars.")
(comment MeaningInSystemFn "A reifiable #$BinaryFunction that returns the meaningful entities indexed by a particular character string in a Cyc-external information source. More precisely, (#$MeaningInSystemFn INFOSOURCE STRING), applied to the #$CharacterString STRING and the #$IndexedInformationSource INFOSOURCE, returns whatever is indexed by STRING in that system.  For example, (#$MeaningInSystemFn #$WordNet-1997Version \"N03585958\") returns the WordNet `synset' (`synonym set') represented by (rampart|bulwark|wall), meaning \"an embankment built around a space for defensive purposes\".
<p>
STRING can have any format chosen by the person who builds the representation of the external system.  In addition, STRING may contain arbitrary further characters or information, depending on how the external information is selected and processed.  This function makes it possible to relate a concept in an external system to an arbitrarily complicated expression composed of Cyc concepts.  If the external concept has a direct, exact mapping to a single Cyc constant, then use the predicate #$synonymousExternalConcept rather than this function.  If there is correspondence only to one Cyc constant, but it is only approximate, use #$overlappingExternalConcept.")
(comment arg5Genl "A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg5Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the fifth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG5 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment maleficiary "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectHarmed.  <code>(#$maleficiary EVENT AGENT)</code> means that the #$Agent-Generic <code>AGENT</code> is harmed (in some sense) by the occurrence of the #$Event <code>EVENT</code>.  In some cases, this is consistent with <code>EVENT</code> being (in some sense) <i>beneficial</i> to <code>AGENT</code>.  For example, being punished might harm one in the short run, but benefit one in the long run.  Thus the fact that #$maleficiary is a required actor slot for #$Punishing does not entail that #$Punishing is always (completely) detrimental to the person punished.")
(comment Anime "The collection for anime, a style of animation. The word `anime' is a shortened form of the Japanese word `animeishon', derived from the English word `animation'.  #$Anime is also referred to as 'Japanimation'.  Anime often contains references to Japanese culture.
<p>
Some common characteristics of characters in anime (but are not limited to or restricted by) abnormally large eyes, heavily stylized, unique, and impossible hair styles, and/or an extraordinary amount supernatural powers.
<p>
This collection is agnostic about medium; an instance of this collection could be an instance of #$MediaSeriesProduct, #$TVSeriesProduct, #$Movie-CW, or another relevant medium for animation.
<p>
Some examples of anime well known in the US are: #$Robotech-TheTVShow, #$Pokemon-TVShow, #$Akira-TheMovie, #$SailorMoon-TVShow, and #$DragonBallZ.
<p>
A borderline case is #$PowerpuffGirls-TheTVShow, which has anime-ish animation, but no references to Japanese culture.")
(comment distributesOutOfArg "(#$distributesOutOfArg <reln> <pred> <n>) is a directive to the canonicalizer that relation <reln> distrbutes out of a predicate <pred> when it appears as the top-most relation in the predicate's arg <n>.  Specifically, it denotes (#$distributesOutOfArg <reln> <pred> <n>) denotes that the canonicalizer will translate
<p>
(<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> (<reln> <arg-n1> <arg-n2> ... <arg-nm>) <arg-n+1> ..)
<p>
into
<p>
(<reln> (<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> <arg-n1> <arg-n+1> ...)
(<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> <arg-n2> <arg-n+1> ...)
...
(<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> <arg-nm> <arg-n+1> ...))")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn SecondRib) Tuberosity) "<p>")
(comment accused "A #$BinaryRolePredicate and a specialization of #$claimsResponsible.  #$accused relates an instance of #$Accusing to an instance of #$Agent-Generic.  <code>(#$accused ACCUSING AGT)</code> means that <code>AGT</code> is the agent being accused of something in <code>ACCUSING</code>.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn FirstRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment genlInverse "A #$MetaPredicate for stating that one binary  predicate is a generalization of the <i>inverse</i> of another binary predicate.   <code>(#$genlInverse PRED GENL-INV-PRED)</code> means that <code>GENL-INV-PRED</code> is a  \"generalized inverse\" of <code>PRED</code>.  That is, <code>(PRED ARG1 ARG2)</code> implies  <code>(GENL-INV-PRED ARG2 ARG1)</code>.  For example, <code>(#$genlInverse #$grandchildren #$ancestors)</code> means that grandparents are  ancestors of their grandchildren.  See also #$genlPreds and #$negationInverse.")
(comment arg3Isa "A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg3Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the third argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of COL.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3</code> ...) is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$isa ARG3 COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a similar but more flexible ternary  predicate.")
(comment LowAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.   In a given context, <code>(#$LowAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>low amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$LowAmountFn #$Happiness) is a low amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$lowAmountOf.")
(comment quantityToGenericValuePredicate "A binary predicate that relates a given #$GenericQuantity (q.v.) to the #$GenericValuePredicate (q.v.) that corresponds to the \"amount\" of that quantity.  <code>(#$quantityToGenericValuePredicate QUANT PRED)</code> means that <code>PRED</code> is the #$correspondingGenericValuePredicate to the #$GenericValueFunction <code>(FUNC)</code> that returns <code>QUANT</code> for some argument <code>(QUANTTYPE)</code>.
<p>
In practice, <code>QUANT</code> will itself usually be given in the form <code>(FUNC QUANTTYPE)</code>.  So, for example, this holds:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$quantityToGenericValuePredicate
    (#$HighAmountFn #$Visibility)
    #$highAmountOf) .
</pre>
<p>
See also #$quantityOrderedQuantityType.")
(comment IntegerFromToFn "A #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  Where <code>LOW</code> <= <code>HIGH</code>, <code>(#$IntegerFromToFn LOW HIGH)</code> is the collection of all integers from <code>LOW</code> to <code>HIGH</code>, inclusive.  (If <code>LOW</code> > <code>HIGH</code>, the non-atomic term is #$undefined.)
<p>
For example, (#$IntegerFromToFn 1 3) has the integers 1, 2 and 3 as its only instances.
<p>
See also the predicate #$integerBetween.")
(comment executableProgramName "A binary predicate that relates an instance of #$ComputerFile-CW (which may be a program or a #$LibraryFile) to an instance of #$ProperNameString. (#$executableProgramName PROGRAM EXE-NAME) means that EXE-NAME is a string used as the executable name for the computer program PROGRAM. EXE-NAME is the name that the package manager on a computer uses to keep track of programs that are installed. Note that the way Windows operating systems use executable names is case-insensitive, whereas for UNIX systems it is case-sensitive.
<p>
Also note that for many programs that run in UNIX environments the 'executable name' may actually only name a startup script which launches the program proper. This is the case, for instance, with #$Mozilla-TheProgram, where \"mozilla\" is a startup script which launches \"mozilla-bin\", which does the real work.")
(comment hitman "<code>(#$hitman HIT AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> contracted to do the #$MurderForHire <code>HIT</code>.")
(comment equals "The binary identity relation.  <code>(#$equals THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> are \"numerically\" (as opposed to \"qualitatively\") identical, i.e. they are one and the same thing.  A sentence of the above form is true if and only if the terms occupying the two argument-places of '#$equals' denote the same thing.")
(comment runsOS "<code>(#$runsOS COMPUTER OS)</code> means that the individual #$Computer <code>COMPUTER</code> is running the #$OperatingSystem <code>OS</code>. This predicate should not be confused with #$runsUnderOS (q.v.), which relates computer programs to the operating systems they require in order to run.")
(comment bodilyDoer "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$animateDoer and #$nonDeliberateActors (qq.v.).  The predicate #$bodilyDoer relates a given physical event to any organism that is a non-deliberate \"doer\" of the event (i.e. the organism acts without conscious intention or volition).  (#$bodilyDoer EVENT DOER) means that DOER does EVENT (which entails that DOER is not merely subjected to EVENT by external forces), but does it non-deliberately.
<p>
Note that for certain kinds of actions (e.g. physical growth, peristalsis, and reflex actions) organisms are always merely #$bodilyDoers; for certain other actions (e.g. breathing, flinching, and shouting) an organism commonly acts as a #$bodilyDoer, but sometimes acts instead as a deliberate performer (see #$performedBy).
<p>
Note also that an organism that dies of natural causes is the #$bodilyDoer of that event, because of the internal processes the body does during its #$Dying.  And in certain #$PhysiologicalConditions, including an #$AilmentCondition (e.g. diabetes) or a healing process, organisms are considered to be #$bodilyDoers because their own bodily processes are creating or sustaining those conditions.  An organism killed by an external agent, however, is just a #$bodilyActedOn (q.v.) actor in that event; thus instances of #$KillingByOrganism should use #$bodilyActedOn to describe the organism killed.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn FifthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment MediumToVeryHighAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.   In a given context, <code>(#$MediumToVeryHighAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>medium-to-very-high amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$MediumToVeryHighAmountFn #$Happiness) is the range from medium to very high happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$LowAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$mediumToVeryHighAmountOf.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn SecondRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment relationExistsCountInstance "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that is used to state that a given binary predicate holds between a specified number of instances of a given collection and a given thing.  (#$relationExistsCountInstance BINPRED COL THING N) means that there are exactly N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL such that (BINPRED INST(i) THING) holds (for 0 <= i <= N).  It is equivalent to (and so can be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$thereExistExactly N ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL)
(BINPRED ?INST THING))) .
<p>
For example, (#$relationExistsCountInstance #$authorOfLiteraryWork-CW #$Person #$Waverley 1) means that there is exactly one author of Waverley.  Cf.#$relationInstanceExistsCount, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment NorthEasternRegionFn "A unary function that returns the 'northeastern region' of an instance of #$GeographicalRegion. What counts as the           'northeastern region' is contextually determined. Some of the factors influencing the determination may be local geography, cultural identity, and political considerations.")
(comment ethnicGroupsHere "<code>(#$ethnicGroupsHere PLACE PERSONTYPE)</code> means that people of the ethnic group <code>PERSONTYPE</code> inhabit the territory of the #$GeopoliticalEntity, <code>PLACE</code>.")
(comment senderOfInfo "The predicate #$senderOfInfo is used to indicate the sending agent in a particular transfer of information.  <code>(#$senderOfInfo TRANSFER SENDER)</code> means that <code>SENDER</code> is an agent who is the source of information transferred in the #$InformationTransferEvent <code>TRANSFER</code>.  At the time of <code>TRANSFER</code> (and typically for some period beforehand), <code>SENDER</code> conceives of the information transferred in <code>TRANSFER</code>.  It's not the case that SENDER necessarily knows the 'information' or has it among his/her beliefs (see the predicates #$knows and #$beliefs), because <code>TRANSFER</code> could involve lying (e.g., #$GeorgeWashington's telling his father, 'I didn't chop down the cherry tree').  Note that <code>(#$senderOfInfo TRANSFER SENDER)</code> implies that <code>SENDER</code> performs <code>TRANSFER</code> intentionally, in the sense that there is some other agent (related to <code>TRANSFER</code> via #$communicationTarget) that <code>SENDER</code> is attempting to transfer information to via <code>TRANSFER</code>.  Of course, <code>SENDER</code> may or may not be successful in transferring that information to the other agent.")
(comment MaxRangeFn "<code>(#$MaxRangeFn QUANTITY<sub>1</sub> ... QUANTITY<sub>N</sub>)</code> denotes a quantity which subsumes each of <code>QUANTITY<sub>1</sub></code> through <code>QUANTITY<sub>N</sub></code>.  For example, <code>(#$MaxRangeFn (#$Meter 1 3) (#$Meter 2 4))</code> denotes (#$Meter 1 4).  See also #$MinRangeFn.")
(comment collectionBackchainEncouraged "<p>
(#$collectionBackchainEncouraged COLLECTION)
<p>
means that rules that conclude
<p>
(#$isa <anything> COLLECTION)
<p>
are most likely going to have to be used in order to prove literals of the form
<p>
(#$isa <anything> COLLECTION),
<p>
and rules that conclude
<p>
(#$genls <anything> COLLECTION)
<p>
are most likely going to have to be used in order to prove literals of the form
<p>
(#$genls <anything> COLLECTION).
<p>
This directs the inference engine to use such rules even if HL predicate transformation is disabled. Note that it will not necessarily use rules of the form
<p>
([#$isa|#$genls] <anything> <variable>)
<p>
where <variable> might bind to COLLECTION, and it will not necessarily use rules of the form
<p>
([#$isa|#$genls] <anything> SPEC), where (#$genls SPEC COLLECTION).
<p>
See also #$backchainEncouraged for the analogue of this predicate applicable to predicates rather than collections.")
(comment arg4Isa "A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg4Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the fourth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4</code> ...) is semantically well-formed only if  <code>(#$isa ARG4 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment objectExposed "<code>(#$objectExposed EXPOSURE OBJECT)</code> means that during <code>EXPOSURE</code>, the object that is exposed is <code>OBJECT</code>.")
(comment Extortion "A specialization of #$CoercingAnAgent, #$CriminalAct, and #$HostileSocialAction (qq.v.).  In each instance of #$Extortion, an agent extracts some kind of payment from another by threatening harm to the latter.  The payment extracted is usually a sum of money, though it might be some other kind of object (see #$objectExtorted), or it might be in the form of an action performed by the victim.
<p>
Specializations of #$Extortion include #$Blackmail and #$ProtectionRacketeering.")
(comment performSubL "<code>(#$performSubL SUBL)</code> states that the SubL expression <code>SUBL</code> has been successfully evaluated for side-effect.  #$performSubL is evaluated as late in an inference proof as possible.  See also #$trueSubL which evaluates usually as soon as possible in an inference.  For example, <code>(#$performSubL (#$ExpandSubLFn () (print \"Done\")))</code> will print out the string \"Done\" as a given proof path completes.  See #$ExpandSubLFn for a way to denote SubL within CycL.  See also #$EvaluateSubLFn.")
(comment CoexistingObjectsPredicate "A specialization of #$CotemporalPredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate is a binary predicate that holds only between #$TemporalThings, and holds between two things <code>TEMP1</code> and <code>TEMP2</code> at a given #$TimeInterval <code>TIME</code> only if both <code>TEMP1</code> and <code>TEMP2</code> temporally subsume <code>TIME</code>.
<p>
For example, #$owns is a coexisting-objects predicate.  So the the fact that the sentence <code>(#$owns #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> is true at the time-index (see #$mtTimeIndex) (#$YearFn 2003) entails that both Curtis and Patches were alive throughout 2003.  In contrast, #$awareOf is not a coexisting-objects predicate, as one can be aware of something (e.g. an historical figure) that no longer exists.
<p>
Note that many reified #$CoexistingObjectsPredicates are also #$InterExistingObjectPredicates (q.v.).
<p>
For the most general coexisting-object predicate (which all the others are specializations of), see #$temporallyCoexist.  Also see #$contemporaryInArg.")
(comment objectAdheredTo "(#$objectAdheredTo ATTACHING OBJ) means that OBJ is the thing to which some object is attached in ATTACHING.
<p>
Often it is useful to think of actors in #$AttachmentEvents as \"attached\" and \"attachees\", i.e., the thing attached versus the thing to which something is attached.  For instance, if I attach a rearview mirror to my windshield, the rearview mirror seems to be the item attached while the windshield is the \"attachee\".   We use the predicate #$objectAdheredTo to relate an attachment event to the \"thing to which something is attached\" in an attachment event.
<p>
When a much smaller object gets stuck to a larger object, the larger object is usally deemed to play the role #$objectAdheredTo.  For instance, if a person becomes attached to a burr, the burr plays the role #$objectAttached and the person plays the role #$objectAdheredTo in the attachment event.  If two burrs become attached to each other they both play the role #$objectOfAttachment and there is no designated \"attached\" and \"attachee\".  Note that as a default rule (#$objectAdheredTo ATTACHING LARGE) relates an attaching event to the larger of the relevant actors in the ATTACHING, but there may be counterexamples.  For instance, a trailer may be larger than the car by which it is towed and yet the car may be deemed to play the role #$objectAdheredTo in the event in which the car is connected to the trailer.")
(comment eventPartiallyOccursAt "A predicate used to state that some part of an event occurs at a certain location.  <code>(#$eventPartiallyOccursAt EVENT PLACE)</code> means that some sub-event of that event, <code>SUB-EVENT</code>, is such that (#$eventOccursAt <code>SUB-EVENT PLACE)</code>.  However, <code>SUB-EVENT</code> need not be reified.")
(comment relationAllExistsMin "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  (#$relationAllExistsMin BINPRED COL1 COL2 N) means that for any instance INST of COL1 there are at least N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL2 such that (BINPRED INST INST(i)) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$implies
(#$isa ?INST COL1)
(#$thereExistAtLeast N ?INST-I
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST-I COL2)
(BINPRED ?INST ?INST-I)))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationAllExistsMin #$subEvents #$EatingEvent #$Swallowing 1)' means that every act of eating includes at least one swallowing. Cf. #$relationExistsMinAll, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment relationExistsMinAll "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to two collections.  (#$relationExistsMinAll BINPRED COL1 COL2 N) means that for any instance INST of COL2, there are at least N instances of (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL1 such that (BINPRED INST(i) INST) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$implies
(#$isa INST COL2)
(#$thereExistAtLeast N ?INST-I
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST-I COL1)
(BINPRED ?INST-I INST)))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationExistsMinAll #$physicalParts #$Hand #$Finger 1)' means that every finger is part of at least one hand.  Cf. #$relationAllExistsMin, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment temporalBoundsIntersect "<code>(#$temporalBoundsIntersect TEMP1 TEMP2)</code> means that the continuous time interval between the start and end of <code>TEMP1</code> (inclusive) temporally intersects the continuous time interval between the start and end of <code>TEMP2</code> (inclusive).  Clearly, if <code>TEMP1</code> and <code>TEMP2</code> are themselves temporally continuous, then the above entails the stronger statement <code>(#$temporallyIntersects TEMP1 TEMP2)</code>.  However, if either <code>TEMP1</code> or <code>TEMP2</code> is temporally discontinuous, it is possible for their bounds to intersect without their having any time point in common.  For example, the bounds of the discontinuous event of \"Fred sleeping this week\" might intersect the bounds of \"Fred eating this week\", even though the two events share no time points.")
(comment quantityOrderedQuantityType "A (typed) specialization of #$isa that relates     a given #$Quantity to the #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (qq.v.) of which it is an instance.  <code>(#$quantityOrderedQuantityType QUANT QUANTTYPE)</code> means that <code>QUANT</code> is an instance of the totally-ordered quantity-type <code>QUANTTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, both of these hold:
<pre>
  (#$quantityOrderedQuantityType
    (#$HighAmountFn #$Goodness-Functional)
    #$Goodness-Functional)
</pre>
<p>
<pre>
  (#$quantityOrderedQuantityType #$SpeedOfLight #$Speed)</code> .
</pre>
<p>
See also #$quantityToGenericValuePredicate.")
(comment conveyor-Stationary "<code>(#$conveyor-Stationary CONVEY CONVEYOR)</code> means that in the #$Conveying-Stationary <code>CONVEY</code>, <code>CONVEYOR</code> facilitates the movement of some object (the object is related to <code>CONVEY</code> by #$transportees (q.v.)), but <code>CONVEYOR</code> does not move together with that object along the path of conveyance.  For example, an instance of #$River can move a boat (an instance of #$Watercraft) from one location to another, without itself moving translationally; i.e., the river does not change its location (although some pieces of #$Water in the river do) together with the boat.")
(comment ModeratelyToHighlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyToHighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate-to-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$ModeratelyToHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately to highly hilly: those that are moderate-to-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment biologicalOffspring "A specialization of #$biologicalRelatives.  <code>(#$biologicalOffspring PARENT OFFSPRING)</code> means that <code>PARENT</code> is one of the biological parents of <code>OFFSPRING</code>.  Specializations of this predicate include #$biologicalDaughters and #$biologicalSons.  Cf. #$children.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn EighthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment presidentOfCountry "<code>(#$presidentOfCountry COUNTRY PERSON)</code> means that the #$LegalAgent <code>PERSON</code> is a president (see #$President-HeadOfGovernmentOrHeadOfState) of the #$GeopoliticalEntity <code>COUNTRY</code>.  National presidents may be elected by the people, appointed by the legislature or other ruling group, or they may be be self-declared.")
(comment symmetricPhysicalPartTypes "<code>(#$symmetricPhysicalPartTypes BIG SMALL)</code> means that every instance of <code>BIG</code> has exactly two, symmetrically positioned, instances of <code>SMALL</code> as parts.")
(comment typeMeasuresQuantity "A predicate for stating the #$QuantitySlot(s) (q.v.) measured by a given type of #$Evaluating (q.v.).  (#$typeMeasuresQuantity EVALTYPE SLOT) means that SLOT expresses the sort of quantity that EVALTYPE measures.  For example, (#$typeMeasuresQuantity #$MeasurementOfHeight #$heightOfObject) means that every instance of #$MeasurementOfHeight yields a value for the second argument of #$heightOfObject (given the object being measured as the first argument).")
(comment interArgIsa "A quintary instance of #$InterArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).   <code>(#$interArgIsa RELN INDEP-ARGNUM INDEP-COL DEP-ARGNUM DEP-COL)</code> means that,  when an instance of <code>INDEP-COL</code> appears as the <code>INDEP-ARGNUM</code>th argument to <code>RELN</code>,  a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness is that the <code>DEP-ARGNUM</code>th argument must be an instance of <code>DEP-COL</code>.  That is, if <code>INDEP-INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEP-COL</code>, then <code>(RELN</code> ... <code>INDEP-INST</code> ...), where <code>INDEP-INST</code> is the <code>INDEP-ARGNUM</code>th (or \"independent\") argument to <code>RELN</code>, is semantically well-formed only if the <code>DEP-ARGNUM</code>th (or \"dependent\") argument (which is some item in the sequence <... <code>INDEP-INST</code> ...> other than the <code>INDEP-ARGNUM</code>th) is an instance of <code>DEP-COL</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$interArgIsa #$performedBy 1 #$PurposefulAction 2 #$IntelligentAgent) means that, if <code>ACT</code> is a #$PurposefulAction, then <code>(#$performedBy ACT AGENT)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>AGENT</code> is an #$IntelligentAgent.
<p>
For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations.  For a more general explanation of independent and dependent arguments in the present sense, see #$InterArgConstraintPredicate.  For similar to but less flexible predicates than #$interArgIsa, which have already-fixed positions for the independent and dependent arguments, see the ternary #$interArgIsa1-2 et al.")
(comment MathematicalFunctionOnScalars "A specialization of #$ScalarDenotingFunction (q.v.).  Each instance of #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars is a function that takes one or more #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) as its argument(s), and returns a scalar interval as its value.
<p>
Most currently-reified instances of #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars are applicable both to pure numbers (see #$NumericInterval) and to quantities (see esp. #$MeasurableQuantity).  For example, #$PlusFn can be used to add real numbers together and to add distances together.  But some mathematical functions on scalars (e.g. #$ExponentFn and #$LogFn) are defined only for numeric-intervals. And some (e.g. some #$TrigonometricFunctions) take numbers as arguments and return
quantities, or vice versa.
<p>
Other instances of this collection include #$TimesFn, #$SineFn and #$AbsoluteValueFn.")
(comment ListOfLengthFn "A unary #$CollectionDenotingFunction that takes a non-negative integer (or integer-bounded numeric-interval) and returns the collection of lists with that length (or a length within that range).
<p>
If <code>INTERVAL</code> is a #$NonNegativeInteger, then <code>(#$ListOfLengthFn INTERVAL)</code> is the collection of all and only those #$Lists whose length is exactly <code>INTERVAL</code>.  If <code>INTERVAL</code> is a #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine <code>(#$Unity M N)</code> whose minimum (see #$minQuantValue) is the non-negative integer <code>M</code> and whose maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) is the non-negative integer <code>N</code>, then <code>(#$ListOfLengthFn INTERVAL)</code> is the collection of all and only those #$Lists whose length is between <code>M</code> and <code>N</code> inclusive (see #$ListOfMinimumLengthFn and #$ListOfMaximumLengthFn).  #$ListOfLengthFn is not defined (see #$undefined) for any other type of argument.
<p>
For example, (#$TheList #$Dog #$Cat) is an instance of both (#$ListOfLengthFn 2) and (#$ListOfLengthFn (#$Unity 1 3)); but (#$TheList #$Cat) is an instance of the latter collection only.
<p>
Cf. #$LengthOfListFn.")
(comment Collection "A specialization of #$SetOrCollection (q.v.).  This is the collection of all collections of things.  Each #$Collection is a kind or type of thing whose instances share a certain property, attribute, or feature.  For example, #$Cat is the collection of all and only cats, and #$BinaryFunction is the collection of all and only functions taking two arguments.
<p>
The notion of collection is fundamental to the Cyc ontology, and is thus difficult to define in a precise and substantive way.  But it is instructive to compare it to the mathematical notion of a set (see #$Set-Mathematical).  Like a set, a collection is an #$AbstractThing (i.e. aspatial and atemporal) that is not an #$Individual (cf.), but instead has elements (see #$elementOf).  Unlike a set, however, the elements or \"instances\" (see #$isa) of a given collection can vary from context to context, and it is possible for distinct collections to have exactly the same elements (i.e. to have the same #$extent) with respect to a given context. For example, the (non-reified) collections \"Chordate\" and \"Renate\" are co-extensional in the context of the present-day actual world, but have differring extents with respect to hypothetical contexts in which there exist creatures having hearts but not kidneys.  This difference is sometimes summarized by saying that sets are \"extensional\" things and collections are \"intensional\" things.
<p>
Many, though by no means all, of the collections reified in the Cyc ontology correspond to natural kinds or classes.  It is particulary useful to reify collections that are difficult to define precisely, but about which there are many common-sense rules and other things to state.  For example, it would not be worthwile to introduce a constant 'WhiteCat', both because it's easy to define the collection of white cats in terms of more general, already-reified notions (e.g. '(#$CollectionIntersection2Fn #$Cat #$WhiteColor)'), and because there's not much to say about that particular collection.  #$WhiteCollarWorker, on the other hand, is a good collection to have reified, as it is hard to define precisely, yet is a collection about which there are many things to say.
<p>
Some of the many reified specializations of #$Collection are #$FirstOrderCollection, #$TimeDependentCollection, and #$ObjectType.")
(comment valueInRange "A specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$quantitySubsumes that a given numerically measurable #$ScalarPointValue to those scalar intervals that subsume it or within which it \"falls completely inside\" it.  <code>(#$valueInRange VALUE RANGE)</code> means that both (i) the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>RANGE</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo <code>VALUE</code> and (ii) the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>RANGE</code> is #$lessThanOrEqualTo <code>VALUE</code>.
<p>
For example, the range (#$Meter 2 5) -- the #$Distance of between 2 and 5 meters inclusive -- subsumes both (#$Meter 3) and (#$Meter 5), but not (#$Meter 6).")
(comment VerySlightlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$VerySlightlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>very-slight</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$VerySlightlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are very slightly hilly: those that are very-slight in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment EvaluateSubLFn "<code>(#$EvaluateSubLFn SUBL)</code> denotes the SubL term resulting from the invocation of the SubL EVAL facility on the expression <code>SUBL</code>.  For example, <code>(#$EvaluateSubLFn (#$ExpandSubLFn () (+ 1 2)))</code> evaluates to 3.  See #$ExpandSubLFn for a way to denote SubL within CycL.  See also #$trueSubL.")
(comment diagonalScreenMeasurement "The expression `(#$diagonalScreenMeasurement SCREEN MEASURE)' means that the
#$VideoScreen SCREEN has a diagonal measurement MEASURE.
<p>")
(comment typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional "A type-level version of #$primaryFunction. <code>(#$typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional AGTTYPE SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that in ordinary or normal circumstances, instances of <code>AGTTYPE</code> have playing <code>ROLE</code> in situations of type <code>SITTYPE</code> as their most important function in some immediate functional context. Like #$primaryFunction, the relation is agnostic as to whether the functionality derives from the intent of some intentional designer: i.e., the relation is not a spec-pred of #$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable.  This is not to say that usage of #$typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional implies that no design exists: the relation is simply agnostic as to whether a design exists or not.  For this reason #$typePrimaryFunction is deemed to be a specialization.  In cases where <code>SITTYPE</code> is itself a #$FunctionalSystem (say, a kind of #$Cell or a kind of #$Organization in the #$FunctionalRoleAnalysisMt), <code>SITTYPE</code> itself specifies the functional context: otherwise, this context is taken to be specified by the #$Microtheory in which the assertion is made.")
(comment preActors "An #$ActorSlot (q.v.) that relates an event to  those of its participants that pre-exist it. <code>(#$preActors EVENT OBJECT)</code>  means that <code>OBJECT</code> is a participant (see #$actors) in <code>EVENT</code> and that <code>OBJECT</code>  exists before <code>EVENT</code> begins.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> also exists during at least part of <code>EVENT</code> (as #$actors generalizes to #$temporallyIntersects).  #$preActors is a very general actor-slot, subsuming #$doneBy, #$objectActedOn, #$objectMoving,  and many others.")
(comment movesWith "<code>(#$movesWith MOVED MOVER)</code> means that if <code>MOVER</code> undergoes translation (see #$Movement-TranslationEvent) then <code>MOVED</code> by default also undergoes a translation in the same direction and same speed.  However, this default is defeated if <code>MOVED</code> is undergoing motion relative to <code>MOVER</code>. For example, a person would #$movesWith the Titanic if they were #$on-Physical it.  However, if <code>MOVED</code> is moving relative to <code>MOVER</code>, then its 'absolute' motion is computed by subtracting its motion relative to <code>MOVER</code> from the 'absolute' motion vector of <code>MOVER</code>.")
(comment StrictlyMentalEvent "A specialization of #$AtLeastPartiallyMentalEvent. Each instance of this collection is an event which is 'strictly mental' in the sense that if the event is instantiated by some physical situation, that instantiating situation is not essential to the event being an event of that type.  For instance, perceiving an apple is not an instance of #$StrictlyMentalEvent insofar as it essentially requires sense organs and an apple (and thus #$Perceiving is not a specialization of #$StrictlyMentalEvent). However, the phenomenological aspects of this event, e.g., the experience of redness or roundness, do not require the sense organs or the apple, and it is conceivable that a disembodied mind could have such experiences. (Thus, #$ExperiencingPerception is a specialization of #$StrictlyMentalEvent.)
<p>
Note that the property defining this collection is primitive and notoriously difficult to define, except via examples. If an event is not clearly an instance of #$ExperiencingPerception, entertaining a thought (#$Thinking), or #$ExperiencingEmotion, or fully decomposable into a collection of such events, then one should be very careful about making it in an instance of #$StrictlyMentalEvent.")
(comment superTaxons "A specialization of #$genls used to relate instances of #$BiologicalTaxon.  <code>(#$superTaxons TAXON SUPERTAXON)</code> means that <code>SUPERTAXON</code> is above the more specific <code>TAXON</code> in the biological taxon hierarchy; i.e. the later taxon is more general and inclusive than the former.  Every instance of <code>TAXON</code> must therefore also be an instance of <code>SUPERTAXON</code>.  For example, (#$superTaxons #$DomesticCat #$FelisGenus) holds.  See also #$taxonMembers.")
(comment holdsSometimeDuring "An instance of both #$TemporalPredicate and #$ModalPredicate that is used to state (in a somewhat indirect way) that a given sentence is true at some point within a certain time period.  <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring TEMP SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> is true at some  moment in the #$temporalExtent (q.v.) of the #$TemporalThing <code>TEMP</code>.
<p>
For example, the sentence <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 2003)) (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> means that at some moment in the  month of July 2003 Curtis had Patches as a pet.  This entails (e.g.) that Curtis had Patches at some moment in 2003:
<pre>
  (#$holdsSometimeDuring
    (#$YearFn 2003)
    (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)).
</pre>
<p>
Similarly, <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring #$Patches-Cat (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> means that Curtis has Patches as a pet at some point during Patches's lifetime.
<p>
See also the specialization #$holdsIn.
<p>
Note that a sentence of the form <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring TEMP (PRED ... ARGi ...))</code>, where <code>ARGi</code> is a #$TemporalThing, does <i>not</i> in general imply that <code>ARGi</code> temporally intersects <code>TEMP</code>.  For example, <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring (#$YearFn 2003) (#$awareOf #$Curtis #$GeorgeWashington))</code> obviously doesn't imply that #$GeorgeWashington was alive at some point in 2003.  <code>ARGi</code>'s temporally intersecting (indeed, its temporally <i>subsuming</i>) <code>TEMP</code> <i>does</i> follow, however, if <code>PRED</code> is a #$CotemporalPredicate that is #$contemporaryInArg (q.v.) in its <code>i</code>th argument-place (as #$hasPets is, in both argument-places).
<p>
Note also that there are two other ways to temporally-qualify sentences in CycL, which do not involve using #$holdsSometimeDuring (or #$holdsIn).  Both involve temporally qualifying the #$Microtheory in which the (otherwise unqualified) sentence is asserted, and either can be used to state something roughly equivalent to a #$holdsSometimeDuring sentence.  Consider the second (displayed)  Curtis/Patches example above.  First, one could assert <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in some microtheory <code>MT</code> such that <code>(#$holdsInTime-Sometime MT (#$YearFn 2003))</code>.  Second, one could assert <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in a microtheory <code>MT'</code> such that both <code>(#$mtTimeIndex MT' (#$YearFn 2003))</code> and <code>(#$mtTimeParameter MT' #$Null-TimeParameter)</code> hold.  This later method, being the most developed and well-supported, is generally preferred over the others.
<p>
Note finally that it would be incorrect to assert a sentence like <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in a non-temporally-qualified microtheory like the #$BaseKB, since Curtis didn't have Patches when (e.g.) he was a young boy, let alone in 3500 BCE.")
(comment (RightObjectOfPairFn (PartTypeFn FirstThoracicVertebra VertebralTransverseProcess)) "<p>")
(comment (RightObjectOfPairFn (PartTypeFn SecondThoracicVertebra VertebralTransverseProcess)) "<p>")
(comment goals "<code>(#$goals AGENT SENT)</code> means that the proposition <code>PROP</code> expressed by the sentence <code>SENT</code> is the content of a goal that <code>AGENT</code> has. That is, one of <code>AGENT</code>'s goals is that <code>PROP</code> be made (or kept) true.  Goals are typically \"future-directed\": either (i) <code>PROP</code> is not currently true (or at least is believed by <code>AGENT</code> not to be currently true) and <code>AGENT</code>  wants it to become true or (ii) <code>PROP</code> is currently true (or at least is believed by <code>AGENT</code> to be currently true) and <code>AGENT</code> wants it to remain true.  Also, <code>AGENT</code> intends to play some sort of active role in bringing about the truth of <code>PROP</code>, and plans accordingly.  This distinguishes goals from mere desires or expectations (see #$desires and #$expects).  See also #$Goal and #$goalCategoryForAgent.")
(comment comment "A #$DocumentationPredicate (q.v.) that is used to relate a #$CycLIndexedTerm (usually a #$CycLConstant) to a #$SubLString containing an English explanation of the term's meaning and use, as an aid to humans (whether #$Cyclists or not) browsing the Cyc Knowledge Base.  <code>(#$comment TERM STRING)</code> means that <code>STRING</code> is a piece of Cyc documentation that explains the meaning and use of <code>TERM</code>.  For example, the passage you are reading now is the comment for the CycL constant `#$comment'.  See also #$cyclistNotes.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn ThirdRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment purposeOfEvent-GoalType "This predicate relates actions to their purposes. <code>(#$purposeOfEvent-GoalType ACTION GOALTYPE)</code> means that the purpose of <code>ACTION</code> is <code>GOALTYPE</code>.  Typically, actions will inherit purposes from the purpose that the performer has in carrying out the action, see #$purposeInEvent-GoalType and #$purposeInEvent.  See documentation on these preds for discussion of these issues.  Also see #$purposeOfEvent which relates a formula, i.e. the goal statement, to an event.")
(comment constantID "A predicate which relates each Cyc constant to an internal identification number for it. This number is unique within any given image, though not necessarily across images (for that, see #$constantGUID). <code>(#$constantID CONSTANT ID)</code> states that the #$NonNegativeInteger <code>ID</code> is the unique internal id for the #$CycLConstant <code>CONSTANT</code> (in the current image). For obvious reasons, this predicate is #$notAssertible.")
(comment trueSubL "<code>(#$trueSubL SUBL)</code> states that the SubL expression <code>SUBL</code> evaluates to a value other than NIL.  This predicate allows for a procedural test for programmatic 'truth' in the implementation language to be reflected as a CycL test for logical truth in the logical language.  For example, <code>(#$trueSubL (#$ExpandSubLFn () (integerp 42)))</code> states that the SubL expression <code>(integerp 42)</code> evaluates to something other than NIL.  See #$ExpandSubLFn for a way to denote SubL within CycL.  See also #$evaluate and #$EvaluateSubLFn, and #$performSubL.")
(comment CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm "A subcollection of both #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm and #$CycLIndexedTerm (qq.v.).  #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm is the collection of all CycL terms that both may be reified and may denote something in the universe of discourse. It thus includes all instances of #$CycLConstant as well as any NAT (see #$CycLNonAtomicTerm) whose main functor denotes an instance of #$ReifiableFunction.
<p>
For example, the NAT '(#$GovernmentFn #$France)' is a #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm, since #$GovernmentFn is a #$ReifiableFunction.  Similarly,  '(#$JuvenileFn #$Platypus)' is a CycL reifiable denotational term; although it is not currently reified in the KB, it is reifiable and denotational (see #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm).  Finally, '(#$BorderBetweenFn #$Canada #$Mexico)' is a CycL reifiable denotational term; although it happens not to denote anything in the universe of discourse, it is nonetheless the kind of NAT that can and usually does denote.
<p>
Note that #$CycLVariables are not considered reifiable terms.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn ThirdRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment InterconvertibleUnitType "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$FunctionType.  Each instance IUT of #$InterconvertibleUnitType is a collection of #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) functions that are interconvertible.  That is, each instance of IUT is a function used to measure quantities in units that are convertible into units measured by any of the other functions in IUT (see #$interconvertibleUnits).
<p>
Examples of #$InterconvertibleUnitTypes include #$UnitOfTime, #$UnitOfDistance and #$UnitOfComputerStorageCapacity.  Thus, #$UnitOfTime includes the functions #$HoursDuration and #$MinutesDuration, and any duration denoted by applying one  of these functions can also be expressed in terms of the other; e.g. (#$HoursDuration 1) equals (#$MinutesDuration 60).
<p>
Note that not all ontologically interesting specializations of #$UnitOfMeasure constitute interconvertible types.  For example, #$UnitOfRate has both #$TimesPerDay and #$MilesPerHour as instances; but the quantities returned by either one of these functions are not convertible into quantities returned by the other.")
(comment correspondingPreds-Capability "<code>(#$correspondingPreds-Capability INST-PRED TYPE-PRED)</code> means that <code>INST-PRED</code> is the #$CapabilityPredicate-InstanceLevel corresponding to the #$CapabilityPredicate-TypeLevel <code>TYPE-PRED</code>.  An instance-level capability predicate restricts its first argument to individuals, whereas a type-level capability predicate restricts its first argument to collections.  What makes two such predicates correspond is that each represents the same type of capability.")
(comment Telecommunication "A specialization of #$CommunicatingUsingADevice.  Each instance of #$Telecommunication is a communication event which utilizes an electronic communication device that is designed to convey messages over geographically signficant distances (roughly, one #$Kilometer or more).  Specializations include #$SendingInformationByTelephone, #$FaxingADocument, and #$Broadcasting.
<p>
Note that talking to someone in the next room on a #$Telephone would be an instance of #$Telecommunication even though the communication does not span a geographically significant distance; for the communication uses a device which is <i>designed</i> for communication across such distances.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn FirstRib) Tuberosity) "<p>")
(comment transitiveInArgs "The expression `(#$transitiveInArgs PRED N M)' means that the #$Predicate PRED is transitive in its Nth and Mth arguments, if  its other arguments are held fixed.  Specifically, if the Mth argument in one assertion using PRED is the same as the Nth argument in another assertion using PRED, and all arguments other than the Nth and Mth are the same in the two assertions, then the sentence obtained by replacing the Mth argument in the first assertion with the Mth argument in the second sentence holds.
<p>
Thus, for example, if #$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar is ternary and (#$transitiveInArgs #$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar 1 2), (#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar VAL1 VAL2 PRED), and (#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar VAL2 VAL3 PRED) all hold, then (#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar VAL1 VAL3 PRED) also holds.")
(comment CommercialActivity "A collection of events and a specialization of both #$EconomicAction and #$Transaction.  Instances of this collection include all types of buying, selling, offering to sell, offering to buy, marketing, paying for bought items, requesting bids, performing services for hire, advertising, etc. Its many specializations include #$LoanRepaying, #$Renting and #$ProductRecall.
<p>
Though there is a great deal of overlap between this collection and #$BusinessEvent, they are not the same. Firstly, #$BusinessEvent does not include any purely social or recreational events (whereas a weekend swap-meet, for example, would be an instance of #$CommercialActivity). Secondly, not all activities carried out in pursuit of business interests fall under the definition of commercial activity above (consider head-hunting, for example).")
(comment surroundsHorizontally "<code>(#$surroundsHorizontally OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> means that <code>OUTSIDE</code> surrounds a horizontal cross-section of <code>INSIDE</code>.  That is, there is some horizontal cross-section of <code>INSIDE</code> such that all rays drawn horizontally from points in that cross-section pass through <code>OUTSIDE</code>, and along each of those rays there are points which are in <code>OUTSIDE</code> but not in <code>INSIDE</code>.  Note that <code>(#$surroundsHorizontally OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> is <i>not</i> true if <code>INSIDE</code> shares a boundary with <code>OUTSIDE</code>; for example, Texas is not surrounded by the USA.  Positive cases: water surrounds islands, and foothills may surround a mountain range.  See also #$surroundsCompletely.")
(comment arg6Isa "A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg6Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the sixth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5 ARG6 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if  <code>(#$isa ARG6 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment relationAllInstance "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that is used to state that a given binary predicate holds between all instances of a given collection and a given thing. <code>(#$relationAllInstance PRED COL THING)</code> means that every instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> bears the relation <code>PRED</code> to <code>THING</code> such that <code>(PRED INST THING)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
     (#$implies
          (#$isa ?INST COL)
          (PRED ?INST THING)) .
</pre>
<p>
For example, '(#$relationAllInstance #$maxSpeed #$GolfCart (#$MilesPerHour 15))' means that the maximum speed of every golf cart is 15 miles per hour. Cf. #$relationInstanceAll, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment outputsCreated "An #$ActorSlot that relates #$CreationEvents to things -- tangible objects or other \"enduring\" things (see #$SomethingExisting) -- created by those events. <code>(#$outputsCreated EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> doesn't exist before <code>EVENT</code> but comes into existence sometime during <code>EVENT</code>, as a result of <code>EVENT</code>.  #$outputsCreated is a specialization of #$nonDeliberateActors, since it would in general be impossible for <code>OBJECT</code> to act deliberately in its own creation.")
(comment backchainForbidden "#$backchainForbidden is a predicate that allows users to cut down on the number of rules used to prove certain literals, and thus to increase the speed of inferences that involve proving those literals.  <code>(#$backchainForbidden PREDICATE)</code> means that no rules containing a positive literal with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 (in other words, no #$implies assertions in which a literal with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 appears in the consequent) will be used when attempting to prove (non-negated) literals with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0.  Asserting <code>(#$backchainForbidden PREDICATE)</code> is useful when all (non-negated) literals with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 are expected to be solvable by lookup of ground atomic formulas (GAF's) already in the KB, and so rules won't be needed to prove them.  However, asserting <code>(#$backchainForbidden PREDICATE)</code> is usually counter-productive in contexts where some literals with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 are expected to be proven via backchaining on rules containing a positive literal with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0.  See also the related predicates #$backchainRequired, #$backchainEncouraged, and #$backchainDiscouraged.")
(comment deviceUsed "The predicate #$deviceUsed relates an event to a device used in that event. <code>(#$deviceUsed EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that the #$PhysicalDevice <code>OBJECT</code> plays an instrumental role in the #$Event <code>EVENT</code> (see the more generalized predicate #$instrument-Generic), <code>OBJECT</code> is intentionally used in <code>EVENT</code>, and standardly (for example, in the #$HumanActivitiesMt) <code>OBJECT</code>'s role in <code>EVENT</code> is consistent with the object's #$primaryFunction.")
(comment eventTypeOccursAtLocationType "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a specialization of #$Event-Localized to a specialization of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  <code>(#$eventTypeOccursAtLocationType EVENT-TYPE LOC-TYPE)</code> means that every instance of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code> occurs at an instance of <code>LOC-TYPE</code>. For example (#$eventTypeOccursAtLocationType #$HavingAPicnic #$OutdoorLocation) means that all picnics occur outdoors.")
(comment extent "A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot predicate that relates a given set or collection (see #$SetOrCollection) to its extension: the set consisting of all of (and only) its elements (see #$elementOf).  <code>(#$extent SETORCOL SET)</code> means that every element of <code>SETORCOL</code> is an element of <code>SET</code> and every element of <code>SET</code> is an element of <code>SETORCOL</code>. If <code>SETORCOL</code> happens to be a set (see #$Set-Mathematical) itself, then <code>SET</code> and <code>SETORCOL</code> are identical (see #$equals), as sets are identified and distinguished strictly in terms of their elements.  Conversely, if <code>SETORCOL</code> happens to be a collection (see #$Collection), then <code>SET</code> and <code>SETORCOL</code> are distinct (see #$different), as no collection is a set.  See also #$coextensionalSetOrCollections.")
(comment inDomainOf "A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates instances of #$Relation
to things in their domains.  (#$inDomainOf ELEMENT RELATION)
means that ELEMENT is an element of the #$relationDomain (q.v.)
of RELATION.  That is, RELATION relates ELEMENT to at least
one thing in RELATION'S range (see #$relationRange and
#$inRangeOf).  If the #$relationalArity (q.v.) of RELATION
is N (for N > 1), then ELEMENT is an (N-1)-length instance of
#$List such that RELATION holds among ELEMENT's N-1
members (taken in the order of their occurrence in the list)
and some THING (taken as filling RELATION's Nth argument-
position).  In other words, ELEMENT consists of the first
N-1 members (taken in order) of some N-length list in the
#$relationExtension of RELATION.  If RELATION is unary it
has no domain, and #$inDomainOf is undefined for such a
RELATION.  If RELATION is a #$VariableArityRelation (q.v.)
there will be no particular length N-1 shared by each of
the lists in its domain.
<p>
Though this predicate can be applied to polyadic relations
generally, asserting that something is in RELATION's domain
will normally be of particular interest only when RELATION
is a function (i.e. an instance of #$Function-Denotational),
in which case RELATION relates each element of its domain to
exactly one element of its range.  See also #$DomainOfFunctionFn.")
(comment inputsDestroyed "The predicate #$inputsDestroyed is used to relate a particular event to the item or items destroyed in that event.  <code>(#$inputsDestroyed EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> exists before <code>EVENT</code>, is affected by <code>EVENT</code>, and due to that involvement, ends its existence at some time during <code>EVENT</code>.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> may or may not be acting deliberately (see #$deliberateActors) in <code>EVENT</code>.")
(comment subSituations "This predicate relates situations to further situations which are their parts.  <code>(#$subSituations SIT SUBSIT)</code> means that the #$Situation <code>SUBSIT</code> is a part of the #$Situation <code>SIT</code> such that a complete description of <code>SIT</code> would have to involve a description of <code>SUBSIT</code>.  For example, a situation in which a car engine is non-functioning might have as a sub-situation the situation of a carburetor being misconfigured.")
(comment quantitySubsumes "A specialization of both #$quantityIntersects and #$intangibleParts, this predicate relates a given numerically measurable #$ScalarInterval to those scalar intervals that it subsumes or that \"fall completely inside\" it.    <code>(#$quantitySubsumes SUPER SUB)</code> means that both (i) the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>SUPER</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo the maximum of <code>SUB</code> and (ii) the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>SUPER</code> is #$lessThanOrEqualTo the minimum of <code>SUB</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$Meter 2 5) -- the #$Distance of between 2 and 5 meters inclusive -- subsumes both (#$Meter 3 4) and (#$Meter 3 5), but not (#$Meter 3 6).")
(comment IncarnationPhysicalFn "A function which returns the set of individuals that collectively comprise the physical incarnations of the stated divine being. Thus, in the #$ChristianityMt, #$JesusOfNazareth is the physical incarnation of both #$JesusChrist (the divine being representing the son of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob) and the physical incarnation of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob itself. Likewise, the resurrected Christ who appeared to the apostles is also a physical incarnation of #$JesusChrist. This introduces issues of the holy Trinity that should be investigated further in Cyc.
<p>
Religions which believe in reincarnation may also be axiomatized with this function, if one considers sentient animals as physical incarnations of a non-physical 'soul', where soul denotes a #$DivineBeing. Since some religions believe that people can be reincarnated as lower animals, the result of #$IncarnationPhysicalFn must be specified (hence it is a binary function).  Thus, we can tell Cyc that B is a reincarnation of A if there exists some divine-being (call it a soul) such that A and B are both physical incarnations of this being, and B is born after A dies (see #$Reincarnation)")
(comment PerFn "A binary #$UnitOfMeasureDenotingFunction (q.v.) that takes two #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) functions and returns a #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.) composed of them.  <code>(#$PerFn UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> is a unit <code>RATIO-FN</code> for measuring the ratio of <code>UNIT1</code> units to <code>UNIT2</code> units.   That is, for any number or other #$NumericInterval <code>NUM</code>, <code>(RATIO-FN NUM)</code> = <code>(#$QuotientFn (UNIT1 NUM) (UNIT2 1))</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$PerFn #$Meter #$SecondsDuration) is a #$UnitOfSpeed function that measures meters-per-second.  When applied to (e.g.) the integer 20, this unit-of-speed returns the particular #$Speed equal to
<pre>
  (#$QuotientFn (#$Meter 20) (#$SecondsDuration 1))
</pre>
or 20 meters-per-second.
<p>
Note that <code>UNIT1</code> and <code>UNIT2</code> must <i>not</i> be #$interconvertibleUnits (q.v.), or <code>(#$PerFn UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> is #$undefined.  Otherwise, <code>UNIT1</code> and <code>UNIT2</code> would effectively \"cancel each other out\", and the value returned by #$PerFn for those arguments would not be a #$UnitOfRatio.
<p>
See also #$UnitProductFn.")
(comment dateOfEvent "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that is a specialization of both #$startingDate and #$endingDate (qq.v.).  #$dateOfEvent relates an event to a #$Date on or in which it transpired.  <code>(#$dateOfEvent EVENT DATE)</code> means that <code>EVENT</code> both starts and ends during <code>DATE</code>.  For example, the sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$dateOfEvent
        #$GettysburgAddress-Speech
        (#$DayFn 19 (#$MonthFn #$November (#$YearFn 1863))))
</pre>
<p>
is true because Lincoln both started and finished that address on the date mentioned.
<p>
See also #$occursDuring.")
(comment startTogetherInSituationType "<code>(#$startTogetherInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> start at the same time.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$startTogetherInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment transitiveViaArg "A #$MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(#$transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)</code> means that the <code>N</code>th argument position of <code>PRED</code> is \"transitive\" with respect to <code>BINPRED</code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)</code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)</code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)</code>.  For example, (#$transitiveViaArg #$relationAllExists #$genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (#$relationAllExists #$anatomicalParts #$Mammal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (#$genlPreds #$anatomicalParts #$physicalParts) it follows that (#$relationAllExists #$physicalParts #$Mammal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also #$transitiveViaArgInverse.")
(comment perpetrator "An #$AgentiveRole and a specialization of #$performedBy. <code>(#$perpetrator ACTION PERP)</code> means that the #$SocialBeing <code>PERP</code> is a perpetrator of the arguably criminal <code>ACTION</code>.  More  precisely: <code>PERP</code> performs <code>ACTION</code>, where <code>ACTION</code> is likely to  be considered criminal by some observers (or at least would  be so considered in some contexts).  Note that the first argument of #$perpetrator is not constrained (see #$argIsa) to #$CriminalActs, since an act that counts as criminal in one context might not count as criminal in another context, and yet we might still want to talk about the \"perpetrator\" of that act in the latter context.")
(comment hasBeenIn "A predicate linking a thing to its prior history; <code>(#$hasBeenIn OBJECT PLACE)</code> means that the thing <code>OBJECT</code> has at one time during its lifetime been in (in the general sense of #$inRegion) the thing <code>PLACE</code>.")
(comment arg1Isa "A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg1Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the first argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG<sub>1</sub> ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$isa ARG<sub>1</sub> COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment constituents "The predicate #$constituents is used to indicate a particular #$PartiallyTangible which makes up another (possibly non-distinct) #$PartiallyTangible thing.  <code>(#$constituents WHL PART)</code> means that the individual object <code>WHL</code> is partially constituted by <code>PART</code>, and <code>PART</code> is more or less uniformly distributed in <code>WHL</code>.  For example, the two teaspoons of chocolate syrup that I put in my milk become #$constituents of my glass of chocolate milk.  Note that #$constituents does not entail any special kind of association or bond among the constituents of a thing; they might be simply mixed, they might be chemically bonded, and they might be part of some complex structure.")
(comment Injury-Object "Mechanical damage to (a portion of) the body.
<p>
See #$IncurringAnInjury for the action of incurring an injury, and #$InjuryCondition for the condition of being injured.")
(comment purposeOfEvent "When <code>(#$purposeOfEvent EVENT GOAL)</code> is asserted within the context of a           particular planning context, this means that the purpose of <code>EVENT</code> within the           planning context is <code>GOAL</code>. What this implies will vary from context to context:           however there will often be the entailment that rational agents in the context who are           tasked with performing the <code>EVENT</code> in question will themselves have <code>GOAL</code> as a           goal (see #$goals). For the present, we remain agnostic as to how an event may inherit a           'purpose', apart from stipulating that <code>(#$purposeInEvent AGENT EVENT GOAL)</code>           implies <code>(#$purposeOfEvent EVENT GOAL)</code>. However, it seems fairly certain that           <code>(#$purposeOfEvent EVENT GOAL)</code> implies that there is an Agent performing <code>EVENT</code>           who has <code>GOAL</code> as a purpose: for this reason the first argument is constrained to be a           #$PurposefulAction.  Also, see  #$purposeOfEvent-GoalType.")
(comment FlowRate "A specialization of #$Rate and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.).
Each instance of #$FlowRate is a #$Volume taken with respect to (i.e. divided by; see #$QuotientFn) an amount of time (see #$Time-Quantity).  For example, (#$CubicFootPerSecond 10) -- the flow rate of 10 cubic feet per second -- might be the  rate at which the water in a given stream is flowing.
<p>
See also #$UnitOfFlowRate.")
(comment children "A #$FamilyRelationSlot and specialization of #$coreRelatives that relates  a given person or other animal to each of his or her children.   <code>(#$children PARENT CHILD)</code> means that <code>PARENT</code> fulfills the role of parent  with respect to <code>CHILD</code>.  Note that, in the case of humans, <code>PARENT</code>'s  being one of <code>CHILD</code>'s biological parents (see #$biologicalOffspring) is  neither necessary nor sufficient for this.  Thus, <code>CHILD</code> might be an adopted child (see #$adoptedChildren), stepchild, or foster child of  <code>PARENT</code>'s.  Conversely, a biological parent who has given a child up for  adoption would <i>not</i> normally be related to that child by the predicate  #$children.  In the case of nonhuman animals (see #$NonPersonAnimal),  however, #$children holds between two given creatures if and only if  #$biologicalOffspring does.")
(comment VeryHighAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$VeryHighAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>very high amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$Happiness) is a high amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$veryHighAmountOf.")
(comment unitMultiplicationFactor "A ternary #$MetaFunction predicate that relates two interconvertible #$UnitOfMeasures (q.v.) to the real number by which the larger unit is a multiple of the smaller.  <code>(#$unitMultiplicationFactor SMALLUNIT BIGUNIT N)</code> means that there are <code>N SMALLUNIT</code>s in one <code>BIGUNIT</code>.  In other words, to convert from a measure <code>BIGUNIT</code>s to a measure in <code>SMALLUNIT</code>s, multiply by <code>N</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$unitMultiplicationFactor #$Inch #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure 12) entails that, when converting from feet to inches, one multiplies the number of feet by twelve.
<p>
Note that <code>SMALLUNIT</code> and <code>BIGUNIT</code> must be instances of the same #$InterconvertibleUnitType (q.v.), and that <code>N</code> must be greater than or equal to 1.
<p>
See also #$QuantityConversionFn.")
(comment propositionalInfoAbout "A binary #$IntangibleObjectPredicate that relates intangible \"information stores\" (see #$AspatialInformationStore) to the things they are \"about\".  (#$propositionalInfoAbout AIS THING)  means that the AIS is or contains propositional information about THING.  For instance, AIS might be a #$Microtheory with assertions concerning THING or a #$TextualPCW containing information about THING.  This relation constitutes a kind of \"intentionality\" in the philosophers' sense (see e.g. #$IntentionalMentalSituation), though some would argue that it is only an indirect or derived sort of intentionality in that it always presupposes an underlying intention on the part of some agent(s).
<p>
Although the notion of a written text or other sort of conceptual work being (propositionally) \"about\" something is a completely familiar one -- it is regularly appealed to in everyday discourse as well as in academic studies  pertaining to meaning or interpretation -- giving a precise analysis of the notion (in the form of necessary and sufficient conditions, e.g.) is a  notoriously difficult task, shot-through with controversy.  Still, a couple of non-contentious points can be made in this direction.  First, it is clear that the information in question must be sufficiently specific to the thing it is \"about\".  That is, in order for AIS to \"contain propositional information about\" THING it is _not_ sufficient that AIS merely involve general information about the elements of some collection (or set or class) to which THING belongs, but must involve some way of singling-out THING, e.g. by mentioning THING by name or containing a description that uniquely applies to THING.  For example, the sentence 'Dogs are carnivores' is too general to  be \"about\" Lassie (though it _is_ arguably about the collections #$Dog and  #$Carnivore); 'Television's best-loved canine was a collie', on the other hand, is certainly about her.  Second, since the information must be _propositional_ (see e.g. #$Proposition), it is also _not_ sufficient that AIS merely contain a term or symbol that specifies or denotes or represents THING; AIS must \"say something about\" THING.  Thus, a mere unannotated list of the names of famous show-biz animals would _not_ be propositionally about Lassie, even though her name would undoubtedly appear on it.  Finally, note that #$propositionalInfoAbout does _not_ insist that THING is what AIS is mainly or focally about; thus the selfsame AIS might easily be about several (even many) different things at the same time.")
(comment actors "The most general instance of #$ActorSlot (q.v.): all other actor slots are specializations of this predicate.  Thus #$actors is a broad relation that holds between a given event and any contemporary existing thing (see #$SomethingExisting) that is meaningfully involved in the event. <code>(#$actors EVENT ACTOR)</code> means that <code>ACTOR</code> is somehow saliently (directly or indirectly) involved in <code>EVENT</code> during <code>EVENT</code>.  An object's merely being cotemporal (see #$cotemporal) with an event is obviously not sufficient for being an actor in it, nor is participating (however centrally) in a representation or re-enactment of the event.  But note that being an \"actor\" in the relevant sense is <i>not</i> restricted to things that play \"active\" (as opposed to passive or instrumental) roles in a given event.")
(comment relationInstanceExistsRange "<code>(#$relationInstanceExistsRange PRED INS1 COL2 EXTENT)</code> means that there exist <code>EXTENT</code> many instances (e.g., <code>INS2)</code> of <code>COL2</code> that have the relation <code>PRED</code> with <code>INS1</code>, i.e. <code>(PRED INS1 INS2)</code> holds. Thus, (#$relationInstanceExistsRange #$legalOwnerOf #$Blake #$RaceCar #$AFew-Quant) means simply \"Blake owns a few race cars.\".")
(comment ReciprocalFn "An instance of both #$UnaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction.  When applied to an instance SCALAR of #$ScalarInterval, #$ReciprocalFn returns an instance RECIPROCAL of #$ScalarInterval that is the reciprocal of SCALAR.  If SCALAR is an instance of #$ScalarPointValue, then RECIPROCAL is an instance of #$ScalarPointValue whose product (see #$TimesFn) with SCALAR is 1.  For instance, (#$ReciprocalFn 2) is 0.5 and (#$ReciprocalFn (#$Meter 2)) is ((#$PerFn #$Unity #$Meter) 0.5), a quantity whose product with (#$Meter 2) is 1.  If SCALAR is a range of values (rather than an instance of #$ScalarPointValue), then RECIPROCAL is also a range of values, with the reciprocal of the maximum value of SCALAR serving as RECIPROCAL's minimum value, and the reciprocal of the minimum value of SCALAR serving as RECIPROCAL's maximum value.  For example, (#$ReciprocalFn (#$Unity 1 2)) (i.e., the reciprocal of the range of numbers from 1 to 2) is (#$Unity 0.5 1).  Note that when SCALAR and RECIPROCAL are ranges of values, the product of SCALAR and RECIPROCAL may not be 1; for example, the product of (#$ReciprocalFn (#$Unity 1 2)) and (#$Unity 1 2) is (#$Unity 0.5 2).  Nevertheless, even when the product of SCALAR and RECIPROCAL is not 1, 1 will lie within the range (see #$valueInRange) of the product of SCALAR and RECIPROCAL.  Finally, note that (#$ReciprocalFn 0) is undefined.")
(comment NonVerbalCommunicating "A collection of information transfer events; a specialization of #$Communicating. Each instance of #$NonVerbalCommunicating is a transmission of information that is not in the form of language. Thus spoken, written, and sign-language communications are excluded.  Specializations of #$NonVerbalCommunicating include #$ShakingHands and #$Cuddling.
<p>
Note that #$MakingAGesture is <i>not</i> a subcollection of #$NonVerbalCommunicating, since instances of #$MakingAGesture are merely information-encodings, upon which communication (even <i>verbal</i> communication, in the case sign-language) might or might not occur).")
(comment homepage "A #$FunctionalSlot that relates anything to the URL of its homepage on the World Wide Web. <code>(#$homepage THING STRING)</code> means that <code>THING</code> has a World Wide Web home page with the #$UniformResourceLocator <code>STRING</code>.  For example, (#$homepage #$NationalFootballLeague \"http://www.nfl.com\") means that if a browser is directed to the URL \"http://www.nfl.com\", it will display the homepage for the NFL.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn SixthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment acquaintedWith "This predicate relates an agent to other agents with whom s/he is acquainted. <code>(#$acquaintedWith AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT1</code> is acquainted with <code>AGENT2</code> in the sense that <code>AGENT1</code> has met, has knowingly come into physical contact with, or has knowingly communicated (bi-directionally) with <code>AGENT2</code>.  Typically, this means that <code>AGENT1</code> is aware of some facts about <code>AGENT2</code> and can recognize <code>AGENT2</code> by appearance, voice, or some other physical characteristic.  Note that #$acquaintedWith is <i>not</i> symmetric: it is possible for <code>AGENT1</code> to be #$acquaintedWith <code>AGENT2</code> and not vice versa.  This would be the case (e.g.) if <code>AGENT2</code> is nonsentient (cf. #$SentientAnimal) or comatose, and thus incapable of recognizing <code>AGENT1</code>.  Another example of asymmetric acquaintance is where <code>AGENT1</code> is \"introduced\" to <code>AGENT2</code> while (the former is) in disguise and using a faked identity.  See also the important symmetric specialization of this predicate, #$mutualAcquaintances.")
(comment arg2Genl "A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg2Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the second argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2</code> ...) is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG2 COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a similar but more flexible ternary  predicate.")
(comment argGenl "A ternary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$argGenl RELN N COL)</code> means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the <code>N</code>th argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ... ARGN ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARGN COL)</code> holds.  For example, <code>(#$argGenl #$AtomFn 1 #$ElementStuff)</code> and <code>(#$argGenl #$argGenl 3 #$Thing)</code> both hold. In the special case where N is 0, #$argGenl is used to state that a certain #$genls constraint applies to <i>each</i> of <code>RELN</code>'s argument-places. Thus, <code>(#$argGenl RELN 0 COL)</code> means that <i>any</i> semantically appropriate argument to <code>RELN</code>, regardless of argument-place, must be a specialization of <code>COL</code>. For example, <code>(#$argGenl #$AtomicCompositionFn 0 (#$GroupFn #$Atom))</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  For similar but less flexible binary predicates, see #$arg1Genls et al. See also #$argIsa.")
(comment firstProperSubSituationTypes "<code>(#$firstProperSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUB-SIT-TYPE)</code> means that one of the first sub-situations in each instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> is of type <code>SUB-SIT-TYPE</code>.")
(comment ibtGenerated "A binary predicate which relates instances of #$IBTGeneration to instances of #$InformationBearingThing. <code>(#$ibtGenerated GEN-EVENT IBT)</code> means that the #$InformationBearingThing <code>IBT</code> (be it a gesture, a novel, a picture projected on a screen or a computer program copy) comes into existence in, or as an immediate consequence of, the event <code>GEN-EVENT</code>.")
(comment HighToVeryHighAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$HighToVeryHighAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>high-to-very-high amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$HighToVeryHighAmountFn #$Happiness) is the range from high to very high happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$highToVeryHighAmountOf.")
(comment ChemicalGeneticIBT "A specialization of #$InformationBearingThing and #$ChemicalObject. Instances of #$ChemicalGeneticIBT are chemical objects that are considered to be bearing information in virtue of their ability to play functional roles in handling, reproduction, transmission and utilization of genetic information. Main specs of #$ChemicalGeneticIBT are
#$Nucleotide, #$Gene-HereditaryUnit and #$PolynucleotideMolecule. See
#$GeneticInformationStructure, #$instantiationOfGIS.
<p>
While the abstract information instantiated by instances of this collection could be instantiated in a non-chemical format, for example a list of letters, numbers and parentheses, a diagram of letters and line segments, a plastic model of balls and sticks, or some other means of conventionally representing the structure of such a chemical object, these other instantiations would not be instances of #$ChemicalGeneticIBT but would be instances of #$SymbolicObject or
#$WrittenCharacterSequence.")
(comment likesObject "An instance of both #$CognitiveAwarenessPredicate and #$LikesPredicate. <code>(#$likesObject AGENT OBJ)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> likes <code>OBJ</code>.  The intended sense  of \"likes\" here is fairly general, encompassing a variety of ways in  which somebody can be said to like various kinds of things.  Typically,  it implies at least that <code>AGENT</code> feels some measure of #$Enjoyment when s/he interacts with <code>OBJ</code>. There are specializations of #$likesObject that give more information about the kind of interaction between <code>AGENT</code> and <code>OBJ</code> that results in #$Enjoyment; see e.g. #$likesAsFriend and #$loves.")
(comment postActors "The predicate #$postActors is used to indicate that a participant in an event continues to exist after that event is over.  <code>(#$postActors EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is a participant in <code>EVENT</code> (so that <code>(#$actors EVENT OBJECT)</code> also holds), and that <code>OBJECT</code> exists after <code>EVENT</code> ends.  #$postActors is a very general instance of #$ActorSlot; it subsumes #$outputsCreated, #$transferredThing, and many others.")
(comment subEvents "The most general instance of #$SubEventPredicate, this predicate relates a given event to the events that are its parts.   <code>(#$subEvents WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is a part, or subevent, of <code>WHOLE</code>. Events can be decomposed into subevents events temporally, spatially, and  in other ways; see the specializations of #$subEvents for more on this.   For decomposing an event in a purely temporal way, see #$subProcesses.")
(comment BiologicalDevelopmentEvent "A collection of events, and a subcollection of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent.  In each instance of #$BiologicalDevelopmentEvent, one or more living things undergo biological changes in which they progress from one developmental stage to another.  For example, the germination of a seed, the opening of a flower, the pupating of a caterpillar, a teenager reaching puberty.  Some borderline non-examples include: Groucho Marx being born, Groucho Marx dying. A notable specialization of this collection is #$BiologicalMaturationEvent.
<p>
Note that the distinction between this collection and #$BiologicalProductionEvent is not always sharp, but instances of #$BiologicalProductionEvent are distinguished insofar as the event in question produces a reasonably distinct entity from start to finish.")
(comment (LeftObjectOfPairFn (PartTypeFn FifthThoracicVertebra VertebralTransverseProcess)) "<p>")
(comment conservativeViaArgInverse "A #$MetaPredicate that is a generalization of #$transitiveViaArgInverse (q.v.) in that it can take <i>any</i> binary predicate (transitive or not) as its second argument.  <code>(#$conservativeViaArgInverse PRED RELN N)</code> means that if both of these hold:
<pre>
  (PRED ... ARGN ... )
  (RELN NEW-ARGN ARGN),
</pre>
<p>
then so does
<pre>
  (PRED ... NEW-ARGN ...)
</pre>
(where the argument-sequence <code>... NEW-ARGN ...</code> is just like <code>... ARGN ...</code> except for having <code>NEW-ARGN</code> in place of <code>ARGN)</code>.
<p>
Note that if <code>RELN</code> is a #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate, the more specialized #$transitiveViaArgInverse can be used instead.  See also #$conservativeViaArg.")
(disjointWith CycLAtomicTerm CycLFormula)
(comment notBeforeInSituationType "<code>(#$notBeforeInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> does not occur before <code>SIT2</code>.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$notBeforeInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment correspondingGenericValuePredicate "A binary #$MetaRelation predicate that links each #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) with its corresponding #$GenericValuePredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$correspondingGenericValuePredicate GENVALFUNC GENVALPRED)</code> means that <code>GENVALFUNC</code> and <code>GENVALPRED</code>, by definition, represent the same \"generic amount\" with respect to arbitrary #$TotallyOrderedQuantityTypes (q.v.).
<p>
For example, this holds: <code>(#$correspondingGenericValuePredicate #$LowAmountFn #$lowAmountOf)</code> .")
(comment argIsa "A ternary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$argIsa RELN N COL)</code> means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the <code>N</code>th argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ... ARG<sub>N</sub> ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$isa ARG<sub>N</sub> COL)</code> holds.  For example, <code>(#$argIsa #$mother 1 #$Animal)</code> and <code>(#$argIsa #$argIsa 3 #$Collection)</code> both hold.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  For similar but less flexible binary predicates, see #$arg1Isa et al. See also #$argGenl.")
(comment awareOf "<code>(#$awareOf AGENT THING)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is aware of the (present or past) existence of <code>THING</code>. At minimum <code>AGENT</code> must have a belief pertaining to <code>THING</code>, one that could be expressed in the form '<code>AGENT</code> believes that X is ...', where 'X' is an expression that refers to <code>THING</code>.")
(comment vulnerableTo-TypeType "<code>(#$vulnerableTo-TypeType THREAT-OBJ-TYPE OBJECT-TYPE HARM-TYPE)</code> means that instances of the collection <code>THREAT-OBJ-TYPE</code> generally pose harm results of type <code>HARM-TYPE</code> to instances of <code>OBJECT-TYPE</code>.  The purpose of this predicate is to represent knowledge about object-object vulnerabilities at the type level.  Note that this is a type-level predicate in the first and second argument places and that care needs to be taken in developing rules that draw #$vulnerableTo conclusions from #$vulnerableTo-TypeType GAFs so that we do not overpopulate the KB with potentially questionable #$vulnerableTo judgments.")
(comment PhysicalTableSchema "A specialization of #$SISPhysicalSchema. Instances of #$PhysicalTableSchema represent physical database tables. They are often referred to as 'physical tables'. The current vocbulary does not distinguish between the abstract structure, perhaps shared by many copies, and the copies themselves, perhaps containing different data.
<p>
When representing a database table, a 'logical schema', an instance of #$LogicalTableSchema, is mapped to a physical schema.  The logical schema is the context for the axioms which interpret the physical schema. The physical schema is associated with a physical database (some instance of #$Database-Physical).")
(comment inRegion "This is a general locative predicate that has a diverse group of specializations.  <code>(#$inRegion OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means, roughly, that <code>OBJ1</code> is located \"at\" or \"in\" <code>OBJ2</code>.  <code>OBJ1</code> might or might not be a part (see #$parts) of <code>OBJ2</code>.  Certain important specializations (and inverse-specializations) of #$inRegion, however, differ on this point.  For example, #$spatiallySubsumes and #$objectFoundInLocation are noncommital on the parthood issue, while #$physicalDecompositions is a specialization of #$parts.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn FirstRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment physicalParts "<code>(#$physicalParts WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>WHOLE</code> is an at least partially tangible object and <code>PART</code> is one of its distinct, non-diffuse, identifiable, partially tangible parts.  This predicate can be used, for example, to relate a dog to its tail, or an automobile to its wheels. Note that any partially tangible object is considered to be a distinct, non-diffuse, identifiable part of itself; consequently, #$physicalParts is an instance of #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate.")
(comment GameSubunit "A collection of individual #$Events.  Each instance of #$GameSubunit is a standard subdivision of a #$GameEvent, such as trick in bridge, a down in American football, or a turn when playing a #$BoardGame.  Each #$GameSubunit has one or more instances of PlayingAGame as #$subEvents, but may have other sorts of significant #$subEvents as well.  For example, a particular quarter in a #$GameEvent of #$FootballGame-American would be an instance of #$GameSubunit.  #$subEvents of the quarter would include, among other things, instances of (#$PlayingFn #$FootballGame-American) performed by instances of #$FootballPlayer-American, instances of #$EvaluatingAnAction performed by a #$Referee, and instances of #$MeasuringSomething performed by some #$SportsOfficial.
<p>
Associated significant #$subEvents of the #$GameEvent are not necessarily #$subEvents of the #$GameSubunit.  For example instances of #$MeasuringSomething performed by some #$SportsOfficial after the down is over are not #$subEvents of the down #$GameSubunit.")
(comment origin-RoundTrip "<code>(#$origin-RoundTrip TRIP PLACE)</code> means that the #$Translation-RoundTrip event <code>TRIP</code> leaves from and returns to the location <code>PLACE</code>.  <code>PLACE</code> is both the #$fromLocation and #$toLocation of the entire <code>TRIP</code>, considered as a whole.  For example, some commuters to Manhattan leave and return each day to #$Connecticut-State, which is their #$origin-RoundTrip.  See also #$destination-RoundTrip.")
(comment BorrowingLibraryItem "A collection of events; a subset of #$BorrowingSomething.  In an instance of #$BorrowingLibraryItem, the borrower (the #$toPossessor) is temporarily granted the right to be the primary user of one particular item which belongs to a library (the #$fromPossessor).  That may or may not include the right to remove it from the library building.  Typically, the borrower is granted such #$PrimaryUserRights for a stipulated lending period, and that limited term is enforced by fines.  The borrower is expected to use the item for its intended function; i.e., the borrower is granted #$PrimaryRoleUseRights (q.v.).")
(comment runsUnderOS "<code>(#$runsUnderOS PROGRAM OS)</code> means that <code>PROGRAM</code> will run on computers that are running the operating system <code>OS</code>.  Note that the first argument position of this predicate is constrained to instances of #$ComputerProgram-CW, so both versioned and unversioned programs can occupy that position.  It is assumed that if the first argument to #$runsUnderOS is an instance of #$UnversionedProgram that all the versions of <code>PROGRAM</code> (via #$versionOfSoftware) also run under <code>OS</code>.  Similarly for the second argument position of this predicate: if <code>OS</code> is also an instance of #$UnversionedProgram, then <code>PROGRAM</code> runs under all versions of <code>OS</code> (via #$versionOfSoftware).")
(comment relationExistsMinInstance "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to a given collection and a given thing.  (#$relationExistsMinInstance BINPRED COL THING N) means that there exists at least N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL that bear the relation PRED to THING such that (PRED INST(i) THING) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$thereExistAtLeast N ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL)
(BINPRED ?INST THING))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationExistsMinInstance #$physicalParts #$Hand MyLeftThumb 1)' means that my left thumb is part of at least one hand.  Cf. #$relationInstanceExistsMin, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment IntrinsicStateChangeEvent "A specialization of #$Event-Localized. Each instance of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent is an event characterized primarily by a change in some intrinsic property of at least one main actor involved in the event.  Such intrinsic changes may include changes of a thing's color, temperature, device state, or size.  Events where the main change is extrinsic (such as a change in location or ownership) are not instances of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent.  In events which have more than one actor, the event may be an #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent for one actor but not for another.  For example, in a #$FasteningSafetyBelt event, the #$SafetySeatBelt (the device used) goes from unconnected to connected (to itself), which is an intrinsic change; however, the agent who does the fastening does not change intrinsically, but only in his configuration to the belt, an external object.  Another example: in a #$HairCuttingEvent, the hair that is cut undergoes an #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent due to its role in that kind of event, but the barber undergoes no intrinsic change due to the hair cutting.
<p>
Note that some events, such as an ice cube melting into a small puddle of water, could be represented either as an #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent or as a #$PhysicalTransformationEvent, but not (within a single micro-theory) as both.")
(comment hasClimateType "The predicate #$hasClimateType relates a particular region to the type of climate had in that region.  <code>(#$hasClimateType AREA CLIME)</code> means that the climate in the #$GeographicalRegion <code>AREA</code> is of the #$ClimateCycleType <code>CLIME</code>.  See also #$ClimateCycleType, #$AnnualClimateCycle.")
(comment products "<code>(#$products EV OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is one of the intended outputs of event <code>EV</code>.  For unintended outputs, see #$byProducts.  For a particular <code>EV</code> and <code>OBJ</code>, it will not be true that both <code>(#$products EV OBJ)</code> and <code>(#$byProducts EV OBJ)</code>.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn NinthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment nonDeliberateCausalActors "<code>(#$nonDeliberateCausalActors EVENT ACTOR)</code> means that <code>ACTOR</code> is the cause or the most important causal factor in bringing about <code>EVENT</code>, but that <code>ACTOR</code> does not act deliberately in so doing.")
(comment properSubSituationTypes "A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a specialization of #$subSituationTypes (q.v.) and a type-level correlate of #$properSubSituations (q.v.). This predicate is used to state that situations of one given type always have situations of another given type as proper subsituations. <code>(#$properSubSituationTypes SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE)</code> means that <code>SITTYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation) has the specialization of #$Situation <code>SUBSITTYPE</code> as a proper subsituation type.")
(comment relationInstanceExistsCount "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that is used to state that a given binary predicate holds between a given thing and a specified number of instances of a given collection. (#$relationInstanceExistsCount BINPRED THING COL N) means that there are exactly N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL such that (BINPRED THING INST(i)) holds (for 0 <= i <= N).  It is equivalent to (and so can be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$thereExistExactly N ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL)
(BINPRED THING ?INST))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationInstanceExistsCount #$geographicalSubRegions #$Iran #$OilField 3)' means that Iran has exactly three oil fields. Cf. #$relationExistsCountInstance, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment centerLineOfPath "(#$centerLineOfPath CENTER PATH) means that CENTER is the centerline of the #$Path-Generic PATH, where the
'centerline' is the one-dimensional spatial object that is
equidisdant from the edges of the path and runs roughly parallel to them.   In the
#$ModernMilitaryTacticsMt, the centerline is a #$Traversal.
<p>")
(comment inhabitantTypes "The predicate #$inhabitantTypes relates an instance of #$GeographicalRegion to a specialization of #$Person.  <code>(#$inhabitantTypes REGION TYPE)</code> means that <code>TYPE</code> is one of the primary types of people living in the #$GeographicalRegion <code>REGION</code>.  <code>TYPE</code> may be based on ethnicity, nationality, age, economics -- in short, on any demographic class.  For example, (#$inhabitantTypes #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$AdultMiddleClassAmerican), (#$inhabitantTypes #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$WorkingAdultAmericanWoman), (#$inhabitantTypes #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$MexicanImmigrantToUSA).")
(comment TimeElapsedFn "A commutative binary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that returns the duration (see #$Time-Quantity) of the time interval between the starting points of two #$Dates.  Thus, sentences of the following form hold:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$duration
    (#$TimeIntervalBetweenFn (#$StartFn DATE1) (#$StartFn DATE2))
    (#$TimeElapsedFn DATE1 DATE2)
</pre>
<p>
For example, (#$TimeElapsedFn (#$YearFn 1990) (#$YearFn 1992)) is equal to (#$YearsDuration 2), the duration of 2 years.  An example of an axiom using this function would be one saying that a female animal generally won't give birth more than once per gestation-period-of-time (for that type of animal).")
(comment culturePractices "This predicate relates human cultures to types of    practices that are characteristic of and important to their participants.  (#$culturePractices CULTURE ACTTYPE ROLE) means that most or many participants in CULTURE play ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE from time to time, and would (at least upon reflection) consider such activities important to or distinctive of CULTURE.  These activities might be ones commonly engaged in on a regular basis (like praying thrice daily), or might be ones normally engaged in only in particular situations or on special occasions (like joining in group singing at parties); all that's essential is that many or most participants in the culture do them sometimes.
<p>
For a similar predicate that applies to practices distinctive of a culture but participated in by only a relatively small, specialized subcollection of its participants, see #$cultureSpecialPractices.
<p>
Note that when using this predicate it is important to avoid unjustified    stereotypes of the culture in question and to refrain from relating the culture to practices that do not accurately characterize it.")
(comment SquareRootFn "A unary #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is the arithmetical operation that takes a (non-negative) number to its non-negative square root, extended to measurable #$ScalarIntervals generally.  <code>(#$SquareRootFn SCALAR)</code> is by definition equal to <code>ROOT</code>: the #$NonNegativeScalarInterval such that <code>(#$TimesFn ROOT ROOT)</code> is equal to <code>SCALAR</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$SquareRootFn 81) is equal to 9 and (#$SquareRootFn (#$SquareMile 9)) is equal to (#$Mile 3).
<p>
Note that while this holds:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric #$SquaredFn #$SquareRootFn) ,
</pre>
<p>
this does <i>not</i>:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric #$SquareRootFn #$SquaredFn) .
</pre>
<p>
For the square of the non-negative square root of <code>X</code> is always <code>X</code>, but the non-negative square root of the square of <code>X</code> is not always <code>X</code> (e.g. where <code>X</code>  is -2).  Thus, #$SquareRootFn and #$SquaredFn might be considered \"quasi-inverses\".")
(comment thereExists "An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) that corresponds to the standard existential quantifier of predicate calculus.  #$thereExists takes as its arguments an #$ELVariable and an #$ELSentence-Assertible (such that, typically, the former occurs free in the latter).  (#$thereExists VAR SENT) means that there is at least one thing THING such that SENT is true of it.  That is, if some given CycL term that denotes THING were substituted for each free occurrence of VAR in SENT, the result would be a true sentence.  For example, `(#$thereExists ?X (#$mother ?X #$GeorgeWBush))' means that George W. Bush has a (i.e. at least one) mother; and
<p>
(#$forAll ?PER
(#$implies
(#$isa ?PER #$Person)
(#$thereExists ?MOM
(#$mother ?MOM ?PER))))
<p>
means that every person has a mother.  Note that some existential statements can be represented more tersely in CycL using other instances of #$ExistentialQuantifier, such as #$thereExistAtMost or #$thereExistExactly.  Also see the #$cyclistNotes for #$thereExists.")
(comment mtCultureBeliefs "<code>(#$mtCultureBeliefs MT CULTURE)</code> means that <code>MT</code> contains assertions having the propositional content of the beliefs that  characterize <code>CULTURE</code> (see #$cultureBeliefs).")
(comment forAll "A binary #$Quantifier that corresponds to the standard universal quantifier of predicate calculus.  The relation #$forAll takes as its arguments a variable (#$ELVariable) and a sentence (#$ELSentence-Assertible) in which, typically, that variable occurs free.  A closed formula (see #$CycLClosedFormula) of the form (#$forAll VAR SENT) is a \"universally quantified\" sentence that states (roughly) that every thing in the universe of discourse satisfies SENT.  For example,
<p>
<pre>
  (#$forAll ?THING (#$isa ?THING #$Individual))
</pre>
<p>
means that everything is an individual.  And the sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$forAll ?X
    (#$implies
      (#$isa ?X #$Cat)
      (#$eatsWillingly ?X #$Grass-Plant)))
</pre>
<p>
means that all cats eat grass.
<p>
A more precise definition of #$forAll is as follows.  Any occurence of VAR that is free in SENT is bound in (#$forAll VAR SENT).  If VAR is the only variable free in SENT, then (#$forAll VAR SENT) is a closed sentence that is true if and only if every thing that meets all of the argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) to which the position(s) in SENT occupied by VAR is (are) subject to satisfies SENT. If the variable VAR does _not_ occur free in SENT, then (#$forAll VAR SENT) is equivalent to SENT (and the '#$forAll' is said to be \"vacuous\").  If variables other than VAR occur free in SENT, then (#$forAll VAR SENT) is an open sentence in which those other variables are still free.
<p>
Note that, for the sake of convenience and readability, universally quantified assertions (i.e. \"rules\") in the Cyc Knowledge Base appear in the KB browser -- and may be asserted to the KB -- without (any of) their initial '#$forAll's.  Such assertions are considered to be \"implicitly quantified\": they are interpreted by the system just as if their initial quantifiers were explicitly present.  Thus the above sentence about cats would actually appear in the KB as:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?X #$Cat)
    (#$eatsWillingly ?X #$Grass-Plant)) .
</pre>
<p>
Note also that certain #$RuleMacroPredicates (e.g. those with 'All' in their names) can be used to abbreviate universally quantified sentences.  Thus the same statement about cats can be even more tersely expressed with the atomic sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$relationAllInstance #$eatsWillingly #$Cat #$Grass-Plant) .
</pre>
<p>
Cf. the existential quantifier #$thereExists.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn EighthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment physicalDecompositions "<code>(#$physicalDecompositions WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is a spatial part or component of <code>WHOLE</code>, in a very broad sense of 'part' whereby <code>PART</code> might or might not be spatially continuous or discrete.  Thus <code>PART</code> might be discontinuous, or even diffused throughout <code>WHOLE</code>.")
(comment elInverse "A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate and a specialization of #$genlInverse that relates a binary EL-only (see #$ELRelation-OneWay) predicate with its non-EL-only \"inverse correlate\".   (#$elInverse PRED EL-PRED) means that whenever a sentence of the form (EL-PRED ARG1 ARG2) is used to make an assertion, the #$CycCanonicalizer \"transforms\" it into the logically equivalent form (PRED ARG2 ARG1), and the latter is what actually gets stored in the Knowledge Base.
<p>
For example, (#$genlInverse #$genls #$specs) entails that if I assert the sentence '(#$specs #$Animal #$Cat)', what gets stored in the KB is the sentence '(#$genls #$Cat #$Animal)'.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn TenthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment informationOrigin "A specialization of #$informationTerminal  (q.v.) that is used to indicate the source of information for a particular  #$InformationTransferEvent (q.v.).  <code>(#$informationOrigin TRANSFER ORIGIN)</code>  means that the information being transferred in <code>TRANSFER</code> comes from <code>ORIGIN</code>. <code>ORIGIN</code> is either an instance of #$InformationBearingThing or an agent.   Note that if <code>ORIGIN</code> still exists after the ending of <code>TRANSFER</code>, it  presumably also still contains the information that was transferred.")
(comment constructActionType "(#$constructActionType NAME SCRIPTTYPE (#$TheList ACTORSLOT ...)) means construct a
function that is a #$CollectionDenotingFunction, whose name is NAME and whose arguments must be appropriate for
assertions of the form (ACTORSLOT ?x@SCRIPTTYPE <argument>).  By having this predicate actions in plans
can be stated more teresely in an active form, and we should be able to do plan and plan-recognition level reasoning
over such GAFs. What happens as a result of asserting this is a bit of SubL gets called to actually add a new
action-type constructor named NAME.
<p>
The effect of the #$afterAdding is to assert (for the following sample)
Add: (#$constructActionType #$HitsFn #$HittingAnObject (#$TheList #$doneBy #$objectActedOn))
<p>
Results in:
(#$isa #$HitsFn #$BinaryFunction); from number of items in #$TheList
(#$isa #$HitsFn #$CollectionDenotingFunction) ; because it denotes a type
(#$resultGenl #$HitsFn #$HittingAnObject)
(#$arg1Isa #$HitsFn #$SomethingExisting) ; arg2Isa from the #$doneBy
(#$arg2Isa #$HitsFn #$SomethingExisting) ; arg2Isa from the #$objectActedOn
(#$expansion #$HitsFn
(#$CollectionSubsetFn #$HittingAnObject
(#$TheSetOf ?hitting (#$and (#$doneBy ?hitting :arg1)
(#$objectActedOn ?hitting :arg2)))))")
(comment EventPredicate "#$EventPredicate is a collection of predicates that are used to make assertions about events. The representation of events in terms of #$EventPredicates is an alternative to the Davidsonian representation of events (so-called after the philosopher Donald Davidson) in which the \"participants\" in  events are related to the reified individual events in which they participate (for the representational approach, see #$Role, #$ActorSlot, and #$SubEventPredicate). The first argument to an event predicate will always be the \"agentive force\" in the event. For instance, in the event described by the formula
<p>
<code>(#$transports LOTUSBLOSSUM099 OBJECT056 FROM TO VIA)</code> ,
<p>
LOTUSBLOSSUM099 is the agentive force -- it is what transports OBJECT056.
<p>
Note that the agentive force need not be a volitional or intelligent agent; it may just be a causal agent.  For example, a bacterium (or a mob of bacteria) can be the agentive force in an infection.")
(comment MediaSeriesByDateFn "(#$MediaSeriesByDateFn MEDIA_SERIES_PRODUCT DATE), given MEDIA_SERIES_PRODUCT (a #$MediaSeriesProduct) and DATE (a #$Date), returns the member of this series that was distributed via the mass media on that date.
<p>
Each regularly distributed #$MediaSeriesProduct, such as a #$PeriodicalSeries or a #$TVSeriesProduct, has a series of #$MediaProducts as #$subWorks.  These #$subWorks can be ordered by date of media distribution of instantiations of the works.  Instances of #$BroadcastMediaProduct often have different instantiations distributed on different date.  Thus for a  #$TVSeriesProduct that has reruns, the #$MediaSeriesByDateFn of that series for several dates would be the same.
<p>
<p>
For instance, (#$MediaSeriesByDateFn #$TheNewYorkTimes-TheNewspaper (#$DayFn 15 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 1998)))) would represent the #$NewspaperIssue comprising the 1/15/98 edition of The New York Times. Compare #$MediaSeriesNthFn, which specifies the nth #$MediaSeriesProduct of a series published by the date of ORIGINAL publication -- i.e., the date when the first instantiation was published.")
(comment lastProperSubSituationTypes "<code>(#$lastProperSubSituationTypes SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE)</code> means that one of the last sub-situation types in <code>SITTYPE</code> is of type <code>SUBSITTYPE</code>.  Note that a situation type may have more than one final sub-situation type because the sub-situation types are only partially ordered by type-level quasi-temporal relations.")
(comment employees "A specialization of #$hasWorkers (q.v.) that relates employers to their  paid employees.  <code>(#$employees EMPLOYER WORKER)</code> means <code>WORKER</code> regularly  performs work for <code>EMPLOYER</code>, and <code>EMPLOYER</code> pays <code>WORKER</code> for that work (often  by paycheck).  <code>EMPLOYER</code> directs the manner in which <code>WORKER</code> performs the  work and might provide the workplace, tools, materials, capital, or other  assistance for the work.  <code>EMPLOYER</code> is commonly an organization but may be  a person; e.g. (#$employees PerryMason PaulDrake).  Note that in many contexts the use of this predicate require proper temporal qualification;  e.g. (#$ist (#$MtSpace #$PeopleDataMt (#$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn (#$YearFn 1999) #$TimePoint))) (#$employees #$NBC-TVNetwork #$KatieCouric)).")
(comment StudyOfFn "<code>(#$StudyOfFn THING)</code> denotes the collection of activities in which <code>THING</code> is studied.  The instances in these collections are described in common English as instances of 'the study of <code>THING</code>'. <code>(#$StudyOfFn THING)</code> may well include direct encounters with <code>THING</code>, and need have nothing to do with #$Education. #$StudyOfFn is similar to, but more general than, #$ResearchInFn.  For example, reading extant material about <code>THING</code> would generally be included in <code>(#$StudyOfFn THING)</code>, while it may well not be in <code>(#$ResearchInFn THING)</code>.")
(comment parts "This predicate relates individuals to their (individual) \"parts\", where this is understood in a very broad sense that includes spatial parts, temporal parts, \"conceptual\" parts, members of groups, and so forth.  <code>(#$parts WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is in some sense a part of <code>WHOLE</code>.  Note that <code>PART</code> need not be a <i>proper</i> part of <code>WHOLE</code>: #$parts is a #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate. (For representing proper parthood - see #$properParts.) Important specializations of #$parts include the predicates #$physicalParts, #$subEvents, #$timeSlices, #$subInformation, and #$groupMembers.")
(comment Unity "An instance of #$ZeroDimensionalUnitOfMeasure (q.v.) that takes one or two numbers or other #$NumericIntervals (q.v.) as argument(s) and returns the continuous numeric-interval that spans between and includes those arguments.  Given one argument, #$Unity behaves like an identity function.
<p>
More precisely: for any numeric-interval <code>NUM</code>, <code>(#$Unity NUM)</code> = <code>NUM</code>; and for any two numeric-intervals <code>LO</code> and <code>HI</code> such that <code>(#$greaterThanOrEqualTo HI LO)</code>, <code>(#$Unity LO HI)</code> is the numeric-interval that ranges from the least point-value (see #$Number-General) subsumed by (see #$quantitySubsumes) <code>LO</code> to the greatest point-value subsumed by <code>HI</code>.
<p>
In practice, <code>LO</code> and <code>HI</code> will typically be two distinct #$RealNumbers, and <code>(#$Unity LO HI)</code> will be the continuous #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine that ranges from <code>LO</code> to <code>HI</code>.  For example, (#$Unity 1 10) is the interval from 1 to 10 inclusive.  But note that <code>LO</code> and/or <code>HI</code> might themselves be proper intervals: (#$Unity (#$Unity 7 9) 11) is thus the interval from 7 to 11 inclusive.
<p>
#$Unity is classified as a #$UnitOfMeasure mainly in order to simplify the application of quasi-mathematical operations (e.g. #$PerFn, #$UnitProductFn) to units-of-measure generally.  For example, #$TimesPerDay is defined as the result of \"dividing\" (via #$PerFn) #$Unity by #$DaysDuration.")
(comment transitiveViaArgInverse "A #$MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to the inverse of a given binary predicate.  <code>(#$transitiveViaArgInverse PRED BINPRED N)</code> means that the <code>N</code>th argument position of <code>PRED</code> is \"transitive\" with respect to the inverse of <code>BINPRED</code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED</code> ... <code>ARGN</code> ...) and <code>(BINPRED ARGN-PRIME ARGN)</code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED</code> ... <code>ARGN-PRIME</code> ...).  For example,(#$transitiveViaArgInverse #$relationAllExists #$genls 2) holds; thus from (#$relationAllExists #$anatomicalParts #$Mammal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (#$genls #$Horse #$Mammal) it follows that (#$relationAllExists #$anatomicalParts #$Horse #$Head-AnimalBodyPart).  See also #$transitiveViaArg.")
(comment temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType "A #$TemporalPredicate and a specialization of #$temporallyIntersects-TypeType (q.v.) that is a type-level inverse correlate of #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.).  This predicate is used to state that things of one given type are always temporally subsumed by things of another given type.  (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType SHORTTYPE LONGTYPE) means that for every instance SHORTINST of SHORTTYPE, there is an instance LONGINST of LONGTYPE such that (#$temporallySubsumes LONGINST SHORTINST).
<p>
For example, (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType #$AIDS #$HIVPositive) means that every case of having AIDS is temporally subsumed by a case of being HIV+.
<p>
See also #$temporallySubsumes-TypeType; and note that (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType LONGTYPE SHORTTYPE) is neither necessary nor sufficient for (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType SHORTTYPE LONGTYPE).")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn TenthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment DistributingMetaKnowledgePredicate "A subcollection of #$MetaKnowledgePredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$DistributingMetaKnowledgePredicate is a meta-knowledge predicate that can be used to make meta-assertions (i.e it can take a #$CycLAssertion as an argument) and is such that, when applied to an EL-level assertion that polycanonicalizes (i.e. gets transformed by the Cyc canonicalizer into multiple HL-level assertions), it is automatically \"distributed\" over each of the multiple assertions resulting from the polycanonicalization.
<p>
Let METAPRED be an instance of #$DistributingMetaKnowledgePredicate. Suppose that one asserts <code>(METAPRED ASSERTION FOO)</code> and that <code>ASSERTION</code> polycanonicalizes into <code>ASSERTION1</code> and <code>ASSERTION2</code>.  Then <code>(METAPRED ASSERTION1 FOO)</code> and <code>(METAPRED ASSERTION2 FOO)</code> will both be asserted to the Knowledge Base automatically.
<p>
For example, if I assert this at the EL-level:
<pre>
  (#$comment
    (#$and (#$isa #$Muffet #$Dog) (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Patches-Cat))
    \"Muffet digs Patches.\"),
</pre>
<p>
both of these will automatically be asserted a the HL-level:
<p>
    <code>(#$comment (#$isa #$Muffet #$Dog) \"Muffet digs Patches.\")</code>
<p>
    <code>(#$comment (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Patches-Cat) \"Muffet digs Patches.\")</code> .
<p>
See also the shared note #$MetaAssertionsForPolycanonicalizingAssertions.")
(comment interArgIsa1-2 "A ternary instance of #$InterArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$interArgIsa1-2 RELN INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL DEPENDENT-ARG-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> appears as the first argument to <code>RELN</code>, a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness is that the second argument must be an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>.  That is, if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>, then <code>(RELN INST ARG2 ... ARGN)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>ARG2</code> is an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$interArgIsa1-2 #$performedBy #$PurposefulAction #$IntelligentAgent) means that, if <code>ACT</code> is a purposeful action, then (#$performedBy <code>ACT AGENT</code>) is semantically well-formed only if <code>AGENT</code> is an intelligent agent.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations.  See #$interArgIsa for a similar but more general predicate that is quintary, allowing the user to specify the positions of the independent and dependent arguments.
<p>
Use #$interArgCondIsa1-2 to conclude that arg2 is an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> instead of having that as a constraint.")
(comment relationInstanceExistsMany "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relationship with respect to a given thing and a given collection.  (#$relationInstanceExistsMany BINPRED THING COL) means that there are many instances INST(i) of COL such that (BINPRED THING INST(i)) holds.  It is equivalent to (#$relationInstanceExistsRange BINPRED THING COL #$Many-Quant), and thus to the more complex form (for which it can in effect be used as shorthand):
<p>
(#$thereExistRange #$Many-Quant ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL2
(BINPRED THING ?INST))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationInstanceExistsMany #$anatomicalParts #$Patches-Cat #$Hair-Strand)' means that Patches has many hairs.  See also #$relationInstanceExistsMin, #$relationInstanceExistsMax, #$relationInstanceExistsCount, and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment relationExistsMaxInstance "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to a given collection and a given thing.  (#$relationExistsMaxInstance BINPRED COL THING N) means that there exists at most N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL that bear the relation PRED to THING such that (PRED INST(i) THING) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$thereExistAtMost N ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL)
(BINPRED ?INST THING)))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationExistsMaxInstance #$physicalParts #$Hand MyLeftThumb 1)' means that my left thumb is part of at most one hand.  Cf. #$relationInstanceExistsMax, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment movieActors "A binary predicate that relates a given #$Movie-CW to each agent who acted in that movie.  <code>(#$movieActors MOVIE ACTOR)</code> means that <code>ACTOR</code> played a role in <code>MOVIE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$movieActors #$CitizenKane-TheMovie #$OrsonWelles)</code> holds.")
(comment Rate "A specialization of #$PhysicalQuantity and #$RatioQuantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Rate is a multi-dimensional quantity made up of some quantity (e.g. distance traveled or money spent), taken with respect to -- i.e. divided by (see #$QuotientFn) -- a quantity of time (see #$Time-Quantity).
<p>
Specializations of #$Rate include #$Speed (e.g. 55 mph), #$Frequency (e.g. 55 kHz), and #$MonetaryRate (e.g. 55 cents per minute).
<p>
See also #$UnitOfRate, the collection of functions used to measure rates (e.g. #$DollarsPerYear, #$MetersPerSecond).")
(comment relationAllExists "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relation to two collections. <code>(#$relationAllExists BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code>, there exists some instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)
    (#$thereExists ?INST2
      (#$and
        (#$isa ?INST2 COL2)
        (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)))).
</pre>
<p>
For example, <code>(#$relationAllExists #$temporalBoundsContain #$CalendarWeek #$Wednesday)</code> means that every calendar week contains a Wednesday.  See also #$relationExistsAll and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment fromLocation "An #$ActorSlot that is a specialization of #$preActors (q.v.).  This predicate is used to indicate the starting point of a particular movement from one place to another.  <code>(#$fromLocation MOVE LOC)</code> means that <code>LOC</code> is where some salient moving-object (see #$objectMoving) in the #$Translocation <code>MOVE</code> is found at the beginning of <code>MOVE</code> and is where it begins this motion.  This moving-object might or might not be #$Stationary at <code>LOC</code> at the beginning of <code>MOVE</code>.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a single-pathway translation (see #$Translation-SingleTrajectory), then each of its salient moving-objects is found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> starts.  If <code>MOVE</code> has multiple movers and multiple pathways (see #$Translation-MultiTrajectory), then at least some of the moving-objects can be found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> starts.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a flow (such as a river flowing, a wind blowing, a tornado, a typhoon, some clouds moving, or air filling your lungs when you take a breath; see #$FluidFlow-Translation), then at least some portion of the fluid moving-object can be found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> starts.  See also #$motionPathway-Complete and #$pathConnects.")
(comment negationInverse "A #$MetaPredicate for stating that each of two binary predicates is a logical \"contrary\" of the other's inverse. <code>(#$negationInverse BINPRED1 BINPRED2)</code> means that if <code>BINPRED1</code> holds between a pair <code><THING1, THING2></code>, then <code>BINPRED2</code> does <i>not</i> hold between the inverse pair <code><THING2, THING1></code> (and vice versa).  In other words, <code>(#$negationInverse BINPRED1 BINPRED2)</code> is equivalent to <code>(#$not (#$and (BINPRED1 ARG1 ARG2) (BINPRED2 ARG2 ARG1)))</code>.
<p>
Note that any #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate is related to itself by #$negationInverse.  For example, (#$negationInverse #$subordinates #$subordinates) holds, since if one person is a subordinate of another, the latter person cannot at the same time also be a subordinate of the former.  See also #$negationPreds and #$genlInverse.")
(comment MonetaryValue "A specialization of #$ScalarInterval. Each instance of this collection is a value by means of which payments, wealth, capital, assets, etc., are measured.  Each instance of this collection is thus an abstract object, not to be confused with the physical objects used as legal tender. For those, see #$Currency, though it is worth noting that each coin, bill, etc. that is an instance of #$Currency, is typically _stably associated with_ some particular instance of #$MonetaryValue.
<p>
Nor is #$MonetaryValue to be confused with the many and varied social practices (such as a backed claim for a certain amount of physical wealth payable by the government on demand), which create money qua social institution and underpin all financial transactions. For that, see #$Money-DevisedPractice.
<p>
Since instances of #$MonetaryValue are not pure numbers but quantities of some particular currency, they are denoted by means of the collection #$UnitOfMoney. Each instance of this collection is a function which (like many Cyc functions which are instances of #$UnitOfMeasure) either takes one argument and returns a fixed amount of money, e.g. (#$Dollar-Australia 4) or takes two arguments and returns a range, e.g. (#$Shilling-Uganda 2 2000). Since currencies may appreciate or depreciate in value over time, and this information is sometimes relevant, it is possible to index instances of #$UnitOfMoney temporally by means of further functions. For example, ((#$USDollarFn 1997) 2) denotes the monetary value of 2 US dollars in the year 1997. See also #$MoneyTransfer, #$monetaryValueOfThing, #$cost, #$basicPrice.")
(comment relationAllExistsCount "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that is a specialization of both #$relationAllExistsMin and #$relationAllExistsMax (qq.v.).  <code>(#$relationAllExistsCount BINPRED COL1 COL2 N)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL1</code> there are exactly <code>N</code> instances <code>INST1</code>, ..., <code>INSTn</code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST INSTi)</code> holds (for 1 <code><= i <= N)</code>. It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?INST COL1)
    (#$thereExistExactly N ?INST-I
      (#$and
        (#$isa ?INST-I COL2)
        (BINPRED ?INST ?INST-I))))
</pre>
<p>
(wherein the variable <code>?INST</code> is interpreted as if it were bound by an initial universal quantifier).
<p>
For example, <code>(#$relationAllExistsCount #$anatomicalParts #$Dog #$Leg 4)</code> means that all dogs have four legs.
<p>
Cf. #$relationExistsCountAll, and see the #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment Relation "A specialization of #$MathematicalObject and the collection of all relations.  Each instance of #$Relation is a relation that can hold among one or more things, depending on whether the relation is unary, binary, ternary, or whatever (see #$arity and #$relationalArity).  A unary relation (such as #$unknownSentence) is a sort of degenerate case that holds of  certain individual things (in this case, all sentences that are unknown to Cyc).  A binary relation (such as #$likesAsFriend) relates one thing to another (in this case, it relates one sentient animal to another just in case the first likes the second).  A ternary relation relates certain triples of things.  And so on.  There are also relations of no particular fixed arity; see #$VariableArityRelation.
<p>
Names of relations can be used to construct sentences and other formulas.  More precisely, CycL terms that denote #$Relations can appear in the \"0th\" argument (or \"arg0\") position of a #$CycLFormula (q.v.), i.e. as the term immediately following the formula's opening parenthesis.
<p>
An important subcollection of #$Relation is #$TruthFunction (q.v.), whose instances are intimately related to truth-values, as reflected in the fact that the CycL expressions that denote truth-functions can appear in the arg0 position of a #$CycLSentence; and a sentence (if quantificationally closed; see #$CycLClosedSentence), will generally be either true or false (with respect to a given context or interpretation).  The major subcollections of #$TruthFunction are #$Predicate, #$LogicalConnective, and #$Quantifier (qq.v.).
<p>
Another important subcollection of #$Relation is #$Function-Denotational (q.v.), the collection of all functions.  A CycL term that denotes a function can appear in the arg0 position of a #$CycLNonAtomicTerm (q.v.).
<p>
See also #$relationExtension and #$relationHoldsAmong.")
(comment IntelligentAgent "A specialization of #$Agent-Generic and #$InformationStore (qq.v.).  An agent is an #$IntelligentAgent if and only if it is capable of knowing and acting, and capable of employing its knowledge in its actions.  An intelligent agent typically #$knowsAbout certain things, and its #$beliefs concerning those things influences its actions.  As with agents generally, an intelligent agent might either be a single individual, such as a person, or a group consisting of two or more individual agents, such as a business or government organization.  Specializations of #$IntelligentAgent include #$SocialBeing and #$MultiIndividualAgent-Intelligent.
<p>
Note that non-human #$Vertebrates that are sentient and somewhat intelligent, such as (arguably) cats and horses, can be considered intelligent agents in some contexts.")
(comment arg4Genl "A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg4Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the fourth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if  <code>(#$genls ARG4 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment eMailAddressText "<code>(#$eMailAddressText OBJ ADDR)</code> means that an e-mail address of <code>OBJ</code> is <code>ADDR</code>.  This predicate can be used to associate multiple e-mail addresses with one instance of #$PartiallyIntangible.  See also the more specializaed predicate, #$preferredEMailAddressText, which associates a unique e-mail address with an instance of #$PartiallyIntangible.")
(comment FunctionCompositionFn "A binary #$FunctionDenotingFunction (q.v.) that in effect combines two functions into one.  (#$FunctionCompositionFn FUNC1 FUNC2)  is the function COMP (if there is such) that is the composition of FUNC1 and FUNC2.  That is, for any element D #$inDomainOf FUNC1, the value of COMP applied to D is the same as the result obtained by applying FUNC1 to D, and then applying FUNC2 to that result.  Symbolically, (COMP D) = (FUNC2 (FUNC1 D)).
<p>
For example, (#$FunctionCompositionFn #$MotherFn #$FatherFn) is a function that takes an animal to its maternal grandfather, since A is the maternal grandfather of B if and only if A is the father of the mother of B.
<p>
Note that in the general description above, the domain of COMP is identical to the domain of FUNC1, and the range of COMP is a (proper or otherwise) subset of the range of FUNC2. (Strictly speaking, the range of COMP is the set of elements of the range of FUNC2 that are the values for some element or other of the range of FUNC1 taken as argument for FUNC2.)  This entails that COMP and FUNC1 must have the same #$arity (q.v.), and that the arity of FUNC2 must be 1.  (#$FunctionCompositionFn FUNC1 FUNC2) is defined (cf. #$undefined) whenever the range of FUNC1 is a (proper or otherwise) subset of the domain of FUNC2 (see  #$DomainOfFunctionFn and #$RangeOfFunctionFn).
<p>
See #$compositionOfFunctions for the predicate corresponding to this function.")
(comment identificationStrings "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates instances of #$Thing to instances of #$IDString.  <code>(#$identificationStrings THING STRING)</code> means that <code>STRING</code> is used somehow to identify <code>THING</code>. Its many notable specializations include #$constantID, #$taxIDNumber and #$computerIP. <code>STRING</code> may be a unique identifier for <code>THING</code> or it may be possible that more than one thing is identified by <code>STRING</code>.")
(comment ModeratelyToHighlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate-to-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$ModeratelyToHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately to highly hilly: those that are moderate-to-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment startsAfterEndingOf "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.) that relates any pair of temporal things such that the first starts after the  second ends.  <code>(#$startsAfterEndingOf AFTER BEFORE)</code> means that the time  point (see #$TimePoint) at which <code>AFTER</code> first starts to exist or occur  is temporally later than (see #$after) the time point at which <code>BEFORE</code> finally ceases to exist or occur.  Thus it is equivalent to the form <code>(#$after (#$StartFn AFTER) (#$EndFn BEFORE))</code>.  That is, the  #$startingPoint of <code>AFTER</code> is later than the #$endingPoint of <code>BEFORE</code>.")
(comment nextSubSituationTypeToStart "<code>(#$nextSubSituationTypeToStart SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> is the next situation to start after <code>SIT2</code>.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$nextSubSituationTypeToStart SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment InformationBearingThing "A collection of spatially-localized  individuals, including various actions and events as well as physical  objects.  Each instance of #$InformationBearingThing (or \"IBT\") is an  item that contains information (for an agent who knows how to interpret it).   Examples: a copy of the novel <i>Moby Dick</i>; a signal buoy; a photograph; an  elevator sign in Braille; a map; a US dollar bill; a resume; a musical score;  copies of the #$CycProgram.
<p>
For representations of the propositional content  of information bearing things, see #$PropositionalInformationThing; but note  that not all IBTs have a propositional content (cf. #$ArtObject).  An important specialization of #$InformationBearingThing is #$InformationBearingObject,  which comprises all of those IBTs that are also physical objects (i.e.  #$PartiallyTangibles).  Though often a subtle task in particular contexts,  it is important to distinguish the various specializations of  #$InformationBearingThing from those of #$AspatialInformationStore (whose  instances are the chunks of information instantiated in particular IBTs;  see #$instantiationOfAIT) and from those of #$ConceptualWork (whose  instances are the conceptual or artistic creations that are instantiated  in particular IBTs; see #$instantiationOfWork).
<p>
For instance, #$TextString  is a specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore, #$TextualMaterial is  a specialization of #$InformationBearingThing, and #$TextualPCW is a  specialization of #$ConceptualWork; to conflate any of these with  another would be to make a \"category error\".  Also note that events  in which information is transferred (see #$InformationTransferEvent) are  not considered instances of #$InformationBearingThing.  Rather, such  transfer events have as one of their participants (see #$actors) some  instance of #$InformationBearingThing; though in cases where IBTs are  themselves events this will require distinguishing very finely between  the event that encodes the information and the event that is the information transfer.  See also the sense-modality-based specializations, #$SoundInformationBearingThing and #$VisualInformationBearingThing.")
(comment relationAllExistsRange "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  <code>(#$relationAllExistsRange BINPRED COL1 COL2 RANGE)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL1</code> there are between the minimum and maximum of <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange) many instances <code>INST</code>(i) of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST INST</code>(i)) holds.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$implies
  (#$isa ?INST COL1)
  (#$thereExistRange RANGE ?INST-I
    (#$and
      (#$isa ?INST-I COL2)
      (PRED ?INST ?INST-I)))) .
</pre>
For example, '(#$relationAllExistsRange #$anatomicalParts #$Dog #$Hair-Strand #$Many-Quant)' means that dogs have many hairs.  Cf. #$relationExistsRangeAll, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment relationExistsAll "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relation to two collections.  <code>(#$relationExistsAll BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code>, there exists some instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?INST2 COL2)
    (#$thereExists ?INST1
      (#$and
        (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)
        (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)))).
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$relationExistsAll #$adjacentTo #$Harbor #$PortCity)</code> means that for every port city there exists a harbor to which the city is adjacent.  See also #$relationAllExists and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment subEventTypes "A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a specialization of #$subSituationTypes (q.v.) and a type-level correlate of #$subEvents (q.v.).  This predicate is used to state that events of one given type always have events of another given type as subevents. <code>(#$subEventTypes SUPERTYPE SUBTYPE)</code> means that for every instance <code>SUPERINST</code> of <code>SUPERTYPE</code>, there is an instance <code>SUBINST</code> of <code>SUBTYPE</code> such that <code>(#$subEvents SUPERINST SUBINST)</code>.  For example, (#$subEventTypes #$Pedaling #$LiftingALeg) means that every pedaling involves a leg-lifting.  See also #$properSubEventTypes and #$superEventTypes; and note that <code>(#$subEventTypes SUPERTYPE SUBTYPE)</code> is neither necessary nor sufficient for <code>(#$superEventTypes SUBTYPE SUPERTYPE)</code>.")
(comment temporallyIntersects "A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that relates things whose temporal extentions overlap, i.e. temporal things that exist concurrently for at least part of their lifetimes.  More precisely: <code>(#$temporallyIntersects OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that there is some #$TimeInterval (possibly as small as a #$TimePoint) temporally subsumed by (see #$temporallySubsumes) both <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code>.")
(comment anatomicalParts "<code>(#$anatomicalParts ORGANISM PART)</code> means that the #$OrganismPart <code>PART</code> is an anatomical part of the #$Organism-Whole <code>ORGANISM</code>.  One should use the predicate #$physicalParts (q.v.), rather than #$anatomicalParts, to relate an instance of #$OrganismPart (for example, a hand) to other #$OrganismParts (for example, the fingers of the hand) that are part of it.")
(comment relationalArity "A #$MetaRelation predicate that is used to state how many things a given #$FixedArityRelation (q.v.) holds among or relates to one another.  <code>(#$relationalArity RELATION N)</code> means that <code>RELATION</code> only holds among the members of <code>N</code>-item sequences or #$Lists (see #$relationHoldsAmong and #$relationExtension).  If <code>RELATION</code> is any fixed-arity relation other than a function its #$relationalArity is the same as its ordinary #$arity (q.v.).  But if <code>RELATION</code> is a #$FixedArityFunction its #$relationalArity is <i>1 greater</i> than its #$arity.  This reflects the fact that, qua relation, a function <code>FUNC</code> of #$arity <code>N</code> actually holds among <code>N</code><i>+1</i> things: <code>FUNC</code>'s <code>N</code> arguments together with the corresponding value <code>FUNC</code> has for those arguments (see #$valueOfFunctionForArgs).  For example, the function #$MotherFn has #$arity 1 while the corresponding predicate (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate) #$biologicalMother has #$arity 2; but both of these relations have #$relationalArity 2.")
(comment FirstOrderCollectionPredicate "A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$SetOrCollectionPredicate (q.v.).  First-order collection predicates are used to make statements about first-order collections or types, primarily in order to say something about their instances.  They generally provide a simpler way of stating what could be stated (albeit less tersely) without the use of collection-predicates (viz. by referring more directly to the instances themselves).  More precisely: each instance of #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate is a predicate at least one of whose relata is always a #$FirstOrderCollection (q.v.).  Such predicates are typically used to make ground-atomic sentences (see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) that are in principle equivalent to certain quantified sentences that involve no set-or-collection-predicates.
<p>
For example, #$argIsaForType is a \"type-level\" correlate of #$argIsa that enables one to place a particular argument-type constraint on every instance of a #$RelationshipType in one fell-swoop.  Thus, a ground-atomic sentence <code>(#$argIsaForType RELNTYPE ARGNUM COL)</code> is equivalent to the quantified sentence
<p>
<pre>
  (#$forAll ?RELN
    (#$implies
      (#$isa ?RELN RELNTYPE)
      (#$argIsa ?RELN ARGNUM COL))) .
</pre>")
(comment immediatelyAfterInSituationType "<code>(#$immediatelyAfterInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts immediately after <code>SIT2</code> ends.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$immediatelyAfterInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment DurationFn "A unary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that maps a given #$TemporalThing to the time-quantity that measures the (possibly discontinuous) time-interval during which the thing existed or transpired.  (#$DurationFn TEMP) is the #$Time-Quantity that the predicate #$duration (q.v.) relates to TEMP.  For example, (#$DurationFn #$TheTwentiethCenturyCE) is equal to (#$YearsDuration 100).
<p>
#$DurationFn is the function that corresponds to the predicate #$duration. (Cf. #$timeSpan.)")
(comment divisorTypeForQuantityType "A binary predicate that relates a #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType (q.v.) (that is a specialization of #$RatioQuantity) to a #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType (q.v.) from which the former's instances are derived via division.  <code>(#$divisorTypeForQuantityType DERIVED-TYPE PRIMITIVE-TYPE)</code> means that each instance of <code>DERIVED-TYPE</code> is the result of dividing (see #$QuotientFn ) some quantity by an instance of <code>PRIMITIVE-TYPE</code>.

For example, <code>(#$divisorTypeForQuantityType #$Speed #$Time-Quantity)</code> means that each speed is the result of dividing some quantity by a time-quantity.  Thus the speed (#$MilesPerHour 35) is equal to the result of dividing (#$Mile 35) by (#$HoursDuration 1).

See also #$multiplierTypeForQuantityType.")
(comment assertionMt "A predicate which relates Cyc assertions to the microtheories in which they reside. (#$assertionMt ASSERTION MT) states that the #$CycLAssertion ASSERTION resides in the #$Microtheory MT.
<p>
Note that the first argument to this predicate is #$CycLAssertion, rather than #$ELSentence-Assertible. This renders the predicate #$functionalInArgs 2, since any given #$CycLAssertion only appears in one microtheory (though another assertion with an identical #$ELSentence-Assertible may well appear in a different microtheory - cf. #$assertionSentence).")
(comment publicationTimeOfMTAssertions "(#$publicationTimeOfMTAssertions MICROTHEORY TIME-INTERVAL) means that the assertions in the #$Microtheory MICROTHEORY were published (or at least publically made) during the #$TimeInterval TIME-INTERVAL.  For example, the 'publication time' of the #$CIAWorldFactbook1997Mt is the year 1997.
<p>
Note that the time interval in question is not necessarily that referenced in assertions within MICROTHEORY; for example, the #$CIAWorldFactbook1997Mt contains assertions about the 1996 gross domestic product of various countries.
<p>
Nor should it be interpreted as the time-interval at which the assertions within MICROTHEORY are true; for an expression of that idea, see #$mtTime (and #$MtTimeFn).
<p>
Nor is it the date on which assertions in MICROTHEORY were entered in Cyc. Again, the #$CIAWorldFactbook1997Mt is a good example, since it was created in 1998.  Generally, when the source of the assertions in a microtheory is a publication, the 'publication time' will be the date of publication.  (Specifically, the date of first publication, in the case of unrevised subsequent editions.  However, if the material in the source is made publically available prior to the formal publication date, the 'publication time' will be the earlier date.)  Note that it will only make sense to assign an 'publication time' to a microtheory when it represents a set of propositions that were all asserted (or hypothesized, or...etc.) by some specific source or agent -- many microtheories do not fall into this category.")
(comment ModeratelyToVeryHighlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyToVeryHighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate-to-very-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$ModeratelyToVeryHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately to very-highly hilly: those that are moderate-to-very-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment relationExistsRangeAll "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  <code>(#$relationExistsRangeAll BINPRED COL1 COL2 RANGE)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL2</code> there are between the minimum and maximum of <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange) many instances <code>INST(i)</code> of <code>COL1</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST(i) INST)</code> holds.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
     (#$isa ?INST COL2)
     (#$thereExistRange RANGE ?INST-I
        (#$and
           (#$isa ?INST-I COL1)
           (PRED ?INST-I ?INST)))).
</pre>
<p>
For example, '(#$relationExistsRangeAll #$anatomicalParts #$Mammal #$Foot-AnimalBodyPart #$AFew-Quant)' means every mammal has a few feet.  Cf. #$relationAllExistsRange, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment Informing "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$Informing is a #$CommunicationAct-Single in which the #$senderOfInfo conveys some piece of <i>propositional</i> information--i.e. information of the sort that can be true or false.
<p>
The information conveyed in an #$Informing is presented by the sender as being true, though it may in fact be false.  Thus, passing on misinformation counts as #$Informing, as do acts of #$Lying.
<p>
Note that the propositional nature of the information conveyed in an #$Informing may be implicit; for example, if Alice asks 'What's your favorite color?' and Bob simply responds 'Red', Bob has <i>implicitly</i> conveyed the proposition <i>Bob's favorite color is red</i>.
<p>
Specializations of #$Informing include #$Reporting, #$Testifying, and #$AnnouncingSomething.")
(comment transporter "This predicate relates objects to the transportation events in which they facilitate movement.  <code>(#$transporter MOVE OBJ)</code> means that #$SolidTangibleThing <code>OBJ</code> enables or facilitates the conveyance of the #$transportees in the #$TransportationEvent <code>MOVE</code>.  <code>OBJ</code> is an #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code> that moves along with the #$transportees.  <code>OBJ</code> will generally hold, support, contain, pull, or push the #$transportees throughout the <code>MOVE</code> #$Event.  <code>OBJ</code> may or may not be the #$providerOfMotiveForce in the event <code>MOVE</code>.  If <code>OBJ</code> stays with each #$primaryObjectMoving from the #$fromLocation to the #$toLocation, moving along the same trajectory, then it is also a #$primaryObjectMoving.
<p>
If <code>OBJ</code> facilitates the motion of the #$primaryObjectMoving but does not itself engage in translational motion it is merely the conveyor of the action, and the role #$conveyor-Stationary should be asserted.  If it is unclear whether the conveyor is stationary or not, the role #$conveyor-Generic is used.  Specializations of the role #$transporter should be used when possible: although automobiles are #$transporters in many events, they should normally have the more specific role designation of #$vehicle because they are also instances of #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle.   When a car is being towed by a tow truck, the car is just a #$transporter of any of its contents while the tow truck is the #$vehicle of that event.  An additional role designation for some #$transporters -- those which are #$PhysicalDevices -- is #$deviceUsed; e.g., the use of crutches in hobbling or ice skates in skating.  See the #$comment on #$TransportationEvent.  Note that an organism may be a #$transportees in a #$Bicycle riding or #$Skating event as well as being the #$providerOfMotiveForce in such cases.")
(comment startsAfterEndOfInSituationType "<code>(#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts after <code>SIT2</code> ends.
<p>
For example, (#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType #$GoingOutToEat #$Paying #$HavingAMeal) means that when going out to eat, people pay after they finish eating.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment genlFuncs "A binary #$MetaRelation and a specialization of #$generalizations.  #$genlFuncs can be used to relate an instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction to other instances that are correlatively more general with respect to the collections they return as values for the same arguments.  <code>(#$genlFuncs SPECFN GENLFN)</code> means that the collection that is the value of <code>GENLFN</code> for any given sequence of arguments is a generalization (see #$genls) of the value (if any) of <code>SPECFN</code> for that same sequence of arguments.  That is, whenever <code>SPECFN</code> is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <<code>X1, ..., Xn</code>>, the following holds:
<p>
    <code>(#$genls (SPECFN X1 ... Xn) (GENLFN X1 ... Xn))</code> .
<p>
For example, the sentence <code>(#$genlFuncs #$MobFn #$GroupFn)</code> entails that (e.g.) the collection (#$MobFn #$Person) is a specialization of (#$GroupFn #$Person).
<p>
Cf. #$genlFunctions.")
(comment Integer "A specialization of #$RationalNumber.  Each instance of #$Integer is a whole number with no fractional remainder.  An integer may be positive (e.g. 42), zero, or negative (e.g. -42).
<p>
Note that, in the KB browser, a fixed- or floating-point number representation such as `42.00' should not be assumed to refer to the integer 42. For it might only appear to correspond to that integer within the tolerance of its representation, and might in fact be an approximation to a non-integer number such as 42.0003.
<p>
Also note that, unlike #$Integer, #$SubLInteger (q.v.) is a collection of integer-denoting expressions in the SubL programming language rather than a collection of integers themselves. Still, (#$genls #$SubLInteger #$Integer) holds, but only under a special interpretation of #$genls forced by the fact that #$SubLInteger is a #$quotedCollection (q.v).)")
(comment middleName "A specialization of #$nameString (q.v.).  <code>(#$middleName PERSON STRING)</code> means that <code>PERSON</code> is known by <code>STRING</code> as his or her middle name.   Persons usually  have no more than one middle name.  <code>STRING</code> is typically a #$HumanGivenNameString, though in some cultures certain people have #$HumanFamilyNameStrings for their middle names.  See also #$middleNameInitial.")
(comment quantityRange "A binary predicate that relates a #$QuantityType to a collection to which anything possessing a #$Quantity of that type belongs. <code>(#$quantityRange QUANTTYPE COL)</code> means that anything that is related to any instance of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> via some #$QuantitySlot (q.v.) is itself an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is, for any thing <code>THING</code>, any instance <code>QUANT</code> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>, and any quantity-slot <code>SLOT</code>, <code>(SLOT THING QUANT)</code> entails <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$quantityRange #$Viscosity #$PartiallyTangible)</code> means that only tangible things have viscosities.  But note that it does <i>not</i> imply that all tangible things have viscosities.
<p>
When making an assertion of the form <code>(#$quantityRange QUANTTYPE COL)</code> to the Cyc Knowledge Base it is normally advisable to select the most restricted collection possible (among those that are reified in the Cyc ontology) for <code>COL</code>.")
(comment objectActedOn "The predicate #$objectActedOn is used to relate an event to any object significantly affected in that event.  The entity or entities in question must exist before the event, but may be either destroyed in the event (see the more specific predicate #$inputsDestroyed), or merely affected by it (for example, see the more specific predicates #$damages and #$objectOfStateChange). <code>(#$objectActedOn EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is altered or affected in <code>EVENT</code>, and the change that <code>OBJECT</code> undergoes is central or focal to understanding <code>EVENT</code>.  Thus, scissors are <i>not</i> an #$objectActedOn in a #$HairCuttingEvent.  The focal change in a haircut is hair getting shorter; thus, hair is a legitimate #$objectActedOn in a #$HairCuttingEvent.  The almost microscopic dulling that scissors undergo in a single haircut is a comparatively insignificant change with respect to a single haircut, considered as a #$HairCuttingEvent.")
(comment computerMACAddress "A predicate relating a instance of #$Computer with the instance of #$MACAddress associated with one of its network cards. <code>(#$computerMACAddress COMP MAC-ADDR)</code> means that <code>COMP</code> is a computer with a network card installed whose MAC address is <code>MAC-ADDR</code>. Note that a single computer can have more than one network card and each <code>MAC-ADDR</code> address is unique to a network card, hence one computer can be associated with more than one MAC address.")
(comment fieldsOfActivity "A specialization of #$fieldsOfCompetence.  <code>(#$fieldsOfActivity PERSON FIELD)</code>  means that <code>PERSON</code> is or was active in the #$FieldOfStudy <code>FIELD</code>.  Usually,  <code>PERSON</code> has made, is making, or intends to make some contribution to  <code>FIELD</code>.  E.g. (#$fieldsOfActivity #$Thales #$MilesianSchool) holds.")
(comment NumericComparisonPredicate "A specialization of #$ComparisonPredicate.  The collection of binary predicates used to compare instances of #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) with respect to their  numeric properties or magnitudes.  Instances include #$greaterThan,  #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, and #$numericallyEquals.
<p>
The numeric-comparison predicates are effectively the mathematical relations of greater-than et al., generalized to  hold between scalars of all sorts, including quantitative intervals as  well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  Generally speaking, a given numeric comparison predicate holds between two scalars only  if they are both instances of the same #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType (q.v.).
<p>
See also #$quantitiesShareScale and #$colinearQuantities.")
(comment conservativeViaArg "A #$MetaPredicate that is a generalization of #$transitiveViaArg (q.v.) in that it can take <i>any</i> binary predicate (transitive or not) as its second argument.  <code>(#$conservativeViaArg PRED RELN N)</code> means that if both of these hold:
<pre>
  (PRED ... ARGN ... )

  (RELN ARGN NEW-ARGN),
</pre>
<p>
then so does
<pre>
  (PRED ... NEW-ARGN ...)
</pre>
(where the argument-sequence <code>... NEW-ARGN ...</code> is just like <code>... ARGN ...</code> except for having <code>NEW-ARGN</code> in place of <code>ARGN)</code>.
<p>
Note that if <code>RELN</code> is a #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate, the more specialized #$transitiveViaArg can be used instead.  See also #$conservativeViaArgInverse.")
(comment temporallySubsumedBy-InsType "A binary #$TemporalPredicate that relates a #$TemporalThing to a type of #$TimeInterval or #$QualitativeTimeOfDay (q.v.) an instance of which the thing is temporally subsumed by.  (#$temporallySubsumedBy-InsType TEMP INTERVAL-TYPE) means that some instance of INTERVAL-TYPE #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.) TEMP.
<p>
For example, '(#$temporallySubsumedBy-InsType FredsBirth #$Wednesday)' means that Fred was born on a Wednesday.
<p>
See also #$temporallySubsumes-InsType and #$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType.")
(comment RelativeGenericValueFn "A ternary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that can be used for specifying \"relativized generic quantities\" of various types and amounts.  #$RelativeGenericValueFn takes as arguments a #$QuantitySlot, a #$Collection, and a #$GenericValuePredicate, and returns a #$GenericQuantity (qq.v.).  <code>(#$RelativeGenericValueFn SLOT COL GENVALPRED)</code> is the <code>GENVALPRED</code> amount (e.g. <i>very-low</i>, <i>low</i>, <i>medium</i>, <i>high</i>) of generic quantity of the type to which <code>SLOT</code>'s second argument-place is constrained (via #$arg2Isa), relativized to instances of <code>COL</code> as occupants of it's first argument-place.
<p>
For example, (#$RelativeGenericValueFn #$heightOfObject #$BasketballPlayer #$mediumAmountOf) is the range (see #$ScalarProperInterval) of #$Distance that is roughly a medium height for a basketball player.
<p>
See also the collection #$GenericValueFunction.")
(comment domainAssumptions "A #$MicrotheoryPredicate that relates a given #$Microtheory to an #$ELSentence-Assertible that expresses one of its 'domain assumptions'. (#$domainAssumptions MT PROP) means that the microtheory MT has the proposition PROP as a domain assumption, which means that all assertions explicitly made in MT assume that PROP is true.  For example, (#$domainAssumptions #$ChristianTrinityMt (#$equals #$JesusChrist #$GodTheSon)).
<p>
Another way of thinking of this is that the various `content' assertions of MT only apply to objects that satisfy all its #$domainAssumptions (i.e. that the quantifiers #$forAll and #$thereExists will be restricted in MT in this way).  So if it's true in MT that:
<p>
(#$forAll ?x Q)
<p>
and if we lift this axiom to another microtheory (MT2) that does not make the domain assumption P, then the axiom becomes:
<p>
(#$forAll ?x (#$implies P Q))
<p>
(Or, of course ((#$forAll ?x (#$implies (#$and P1 P2 P3...) Q)), where there is more than one relevant domain assumption).  See also #$mtAdditionConstraint.")
(comment toLocation "The predicate #$toLocation is used to indicate the ending point of a particular movement.  <code>(#$toLocation MOVE LOC)</code> means that <code>LOC</code> is where the #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code> (a #$Movement-TranslationEvent) is found when the event <code>MOVE</code> ends; the final location of the #$objectMoving in that event is <code>LOC</code>.  The #$objectMoving may or may not be #$Stationary at <code>LOC</code>.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a single-pathway translation (see #$Translation-SingleTrajectory), then every #$objectMoving in it is found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> ends.  If <code>MOVE</code> has multiple movers and multiple pathways (see #$Translation-MultiTrajectory), then at least some of the #$objectMoving(s) can be found at <code>LOC</code> at the end of <code>MOVE</code>.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a fluid flow (see #$FluidFlow-Translation), then at least some portion of the fluid #$objectMoving can be found at location <code>LOC</code> at the end of <code>MOVE</code>.  See also #$motionPathway-Complete, #$pathConnects.")
(comment hasWorkers "The predicate #$hasWorkers relates two instances of #$Agent-Generic.  <code>(#$hasWorkers AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT2</code> regularly works for <code>AGENT1</code>.  This predicate can be used to relate a business to its employees, a master craftsman to his apprentices, or an organization to its subcontractors.  Note that <code>(#$hasWorkers AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> does not necessarily imply that <code>AGENT2</code> is an employee of <code>AGENT1</code>, since <code>AGENT2</code> may be working voluntarily for <code>AGENT1</code>.  Note also that assertions using #$hasWorkers should usually be temporally qualified, since when one agent regularly works for another, that agent usually does so only for a portion of  his, her, or its life, rather than for all of it.")
(comment isaInstanceOfType "(#$isaInstanceOfType INS COL-TYPE) means that the #$Thing INS is an instance of a #$Collection which is itself an instance of COL-TYPE, a #$CollectionType. This predicate can be used when referring to instances, say INS1, of instances, say COL1 with (isa INS1 COL1) of a given type, TYPEA, such that (isa COL1 TYPEA) when the narrower collection COL that would stand in a #$typeGenls relationship with TYPEA and such that (#$isa INS1 COLA) and (#$genls COL1 COLA) is not reified.
<p>
Example of use: #$BacteriumIdentificationTestType is the collection of collections of tests conducted to identify bacteria. All these tests are such that objects on which tests are conducted are instances of #$Bacterium. This would be expressed as follows:
<p>
(#$implies
(#$and
(#$isaInstanceOfType TEST #$BacteriumIdentificationTestType)
(#$objectTested TEST OBJ))
(#$isa OBJ #$Bacterium)).
<p>
To specify that during each of these tests there is at least one bacterium tested, one would assert the following GAF:
<p>
(#$relationAllExists #$objectTested
(#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$TrueOrFalseTest
#$isaInstanceOfType
#$BacteriumIdentificationTestType)
#$Bacterium).
<p>
This predicate may be used when one wants to avoid reification of the union of all instances of a collection type, but note that extensive use of this predicate may amount to a good justification for reification of such a collection.")
(comment payer "<code>(#$payer MONEY-TRANSFER PAYER)</code> means that <code>MONEY-TRANSFER</code> is an instance of #$MoneyTransfer in which <code>PAYER</code> is an agent which provides (some of) the money.")
(comment succeedingIntervalType "A binary #$TemporalPredicate and specialization of #$followingIntervalTypes (q.v.) that relates types of #$TimeIntervals.  (#$succeedingIntervalType PRECEDING-TYPE SUCCEEDING-TYPE) means that every instance of PRECEDING-TYPE is immediately followed by (see #$contiguousAfter) some instance of SUCCEEDING-TYPE, and every instance of SUCCEEDING-TYPE is immediately preceded by some instance of PRECEDING-TYPE.
<p>
For example,  (#$succeedingIntervalType #$Saturday #$Sunday) holds.
<p>
See also #$followingIntervalType-Cumulated.")
(comment RuleMacroPredicate "A specialization of both #$MacroRelation and #$SetOrCollectionPredicate (qq.v.) whose instances are used to form shorthand expressions for more complex sentences.  Each instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate is correlated -- via an #$expansion (q.v.) assertion -- with a certain template for a class of (possibly quantified) CycL sentences.  A closed sentence (see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) built from (i.e. whose \"0<sup>th</sup>\" argument is) a given rule-macro-predicate is treated by the Cyc system as fully semantically equivalent to the (usually more complex) corresponding sentence fitting the template.  For example, #$genls is a rule-macro-predicate whose expansion assertion is
<pre>
  (#$expansion #$genls
    (#$implies
      (#$isa ?OBJ :ARG1)
      (#$isa ?OBJ :ARG2))
</pre>
Thus <code>(#$genls #$Poodle #$Dog)</code> is shorthand for the rule
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?OBJ #$Poodle)
    (#$isa ?OBJ #$Dog))
</pre>
Note that virtually any #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate (q.v.) could in principle be made into a #$RuleMacroPredicate and given a similar treatment.")
(comment SkolemFunction "A specialization of #$IndeterminateTermDenotingFunction and #$ReifiableFunction (qq.v.).  #$SkolemFunction is a collection of system-generated functions that implement existential quantification in Cyc.  Whenever someone asserts to the Knowledge Base a sentence that contains '#$thereExists' (in an arg0 position), Cyc automatically creates a new instance of #$SkolemFunction and rewrites the assertion using that Skolem function.
<p>
For example, suppose we want Cyc to know that every animal has a birth date.   So we assert the following sentence:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?X #$Animal)
    (#$thereExists ?Y
      (#$birthDate ?X ?Y))) .
</pre>
<p>
In response, Cyc automatically reifies a new unary instance of #$SkolemFunction (call it 'BirthDateFn') that takes any given animal to its date of birth, and then rewrites our rule using BirthDateFn instead of #$thereExists, as
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$and
      (#$isa ?X #$Animal)
      (#$termOfUnit ?Y (BirthDateFn ?X)))
    (#$birthDate ?X ?Y)) .
</pre>
<p>
Note that actual Cyc-generated Skolem function names currently (02/04) consist of the prefix 'SKF-' follow by a numeral.")
(comment paAppropriateQuestion "A predicate that states a given question is appropriate for
the #$CycProductAdvisor to ask.  (paAppropriateQuestion ID FORMULA)
means that the question specified by the string id ID is
appropriate to ask at the current point in the user's
interaction with the Cyc Product Advisor.  FORMULA is
a template for a formula that should be asserted
when the user answers the question.  This allows
OEers working on the Product Advisor to 'special-case' certain questions.
<p>
It is assumed that ID refers to a question which has already
been written for such a situation, and the Cyc-PA code will be able
to retrieve the question given its id.")
(comment vehicle "A binary predicate that relates instances of #$TransportationEvent to instances of #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle.  <code>(#$vehicle EVENT VEHICLE)</code> means that <code>VEHICLE</code> is a #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle which is both the #$providerOfMotiveForce and the #$transporter in EVENT.  If an object is a #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle and plays the role of #$transporter in some moving event, then it generally  will play the role of #$vehicle in that event.  Examples: a car plays the role of #$vehicle in driving.  Note, however, that a bicycle does not play the role of #$vehicle in bike riding since it is not a provider of motive force.  A borderline non-example is someone sitting in a car while it's being pulled by a tow truck; the car is not playing the role of #$vehicle in that event (because it is not the provider of motive force - the tow truck is).")
(comment startsDuringInSituationType "<code>(#$startsDuringInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts during <code>SIT2</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$startsDuringInSituationType #$UnwindingOfDNAHelix #$Transcription-DNAToRNA #$GeneExpression) means that when DNA unwinds, DNA to RNA transcription starts during gene expression.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$startsDuringInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment IntervalOnNumberLine "A specialization of #$NumericInterval (q.v.).  Each instance of #$IntervalOnNumberLine is either a particular #$RealNumber or a proper interval on the real number line (see #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine).  For example, the phrase \"numbers greater than zero and less than or equal to 10\" describes such an interval.
<p>
Note that an interval on the real number line need not be continuous.  E.g. the phrase \"even integers between Pi and the square root of 1000\" describes a noncontinuous instance of #$IntervalOnNumberLine.  Cf. the specialization #$ContinuousIntervalOnNumberLine.")
(comment colinearQuantities "A #$CoEquivalenceBinaryPredicate and a specialization of #$comparableScalars (q.v.) that holds only between #$Quantity[ies] (q.v.).  <code>(#$colinearQuantities QUANT1 QUANT2)</code> means that there exists some linear ordering <code>SCALE</code> (see #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType) such that <code>QUANT1</code> and <code>QUANT2</code> are distinct instances of <code>SCALE</code>.  This implies both (i) <code>(#$quantitiesShareScale QUANT1 QUANT2 SCALE)</code> holds and (ii) either <code>(#$followingValue QUANT1 QUANT2)</code> or <code>(#$followingValue QUANT2 QUANT1)</code> holds.
<p>
#$followingValue is in turn a specialization of #$colinearQuantities.  The latter can be thought of as a version of former that is unconcerned with the order of its arguments.")
(comment partTypes "A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a type-level correlate of #$parts (q.v.).  This predicate relates types of individuals to the types of parts they have.  <code>(#$partTypes WHOLETYPE PARTTYPE)</code> means that for every instance <code>WHOLEINST</code> of <code>WHOLETYPE</code> there exists at least one instance <code>PARTINST</code> of <code>PARTTYPE</code> such that (#$parts <code>WHOLEINST PARTINST)</code> holds.  Important specializations of this predicate include #$physicalPartTypes, #$subEventTypes and #$properSubEventTypes (qq.v.).")
(comment PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType "A collection of collections and a specialization of both #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and #$MeasurableScalarIntervalType (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType is a totally-ordered collection of instances of #$OneDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).  Examples include #$Mass, #$Distance, #$Temperature, and #$ElectricalCharge.
<p>
Cf. #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType.")
(comment arg3Genl "A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg3Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the third argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG3 COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a similar but more flexible ternary  predicate.")
(comment TotallyOrderedQuantityType "A collection of collections and a specialization of both #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType and #$QuantityType (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType is a totally-ordered collection of #$Quantity[ies], and thus an appropriate argument for a #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.), such as #$LowAmountFn or #$MediumToHighAmountFn.
<p>
Instances of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType include #$Toxicity, #$DiplomaticSupport, and #$LevelOfDifficulty.  Specializations include #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and #$MeasurableQuantityType.")
(comment AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate "A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and thus a specialization of #$BinaryPredicate.  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate if and only if it has the following two properties.  (i) For any <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> such that both <code>(PRED THING1 THING2)</code> and <code>(PRED THING2 THING1)</code> hold, <code>THING1</code>  = <code>THING2</code>.  (ii) <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places must be \"co-satisfiable\" with respect to their (single-argument) type constraints (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement).
<p>
Instances of #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate include #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, #$parts, and #$subEvents.  See also the specialization #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate; and cf. #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.")
(comment (LeftObjectOfPairFn (PartTypeFn FourthThoracicVertebra VertebralTransverseProcess)) "<p>")
(comment BinaryRolePredicate "A specialization of #$Role.  An instance <code>ROLE</code> of #$Role is also an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate just in case <code>ROLE</code> is an instance of #$BinaryPredicate.  In other words, <code>ROLE</code> is a binary predicate that relates a situation to a single individual that is involved in that situation, For example, #$subSituations, #$actors, and #$affectedAgent are all binary role predicates.  See also #$BinaryRoleTypePredicate.")
(comment typeGenls "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections (q.v.) that can be used to relate a given collection to a collection-of-collections (see #$CollectionType) all of whose instances are specializations of the given collection.  <code>(#$typeGenls COLTYPE COL)</code> means that every instance of <code>COLTYPE</code> has <code>COL</code> as a generalization (see #$genls).
<p>
For example, <code>(#$typeGenls #$CreditCardTypeByBrand #$CreditCard)</code> means that each type of credit card is a subcollection of the collection of all credit cards.  Note that this does <i>not</i> imply that every subcollection of #$CreditCard is an instance of #$CreditCardTypeByBrand.")
(comment buyingAgent "<code>(#$buyingAgent SALE AGENT)</code> means that in the #$CommercialActivity <code>SALE</code>, the #$SocialBeing <code>AGENT</code> acts on behalf of a would-be buyer to bring about a purchase.  <code>AGENT</code> is a performer in <code>SALE</code> (see the predicate #$performedBy), but is not the buyer (see the predicate #$buyer).")
(comment expansion "Some relations (namely, instances of #$MacroRelation (q.v.)) can be defined in terms of more basic or primitive constructs. <code>(#$expansion RELATION FORMULA)</code> associates <code>RELATION</code> with the <code>FORMULA</code> that defines it. The arg2 is allowed to reference generic-argument keywords, such as :ARG1 :ARG2 which represent respectively the arg1 and the arg2 within uses of <code>RELATION</code>.  For example, <code>(#$expansion #$genls (#$implies (#$isa ?object :ARG1) (#$isa ?object :ARG2)))</code> indicates that the gaf <code>(#$genls #$Poodle #$Dog)</code> is defined as <code>(#$implies (#$isa ?object #$Poodle) (#$isa ?object #$Dog)))</code>. Importantly, the expansion-formula arg2 must be necessary and sufficient; it denotes the definition of the uses of relation arg1; there can be only one expansion for any relation.  Furthermore, no two relations can share a common expansion; thus, there can be only one possible contraction from a formula that corresponds to an expansion into a compact form that references expandable relations.  For example, <code>(#$implies (#$isa ?object #$Poodle) (#$isa ?object #$Dog)))</code> has the unambiguous contraction of <code>(#$genls #$Poodle #$Dog)</code>. The expansion arg2 can (and should when possible) make use of relations that have expansions.  Note that the example above references #$implies which would itself have the expansion <code>(#$or (#$not :ARG1) :ARG2)</code>.  Thus, the full expansion of a relation can involve recursive expansions.  For example, the full expansion of #$genls would be <code>(#$or (#$isa ?object :ARG1) (#$not (#$isa ?object :ARG2))))</code>.  No relation may reference itself (either directly or indirectly via recursion) in its expansion (or in its full recursive expansion).  See #$ELRelation, #$TermMacroFunction, #$expansionAxiom.")
(comment multiplicationUnits "A ternary predicate that relates #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) functions.  <code>(#$multiplicationUnits UNIT1 UNIT2 PRODUNIT)</code> means that <code>PRODUNIT</code> is the unit-of-measure that always returns the product of the values returned by <code>UNIT1</code> and <code>UNIT2</code> for two respective arguments (or argument-sequences) when it is itself applied to the product of those arguments (or sequences).  That is, whenever <code>(UNIT1 . ARGS1) = VALUE1</code> and <code>(UNIT2 . ARGS2) = VALUE2</code>, then <code>(PRODUNIT (#$TimesFn ARGS1 ARGS2)) = (#$TimesFn VALUE1 VALUE2)</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$multiplicationUnits #$Mile #$Mile #$SquareMile) holds, and thus (#$TimesFn (#$Mile 2) (#$Mile 3)) is equal to (#$SquareMile 6).
<p>
The predicate #$multiplicationUnits corresponds to (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate) both of the functions #$UnitProductFn and #$PerFn (qq.v.).  Thus,
<p>
    <code>(#$multiplicationUnits UNIT1 UNIT2 PRODUNIT)</code>
<p>
is equivalent to
<p>
    <code>(#$equals PRODUNIT (#$UnitProductFn UNIT1 UNIT2))</code>
<p>
is equivalent to
<p>
    <code>(#$equals UNIT1 (#$PerFn PRODUNIT UNIT2))</code> .")
(comment arityMax "A #$MetaRelation for specifying the maximum number
of arguments a given #$VariableArityRelation can take if the 
resulting formula is to be semantically well-formed.  <code>(#$arityMax RELN N)</code> means 
that semantic well-formedness requires that <code>RELN</code> take at most <code>N</code> arguments at 
a time.  More precisely: a formula <code>(RELN ARG<sub>1</sub> ... ARG<sub>M</sub>)</code> is semantically 
well-formed only if <code>M</code> is less than or equal to <code>N</code>.  For example, the 
maximum-arity of any instance of #$UnitOfMeasure is 2.  Note that full semantic 
well-formedness requires obeying argument-type constraints (see 
#$ArgTypePredicate) as well as arity constraints.  For a general explanation 
of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible.  See also 
#$arityMin and #$arity.")
(comment InternationalAgreement "A specialization of #$Agreement (q.v.). Each instance of #$InternationalAgreement is a formal agreement among (see #$agreeingAgents) countries or organizations with international scope.
<p>
Examples of #$InternationalAgreement include treaties to end wars, trade agreements, the #$NuclearNonProliferationTreaty, the #$ChemicalWeaponsConvention, and the Geneva Convention.  Specializations include #$Treaty and #$PeaceAccord.")
(comment destination-RoundTrip "<code>(#$destination-RoundTrip TRIP PLACE)</code> means that the #$PartiallyTangible <code>PLACE</code> is the destination of the outbound leg (see the predicate #$outboundLegOfRoundTrip) of the #$Translation-RoundTrip <code>TRIP</code>, and that <code>PLACE</code> is the starting point of the inbound leg (see the predicate #$inboundLegOfRoundTrip) of <code>TRIP</code>.")
(comment MonetaryRate "A specialization of #$Rate and #$MoneyRatio (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$MonetaryRate is an amount of money (see #$MonetaryValue) taken with respect to an amount of time (see #$Time-Quantity).  Instances of #$MonetaryRate include rates at which money is earned, spent, transferred, lost, and invested.
<p>
An example of #$MonetaryRate is (#$DollarsPerHour 10), the rate of ten dollars per hour.
<p>
See also #$UnitOfMonetaryRate.")
(comment FunctionalPredicate "A specialization of #$Predicate whose instances are those predicates that are \"functional\" in at least one argument-place (see #$functionalInArgs and #$singleEntryFormatInArgs).  That is, given any sequence of legal arguments placed in such a predicate's other argument positions, there is at most one argument such that, when it is placed in the predicate's functional position, the resulting formula is true.  More precisely: Suppose PRED is an N-ary instance of #$FunctionalPredicate that is functional in its Kth place.  Given any (N-1)-tuple <O(1),.., O(K-1), O(K+1),.., O(N)> consisting of things that are, respectively, legal arguments for (i.e. satisfy all argument-constraints on) the N-1 argument-places in PRED other than  its Kth-place, there is at most one thing -- call it O(K) -- such that  (PRED O(1)..O(N)) is true.  For example, #$biologicalMother is functional in its second argument-place, since every animal has one and only one biological mother.
<p>
The one exception to the above is when the entry-format for PRED's functional argument-place is #$intervalEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.) instead of #$singleEntryFormatInArgs.  In that case there might be multiple instances INT-1..INT-M of either #$ScalarInterval or #$TimeInterval that, when put in this argument-place, each yield a true formula; but only if all of the INT-i overlap one another and thus have a common sub-interval; see #$quantityIntersects and #$temporallyIntersects.  Cf. #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate.
<p>
Note that it might be the case that, for some ways of fixing PRED's other arguments, there will be _nothing_ that would yield a true formula when put into PRED's functional argument-place; for example, while a spatial thing has at most one length (see #$lengthOfObject), if it is (say) a sphere it has none.
<p>
A binary #$FunctionalPredicate that is functional in its second argument is an instance of #$FunctionalSlot (q.v.).")
(comment objectOfPossessionTransfer "#$objectOfPossessionTransfer relates an event (more specifically, an instance of #$ChangeInUserRights) to an object (an instance of #$SomethingExisting) the user rights to which are transferred during that event.  <code>(#$objectOfPossessionTransfer EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that in <code>EVENT</code>, all or some rights to use <code>OBJECT</code> are transferred from one agent (the #$fromPossessor) to another (the #$toPossessor).  Since <code>EVENT</code> is an instance of #$ChangeInUserRights, it could be (among other things) a buying, renting, lending, or repossessing.")
(comment SymmetricBinaryPredicate "A specialization of both #$BinaryPredicate and #$CommutativeRelation (qq.v.).  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate if and only if (i) for any things <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code>, if <code>(PRED X Y)</code> then <code>(PRED Y X)</code> and (ii) the single-argument argument type constraints (see #$argIsa and #$argGenls) on <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places are co-satisfiable (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement).  Note that if <code>PRED</code> satisfies condition (i), then it also satisfies condition (ii) as long as <code>(PRED A B)</code> holds for some <code>A</code> and <code>B</code>.
<p>
Instances of #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate include #$siblings, #$teammates, #$connectedTo, and #$bordersOn.")
(comment valueOfFunctionForArgs "A ternary #$MetaRelation that relates a given function and a sequence of arguments on which it is defined to the value the function has for those arguments. <code>(#$valueOfFunctionForArgs FUNCTION ARGLIST VALUE)</code> means that <code>FUNCTION</code> maps <code>ARGLIST</code> to <code>VALUE</code>.  More precisely: when <code>FUNCTION</code> is applied to the (say) <code>N</code> items in <code>ARGLIST</code> (with the <code>i</code>-th item of <code>ARGLIST</code> serving as the <code>i</code>-th  argument to <code>FUNCTION</code>, for 1 <= <code>i</code> <= <code>N)</code>, it yields <code>VALUE</code>.
<p>
A necessary condition for the above is that <code>ARGLIST</code> is #$inDomainOf (q.v.) <code>FUNCTION</code> and <code>VALUE</code> is #$inRangeOf (q.v.) <code>FUNCTION</code>.")
(comment SetOrCollectionPredicate "The #$PredicateType consisting of all predicates that (always) hold of #$SetOrCollections (q.v.).  Each instance of #$SetOrCollectionPredicate has at least one argument-place constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$SetOrCollection or some specialization thereof.  Some set-or-collection predicates are used to make statements that are more or less directly about a given set-or-collection itself (e.g. #$cardinality relates a set-or-collection to its size); but most are used to make more general statements about some or all of the elements of a given set-or-collection (e.g. #$inhabitantTypes relates a geographical region to a collection of persons such that at least one person in the collection resides in that region).
<p>
Specializations of #$SetOrCollectionPredicate include #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
<p>
Cf. #$ObjectPredicate.")
(comment VeryHighlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$VeryHighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>very-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$VeryHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are very highly hilly: those that are very-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment ScalarIntervalSlot "A specialization of #$BinaryPropertyPredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ScalarIntervalSlot is a binary predicate that is used to attribute a property to a thing, by relating it to a #$ScalarInterval (i.e. to a #$Quantity or #$NumericInterval of some sort).
<p>
Examples of #$ScalarIntervalSlot include #$lengthOfObject, #$levelOfMorale, and #$iQ.
<p>
See also the specializations #$QuantitySlot and #$NumericIntervalSlot.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn FifthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment PartiallyCommutativeRelation "An instance of #$RelationTypeByLogicalFeature (q.v.) and a specialization of #$AtLeastPartiallyCommutativeRelation.  Each instance  of #$PartiallyCommutativeRelation <code>PARTCOMRELN</code> is a predicate or function that is commutative in some, but not all, of its argument-places (see  #$commutativeInArgs).  More precisely, suppose for example that <code>PARTCOMRELN</code> is a predicate (function) that is commutative only in its odd-numbered  argument-places, and that <code>PARTCOMRELN</code> holds among (has the value <code>VAL</code> for) a given sequence of arguments <code>SEQ</code>.  It follows that <code>PARTCOMRELN</code> also holds among (has the same value <code>VAL</code> for) any other sequence <code>SEQ-PRIME</code> obtainable from <code>SEQ</code> by permuting some or all of the odd-numbered members of <code>SEQ</code> while keeping all of its even-numbered members fixed.
<p>
Note that <code>PARTCOMRELN</code> must  have an arity (see #$arity) of at least three and <i>cannot</i> be an instance of #$CommutativeRelation (as any instance of the latter is commutative in <i>all</i> of its argument-places).
<p>
Instances of #$PartiallyCommutativeRelation include #$pathsJoinAt, #$formsBorderBetween, and #$BlockOfStreetBetweenFn.")
(comment interArgReln1-3 "A binary #$InterArgRelnPredicate (q.v.).  (#$interArgReln1-3 RELN BINPRED) means that a formula built from RELN (i.e. a \"GAF\" or \"NAT\"; see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence and  #$CycLClosedNonAtomicTerm) is semantically well-formed only if BINPRED holds between the first and third arguments to RELN (in that order) in the GAF/NAT.  That is, a formula (RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ..) is semantically well-formed only if (BINPRED ARG1 ARG3) holds.  For further explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations. See also the more general quaternary predicate #$interArgReln, which has two argument-places for specifying the pair of RELN's arguments that must be related by BINPRED.
<p>
If the relationship between the first and third argument is not a constraint, but should be inferred, use the related predicate: #$interArgReln-Inferred.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn FourthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment ProductTypeByMarketCategory "A collection of collections.  Each element of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory is a collection of items that are commonly offered for sale in the same (type of) store, (type of) section of a store, or (type of) catalog.  A specialty store might be devoted to a single element of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory.  The basis for a #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory includes both (1) different brands or variations of a certain kind of product (e.g., hosiery or hammers); and (2) products that are often used together (e.g., kitchen pans and cooking utensils).  Thus, members of a single market category collection need not have any intrinsic similarity; they may simply be things that are often used or purchased together.  For example, work gloves, a soft clothing item, are usually found with hardware or gardening tools.  Commonly, a single element of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory will include elements of several instances of #$ProductType as members; e.g., many brief cases, suit cases, garment bags, trunks, etc., will all be marketed as instances of #$LuggageMarketCategory  Elements of #$ProductType are related to their #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory by #$insMarketedAs.
<p>
Note that #$Briefcase et al. are not specs of #$LuggageMarketCategory; instances of them are merely marketed as such.
<p>
Examples of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory: #$BuildingMaterial, #$HealthAndBeautyMarketCategory, #$BooksAndMagazinesMarketCategory, #$ElectricalSupplyMarketCategory, #$AutoSoundMarketCategory, #$HouseholdFurnishingsMC, #$OfficeProductMarketCategory.")
(comment genlFunctions "A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a given function to another function that \"agrees\" with the first but might be more general in the sense of being defined for a broader class of arguments (i.e. having a #$relationDomain that is a superset of that of the first function). <code>(#$genlFunctions SPECFN GENLFN)</code> means that for any sequence of arguments that <code>SPECFN</code> maps to some particular <code>VALUE</code>, <code>GENLFN</code> also maps that sequence to <code>VALUE</code>.  <code>GENLFN</code> might also be defined for certain argument-sequences for which  <code>SPECFN</code> is not.
<p>
For example, since the integers are a subset of the real numbers, the square-function defined on the integers is related by #$genlFunctions to the square-function defined on the reals.
<p>
Cf. #$genlFuncs for a different predicate not to be confused with this one.")
(comment SquaredFn "A unary #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is an extension of the arithmetical operation of squaring to measurable #$ScalarIntervals generally.  <code>(#$SquaredFn SCALAR)</code> is by definition equal to <code>SCALAR</code> multiplied by itself: <code>(#$TimesFn SCALAR SCALAR)</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$SquaredFn 9) is equal to 81 and (#$SquaredFn (#$Mile 3)) is equal to (#$SquareMile 9).
<p>
See also #$SquaredFn's quasi-inverse, #$SquareRootFn.")
(comment ModeratelyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$ModeratelyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately hilly: those that are moderate in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment startsAfterStartingOf "<code>(#$startsAfterStartingOf LATER-START EARLIER-START)</code> means that <code>LATER-START</code> begins to exists or occur after <code>EARLIER-START</code> begins to exist or occur.  That is, the #$startingPoint of <code>LATER-START</code> is later than the #$startingPoint of <code>EARLIER-START</code> or, equivalently, <code>(#$after (#$StartFn LATER-START) (#$StartFn EARLIER-START))</code>.  This implies nothing about whether, or by how much, <code>LATER-START</code> and <code>EARLIER-START</code> overlap, except that they can't be fully #$cotemporal.")
(comment providerOfMotiveForce "<code>(#$providerOfMotiveForce MOVE OBJ)</code> means that the #$PartiallyTangible <code>OBJ</code> provides, through its expenditure of energy, a significant fraction of the physical force required to make the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> happen. For example, if <code>MOVE</code> is an instance of #$PedalingABicycle, <code>OBJ</code> will be the person who is operating the bike. Another example: if <code>MOVE</code> is a use of a belt sanding tool, then #$providerOfMotiveForce will relate <code>MOVE</code> to both the electric sander and the person operating the sander, since both the sander and the operator supply a significant fraction of the force required to make <code>MOVE</code> happen.")
(comment timeSlices "A #$TemporalPartPredicate and a specialization of both #$temporallySubsumes and #$parts (qq.v.) that relates a temporal thing to any of its \"pure\" temporal parts.  (#$timeSlices TEMP SLICE) means that SLICE is a part of TEMP that is distinguished from TEMP purely in terms of its temporal extent, which is subsumed by the temporal extent of TEMP itself.  In other words, SLICE is \"everything there is to\" TEMP during the particular #$TimeInterval that SLICE occupies.  For example, today's noon hour has the interval from 12:00 noon to 12:30 p.m. today as one of its time-slices; the first act of a particular performance of \"Hamlet\" is a time-slice of that performance of the whole play.
<p>
There is no general agreement on exactly what types of temporal things have time-slices.  Still, a few generalizations can be made.  All and only time-slices of #$TimeIntervals are themselves #$TimeIntervals.  Indeed, one time interval temporally subsumes another if and only if the latter is a time-slice of the former.  All and only time-slices of #$Situations are themselves #$Situations.  It is controversial whether any temporal things besides pure time intervals and temporal situations -- such as ordinary tangible objects -- have time-slices.")
(comment relationExistsExists "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that is used to state that a given binary predicate holds between some two instances of two respective collections. <code>(#$relationExistsExists BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that there is some instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code> and some instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds.  This is equivalent to (and so can be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<pre>
  (#$thereExists ?INST1
    (#$thereExists ?INST2
      (#$and
        (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)
        (#$isa ?INST2 COL2)
        (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)))).
</pre>
<p>
For example, <code>(#$relationExistsExists #$possesses #$Dog #$Bone-BodyPart)</code> means that some dog has a bone.  See the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment arityMin "A #$MetaRelation for specifying the minimum number
of arguments a given #$VariableArityRelation must take if the resulting formula is to be semantically well-formed.  <code>(#$arityMin RELN N)</code> means that semantic well-formedness requires that <code>RELN</code> take at least <code>N</code> arguments at a time.  More precisely: a formula <code>(RELN ARG<sub>1</sub> ... ARG<sub>M</sub>)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>M</code> is greater than or equal to <code>N</code>.  For example, the minimum-arity of any instance of #$UnitOfMeasure is 1.  Note that full semantic well-formedness requires obeying argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) as well as arity constraints. For a general explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible.  See also #$arityMax and #$arity.")
(comment Date "A specialization of #$ContinuousTimeInterval (q.v.).  A continuous time-interval is an instance of #$Date if and only if it has a standardly-defined location on particular calendar.  Here, having a \"standardly-defined location\" means that the interval is a standard unit of time (with respect to some calendar) whose start and end times can be specified in whole-number multiples of that and larger units.  Thus the midnight hour GMT on 1 January 2000 CE is a date; but neither the five-hour-long interval between midnight and 5:00 AM GMT nor the hour-long interval between 12:15 and 1:15 AM GMT on that same day are dates.
<p>
Specializations of #$Date include #$CalendarMilliSecond, #$CalendarMinute, #$CalendarHour, #$PolarDay, #$CalendarWeekend, #$FiscalYear, and  #$CalendarMillennium.")
(comment relationInstanceExists "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate holds between a specified thing and some instance of a given collection.  <code>(#$relationInstanceExists PRED THING COL)</code> means that there exists an instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(PRED THING INST)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
     (#$thereExists ?INST
          (#$and
               (#$isa ?INST COL)
               (PRED THING ?INST))) .
</pre>
<p>
For example, '(#$relationInstanceExists #$geographicalSubRegions #$Iran #$OilField)' means that there exists an oilfield in Iran.  Cf. #$relationExistsInstance, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment integerBetween "A ternary #$IntangibleObjectRelatingPredicate that holds among #$Integers only.  <code>(#$integerBetween LOW MED HIGH)</code> means that <code>HIGH</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo <code>MED</code>, and <code>MED</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo <code>LOW</code>.")
(comment arg5Isa "A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg5Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the fifth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5</code> ...) is semantically well-formed only if  <code>(#$isa ARG5 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.")
(comment multiplierTypeForQuantityType "A binary predicate that relates a #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType (q.v.) to a #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType (q.v.) from which the former's instances are derivable via multiplication.  <code>(#$multiplierTypeForQuantityType DERIVED-TYPE PRIMITIVE-TYPE)</code> means that each instance of <code>DERIVED-TYPE</code> is the result of multiplying (see #$TimesFn ) some quantity by an instance of <code>PRIMITIVE-TYPE</code>.

For example, <code>(#$multiplierTypeForQuantityType #$Volume #$Distance)</code> means that each #$Volume is equal to the result of multiplying some quantity by a #$Distance.  Thus the volume (#$CubicMeter 40) is the result of multiplying the area (#$SquareMeter 10) by the distance (#$Meter 4).

Note that the multiplicand quantity might itself be 1 (or another number) over some quantity.  Thus <code>(#$multiplierTypeForQuantityType #$Speed #$Distance)</code> is exemplified by the fact that the speed (#$MilesPerHour 35) is equal to the result of multiplying the quantity (#$QuotientFn 1 (#$HoursDuration 1)) by the distance (#$Mile 35).

See also #$divisorTypeForQuantityType.")
(comment Ion "A collection of microscopic-scale objects; a subset of #$ChemicalObject.  Every instance of #$Ion is an atom or radical which has either a negative or positive net electrical charge, due to having either more or fewer electrons than there are protons in its nuclei.
<p>
Note: atomic ions violate Cyc's default assumption that instances of #$Atom have a net electrical charge of zero, so #$Ion constitutes a class of exceptions to that rule.")
(comment relationExistsCountAll "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that is used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  (#$relationExistsCountAll BINPRED COL1 COL2 N) means that for every instance INST of COL2 there are exactly N instances of COL1 (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) such that (BINPRED INST(i) INST) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  It is equivalent to (and so can be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?INST COL2)
    (#$thereExistExactly N ?INST-I
      (#$and
             (#$isa ?INST COL1)
             (BINPRED ?INST-I ?INST)))) .
</pre>
<p>
For example, (#$relationExistsCountAll #$anatomicalParts #$Animal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart 1) means that every animal head is a part of exactly one animal.  See also #$relationAllExistsCount and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment relationInstanceExistsMax "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to a given thing and a given collection.  (#$relationInstanceExistsMax BINPRED THING COL N) means that there exists at most N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL that THING bears the relation PRED to, such that (PRED INST(i) THING) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$thereExistAtMost N ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL)
(BINPRED THING ?INST)))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationInstanceExistsMax #$physicalParts MyLeftHand #$Thumb 1)' means that my left hand has at most one thumb.  Cf. #$relationExistsMaxInstance, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment relationAllExistsMany "A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relationship with respect to two collections.  <code>(#$relationAllExistsMany BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL1</code> there are many (see #$Many-Quant) instances <code>INST<sub>i</sub></code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST INST<sub>i</sub>)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to <code>(#$relationAllExistsRange BINPRED COL1 COL2 #$Many-Quant)</code>, and thus to the more complex form (for which it can in effect be used as shorthand):
<p>
<pre>
  (#$implies
     (#$isa ?INST COL1)
     (#$thereExistRange #$Many-Quant ?INST-I
        (#$and
           (#$isa ?INST-I COL2)
           (BINPRED ?INST ?INST-I)))).
</pre>
<p>
For example, <code>(#$relationAllExistsMany #$anatomicalParts #$Dog #$Hair-Strand)</code> means that every dog has many hairs.  See also #$relationAllExistsMin, #$relationAllExistsMax, #$relationAllExistsCount, and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment arity "A #$MetaRelation used for stating that a given  relation takes a specified number of arguments.  <code>(#$arity RELN N)</code>  means that that semantic well-formedness requires that <code>RELN</code> take  exactly <code>N</code> arguments at a time.  That is, a formula  <code>(RELN ARG<sub>1</sub> ... ARG<sub>M</sub>)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>M</code> = <code>N</code>.
<p>
For example, the #$arity of any instance of #$BinaryPredicate is 2.
<p>
Note that full semantic well-formedness requires obeying argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) as well as arity constraints.  For a general explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible.  See also #$CycLExpression-Askable.")
(comment uniquePhysicalPartTypes "The predicate #$uniquePhysicalPartTypes relates two sub-collections of #$PartiallyTangible.  <code>(#$uniquePhysicalPartTypes BIG SMALL)</code> means that every instance of  <code>BIG</code> has exactly one instance of <code>SMALL</code> as a physical part (see the predicate #$physicalParts).")
(comment holdsIn "An instance of both #$TemporalPredicate and #$ModalPredicate that is used to state (in a somewhat indirect way) that a given sentence is true during a certain time period.  <code>(#$holdsIn TEMP SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> is true at every moment in the #$temporalExtent (q.v.) of the #$TemporalThing <code>TEMP</code>.
<p>
For example, the sentence <code>(#$holdsIn (#$YearFn 2003) (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> means that throughout all of the year 2003 Curtis had Patches as a pet.  This entails (e.g.) that Curtis had Patches on 5 July 2003:
<pre>
  (#$holdsIn
    (#$DayFn 5 (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 2003)))
    (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> .
</pre>
<p>
Similarly, <code>(#$holdsIn #$Patches-Cat (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> means that Curtis has Patches as a pet for Patches's entire life.
<p>
See also the more general predicate #$holdsSometimeDuring.
<p>
Note that a sentence of the form <code>(#$holdsIn TEMP (PRED ... ARGi ...))</code>, where <code>ARGi</code> is a #$TemporalThing, does <i>not</i> in general imply that <code>ARGi</code> temporally subsumes or even temporally intersects <code>TEMP</code>.  For example, <code>(#$holdsIn (#$YearFn 2003) (#$awareOf #$Curtis #$GeorgeWashington))</code> obviously doesn't imply that #$GeorgeWashington was alive in 2003.  <code>ARGi</code>'s temporally subsuming <code>TEMP</code> <i>does</i> follow, however, if <code>PRED</code> is a #$CotemporalPredicate that is #$contemporaryInArg (q.v.) in its <code>i</code>th argument-place (as #$hasPets is, in both argument-places).
<p>
Note also that there are two other ways to temporally-qualify sentences in CycL, which do not involve using #$holdsIn (or #$holdsSometimeDuring).  Both involve temporally qualifying the #$Microtheory in which the (otherwise unqualified) sentence is asserted, and either can be used to state something roughly equivalent to a #$holdsIn sentence.  Consider the first Curtis/Patches example above.  First, one could assert <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in some microtheory <code>MT</code> such that <code>(#$holdsInTime-Always MT (#$YearFn 2003))</code>.  Second, one could assert <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in a microtheory <code>MT'</code> such that both <code>(#$mtTimeIndex MT' (#$YearFn 2003))</code> and <code>(#$mtTimeParameter MT' #$TimePoint)</code> hold.  This later method, being the most developed and well-supported, is generally preferred over the others.
<p>
Note finally that it would be incorrect to assert a sentence like <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in a non-temporally-qualified microtheory like the #$BaseKB, since Curtis didn't have Patches when (e.g.) he was a young boy, let alone in 3500 BCE.")
(comment quantityIntersects "A binary predicate that relates numerically measurable #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) that intersect or overlap.  <code>(#$quantityIntersects SCALAR1 SCALAR2)</code> means that  <code>SCALAR1</code> and <code>SCALAR2</code> intersect. More precisely: there is  some scalar interval (which might even be as small as an  instance of #$ScalarPointValue) that is subsumed by (see #$quantitySubsumes) both <code>SCALAR1</code> and <code>SCALAR2</code>.
<p>
For example, (#$Meter 2 5) -- the #$Distance of between 2 and 5 meters inclusive -- intersects both (#$Meter 3 4) and (#$Meter 3 7), but does not intersect (#$Meter 6 7).")
(comment apprentices "A #$PersonalAssociationPredicate and a specialization of #$teacherOf.  <code>(#$apprentices PERSON APPRENTICE)</code> means that <code>APPRENTICE</code> is an apprentice of <code>PERSON</code>.")
(comment purposeInEvent "(#$purposeInEvent <code>AGENT EVENT SENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> takes part in <code>EVENT</code> deliberately (see #$deliberateActors), and does so  because both (1) <code>AGENT</code> wants the #$Proposition (q.v.) expressed by <code>SENT</code> to be (or become) true and (2) <code>AGENT</code> believes that <code>EVENT</code>'s occurring will make (or help to make) this happen.")
(comment dictator "A #$BinaryPredicate and a specialization of #$hasAsHeadOfGovernment.  <code>(#$dictator C D)</code> means that <code>D</code> is the #$Dictator-HeadOfGovernment of the #$Country <code>C</code>.")
(comment Determiner-ClassA "The collection of #$Determiners which can occur before certain instances of #$Determiner-Central.  #$Predeterminers include
(i) <i>all</i>, <i>both</i> and <i>half</i> (e.g. <i>all</i> (the) girls, <i>both</i> those cars, <i>half</i> an hour)
(ii) the multipliers <i>double</i>, <i>twice</i>, <i>three times</i>, etc. (e.g. <i>double</i> the sum, <i>twice</i> my salary)
(iii) the fractions <i>one-third</i>, <i>one-fifth</i>, etc. (e.g. <i>one-third</i> the time)
(iv) <i>such</i>, <i>what</i> (e.g. <i>Such</i> a surprise! <i>What</i> a fine day!)")
(comment Molecule "A subcollection of #$ChemicalObject.  Each instance of #$Molecule is a microscopic-scale object whose component atoms are all chemically bonded to each other; typically, this means that the atoms are covalently bonded.  Examples: some instances of #$Molecule, including molecules of oxygen (O2), ozone (O3), water (H2O), carbon monoxide (CO), carbon dioxide (CO2), etc., contain covalently bonded atoms belonging to the collection #$Oxygen.  Some highly complex examples of #$Molecule are those belonging to the collection #$DNAMolecule.  See also #$MoleculeFn.
<p>
Note: #$Diamond and other macroscopic covalent solids are excluded from belonging to #$Molecule by the constraint that instances of #$Molecule are microscopic.")
(comment PlanningFn "A #$UnaryFunction that takes specializations of #$PurposefulAction as arguments.  <code>(#$PlanningFn ACTION-TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of all instances of #$Planning in which some instance of <code>ACTION-TYPE</code> is planned.  For example, (#$PlanningFn #$MilitaryAttack) denotes the collection of all #$Plannings in which some #$MilitaryAttack is planned.")
(comment Determiner "The collection of all determiners. Determiners are function words which can precede nouns (or its premodifiers). Example: <i>these</i>.  #$Determiners are further classified into #$Predeterminer, #$Determiner-Central and #$Postdeterminer.  Also note the determiners <i>the</i> (see #$Determiner-Definite) and <i>a</i>/<i>an</i> (see #$Determiner-Indefinite).")
(comment CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder is a collection whose instances are conventionally regarded as being ordered by some relation <code>RELN</code>, where <code>RELN</code> orders the members of <code>COL</code> in the manner in which events are ordered in linear time.
<p>
More precisely, a collection is an instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder just in case its instances are conventially regarded as being ordered by a relation <code>RELN</code> such that the #$MathematicalOrdering whose #$baseSet is <code>COL</code>, and whose #$orderingRelation is <code>RELN</code>, is an instance of #$EventLikeOrdering (q.v.).
<p>
For example, the instances of #$Distance are conventionally regarded as being ordered by the relation <i><b>greater than</b></i>, and this ordering is event-like.  So #$Distance is a collection with an event-like order.")
(comment GroupFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  When applied to an instance <code>COL</code> of #$FirstOrderCollection, #$GroupFn returns the collection of all groups whose members (see #$groupMembers) are instances of <code>COL</code>.  Note that an application of #$GroupFn is a <i>collection</i> that has groups as instances, rather than an individual group. For example, (#$GroupFn #$BallisticMissile) denotes the collection of all groups of ballistic missiles, which includes Russia's ballistic missiles, China's ballistic missiles, the US's ballistic missiles, and so on.")
(comment endsAfterEndOfInSituationType "<code>(#$endsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> ends after <code>SIT2</code> ends.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$endsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment arg1Genl "A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg1Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the first argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG1</code> ...) is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG1 COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a similar but more flexible  ternary predicate.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (RightObjectOfPairFn SecondRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment hasBeliefSystems "This predicate relates intelligent agents to belief-systems (such as religions, philosophical schools, political theories, etc.) that they accept. <code>(#$hasBeliefSystems AGENT SYSTEM)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> holds the #$BeliefSystem <code>SYSTEM</code>.  For example, (#$hasBeliefSystems BorisYeltsin #$RussianOrthodoxReligion) holds.")
(comment diameterOfObject "This predicate relates two- or three-dimensional circular- or spherical-shaped objects to their diameters, represented as #$Distances. <code>(#$diameterOfObject OBJECT DIAMETER)</code> means that he diameter of <code>OBJECT</code> is <code>DIAMETER</code>.  See also #$radius.")
(comment DensityOfPopulation "A #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (q.v.) and a specialization of #$RatioQuantity (q.v.).  Quantities that are instances of #$DensityOfPopulation apply to (see #$quantityRange) #$GeographicalRegions, and measure -- typically in a generic way (e.g. via a #$GenericValueFunction) -- the number of human inhabitants per some (specified or unspecified) unit of area (e.g. #$SquareMile).
<p>
Generic instances of #$DensityOfPopulation include #$UrbanPopulationDensity (defined to be equal to (#$HighToVeryHighAmountFn #$DensityOfPopulation)) and #$DesolateAreaPopulationDensity (defined to be equal to (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$DensityOfPopulation)).  A non-generic instance is
<p>
<pre>
    (#$QuotientFn ((#$UnitOfCountFn #$HomoSapiens) 1000) (#$SquareMile 1)) ,
</pre>
<p>
the population density of <i>1000-people-per-square-mile</i>.
<p>
To relate a given region to a #$DensityOfPopulation, use the predicate #$populationDensity.  See also #$populationOfRegion, #$populationGrowthRate, and #$populationRange.")
(comment arg2Format "An instance of #$ArgFormatPredicate (q.v.) used to place a particular #$Format (q.v.) constraint on the second (or \"arg2\") argument-place of a given predicate.  <code>(#$arg2Format PRED FORMAT)</code> means that <code>PRED</code>'s arg2 is constrained to <code>FORMAT</code>.  See the reified instances #$Format for
further details.")
(comment GenericValueTypeFunction "A specialization of #$CollectionDenotingFunction whose instances map event-like-ordered collection-types to certain instances of those collection-types.  Each instance <code>GVT-FUNC</code> of #$GenericValueTypeFunction corresponds to a certain generic, non-numerically-specified \"degree\" (e.g. <i>slightly, moderately, highly</i>) to which a property can be exemplified; when applied to a collection-type <code>COLTYPE</code> that is a #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder (q.v.), <code>GVT-FUNC</code> returns the instance <code>COL</code> of <code>COLTYPE</code> such that <code>COL</code>'s instances are those that exemplify the relevant property to the appropriate degree.
<p>
For example, #$HighlyFn is a #$GenericValueTypeFunction, and #$RegionTypeByHilliness is an ordered collection-type that \"divides up\" the collection of geographical regions according to how hilly they are.  So (#$HighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the instance <code>HIGHLY-HILLY</code> of #$RegionTypeByHilliness that is the collection of all geographical regions that are <i>highly hilly</i> in relation to other geographical regions.  Each instance of <code>HIGHLY-HILLY</code> will have a higher degree of hilliness than the instances of any other collection (i.e. instance of #$RegionTypeByHilliness) lower in the same ordering.
<p>
Some other instances of #$GenericValueTypeFunction are #$SlightlyFn, #$ModeratelyFn, and #$ModeratelyToHighlyFn.
<p>
The #$GenericValueTypeFunctions can be loosely thought of as type-level versions of the #$GenericValueFunctions (cf.).")
(comment HighlyFn "An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$HighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.
<p>
For example, (#$HighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are highly hilly: those that are high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.")
(comment isa "A #$TaxonomicSlot (q.v.) that relates things of any  kind to collections (see #$Collection) of which they are instances.   <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is an instance of the collection <code>COL</code>.   #$isa is by far the most commonly-occurring predicate in the Cyc Knowledge  Base, and is one of the relations most fundamental to the Cyc ontology. An important logical feature of #$isa is that it \"transfers through\"  #$genls (q.v.): <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code> and <code>(#$genls COL SUPERCOL)</code> jointly  imply <code>(#$isa THING SUPERCOL)</code>.  This logical feature is expressed in a  rule that -- along with various other rules that also sanction the  derivation of #$isa sentences -- has been asserted to the Knowledge Base.   As a result, only a small fraction of the #$isa based ground atomic  formulas (or \"GAF\"s; see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) the Cyc system  \"knows\" to be true have been manually asserted; the vast majority  have been derived, or are derivable, automatically by the Cyc inference  engine via the use of such rules.  See also #$elementOf.")
(comment Language "A specialization of #$CommunicationConvention. Each instance of this collection is a generative system of signs for transmitting information. These signs are normally conventional and arbitrary in nature, though the case of body language is a (borderline) exception to this.
<p>
The reality of any given language consists in its being interpretable by some community (usually human -- cf. #$HumanLanguage). This is not quite true of #$ComputerLanguages, however such languages are only possible insofar as some kind of automation of the process of interpretation (by computers) has been effected. In the case of #$DeadLanguages, the community may not longer exist (although even if no-one can _speak_ such languages any longer, currently-interpretable texts often remain, cf., for example, #$AtticGreekDialect).
<p>
Languages are most often spoken, but not always (cf. #$SignLanguage). It is also worth noting that not every system of signs is a language; a certain richness of possible expression is required. Thus #$MorseCode, for example, though a #$CommunicationConvention, is not an instance of #$Language. Finally, this collection is not intended to cover the special vocabulary and usages of a scientific, professional or other group, or a characteristic style of speech or writing (e.g. 'Shakespearean language').")
(comment agreeingAgents "A #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate and specialization of #$normativelyBoundAgents that relates an #$Agreement (q.v.) to the agents who made or are making the agreement.  <code>(#$agreeingAgents AGREE PARTY)</code> means that <code>AGREE</code> has <code>PARTY</code> among its agreeing parties.  Thus, <code>PARTY</code> will be mentioned in the content of <code>AGREE</code>.
<p>
Specializations of #$agreeingAgents include #$agreeingBuyer, #$agreeingSeller, #$policyHolder, #$insuringAgent, #$employedAgent, and #$employingAgent.")
(comment BinaryPropertyPredicate "The collection of #$BinaryPredicates that are used to attribute properties to things, by relating them either to (i) #$ScalarIntervals (especially #$Quantity[ies] or #$RealNumbers), (ii) property-like collections (i.e. collections of things that, intuitively, share an intrinsic property), or (iii) #$GenericValuePredicates (q.v.).
<p>
For example, #$eyeColor is a #$BinaryPropertyPredicate that relates an #$Animal to a collection that is a #$Color, according to the color of the animal's eyes.  And #$thirstLevel is a binary property-predicate that relates an animal to a generic-value predicate (e.g. #$highAmountOf), according to how thirsty the animal is.
<p>
For binary property-predicates that relates things to #$ScalarIntervals only, see the specialization #$ScalarIntervalSlot.")
(comment playsRoleInActionTypeRegularly "<code>(#$playsRoleInActionTypeRegularly THING SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that the #$TemporalThing <code>THING</code> takes part in #$Situations of type <code>SITTYPE</code> in the role <code>ROLE</code> multiple times, and there is a fairly predictable temporal interval between such occurrences. If the frequency is known and/or relevant, the more specific predicate #$frequencyOfActionType can be used.")
(comment NoAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$NoAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is approximately <i>no-amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context (see #$ApproximatelyFn and #$ScalarProperInterval).
<p>
For example, (#$NoAmountFn #$Elasticity) is roughly zero elasticity: a property of things that (for all intents and purposes) don't re-expand at all following compression.  It is less than (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Elasticity).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$noAmountOf.")
(comment QuantitySlot "A specialization of #$ScalarIntervalSlot (q.v.).  Each instance of #$QuantitySlot is a binary predicate that is used to attribute a property to a thing, by relating it to a #$Quantity (q.v.) of some sort.
<p>
Instances of #$QuantitySlot include #$lengthOfObject and #$levelOfMorale.
<p>
See also the specializations #$MeasurableQuantitySlot and #$NonNumericQuantitySlot.")
(comment constrainingObject "An #$ActorSlot for objects that serve physically to constrain the movements of other objects in #$MovementEvents, either by affecting their trajectories, by limiting or reducing their speeds, by determining the general types of movements they undergo (e.g. rotational vs. translational), or by having some other constrictive effect on their motions. <code>(#$constrainingObject MOTION OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> physically constrains the motion of some moving object (see #$objectMoving) in <code>MOTION</code>.  <code>OBJECT</code> must be in physical contact with (see #$touches) this moving object during at least part of <code>MOTION</code>.   <code>OBJECT</code> might or might not be in motion itself.  Situations in which #$constrainingObjects applies include: an axle constraining a wheel turning, a car or other #$transporter carrying passengers, and a railroad track guiding a train.  A road driven on by a car is a marginally negative example of a constraining-object: although the road facilitates the car's motion and in typical cases indirectly determines (via the driver's steering) its trajectory, the car can be driven off the road.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn FourthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment buyingPerformer "<code>(#$buyingPerformer COM AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is the agent actually attempting to obtain goods or services in a purchase, by performing an active role in the #$CommercialActivity <code>COM</code>.  Generally, the agent who is the #$buyingPerformer is identical with the #$buyer, but in some cases the #$buyingPerformer is instead a #$buyingAgent representing the #$buyer.")
(comment (PartTypeFn (LeftObjectOfPairFn SixthRib) BoneShaft) "<p>")
(comment greaterThan "A #$NumericComparisonPredicate that is a generalization of the  mathematical greater-than ( > ) relation to #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see #$NumericInterval and #$MeasurableQuantity) as well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  <code>(#$greaterThan VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> means that  <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than <code>VALUE2</code> with respect to some scale that they  are both on.  More precisely, there is some #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType SCALE that both <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> are instances of and either (i) <code>SCALE</code> is a specialization of  #$NumericInterval (e.g. #$RealNumber) and the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE2</code> or (ii) <code>(#$followingValueOnScale VALUE2 VALUE1 SCALE)</code> holds.")
(comment relationInstanceExistsMin "A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to a given thing and a given collection.  (#$relationInstanceExistsMin BINPRED THING COL N) means that there exists at least N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL that THING bears the relation PRED to, such that (PRED THING INST(i)) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:
<p>
(#$thereExistAtLeast N ?INST
(#$and
(#$isa ?INST COL)
(BINPRED THING ?INST))) .
<p>
For example, `(#$relationInstanceExistsMin #$physicalParts MyLeftHand #$Thumb 1)' means that my left hand has at least one thumb.  Cf. #$relationExistsMinInstance, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment infoTransferred "A #$BinaryRolePredicate that is used to specify some or all of the meaningful content of a particular transferral of information (see  #$InformationTransferEvent).  <code>(#$infoTransferred TRANSFER INFO)</code> means  that <code>INFO</code> is information (see #$AspatialInformationStore) that is  transferred due to <code>TRANSFER</code>.  <code>INFO</code> originates (see #$informationOrigin) from some agent or #$InformationBearingThing (\"IBT\") and, after <code>TRANSFER</code>,  is had by another agent or is contained in another IBT (see  #$informationDestination); <code>INFO</code> may persist in the origin as well.  For example, when I read the front page of the newspaper, some  information about current world events is transferred from the  newspaper to me.")
(comment CharacterString "A specialization of #$AbstractInformationStructure.  Each instance of #$CharacterString is a string of characters or an abstract sequence of symbols.  Note that an instance of #$CharacterString is <i>not</i> any particular physical, tangible representation, since different encodings may represent the same string of characters.  An instance of #$CharacterString is a list (see the collection #$List) of characters (instances of #$Character-Abstract) from some fixed character set. An instance of #$CharacterString may be any finite length, including zero (the zero-length string is the empty string, which has no characters).  Notable specializations of #$CharacterString include #$EMailAddress, #$AreaCode, #$PhoneNumber, and #$TelephoneCountryCode.  Note that a #$CharacterString is ordered formally as a #$List (rather than physically left-to-right or top-to-bottom); thus the 'first' character in the #$CharacterString for an Arabic word happens to be the rightmost (first pronounced) character, not the leftmost character, due to the letter-order and word-order convention for Arabic writing.")
(disjointWith BabyPlayYard AmmunitionBelt)
(comment FundamentalUnitType "A #$ConventionalClassificationType and a specialization of #$InterconvertibleUnitType (q.v.).  Each #$FundamentalUnitType is a collection of interconvertible #$UnitOfMeasures (q.v.) that is taken to be basic in the sense that it not analyzable in terms of other unit-types (see #$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis).  Thus, the instances of a fundamental unit-type are not defined in terms of other units (see #$unitDefinitions).  Other, \"derived\" unit-types are analyzible into fundamental unit-types.
<p>
For example, #$UnitOfDistance and #$UnitOfTime are standardly taken to be fundamental unit-types, but #$UnitOfSpeed (which is analyzable in terms of them) is not.
<p>
Note that it is a matter of convention which unit-types are considered to be fundamental.  It is possible to have a system wherein, e.g., speed and time are fundamental and distance is derived from them.  The unit-types that are standardly taken to be fundamental in modern science were chosen because they are the easiest to measure.")
(disjointWith BabyPlayYard ClosetOrganizer)
(disjointWith BabyPlayYard Coffin)
(disjointWith BiologicalLivingObject SomethingToWearPart)
(disjointWith ClosetOrganizer Coffin)
(disjointWith AmmunitionBelt Coffin)
(disjointWith ClosetOrganizer Cradle)
(comment DeployingAChemicalOrBiologicalAgent "The collection of all purposeful #$DistributionEvents in which an #$IntelligentAgent deploys a #$BiochemicallyHarmfulSubstance via dispersal into the air, into water, our into a food supply.  Under ordinary circumstances we can reasonably infer that this will be done as part of a #$WeaponOfMassDestructionAttack on some group of opponents, either civilian or military.")
(comment schemaFields "(#$schemaFields SCHEMA FIELD) means that FIELD is a component field in the schema SCHEMA.  This is the most general relation in the SKSI vocabulary for linking a schema with its component fields.  The arg 1 may be
either a logical schema or a physical schema (with SCHEMA an instance of #$Schema-LogicalOrPhysical), and the arg
2 may be either a logical field or a physical field (with FIELD an instance of #$Field-LogicalOrPhysical). This predicste is partitioned by the specializations #$logicalFields and #$physicalFields.")
(comment DangerousTangibleThing "#$DangerousTangibleThings are those #$PartiallyTangibles which have some combination of density, size, material strength, etc., that makes them particularly efficient at damaging (see #$damages) other #$PartiallyTangible things. The classic examples are those things whose #$typePrimaryFunction are to be #$instrument-UsedAsWeapon, namely #$Weapons. Simply being a spec of this collection does not imply that something is intended to be used to harm others; for example, #$Automobiles are #$DangerousTangibleThings even though that's hardly their #$typePrimaryFunction.")
(comment DisjointCollectionType "A collection of collections of collections and a specialization of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType (q.v.).  A disjoint collection type is such that its instances are collections that are all disjoint from one another.  That is, each instance <code>DISCOLTYPE</code> of #$DisjointCollectionType is a collection whose instances are mutually disjoint collections: no two instances of <code>DISCOLTYPE</code> have any instances in common.  Instances of #$DisjointCollectionType include #$BiologicalSpecies and #$SportsTeamTypeBySport.")
(comment increasesSlotFor "(#$increasesSlotFor EVT OBJ SLOT) says that as a result of OBJ's participation in EVT, the SLOT for OBJ is increased.  For example, (#$increasesSlotFor IGainWeight Me #$massOfObject) says that in the particular weight-gaining event in question, my mass is increased.  Note that where EVT is an instance of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent, (#$increasesSlotFor EVT OBJ SLOT) is equivalent to (#$increasesSlot EVT SLOT).")
(comment NaturalTangibleStuff "An instance of #$ExistingStuffType and a sub-collection of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$NaturalTangibleStuff is a naturally occurring partially tangible thing. Specializations of #$NaturalTangibleStuff include #$LandStuff, #$Wood, and #$Air.  Man-made materials are <i>not</i> instances of #$NaturalTangibleStuff.")
(comment (TransportViaFn LandTransportationDevice) "A collection of events.  An instance of (#$TransportViaFn #$LandTransportationDevice) is a transportation event in which a #$vehicle designed to travel over land is used.  Examples include instances of the collections (#$TransportViaFn #$RoadVehicle), #$Skateboarding, and #$SnowSkiing.  Note: This collection does not include instances of #$AnimalWalkingProcess, which is a #$LocomotionProcess-Animal (q.v.), not a form of transportation as defined in Cyc.  A marginal example of this would be a (fictional) event in which someone burrowed through the earth in a Jules-Vernesque real-time tunnel-digging machine.")
(comment in-ContOpen "An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  <code>(#$in-ContOpen OBJ CONT)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is contained in <code>CONT</code> (so that <code>CONT</code> confines or holds <code>OBJ</code> in), but <code>OBJ</code> can be removed from <code>CONT</code> without having to open any portals in <code>CONT</code>, break some part of <code>CONT</code>, or break <code>OBJ</code> into smaller pieces such that it can fit through some portal of <code>CONT</code>.  Note that <code>(#$in-ContOpen OBJ CONT)</code> is a relationship based on the size of <code>OBJ</code> and the largest portal of <code>CONT</code> through which <code>OBJ</code> might be able to move.  For example, a paperclip might be #$in-ContOpen a jail cell even though the person locked inside would be #$in-ContClosed (q.v.) the jail cell.  This is because the paper clip is small enough to fit in between the bars of the cell, whereas the person is not.")
(comment traverses-Complete "<code>(#$traverses-Complete MOVE TRAVER)</code> means that the #$Movement-TranslationEvent <code>MOVE</code> traverses a path or series of paths (#$Path-Generics forming the #$Traversal <code>TRAVER</code>) during its motion, and that <code>TRAVER</code> is the entire traversal from beginning to end of the movement.  The traversal may cross itself, go back and forth along the same path, or repeat cycles of paths (unlike the #$ActorSlot #$motionPathway-Complete, which can follow only one, non-self-crossing, pre-existing #$Path-Generic.)  See also #$traverses-Partial.  Contrast #$traverses-Complete with #$motionPathway-Complete, that relates to one existing #$Path-Generic, and with #$trajectory-Complete that relates to the pure #$Trajectory of the motion.")
(comment LoanRepaying "The process of making payments in order to bring the balance towards 0.")
(comment BorderBetweenFn "An instance of both #$PartialFunction and #$BinaryFunction.  When applied to instances <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code> of #$GeographicalRegion, #$BorderBetweenFn returns the entire #$Border (if any) that separates <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code>.  
<p>
For instance, (#$BorderBetweenFn (#$TerritoryFn #$France) (#$TerritoryFn #$Germany)) is the entire border between (#$TerritoryFn #$France) and (#$TerritoryFn #$Germany).  
<p>
Note that the border returned by #$BorderBetweenFn may be discontinuous (such as the border between France and Spain, interrupted by Andorra), or circular (such as the border between Italy and San Marino), or in abnormal cases it may even have multiple disconnected and nested cyclic fragments (e.g., the border between Belgium and the Netherlands, due to the Baarle-Nassau and Baarle-Hartog enclaves), or move back and forth periodically (such as at the Isle des Faisans).  Note also that #$BorderBetweenFn is undefined for pairs of regions that do not actually share a border, such as (#$TerritoryFn #$France) and (#$TerritoryFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica).  See also #$borderBetweenRegions, which is a (canonically) corresponding predicate for this function.")
(comment traverses-Partial "<code>(#$traverses-Partial MOVE TRAV)</code> means that the moving object <code>OBJ</code> in the #$Translation-SingleTrajectory <code>MOVE</code> (see #$objectMoving) is partially confined to the #$Traversal <code>TRAV</code> during <code>MOVE</code>.  To say that <code>OBJ</code> is partially confined to <code>TRAV</code> during <code>MOVE</code>, we mean that (1) there is a subevent <code>SUBEVENT</code> of <code>MOVE</code> such that <code>(#$subTranslations-SingleTrajectory MOVE SUBEVENT)</code> holds, and (2) there is a sub-Traversal <code>SUBTRAV</code> of <code>TRAV</code> (see #$subTraversals) such that <code>SUBEVENT</code> is confined to <code>SUBTRAV</code>, i.e., <code>(#$traverses-Complete SUBEVENT SUBTRAV)</code> holds.")
(comment borderBetweenRegions "<code>(#$borderBetweenRegions BORDER REG1 REG2)</code> means that <code>BORDER</code> is the entire intangible border shared by the adjacent geographical regions <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code>.  Note that <code>BORDER</code> might be discontinuous (such as the border between France and Spain, interrupted by Andorra), or circular (such as the border between Italy and San Marino), or even have multiple disconnected and nested cyclic fragments (the border between Belgium and Netherlands, due to the Baarle-Nassau and Baarle-Hartog enclaves).  To identify arbitrarily large or small segments of borders, see #$formsBorderPart.  See also #$BorderBetweenFn, to which #$borderBetweenRegions is a (canonically) corresponding predicate.")
(comment formsBorderPart "This predicate is used to assert that a particular #$SpatialThing-Localized is part of the conventional abstraction that is a #$GeopoliticalBorder. <code>(#$formsBorderPart PART WHOLE)</code> means that all of the points along the border-part <code>PART</code> are also points along the border <code>WHOLE</code>. We would use this predicate, eg, to say that the border between Texas and Mexico forms part of the border of the United States:  (#$formsBorderPart (#$BorderBetweenFn #$Texas-State #$Mexico) (#$BorderFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)).")
(comment TransportationEvent "A specialization of both #$Conveying-Generic and #$Translation-Complete (qq.v.), instances of which are events in which one or more objects transport one or more other objects.  Each instance  of #$TransportationEvent is an event in which an object (in the role of #$transporter) aids in the translational movement of another object (having the role of #$transportees), so that both objects move together along the same complete pathway (see #$motionPathway-Complete).  Optionally, one of these objects, or some third object moving along with them, provides the force to make the movement happen (see #$providerOfMotiveForce).
<p>
Examples of transportation events include automobile transportation, riding a bicycle, dogs pulling goods on a sled, a wagon with groceries rolling down a hill, a person carrying clothes in a suitcase, etc.  In that last case, note that the #$transporter is the suitcase, not the person.  
<p>
Note that the transporter in a transportation event need not be in motion relative to its destination throughout the transportation event; an automobile transportation is a single transportation event even if it has sub-events in which the driver and all of the passengers disembark while the car is parked and refueled.  A single transportation event may also have more than one transporter.  For example, a sofa may be transported accross a living room floor by two people working together.  
<p>
Events which are <i>not</i> #$TransportationEvents include a river conveying some flotsam, the wind blowing a leaf, a conveyor belt moving a widget to the next person on an assembly line, or a walking beast of burden that is carrying nothing.  The first three of these negative examples are instances of #$Conveying-Stationary, since the would-be #$transporter doesn't actually change its overall location (see #$conveyor-Stationary); in the fourth case the unburdened beast has no #$transportees and the event is an instance of #$AnimalWalkingProcess.")
(comment trueSentence "A #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) and specialization of #$consistent (q.v.) that is used to state that a given CycL sentence is true.  <code>(#$trueSentence SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> is true.
<p>
#$trueSentence is a non-assertible predicate (see  #$notAssertible).  But this puts no real limitation on the expressive power of the Cyc system since one can assert that <code>SENT</code> is true by simply asserting <code>SENT</code> itself.  
<p>
Most occurrences of the expression '#$trueSentence' in CycL assertions have been added by the canonicalizer, so that certain nested sentences (or variables occurring in argument-positions intended for sentences) can receive special handling during canonicalization or inference. See the accompanying #$cyclistNotes for details.")
(comment transporterTypeOfTransportType "The corresponding predicate of #$TransportViaFn.  <code>(#$transporterTypeOfTransportType EVENT-TYPE TRANS-TYPE)</code> means that in events that are instances of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code>, instances of <code>TRANS-TYPE</code> are the types of transportation devices used.")
(comment BorderFn "<code>(#$BorderFn OBJECT)</code> denotes the entire border <code>BORDER</code> of <code>OBJECT</code>.  If <code>OBJECT</code> is two-dimensional <code>BORDER</code> is one-dimensional (e.g. a linear curve or perimeter), while if <code>OBJECT</code> is three-dimensional <code>BORDER</code> is two-dimensional (i.e. a surface).")
(comment borderOf "<code>(#$borderOf BORDER REGION)</code> means that the entire border of <code>REGION</code> is <code>BORDER</code>.")
(comment topicOfInfoTransfer "The predicate #$topicOfInfoTransfer is used to indicate what a particular communication is about.  <code>(#$topicOfInfoTransfer TRANS TOPIC)</code> means that <code>TOPIC</code> is a primary topic of the information communicated in the #$InformationTransferEvent <code>TRANS</code>.  For example, a result reported after an instance of #$BloodTest will contain information about the #$objectTested (viz., the blood sample); the patient's blood is thus a #$topicOfInfoTransfer of that report.")
(comment objectTested "<code>(#$objectTested EVAL OBJ)</code> means that the #$PartiallyTangible <code>OBJ</code> is an object tested in the #$Evaluating <code>EVAL</code>.  For example, the portion of blood used to run a blood test fills the role of #$objectTested in that test.")
(comment CriminalInvestigation "A collection of instances of #$LawEnforcementActivity: specifically, the collection of all formal criminal investigations carried out by a law enforcement agency of some type.  All include as a #$subEvents some instance of #$CrimeDetectionActivity.  The scope of an instance of #$CriminalInvestigation is broader than just the activities carried out to detect the crime, however: it also includes the effort to identify and prosecute the perpetrator, the bureacratic procedures that accompany the investigation, and the actual arrest of the perpetrator, in cases where the investigation culminates in such.")
(comment TransportViaFn "A #$ReifiableFunction used to denote collections of events in which a certain means of transportation is used. <code>(#$TransportViaFn TRANSPORT-TYPE)</code> is the subcollection of #$TransportationEvent in each instance of which the transportation (cf. #$transporter) is being done by <code>TRANSPORT-TYPE</code>, which is an instance of #$ExistingObjectType. For instance, '(#$TransportViaFn #$Horse)' denotes the collection of horse-transportation events.
<p>
See also the more general #$ConveyViaFn, particularly for conveyors which do not necessarily undergo translation while they convey things (e.g. pipelines, chair lifts, conveyor belts).")
(disjointWith BiologicalLivingObject GiftBasket)
(comment Event "An important specialization of #$Situation and thus also of #$IntangibleIndividual and #$TemporallyExistingThing (qq.v).  Each instance of #$Event is a dynamic situation in which the state of the world changes; each instance is something one would say \"happens\".  Events are intangible because they are changes <i>per se</i>, not tangible objects that effect and undergo changes.
<p>
Notable specializations of #$Event include #$Event-Localized, #$PhysicalEvent, #$Action, and #$GeneralizedTransfer.
<p>
#$Events should not be confused with #$TimeIntervals (q.v.).  The temporal bounds of events are delineated by time intervals, but in contrast to many events time intervals have no spatial location or extent.")
(comment preservesGenlsInArg "<code>(#$preservesGenlsInArg FUNCTION N)</code> means that the #$CollectionDenotingFunction <code>FUNCTION</code> has the following properties: 
<p>
(1) argument place <code>N</code> of <code>FUNCTION</code> is constrained to instances of #$Collection, and <br>
(2) if <code>COLLECTION1</code> and <code>COLLECTION2</code> can both serve as the <code>N</code>th argument of <code>FUNCTION</code>, and <code>COLLECTION2</code> is a specialization of <code>COLLECTION1</code> (so that <code>(#$genls COLLECTION2 COLLECTION1)</code> holds), then <code>(#$genls (FUNCTION ARG_1 ARG_2 ... ARG_N-1 COLLECTION2   ... ARG-M) (FUNCTION ARG_1 ARG_2 .. ARG_N-1 COLLECTION1 ... ARG-M))</code> also holds, where <code>ARG_1</code>, <code>ARG_2</code>, ..., <code>ARG_M</code> (where <code>M</code> is the #$arity of <code>FUNCTION</code>) are admitted arguments for <code>FUNCTION</code> (see #$admittedArgument).  
<p>
For example, (#$preservesGenlsInArg #$GroupFn 1) holds because <code>(#$genls COL-2 COL-1)</code> implies <code>(#$genls (#$GroupFn COL-2) (#$GroupFn COL-1))</code>, for all collections <code>COL-1</code> and <code>COL-2</code> that satisfy the argument constraints for #$GroupFn.  
<p>
Similarly, (#$preservesGenlsInArg #$SubcollectionOfByTypeFn 2) holds because <code>(#$genls COL-2 COL-1)</code> implies <code>(#$genls (#$SubcollectionOfByTypeFn FIXED-ARG COL-2) (#$SubcollectionOfByTypeFn FIXED-ARG COL-1))</code> for all collections <code>COL-1</code> and <code>COL-2</code>, and all arguments <code>FIXED-ARG</code>, that satisfy the appropriate argument constraints for #$SubcollectionOfByTypeFn.  See also the collection #$GenlsPreservingFunction.")
(comment TimeInterval "A specialization of #$TemporalThing, #$IntangibleIndividual, and #$ModalIndex (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TimeInterval is an intangible temporal thing that is characterized fully by its temporal extent.  In this way, time intervals differ from #$Situations such as #$Events.  For example, the year 1969 C.E. is a #$TimeInterval; although many interesting things happened during that year, the year itself is completely defined by its temporal extent.  Conversely, Neil Armstrong's walking on the Moon is an event and not a time interval, since it is not fully characterized by its temporal extent or any other purely temporal attributes (e.g. its taking place on the Moon and being done by Armstrong are not temporal attributes).  Specializations of #$TimeInterval include #$CalendarYear, #$CalendarMonth, and #$FiscalQuarter.")
(comment ConveyViaFn "<code>(#$ConveyViaFn OBJ-TYPE)</code> denotes a subcollection of #$Conveying-Generic. In each instance of this collection there is an instance of <code>OBJ-TYPE</code> that is a #$conveyor-Generic.  Note that although (#$ConveyViaFn #$Truck) is a legitmate means of referring to the collection of events in which something is conveyed via a truck, (#$TransportViaFn #$Truck) is a stronger and thus more appropriate constant to use.")
(comment FindingAPhysicalObjectFn "A unary function that returns the class of events in which instances of the function argument are located.  For example, (#$FindingAPhysicalObjectFn #$Anthrax-Bacterium) returns those events in which #$Anthrax-Bacterium are located.  However, this implies nothing about one's ability to acquire or control the located object.  This function is particularly useful for type-level claims about certain kinds of events (e.g., (#$preconditionFor-EventTypeEventType (#$SmugglingFn ?STUFF) (#$FindingAPhysicalObjectFn ?STUFF))).")
(comment TimePoint "A subcollection of #$TimeInterval (q.v.).  An instance of #$TimePoint is an interval of time that has no duration (or, if you prefer, an infinitely small duration).  A time point corresponds to what is colloquially described as an \"instant\" or \"moment\".  If time is likened to a (perhaps infinitely long) straight line, then each #$TimePoint is like a particular point on that line.  Given that time intervals are defined purely by their locations in time, no two time intervals can occupy exactly the same times (see #$cotemporal); and since time points are intervals with no duration, no two time points can even overlap (see #$temporallyIntersects).  Some temporal properties of #$TemporalThings are given in terms of time points, e.g. #$startingPoint and #$endingPoint.  See also #$Now.")
(comment GeneralizedTransfer "A specialization of #$Event.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedTransfer is an event in which something (tangible or intangible) is transferred from one 'place' to another.  #$GeneralizedTransfer includes changes in physical location, in ownership or possession, transfer of information, and propagation of wave phenomena through space.  See also the related predicate #$transferredThing, and the specializations of this collection.")
(comment ComputerActivity "A collection of events, and a specialization of both #$Action and #$PhysicalEvent. In each instance of #$ComputerActivity, at least one instance of #$Computer is somehow involved. An instance of #$Person may or may not be involved. (Instances of #$ComputerActivity where no #$Person is involved fall under the more specialized collection #$ComputerProcessRunning.) A wide variety of activities falls into this collection - from sending an email (see the more specialized collection #$EMailSending), to scanning a set of ports (see #$PortScan), to a computer crash (see #$ComputerCrashing).")
(comment SpaceExploration "Exploration of outer space.")
(disjointWith ReversibleProcess AttackOnTangible)
(comment causes-SitTypeSitType "<code>(#$causes-SitTypeSitType CAUSE EFFECT)</code> means that each instance of #$Situation <code>CAUSE</code> is normally a cause of an instance of #$Situation <code>EFFECT</code>.  Often <code>EFFECT</code> is a specialization of #$Event and <code>CAUSE</code> is a specialization of #$Event or #$Action.  The occurrence of an instance of <code>CAUSE</code> is typically associated with the occurrence of an instance of <code>EFFECT</code>.  However, this association is not necessarily iron-clad, and may depend on certain prerequisites, or may be defeated by the occurrence of other events or situations. Cf. #$preconditionFor-SitTypeSitType, which is not a generalization of this relation, and #$fosters-SitTypeSitType, which is.")
(disjointWith IntelligentAgent EnergyStuff)
(comment ControllingFn "A unary #$ReifiableFunction used to denote the collection of events in which an agent controls some specific type of object. <code>(#$ControllingFn OBJ-TYPE)</code> denotes the specialization of #$ControllingSomething in which some instance(s) of <code>OBJ-TYPE</code> is controlled (where <code>OBJ-TYPE</code> is an instance of #$ExistingObjectType and a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible). Examples include the use of a spoon, the driving of a car and the disciplining of a child.")
(comment preconditionFor-SitTypeSitType "<code>(#$preconditionFor-SitTypeSitType CONDTYPE OUTCOMETYPE)</code> means that under normal conditions, a #$Situation of type <code>CONDTYPE</code> is a necessary precondition for a #$Situation of type <code>OUTCOMETYPE</code> happening or coming about.")
(disjointWith HavingAMeal AttackOnObject)
(comment objectControlled "This predicate is used to indicate that a particular object is being controlled in a particular event.  <code>(#$objectControlled EVENT OBJ)</code> means that the object <code>OBJ</code> is being controlled in the #$Event <code>EVENT</code>.
Note: #$objectControlled does not assume or require physical contact between controller and object controlled.")
(comment MoneyRatio "A specialization of #$RatioQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$MoneyRatio is an amount of #$MonetaryValue (q.v.) taken with respect to an amount of some other quantity: e.g. $1.14 per gallon, $10,000 per acre, $250 per week, and $0.89 per pound.  Notable specializations of #$MoneyRatio include  #$MoneyPerVolumeRatio, #$MoneyPerAreaRatio, #$MoneyPerMassRatio, and #$MonetaryRate.")
(comment MortgagePayment "The collection of individual mortgage payments.")
(comment ImitationOfCollectionFn "<code>(#$ImitationOfCollectionFn ORIGINAL-COLLECTION)</code> returns a collection that is an instance of #$ImitationOfCollection.  Instances of the instance of #$ImitationOfCollection returned by <code>(#$ImitationOfCollectionFn ORIGINAL-COLLECTION)</code> are designed by someone to seem as though they are instances of the collection            <code>ORIGINAL-COLLECTION</code>. 
<p>
For example, (#$ImitationOfCollectionFn #$EdibleFruit) is the instance of #$ImitationOfCollection whose instances are only every imitation edible fruit.  
<p>
Note that the collection returned by <code>(#$ImitationOfCollectionFn ORIGINAL-COLLECTION)</code> <i>will not</i> itself be a specialization of the collection <code>ORIGINAL-COLLECTION</code>.  Compare this function to #$ImitationOfIndividualFn.")
(comment Republican "The collection of all members of the #$RepublicanParty-US.")
(comment objectOfMentalSituation "This predicate relates #$IntentionalMentalSituations (q.v.) to their \"intentional objects\": the things they are \"of\" or \"about\".  <code>(#$objectOfMentalSituation SIT THING)</code> means that <code>SIT</code> is of, about, or directed toward <code>THING</code>.  <code>SIT</code> might be an occurrent intentional mental act, such as a perceptual act of seeing the Eiffel Tower, or a more long-term intentional mental state, such as a propositional attitude of believing that the Eiffel Tower is located in Paris.  In both examples, an object of <code>SIT</code> would be the Tower; the second example also has the city of Paris (and arguably the located-in relation as well) as an object.  
<p>
Note that, although they are indirectly related (see e.g. #$PurposefulMentalActivity), the sense of \"intentional\" described above is not to be confused with its other sense as an antonym of \"accidental\".  
<p>
See also #$subjectOfMentalSituation and #$thinksAbout.")
(comment notAssertible "A #$MetaPredicate for stating a certain restriction regarding the use of a given #$Predicate in assertions to the Cyc Knowledge Base.  (#$notAssertible PRED) means that no ground atomic formula (or \"GAF\"; see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) whose initial (or \"0th\" argument-place) term denotes PRED can be asserted to the KB.  In many cases, PRED will be an #$EvaluatablePredicate whose extent is (re)computed as necessary and never cached in the KB.  
<p>
See also #$notAssertibleCollection, which constrains collections 
in a similar fashion.")
(comment indexValue "(#$indexValue AGREEMENT RATE) identifies RATE as the value of the index that the instance AGREEMENT of #$EconomicAgreement uses to determine certain features, such as its interest rate or yield.  In general, where (#$indexForAgreement INDEX AGREEMENT) and the value of INDEX at time T is RATE, the at T the #$indexValue for AGREEMENT is RATE.")
(comment CitizenFn "(#$CitizenFn GEO-POL-ENTITY) denotes the collection of all people who (at some specified or implied time) are citizens -- having the rights and obligations entailed by affiliation -- of GEO-POL-ENTITY.  See also #$ResidentFn.")
(comment typeIntendedBehaviorCapable "An instance of #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate.  This predicate is used to describe the ways that types of existing things can function and are intended by their designers to function. <code>(#$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable OBJTYPE SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that instances of <code>OBJTYPE</code> are intended by <code>OBJTYPE</code>'s designer to play role <code>ROLE</code> in situations or events of type <code>SITTYPE</code>, and that instances of <code>OBJTYPE</code> can play role <code>ROLE</code> in situations or events of type <code>SITTYPE</code>. For example, instances of #$Clothing-Generic are intended to be capable of being removed and being put on. We can represent this by (#$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable #$Clothing-Generic #$Undressing #$objectRemoved), and (#$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable #$Clothing-Generic #$PuttingOnAClothingItem #$objectActedOn) See also (#$TypeCapableFn #$behaviorCapable) and #$typePrimaryFunction.")
(comment ArtificialOrgan "A specialization of #$PhysicalDevice.  Each instance of #$ArtificialOrgan is an device designed to function as an organ (see #$Organ).")
(comment OutdoorLocation "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion (q.v.).  Each instance of #$OutdoorLocation is a region of outdoor space; i.e. a region that is directly subject to atmospheric weather.  Thus, the collection #$OutdoorLocation does <i>not</i> include as instances any instances of #$UnderwaterLocation (q.v.) or any places that are underground (see #$Underground).  Specializations of #$OutdoorLocation include #$MountainRange, #$Stream, and #$Highway.")
(comment ComputerProgram-CW "A specialization of #$PropositionalConceptualWork, #$ComputerFile-CW and #$SoftwareObject-Individual. Each instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW is a deliberately created abstract object composed of propositions that together constitute a list of instructions for computers to execute.  
<p>
Example instances include #$Emacs-TheProgram and #$LinuxKernel-TheProgram.
<p>
Instances of this collection are distinct from computer code and from both running and installed programs. The instructions that comprise an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW can be expressed as abstract computer code (see #$ComputerCode), but no list of instructions expressed in code constitutes an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW.  Rather, the code in which an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW is expressed constitutes an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure that can be related to the program it expresses using the predicate #$programCode.  More than one instance of #$ComputerCode can express the same instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW; for example,  a single instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW can be written in source code (see #$ComputerCode-Source) in several different computer languages, or it can be complied as a binary executable (see #$ComputerCode-Binary).  A physical instantiation of a #$ComputerProgram-CW is a #$ComputerProgramCopy which is an #$InformationBearingThing and is related to its #$ComputerProgram-CW by the predicate #$programCopies.  An instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW that is being executed is a #$ComputerProcessRunning, related to its #$ComputerProgram-CW by the predicate #$programOfProcess.")
(disjointWith TypewriterRibbon Decoration)
(disjointWith Late20thCenturyPerson ArchaicAgeGreekPerson)
(comment Porcelain "The ceramic material of which tiles and some other things are made.")
(comment SpeedOfLight "This is the #$Speed that #$Light travels in a #$Vacuum: 299,792,458 #$MetersPerSecond, or #$approximately 186,282 miles per second.")
(comment BusinessProcess "A specialization of #$BusinessEvent. Each specialization of this collection is a kind of event which regularly occurs as part of normal business activities. Examples include weekly meetings, the production of an annual report and the paying of employees.")
(comment PieCrust "An instance of #$DefaultDisjointEdibleStuffType and a specialization of #$Pastry-Stuff and #$FoodIngredientOnly.  Each instance of #$PieCrust is a crust that is part of, or intended to be part of a #$Pie.")
(comment massNounSemTrans "(#$massNounSemTrans NOUN INT FRAME TRANS) means that the translation of word sense number INT of mass noun NOUN in frame FRAME is TRANS.")
(comment HomemadeFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction
and #$GenlsPreservingSubcollectionDenotingFunction that takes for its
value specializations of #$PartiallyTangible TYPE and returns the
collection of TYPE that is also a specialization of
#$HomemadeArtifact.  For example, (#$HomemadeFn #$Cookie) denotes the
collection of homemade cookies.")
(comment largerThan "<code>(#$largerThan LARGER SMALLER SLOT)</code> means that <code>LARGER</code>'s value on the #$QuantitySlot <code>SLOT</code> is larger than <code>SMALLER</code>'s value on that slot.")
(disjointWith Person IBTGeneration)
(comment ResearchInFn "#$ResearchInFn is an instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction.  <code>(#$ResearchInFn FIELD)</code> denotes the collection of events that comprises research activities in the #$FieldOfStudy <code>FIELD</code>.  For example, (#$ResearchInFn #$ArtificialIntelligence) is the collection of AI research events.")
(comment PartiallyCloudCoveredArea "Cloud coverage is 3/8 to 5/8 of the sky.")
(comment sizeParameterOfObject "A #$PhysicalFeatureBinaryPredicate and #$BinaryQuantityPredicate for indicating the size of a given spatial thing with respect to some spatial parameter or dimension.  <code>(#$sizeParameterOfObject OBJECT SIZE)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> measures <code>SIZE</code> in some (unspecified) parameter, where <code>SIZE</code> is either a #$Distance (q.v.) or some other sort of #$SpatialQuantity (q.v.) that is analyzable in terms of distances (e.g. an #$Area or a #$Volume). Thus <code>SIZE</code> might be <code>OBJECT</code>'s length, width, surface area, volume, or other distance-derived quantity.
<p>
#$sizeParameterOfObject is the common generalization of all those #$PhysicalAmountSlots (q.v.) that relate objects to spatial quantities.  As such, it is a general size predicate for expressing (in a parameter-nonspecific way) all sorts of size information about an object or other spatial thing.  For parameter-specific size predicates, see the various specializations of #$sizeParameterOfObject.")
(comment volumeOfObject "<code>(#$volumeOfObject OBJECT VOLUME)</code> means that the #$SpatialThing <code>OBJECT</code> has the #$Volume <code>VOLUME</code>.")
(comment Volume "A #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType and a 
specialization of #$CubicQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Volume 
is an amount of three-dimensional space; it might be either a 
specific amount (such as the volume of a five-gallon aquarium) or 
a range of amounts (such as #$PlanetSized).")
(comment hasVulnerability "<code>(#$hasVulnerability OBJECT VULNERABILITY)</code> means that the #$Individual <code>OBJECT</code> has a weakness (i.e. #$VulnerableObjectType) <code>VULNERABILITY</code>, which if exploited would result in adverse effects for <code>OBJECT</code>. This predicate expresses the notion that something simply has a vulnerability, where the kind of exploit and the kind of adverse effects are left open. It is a specialization of #$hasPassiveCapability.")
(comment Password-Computer "A collection of #$CharacterStrings. Each #$Password-Computer is a #$CharacterString that a user inputs to a #$Computer (usually in conjunction with a #$Username (q.v.)) that verifies to a program running on the computer that the user is who he claims to be. Note that an instance of #$Password-Computer is an abstract #$CharacterString, and not something with temporal extent like a sequence of keystrokes or the representation of those keystrokes inside the computer. The process of entering a valid #$Username and #$Password-Computer constitutes #$LoggingInOnAComputer or #$LoggingInToAComputerProgram (qq.v.). A #$Password-Computer is typically between 4 and 10 characters long.")
(comment Insomnia "A specialization of #$PhysiologicalCondition.
Each instance of this collection is a condition that arise when a
mammal is unable to sleep adequately over a prolonged period of time.
See also #$SleepDeprivation.")
(disjointWith UnemployedPerson Employee)
(comment ComputerAccount "A person or process with an instance of #$ComputerAccount is able to access storage space and CPU time on an instance of #$AccountSystem. Important subcollections of #$ComputerAccount are #$LoginAccount, #$UserAccount and #$PrivilegedAccount.  See also #$ApplicationAccount.")
(comment ElectricBabySwing "The collection of all electric #$Swings for babies.  A #$HumanInfant or #$HumanToddler sits in the baby swing while it swings the baby back and forth.  Most instances of #$ElectricBabySwing are battery powered.")
(disjointWith HouseholdAppliance RoomInAConstruction)
(comment SomethingExisting "A specialization of #$TemporallyExistingThing whose instances are more or less static, as compared (e.g.) to the more dynamic instances of #$Event. The clearest examples of #$SomethingExistings are tangible things, such as people, lakes, stars, and the Earth's ionosphere.  But #$SomethingExisting also includes certain intangible temporal things, such as #$Agreements and #$Obligations, that remain relatively stable throughout their lifetimes. On the other hand, #$SomethingExisting excludes \"purely temporal\" intangibles such as #$TimeIntervals.")
(comment Hand "A specialization of #$HolderGripper and #$Appendage-AnimalBodyPart.  Each instance of #$Hand is a terminal part of a vertebrate forelimb that is structurally suited to function as a grasping organ (as in people, newts, etc.).")
(comment ProgramTypeCopyFn "<code>(#$ProgramTypeCopyFn PROG-TYPE)</code> is the collection of all copies of computer programs of type <code>PROG-TYPE</code>. Compare this function to #$ProgramCopyFn, which takes as an argument a specific instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW instead of a specialization of #$ComputerProgram-CW.")
(comment ProgramCopyFn "<code>(#$ProgramCopyFn PROG-CW)</code> is the collection of all copies of computer programs of the instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW, <code>PROG-CW</code>. Contrast this function to #$ProgramTypeCopyFn, which takes as an argument a specialization of #$ComputerProgram-CW rather than an instance of it. ")
(disjointWith NLUtteranceAttribute MathematicalObject)
(comment typesHaveRelationInSubSituationType "(#$typesHaveRelationInSubSituationType SCRIPT SCENE TYPE1 TYPE2 BINREL) means that in an instantiation of SCRIPT, SCRIPT-INST, it will hold in the sub-event that is the instantiation of SCENE (see #$holdsIn) that an instance of TYPE1 and an instance of TYPE2 will have the relation BINREL to each other.")
(disjointWith HumanInfant CovertAgent)
(comment Dancing-Human "A specialization of #$Dancing. Each instance of this collection is a dance performed by a human. Such events might occur during a recreational event (such as a #$Dance-Party), or a professional dancer's #$EntertainmentPerformance.")
(comment Dancing "A specialization of #$CoordinatedBodyMovement. Instances of this collection are events in which an animal dances. For human dancing, see the more specialized #$Dancing-Human. For the individual motions which make up a dance, see #$PerformingADanceMotion.")
(comment PersonTypeByNationality "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$PersonTypeByNationality is the collection of all people who are citizens of some country, or who are participants in its #$NationalCulture to some significant degree -- or else are instances of #$StatelessPerson. Instances include #$UnitedStatesPerson, #$FrenchPerson, and #$AustralianCitizenOrSubject.  See also the first-order collection #$PersonWithNationality.")
(comment Sightseeing "A specialization of #$VisitingASite.  Instances of #$Sightseeing are events in which people travel around a geographic location (e.g., a city), visiting the notable sites (e.g., landmarks, museums, zoo, ...) there.  This collection does not include more participatory activities, such as going to the beach to swim or tan, running with the bulls in Spain, attending a play, opera, movie, ... unless those activities are strictly aimed at tourists, e.g., riding a camel in front of the pyramids.")
(comment GoldenRatio-Number "#$GoldenRatio-Number is the mathematical constant which represents the Golden Ratio and is defined to be (#$QuotientFn (#$PlusFn 1 (#$SquareRootFn 5)) 2).  The Golden Ratio has many surprising and curious mathematical properties.  It is also sometimes called the 'Divine Proportion', and has been extensively used in architecture because objects in this proportion tend to be visually appealing.  Although #$GoldenRatio-Number is an instance of #$IrrationalNumber, a reasonable rational approximation is 1.6180340")
(comment disabilityToPlayRole "(#$disabilityToPlayRole CONDITION ACT-TYPE ROLE) means that an animal with the #$PhysiologicalConditionType CONDITION is not physically capable of playing the #$Role ROLE in actions of type ACT-TYPE.  For example, (#$disabilityToPlayRole #$Blindness #$VisualPerception #$doneBy) means that a blind animal cannot see.")
(disjointWith SocialOccurrence PhysiologicalCondition)
(comment FrequentPerformerFn "A unary #$ReifiableFunction that takes a specialization of #$Action and returns the collection of #$Persons who play #$performedBy roles in instances of that type of action with significant #$Frequency.  (What counts as \"significant frequency\" depends on the context.)  For example, <code>(#$FrequentPerformerFn #$Running)</code> denotes the collection of people who perform instances of #$Running with significant frequency -- i.e., the collection  #$Runner.")
(disjointWith BiologicalLivingObject NonNaturalThing)
(comment AccessingACWFn "This function, given a #$ConceptualWork, returns the collection of #$AccessingAnIBT events involving #$InformationBearingThings that are instantiations of this type.  For instance, (#$AccessingAnIBTTypeFn #$PeloponnesianWarHistoryByThucydides) would return all the events of reading (portions of instances of) a copy (#$instantiationOfWork) of Thucydides' history of the Peloponnesian Wars.  If I ask, 'Have you ever seen the movie 2001?', I am asking if you have played the role of #$recipientOfInfo in an instance of (#$AccessingACWFn #$2001-ASpaceOdyssey-TheMovie).")
(comment most-GenQuantRelnTo "<code>(#$most-GenQuantRelnTo BINPRED SOC THING)</code> means that most elements of <code>SOC</code> bear <code>BINPRED</code> to <code>THING</code> -- i.e., most elements <code>X</code> of <code>SOC</code> are such that <code>(BINPRED X THING)</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$most SOC (#$TheSetOf ?X (BINPRED X THING))).
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$most-GenQuantRelnTo #$fanOf #$Liberal-SocialDemocrat #$FranklinRoosevelt)</code> means that most liberals are fans of FDR.
<p>
When <code>SOC</code> is a collection, use the more specialized #$relationMostInstance.")
(comment GeneralizedQuantifier-Extensional "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-Extensional is a #$GeneralizedQuantifier PRED (of arity n) such that the truth value of (PRED ARG1 ... ARGn) is a function of the extentions of the elements of {ARG1, ..., ARGn} that are sets or collections, together with any elements of {ARG1, ..., ARGn} that are not sets or collections.  Instances of #$GeneralizedQuantifier-Extensional include #$most, #$no, #$some, #$subsetOf, and #$relativelyFewComparedTo.  Non-instances include #$many, #$few, and #$relativelyFew.")
(comment CorrespondingGenQuantFn "(#$CorrespondingGenQuantFn ATTR) denotes the instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier QUANT such that (#$correspondingGenQuant ATTR QUANT).  For example, (#$CorrespondingGenQuantFn #$Most-NLAttr) is #$most.")
(comment interArgIsa2-1 "A ternary instance of #$InterArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).   <code>(#$interArgIsa2-1 RELN INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL DEPENDENT-ARG-COL)</code> means that,  when an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> appears as the second argument to <code>RELN</code>,  a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness is that the first argument  must be an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>.  That is, if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>, then <code>(RELN ARG1 INST ... ARGN)</code>  is semantically well-formed only if <code>ARG1</code> is an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>. For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible  and its direct specializations.")
(comment resourceAvailable "<code>(#$resourceAvailable AGENT RESOURCE)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has access to the object <code>RESOURCE</code> in such a way that <code>AGENT</code> could make some immediate use of <code>RESOURCE</code> in any type of action which required it.")
(comment resourceTypeAvailable "(#$resourceTypeAvailable AGENT RESOURCE) means that AGENT has RESOURCE available to it.")
(comment mainConstituent "A predicate that relates objects to further objects which are their main constituents.
<p>
<code>(#$mainConstituent WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>WHOLE</code> is partially constituted by <code>PART</code> (so that <code>(#$constituents WHOLE PART)</code> holds), and (as a default) the physical properties of <code>WHOLE</code> are those of <code>PART</code>.
<p>
For example, for a particular instance of #$Lemonade, there is an instance of #$Water which is the #$mainConstituent, and most of the physical properties of that instance of #$Lemonade derive from that instance of #$Water.  On the other hand, a person is <i>not</i> considered to have some portion of #$Water as #$mainConstituent, even though much of a person's mass is #$Water, since the physical properties of a person are not the same as those of water.")
(comment no "An instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier.  <code>(#$no SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> means that nothing is an element of both <code>SETORCOL1</code> and <code>SETORCOL2</code>.  Note that this predicate is <i>not</i> irreflexive: it is possible for a #$SetOrCollection to be disjoint with itself, but only if it is empty (i.e. has no elements). Thus, for example, (#$no #$TheEmptySet #$TheEmptySet) holds. Also, note that #$no is a more general relation than #$disjointWith (q.v.) in that it has more general argument constraints.")
(comment most-GenQuantRelnToType "A #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$most-GenQuantRelnToType BINPRED SOC1 SOC2)</code> means that most elements of <code>SOC1</code> bear <code>BINPRED</code> to an element of <code>SOC2</code> -- i.e., most elements <code>ELEM1</code> of <code>SOC1</code> are such that, for some element <code>ELEM2</code> of <code>SOC2</code>, <code>(BINPRED ELEM1 ELEM2)</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$most SOC1
  (#$TheSetOf ?X
    (#$thereExists ?Y
      (#$and
        (#$elementOf ?Y SOC2)
        (BINPRED ?X ?Y))))).
</pre>
For example, (#$most-GenQuantRelnToType #$knowsAbout #$UnitedStatesPerson #$UnitedStatesPresident) means that most Americans know about some U.S. president.
<p>
When <code>SOC1</code> and <code>SOC2</code> are #$Collections, use the more specialized #$relationMostExists.")
(comment exactly2 "A #$GeneralizedQuantifier-NonMonotone.  <code>(#$exactly2 SOC1 SOC2)</code> means that there are exactly two things that are instances of both <code>SOC1</code> and <code>SOC2</code>, i.e. the cardinality of <code>(#$CollectionIntersection2Fn SOC1 SOC2)</code> is two.")
(comment most-GenQuantRelnFromType "A #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$most-GenQuantRelnFromType BINPRED SOC1 SOC2)</code> means that most elements <code>ELEM2</code> of <code>SOC2</code> are such that for some element <code>ELEM1</code> of <code>SOC1</code>, <code>(BINPRED ELEM1 ELEM2)</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$most SOC2
  (#$TheSetOf ?OBJ
    (#$thereExists ?THING
      (#$and
        (#$elementOf ?THING SOC1)
        (BINPRED ?THING ?OBJ))))).
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$most-GenQuantRelnFromType #$musicTracks #$GreatestHitsAlbum-CW #$HitSong-CW)</code> means that most hit songs are tracks on some greatest hits album.
<p>
When <code>SOC1</code> and <code>SOC2</code> are #$Collections, use the more specialized #$relationExistsMost.")
(comment FilterSystem-Artificial "A collection of physical devices designed to be used in instances of  #$FluidFiltrationProcess-Artificial.  To be a member of this collection, the device must consist of more than simply an instance of #$Filter, but include other parts intended to work together with the #$Filter in the #$FluidFiltrationProcess-Artificial.  Although some biological organs (e.g., kidneys) function as filters they are not instaces of this collection because they are not physical devices (see #$PhysicalDevice).")
(comment interArg1ResultIsa "A ternary #$MetaRelation predicate for stating that a certain kind of complex, partly #$isa based relationship holds between the first argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned. <code>(#$interArg1ResultIsa FUNC INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL RESULT-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> appears as the first argument to <code>FUNC</code>, then -- regardless of what the other arguments to <code>FUNC</code> might be -- the value (if any) of that function for those arguments is an instance of <code>RESULT-COL</code>.  That is: if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> and (<code>FUNC INST</code> ...) = <code>VALUE</code>, then <code>VALUE</code> is an instance of <code>RESULT-COL</code>. Note that, unlike (e.g.) the #$InterArgTypePredicates, #$interArg1ResultIsa <i>cannot</i> be used to put semantic well-formedness constraints on functions.  See also the similar but more flexible quaternary  #$interArgResultIsa.")
(comment aFew-RelationToType "A #$GeneralizedQuantifierRelation.  <code>(#$aFew-RelationToType PRED SOC1 SOC2)</code> means that a few (#$aFew) elements of <code>SOC1</code> bear <code>PRED</code> to an element of <code>SOC2</code> -- i.e., a few elements <code>X</code> of <code>SOC1</code> are such that, for some element <code>Y</code> of <code>SOC2</code>, <code>(PRED X Y)</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$aFew SOC1
  (#$TheSetOf ?X
    (#$thereExists ?Y
      (#$and
        (#$elementOf ?Y SOC2)
        (PRED ?X ?Y))))).
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$aFew-RelationToType #$owns #$Plumber #$Mansion)</code> means that a few plumbers own mansions.")
(comment CycLVariableFn "#$CycLVariableFn is an evaluatable function that takes any instance of #$CycLConstant and returns a character string, unique for the input, interpreted by the system as an CycL variable.  This uniqueness is guaranteed by CycL naming conventions and the syntactic properties of the resultant variable.  For example, (#$CycLVariableFn #$Thing) evaluates to ?THING, whereas (#$CycLVariableFn #$isa) evaluates to ?ISA.")
(comment aFew-RelationFromType "A #$GeneralizedQuantifierRelation.  <code>(#$aFew-RelationFromType PRED SOC1 SOC2)</code> means that a few (#$aFew) elements <code>X</code> of <code>SOC1</code> are such that, for some element <code>Y</code> of <code>SOC2</code>, <code>(PRED Y X)</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$aFew SOC1
  (#$TheSetOf ?X
    (#$thereExists ?Y
      (#$and
        (#$elementOf ?Y SOC2)
        (PRED ?Y ?X))))).
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$aFew-RelationFromType #$owns #$Mansion #$Plumber)</code> means that a few mansions are owned by plumbers.")
(comment aFew-RelationTo "A #$GeneralizedQuantifierRelation.  <code>(#$aFew-RelationTo PRED COL THING)</code> means that a few (#$aFew) instances of <code>COL</code> bear <code>PRED</code> to <code>THING</code> -- i.e., a few instances <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> are such that <code>(PRED INST THING)</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$aFew COL (#$TheSetOf ?X (PRED ?X THING)))
</pre>
For example, <code>(#$aFew-RelationTo #$objectFoundInLocation #$Skyscraper #$CityOfDallasTX)</code> means that there are a few skyscrapers in Dallas.")
(comment logicalPhysicalFieldMap "Formally, (#$logicalPhysicalFieldMap LF PF) means that the indexical term for the #$LogicalField LF (#$indexicalForLogicalField) shares one or more #$fieldDecoding and one or more #$fieldEncoding assertions with the indexical term for the #$PhysicalField, PF (#$indexicalForPhysicalField). What this means is that values for LF are constructed at least in part from the values of PF.")
(comment collectionUnion "A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections (q.v.) that relates a set of collections to the collection whose extent (see #$extent) is by definition the union of the extents of all the collections in that set. <code>(#$collectionUnion UNION SET-OF-COLS)</code> means that <code>UNION</code> is the \"collection-union\" of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>: any given thing <b>THING</b> is an instance of <code>UNION</code> if and only if <b>THING</b> is an instance of some collection that is an element of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>.  It follows that any subcollection of some element of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code> is also a subcollection of <code>UNION</code>; in particular each element of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code> is itself a subcollection of <code>UNION</code>. 
<p>
Note that, since collections are supposed to be \"intensionally defined\", it is theoretically possible that there be more than one collection having exactly the extent (i.e. set of instances) characterized above.  But <code>UNION</code> is to be thought of as the collection which <i>by definition</i> contains all and only the instances of any of the collections in <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>.  In other words, the criterion for instanceship associated with <code>UNION</code> (i.e. its \"intensional definition\") is in effect simply the disjunction of each of the criteria associated with the various members of <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>.  This is what justifies the above description of <code>UNION</code> as <i>the</i> collection-union corresponding to <code>SET-OF-COLS</code>, and what justifies #$collectionUnion's being defined as a strictly functional predicate.  
<p>
See the corresponding function #$CollectionUnionFn; also see #$collectionIntersection.")
(comment bodyguard "(#$bodyguard PERSON GUARD) means that in the temporal and spatial context of the assertion, the job of GUARD is to protect PERSON from bodily injurythat usually has the potential to stem from the actions of a person or people who want to do PERSON some sort of bodily harm.")
(comment InsurancePlan "A collection of agreements; a subset of #$LegalAgreement.  Each element of #$InsurancePlan is an agreement between two or more agents.  For each such plan, there is at least one #$insuredAgent who is covered by the insurance plan, and one #$insuringAgent who is the insurance provider (e.g., HMO, insurance company, or U.S. Federal government agency).  An insurance plan may be funded by (see #$insuranceFundedBy) the insured or by a third party, such as the insured's employer.  Some subsets of #$InsurancePlan representing insurance for different insured event types are: #$LifeInsurance, #$MedicalInsurance, #$DisabilityInsurance, #$HomeOwnersInsurance, #$AutomobileInsurance, #$LiabilityInsurance.")
(comment yieldToMaturity "(#$yieldToMaturity BOND MONEY) means that the total of expected interest payments (plus any discounts from face value and less any premium) on BOND, from the issue date or the current date (whichever is later) until it is scheduled to be retired, is the amount MONEY.  This takes into account the future gain on a discount or loss on a premium.")
(comment Bond-Agreement "A specialization of #$TradeableSecurity and #$AuthorizedAgreement.  Each instance of #$Bond-Agreement is an agreement in which one party promises to repay another within a specified amount of time.  The agreement is often represented by a #$BondCertificate, a formal certificate of debt, issued by a #$LegalCorporation or a unit of government (see #$bondIssuedBy).  See also #$yieldToMaturity.")
(comment SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn "<code>(#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn COLLECTION PREDICATE TYPE)</code> specifies the collection of all instances <code>INSTANCE</code> of <code>COLLECTION</code> for which <code>(PREDICATE THING INSTANCE)</code> holds for some instance <code>THING</code> of <code>TYPE</code>.  For example,  (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn #$Author #$createdBy #$Biography) refers to the collection of biographers.")
(comment OverdrawingABankAccount "An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$MakingABankWithdrawal.  Each instance of #$OverdrawingABankAccount is a #$BankTransaction in which the amount of money withdrawn exceeds the amount of money in the account (or the amount of the money in the account minus some predetermined minimum balance).")
(comment unknownSentence "A unary #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) that takes instances of #$CycLSentence as arguments.  <code>(#$unknownSentence SENTENCE)</code> means that <code>SENTENCE</code> is not currently  \"known\" by the Cyc system to be true.  More precisely, the inference engine cannot prove <code>SENTENCE</code> <i>using only removal</i> (see #$CycRemovalModule) from the current state of the knowledge base.  <code>SENTENCE</code> might actually be known to be false (see the specialization #$contradictorySentence), or its truth-value might be simply unknown in the present sense.  
<p>
Note that, as a KB dependent relation, #$unknownSentence is #$notAssertible (q.v.).")
(comment bondIssuedBy "(#$bondIssuedBy BOND AGENT) means that BOND is a #$Bond-Agreement that was issued by AGENT.")
(comment SetOrCollectionIntersection2Fn "A binary #$EvaluatableFunction that takes two instances of #$SetOrCollection and returns their intersection.")
(comment coextensionalSetOrCollections "An instance of #$TaxonomicSlot and a generalization of #$coExtensional (q.v.).  #$coextensionalSetOrCollections relates mathematical sets as well as collections; thus it holds between coextensional #$SetOrCollections.  <code>(#$coextensionalSetOrCollections SETORCOL1 SETORCOL2)</code> means that <code>SETORCOL1</code> and <code>SETORCOL2</code> have all their elements in common: any element of (see #$elementOf) one is an element of the other.  Since coextensionality entails identity (see #$equals) in the case of sets but <i>not</i> in the case of collections, it <i>cannot</i> be inferred from the above that <code>SETORCOL1</code> and <code>SETORCOL2</code> are identical.")
(comment MunicipalBond-Agreement "A specialization of #$Bond-Agreement.  Each instance of #$MunicipalBond-Agreement is a debt oligation issued by a state, city, county, or other governmental entity (see #$GeopoliticalEntity) to pay for the development of public goods, such as schools, sewers or hospitals.")
(comment DitransitiveNP-NPFrame "This frame is used for words that take exactly two NP complements, where one NP plays the role of direct object and the other the role of indirect object.  Example: 'Fred gave Sally a book'.  One salient way in which this frame is distinguished from #$DitransitiveNP-NPObjComplementFrame is that the former undergoes #$DoubleObjectAlternations, while the latter does not. Example: 'Fred gave Sally a book' alternates with 'Fred gave a book to Sally' but 'Fred called Sally a liar' does not -- *'Fred called a liar of Sally'.")
(comment House-Modern "A specialization of #$Building and #$ModernHumanResidence.  Each instance of #$House-Modern is a building constructed for people to live in for a long period of time.  Generally, instances of this collection are between one and three stories tall (though they may be taller).  Instances of this collection usually have indoor plumbing, a kitchen, at least one bathroom, one or more bedrooms, and at least one general indoor living area.")
(comment TransitiveFiniteClauseFrame "This frame is used for transitive words that take exactly one finite clausal complement which may be a finite that-clause, a finite wh-clause, or a simple (that-less and wh-less) finite clause.  Examples of constructions allowed by this frame: 1. I know that he left. 2. I know he left. 3. I wonder why he left. Examples of words in this class: 'know', 'learn', 'notice' and 'prove'.")
(comment TransitiveThatClauseFrame "This frame is used for transitive words that take exactly one finite clausal complement which may be a finite that-clause or a simple (that-less and wh-less) finite clause. The clausal complement may *not* be a finite wh-clause.  To handle those constructions as well, use #$TransitiveFiniteClauseFrame.  Examples of constructions allowed by this frame: 1. I know that he left. 2. I know he left. Examples of words in this class: 'think', 'suppose', 'assume'.")
(comment TransitiveFrameType "Frames used for words that take exactly one (not necessarily            an NP) complement. In HPSG terms: [COMPS  <synsem>]")
(comment PleonasticInfinitivePhraseFrameType "Frames used for transitive words (usually verbs or adjectives) that take exactly one complement--an infinitive phrase--in a pleonastic construction. In HPSG terms: [COMPS <CP[infinitive]>]. Examples: 'It is disappointing to not get a raise', 'It stinks to get fired'.")
(comment teaserInterestRate "The predicate used to state the teaser interest rate at which some adjustable rate loan is offered.  A teaser interest rate is an unusually low introductory rate, used to attract borrowers and to allow for easier qualification.  (#$teaserInterestRate LOAN RATE) means that RATE is the teaser rate offered by the #$creditor for the LOAN.")
(comment situationExpenses "<code>(#$situationExpenses SITUATION MONETARY-VALUE)</code> holds just in case partaking of SITUATION requires costs MONETARY-VALUE.  For example, consider the situation of having a month long bus pass.  Being in that situation cost someone (most likely the owner of the pass) the money it took to buy the pass.  In that case the #$situationExpenses would be exactly the same as the money it took to buy the pass.  Another example would be in the situation of owning a drivable automobile.  In that case the cost of being in that situation would be equal to the initial cost of the vehible and the cost of maintaining the vehicle in drivable condition throughout the course of that situation.")
(comment IndividualRetirementAccount "A type of #$RetirementAccount.  The money a person contributes to her #$IndividualRetirementAccount may be invested in various ways (e.g. stocks, bonds).  The income from such investment is added to the account and is not taxable until it is actually withdrawn at her retirement.")
(comment ComputerFileCopy "A specialization of #$ComputerDataArtifact-Complete.  Each instance of #$ComputerFileCopy is an information bearing thing that is identified as a unit by a unique name, and which is object-like in an important respect.  Although some files may have other files as parts, every file is such that if some information were removed from it -- if it were truncated in a certain respect -- it would no longer be a file. Examples include individual image files, text files, sound files and executables (instances of #$ComputerProgramCopy) that are stored on some #$ComputerStorageDevice.  Instances of #$ComputerFileCopy are actual physical objects or events (e.g., the magnetic domains, holes in a piece of paper, or voltage fluctuations in a device corresponding to bits or bytes) which encode information.")
(comment portionOf "(#$portionOf SET1 SET2 NUM) means that the portion of members of SET1 that are also members of SET2 is NUM. In other words, the fraction of the cardinality of SET1 and the cardinality of the intersection of SET1 and SET2. For example, (#$portionOf (#$ResidentFn #$Netherlands) #$UnemployedPerson X) means that unemployment in the Netherlands is X. ")
(comment GeneralizedQuantifier "A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier is a #$Predicate that expresses a quantificational relationship between two or more instances of #$SetOrCollection.  Some #$GeneralizedQuantifiers, such as #$subsetOf, #$some, and #$no, are definable in terms of the standard quantifiers of first-order logic, #$forAll and #$thereExists (together with the basic logical connectives #$not, #$and, etc.).  For example, <code>(#$no SOC1 SOC2)</code> can be defined as:
<pre>
(#$forAll ?X
  (#$implies
    (#$isa ?X SOC1)
    (#$not (#$isa ?X SOC2))))
</pre>
Others, such as #$most, #$few, and #$aFew, are not so definable.  See also #$GeneralizedQuantifierRelation.")
(comment ComputerFile-AIS "A specialization of #$DataStream (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ComputerFile-AIS is an abstract series of bits encoding some information and conforming to some file system protocol.  The instantiation (see #$fileCopies) of this abstract series of bits (e.g. on magnetic media) is a #$ComputerFileCopy (q.v.).")
(comment aFew "A #$GeneralizedQuantifier and #$RuleMacroPredicate.  <code>(#$aFew SOC1 SOC2)</code> means that at least three elements of <code>SOC1</code> are elements of <code>SOC2</code>.  It is equivalent to the more complex form:
<pre>
(#$thereExistAtLeast 3 ?X
  (#$and
    (#$elementOf ?X SOC1)
    (#$elementOf ?X SOC2)))
</pre>
#$aFew should not be confused with #$few (q.v.).")
(comment enforcingAgent "(#$enforcingAgent NORM AGENT) means that AGENT is the agent authorized to impose (and enforce) #$NormativeSpecification (e.g., #$CodeOfConduct, #$Punishment, etc.) NORM on agents that are expected to satisfy  NORM.")
(comment presentationMedium "An instance of #$ActorSlot.  (#$presentationMedium EVENT IBT) means that the propositional content of the instance of #$Communicating EVENT is conveyed through IBT, an instance of #$InformationBearingThing.")
(comment PPCompFrame "The collection of all frames for words that take a prepositional phrases complement.  The two major specs of #$PPCompFrame are #$TransitivePPFrameType and #$DitransitivePPFrameType, but there are others as well.")
(comment headquarters "The predicate #$headquarters relates a particular organization to the construction artifact(s) which serve as its headquarters.  <code>(#$headquarters ORG HEADQUARTERS)</code> means that the #$ConstructionArtifact <code>HEADQUARTERS</code> (which typically will be a group of buildings, rooms, or offices) houses the main administrative and decision-making bodies of the #$Organization <code>ORG</code>.")
(comment basedInRegion "<code>(#$basedInRegion AGENT REGION)</code> means that the #$SocialBeing, <code>AGENT</code>, operates out of the #$GeographicalRegion, <code>REGION</code>.  A person based in a region normally resides in that region, even though that may not be the person's permanent residence.  An #$Organization based in a region has its headquarters there.  See #$hasHeadquartersInCountry.")
(comment initialInterestRate "The predicate used to state the rate of interest charged on a #$LoanAgreement at the beginning of its term.  (#$initialInterestRate LOAN RATE) means that RATE is the initial interest rate on LOAN.  See the specialization, #$teaserInterestRate to express the introductory rates of loans that are used as enticements to lure borrowers.")
(comment storedOnMedium "(#$storedOnMedium DATA-ARTIFACT MEDIUM) means that DATA-ARTIFACT is stored on MEDIUM.  Using this predicate the storage location of any instance of #$ComputerDataArtifact can be specified.")
(comment slotsAdditive "(#$slotsAdditive SUM-SLOT ADD-SLOT) expresses the following relationship between two #$QuantitySlots: the value of SUM-SLOT applied to OBJ is (by definition or convention) the sum of OBJ's value on ADD-SLOT plus zero or more other values.  For example, it is true that (#$slotsAdditive #$financialAssetsOfAgent #$cashAssetsOfAgent), because the value of an agent's financial assets is the sum of the value of her cash assets plus other values (e.g., of her stock holdings, securities, etc.).")
(comment initialLoanAmount "(#$initialLoanAmount LOAN AMOUNT) means that the inital amount borrowed at the origination of LOAN is AMOUNT.")
(comment basicPrice "<code>(#$basicPrice OBJECT MONEY)</code> means that to buy the #$TemporalThing <code>OBJECT</code> one needs to pay the #$MonetaryValue <code>MONEY</code>, where <code>MONEY</code> can be either a discrete quantity or an interval.  #$basicPrice should be distinguished from the (rather different) predicates #$appraisedValue and #$cost (qq.v). For the price at which an item was bought some time in the past, see #$bookValue. For the total charge for purchase of an object, including tax, shipping, handling, etc., see #$totalCharge. For relating #$Buying events to the basic price of items bought during those events, see #$basicPrice-Event. Since prices are time-sensitive, assertions about #$basicPrice should be suitably qualified.")
(comment cost "<code>(#$cost OBJECT MONEY)</code> means that the #$TemporalThing <code>OBJECT</code> has a cost in #$MonetaryValue of <code>MONEY</code>. Note that this amount can be a discrete quantity or an interval.  This concept is subtly different from (and considerably more complex than) that of #$basicPrice, which denotes the amount of money requested for an item which is for sale. For instance, the price of an airconditioning unit might be $200 while its #$cost (over a lifetime of repairs, and to the environment, if it is environmentally destructive) might be considerably higher. See also the ternary predicate #$costTo.")
(comment CreditScoring "A specialization of both #$FinancialActivity and #$Evaluating.  Each instance of #$CreditScoring is a standarized act of evaluating an agent's #$CreditWorthiness (q.v.).  Credit scoring is typically done by a bank, credit card company, or similar financial organization, whenever a person applies for a credit card, personal loan, or mortgage.")
(comment FinancialCompany "#$FinancialCompany is a specialization of #$PrivateSectorOrganization, #$FinancialOrganization, and #$CommercialServiceOrganization.  Each instance of #$FinancialCompany is a business engaged in financial activities as its main concern.  An instance of #$FinancialCompany is a commercial service organization that buys, sells, trades, converts, or lends money, in the form of currency or negotiable financial instruments (such as stocks, bonds, commodities futures, etc.), as (one of) its major function(s).  The collection #$FinancialCompany does not include businesses, such as retailers, which simply exchange goods or non-financial services for money.  Examples: Chase Bank, University Federal Credit Union, Fidelity Investments (but NOT the IMF). Cf. the supercollection #$FinancialOrganization for non-business organizations engaged in financial activities (such as the IMF).")
(comment TechnologyOrganization "The collection of all instances of #$Business that are primarily or significantly engaged in the #$TechnologyIndustry or whose activities focus on that industry.")
(comment PriceQuoteDocument "The collection of all pieces of #$TextualMaterial containing information about prices. Examples include #$BidForContracts, quotes for insurance or loans, and restaurant menus with prices.")
(comment stockPrice-Ask "A predicate used to state the asked market price of a particular share of a company's #$Stock (of a particular \"class\", e.g. common or preferred).  (#$stockPrice-Ask STOCK PRICE DATE EXCHANGE) means that PRICE is the lowest price per share that any owner or dealer of stock in STOCK is willing to sell for at DATE on the exchange EXCHANGE.  Cf. #$stockPrice-Bid.")
(comment stockPrice-Bid "A predicate used to state the bid market price of a particular share of a certain company's #$Stock (of a particular class, e.g. common or preferred) at a given time.  (#$stockPrice-Ask STOCK PRICE DATE EXCHANGE) means that PRICE is the highest price per share that any prospective buyer of stock in STOCK is willing to pay at DATE on the exchange EXCHANGE.  Cf. #$stockPrice-Ask.")
(disjointWith Loan-InformalOralAgreement Loan-WrittenLegalAgreement)
(comment NaturalGas "An instance of #$ChemicalSubstanceType and a specialization of #$FossilFuel and #$NaturalTangibleStuff.  Each instance of this collection is some amount of natural gas (including the gaseous form and Liquefied Natural Gas or LNG), the gas used in home appliances, etc.  This collection includes as specializations unrefined natural gas and refined natural gas.")
(comment PassivizableFrame "The collection of all frames which can be used in the passive voice, i.e. where an object of the active voice use of this frame 'becomes' the syntactic (though not semantic) subject in the passive voice, and the subject of the active voice use of the frame can optionally become a 'by'-PP syntactic (though not semantic) object. Examples are #$TransitiveNPFrameType, #$DitransitiveNP-GenericFrameType and the #$DitransitivePPFrameTypes:. 'John likes Mary' -> 'Mary is liked (by John)', 'John gives Mary a book' -> 'Mary is given a book (by John)', 'John gives a book to Mary' -> 'A book is given to Mary (by John)'")
(comment agreementPeriod "The predicate #$agreementPeriod is used to indicate the period of time during which a particular agreement is in force.  <code>(#$agreementPeriod AGREE TIME)</code> means that the #$Agreement <code>AGREE</code> holds during the #$TimeInterval <code>TIME</code>.  <code>TIME</code> may or may not begin at the moment that <code>AGREE</code> is created; e.g., a person may sign an employment contract on the very day she begins working or several weeks before, to begin on a specified future date.  An #$agreementPeriod could even begin before the agreement was made, e.g., when an agent agrees to pay disputed royalties retroactively.")
(comment interestRate "<code>(#$interestRate AGREE RATE)</code> means that the interest rate on #$Agreement <code>AGREE</code> is <code>RATE</code> expressed as a percent per year (or other unit of time).  The second argument should be expressed as in the following example: <code>(#$interestRate LOAN ((#$PerFn #$Unity DURATION) (#$Percent N)))</code> where <code>DURATION</code> is some amount of time such as #$YearsDuration.  An #$interestRate may be on some #$LoanAgreement or it may be on some other #$Agreement, such as a #$Stock.")
(comment minimumDurationOfType "This predicate relates collections of temporal things to the minimum duration of typical instances of that type.
Err on the side of caution when making assertions with this predicate, but also keep in mind that it is a default rule.  For instance, in the #$UnitedStatesSocialLifeMt one might assert that (#$minimumDurationOfType #$Person (#$YearsDuration 40)) because it is true that the vast majority of Americans in the late 20th century live for at least 40 years; this despite the fact that there are numerous counterexamples of Americans who die young.")
(comment ComplexNumber "A specialization of both #$Number-General and  #$ScalarPointValue.  Each instance of #$ComplexNumber can be thought of as a vector of two numbers, which are usually called the \"real part\" and the \"imaginary part\" of the complex number.  
<p>
Every complex number can be represented as <i>a</i> + i<i>b</i> where i is the square root of negative one (see #$I-TheNumber), and <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> are reals (see #$RealNumber).  When <i>b</i>=0, the complex number has no imaginary part, and therefore is a real number.  When <i>b</i> is not zero the complex number has an imginary part and so it is an imaginary number (see #$ImaginaryNumber).  
<p>
Complex numbers may also be considered as corresponding to points in the real plane, where the x axis determines the real component of a complex number and the y axis the imaginary component.  The unit value on the real number line is 1, the unit value on the imaginary number line is the square root of -1, generally written 'i' in mathematics and 'j' in engineering.")
(comment ExistingObjectType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ExistingObjectType is a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible (q.v.).  Moreover, each instance of #$ExistingObjectType is a collection that is temporally stuff-like (see #$TemporalStuffType) but physically-spatially object-like (see #$ObjectType) -- i.e.  object-like with respect to some physical-spatial part-whole predicate (e.g. some specialization of #$properPhysicalDecompositions).
<p>
Consider the collection #$BookCopy. #$BookCopy is a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible. Further, #$BookCopy is temporally stuff-like: any one of the #$timeSlices of a given copy <code>COPY</code> of (say) \"Moby Dick\" is still a copy of \"Moby Dick\". (Most collections of tangible objects are temporally stuff-like in this way.)   But #$BookCopy is spatially <i>object-like</i>: if we take a scalpel and slice <code>COPY</code> into ten pieces, each piece is not a copy of \"Moby Dick\".  So #$BookCopy is an instance of #$ExistingObjectType.
<p>
See also #$StuffType and #$ExistingStuffType.")
(comment Volatility-Physical "A specialization of #$TangiblePhysicalQuantity and #$DispositionalQuantity.  Each instance of #$Volatility-Physical is a specific readiness of some liquid to evaporate.  Higher volatility indicates a faster rate of evaporation.  Volatilities of substances are indicated with the predicate #$volatilityOfSubstance (q.v.).")
(comment volatilityOfSubstance "(#$volatilityOfSubstance SUBST DEGREE) indicates how volatile SUBST is.  Objects with a higher DEGREE of #$Volatility-Physical evaporate more readily than objects with a lower DEGREE.")
(comment affiliatedWith "<code>(#$affiliatedWith AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT1</code> and <code>AGENT2</code> are somehow affiliated.  This is a broad relation, involving at least the voluntary entry into an understood formal or semi-formal relationship, which entails certain rights and obligations, on the part of at least one of the affiliated agents.  For example, the agents might be business partners, kin, employer/employee, parent-company/subsidiary, or organization/member.")
(comment firstSubEvents "A #$SubEventPredicate that is a specialization of both #$properSubEvents and  #$firstProperSubSituations (qq.v.). (#$firstSubEvents EVENT START) means that START is a beginning part of EVENT.  That is, START is a proper subevent of EVENT that starts exactly when EVENT starts and ends before EVENT ends.  Also, START should be something that it would seem intuitively correct (in the context in question) to describe as \"the first part\" of EVENT.  Typically, START ends no later than halfway through (i.e. the temporal  mid-point of) the occurring of EVENT.  Note that more than one event might be considered a first part of a given event; for example, either the first inning or the first pitch of the first inning could be considered a first subevent of a baseball game.  See also #$lastSubEvents.")
(comment StockTypeByBusinessAndClass "A collection of specializations of #$Stock.  Each element of #$StockTypeByBusinessAndClass is a collection of instances of #$Stock that are offered by a particular #$LegalCorporation and all of whose instances are of the same stock type (e.g. all are #$PreferredStock).")
(comment ventureCapital "This is the predicate used to relate a #$CommercialOrganization or other #$IntelligentAgent to the amount of venture capital (some #$MonetaryValue) that has been invested by outside investors to start the company. (#$ventureCapital AGENT AMOUNT) means the AMOUNT of venture capital that has been invested in the AGENT.")
(comment EquipmentTypeForStructuredActivityFn "(#$EquipmentTypeForStructuredActivityFn ACTIVITY) denotes the collection of equipment types whose elements are used in performances of ACTIVITY [a #$DevisedStructuredActivity].  There are two sufficient conditions for a given collection being a subset of this collection.  One such condition applies to device collections whose major, predominant, or primary function is to play the role of #$deviceUsed in performances of ACTIVITY (see #$primaryFunction).  Another such condition applies to device collections which are always used in performances of ACTIVITY (see #$eventTypeUsesDeviceType).  For example, (#$EquipmentTypeForStructuredActivityFn #$Baseball-TheGame) denotes ``baseball equipment''.  Thus, because #$BaseballBat and #$BaseballMitt have #$BaseballGame as one of their main functions, Cyc should infer that these two device types are instances of (#$EquipmentTypeForStructuredActivityFn #$Baseball-TheGame).")
(comment BoardGame "A specialization of #$Game.  All instances of #$BoardGame are specifications of the rules and equipment required for playing certain games using specially designed boards.")
(comment SchemaObjectFn "If ODS is an instance of #$ObjectDefiningSchema and INDEXICAL is an instance of #$PhysicalFieldIndexical, then (#$SchemaObjectFn ODS INDEXICAL) is equal to *the* object defined by ODS which is indexed by INDEXICAL.  This function is often used in #$fieldDecoding sentences in which the physical field value is itself an object defined by ODS or another schema.")
(comment Gearing-Mechanical "Spinning of a #$Gear along a fixed #$Axle [sometimes called 'shaft'] (through its center of gravity) while constrained by a #$Gear along the surface. #$Gearing-Mechanical is used for transmission of energy between the #$Axle and the surface in either or both directions.  There is no #$Slipping in #$Gearing-Mechanical between the #$Gear and the external surface (usually a #$Gear) with which it is in contact.  Both the #$Gear and the surface with which it is in contact move.  If a #$Gear is merely rolling along a fixed track, the process is considered #$WheelRolling (albeit with slippage protection) instead of #$Gearing-Mechanical.  Gear teeth may be #$Sliding-Generic against each other by design.  The ratio of rotational velocities between two gears is a design feature, dependent upon the number, shape, and arrangement of teeth on each #$Gear, called the gear ratio.")
(comment IntervalEndedByFn "A unary function that takes an instance of #$TemporalThing as an argument and returns an instance of #$TimeInterval.  (#$IntervalEndedByFn TEMP-OBJ) denotes the time interval which ends when TEMP-OBJ starts.  (#$IntervalEndedByFn TEMP-OBJ) is strictly before TEMP-OBJ -- i.e. (#$startsAfterEndingOf TEMP-OBJ (#$IntervalEndedByFn TEMP-OBJ)).  The beginning of this interval coincides with the beginning of all time (#$Always-TimeInterval), if time has a beginning.")
(comment IrishPerson "The collection of people who are #$citizens of #$IrishRepublic, or participate in its #$NationalCulture.")
(comment trainer "A #$PersonalAssociationPredicate and specialization of #$frequentlyInContactWith and #$receivesServicesFrom.  (#$trainer TRAINEE TRAINER) means that TRAINER provides training to TRAINEE.  The type of training provided could be fitness training, on-the-job training, martial arts training, etc.")
(comment TransitiveInfinitivePhraseNPGapFrame "This frame is used for transitive adjectives that take an infinitival complement that has a gap in one position, i.e. it is for 'tough'-class adjectives.  In HPSG terms: [COMPS <CP[Infinitive]/NP>].  Can be used with or without a 'for NP' at the beginning of the infinitival.  Examples: 'The movie was exciting to see __', 'The game was tough for the team to win __'.  Note that all adjectives in #$TransitiveInfinitivePhraseNPGapFrame can also be used in the #$PleonasticInfinitivePhraseFrameType.")
(comment PleonasticThatClauseFrame " This frame is used for transitive words (usually verbs or adjectives) that take an expletive 'it' subject and exactly one complement which is a that-clause, but can not take a that-clause as a subject. In HPSG terms: [SUBJ <NP[it]>], [COMPS <S[that]>. Example: 'it seems that he left', 'it appears that the candidate wins the election next year', but not *'that he left seems'. 'it is likely that John and Mary will get along', 'it is nice that Fred invited us' do not have this frame, since they can also be used with a clausal subject as in 'That Fred invited us to the party is nice'. Use #$ClausalSubjectTransitiveNPFrame for those cases.")
(disjointWith BodyOfWater LandTopographicalFeature)
(comment CreditCard "A specialization of both #$Card and #$TenderObject.  Each instance of #$CreditCard is a piece of plastic that enables authorized users to spend the card-issuing company's money, drawn as a (usually unsecured) loan through an associated instance of #$CreditCardAccount (q.v.) under a pre-arranged credit agreement.  The credit card company credits the vendor of the purchased goods or services and bills the card user (see #$Bill-PaymentRequest), usually with interest. See also #$creditor, #$agentOwesAgentAmount.")
(comment hasAssistants "A #$PersonalAssociationPredicate.  (#$hasAssistants PERSON ASSISTANT) means that PERSON has ASSISTANT as an assistant.")
(comment EscapeQuote "A quotation-related device for making general statements about CycL expressions.  #$EscapeQuote is used to allow certain free variables occurring inside an expression that is quoted to \"escape\" (i.e. remain free with respect to) the quotation.  It is thus similar in function to, though more flexible than, #$QuasiQuote (q.v.).
<p>
To be more precise: the syncategorematic symbol <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> is used in conjunction with the special quotation symbol <code>#$Quote</code> (q.v.) as a device for allowing genuinely free variables to occur inside quoted expressions, in order that one may meaningfully \"quantify into\" such expressions and thereby generalize over them.  Thus, <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> is used to indicate that the free variables occurring within its scope (which is itself inside the wider scope of an occurrence of <code>#$Quote</code>) are <i>not</i> themselves to be interpreted as being quoted.
<p>
The syntax of <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> is such that it can only be used in conjunction with <code>#$Quote</code>.  For any CycL expression <code>EXPR</code>, <code>(#$EscapeQuote EXPR)</code> is not by itself a syntactically well-formed CycL expression; but the latter can appear as <i>part of</i> a well-formed expression, subject to the following restrictions.  (i) Any occurrence of <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> in a CycL expression must itself be inside the scope of an occurrence of <code>#$Quote</code>.  (ii) One occurrence of <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> can be inside the scope of another only if there is an intermediate occurrence of <code>#$Quote</code> (i.e. one whose scope subsumes that of the former and is subsumed by that of the latter).
<p>
The semantics of <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> is such that a formula <code>(#$Quote ... (#$EscapeQuote EXPR) ...)</code> that contains only one occurrence of <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> means just what the corresponding formula <code>(#$Quote ... EXPR ...)</code> means, except that any free occurrence of a variable in <code>EXPR</code> is to be interpreted as still being genuinely free with respect to <code>(#$Quote ... (#$EscapeQuote EXPR) ...)</code>.  In <code>(#$Quote ... EXPR ...)</code>, by contrast, any such variables are taken to be regular quoted constituents of a quoted compound expression; thus they do not function there as variables, but as <i>quoted-names of</i> variables -- i.e. as terms that denote variables.  
<p>
For example, while the term <code>(#$Quote (#$isa ?X #$Cat))</code> simply denotes the open sentence <code>(#$isa ?X #$Cat)</code>, the expression
<pre>
  (#$Quote (#$EscapeQuote (#$isa ?X #$Cat)))
</pre>
is itself an open term in which <code>?X</code> occurs free; it thus does not denote in its own right, though many <i>instantiations</i> of it do denote.  E.g. the instantiation <code>(#$Quote (#$EscapeQuote (#$isa #$Patches-Cat #$Cat)))</code> denotes the sentence <code>(#$isa #$Patches-Cat #$Cat)</code>).
<p>
The foregoing explanation generalizes to cases where multiple occurrences of <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> are within the scope of the same <code>#$Quote</code>.  For example, in the expression
<pre>
  (#$Quote (#$loves (#$EscapeQuote ?X) (#$EscapeQuote ?Y)))
</pre>
-- which is equivalent to <code>(#$Quote (#$EscapeQuote (#$loves ?X ?Y)))</code> -- both <code>?X</code> and <code>?Y</code> occur free.
<p>
As mentioned above, the purpose of the <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> device is to enable \"quantifying into\" quoted contexts: to provide a coherent interpretation of formulas in which an occurrence of a #$Quantifier (or other #$ScopingRelation) that is outside the scope of a given occurrence of <code>#$Quote</code> can be viewed as binding occurrences of variables that are <i>inside</i> the scope of that <code>#$Quote</code>.  For example, the sentence
<pre>
  (#$thereExists ?X
    (#$and
      (#$isa ?X #$Cat)
      (#$isa (#$Quote (#$EscapeQuote ?X)) #$CycLAtomicTerm)))
</pre>
says that there is at least one cat that is denoted by a CycL atomic term.  Without the <code>#$EscapeQuote</code> wrapper, the second conjunct of the above would be a <i>closed</i> sentence, and the entire quantified sentence would express the far less interesting claim that there exists at least one cat and the expression <code>?X</code> is an atomic term of CycL.
<p>
Note that the above (interesting) existential claim could be expressed more tersely using #$QuasiQuote.  #$EscapeQuote is indispensible only in cases where one wants to allow <i>some but not all</i> of the free variables in a given expression to escape quotation.  For example, in the (implicitly quantified) rule
<pre>
  (#$isa
    (#$Quote (#$loves ?X (#$EscapeQuote ?Y))) 
    #$CycLOpenSentence)
</pre>
only the variable <code>?Y</code> occurs free; the <code>?X</code> is part of what gets quoted (and thus functions like a closed term that <i>denotes</i> the CycL variable <code>?X</code>).  What the rule says, in effect, is that any well-formed expression built with the predicate-expression <code>#$loves</code> followed by the variable <code>?X</code> followed by a CycL term is an open sentence of CycL.
<p>
For more on the use of #$EscapeQuote and #$Quote for quantifying into quoted contexts, see the shared #$NoteAboutQuotingInCycL. See also #$denotes, #$quotedIsa, #$quotedArgument, and #$NoteAboutSyncategorematicSymbols.")
(comment AutomobileTypeByBrand "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$RoadVehicleType.  Each instance of #$AutomobileTypeByBrand is a collection of automobiles that are of the same brand as one another. ")
(comment CreditCardTypeByBrand "#$CreditCardTypeByBrand is a specialization of  #$SecondOrderCollection that facets #$CreditCard.  Each instance of #$CreditCardTypeByBrand is a specialization of #$CreditCard, instances of which are all of the same brand.")
(comment ambassadorFromTo "(#$ambassadorFromTo PERSON GPE1 GPE2) means that PERSON is an ambassador from GPE1 to GPE2.")
(disjointWith PurposefulAction DecompositionProcess)
(comment Lumberjack "A #$PersonTypeByOccupation.  Each instance of #$Lumberjack is a person who makes his living by cutting down trees and/or cutting fallen trees into logs.")
(comment CriminalAct "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$CriminalAct is a relatively serious #$IllegalAction.  This excludes relatively minor infractions such as (in most legal systems) #$Jaywalking and #$Littering.")
(comment NationalTaxAgency "#$NationalTaxAgency is a specialization of #$TaxAgency and #$NationalOrganization.  Each instance of #$NationalTaxAgency is a revenue agency that is part of the government of a #$Country, and which is concerned primarily with collecting tax money (from national taxes) from the residents of that #$Country.  This collection does _not_ include customs agencies and agencies that collect minor fees only.")
(comment WeaponType "A #$SecondOrderCollection.  #$WeaponType is the collection of all specializations of #$Weapon.")
(comment Arson "A #$TemporalObjectType.  Each instance of #$Arson is an instance of #$SettingSomethingOnFire that is also a #$CriminalAct.")
(disjointWith SportsPenalizing HarmingAnAgent)
(disjointWith Artifact-NonAgentive (GroupFn Agent-Generic))
(comment ActorSlot "A collection of binary predicates; a specialization of #$Role.  Each instance of #$ActorSlot relates some instance of #$Event to a temporal thing involved in that event (here called a \"participant\", although the thing in question might not be playing an active role in the event).  The first argument of every instance of #$ActorSlot is constrained to be an instance of some specialization of #$Event, and the second argument is constrained to be an instance of some specialization of #$SomethingExisting.  
<p>
All instances of #$ActorSlot have #$actors as their #$genlPreds, directly or indirectly, so that the actor slots form a kind of hierarchy.  Each specialized actor slot indicates <i>how</i> its participant participates in the event, i.e., in what role (e.g., #$inputs, #$outputs, #$doneBy).  
<p>
Actor slots are <i>not</i> used to indicate the time of an event's occurrence, external representations of the event, and other more remotely related things that are not directly or indirectly \"involved\" in the occurrence of the event.  Time and other quantities are relevant to events but are not instances of #$SomethingExisting; thus, they are related to events by some non-#$ActorSlot predicate.  
<p>
Things which are remotely related to the event -- for instance, someone who is affected by the event but doesn't exist when the event occurs -- may be related using some instance of #$Role that does not belong to #$ActorSlot, such as #$affectedAgent.  See also #$Role.")
(comment GeographicalHemisphere "A specialization of #$GeographicalRegion.  Each instance of the #$GeographicalHemisphere is a hemisphere that is the surface of a planet.")
(comment MetaLanguagePredicate "A subcollection of #$MetaKnowledgePredicate (q.v.) whose instances are used to state certain syntactic, semantic, or pragmatic facts about particular CycL expressions.  As such, #$MetaLanguagePredicates typically have at least one of their argument-places constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$CycLExpression or some specialization thereof; such argument-places might or might not be \"implicitly quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument).  Examples include #$termFormulas, #$denotes, and #$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration.
<p>
Note that merely having an argument-place that is restricted to CycL expressions is <i>not</i> a sufficient condition for being a #$MetaLanguagePredicate.  For example, although both arguments to #$causes-PropProp are required to be instances of #$CycLSentence-Assertible, it is not a metalanguage predicate since it is used to state a <i>causal</i> relationship between the states of affairs those sentences happen to describe.  In contrast, #$denotes qualifies as a metalanguage predicate since it is used to make <i>semantic</i> statements regarding what the denotation of some CycL term is.")
(comment pathFromToInSystem "This predicate is used to state that a certain directed path goes from one given point to another.  <code>(#$pathFromToInSystem PATH X Y SYS)</code> means that, in <code>SYS</code>, <code>PATH</code> goes from <code>X</code> to <code>Y</code>, and this does not go against the directions of any of <code>PATH</code>'s constituent links.  
<p>
To be precise, where <code>SYS</code> is a semi-directed  path system, <code>PATH</code> is a path in <code>SYS</code>, and both <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> are points in <code>SYS</code>,  #$pathFromToInSystem can be defined inductively as follows: 
<p>
(i) For each  link <code>LINK</code> in <code>SYS</code>, <code>(#$pathFromToInSystem LINK X Y SYS)</code> iff  <code>(#$linkFromToInSystem LINK X Y SYS)</code>. 
<p>
(ii) For each link <code>LINK</code> in <code>SYS</code>, and  for any points <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> in <code>SYS</code> such that either <code>(#$betweenOnPath B A Y LINK)</code>  or <code>(#$betweenOnPath A X B LINK)</code> holds, <code>(#$linkFromToInSystem LINK X Y SYS)</code>  holds iff <code>(#$pathFromToInSystem (#$SubPathBetweenFn PATH A B) A B SYS)</code> holds.
<p>  
(iii) If both <code>(#$pathFromToInSystem PATH1 X Y SYS)</code> and <code>(#$pathFromToInSystem  PATH2 Y Z SYS)</code> hold, and if no point in <code>SYS</code> except <code>Y</code> is on both <code>PATH1</code> and  <code>PATH2</code>, then <code>(#$pathFromToInSystem PATH X Z SYS)</code> holds, where <code>PATH</code> is the  concatenation of <code>PATH1</code> and <code>PATH2</code>.")
(comment quotedIsa "A binary #$MetaLanguagePredicate (q.v.) that relates CycL expressions to the #$SubLExpressionTypes (q.v.) of which they are instances.  #$quotedIsa is thus like a restricted version of #$isa (q.v.), but with one important difference: the first argument-place of #$quotedIsa is \"implicitly quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument).  So a ground atomic sentence of the form <code>(#$quotedIsa THING EXPR-TYPE)</code> does <i>not</i> mean that <code>THING</code> itself is an instance of <code>EXPR-TYPE</code>.  Rather, such a sentence is partly self-referential, and means that the particular <i>CycL expression</i> appearing in the sentence's own first argument-position is an instance of <code>EXPR-TYPE</code>.  Thus, #$quotedIsa provides a convenient shorthand for stating certain things that would otherwise require explicit quotation (or some other device for naming expressions).
<p>
This is better illustrated with a specific example.  Suppose we wish to state that the CycL constant <code>#$IndianOcean</code> is an instance of the CycL expression type #$PublicConstant.  We cannot express this with the straightforward #$isa sentence <code>(#$isa #$IndianOcean #$PublicConstant)</code>, as that states the falsehood that the IndianOcean itself -- which is <i>not</i> a constant but a body of water -- is a public constant.  But we can express precisely what we want like this:
<pre>
  (#$quotedIsa #$IndianOcean #$PublicConstant) .
</pre>  
Now #$quotedIsa is a #$MacroRelation (q.v.), and by its #$expansion any given #$quotedIsa sentence is equivalent to some #$isa sentence with an <i>explicitly</i> quoted first argument.  The sentence displayed above turns out to be equivalent to: 
<pre>
  (#$isa (#$Quote #$IndianOcean) #$PublicConstant).
</pre> 
But the #$quotedIsa version has two related practical advantages over the #$isa version.  First, the former is syntactically simpler than the latter.  Second, the simpler syntax of the former makes it easier to browse in the Knowedge Base: while the #$quotedIsa version is conveniently indexed under the KB Browser page for the constant <code>#$IndianOcean</code>, the #$isa version would apparently be indexed under a separate, brand new page for the term <code>(#$Quote #$IndianOcean)</code>. Generalizing the point, #$quotedIsa lets us avoid having potentially to double the number of pages currently in the browser.
<p>
For the semantically more complicated (but rarely encountered) case in which #$quotedIsa's first argument-place is filled with an <i>open</i> expression, see the accompanying #$cyclistNotes.")
(comment StrobeLight "A specialization of #$ElectricalLightingDevice.  Each instance of #$StrobeLight is a lamp that emits light pulses of high intensity and short duration.  Typically, the pulses are emitted in rapid succession at regular intervals, although some stobe lights permit adjustment of these parameters.")
(comment SpecificEntryFormatPredicate "A specialization of  #$ArgFormatPredicate (q.v.) whose instances correspond to particular entry-formats for the argument-places of predicates.   Each instance FORMAT of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate relates a  predicate PRED to an integer N just in case PRED's Nth argument-place  is subject to the particular entry-format restriction that FORMAT  represents.  An argument entry-format is a kind of restriction  on a predicate regarding how many things, or the range of things,  that the predicate holds of with respect to a given one of its  argument-places.  More precisely: given any particular way of fixing  the arguments in the rest of the predicate's argument-places, the  entry-format tells us something about the number of different things,  or the range of things, that can occupy the given argument-place such  that the relation holds of those arguments.  That is, supposing the  given argument-place is the Nth, the entry-format tells us something  about the number or range of different sequences of arguments of  which the predicate holds and that differ from each other only in  their Nth items.  For example, #$singleEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.) is  used to state that in every case there is at most only one such  sequence, #$openEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.) to state that there is no  particular (upper or lower) limit to the number of such sequences  that applies to every case, and #$intervalEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.)  to state that the Nth items of any two such sequences must be  #$ScalarIntervals that \"overlap\" (see #$overlapsIntervals).  Thus,  #$biologicalMother has the format specification #$openEntryFormatInArgs with respect to its first argument-place, since a given female animal  might have any number (including zero) of offspring; but this predicate has the format specification #$singleEntryFormatInArgs with respect to its second argument-place, since any given animal has at most (in fact,  exactly) one biological mother.  And the format specified for the second  argument-place of #$bodyTemperature is #$intervalEntryFormatInArgs  because, while a given creature (at a given moment in time) has a  single exact (see #$ScalarPointValue) body temperature, we want the  predicate #$bodyTemperature to be flexible enough to allow us to  specify this temperature with varying degrees of precision (e.g. as  \"98.6 degrees Fahrenheit\" or as \"between 98 and 99 degrees\" or as  \"less than 100 degrees\").  See the various instances of  #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate for further details.  See also #$GeneralEntryFormatPredicate.  Note that, in contrast with what  #$ArgTypePredicates are used for, specifying an argument entry-format  for a predicate does _not_ impose any sort of necessary condition for  semantic well-formedness.  A violation of a legitimate entry-format  constraint necessarily involves two or more statements, and at least  one them must be _untrue_; but there is no implication that any of  them is malformed.")
(comment GeneralEntryFormatPredicate "A specialization of  #$ArgFormatPredicate (q.v.) whose instances are used to relate predicates to the specific entry-formats (see  #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate) that apply to their various  argument-places.  For example, #$arg1Format relates a predicate  PRED to the #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate that holds between  PRED and its first (or \"arg1\") argument-place.  #$argFormat  is similar, but has an additional argument-place for specifying the relevant argument-place of PRED.  The primary utility of the  above sorts of #$GeneralEntryFormatPredicates is to provide standard forms for assigning _any_ specific entry-format to a predicate with respect to a given argument-place.  There are also the #$InterArgFormatPredicates (q.v.), which specify particular entry-formats that are to apply to a given argument-place  _conditionally_ (i.e. when a certain specified condition  obtains).")
(comment coextensionalEntryFormatInArgs "A instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that amounts
to what might be called \"interchangeability of coextensional
arguments\", taken in a broad sense whereby sets and collections
(see #$SetOrCollection) as well as pairs of relations (see #$Relation)
can be \"coextensional\".  #$coextensionalEntryFormatInArgs can be
used to characterize (truth-condition-wise) a given argument-place
of a predicate that is constrained (semantic well-formedness-wise)
to being filled with instances of only either #$Collection,
#$SetOrCollection, or #$Relation.  That is, the given argument-
position's #$argIsa constraint (or the \"intersection\" of the
position's #$argIsa constraints, if there are more than one) is to
some (proper or improper) specialization of either #$SetOrCollection
or #$Relation. Suppose that #$coextensionalEntryFormatInArgs holds
of PRED with respect to its Nth argument-place, and that the
sentence (PRED ... ARGN ...) is true.  It follows that any sentence
(PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) that differs from the first sentence at
most in its Nth argument is true if and only if ARGN-PRIME is
coextensional with ARGN.
<p>
\"Coextensional\" is here defined as follows.  Collections are
coextensional just in case they have exactly the same instances
(see #$coExtensional); set-or-collections are coextensional
just in case they have exactly the same elements (see
#$coextensionalSetOrCollections); predicates are coextensional
just in case they hold of exactly the same sequences of arguments;
and functions are coextensional just in case they they are defined
for exactly the same sequences of arguments and have the same
value for any such sequence.
<p>
#$coextensionalEntryFormatInArgs is useful because sometimes
a certain argument-place of a given predicate is constrained to
be a collection or set-or-collection or predicate or function,
and all that's essential to the predicate's holding, with
respect to that argument place (and a given fixed array of
arguments for its other argument places), is the (set-or-)
collection's _extent_ (see #$extent), the predicate's
\"predicate-extent\", or the function's domain-to-range
correlations.  Such argument-places should not have the
format #$singleEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.), since it is
possible for two different collections, or a set and a
collection, or two different predicates, or two different
functions to have the same extension.  On the other hand,
the format #$openEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.) would place no
real restriction on such an argument-place.
#$coextensionalEntryFormatInArgs characterizes such
argument places appropriately.")
(comment subEventsEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$Event (q.v.) or some specialization thereof.  (#$subEventsEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, for any two events such that if either one is taken as the Nth argument PRED holds of those arguments, one of those events must be a subevent (see #$subEvents) of the other.  That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then either (#$subEvents ARGN ARGN-PRIME) or (#$subEvents ARGN-PRIME ARGN) must hold.  For example, #$subEventsEntryFormatInArgs holds of #$finalSportsEvent with respect to its first argument-place.  Given that (#$relationAllExists #$finalSportsEvent #$TennisTournament #$TennisMatch), (#$relationAllExists #$finalSportsEvent #$TennisTournament #$TennisSet), and a couple of other simple facts about tennis, this allows us to infer that the final set of a given tennis tournament is a subevent of its final match.")
(comment partsEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$PartiallyTangible (q.v.) or some specialization thereof.  (#$partsEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, for any two objects such that if either one is taken as the Nth argument PRED holds of those arguments, those objects must both be \"parts\" (see #$physicalDecompositions) of the same object.  That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then there  must be some WHOLE such that both (#$physicalDecompositions WHOLE ARGN) and  (#$physicalDecompositions WHOLE ARGN-PRIME) hold.  For example,  #$partsEntryFormatInArgs holds of #$physicalParts (q.v.) with respect to its second argument-place, reflecting the fact that if a given human thumb (e.g.) is a part both of some human hand and some human arm, then both the hand and the arm must be parts of the same human body.")
(comment genlsEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$Collection (q.v.) or some specialization thereof.  (#$genlsEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, for any two collections such that if either one is taken as the Nth argument PRED holds of those arguments, those collections must not be disjoint (see #$disjointWith).  That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then (#$disjointWith ARGN ARGN-PRIME) must _not_ hold.  For example, #$genlsEntryFormatInArgs holds of #$resultIsa (q.v.) with respect to it second argument-place.")
(comment genlPredsEntryFormatInArgs "An instance of  #$SpecificEntryFormatPredicate (q.v.) that is used only with argument-places  that are constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$Predicate (q.v.) or  some specialization thereof.  (#$genlPredsEntryFormatInArgs PRED N) means  that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides  the Nth, for any two predicates such that if either one is taken as the Nth  argument PRED holds of those arguments, one those predicates must be a  generalization (see #$genlPreds) of the other.  That is, if the sequences  <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items  and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then  either (#$genlPreds ARGN ARGN-PRIME) or (#$genlPreds ARGN-PRIME ARGN) must  hold.  For example, #$genlPredsEntryFormatInArgs holds of #$soleFunction  with repsect to its third argument-place.  This allows us to state both  (#$typeSoleFunction #$Bus-RoadVehicle #$TransportInvolvingADriver #$transporter)  and (#$typeSoleFunction #$Bus-RoadVehicle #$TransportInvolvingADriver #$vehicle), given (#$genlPreds #$vehicle #$transporter).")
(comment ComputerDisplayActiveArea-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as an area on a computer screen which can be used by the user for interaction with the system via some input device.  The area on the screen which is active is normally indicated on the screen, as is the interaction that does occur")
(comment ComputerDisplayFramePage "The collection of all frame-filling ``pages'' in a window [#$ComputerDisplayWindow] on a computer screen.  Only one such #$ComputerDisplayFramePage may be displayed on a given #$ComputerDisplayFrame at a time.")
(comment ComputerDisplayActiveArea "The collection of all areas on a computer screen [#$ComputerDisplayScreenRegion] which can be used by the user for interaction with the system via some input device.  Such an area is normally indicated on the screen, as is the interaction that does occur.")
(comment ComputerDisplayFrame "The collection of all frames within instances of #$ComputerDisplayWindow.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayFrame ComputerDisplayFramePage)
(comment ComputerDisplayWindow "The collection of all regions of a computer screen [see #$ComputerDisplayScreenRegion] on which an active computer ``window'' is displayed.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayFrame ComputerDisplayWindow)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayFramePage ComputerDisplayWindow)
(comment ComputerDisplayWindow-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as ``windows'' on a computer screen.")
(comment ComputerDisplayFrame-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as ``frames'' in a window on a computer screen.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayFrame-CW ComputerDisplayWindow-CW)
(comment ComputerDisplayFramePage-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as frame-filling ``pages'' in a window on a computer screen.  Only one such #$ComputerDisplayFramePage-CW may be instantiated in a given instantiation of a given #$ComputerDisplayFrame-CW at a time.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayFrame-CW ComputerDisplayFramePage-CW)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayFramePage-CW ComputerDisplayWindow-CW)
(comment ComputerDisplayScreenRegion-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as regions on a computer screen.")
(comment doOutputCommentString "(#$doOutputCommentString HACKER FILE STRING) means that the HACKER adds the comment 
string STRING to the end of FILE. This method should be decomposed differently in
specific programming domains depending on that language's style of comment. For
instance, in the C language comments are bracketed by \"/*\" and \"*/\", and in
lisp programs comments are preceded by a \";\" on each line.")
(comment ComputerDisplayTypeInField-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as a type-in field on a computer screen (as an instance of #$ComputerDisplayTypeInField).  An instantiation of #$ComputerDisplayTypeInField-CW will (normally) display the characters typed on the appropriate keyboard while the type-in field is active.  Special varieties of type-in fields that do not output the characters typed are used for security purposes.  One variety produces no output, while another outputs asterisks or other default characters to indicate a character has been input, but not the identity of that character.  If the output is to hardcopy instead of a computer screen, sometimes the field is initially blackened with a large amount of overtyping before the secure characters are typed.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayTypeInField-CW ComputerDisplayLink-CW)
(comment ComputerDisplayLink-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as ``links'' on a computer screen.  When such a link is activated, another page is accessed.")
(comment ComputerDisplayLink "The collection of all instances of #$ComputerDisplayActiveArea that are ``links'' on a computer screen.  When such a link is activated, another page is accessed.")
(comment ComputerDisplayTypeInField "The collection of all instances of #$ComputerDisplayActiveArea which are type-in fields, normally on a computer screen.  An instance of #$ComputerDisplayTypeInField will (normally) display the characters typed on the appropriate keyboard while it is active.  Special varieties of type-in fields that do not output the characters typed are used for security purposes.  One variety produces no output, while another outputs asterisks or other default characters to indicate a character has been input, but not the identity of that character.  If the output is hardcopy instead of a computer screen, sometimes the field is initially blackened with a large amount of overtyping before the secure characters are typed.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayTypeInField ComputerDisplayLink)
(comment ComputerDisplayButton-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as an image of a button on a computer screen.  A user may activate the button by hitting the Enter/Return key on a keyboard if the button has been selected or by clicking a mouse button while the cursor is pointing at the button.  This collection does not include physical buttons used to communicate with a computer, even if the button lights up.")
(comment ComputerDisplayButton "The collection of all instances of #$ComputerDisplayActiveArea that consist of an image of a button on a computer screen.  A user may activate the button by hitting the Enter/Return key on a keyboard if the button has been selected or by clicking a mouse button while the cursor is pointing at the button.  Such an activation initiates the execution of an instantiation of a specific #$ProgramStep associated with the button.  This collection does not include physical buttons used to communicate with a computer, even if the button lights up.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayTypeInField-CW ComputerDisplayMenuItem-CW)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayLink-CW ComputerDisplayMenuItem-CW)
(comment ComputerDisplayMenuItem-CW "The collection of all instances of #$ComputerDisplayActiveArea-CW that are to be instantiated as active items in menus on a computer screen.  The menu items may act as buttons or links.")
(comment ComputerDisplayMenu-CW "The collection of all instances of #$VisualWork that are to be instantiated as menus on a computer screen.  Each menu is comprised of a list of active links.")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayTypeInField-CW ComputerDisplayMenu-CW)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayLink-CW ComputerDisplayMenu-CW)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayButton-CW ComputerDisplayMenu-CW)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayMenu-CW ComputerDisplayMenuItem-CW)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayTypeInField ComputerDisplayMenuItem)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayLink ComputerDisplayMenuItem)
(comment ComputerDisplayMenuItem "The collection of all instances of active items in menus on specific computer screens.  The menu items may act as buttons or links.  See #$ComputerDisplayMenuItem-CW to refer to a menu item for a program instead of a physical region on a specific screen.")
(comment ComputerDisplayMenu "The collection of all instances of menu #$ComputerDisplayActiveAreas on a computer screen.  Each menu is comprised of a list of active menu items [#$ComputerDisplayMenuItem].")
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayTypeInField ComputerDisplayMenu)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayLink ComputerDisplayMenu)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayButton ComputerDisplayMenu)
(disjointWith ComputerDisplayMenu ComputerDisplayMenuItem)
(comment windowOfFrame "(#$windowOfFrame FRAME WINDOW) means that FRAME is an instance of #$ComputerDisplayFrame that is displayed in the instance of #$ComputerDisplayWindow, WINDOW.")
(comment frameDisplaysPage "(#$frameDisplaysPage FRAME PAGE) means that FRAME currently displays the #$ComputerDisplayFramePage, PAGE.  Given frames display different pages at different times but only display one at any given time.")
(comment actionTypeForActiveArea-CW "(#$actionTypeForActiveArea-CW ACT_TYPE ACTIVE_AREA_CW) means that instantiations of ACTIVE_AREA_CW will intiate actions which are instances of ACT_TYPE, when the active area is activated.")
(comment actionTypeForActiveArea "(#$actionTypeForActiveArea ACT_TYPE ACTIVE_AREA) means that ACTIVE_AREA, an instance of #$ComputerDisplayActiveArea, will intiate actions which are instances of ACT_TYPE, when the active area is activated.")
(comment GarmentTypeByRegionOfBodyCovered "A specialization of #$ExistingObjectType.  Each instance of this collection is a strict facet of #$Garment (see #$facets-Strict).  That is, the instances of this collection are mutually exclusive specializations of #$Garment -- each instance is a specialization of garment whose instances, when worn, cover a certain region of the body.  The primary instances are #$Clothing-WholeBody, #$Clothing-Bottom, and #$Clothing-Top.")
(comment outputNewline "(#$outputNewline PROGRAMMER FILE) means that the PROGRAMMER appends a newline to the end of FILE. This action predicate is necessary since in some programming languages newlines are important syntatically. For example, consider the \"//\" style comments in the C++ programming language.")
(comment FloatObject "The float data type is used to store real-valued numbers in

C-like programming languages.")
(comment DataArrayFn "(#$DataArrayFn TYPE N) denotes the data type of arrays with N dimensions and

elements of TYPE.")
(comment EventPredicate-FrameTemplate "A special type of #$EventPredicate (q.v.). Instances of this collection are `template predicates' that can be used with any event type, i.e. spec of #$Event. Think of them as #$ActionPredicates that are not specific to a particular action type: the action type is specified explicitly in each assertion by entering it as the first argument of an #$EventPredicate-FrameTemplate. A template predicate takes as arguments exactly one event type and a fixed (though arbitrary) number of individuals: the resulting gaf expresses the relation existing between these individuals when they play specific roles in events of type TYPE. For instance, in place of (#$cleans SUBJ OBJ) we would use (#$doesTo #$Cleaning SUBJ OBJ): the latter is logically equivalent to the former. Typically, each argument (except the first) of an event-predicate template corresponds to the `participant role' expressed in Davidsonian representation by an #$ActorSlot: in the example above, SUBJ corresponds to the filler of a #$doneBy clause, and OBJ to the filler of an #$objectActedOn. However, this correspondence is not a definitional requirement: as in the case of other action predicates, one can define event-predicate templates that don't have an obvious correspondence to actor slots, although this practice is discouraged. Like  #$NonDavidsonianPredFn (q.v.), event-predicate templates abstract from any particular event types. Unlike that function, however, and like #$EventPredicates of the ordinary kind, each event-predicate template encodes a particular combination of #$ActorSlots or #$Roles in its argument structure.")
(comment does "The simplest event-predicate template: it expresses the fact that the filler of the 2nd argument place is engaged as the agent or performer in activitity of the type denoted by the first argument. A more precise meaning is determined by the temporal qualification parameters of the microtheory in which a #$does GAF is asserted. Example: (#$does #$Running #$PeterWagner) is true at time T with parameter #$CalendarSecond iff Peter runs at T and also at every sub-interval of T that is no smaller than a #$CalendarSecond -- which means <i>any</i> sub-interval of T. So the meaning of this assertion would be close to the English phrase 'Peter <i>is running</i>'. See also #$hasDone.")
(comment doesWith "This event-predicate template expresses the relation between the
agent (active participant) and the tool or device s/he uses in an
activity. Example: (#$thereExists ?X (#$and (#$isa ?X #$Feather)
(#$doesWith #$WritingACheck #$Klein ?X)))  is true iff
Charles Klein writes (or: is writing) a check using a feather.")
(comment doesToAtLocation "This event-predicate template expresses the relation between the
agent (active participant), the object (passive participant) and the
location of an activity. Example: (#$doesToAtLocation #$Teaching #$AlonzoChurch #$StephenCKleene
#$PrincetonUniversity) means that Alonzo Church teaches (or: is teaching) to
Stephen Kleene at Princeton University (something that was actually true at
some time in the 1930s).")
(comment doesToWithAtLocation "This event-predicate template expresses the relation between the agent (active participant), the object (passive participant), the device and the location of an activity. Example: (#$doesToWithAtLocation #$WritingByHand #$Klein DOCUMENT FEATHER #$CycorpHeadquarters) is true iff Charles Klein writes (or: is writing) DOCUMENT with device FEATHER at Cycorp's main offices.")
(comment associateSlotInArg "(#$associateSlotInArg EPFT-PRED SLOT N) means that SLOT is

the #$ActorSlot predicate conceptually associated with the N-th argument place of

EPFT-PRED, in the following sense: Suppose (EPFT-PRED COLL ARG_1 ... ARG_N ...)

is true. Then, were one to represent the same fact within the Davidsonian

framework, i.e., by asserting the existence of a particular event of type

COLL, ARG_N would play in the event the role expressed by SLOT.")
(comment coordinateSlot "For any N greater than or equal to 2, (#$coordinateSlot PRED1
PRED2 N) is true iff both (#$associateSlotInArg PRED1 SLOT N) and
(#$associateSlotInArg PRED2 SLOT N).")
(comment movingToFrom-EPFT "This event-predicate template expresses the relation between the

object in motion, the point of departure and the point of arrival in

types of activity subsumed by #$Movement-TranslationEvent. Example:

(#$movingToFrom-EPFT (#$TransportViaFn #$AirTransportationDevice) OBJECT 

LOC-TO LOC-FROM) is true iff OBJECT is (being) transported by air from place LOC-FROM

to place LOC-TO. It is not implied that OBJECT has either a passive or

active role in the motion, or that it need be a separate object from the

#$conveyor-Generic used.")
(comment transportedToFrom-EPFT "This event-predicate template expresses the relation between the

object transported, the point of departure and the point of arrival in

types of activity subsumed by #$TransportationEvent. Example:

(#$transportedToFrom-EPFT (#$TransportViaFn #$AirTransportationDevice) THING

LOC-TO LOC-FROM) is true iff THING is (being) transported by air from place LOC-FROM

to place LOC-TO. The #$associateSlotInArg condition for arg2 implies that

THING is distinct from the conveyor of transportation -- the package on the plane,

e.g. or the pilot, but not the plane itself.")
(comment decontextualizedCollection "(#$decontextualizedCollection COL) states that COL is a collection in which membership is context-independent.  In effect, any instance of COL is an instance of COL in every microtheory.")
(comment Class-OO "The collection of classes, or descriptions of sets of data objects with common structure, 

behaviour, relationships and semantics. Classes are a basis of the object-oriented  

programming paradigm.")
(comment TypedVariableFn "(#$TypedVariableFn DATATYPE NAME) denotes a variable called NAME, that
is of type TYPE. This function allows the variable to wear its type on
its sleeve. This notion of type need not be represented in the language
of the program in which the denoted variable is used.")
(comment ComputerDatatypeType "The collection of all data types found in programming languages. Every instance

of this collection should be a specialization of #$ProgramObject.")
(comment programFunctionResult "(#$programFunctionResult FUNCTION RESULT) means that
FUNCTION will return a value of type RESULT when it is
called in a computer program.")
(disjointWith ManualTypewriter ElectricalDevice)
(disjointWith ComputerImpactPrinter ComputerPrinter-Inkjet)
(comment doCloseFunctionDefinition "A ternary complex action predicate.
(#$doCloseFunctionDefinition PROGRAMMER FILE FUNCTION) means that PROGRAMMER
appends to the end of FILE code which closes the declaration of the
program function FUNCTION.")
(comment doCloseLocalVariablesScope "A variable arity complex action predicate. (#$doCloseLocalVariablesScope PROGRAMMER FILE . VARIABLES)
means that PROGRAMMER appends code to the end of computer file FILE 
that closes a scope in which VARIABLES are available. Compare with #$doOpenLocalVariablesScope.")
(comment doOutputFunctionReturn "A quaternary complex action predicate.
(#$doOutputFunctionReturn PROGRAMMER FILE ALGORITHM RET-EXPRESSION) means that
PROGRAMMER appends to FILE code which returns RET-EXPRESSION as the value for
a program function which implements ALGORITHM.")
(comment preferredEMailAddressText "<code>(#$preferredEMailAddressText OBJ ADDR)</code> means that #$EMailAddress <code>ADDR</code> is the primary e-mail address for #$PartiallyTangible <code>OBJ</code>.  Agents and organizations often have more than one e-mail address -- use #$eMailAddressText to specify e-mail addresses that are not primary.")
(comment LispConsObject "A specialization of #$LispObject. The collection of \"cons\" pairs in #$Lisp. The concept of the cons pair is closely linked the idea of a #$List (vis. #$LispListObject) in #$Lisp.")
(comment LispListObject "A specialization of #$LispObject. The collection of lists (i.e. sequences of #$LispObject) in #$Lisp. #$LispListObject objects are closely related to #$LispConsObject objects.")
(comment LispSymbol "A specialization of #$LispObject. The collection of symbols in #$Lisp.")
(comment StringObject "The character string data type. In the context of #$ProgrammingDomainVocabularyMt,

it is undetermined if values of this type are implemented as fized length character

arrays, or as null terminated strings.")
(comment LispObject "The generic data type of all lisp objects. This collection includes lists, symbols, strings, and all of the other data types of the  Common Lisp standard.")
(comment LispAtom "A specialization of #$LispObject. The collection of atomic objects in #$Lisp. Positive examples include numbers and symbols, negative examples include lists and vectors.")
(comment doProgramOutputLineToStream "A quaternary complex action predicate. (#$doProgramOutputLineToStream PROGRAMMER FILE STRING STREAM) means that 
PROGRAMMER appends code the end of FILE which will, when executed, write STRING
to STREAM, an instance of #$DataStream.")
(comment ImplementationConstant "The collection of constants that are included in a Cyc Knowledge Base only in order to support or enable one or more Cyc-based applications or capabilities. These constants are not used to represent common sense knowledge; nor are they used to represent knowledge shared by a significant subset of humans, such as specialized domain knowledge (e.g. the knowledge of experts in some field of study, such as nuclear physics).")
(comment asymmetricInArgs "A #$MetaRelation that is used to indicate that a given relation is \"asymmetric\" with respect to two given argument-places.   (#$asymmetricInArgs REL M N) means that REL is asymmetric  in its Mth and Nth arguments: these arguments are _not_  freely permutable.  This entails that, given any true (if REL is a predicate) or denoting (if REL is a function) formula built from (the CycL term for) REL, inter-substituting the formula's Mth and Nth arguments (while leaving the other arguments unchanged) will always result in a change in the formula's truth-value or denotatum).  If REL is a  binary predicate this is equivalent to its being an #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate.  More generally: (i) If REL  is a #$Predicate that holds of given argument-sequence SEQ, then REL does _not_ hold of the sequence SEQ-PERMUTED obtained by permuting the Mth and Nth items in SEQ (while leaving the other items unchanged).  (ii) If REL is a #$Function-Denotational whose value for a given argument-sequence SEQ is VALUE, then REL does _not_ have VALUE as its value for SEQ-PERMUTED (defined as above); REL might have something other than VALUE as its value for SEQ-PERMUTED, or it might be undefined for SEQ-PERMUTED. For example, a (hypothetical) ternary predicate called  `greaterThanOnScale', which took as arguments two physical objects and a scale for measuring some  physical property, would be asymmetric in its first and second arguments; i.e.  (#$asymmetricInArgs greaterThanOnScale 1 2) would hold.  For a function example, a (hypothetical) ternary function called `TransportToFromViaVehicleTypeFn' that  took as arguments two _distinct_ locations and a vehicle-type (and returned, say, the collection of all trips from the first location to the second location in vehicles of that type) would also be asymmetric in its first and second arguments.  Note that (each of) the #$argIsa constraint(s) on REL's Mth argument-place must not be disjoint with  (any of) the #$argIsa constraint(s) 
on REL's Nth  argument-place (see #$disjointWith), and likewise for the corresponding #$argGenl constraints (if any).  For some discussion of why 
this is so, see the #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement.  See also #$antiSymmetricInArgs and #$commutativeInArgs.")
(comment ObjectOrientedProgrammingLanguage "A specialization of #$AbstractProgrammingLanguage, #$ObjectOrientedProgrammingLanguage 
is the collection of languages that implement the objected-oriented 
programming methodology. This methodology is a data-based approach (as opposed
to a process-based approach) and emphasizes the principles of encapsulation,
inheritance, and polymorphism. Examples of OO programming languages include #$JavaProgrammingLanguage and #$CPlusPlus-ProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment doDefineProgramClass "A ternary complex action predicate. (#$doDefineProgramClass PROGRAMMER FILE CLASS) means that PROGRAMMER appends code
to the end of FILE that declares the datatype CLASS.")
(comment doOpenClassDefinition "A ternary complex action predicate. (#$doOpenClassDefinition PROGRAMMER FILE CLASS) means that PROGRAMMER appends code
to the end of FILE that signifies the beginning of a declaration of the datatype CLASS.")
(comment doCloseClassDefinition "A ternary complex action predicate. (#$doCloseClassDefinition PROGRAMMER FILE CLASS) means that PROGRAMMER appends code
to the end of FILE that signifies the end of a declaration of the datatype CLASS.")
(comment BIOS-Microtheory "The collection of all #$Microtheorys containing information specific to the #$Bio-SurveillanceProject.")
(comment SurveillanceSyndrome "A collection of #$AilmentConditions.  Each instance of #$SurveillanceSyndrome consists of at least one #$PhysiologicalCondition, usually an instance of #$AilmentCondition. More often an instance of a #$SurveillanceSyndrome consists of a #$Group of several different #$PhysiologicalConditions or #$AilmentConditions that are suffered by the same #$Animal at about the same time. The co-occurrence of these #$AilmentConditions often indicates the presence of a type of #$Infection. Syndromic surveillance systems track the number of cases of types of #$SurveillanceSyndromes over time for a given geographical region as potential indicators of the presence of #$InfectiousDiseaseTypes and #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemics in the population. Since #$SurveillanceSyndromes are just clusters of symptoms, they can be detected by a clinician during examination of the affected animal/person. Thus syndromic surveillance systems should be capable of detecting disease outbreaks earlier than surveillance systems that monitor diagnoses that require results from diagnostic procedures.")
(comment syndromePartTypes "(#$syndromePartTypes SYN-TYPE COND-TYPE) means that for every instance X of #$SurveillanceSyndrome SYN-TYPE, there exists some instance Y of #$PhysiologicalCondition COND-TYPE, such that (#$subSituations X Y). This is a type-level predicate used to represent which #$PhysiologicalConditions are individually necessary and jointly sufficient for the occurrence of the #$SurveillanceSyndrome. See the #$SharedNoteAboutDefiningSurveillanceSyndromes.")
(comment syndromePartTypes-Disjunctive "(#$syndromePartTypes-Disjunctive SYN-TYPE COND-TYPE) means that instances of the #$SurveillanceSyndrome SYN-TYPE are sometimes partly constituted by instances of the #$PhysiologicalCondition COND-TYPE. For example, some, but not all instances of #$BIOS-URISurveillanceSyndrome have (#$PainInFn #$NasalSinus) as a #$subSituations. If every instance of SYN-TYPE has some instance of COND-TYPE as a #$subSituations, then the relation between should be stated using #$syndromePartTypes. See #$SharedNoteAboutDefiningSurveillanceSyndromes.")
(comment VisualImageFeature "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$VisualImageFeature is a collection of instances of #$VisualImage that share some meta-characteristic.")
(comment StateTaxExpenseAccount "An account for the money which goes for paying state taxes.")
(comment CablingPerson "Someone who hooks up computer (and video, etc.?) cables as a job.")
(comment VisualImageTypeByPerspective "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$VisualImageTypeByPerspective is a collection of instances of #$VisualImage that share a perspective.")
(comment USCensusRaceAndOriginType "Categories used by the U.S. Census Bureau for (self-)classication of U.S. residents.")
(comment SundriesBuyer "Collection of people who purchase objects like cosmetics or medicines to be sold at a retail store.")
(comment SmallGroup "A small group of objects")
(comment Endurance "Performing an activity continuously over a long period of time.")
(comment BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType "A collection of collections. Each instance is a type of #$SurveillanceSyndrome monitored by the EssenceII BioSurveillance System. The instances of #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType form the complete set of #$SurveillanceSyndromes types monitored by the EssenceII BioSurveillanceSystem.")
(comment BIOS-LRISurveillanceSyndrome "The  #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of an infection of the lower respiratory system (see #$LowerRespiratoryInfection). The syndrome consists in part of cough, chest pain, or difficulty breathing.")
(comment BIOS-URISurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of an infection of the upper respiratory system (see #$UpperRespiratoryInfection). The syndrome consists in part of cold symptoms such as runny nose, sneezing or sinus pain.")
(comment BIOS-LGISurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of an infection of the lower gastrointestinal system (see #$LowerGastrointestinalInfection). The syndrome consists in part of diarrhea or abdominal pain.")
(comment Vomiting-AilmentCondition "The collection of instances of #$AilmentCondition in which one suffers from one or more instances of #$Vomiting.")
(comment BIOS-UGISurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of an infection of the upper gastrointestinal system (see #$UpperGastrointestinalInfection). The syndrome consists in part of nausea or vomiting.")
(comment BIOS-NEUROSurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of an infection involving the nerves or the nerve coverings (see #$NeurologicalInfection). The syndrome consists in part of headache, neuralgia or stiff neck.")
(comment BIOS-CNSSurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of an infection of the central nervous system (see #$CentralNervousSystemInfection). The syndrome consists in part of seizures or coma.")
(comment BIOS-FEVERSurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of a systemic infection (see #$FeverInfection). The syndrome consists in part of fever or malaise.")
(comment BIOS-DERMHEMSurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of a hemorrhagic skin infection (see #$HemorrhagicFeverInfection). The syndrome consists in part of bleeding from the skin or orifices.")
(comment Catnip-Plant "The collection of all instances of the plant catnip. It is
most frequently taken by humans in the form of a hot infusion to
relieve a variety of physical discomforts. It is soothing to the
nervous system and calming to the stomache. The leaves of this plant
are dried to protect the essential oils. About 80% of cats are highly
attracted to the odor of catnip, and will chew it, roll in it, and rub
against it to release the oils.")
(comment BIOS-DERMINFSurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that typically indicates the presence of a #$SkinInfection. The syndrome consists in part of rash, pustules, or skin ulcers.")
(comment BIOS-OTHERSurveillanceSyndrome "The #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType that consists of symptoms that do not fall under any of the other #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeTypes.")
(comment syndromeOfInfection "(#$syndromeOfInfection SYN-TYPE INF-TYPE) means that #$Infections of type INF-TYPE are often associated with #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType SYN-TYPE. If someone has both an instance of INF-TYPE and an instance of SYN-TYPE, then it is likely that the infection caused the syndrome. Thus, the presence of an instance of #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType SYN-TYPE is a useful predictor of the presence of an instance of the #$Infection INF-TYPE. However, since #$SurveillanceSyndromes are defined quite broadly, they are indicators of only very general types of #$Infections. Diagnosis of an instance of INF-TYPE usually requires more information than provided by observation of symptoms. For example, confirmed diagnoses require laboratory tests to identify the pathogen responsible for the infection. Thus the rapidity of syndromic surveillance is offset by its lack of precision.")
(comment StandardOutput "StandardOutput is the default output stream for many 
programs.")
(comment UpperRespiratoryInfection "The collection of all infections of the upper #$RespiratorySystem. The upper #$RespiratorySystem includes the nose, nasal cavities, sinuses, larynx, and trachea.")
(comment LowerRespiratoryInfection "The collection of all infections of the lower #$RespiratorySystem. The lower #$RespiratorySystem includes the lungs, bronchi, and alveoli.")
(comment UpperGastrointestinalInfection "The collection of all infections of the upper #$DigestiveSystem. The upper #$DigestiveSystem includes the #$Stomach, #$Esophagus and #$Liver.")
(comment LowerGastrointestinalInfection "The collection of all infections of the lower #$DigestiveSystem. The lower #$DigestiveSystem includes the #$SmallIntestine and #$LargeIntestine.")
(comment NeurologicalInfection "The collection of all infections of the #$Nerves and #$Meninges but excluding infections of the #$Brain and #$SpinalCord. See #$CentralNervousSystemInfection.")
(comment CentralNervousSystemInfection "The collection of all infections of the #$NervousSystem, especially those affecting the #$Brain and #$SpinalCord.")
(comment SkinInfection "The collection of all infections of the #$Skin and infections which manifest as abnormal skin conditions.")
(comment SourceCodePackage "The collection of source code objects used to modularize
computer programs written in certain languages (for example, #$JavaProgrammingLanguage). Packages may have their own 
namespaces for their internal symbols. Most often a
package has a theme or purpose that is shared by all the
functions and/or objects contained within it.")
(comment JavaPackageFn "#$JavaPackageFn is used to denote distinct packages in 
#$JavaProgrammingLanguage. Each package can be uniquely
identified by a dot-seperated pathname.")
(comment doDefineFunction "A ternary complex action predicate. (#$doDefineFunction PROGRAMMER FILE FUNCTION) means that PROGRAMMER appends to the end of FILE 
code that declares FUNCTION, an instance of #$ProgramFunction.")
(comment programFunctionInputs "(#$programFunctionInternals FUNCTION . ARGS) means that 

the computing action of FUNCTION is passed the arguments ARGS.")
(comment programFunctionResultInBodyAction "(#$programFunctionResultInBodyAction FUNCTION ACTIONPRED ACTIONARG) means that 

the result of FUNCTION is passed as the ACTIONARG argument of an action

sentence with functor ACTIONPRED, which computes the body of the function.")
(comment BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt "A collection of #$HypotheticalContexts. Each instance represents a hypothesis about the nature of a putative #$Epidemic. The non-derived assertions made in a #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt form the content of a hypothesis about the epidemic being investigated. These hypotheses will be generated initially in response to an alert generated by the Essence II Biosurveillance alerting system. These assertions can then be examined and made the starting point for inference without carrying the implicature that they are true outside the context of the hypothesis. Each hypothesis when fully developed will contain assertions about the type of infection constituting the epidemic, where and when exposure to the pathogen causing the outbreak occurred, what population is at risk for infection, and whether the outbreak was accidental or the result of a deliberate attack. Facts about the situation that are relevant to the truth of a hypothesis but are not themselves hypothetical should NOT be placed in an instance of #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt. Rather, they should be placed in a #$Microtheory visible from the relevant #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMts.")
(comment repeatAction "(#$repeatAction N ACTION) means that ACTION is repeated

N times in a row.")
(comment predicateConventionMt "(#$predicateConventionMt PRED MT) means that the predicate extent of the predicate PRED, by convention, is asserted in MT.")
(comment collectionConventionMt "(#$collectionConventionMt COL MT) means that assertions of the form (#$isa INS COL), for any INS, are by convention asserted in MT.")
(comment BIOS-AlertReportMicrotheory "The collection of #$BIOS-Microtheorys that contain #$CycL translations of alert reports issued by the Essence II Biosurveillance alerting system.")
(comment programFunctionLocalVariable "(#$programFunctionResult FUNCTION VAR) means that

FUNCTION has a variable VAR in its local scope.")
(comment OrganizationType "A type-level collection, the collection of all types of organizations.  Each instance is a collection of #$Organizations whose instances share some common feature that is important enough to warrant reifying the collection as a 'type'.")
(disjointWith OrganizationType OrganismClassificationType)
(comment ExposureToPathogen "The collection of #$ExposureToHarmfulSubstance events in which an instance of #$Organism-Whole is in proximity to and/or has contact with an instance of #$Microorganism in such a manner that effective transmission of the pathogen to the #$Organism-Whole is possible. If such transmission does occur and the #$Organism-Whole is susceptible to #$Infections caused by that #$Microorganism, the transmission is likely to result in #$Infection in the #$organismExposed. Thus, whether an instance of #$ExposureToSubstance is also an instance of #$ExposureToPathogen depends on the type of #$Microorganism and the possible means by which it can infect a susceptible host. For example, contact with a person suffering from #$InhalationalAnthrax would not be an instance of #$ExposureToPathogen because #$Anthrax-Bacterium cannot be transmitted by close contact with infected hosts. On the other hand, contact with a person suffering from #$SmallpoxInfection would be an instance of #$ExposureToPathogen because #$Smallpox-Virus can be transmitted effectively by close contact with an infected host.")
(comment pathogenMobExposedTo "(#$pathogenMobExposedTo EXPOSURE MOB) means that during #$ExposureToPathogen EXPOSURE, at least one instance of #$Organism-Whole, (which plays the role #$organismExposed during EXPOSURE) was exposed to a #$MicroorganismMob MOB in such a manner that effective transmission of MOB to the #$organismExposed is possible.")
(comment pathogenTypeExposedTo "(#$pathogenTypeExposedTo EXPOSURE MICRO-TYPE) means that during #$ExposureToPathogen EXPOSURE some instance of #$Organism-Whole (which plays the role #$organismExposed during EXPOSURE) was exposed to a #$MicroorganismMob in such a manner that effective transmission of that #$MicroorganismMob is possible, and every member of that #$MicroorganismMob is an instance of #$Microorganism MICRO-TYPE.")
(comment exposureToHarmfulSubstanceOccursAt "(#$exposureToHarmfulSubstanceOccursAt EXPOS LOC) means that the spatial extent of the #$ExposureToHarmfulSubstance event EXPOS is #$EnduringThing-Localized
LOC.")
(comment epidemicResultsFromExposure "(#$epidemicResultsFromExposure EPI EXPOS) means that the #$Epidemic EPI results from the #$ExposureToHarmfulSubstance event EXPOS.")
(comment epidemicExposureOccursAt "(#$epidemicExposureOccursAt EPI LOC) means that the #$Epidemic EPI is the result of an #$ExposureToHarmfulSubstance whose spatial extent is #$EnduringThing-Localized LOC. Epidemiologists investigating an #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemic try to determine if there is a single source of the pathogen responsible for the outbreak. If there is, they try to determine where and under what circumstances animals/people were exposed to the source of the pathogen. Gafs using #$epidemicExposureOccursAt represent the location of the exposure event responsible for a given #$Epidemic.")
(comment exposureToHarmfulSubstanceOccursDuring "(#$exposureToHarmfulSubstanceOccursDuring EXPOS INTERVAL) means that the #$ExposureToHarmfulSubstance event EXPOS occurs entirely within the #$TimeInterval INTERVAL.")
(comment epidemicExposurePeriod "(#$epidemicExposurePeriod EPI INTERVAL) means that the #$Epidemic EPI is the result of an #$ExposureToHarmfulSubstance, which occurs entirely within the #$TimeInterval INTERVAL. Epidemiologists investigating an #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemic try to determine if there is a single source of the pathogen responsible for the outbreak. If there is, they try to determine where, when, and under what circumstances animals/people were exposed to the source of the pathogen. Gafs using #$epidemicExposurePeriod represent the temporal location of the exposure event responsible for a given #$Epidemic.")
(comment BIOS-InfectionTypeBySurveillanceSyndrome "A collection of #$InfectionTypes. Each instance is a specialization of #$Infection whose instances are typically indicated by the presence of instances of the same #$BIOS-SurveillanceSyndromeType.")
(comment possibleActionTypeForActiveArea-CW "(#$possibleActionTypeForActiveArea-CW ACT_TYPE ACTIVE_AREA_CW) means that instantiations of ACTIVE_AREA_CW may intiate actions which are instances of ACT_TYPE, when the active area is activated.  This predicate is useful when there are several different action types that could be taken, e.g. with a computer menu [#$ComputerDisplayMenu-CW].  If such actions are always initiated upon activation of an instance of ACTIVE_AREA_CW, use the more specific predicate:  #$actionTypeForActiveArea-CW.")
(comment Anteater "A collection of mammals with elongated snouts which eat ants and termites native to Central and South America.  The scientific name for this family is Myrmecophagidae.")
(comment OrderForm "The collection of documents sent by an `ordering' agent to a supplying agent to order some item(s).")
(comment Coma "A collection of #$AilmentConditions in which an animal is in a state of deep, often prolonged unconsciousness and is incapable of sensing or responding to external stimuli and internal needs. #$Comas are usually the result of injury, disease, or poison.")
(disjointWith Coma IntentionalMentalSituation)
(comment Seizure-AilmentCondition "A collection of #$AilmentConditions in which an animal's behavior is suddenly affected by an abnormal burst of electrical activity in the brain that can result in a wide variety of clinical manifestations such as: convulsions in which there is an involuntary contraction or series of contractions of the voluntary muscles, staring, tongue biting, urination, loss of consciousness and total body shaking.")
(comment Cross-TortureDevice "A specialization of #$Cross-FixedStructure and #$TortureDevice.  Each instance of #$Cross-TortureDevice is a cross-shaped freestanding structure designed to be used (notably by the Romans) as a device of torture and execution.  A notable instance is #$CrossOfJesus.")
(comment Rash-AilmentCondition "The collection of #$SkinAilments in which there is a #$Rash-Object. A #$Rash-AilmentCondition can be a symptom of an underlying #$Infection or can be part of an allergic reaction.")
(comment DateEncodeStringFn "(#$DateEncodeStringFn TEMPLATE DATE) returns a string encoding of DATE (an instance of #$Date) in the format described by the TEMPLATE string.  For example,    (#$DateEncodeStringFn 'MM/DD/YYYY'      (#$DayFn 20 (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 1969))))  would return '07/20/1969'.  See also #$DateDecodeStringFn.")
(comment DateDecodeStringFn "(#$DateDecodeStringFn TEMPLATE DATE-STRING) returns a #$Date which is the result of decoding DATE-STRING by the format described by the TEMPLATE string.  For example,   (#$DateDecodeStringFn 'MM/DD/YYYY' '07/20/1969') would return (#$DayFn 20 (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 1969))).   See also #$DateEncodeStringFn.")
(comment TextualMaterialTypeByFunction "An instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType and hence a collection of collections.  Each instance of #$TextualMaterialTypeByFunction is a specialization of #$TextualMaterial whose instances perform a particular and distinct type of function -- for instance #$LeaseDocument (instances of which are used to establish legally binding terms of lease) and #$ExpenseReport (used by employees to request reimbursement for expense).")
(comment JuneSolsticeDay "A specialization of #$CalendarDay and an instance of #$AnnualTemporalObjectType.  Each instance of #$JuneSolsticeDay is a calendar day (usually about June 21) which is either a summer solstice day (see #$SummerSolsticeDay) in the northern hemisphere or a winter solstice day (see #$WinterSolsticeDay) in the southern hemisphere.")
(comment DecemberSolsticeDay "A specialization of #$CalendarDay and an instance of #$AnnualTemporalObjectType.  Each instance of #$DecemberSolsticeDay is a calendar day (usually December 21) which is either a summer solstice day (see #$SummerSolsticeDay) in the southern hemisphere or a winter solstice day (see #$WinterSolsticeDay) in the northern hemisphere.")
(comment SolsticeDay "A specialization of #$CalendarDay and an instance of #$AnnualTemporalObjectType.  Each instance of #$SolsticeDay is a calendar day during which the sun is at its greatest distance from the celestial equator.  One solstice occurs annually about June 21 (see #$JuneSolsticeDay) when the sun is at the zenith at the tropic of Cancer -- in the northern hemisphere such a solstice would be an instance of #$SummerSolsticeDay, and in the southern hemisphere it would be an instance of #$WinterSolsticeDay. The other solstice occurs annually about  December 21 (see #$DecemberSolsticeDay) when the sun is at the zenith at the tropic of Capricorn -- in the northern hemisphere such a solstice would be an instance of #$WinterSolsticeDay, and in the southern hemisphere it would be an instance of #$SummerSolsticeDay.")
(comment EquinoxDay "A specialization of #$CalendarDay and an instance of #$AnnualTemporalObjectType.  Each instance of #$EquinoxDay is a calendar day during which  the sun crosses the celestial equator and the length of day and night are approximately equal everywhere on the globe.  One solstice occurs annually about March 21 (see #$VernalEquinoxDay) and another occurs annually about September 21 (see #$AutumnalEquinoxDay).")
(comment SententialRelation "A collection of mathematical objects.  Each instance of #$SententialRelation is by definition either a #$LogicalConnective or a #$Quantifier.")
(comment ComputerDisplayTypeInField-Transmitting "The collection of all instances of #$ComputerDisplayTypeInField whose typed-in contents are transmitted to the running computer program [#$ComputerProcessRunning] due to the content of what is typed into them.  The transmission is often initiated by the typing of an ``ENTER'' key or by the complete filling of the input buffer.")
(comment ComputerDisplayContinueLink "The collection of all #$ComputerDisplayLinks displayed on computer screens whose action is to allow a paused process to continue.  The process is normally paused in order for the user to understand a communication from the computer sytem.")
(comment ComputerDisplaySubmitButton "The collection of all #$ComputerDisplayButtons displayed on computer screens whose action is to transmit a block of information specified elsewhere -- often on the page -- to the #$ComputerProcessRunning which is active for that portion of the screen.")
(comment computerDisplayRegionText "(#$computerDisplayRegionText COMPUTER_DISPLAY_REGION TEXT) means that the text string TEXT is displayed on the #$ComputerDisplayScreenRegion, COMPUTER_DISPLAY_REGION.")
(comment initialTypeInFieldText "(#$initialTypeInFieldText TYPE_IN_REGION TEXT) means that the text string TEXT is initially displayed in the #$ComputerDisplayTypeInField, TYPE_IN_REGION before there is any typing in the field.")
(comment ResortFacility "The collection of all #$LeisureFacilitys located in instances of #$Resort-Attraction.")
(comment MedicalFacility "The collection of all #$physicalQuarters of #$MedicalCareOrganizations.")
(comment AlertingUserToOperationalFault "The collection of all instances of #$ProvidingSystemStatusInformationToUser in which the information provided is about an operational fault of the system and in which an out-of-the-ordinary output is generated to draw the attention of the user to the information.  Flashing screen regions, other flashing lights, sound output, and tactile response on input devices in continuous use are all such methods.  Instances of #$ProvidingContinuousSystemStatusInformationToUser in which the status indicator changes to mark a fault, but in no way calls attention to itself do not fall in this collection.  Changing the color of a screen region or output light may or may not fall within this collection depending upon the ergonomics of the situation.  Informing the user of an operational fault upon request for current status would not be an instance of this collection.")
(comment SubLFunctionSpec "The collection of #$SubLAtoms that are suitable for FUNCALL or APPLY.")
(comment SubLFunctionSymbol "A joint spec of #$SubLSymbol and #$SubLFunctionSpec whose instances are symbols with global function definitions.")
(comment CycTaskSchedulerConstant "The collection of #$CycLConstants that are required in order to schedule tasks to be performed by the #$CycTaskScheduler, through  the #$CycKB. Even when these constants are missing in the KB used by  a given #$Cyc image, programmers can still schedule tasks in their code using certain public SubL functions provided by the #$CycTaskScheduler interface.")
(comment CycTaskSchedulerTask "The collection of tasks that are specified to be scheduled by the #$CycTaskScheduler. Every such task needs to have a #$SExpression specified through the predicate #$taskSchedulerTaskExpression.
Each task also needs to have at least one of the following specified: 
i) a regular time interval through the predicate #$taskSchedulerTaskRegularTimeInterval,
ii) a time pattern through the predicate #$taskSchedulerTaskTimePattern, or
iii) a date pattern through the predicate #$taskSchedulerTaskDatePattern.
There can only be one expression, regular time interval, time pattern and date pattern specified for a given task (i.e. the predicates mentioned above are all constrained to be #$SingleEntry w.r.t. their #$arg2Format). The users of the #$CycTaskSchedulerTask may register themselves as users of instances of #$CycTaskSchedulerTask through the predicate #$taskSchedulerTaskUser.  There are four more predicates used by the #$CycTaskSchedulerTask in order to inform the user of the time/date a given task was last performed and the time/date the task is scheduled to be performed next; see #$taskSchedulerTaskLastPerformanceTime, #$taskSchedulerTaskLastPerformanceDate, #$taskSchedulerTaskNextPerformanceTime, and #$taskSchedulerTaskNextPerformanceDate. Please see #$GreeterTask for an example.")
(comment TaskSchedulerRegularTimeIntervalExpression "The spec of #$Time-Quantity used by the users of the #$CycTaskScheduler    to specify regular time intervals for scheduled tasks. Currently, regular    time intervals for tasks may be specified in seconds (see #$SecondsDuration),    minutes (see #$MinutesDuration), hours (see #$HoursDuration) or days (see #$DaysDuration).    For example, (#$MinutesDuration 20) would ensure that the given task will be performed   every 20 minutes.")
(comment TaskSchedulerTemplateExpression "An instance is a #$List-Extensional where the arguments to the #$TheList operator form a property list where each property is a keyword and has an associated value that follows it. These lists are used by the users of the #$CycTaskScheduler    to specify time or date patterns for scheduled tasks.")
(comment TaskSchedulerDatePatternExpression "The collection of templates used by the users of the #$CycTaskScheduler    to specify date patterns for scheduled tasks. Each such template is a #$List-Extensional where the arguments to the #$TheList operator form a property list where each property is a keyword and has an associated value that follows it.
Currently, date expression templates    may contain the following properties: :DAY, :MONTH, :YEAR, and :DAY-OF-WEEK.    A value for :DAY may be an integer between 1 and 31 (inclusively), or    an EL list of such integers, or an interval of such integers, where an interval   is specified as a binary NAT expression where the operator is #$TaskSchedulerPatternPropertyIntervalFn and the two arguments are integers specifying the lower   and upper boundaries of the interval. A value for :MONTH may be an integer    between 1 and 12 (inclusively) or an instance of #$MonthOfYearType or a list or   interval of such values. A value for :YEAR may be a positive integer    (corresponding to the current year or a forthcoming one) or a list or interval of   such values. A value for :DAY-OF-WEEK may be an integer between 0 and 6    (inclusively) or an instance of #$DayOfWeekType, or a list or interval of such values; it may also be one of the keywords :WEEKDAY and :NON-WEEKDAY.")
(comment TaskSchedulerTimePatternExpression "The collection of templates used by the users of the #$CycTaskScheduler    to specify time patterns for scheduled tasks. Each such template is a #$List-Extensional where the arguments to the #$TheList operator form a property list where each property is a keyword and has an associated value that follows it.
Currently, time expression templates    may contain the following properties: :SECOND, :MINUTE, and :HOUR. A value for   :SECOND may be an integer between 0 and 59 (inclusively). (No lists or intervals   are currently allowed for :SECOND.) A value for :MINUTE may be an integer between    0 and 59 (inclusively) or an EL list of such integers, or an interval of such integers,    where an interval is specified as a binary NAT expression where the operator is #$TaskSchedulerPatternPropertyIntervalFn and the two arguments are integers specifying the lower   and upper boundaries of the interval. A value for :HOUR may be an integer    between 0 and 23 (inclusively) or a list or interval of such values.")
(comment MilitaryBand "The collection of all instances of #$Band-MusicGroup that are #$subOrganizations of some instance of #$MilitaryOrganization.  Typically, such bands are charged with entertaining troops and helping with 

 recruiting.")
(comment KSID "The collection of every #$SubLListOrAtom used to encode structured knowledge source concepts that Cyc is intended to imbibe, e.g., #$SubLAtoms used to physically encode logical fields in a database.")
(comment FileReplacementFault "Some #$OperatingSystems have built-in protection for certain key files, which works by detecting corrupted or lost files and replacing them with uncorrupted copies.  A #$FileReplacementFault occurs when a protected file is deleted and replaced by another file which has been renamed, but the #$OperatingSystem fails to detect the switch and replace the file.")
(comment ProvidingRequestedInformationToComputerProcess "The collection of all communication acts [#$CommunicationAct-Single] in which a computer user provides input to a running computer process [#$ComputerProcessRunning] which the process asked the user to provide.")
(comment aISSpecified "(#$aISSpecified SPECIFYING AIS) means that in SPECIFYING [an instance of #$SpecifyingAnAIS], AIS [an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure] was the abstract structure [#$AbstractInformationStructure] specified as being associated with a #$ConceptualWork.  See #$aISSpecifyingSourceCW.")
(comment developerOfBeliefSystems "A binary predicate that relates an instance of #$BeliefSystem to an instance of #$IntelligentAgent.  (#$developerOfBeliefSystems SYSTEM AGENT) means that AGENT

contributes intellectually to the elaboration of SYSTEM. AGENT needs to play an active and significant role in the elaboration of the content of SYSTEM.")
(comment AskingAYesOrNoQuestion "The collection of events in which some agent asks a yes/no question.")
(comment PneumonicPlague "A collection of (#$InfectionFn #$RespiratorySystem) caused by #$Plague-Bacterium. It can occur naturally as either a primary infection or as a secondary infection to #$BubonicPlague. It could also occur as the result of a #$BiologicalAttack in which there is an intentional release of an aerosol of #$Plague-Bacterium. #$PneumonicPlague would initially present with fever, cough, difficulty breathing, and sometimes watery or bloody sputum. It progresses similar to severe pneumonia. If not treated with antiobiotics it is usually fatal. Unlike, #$BubonicPlague, cases of #$PneumonicPlague do not usually have swollen lymph nodes.")
(disjointWith ComputerDataLibrary-CW ComputerSoftwareLibrary-CW)
(comment InformationRequest-IBT "The collection of all #$InformationBearingThings whose information content is a request for information. An #$InformationRequest-IBT is the #$communicationToken in any act of #$RequestingInformation.")
(comment YesOrNoQuestion-IBT "The collection of all instances of #$InformationRequest-IBT which are yes/no questions.  Note that this collection

 is restricted to genuine yes/no questions -- i.e., questions for which \"yes\" or \"no\" are mutually exclusive answers, exactly one of which will answer the question correctly.  Contrast this with #$ComplexQuestion-IBT, which merely takes the grammatical form of a yes/no question.")
(comment ArchitectureIndependentComputerPatchProgram "A specialization of #$ArchitectureIndependentSoftwareObject-Individual and #$ComputerPatchProgram. Each instance of #$ArchitectureIndependentComputerPatchProgram is a computer patch program that is not intended to fix a program installed on a machine with a particular architecture. Typically, instances of this collection will be #$genlPrograms of some architecture-specific software object.  For example, #$Q246009-TheSoftwarePatch is an architecture independent software patch program that is a #$genlPrograms of #$Q246009a-TheSoftwarePatch (which is an Alpha-specific patch).")
(comment ArchitectureIndependentSoftwareObject-Individual "A specialization of #$SoftwareObject-Individual. Each instance of #$ArchitectureIndependentSoftwareObject-Individual is a software object that is a #$genlPrograms of some architecture-specific software object.")
(disjointWith YesOrNoQuestion-IBT ComplexQuestion-IBT)
(comment ComplexQuestion-IBT "The collection of all instances of #$InformationRequest-IBT which take the grammatical form of yes/no questions, but which are actually two or more yes/no questions lumped together.  Such questions are usually posed in error, or in an attempt to trick the listener into providing conflicting or damning information.  An example, \"Have you stopped beating your wife?\"")
(comment QuestionWithYesOrNoSyntacticForm-IBT "The collection of all instances of #$InformationRequest-IBT which take the form of a yes/no question.  This collection includes genuine yes/no questions -- i.e., questions for which \"yes\" or \"no\" are mutually exclusive answers, as well as so-called \"complex\" questions, which disguise two or more distinct yes/no questions in one form.")
(comment Affirming-Replying "The collection of all events which consist of some agent answering a yes/no question with a \"yes\" (or something equivalent).")
(comment Denying-Replying "The collection of all events which consist of some agent answering a yes/no question with a \"no\" (or something equivalent).")
(comment Denial-IBT "The collection of all information bearing things that are the #$communicationTokens in a #$Denying-Replying.")
(comment Affirmation-IBT "The collection of all information bearing things that are the #$communicationTokens in a #$Affirming-Replying.")
(comment ArchitectureDependentSoftwareObject-Individual "A specialization of #$SoftwareObject-Individual. Each instance of #$ArchitectureDependentSoftwareObject-Individual is a software object that has been desinged to run on a specific hardware architecture. #$ArchitectureDependentSoftwareObject-Individual and ArchitectureIndependentSoftwareObject-Individual partition #$SoftwareObject-Individual.")
(disjointWith ArchitectureIndependentSoftwareObject-Individual ArchitectureDependentSoftwareObject-Individual)
(comment ArchitectureDependentComputerPatchProgram "A specialization of #$ArchitectureDependentSoftwareObject-Individual and #$ComputerPatchProgram. Each instance of #$ArchitectureDependentComputerPatchProgram is a computer patch program that has been designed to patch a program on a certain hardware architecture.  For example, #$Q246009a-TheSoftwarePatch is an instance of #$ArchitectureDependentComputerPatchProgram because it is an Alpha-specific patch.")
(comment PhysicalFieldIndexical "The collection of all indexicals that are used to denote the values associated with the physical fields of instances of #$PhysicalSchema.  Every #$PhysicalFieldIndexical is associated with a unique #$PhysicalSchema via #$physicalFieldIndexicals and is related with a unique #$PhysicalField via #$indexicalForPhysicalField. In the latter case, the relation is a one to one correspondence between #$PhysicalFields and #$PhysicalFieldIndexicals")
(comment UsingProgramToFn "(#$UsingProgramToFn PROGRAM ACTIVITY-TYPE) denotes the collection of instances of ACTIVITY-TYPE that are performed by using the ComputerProgram-CW PROGRAM.  For example, (#$UsingProgramToFn #$MSWord 

(#$WritingFn #$Book-CW)) denotes the collection of book-writing events where #$MSWord is the program used.")
(comment indexicalForPhysicalField "(#$indexicalForPhysicalField FIELD INDEXICAL) states that INDEXICAL is the associated indexical term used to denote values for physical field FIELD in a given row the physical schema.")
(comment EMailAddressDisclosureFault "A program has this fault if it discloses #$EMailAddresses that it shouldn't.")
(comment FlatBedSheet "The sort of sheet which lies flat and must be tucked in underneath the corners of a mattress.")
(comment HoldingInPouch "When a marsupial holds something in its pouch.")
(comment RollOnDispenser "Container for a liquid which has a rolling ball for dispensing the liquid.")
(disjointWith CommercialBoating SportBoating)
(comment CommercialBoating "Boating done for subsistence or financial gain.")
(comment HoldingOnBack "An animal holding something, by supporting the thing's weight on its back.")
(comment BookRack "A type of furniture used for displaying books or magazines, different from a bookshelf in that the books/magazines are placed with their covers facing outwards (not with only their spines exposed.) ")
(comment MedicalReferral "The collection of events in which one medical professional (or group) refers a client to another medical professional (or group).")
(comment DetailPerspective "The collection of instances of #$VisualImage that focus on a piece/portion of an object; these are usually also instances of #$CloseUpPerspective.")
(comment FoodStorageJar "The collection of jars in which foods are stored/packaged")
(comment Thong-TheShoe "Thongs are soles with two bands that wrap around the foot, passing between the big toe and the toe next to it.  KathyB wants this collection to be named FlipFlop.")
(comment Flare-PyrotechnicDevice "A specialization of #$PyrotechnicDevice.  Each instance of #$Flare-PyrotechnicDevice is a device that produces a bright light for signaling, illumination, or identification.")
(disjointWith FloorManager Minister-Governmental)
(comment BenefitsAdministrator "A person who works for a company and deals with the company's employee benefits.")
(disjointWith TechnicalManager Minister-Governmental)
(comment Budget "special kinds of  Plans which deal with financial expenses.")
(comment notAssertibleMt "(#$notAssertibleMt MT) states that the #$Microtheory MT cannot be used as the microtheory of an assertion made to the KB; i.e., MT might be a microtheory used as scaffolding or one whose assertions are known to be complete.  See also #$notAssertibleCollection, and #$notAssertible.")
(comment CompleteDiseaseSituationTypeFn "(#$CompleteDiseaseSituationTypetFn DIS-TYPE) denotes the type of #$Situation for the #$DiseaseType DIS-TYPE. This function is needed because #$DiseaseTypes are instances of #$TemporalStuffType whereas the course of a #$DiseaseType from its onset to death or recovery should be an instance of #$TemporalObjectType. That is, any time slice of an instance of DIS-TYPE will still be an instance of DIS-TYPE, whereas any time slice of an instance of the course of the disease DIS-TYPE from its onset to its end will not be an instance of the complete course of the disease. Since we anticipate developing scripts for the courses of various #$DiseaseTypes, this function will prove useful. The result of this function represents the course of a #$DiseaseType if left untreated and to which the person undergoing it has no immunity. Other functions might be needed if we want to represent the course of a #$DiseaseType when either or both of these conditions are not met.")
(comment InhalationalAnthrax-Natural "The collection of #$InhalationalAnthrax infections that are acquired by inhalational of anthrax spores that occur naturally. Common sources of anthrax spores are soil and animal hides. This type of #$Infection is also called Woolsorters' disease.")
(comment ListTypeByMemberType "A collection of collections. Each element of #$ListTypeByMemberType is a collection of lists of objects of a specified type. For example, (#$ListOfTypeFn #$City), the collection of lists of cities, is a member of #$ListTypeByMemberType. Another instance would be (#$ListOfTypeFn #$Integer), the collection of lists of integers.")
(comment DryerSheet "A specialization of LaundrySuppliesMarketingCategory.  Each instance of #$DryerSheet is a small sheet inserted into a clothes dryer with clothes in order to make drying clothes softer, less charged with static electricity, or less prone to fading in color.")
(comment OrganizedCrimeProfessional "The collection of people who work for organized crime.")
(comment Banker "The collection of bankers.")
(comment MaferikHuntingRifle "The collection of Maferik Hunting rifles.")
(comment M14HuntingRifle "The collection of M14 rifles.")
(comment crimeMotive "(#$crimeMotive CRIME MOTIVE) means that the person who directed CRIME (#$directingAgent) to be committed has motives associated with MOTIVE.  For instance, '(#$crimeMotive MURDER01 #$RevengeAction)' means that the directing agent of MURDER01 acted out of the motive that MURDER01 be an act of revenge.  Note that a hired hitperson often has a different motive in committing a crime than might the contractor.")
(comment CarryingOutAMurder "The collection of events characterized by having some instance of (#$PlanningFn #$Murder) as a #$subEvents, culminating in an actual #$Murder.  Additional 'scenes' may include supporting steps that are necessary conditions in context for the murder.")
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder RecreationalActivity)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder EntertainmentEvent)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder CreatingAnArtifact)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder Communicating)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder BiologicalEvent)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder BodyMovementEvent)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder PreparingFoodOrDrink)
(disjointWith CarryingOutAMurder SimpleRepairing)
(comment PremeditatedMurder "The collection of murdering events involving planning on the part of the person who carries out the murder.")
(comment SecondDegreeMurder "This is the collection of murders carried out intentionally, but with little or no premeditation.")
(disjointWith PremeditatedMurder SecondDegreeMurder)
(comment PublishingIndustry "The collection of local industries concerned with publishing.")
(comment AlcoholAndNarcoticsIndustry "The collection of local industries (see #$Industry-Localized devoted to the production and distribution of intoxicating substances, typified by alcohol and narcotics.")
(comment OccupationTypeFn "(#$OccupationTypeFn OCCTYPE) denotes the collection of jobs in which persons perform the occupation OCCTYPE.  For example, (#$OccupationTypeFn #$Electrician) denotes the class of electrician jobs.  See #$jobsOfType.")
(comment jobsOfType "(#$jobsOfType OCCTYPE POSITIONS) means that POSITIONS is the collection of #$JobPositions whose holders are instances of OCCTYPE as a result of holding that instance of POSITIONS.")
(comment jobHolder "(#$jobHolder JOB AGENT) means that AGENT holds the particular job, JOB.  This distinguishes different instances of #$JobPosition, hence two different electricians working at the same time hold two different instances of the subtype of  #$JobPosition, (#$OccupationTypeFn #$Electrician).  Note that we want to be able to specify, using binary predicates where a person holds a job, whether the job is filled or open, the number of jobs of a given type that are open at a company, etc.  Hence, it becomes easier to use #$JobPosition.")
(comment jobOrganization "(#$jobOrganization JOB ORG) means that if a person, PERSON, holds JOB, i.e.,  (#$jobHolder JOB PERSON), then PERSON is employed by ORG.")
(comment jobType "(#$jobType JOB OCCTYPE) relates a particular job to the #$PersonTypeByOccupation that a person instantiates in virtue of holding a job of this sort.")
(comment EditionFn "(#$EditionFn PERIODICAL DATE) denotes the issue of PERIODICAL that is released for that DATE.  For instance, (#$EditionFn #$TheNewYorkTimes-TheNewspaper (#$DayFn 22 (#$MonthFn #$June (#$YearFn 1998)))) denotes the June 22, 1998 issue of the New York Times.   Note that (#$EditionFn #$HarpersMagazine-TheMagazine (#$MonthFn #$June (#$YearFn 2000))) is the June 2000 issue of Harper's and was likely published in April.")
(comment PoliceReport "The collections of reports made by law enforcement officials.")
(comment phoneNumberPhone "(#$phoneNumberPhone PHONE NUMBER) means that NUMBER is the phone number associated with the telephone PHONE; that is, if one dials NUMBER on a different telephone, it will be connected to PHONE.")
(comment callerNumber "(#$callerNumber CALL NUM) means that NUM is the phone number of the telephone from which the outgoing call was made in CALL.")
(comment receiverNumber "(#$receiverNumber CALL NUM) means that NUM is the phone number of the telephone which receives the incoming call, CALL.")
(comment crimeScene "(#$crimeScene CRIME PLACE) relates a crime to the place where it occurred, i.e., the physical location that investigators would deem to be the crime scene, more specific than just the city in which the crime occurs.")
(comment eventOccursAtLocationType "(#$eventOccursAtLocationType EVENT PLACETYPE) means that EVENT occurs at some instance of PLACETYPE.")
(comment crimeVictim "(#$crimeVictim ACTION AGENT) means that AGENT is in fact a #$victim in the criminal act ACTION and also that ACTION is a #$PurposefulAction whose performer intends that (#$victim ACTION AGENT)--i.e., the relation entails (#$intendedVictim ACTION AGENT).")
(comment occurrentSubeventType "(#$occurrentSubeventType EVENT EVENTTYPE) means that some instance of EVENTTYPE occurs as a subevent of EVENT.")
(comment groupMemberMaleficiary "(#$groupMemberMaleficiary EVENT GROUP) means that one or more members of the group GROUP is a maleficiary (see #$maleficiary) in EVENT.  For example, in a battle we might say that  (#$groupMemberMaleficiary WAR001 Battalion009).")
(comment LethalWeapon "This is the subcollection of #$Weapons capable of killing a person when used as intended.")
(comment disposedToPlayRole "'(#$disposedToPlayRole AGENT SIT-TYPE ROLE)' means that AGENT has a disposition to play the role, ROLE, in situations of type SIT-TYPE.")
(comment objectTypeOfPossessionTransfer "'(#$objectTypeOfPossessionTransfer TRANS TYPE)' means that the type of object transferred in TRANS is TYPE.  This predicate is a type-level version of #$objectOfPossessionTransfer in its second argument.")
(comment BankTransaction "The collection of money transfers to and from bank accounts.")
(comment transactionAccount "'(#$transactionAccount MT ACC)' means that ACC is a/the account used
in a money transfer, MT.  When money is transferred from one account to the
other, this would presumably be distinguished into a withdrawal and deposit
and the transaction account for each distinct subevent would be separately specified by
this predicate.")
(comment PersonnelDirector "The person in charge of personnel in a company.  Often works for a HumanResourcesDepartment.")
(comment MidLevelManager "Someone who is in charge of some part of a company, but is answerable to some other manager in the company.")
(disjointWith GeneralManager-SportsTeam Minister-Governmental)
(comment ProjectManager "Someone who is in charge of a specific project at a company.")
(comment TelevisionSetTypeByProjectionStyle "A collection of collections. Each instance is a spec of #$TelevisionSet whose instances are all of the same projection style.")
(comment MechanicalAutoPart "A specialization of #$AutoPart and #$PhysicalDevice.  Each instance of #$MechanicalAutoPart is an object with moving parts that is designed to be a physical part of a car.")
(comment substituteArgIn "A predicate that relates an integer (representing an argument-position) and a CycL term to a CycL formula in which the term appears (in that position) and a second CycL formula in which has identical arguments to the first in every argument position save the one represented by the integer; #$substituteArgIn is a #$QuaternaryPredicate that relates an instance of #$NonNegativeInteger to an instance of #$CycLTerm and two instances of #$CycLFormula.  
<p>
<code>(#$substituteArgIn N TERM FORMULA-OLD FORMULA-NEW)</code> means that TERM occurs as argument N within FORMULA-NEW and that otherwise FORMULA-OLD and FORMULA-NEW have identical arguments.")
(comment expansionExceptWhen "A #$BinaryPredicate that relates one instance of #$CycLFormula to another.  (#$expansionExceptWhen EXCEPTION RMP-GAF) means that an exception to the #$expansion rule asserted for the #$RuleMacroPredicate in the arg0 slot of RMP-GAF is EXCEPTION.  Any free variables that occur in EXCEPTION should be understood as occuring in the same quantificational context as the #$expansion of the predicate that occurs as the 0th argument to RMP-GAF. Thus,   (#$expansionExceptWhen (#$and (#$genls ?TERM #$FlightlessBird) (#$equals ?SENT  (#$typeBehaviorCapable #$Bird #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy)))(#$transitiveViaArgInverse #$typeBehaviorCapable #$genls 1) means that (#$genls ?TERM #$FlightlessBird) (#$equals ?SENT  (#$typeBehaviorCapable #$Bird #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy) is an exception to the #$expansion for #$transitiveViaArgInverse, which is:  (#$expansion #$transitiveViaArgInverse (#$implies (#$and  (#$knownSentence ?SENT) (#$argN :ARG1 0 ?SENT) (:ARG2 ?TERM ?ARG)  (#$argN ?ARG :ARG3 ?SENT)(#$substituteArgIn :ARG3 ?TERM ?SENT  ?NEWSENT)) (#$trueSentence ?NEWSENT))).")
(comment transitiveViaArgInverseExceptions "A #$QuintaryPredicate used to state exceptions to the applicabilty of #$transitiveViaArgInverse assertions. (#$transitiveViaArgInverseExceptions THING FORMULA PRED BINPRED N) means that the Nth argument position of PRED is \"transitive\" with respect to the inverse of BINPRED except when THING (or a constant that stands in the inverse of BINPRED to THING) occurs as the Nth argument to PRED in a GAF whose arguments are otherwise identical to those of FORMULA.  For example, (#$transitiveViaArgInverseExceptions #$FlightlessBird (#$typeBehaviorCapable #$Bird #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy) #$typeBehaviorCapable #$genls 1) means that (#$typeBehaviorCapable COLL #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy) is true for all specializations of COLL except for those specializations of COLL that are also specializations of #$FlightlessBird.  See also #$transitiveViaArgExceptions.")
(comment BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory "A collection of #$BIOS-AlertReportMicrotheorys. Each instance is an initial alert issued by the Essence II Biosurveillance System. An initial alert is the initial member of a series of alerts, each successive alert updating the temporally prior one. Upon receiving an alert, the hypothesis manager determines whether it is an initial alert. If it is, the hypothesis manager creates (1) an instance of #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory, (2) an instance of #$PhysicalEvent as the actual object of the alert (see #$bios-AlertReportObject), (3) an instance of #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemic as the hypothesized epidemic of the initial alert (see #$bios-AlertReportHypothesizedEpidemic), and (4) several hypotheses (see #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt) about the type of infection, and place and date of exposure. Both the hypothesized epidemic and the hypotheses will be linked to the initial alert (see #$bios-MicrotheoryInitializedBy). Thus the initial alert will enable the hypothesis manager to know which alerts and hypotheses pertain to the same hypothesized epidemic. Our initial assumption is that if an alert is issued more than 31 days after the most recent alert, then it is an initial alert and triggers a new epidemiological investigation.")
(comment BIOS-InitializableMicrotheory "A collection of #$BIOS-Microtheorys. Each instance is capable of standing in the #$bios-MicrotheoryInitializedBy relation to one and only one instance of #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory. All and only those #$BIOS-InitializableMicrotheorys that are created as part of the same epidemiological investigation of an initial alert are initialized by that alert. Some of the important specializations of this collection include #$BIOS-AlertReportMicrotheory, #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt, #$BIOS-HypothesisGenerationVocabularyMt, and #$BIOS-HypothesisGenerationMt. Some negative examples are #$BIOS-BackgroundVocabularyMt and #$BIOS-BackgroundMt.")
(comment bios-MicrotheoryInitializedBy "(#$bios-MicrotheoryInitializedBy MT INIT) means that the #$BIOS-InitializableMicrotheory MT is initialized by the #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory INIT. The initialization relationship provides a convenient way to track which #$BIOS-InitializableMicrotheorys are part of the same epidemiological investigation. A new investigation begins when the hypothesis manager determines that an alert is separated enough temporally from earlier alerts to warrant a new investigation. It then creates a #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory. Whenever the hypothesis manager creates a new microtheory as part of this investigation it asserts that #$bios-MicrotheoryInitializedBy holds between the new microtheoory and the #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory that started the investigation. See #$BIOS-InitializableMicrotheory for further information about the kinds of microtheories created by the hypothesis manager.")
(comment bios-AlertReportInitializedBy "(#$bios-AlertReportInitializedBy ALERT INIT) means that the #$BIOS-AlertReportMicrotheory ALERT is initialized by the #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory INIT.")
(comment bios-AlertReportObject "(#$bios-AlertReportObject ALERT EVENT) means that the #$PhysicalEvent EVENT is the object of the #$BIOS-AlertReportMicrotheory ALERT. The object of the alert is the #$PhysicalEvent that is the underlying distal cause of the indicators that trigger the alert. The alerting algorithm of the Essence II biosurveillance system is designed to detect instances of #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemics. However, there will be situations where the alert is a false alarm (false positive). In these cases, there will still be some event or group of events that trigger the alert (for example, school absenteeism due to severe weather). When an instance of #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory is created by the hypothesis manager, an instance of #$PhysicalEvent will be created that is the object of that alert. All subsequent alerts that update the initial alert will have the same object. (See #$bios-AlertReportHypothesizedEpidemic.) ")
(comment bios-AlertReportHypothesizedEpidemic "(#$bios-AlertReportHypothesizedEpidemic ALERT EPI) means that the #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemic EPI is hypothesized as the object of the #$BIOS-AlertReportMicrotheory ALERT. EPI is hypothesized as the underlying distal cause of the indicators that trigger the alert. If the alert turns out to be real (a true positive) then the hypothesized epidemic will also be the actual object of the alert (see #$bios-AlertReportObject).  If the alert turns out to be a false alarm (false positive), then the hypothesized epidemic exists only in the context of the alert. There will still be an existing object of the alert, but it won't be an instance of #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemic. When an instance of #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory is created by the hypothesis manager, an instance of #$InfectiousDiseaseEpidemic will be created that will be the hypothesized epidemic of the alert. All subsequent alerts that update the initial alert will have the same epidemic as their hypothesized object.")
(comment BIOS-HypothesisGenerationMt "A collection of #$HypotheticalContexts. Each instance is a #$BIOS-Microtheory that is uniquely linked to an initial alert generated by the Essence II Biosurveillance System (see #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory). Each instance is the #$HypotheticalContext that holds the assertions that will be distributed by the hypothesis manager to form the content of the hypotheses (see #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt) generated in response to an initial alert. Thus an instance may contain a pair of mutually incompatible assertions in situations where two incompatible hypotheses will be constructed-- each hypothesis having one of the two assertions as its content.")
(comment BIOS-HypothesisGenerationVocabularyMt "A collection of #$VocabularyMicrotheorys. Each instance is uniquely linked to an initial alert generated by the Essence II Biosurveillance System (see #$BIOS-InitialAlertReportMicrotheory) and is the #$VocabularyMicrotheory for that instance of #$BIOS-HypothesisGenerationMt initialized by the same initial alert.. The hypothetical objects of an initial alert (see #$bios-AlertReportObject and #$bios-AlertReportHypothesizedEpidemic) are defined in an instance of #$BIOS-HypothesisGenerationVocabularyMt because they may have no objective reality outside the context of the various hypotheses about the alert (see #$BIOS-EpidemicHypothesisMt).")
(comment CWCreation "A specialization of #$DevisingAPracticeOrWork. Each instance of this collection is an event in which at least one instance of #$ConceptualWork (related to the instance of #$CreationEvent by the predicate #$outputsCreated) is brought into existence. For an analogous collection regarding instances of #$InformationBearingThing, see #$IBTGeneration.")
(disjointWith WritingAComputerProgram AthleticActivity)
(disjointWith WritingAComputerProgram SportsAction)
(comment CanAccessRegistry "A computer has this capability if it is running a program (e.g. #$MSRegistryEditor) that allows users to view and edit #$RegistryKeys on the system. If said \"users\" include unauthorized persons, see the more specialized #$VulnerableToUnauthorizedRegistryAccess.")
(comment AlgorithmStepFn "An individual denoting function. 
(#$AlgorithmStepFn ALGORITHM ID) denotes the #$ProgramStep of ALGORITHM that
is identified by ID. For example, (#$AlgorithmStepFn #$AbsoluteValueComputation 2)
would denote Step 2 of the algorithm #$AbsoluteValueComputation.")
(comment NumberObject "The number data type. 

In the context of #$ProgrammingDomainVocabularyMt, it is entirely undetermined 

how this data type is implemented.")
(comment GatewayPC "A specialization of #$PersonalComputer. Each instance of this collection is a (popular) desktop computer manufactured by #$GatewayInc. Many are built around a #$PentiumProcessor.")
(comment AssignmentInProgramFn "(#$AssignmentInProgramFn OBJECT1 OBJECT2) 

represents a program step in which the variable OBJECT1 is assigned the value

of OBJECT2.")
(comment algorithmStartStep "(#$algorithmStartStep ALGORITHM PROGRAM-STEP) means that
PROGRAM-STEP is the first step of ALGORITHM.")
(comment kePreferredPrecisionSuggestionFrom "(#$keIrrelevantPrecisionSuggestionFrom ?COL1 ?COL2) states that the constant ?COL1 should be the default \"best\" precision suggestion from the constant ?COL2. (See #$CycPrecisionSuggestor.).")
(comment ProgramAssignmentFn "A binary denotational function. (#$ProgramAssignmentFn PARAM EXPRESSION) denotes a program step in which 
the value of EXPRESSION is assigned to the #$SoftwareParameter PARAM.")
(comment WearingSomethingOnFn "A unary function that returns a specialization of #$WearingSomething when given as an argument a collection that is both an instance of #$AnimalBodyPartType and a specialization of #$ExternalAnatomicalPart.  (#$WearingSomethingOnFn PART-TYPE) denotes the collection of continuous physical contact situations in which an item of clothing is worn on (see #$wornOn) an instance of PART-TYPE. For example,  (#$WearingSomethingOnFn #$Foot-AnimalBodyPart) denotes the collection of events in which clothing is worn on feet.")
(comment wornOn-TypeType "A binary predicate that relates specializations of #$SomethingToWear to instances of #$ExternalAnimalBodyRegionType.  <code>(#$wornOn-TypeType ITEM-TYPE BODY-PART-TYPE)</code>  means that by default instances of <code>ITEM-TYPE</code> are worn on instances of <code>BODY-PART-TYPE</code>.")
(comment GeneratePhraseFn "(#$GeneratePhraseFn FORT) returns the string that is the best describing for this FORT")
(comment programAlgorithmOutputs "(#$programAlgorithmOutputs ALGORITHM . OUTPUTS) relates ALGORITHM
to the list OUTPUTS of the relevant instances of 
#$IndexicalComputationalTerm that are outputs to the algorithmic
process.")
(comment programAlgorithmInputs "(#$programAlgorithmInputs ALGORITHM . INPUTS) relates ALGORITHM
to the list INPUTS of the relevant instances of 
#$IndexicalComputationalTerm that are inputs to the algorithmic
process.")
(comment ComputerHardwareDeviceType "A specialization of #$ExistingObjectType. Each instance of this (second-order) collection is a subcollection of #$ComputerHardwareItem all of whose instances are a particular, distinct type of (entire) computer hardware item. It includes such collections as #$ComputerKeyboard, #$CableModem and #$FloppyDisk. It does not contain collections of computers or computer hardware items sorted by brand (e.g. #$MacintoshComputer). For that, see #$ComputerTypeByBrand.")
(disjointWith ComputerActivityType CourseOfStudyType)
(disjointWith ComputerActivityType DayOfWeekType)
(disjointWith ComputerActivityType DayOfMonthType)
(disjointWith ComputerActivityType SportsCategoryType)
(disjointWith ComputerActivityType ScientificTechnique)
(disjointWith ComputerActivityType CultureClassificationType)
(comment ComputerActivityType "An instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType. Each instance of this (second-order) collection is a subcollection of #$ComputerActivity all of whose instances are a particular and distinct type of activity. Examples include #$EMailSending, #$BootingAComputer, #$ComputerCrashing and #$OnlineChat. #$AttackByComputerOperation is not an instance as it is not distinct enough. #$EnteringAComputerPassword is an instance, but #$EnteringADomainAdministratorPassword is not as it is too specific. This collection is an instance of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType because it is assumed that computer-related activities are analysed fine-grainedly enough that if a putative instance of #$ComputerActivity would fall under two sibling #$ComputerActivityTypes, it should be analysed as two events.")
(comment ComputerHardwareTypeByBrand "A specialization of #$ProductTypeByBrand and #$ExistingObjectType. Each instance of this (second-order) collection is a spec of #$ComputerHardwareItem which is either a type of brand-name computer (for example, #$MacintoshComputer) or a type of brand-name computer hardware item (for example, #$ApplePrinter-TheProduct). See also the more specific #$ComputerTypeByBrand. For another second-order collection of specs of #$ComputerHardwareItem -- sorted by functionality rather than by brand-name -- see #$ComputerHardwareDeviceType.")
(disjointWith ShelterConstruction OutdoorRecreationArea)
(comment OutdoorRecreationArea "A specialization of #$OutdoorLocation and #$Artifact.  Each instance of #$OutdoorRecreationArea is an area designed to provide a venue for outdoor entertainment.  Specializations include #$AmusementPark, #$SwimmingPool-Outdoor, #$SkiSlope, #$GolfCourse and #$DrivingRange.  A natural beach that has not been crafted by human hands to serve as a venue for entertainment is not an instance of #$OutdoorRecreationArea, but a beach with lifeguards or snack bars or a scenic trail is an instance of #$OutdoorRecreationArea.")
(comment Orphan "An attribute possessed by anyone who, as

  a child, lost both parents.")
(comment Orphan "The collection of persons who, as a child, lost both parents.")
(disjointWith PlumbingFixture SwimmingPool)
(disjointWith Virgin Prostitute)
(comment Virgin "The collection of instances of #$Animal that have never had sexual intercourse. In general, this applies only to humans and possibly other primates.")
(comment Bypass-Path "The collection of all instances of #$Path-Generic that are, by design, paths around, or circumventing, some object.")
(comment krakenHelpTextForTool "The text that should be displayed to a user of a given #$RKFTool.")
(comment pathCircumventsObject "(#$pathCircumventsObject PATH OBJECT) says that the #$Path-Spatial PATH is one that goes around OBJECT, in the sense that OBJ lies (roughly) between the starting and ending points of PATH, but does not  lie on PATH, so that PATH curves around OBJ so as to \"avoid\" it.")
(comment BypassingSomething "A specialization of #$Translation-LocationChange.  Every instance of #$BypassingSomething is the following of a path that \"avoids\" some object.")
(comment computerUserHasUsed "(#$computerUserHasUsed USER PROGRAM_MODULE) means that the #$ComputerUser, USER , has used running instantiations of PROGRAM_MODULE at least once.")
(comment computerUserHasActivated "(#$computerUserHasActivated USER ACTIVE_AREA) means that the #$ComputerUser, USER, has activated an instantiation of the #$ComputerDisplayActiveArea, ACTIVE_AREA, at least once.")
(comment computerUserHasUsedNTimes "(#$computerUserHasUsed USER PROGRAM_MODULE N) means that the #$ComputerUser, USER, has used running instantiations of PROGRAM_MODULE N times, with N a #$PositiveInteger.")
(comment humansHairstyle "This relates a person to his or her hairstyle.")
(comment HumanWithAfroHairstyle "The colleciton of all people with an \"afro\" hairstyle: that is, a thick,
tightly curled hairstyle, shaped round around the person's head.")
(comment HumanWithCrewcutHairstyle "The collection of all people with a \"crewcut\" hairstyle: that is, 
a haircut that leaves the hair cropped extremely close to the scalp.")
(comment HumanWithMohawkHairstyle "The collection of all people who wear their hair in a mohawk, that is, 
most of the head on the scalp is shaved save for a long, spiked stripe
in the middle of the head.")
(comment HumanWithMulletHairstyle "The collection of all people with mullet hairstyless.  A mullet is hairstyle 
often worn by Canadian and Latino young males. It is 
characterized by having considerable length differential between the back, 
top and sides. The top and sides are shorter than the
back, which typically covers the neck. The ears are left exposed by 
such a hairstyle.")
(comment HumanWithMulletHairstyle "A hairstyle often worn by Canadian and Latino young males.  It is characterized by having a considerable length differential between the back, top and sides.  The top and sides are shorter than the back, which typically covers the neck.  The ears are left exposed by such a hairstyle.")
(comment HumanWithSpikedHairstyle "The collection of all people who have their hair in spiked hairstyles, that
is, standing straight up with the assistance of some hair gel or mousse.")
(comment officialMarineCorps "A binary predicate that relates instances of #$Organization to instances of #$MarineCorps.   (#$officialMarineCorps ORG CORPS) means that the official marine corps of ORG is CORPS.  See also #$MarineCorpsFn.")
(comment transitiveViaArgExceptions "A #$QuintaryPredicate used to state exceptions to the applicability of #$transitiveViaArg assertions.   (#$transitiveViaArgInverse THING FORMULA PRED BINPRED N)) means that the Nth argument position of PRED is \"transitive\" with respect to BINPRED except when THING (or a constant that stands in the relation BINPRED to THING) occurs as the Nth argument to PRED in a GAF whose arguments are otherwise identical to those of FORMULA.  See also #$transitiveViaArgInverseExceptions.")
(comment DailyTelegraph-NewspaperSeries "An instance of #$NewspaperSeries published in the U.K.")
(comment SituationTypeSuchThatFn "An instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction that allows one to specify subcollections of #$Situation in terms of sentences that truly describe any situation of the relevant type.  For example, (#$SituationTypeSuchThatFn (#$TheSet  (#$postureOfAnimal #$BillClinton #$SittingPosture) (#$objectFoundInLocation #$BillClinton TheOvalOffice))) denotes the collection of all situations in which Bill Clinton is sitting somewhere in the Oval Office.")
(comment populationRange "(#$populationRange #$City (#$Unity 50 25000000)) means that instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity that qualify as instances of #$City can be expected to have a number of inhabitants between 50 and 25,000,000 in their territory.")
(comment situationIsSuchThat "A binary predicate used to relate a #$Situation to sentences that are true because of (or: made true by) the situation. We can think of #$Situations, in this light, as the states of affairs or `parts' of the world that are the truth-makers of sentences. For example, (#$situationIsSuchThat MeetingInTheOvalOfficeOnJune12 (#$postureOfAnimal #$BillClinton #$SittingPosture)) allows us to characterize the state of affairs in the Oval Office on June 12 as a particular situation that `includes' the state of affairs  that Bill Clinton  is sitting.  This is not the same as #$holdsIn, at least given the intended meaning of #$holdsIn. For one thing, it doesn't presuppose that situations must have temporal extent throughout which something holds.  Note that if (#$situationIsSuchThat SIT P) and P entails Q, then (#$situationIsSuchThat SIT Q).  So, any situation in which Clinton is sitting in the Oval Office is a situation in which an #$Individual is sitting in the Oval Office.")
(comment governmentType "The predicate governmentType indicates what kind of system of government a  geopolitical entity has. (#$governmentType REGION GOVSYSTEM) means that the  government of the #$GeopoliticalEntity REGION is an instance of the  #$GeopoliticalEntityBySystemOfGovernment GOVSYSTEM. For example, the  #$governmentType of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica is a #$FederalRepublic and a  #$RepresentativeDemocracy.")
(comment Anarchy "A complete or near complete lack of political order.  A person who believes in #$Anarchism would prefer #$Anarchy over any other system of government.")
(comment CoPrincipality "A system of government where the country is ruled by two princes - they don't
really have to be two princes.")
(comment CommunistGovernment "A system of government where the country is ruled by a communist party. Note
that this should more properly be considered an economic system, not a system
of government.")
(comment CommunistPeoplesRepublic "A system of government where the country is ruled by a communist republic. Note
that communism should more properly be considered an economic system, not a system
of government.")
(comment ConstitutionalMonarchy "An instance of #$GeopoliticalEntityBySystemOfGovernment.  Each instance of #$ConstitutionalMonarchy is a country ruled by a monarch along with the assistance of a written constitution.")
(comment ConstitutionalRepublic "A system of government where the country is a #$Republic with
a written constitution.")
(comment FederationGovernment "A #$SystemOfGovernment in which the headOfState is usually a President-HeadOfState.")
(comment IndependentCommonwealth "a system of representative government using the term COMMONWEALTH instead of STATE --a la Massachusetts-- or REPUBLIC")
(comment IndependentRepublic "An #$IndependentCountry whose system of government is an #$IndependentRepublic.")
(comment IndependentSultanate "A #$SystemOfGovernment which has a sovereign Sultan")
(comment MartialLawGovernment "The system of government when the country is in a state of martial law.")
(comment Marxist-LeninistGovernment "The system of government of some countries, based on the ideals of #$KarlMarx and #$VladimirLenin.")
(comment MilitaryDictatorship "A government in which a political entity is ruled in an authoritarian
manner by some #$MilitaryOrganization.")
(comment MilitaryDictatorship "A system of government in which a political entity is ruled in an authoritarian manner by some #$MilitaryOrganization.")
(comment MilitaryGovernment "The #$SystemOfGovernment of some unfortunate countries.")
(comment Monarchy "An instance of #$GeopoliticalEntityBySystemOfGovernment.  Each instance of #$Monarchy is a country with a system of government in which an hereditary ruler figures prominently. Subsumes both absolute monarchy, where executive, legislative, and judicial functions of government are vested in the monarch, and constitutional  monarchies, in which at least some aspects of the government derive from  a written constitution and not from the monarch's whim.")
(comment argCardinality "An #$ArgConstraintPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to put a cardinality-based constraint on a predicate with respect to a given argument-place. (#$argCardinality PRED N CARD) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, there are at most CARD distinct things such that, if any one of them is taken as the _Nth_ argument, PRED holds of those arguments.  That is, there are at most CARD distinct sequences of the form <... ARGN(i) ...> that differ from one another only in their Nth members (their respective ARGN(i)) and are such that (PRED ... ARGN(i) ...) holds.
<p>
Note that CARD is simply the _maximum_ number of such sequences that there can be.  There is no guarantee that there will be _at_least_ CARD such sequences; in any given case there might be fewer than CARD such sequences, and possibly none at all.  On the other hand, there is a default assumption that the presence of an assertion of the form (#$argCardinality PRED N CARD) in the Knowledge Base indicates that the relation PRED is such that no number lower than CARD could correctly have been used instead.  (Given that the above argument-cardinality assertion is true, (#$argCardinality PRED N CARD+1) is trivially true as well;  the default assumption reflects the idea that it would nevertheless be misleading to assert the later.)
<p>
Most of the argument-cardinality sentences worth asserting are where CARD is 1, which entails that PRED is \"strictly functional\" in its Nth argument (see #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs).  As an upper limit, CARD might be #$PlusInfinity, which amounts to an explicit denial that any finite cardinality restriction is correct for the argument-place in question, which amounts to no restriction at all; so such sentences are worth asserting to the KB only in cases where this lack of any cardinality restriction is likely to be surprising or unexpected, in order to document the fact that the question has already been considered and thereby reduce the potential for an unnecessary duplication of effort.  As a lower limit, CARD might be 0, which entails that PRED holds of _no_ argument-sequence whatsoever; but it is unlikely that any such assertion should appear in the KB, since there would be little point to having such a predicate in the CycL language in the first place.  See also #$interArgCardinality, #$assertedArgCardinality, and #$intraArgReln.")
(comment interArgCardinality "An instance of both #$ArgCardinalityPredicate and #$InterArgConstraintPredicate (qq.v.) that can be used to put a conditional argument-cardinality constraint on a specified argument-place of a predicate, dependent upon the type of thing occupying that or another specified argument-place.
<p>
(#$interArgCardinality PRED M COL N CARD) means that when PRED's Mth argument is an instance of COL then its Nth argument is constrained to a cardinality of CARD.  Thus for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, if the Mth argument is an instance of COL, then there are at most CARD distinct things such that, if any one of them is taken as the _Nth_ argument while keeping the other arguments as already fixed (and keeping the Mth argument an instance of COL if N = M), PRED holds of those arguments.  That is (assuming without loss of generality that M > N), there are at most CARD distinct sequences of the form <... ARGM ... ARGN(i) ...> with an Mth member (ARGM) that is an instance of COL and that differ from one another _only_ in their Nth members (their respective ARGN(i)) and are such that (PRED ... ARGM ... ARGN(i) ...) holds.
<p>
For example, (#$interArgCardinality #$performedBy 1 #$Breathing 2 1) means that for any event BREATH that is a #$Breathing, there is at most one thing BREATHER such that (#$performedBy BREATH BREATHER) holds.  Simply put, an act of breathing is never performed by more than one agent.
<p>
As in the case of the unconditional constraint predicate #$argCardinality (q.v.), most #$interArgCardinality sentences asserted to the Knowledge Base are such that CARD is 1.  For more discussion on argument-cardinality constraints in general, see the comment on #$argCardinality.  See also #$assertedArgCardinality, and #$intraArgReln.")
(comment intersectsWith "A #$TaxonomicSlot that relates collections whose extents (see #$extent) intersect or overlap.  <code>(#$intersectsWith COL1 COL2)</code>  means that <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> have at least one instance in common.   Note that this predicate is <i>not</i> reflexive: it is trivially  possible for a #$Collection to fail to intersect with itself, though only if it is empty (i.e. has no instances).  Thus, for  example, (#$intersectsWith #$Nothing #$Nothing) <i>fails</i> to hold. Cf. #$disjointWith.")
(comment coextensionalSetOrCollectionOrRelations "An instance of #$TaxonomicSlot and a generalization of  #$coextensionalSetOrCollections.   #$coextensionalSetOrCollectionOrRelations relates predicates and  functions as well as sets and collections; thus it holds between  coextensional #$SetOrCollections (q.v.), between coextensional  #$Predicates (q.v.), and between coextensional  #$Function-Denotationals (q.v.).   (#$coextensionalSetOrCollectionOrRelations SET|COL|REL-1 SET|COL|REL-2)  means that SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2 are coextensional.  More  precisely: (i) if SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2 are both  #$SetOrCollections, then they have all their elements in common  (see #$coextensionalSetOrCollections); (ii) if SET|COL|REL-1 and  SET|COL|REL-2 are both #$Predicates, then they hold of exactly the same argument-sequences; and (iii) if SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2  are both #$Function-Denotationals, then they are defined for exactly the same argument-sequences and both have the same value for any such sequence.  Note that, since coextensionality entails identity  (see #$equals) in the case of sets but _not_ in the cases of  collections or relations, it _cannot_ generally be inferred from  the above that SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2 are identical.")
(comment ParliamentaryGovernment "A specialization of #$GeopoliticalEntityBySystemOfGovernment.  Eachinstance of #$ParliamentaryGovernment is a country in which a parliament has significant power (see #$Parliament). Parliamentary governments are multi-party systems in which the executive is formally dependent on the legislature.")
(comment ParliamentaryRepublic "A republican system of government represented by a parliament.")
(comment ParliamentaryRepublic "A systemOfGovernment which has a parliament.")
(comment PeoplesRepublic "A #$SystemOfGovernment in which the headOfState is not a Monarch-HeadOfState and is usually a President-HeadOfState.")
(comment PoliticalDependency "A protectorate whose system of government is a political dependency.")
(comment PoliticalDependency "A protecterate whose system of government is a #$PoliticalDependency.")
(comment ProvisionalGovernment "A system of government which is usually an interim government after a 
military take-over of the previous government.")
(comment ProvisionalGovernment "A #$SystemOfGovernment which usually is an interim government after a military take-over or the previous government.")
(comment RepresentativeDemocracy "A system of government which has some kind of elected legislative body.")
(comment RulingCouncilGovernment "The #$SystemOfGovernment of some countries.")
(comment SinglePartyRepublic "A system of government in which the #$headOfState is not a #$Monarch-HeadOfState, 
and is usually a #$President-HeadOfState, but there is only one political party.")
(comment SocialistGovernment "A socialist system of government.  Note that this is actually an economic, not
political, system.")
(comment SocialistGovernment "#$SocialistGovernment")
(comment TheocraticRepublic "A system of government in which the leader is thought to have divine 
guidance.")
(comment TheocraticRepublic "A system of government in which the leader of the government is

thought to have divine guidance.")
(comment UnresolvedDiscourseAssumptions "This is the collection of assumptions about a discourse which are, in some sense, still unresolved by the discourse.  Examples of  unresolved assumptions would include (among other things) open assumptions about how anaphoric and deictic reference should be resolved and how presupposition satisfaction should be accomodated.")
(comment ArtificialIntelligenceResearcher "A #$ComputerScientist whose main field is artificial intelligence research.")
(comment ProgramRepresentationFn "(#$ProgramRepresentationFn COL) denotes the abstract
program data types used to represent instances of COL
in a computer program. For example, 
(#$ProgramRepresentationFn #$RealNumber)
refers to the data type used to represent real numbers in the
current context.")
(comment jobPositionOpeningStatus "(#$jobPositionOpeningStatus JOB OSTATUS) means #$JobPosition JOB has the #$OpeningStatusOfJobPosition OSTATUS. The latter can be: #$JobOpen and #$JobFilled.")
(comment jobPositionOpeningStatus "(#$jobPositionOpeningStatus JOB STATUS) means that #$JobPosition
has the opening status SATUS.  The latter can be #$JobOpen or
#$JobFilled.")
(comment JobPositionByOpeningStatus "The collection of #$JobPositions faceted by opening status.  Member collections
include #$JobOpen and #$JobFilled.")
(disjointWith JobFilled JobOpen)
(comment JobOpen "The collection of all #$JobPositions that are open.  This means taht there is
no #$Person currently standing in the #$personWithPosition relation to the
#$JobPosition, although there is an #$Organization stainding in the 
#$orgWithJobPosition relation to the #$JobPosition.")
(comment JobOpen "An #$AttributeValue indicating that a #$JobPosition is open. This means that there is no #$Person currently standing in the #$personWithJobPosition relation to the #$JobPosition, although there is an #$Organization standing in the #$orgWithJobPosition to the #$JobPosition.")
(comment AnimalTypeByAlertness "The second order collection #$AnimalTypeByAlertness facets animals based on how
sleepy or alert they are.  Alertness levels include #$Asleep, 
#$Sleepy, #$Awake.")
(comment alertnessState "(#$alertnessState ANIMAL LEVEL) indicates how alert ANIMAL is in the
period during which the predicate holds.  The major levels are #$Unconscious
and #$Awake, but more specific levels such as #$Asleep and 
#$VeryAlert exist as well.")
(comment Asleep "#$Asleep is the collection of all sleeping animals.  #$Asleep 
is a specialization of #$Unconscious.  For example, (#$holdsIn (#$YearFn 1776)
#$alertnessState RipVanWinkle #$Asleep) means that during 1776, 
Rip Van Winkle was a sleeping animal.")
(disjointWith Asleep Awake)
(comment Awake "#$Awake is the collection of all animals that are awake.")
(comment InAHypnoticTrance "An artificially induced #$SemiConscious state superficially resembling
sleep, but with a heightened susceptibility to suggestion.")
(comment ModeratelyAlert "#$ModeratelyAlert is the collection of all #$Awake #$Animals that
are paying particular attention to their environment.  It is a higher
#$alertnessLevel than #$Sleepy, but lower than #$VeryAlert.")
(disjointWith Awake SemiConscious)
(comment SemiConscious "#$SemiConscious is the collection of all animals that meet some,
but not all, of the necessary conditions for being #$Awake. Specifically,
an animal that is a member of #$SemiConscious typically performs some
behavior which is ordinarily considered deliberate, such as walking or 
speaking, but is insensitive to some or all environmental stimuli.")
(disjointWith ModeratelyAlert VeryAlert)
(comment VeryAlert "#$VeryAlert is an #$Alertness attribute which is a specialization of #$Awake.  It is the state of an #$Animal paying particular attention to its environment (or a specific aspect thereof).  It is the state an #$Antelope is in after smelling a #$Lion, or a burglar is in after hearing the front door open.")
(comment VeryAlert "#$VeryAlert is the collection of all animals that are #$Awake and
paying particularly close attention to their environment (or an aspect
thereof).  It is a state of an #$Antelope is in after smelling a #$Lion, 
or a burglar is in after hearing the front door open.")
(disjointWith ModeratelyAlert Sleepy)
(comment Sleepy "#$Sleepy is the collection of all animals that are members of #$Awake
that are very tired.  It is the state an animal normally passes through
before #$GoingToSleep.")
(disjointWith Sleepy VeryAlert)
(disjointWith Awake Unconscious)
(disjointWith SemiConscious Unconscious)
(comment Unconscious "#$Unconscious is the collection of all animals that are unconscious. 
It is disjoint with #$Awake.  A specialization of this collection is
#$Asleep.")
(comment dressState "The predicate #$dressState is used to describe the state of dress or undress that a particular animal (usually a person) is in.  (#$dressState PERSON DRESS) means that PERSON is a member of the 

instance of #$PersonTypeByClothingState DRESS.  DRESS indicates what portion of PERSON's body is clothed.  For example, (#$dressState John 

#$FullyClothed) means that John is fully clothed (during the 

applicable period of time).")
(comment FullyClothed "A person is wearing the appropriate amount of clothing for appearing in 
public.  This could mean different things in different social contexts, but
in modern western culture it usually means that the hips and torso and feet
(at minimum) are covered.")
(comment Gloved "All #$Persons with #$Gloves covering their #$Hands.")
(comment Masked "The collection of all persons wearing a #$Mask over their face.")
(disjointWith FullyClothed Nude)
(disjointWith Masked Nude)
(disjointWith Gloved Nude)
(comment Nude "Having absolutely no clothing on.")
(disjointWith Nude PersonWithSexOrgansCovered)
(comment PersonWithSexOrgansCovered "Having some article of clothing that covers the sex organs.")
(comment PersonWithSexOrgansCovered "some article of clothing covers the groin area")
(comment ToplessPerson "no articles of clothing are worn on the upper torso")
(comment programTypeStrings "A binary predicate. (#$programTypeStrings OBJECT-TYPE STRING) means that when
OBJECT-TYPE is referenced via STRING in a computer program in the 
current context. For example, in #$CSyntaxProgrammingLanguageMt
(#$programTypeStrings #$CharacterObject \"char\") means that the character
data type is referenced by \"char\" in C-like programming languages.")
(comment ArgCardinalityPredicate "The collection of those #$ArgConstraintPredicates used to put cardinality-based argument constraints on predicates.  An argument-cardinality constraint is a kind of restriction on a predicate regarding the maximum number of things the predicate holds of with respect to a given one of its argument-places.  More precisely: given any particular way of fixing the arguments in the rest of the predicate's argument-places (perhaps together with some further specified condition), the cardinality constraint states the maximum number of different things that can occupy the given argument-place such that the relation holds of those arguments.  That is, supposing the given argument-place is the Nth, the cardinality constraint gives the maximum number of different sequences of arguments of which the predicate holds and that differ from each other only in their Nth items (and, where relevant, that meet some further specified condition).
<p>
Instances of #$ArgCardinalityPredicate include #$argCardinality, #$interArgCardinality, and #$assertedArgCardinality. Note that, in contrast with what #$ArgTypePredicates (q.v.) are used for, putting an argument-cardinality constraint on a predicate does _not_ impose a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness on closed atomic sentences built from that predicate.  A violation of a cardinality constraint with respect to the Cyc Knowledge Base normally involves multiple such sentences, where at least one them must be _untrue_ (or otherwise unsuited for assertion in the KB); but there is no implication that any of them is malformed.")
(comment softwareParameterHasType "(#$softwareParameterHasType ICT TYPE) means that in the current

context, ICT is an instantiation of TYPE data type.

For example, (#$softwareParameterHasType #$AlgorithmInput1 #$CharacterObject)

means that #$AlgorithmInput1 is understood to be typed

as a character variable. This information is useful for

software engineering purposes, even when the programming

language used itself does not have the syntatic constructs

for representing strong typing.")
(comment doDeclareFunctionParameters "A variable arity complex action predicate.
(#$doDeclareFunctionParameters PROGRAMMER FILE . ARGS) means that PROGRAMMER appends to the end of FILE code that declares ARGS as part of a function declaration.")
(comment FormulaOperatorFn "A unary function that returns an instance of #$CycLTerm when given an instance of #$CycLFormula as an arguments. (#$FormulaOperatorFn FORMULA) denotes the term appearing as argument 0 within FORMULA; e.g., (#$FormulaOperatorFn (#$genls #$Dog #$Mammal)) denotes #$genls and (#$FormulaOperatorFn (#$ArmyFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)) denotes #$ArmyFn.")
(comment ProgramObjectValueFn "(#$ProgramObjectValueFn OBJECT) denotes the real world object that is represented by OBJECT.  For instance, this function would map a C program #$FloatObject to the actual instance of #$RealNumber that it encodes.")
(comment programObjectRepresentsValue "(#$programObjectRepresentsValue STRUCT VALUE) means that the program object STRUCT is a structure representing VALUE in some programming environment.")
(comment programObjectTypeRepresents "(#$programObjectTypeRepresents TYPE COL) means that the data type TYPE is used
in some programming environment to represent instances of the collection COL.")
(comment ParameterProgramValueFn "(#$ParameterProgramValueFn PARAM) denotes the structure of the data object in memory which holds the value of PARAM during the contextually current #$ComputerProcessRunning.  For example, if HOSTNAME is an environment variable the stores the name of the local machine, (#$ParameterProgramValueFn HOSTNAME) would denote the ASCII/UNICODE/EBCIDIC string which is the structure of the data object in memory which corresponds to the hostname")
(comment softwareParameterProgramObject "(#$softwareParameterProgramObject PARAM STRUCT) means that #$SoftwareParameter PARAM, in the context of a #$ComputerProcessRunning, has as its current in-program representation the #$ProgramObject STRUCT.")
(comment ParameterValueFn "(#$ParameterValueFn PARAM) denotes the real world object that PARAM has as a value in the context of the current state of the contextually determined #$ComputerProcessRunning.  If PARAM has a real numbered-valued data type, then (#$ParameterValueFn PARAM) will denote some instance of #$RealNumber.  This mapping of a parameter to its real world value is defined by the intentions of the program itself. ")
(comment NobelPrizeTypeByField "A collection of types of Nobel prize.  Each instance of #$NobelPrizeTypeByField is a specialization of #$NobelPrize whose instances are all awarded in the same field.  For example, #$NobelPrizeForPhysics is an instance of NobelPrizeTypeByField -- each of its instances is awarded for excellence in physics.")
(comment coDecompositions "This predicate relates tangibles that are 

continuous or discontinuous physical parts (see 

#$physicalDecompositions) of the same whole.

(#$coDecompositions PART1 PART2) means that there

is some tangible WHOLE such that 

(#$physicalDecompositions WHOLE PART1) and 

(#$physicalDecompositions WHOLE PART2) both hold.")
(comment genlOrSpecPreds "A #$TaxonomicSlot (q.v.) that relates two #$Predicates just in case either one of them is a generalization of the other via #$genlPreds (q.v.).  (#$genlOrSpecPreds PRED1 PRED2) means that either (#$genlPreds PRED1 PRED2) or (#$genlPreds PRED2 PRED1) holds.  Thus #$genlOrSpecPreds is in effect the \"union\" of #$genlPreds and its inverse.")
(comment CalendarSeasonTypeByCulture "An instance of #$CyclicalIntervalGroupType and a specialization of #$CalendarSeasonType.  Each instance of #$CalendarSeasonTypeByCulture is a specialization of #$CalendarSeason whose instances' temporal bounds are delineated by the customs of a particular culture.  Examples include #$CalendarWinter-US, whose instances' temporal bounds are fixed at the #$DecemberSolstice and #$VernalEquinox each year by mainstream American culture, and #$CalendarWinter-Australasian, whose instances' temporal bounds are fixed at June 1st and the last day of August each year by mainstream Australasian culture.")
(comment CalendarWinter-Australasian "A specialization of #$CalendarWinter (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CalendarWinter-Australasian is a time interval that begins on a day (June 1st) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to begin a winter season, and ends on a day (the last day of August) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to end a winter season.  Each instance of #$CalendarWinter-Australasian largely intersects an approximately three-month-long instance of #$WinterSeason (q.v.) that occurs in Australasia.")
(disjointWith CalendarWinter-Australasian CalendarSpring-Australasian)
(comment CalendarSpring-Australasian "A specialization of #$CalendarSpring (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CalendarSpring-Australasian is a time interval that begins on a day (September 1st) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to begin a spring season, and ends on a day (the last day of November) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to end a spring season.  Each instance of #$CalendarSpring-Australasian largely intersects an approximately three-month-long instance of #$SpringSeason (q.v.) that occurs in Australasia.")
(comment CalendarSummer-Australasian "A specialization of #$CalendarSummer (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CalendarSummer-Australasian is a time interval that begins on a day (December 1st) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to begin a summer season, and ends on a day (the last day of February) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to end a summer season. Each instance of #$CalendarSummer-Australasian largely intersects an approximately three-month-long instance of #$SummerSeason (q.v.) that occurs in Australasia.")
(disjointWith CalendarWinter-Australasian CalendarSummer-Australasian)
(comment CalendarAutumn-Australasian "A specialization of #$CalendarAutumn (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CalendarAutumn-Australasian is a time interval that begins on a day (March 1st) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to begin an autumn season, and ends on a day (the last day of May) considered in mainstream Australasian culture to end an autumn season. Each instance of #$CalendarAutumn-Australasian largely intersects an approximately three-month-long instance of #$FallSeason (q.v.) that occurs in Australasia.")
(disjointWith CalendarWinter-Australasian CalendarAutumn-Australasian)
(comment EggSalad "The collection of all instances of #$Salad

 whose main ingredient is chicken eggs.")
(comment TurkeyChili "A dish made with chilies or chili powder, pieces of (usually ground) #$Turkey-Meat, chopped onion and pepper, and sometimes kidney beans and tomatoes.")
(comment SubmarineSandwich "A specialization of #$Sandwich.  Each instance of #$SubmarineSandwich is a large sandwich made with a crusty roll of bread that has been cut lengthwise and filled with some combination of meat, cheese, condiments, and vegetables.")
(comment computerDisplayRegion-CWText "(#$computerDisplayRegion-CWText COMPUTER_DISPLAY_REGION-CW TEXT) means that the text str ing TEXT is displayed on the #$ComputerDisplayScreenRegion-CW, COMPUTER_DISPLAY_REGION-CW.")
(comment userToolTypeUsageLevel "(#$userToolTypeUsageLevel AGENT ARTIFACT-TYPE USAGE-LEVEL) means that AGENT can use instances of ARTIFACT-TYPE with the skill level, SKILL-LEVEL (an instance of #$ToolUsageLevel).")
(comment userComputerToolUsageLevel "(#$userComputerToolUsageLevel USER SOFTWARE SKILL-LEVEL) means that USER can use running instantiations of SOFTWARE with the skill level, SKILL-LEVEL (an instance of #$ToolUsageLevel).")
(comment Pizza-NewYorkStyle "A specialization of #$Pizza.  Each instance of #$Pizza-NewYorkStyle is a pizza cooked in a style associated with the pizzerias of New York -- greasy and thin-crusted.")
(comment Burrito "A kind of #$MexicanCuisine.  Each instance of #$Burrito consists of a flour tortilla wrapped around a filling which is typically beef, chicken, or beans, perhaps with with tomatoes, cheese, or guacamole.")
(comment Tortilla "A specialization of #$UnleavenedBread and #$MexicanCuisine. Each instance of #$Tortilla is a disc of unleavened bread made from corn or wheat flour.")
(comment GoalSituationBySuccessStatus "#$GoalSituationBySuccessStatus is a collection of collections.  Each instance is a collection of #$GoalSituations that share a similar success status.  These instances represent statuses that

can apply to #$GoalSituations to describe whether a given goal is

successful, actively being pursued, relevant to the current state of

affairs etc.")
(comment ReportClassification-US "A collection of security classification attributes, used in United States government security circles for specifying the security classification of a given report.  This classification is the primary determiner of who is permitted to view the report in question.")
(comment ScriptTypeByStructuralOrderFeature "Each instance of this collection is a collection of instances of #$ScriptedEventType that share a certain temporal order property.  Each such collection represents the different sort of type-level temporal order properties that #$ScriptedEventTypes can have.")
(comment OutcomeType "A collection of attributes.  Elements of #$OutcomeType are #$AttributeValues used in representing constraints that affect the outcome of different situations.  Possible outcomes are elements of #$OutcomeType; e.g., #$FailureOutcome, #$InjuryOutcome, #$DamageOutcome.  See also #$sitConstraints.")
(comment MilitaryUnitTypeByStrength "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$MilitaryUnitTypeByStrength is a collection of military units with a similar troop strength.  Examples include #$ReinforcedStatus and #$ReducedStatus.")
(comment ConflictEventStatus "A #$ThirdOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$ConflictEventStatus is a type of relative status in a conflict.  Each instance of an instance of #$ConflictEventStatus is the collection of instances of #$IntelligentAgent in some relative conflict position in some particular conflict.    
To assert that some collection of agents has some conflict status in some conflict, use either the predicate #$statusInConflictEvent or the functions or predicates corresponding to the instances of #$ConflictEventStatus (see #$WinningFn).")
(comment TrapState "The states an animal trap may have.")
(comment CoalitionInformationDesignation "A subcollection of #$InformationDesignation.  Instances of this
collection are attributes of information.  Specifically, they are designations or classifications
of information created by coalitions between governments.  For instance, if the US formed a coalition with
Israel for operations in the middle east, they might designate the information accessible by only those members of 
the coalition. The designations in this scheme would be instances of #$CoalitionInformationDesignation.  The scheme might borrow
from the US governments preexisting method of classifying information, but could include novel names to prevent confusion
or misapplication of the scope of information accessible by the coalition members:  for instance, 'US-IsraelSecret' might 
replace the usual 'Secret' classification used by the US government because it refers to distinct information sources,
but it could still preserve the meaning of the term 'secret' as understood by the US government.")
(comment CoalitionClassification-US "A subcollection of #$CoalitionInformationDesignation.
Instances of this collection are attributes.  Specifically, they are the attributes 
#$CoalitionClassificationUS-TopSecret, #$CoalitionClassificationUS-Secret, or 
#$CoalitionClassificationUS-Confidential.  These attributes are similar to the attributes in the collection
#$ClassificationUS (#$TopSecretInformation, #$SecretInformation, and #$ConfidentialInformation), but differ
in that they are applied only to information that is classified and accessible by a
selected members of those governments that comprise an active
coalition formed with the US.  Instances of this collection will not be applied 
to the same information that gets classified by
the US government, but rather to the sum of information that is
used, valued and deemed in need of protection by members of the coalition, although there may indeed be overlap.
Hence, at least some of the information classified using an instance of the collection #$Classification-US will not
be classified by using an instance of the collection #$CoalitionClassication-US, and likewise 
some of the information that gets classified using an instance of the collection #$CoalitionClassification-US will
not be classified using an instance of the collection 
#$Classification-US.  Note, however,
that instances of both collections will
share the US government's classification
scheme--'Confidential', 'Secret', and
'TopSecret'.")
(comment ComputerNetworkBySecurityEnvironment "#$ComputerNetworkBySecurityEnvironment is a collection of collections.  Each instance is a collection of computer networks that have a similar security environment.  These collections can be used to describe the security environment of a system.")
(comment AISTypeByEncryptionStatus "The collection of all instances of #$IndividualTypeBySecurityStatus that have bearing on whether and to what degree an #$AbstractInformationStructure is encrypted.  Notable instances of this collection are #$Plaintext and #$Encrypted.")
(comment HumanEyeColor "The collection of human eye (iris) colors.")
(comment ObjectTypeByLightIntensity "A specialization of #$ObjectTypeBySensibleFeature.  Each instance of #$ObjectTypeByLightIntensity is a characterization of how brightly illuminated an Object is.  The light intensity of an object is indicated with the predicate #$illuminationLevel (q.v.).")
(comment Color "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$Color is a collection of instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized such that each has the same main exterior color.  Use #$mainColorOfObject to assert that a particular thing's main exterior color is some color.  For instance, (#$mainColorOfObject THING COLOR) states that THING is predominantly the color COLOR.  See also: #$significantColorOfObject, #$uniformColorOfObject, and #$objectHasColor. ")
(disjointWith Color ProductTypeByBrand)
(disjointWith Color PersonTypeByCulture)
(comment ChromaticColor "The collection of all colors which are 'colorful'.  This includes colors such as #$RedColor, #$BlueColor, and #$VividOrange-Color, and excludes #$PureBlackColor, #$PureWhiteColor, and any instance of #$PureGrayColor.  More precisely, each #$ChromaticColor has a location or extension in the dimension of hue.  See also #$AchromaticColor.")
(comment HumanHairColor "The collection of the colors in which human (head) hair commonly, naturally occurs.")
(disjointWith DarkColor BrightColor)
(disjointWith LightColor BrightColor)
(comment ObjectTypeByTransparency "A specialization of #$PhysicalAttribute.  Each instance of #$Transparency is a capacity of some tangible object to transmit light.  Instances of #$Transparency include #$Opaque, #$Translucent, and #$Transparent.  See also the predicate #$transparencyOfObject, which relates an object to its #$Transparency.")
(comment Reflectance "#$Reflectance is a collection of collections.  Each instance is a collection of things with a particular degree of reflectance.")
(comment PathTypeBySurfaceFeature "A collection of collections.  Each instance of this collection is a collection of paths that share a certain surface feature.  These collections can be used to describe the properties of

the surfaces of certain instances of #$PathArtifact.")
(comment EukaryoticOrganismTypeByPloidyLevel "The ploidy of an organism or type of organism is a measure of how many copies of its basic chromosome set it has.  This only makes sense for #$EukaryoticOrganisms, because #$ProkaryoticCells don't have #$Chromosomes.  Many organisms, such as mammals and many trees, are #$Diploid, meaning that their somatic (body) cells have two copies of each #$Chromosome from their basic set.  The #$Gametes that are produced by diploid organisms are #$Haploid, meaning that they have only one copy of each chromosome -- half the amount of genetic material as the parent organism has.")
(comment PersonTypeByHairFeature "a collection of collections.  Each instance of #$PersonTypeByHairFeature is a collection of people who share a certain property of their hair.  Examples might include the collection of people with straight hair, split ends, or dry lifeless hair.  An important subcollection is #$HumanTypeByHairstyle.  That collection included all the collections of persons 

classified by hairstyle.")
(disjointWith PersonTypeByHairFeature PersonTypeByActivity)
(comment BacteriumTypeByPhysiology "a collection of
collections each one representing a particular property that is specific to
#$Bacterium. Examples of this are #$Monotrichous and #$Peritrichous.
Negative examples would be #$AerobicPhysiology and #$AnaerobicPhysiology which may
apply to #$EukaryoticCells as well as #$ProkaryoticCells.")
(disjointWith AnimalTypeByPhysiologicalFeature PersonTypeByOccupation)
(comment AnimalTypeByPhysicalBuild "The collection of attributes for describing the physical build of an animal.")
(comment AnimalTypeByAmountOfBodyHair "The degree to which a mammal's body parts are hairy.")
(comment AnimalTypeByVaccinationStatus "A collection of collections that describe an individual as
being either vaccinated or unvaccinated against some particular
disease or virus.  This is a strange collection, as one cannot simply
be vaccinated or unvaccinated.  Rather, one must be vaccinated or
unvaccinated against some particular #$AilmentCondition.  Unless the ailment in question is clear from the context one should not use the binary #$hasPhysiologicalFeature to ascribe vaccination status.  Rather, one should use a ternary predicate that relates an organism an ailment condition and a vaccination status such that the relation holds just in case the organism has that status with respect to the ailment condition.")
(disjointWith AnimalTypeByPhysiologicalFeature PlantTypeByPhysiology)
(comment OrganismTypeByCellularPhysiology "a collection of collections.  Each instance of #$OrganismTypeByCellularPhysiology is a collection of instances of #$BiologicalLivingObject that have the same cellular physiology.  An important specialization of this collection is #$BacteriumTypeByPhysiology.")
(comment HumanTypeByHatSize "Hat sizes of heads.")
(comment HumanTypeByHatSize "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$HumanTypeByHatSize is a collection  of people with the same hat size.")
(comment OrbitalPathClass "A collection of attributes of orbits for particular instances of  satellites, 
#$OrbitalPathClasses are determined by a satellite's distance from the 
surface of the earth (altitude).  See #$hasOrbitalPathClass.")
(comment RegionTypeBySoilType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$RegionTypeBySoilType is collection of #$GeographicalRegions having a common soil type.")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain JustificationStrength)
(disjointWith GeopoliticalEntityBySystemOfGovernment RegionTypeByTerrain)
(disjointWith BLOTypeByPhysiology RegionTypeByTerrain)
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain AISTypeByEvaluationTone)
(disjointWith BioDeteriorationResistance RegionTypeByTerrain)
(comment HumanTypeByShoeSize "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$HumanTypeByShoeSize is a collection of people with the same shoe size.  For instance, MensShoeSize10-Half is the collection of all people who wear men's size 10 1/2 shoes.")
(comment LifeCriticality "How life-critical the script is.  Higher values are more critical")
(comment NLGenQuantAttribute "The collection of all instances of #$NLQuantAttribute that stand in the #$correspondingGenQuant relation to some instance of #$GeneralizedQuantifier.")
(comment CursiveStyleOfWriting "the cursive style of writing for the different writing systems")
(comment CalligraphyStyleOfWriting "An elegant style of handwriting.  The writing implement used is similar to a fountain pen.")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain SituationTypeByUserRights)
(comment MicrotheoryAttribute "A collection containing attribute values used to describe instances of #$Microtheory and their domain assumptions.")
(comment MicrotheoryTypeByAnthropacity "#$MicrotheoryTypeByAnthropacity is a class of #$AttributeValues which are used to organize the #$Microtheory structure in Cyc.  If a microtheory has a very general #$MicrotheoryTypeByAnthropacity value, it assumes less about the existence of a physical universe, living things, and so on. Microtheories which have more specific, detailed, or `dense' #$MicrotheoryTypeByAnthropacity values are assumed to be `about' civilized people living on Earth.  Relative generality (specificity) is indicated by the predicate #$genlAttributes.  (#$genlAttributes ATTRIBUTE-1 ATTRIBUTE-2) means that in a nested hierarchy of values including ATTRIBUTE-1 and ATTRIBUTE-2, ATTRIBUTE-2 is more general than ATTRIBUTE-1.  If a particulary microtheory has an anthropacity of ATTRIBUTE-1, this entails that it also has a (more general) anthropacity of ATTRIBUTE-2.  See also #$mtAnthropacity.")
(comment AssertionConclusionType "Consider an axiom of the form (P -> Q). 

If P is true, we might want the system to go ahead and conclude Q. 

On the other hand, we might want it just to verify that Q is true and 

complain if it is not. If later Q is retracted, we might want the system 

to immediately complain, or to detect this at its leisure. 

All this is indicated by placing instances of the collection

#$AssertionConclusionType as the arg2 of

the predicate #$assertionConclusionActionType, with the rule

in question being the arg1. See the comments on the instances of the 

collection #$AssertionConclusionType for further details.")
(comment NarrativeModeType "A collection of collections.  Instances of #$NarrativeModeType are collections of instances of #$CWInstantiating that are   
 instantiated in a certain mode.  These collections characterize the different modes in which a #$Narrative is or can be presented (i.e. instantiated), e.g.           is it presented in a way intended to be comical or dramatic?")
(comment MPAAAdvisoryClassification-Film "A collection of subcollections of #$Movie-CW. Each instance of #$MPAAAdvisoryClassification-Film is a collection of all movies with a common MPAA Advisory classification. For example, #$ParentalGuidance13Rating is the collection of all movies that have a PG-13 rating.")
(comment ObjectTypeByLicensingAttribute "A collection of collections.  Each instance of this collection is a collection of objects that are licensed by some, perhaps implicit, authority.")
(comment ThingTypeByVulnerability "#$ThingTypeByVulnerability is the collection of collections of things vulnerable to threats.  It is the collection of all collections which are specializations of #$VulnerableThing.")
(comment SocialBeingTypeByFinancialState "A collection of collections each of which is a collection of agents in a particular financial state.  These collections can be used to describe an agent's financial state, financial condition.")
(comment LoanAgreementTypeByPaymentTerms "An instance of #$FacetingCollectionType.  Each instance of #$LoanAgreementTypeByPaymentTerms is a type of loan that is distinguished from its siblings by virtue there being a set of repayment terms (c.f., #$RepaymentPlan, #$repaymentPlanForLoan) unique to that type.  For example, #$BalloonLoan is the collection of loans that have, among their #$subObligations, a repayment schedule that calls for a balloon payment at or near the end of the term of the loan.")
(comment USCoastGuardRank "The scale of ranks employed by the United States Coast Guard. This is a collection of attributes that are used with the predicate #$rank-Military.")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain ActionTypeByWorkflowStatus)
(comment ObjectTypeByStyle "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$ObjectTypeByStyle is a collection of things that share a particular style/genre/school.  ")
(comment SportsComputerGameProgramTypeByGenre "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ComputerSportsGameGenreType is a collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram that are of a certain sports genre or style; e.g. collections of games simulating bowling or American football etc..")
(comment SchoolOfPaintingType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$SchoolOfPaintingType is a collection of conceptual works that are of a particular painting style.  Each instance represents a particular style or genre of painting.")
(comment RockMusicalCompositionTypeByGenre "A collection of rock music genres.  Each #$RockMusicType is a collection of instances of MusicalComposition each instance of which is instantiated by some instance of #$RockMusic.  Note that these genres are collections of [#$ConceptualWorks].  To relate a genre to a type of music
use #$musicTypeIsOfGenre.")
(comment ClothingTypeByStyle "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ClothingTypeByStyle is a collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that are each of a particular style.  Individual items of clothing can be asserted to be of a particular style with the predicate #$clothingStyle.  ")
(comment PathArtifactTypeByStructuralFeature "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$PathArtifactTypeByStructuralFeature is a collection of paths with a certain structural feature.  These collections can be used to describe the properties of the structure of some instance of #$PathArtifact.  See, e.g. the subcollection

#$PathTypeBySurfaceFeature.")
(comment SecurityClearanceLevel "The degree to which information is held secret. Instances can be placed into a partial order.")
(comment ComputerVideoResolution "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ComputerVideoResolution is a collection of instances of ComputerProgram-CW that support some video resolution and color mode.")
(comment USArmyRank "The scale of ranks employed by the United States Army.  This is a collection of attributes that are used with the predicate #$rank-Military.")
(disjointWith Color ObjectTypeByColorTinge)
(comment ObjectTypeByColorTinge "A collection of collections. Each instance is a collection of objects whose main color (c.f. #$mainColorOfObject) has a certain color tinge. For example, #$Purplish is the collection of all things that are mainly purplish.  Every instance of #$Color is a specialization of some instance of #$ObjectTypeByColorTinge.  Having a tinge of a certain color must not be confused with being a shade of that color. More precisely, possessing some instance of #$ObjectTypeByColorTinge, XISH, does not imply having the color X. For instance, an object can be an instance of #$Reddish, without being an instance of #$RedColor. Anything which is #$GrayishReddishBrown-Color is #$Reddish but isn't #$RedColor. Many colors have more than one instance of #$ObjectTypeByColorTinge as generalizations. #$GrayishPink-Color has both #$Grayish and #$Pinkish as generalizations.")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain EdibleStuffTypeByPreparationStyle)
(comment WarrantOfficerTypeByRank "The collection of warrant officer ranks.  Warrant officer ranks are deemed to outrank NonCommissionedOfficerRanks but to be outranked by CommissionedOfficerRanks --- thus they form an intermediate type of rank.")
(comment LocalOrganizationTypeByOpennessStatus "This awkwardly-named collection contains attributes describing whether a #$LocalOrganization is open for business or closed for business.")
(comment TransportationAccommodationType "A collection of collections, each instance of which represents some degree of luxury which is experienced by the passengers who are recieving such accommodations during their travels.  For instance, #$FirstClass-TravelClass is the collection of first class travel accommodations.  This is not based in how they experience their journey, but rather, it is based on the classification given the accommodation by the company providing it.  A concrete example of a use of one of the members of this collection is (#$TravelingByClassFn (#$TravelingByMeansOfPathMediumFn #$Railway) #$FirstClass-TravelClass), which denotes the collection of all railway traveling events that with first-class seating.")
(comment USGovernmentInformationDesignation "A subcollection of #$InformationDesignation.  Instances of

this collection are attributes of information. Specifically, they are

designations or classifications of information according to the scheme that is used by the US 

government to specify the conditions under which the information 

of interest or use to them can be accessed.  Examples include #$ConfidentialInformation, #$SecretInformation,

and #$TopSecretInformation.")
(comment USAirForceRank "The scale of ranks employed by the United States Air Force. This is a collection of attributes that are used with the predicate #$rank-Military.")
(comment VerdictOfTrial "instances are the various results a trial can have.")
(comment NonCommissionedOfficerTypeByRank "The collection of non-commissioned officer ranks.  Such officers usually have only limited formal academic officer training, if any.  There is variation in the command and control structures, and rank hierarchies, of different nations and different branches of the armed services.  See also #$CommissionedOfficerTypeByRank and #$WarrantOfficerTypeByRank.")
(comment RussianArmyRank "The scale of ranks employed by the Russian Army. This is a collection of attributes that are used with the predicate #$rank-Military.")
(comment IranianArmyRank "The scale of ranks employed by the Iranian Army. This is a collection of attributes that are used with the predicate #$rank-Military.")
(comment CommissionedOfficerTypeByRank "The collection of all military commissioned officer ranks.  Such officers usually have extensive formal academic officer training.  There is variation in the command and control structures, and rank hierarchies, of different nations and different branches of the armed services.  See also #$NonCommissionedOfficerTypeByRank and #$WarrantOfficerTypeByRank.")
(comment USMarinesRank "The scale of ranks employed by the United States Marine Corps. This is a collection of attributes that are used with the predicate #$rank-Military.")
(comment CodingSequenceConnectivity "In eukaryotic organisms, genetic sequences exhibited by all

DNA and some RNA molecules have the feature that the actual coding

sequence is not continuous: more specifically, the actual coding

seqence is interspersed with non-coding sequences called introns (see

#$Intron-MolecularSegment) and (#$Intron-GIS).  These introns

have to be excised from primary transcript RNA, and the bits and

pieces of the actual coding sequence (the so-called 'exons': see

#$Exon-MolecularSegment and #$Exon-GIS) stitched together by

special-purpose enzymes to produce a functional messenger RNA

molecule.  #$CodingSequenceConnectivity is the collection of

attributes characteristic of 'interrupted' and 'uninterrupted' genetic

coding sequences, respectively.  Instances are

#$CodingSequence-Interrupted and #$CodingSequence-Uninterrupted.")
(comment InformationDesignation "All information has this attribute if the information has been designated or classified according to a recognized classification scheme.")
(comment NonCorporatePrivateInformationDesignation "A subcollection of #$InformationDesignation.  Instances of
this collection are attributes of information.  Specifically, they are designations of information 
that are recognized and used by non-corporate, non-governmental private organizations.
Non-corporate, non-governmental private organizations might be
clubs such as the Boy Scouts of America, or other existing social entities having 
members and criteria for membership.")
(comment GovernmentInformationDesignation "A subcollection of #$InformationDesignation.  Instances of
this collection are attributes of information. Specifically, they are
designations or classifications of information according to a scheme that is, or could be, used by a 
government (not limited to the US government) to specify the conditions under which the information 
of interest or use to them can be accessed.")
(comment GovernmentAffiliatedInformationDesignation "A subcollection of #$InformationDesignation.  Instances of
this collection are attributes of information.  Specifically, they are designations of information
that are, or could be, used by governments, coalitions that are formed between governments, or 
between a government and other political groups to specify the conditions under which information of interest 
or use to them can be accessed.  For instance, if a government contracted with the political group
Amnesty International for a foreign (or domestic) operation that required access to protected information,
they might designate such information in order to manage and enforce its application, accessibility, and use.
In this case, the designations of the information would be instances of #$GovernmentAffilitatedInformationDesignation. 
Note that the designation scheme might borrow from preexisting schemes that the government might already use, although it
could also include novel naming conventions to prevent mis-application and confusion about the scope of the
designations: 'Classified-AmnestySecret', 
for instance.")
(comment EmploymentStatusType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of this collection is a collection of agents that represents a particular employment status with respect to a particular organization.  For instance, (#$EmployeeFn #$MobilCorporation) is the collection of employees of Mobil Corporation and represents the property of \"being an employee of Mobil Corporation\".")
(comment AgentTypeByInterAgentRelation "Each instance of this collection is a collection of agents with a particular feature.  This feature is usually the result of being involved in a certain type of relationship with some agent, but that needn't be the case.  For example, #$SupportOfSponsorshipOfTerrorism is the collection of instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity that support (in some way) terrorism.  Most instances of this collection are instances by virtue of their support of some particular agents or group of agents.  But, it is conceivable that an agent could be an instance of this collection by virtue of their willingness to support terrorism in general and not by their support of some terrorist group in particular.   ")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain PersonTypeByPersonalAttribute)
(disjointWith ObjectTypeBySurfaceFeature PersonTypeByPersonalAttribute)
(comment PersonTypeByPersonalAttribute "A collection of collections.  Each instance of this collection is a collection of persons with a particular personal attribute such as having a particular gender or being bilingual.")
(comment PersonTypeByPersonalBehavioralAttribute "A specialization of #$PersonTypeByPersonalAttribute.  Each instance of #$PersonTypeByPersonalBehavioralAttribute is a collection of persons that share a particular personal behavioral attribute that pertains to his or her social and personal behavior.  Some of these attributes are related (explicitly or implicitly) to a particular social relationship of the person, while others are qualities attributed to the individual which get their social significance by implicit reference to a social group.")
(disjointWith PersonTypeByEducationLevel PersonTypeByActivity)
(comment PersonTypeByPersonalRelationshipStatus "The collection of attributes that have to do with the description and character of human social relationships.")
(comment PersonTypeByMentalFeature "Each instance of #$PersonTypeByMentalFeature is a collection of agents which represent a particular type of mental state or ability.  The instances of this collection are to be distinguished from instances of #$MentalQuantity.  The latter consists of properties that admit of dense degrees such as the instances of #$IntelligenceLevel.")
(disjointWith SpatialThingTypeByGenericLocation RelationshipType)
(disjointWith SpatialThingTypeByGenericLocation QuantityType)
(disjointWith PhysicalQualityOfTangibleOnly AccountTypeByStatus)
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain AccountTypeByStatus)
(comment AccountTypeByStatus "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$AccountTypeByStatus is the collection of accounts with a particular type of status such as #$PaidInFull, #$AccountInactive etc..  These collections represent the property the account has when it has that status.")
(comment GraphicalStructureType "A collection of collections.  This collection consists of various collections that represent different types of document/list structures.")
(comment TextStringTypeByReferenceType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$TextStringTypeByReferenceType is a collection of instances of #$TextString that share a certain type of reference feature.  These collections can be used to characterize, by function or purpose, a particular #$TextString that is a reference (see #$referenceInWorkTo) and that is contained within (see #$containsStructure-CW) a particular #$StructurallySpecificWork.  For example, a citation within an structurally specific instance of #$Bibliography can be characterized by #$BibliographicReference.  See #$textStringIsReferenceTypeInWork.")
(comment Designation "A very general attribute.  Any thing that has been designated or that has an assigned classification in some recognized classification scheme will have a #$Designation.")
(comment BidTypeByStatus "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$BidTypeByStatus is a collection of bids/proposals that have a particular feature.")
(comment MunsellHue "A collection of attributes of colors used in the Munsell system of color classification. #$MunsellHue, together with #$MunsellChroma and #$MunsellBrightness, comprise three dimensions which are adequate for the classification of colors. In Cyc, #$MunsellHue is partitioned into ten groups, each of which is associated with a family of colors (see, for example, #$MunsellRedHue). #$MunsellHues within a particular group are denoted by means of a functional expression that corresponds to that group (e.g. for #$MunsellRedHue there is #$MunsellRedFn).")
(comment MunsellPurpleHue "A range of MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$StrongViolet-Color to a hue possessed by #$PalePurple-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true purple' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellPurpleFn.")
(comment MunsellPurpleBlueHue "A range of MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$BrilliantBlue-Color to a hue possessed by #$BrilliantViolet-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true violet' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellPurpleBlueFn.")
(comment MunsellBlueHue "A range of MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$VeryDarkGreenishBlue-Color to a hue possessed by #$VividBlue-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true blue' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellBlueFn.")
(comment MunsellBlueGreenHue "A range of MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$DarkGreenishGray-Color to a hue possessed by #$VividBluishGreen-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true cyan' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellBlueGreenFn.")
(comment MunsellGreenHue "A range of #$MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$VeryLightYellowishGreen-Color to a hue possessed by #$BlackishGreen-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true green' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellGreenFn.")
(comment MunsellGreenYellowHue "A range of #$MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$PaleYellowishGreen-Color to a hue possessed by #$VeryDeepYellowishGreen-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true green-yellow' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellGreenYellowFn.")
(comment MunsellYellowHue "A range of #$MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$BrilliantOrangeYellow-Color to a hue possessed by #$BrilliantGreenishYellow-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true yellow' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellYellowFn.")
(comment MunsellYellowRedHue "A range of #$MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$GrayishReddishOrange-Color to a hue possessed by #$LightOrangeYellow-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true orange' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellYellowRedFn.")
(comment MunsellRedHue "A range of #$MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$VividPink-Color to a hue possessed by #$VividReddishOrange-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true red' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellRedFn.")
(comment MunsellRedPurpleHue "A range of MunsellHues, extending from a hue possessed by #$PurplishGray-Color to a hue possessed by #$VividPurplishRed-Color, with a hue possessed by 'true magenta' at the midpoint between the extremes. Elements of this collection are denoted by NATs formed with #$MunsellRedPurpleFn.")
(comment AgentTypeByModeOfTravel "The collection of collections that characterize the means of

travel available to a particular agent in a particular

situation. Each instance is the collection of agents haveing that particular mode available.  Being an instance of an instance of #$AgentTypeByModeOfTravel does not commit one to

travelling by that mode. Rather, it simply means one has the option of

so travelling and is disposed to, if travelling is going to be done,

to travel by this mode.")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain OrganismTypeByExistentialStatus)
(comment PageNumberingSchema "A collection of page numbering attributes; that is,
attributes describing the way the pages of texts are numbered.  Two of
the most notable instances are #$SimpleSequentialSchema,
#$RepetitionBySubsectionSchema, and the
#$SequentialWithPriviledgedSubsectionsSchema.")
(comment TangibleObjectTypeByGenericDamage-NonScalar "A collection of collections.  Each instance of this collection is a collection of #$PartiallyTangibles that are damaged in a similar sort of way.  These collections represent damage of a generic nature such as #$Torn and #$Burnt.")
(comment DeviceTypeByDamage "A collection of collections.  Instances of #$DeviceTypeByDamage are collections of devices that each have a similar type of damage.  Some elements of #$DeviceTypeByDamage are: #$Torn, #$Bent, #$Burnt, and #$Fractured.")
(comment ConfigurationTypeComplexityByDegree "This collection of complexity attributes is the collection of complexity attributes distinguished by degree of complexity.")
(comment LoanAgreementTypeByFeature "The collection of qualities describing a #$LoanAgreement.")
(comment LoanAgreementTypeByUse "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$LoanAgreementTypeByUse is a collection of #$LoanAgreements that are to be put to some particular use, according to their terms.  For example, #$ConsolidationLoan is the collection of loans that must be used to consolidate several preexisting #$LoanAgreements held by the #$borrower.")
(comment MunsellChroma "The collection of degrees of chroma in the Munsell system of color classification.  Chroma, also known as saturation, is what gives a color its 'colorfulness'.  Colors with a low chroma are generally grayish or dull.  Colors with a high chroma are vivid, colorful.  Every chromatic color (i.e. colors that have a #$MunsellHue value) has a degree of chroma.  #$PureWhiteColor does not have any chroma value, nor does any shade of #$PureGrayColor.  #$PureBlackColor need not have chroma but regardless of hue or chroma, if a color has a brightness value of 0, it will be black.  Please also see #$SharedNoteOnMunsellSystemOfColorClassification.")
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain ConfigurationTypeByComplexity)
(comment ConfigurationTypeByComplexity "The collection of attributes held by configurations in virtue of their complexity.  This might be measured in terms of the difficulty of reconstructing or deconstructing the object holding the relevant attribute or in terms of the difficulty of explaining the structure of the objects configured in the situation.  Note that this is intended to hold of configurations because while we speak of complexity in other situations it is not clear that we are referring to the same thing, it is likely best to reify different complexity attributes to talk of other kinds of complexity, e.g., complexity of a math problem.")
(comment InsuranceTypeByFeature "A collection of collections.  Each instance is a collection of insurance policies or plans with a particular feature.  These collections can be used to describe aspects of insurance plans and insurance coverage.")
(comment InsuranceTypeByCoverage "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$InsuranceTypeByCoverage is a collection of insurance plans with the same degree of coverage.  These collections represent some of the common degrees of coverage.")
(comment ObjectTypeByIlluminationMode "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$ObjectTypeByIlluminationMode is a collection of things with a particular mode of illumination.  Example instances include: #$DirectIllumination, #$IndirectIllumination, #$ReflectedIllumination, #$PartDirectPartIndirect.")
(comment ComplianceStatusAttribute "This attribute is usually asserted of objects or events insofar as they comply with some standard or rule.  Typically, attributes of this type would be involved in three-way relations between an object or event and some standard.  Something may have the attribute of #$Compliant with respect to one standard and be #$NonCompliant with respect to another standard.  See #$complianceStatus.")
(comment PersonTypeByClothingState "A collection of collections.  Each element of #$PersonTypeByClothingState is a collection of persons that share a particular state of dress (or undress).  Example elements of #$PersonTypeByClothingState are #$BarefootPerson, #$ToplessPerson, and #$FullyClothed.  See also #$dressState.")
(comment MunsellBrightness "The collection of degrees of brightness with respect to colors.  In the Munsell system of color classification, every color has a brightness value, or a range of brightness values, which expresses the degree of brightness, or lightness, of that color.  Instances of #$MunsellBrightness are denoted by means of the expression #$MunsellBrightnessFn.  Generally, darker colors have a low degree of brightness, while lighter colors have a high degree of brightness.  #$PureBlackColor has a brightness of 0.  #$PureWhiteColor has a brightness of 100.  Please also see #$SharedNoteOnMunsellSystemOfColorClassification.")
(disjointWith AccountTypeByStatus DeviceTypeByState)
(disjointWith DeviceTypeByState JustificationStrength)
(disjointWith PersonTypeByPersonalBehavioralAttribute DeviceTypeByState)
(disjointWith RegionTypeByTerrain DeviceTypeByState)
(disjointWith BLOTypeByPhysiology DeviceTypeByState)
(comment ComputerSoftwareTypeByFeature "Opaque features that software can have.  These are 'opaque' because it is too difficult to express what they do exactly.")
(comment SoftwareTypeByInterfaceFeature "The collection of features that can be present

    in any particular computer interface.  These features describe the interface

    in terms of how it will be perceived by the user.")
(comment PrintedStyleOfWriting "Writing in which characters are printed according to some sort of standard. Unlike the #$CursiveStyleOfWriting, in printed writing, characters tend to be separated from one another by whitespace, since the writing implement is lifted off the page frequently.")
(comment PersonByStudentStatus "A collection of attributes.  Each #$PersonByStudentStatus indicates the currency or bureaucratic phase of processing a student's participation in an educational course or institution; elements of this collection include #$Graduate, #$Enrolled, etc.")
(comment IndividualTypeByFlaw "The collection of flaw types used to specify a particular kind of flaw that something has.  These can be specified relative to roles or agent perspectives.")
(disjointWith ObjectTypeBySensibleFeature ArtifactType)
(disjointWith ObjectTypeBySensibleFeature PersonTypeByActivity)
(comment Odor "An odor is a quality that a tangible object or piece of free space may have, or an agent may detect.  The predicates #$objectEmitsOdor, #$placeHasOdor, #$smells can map from those individuals to an Odor respectively.  All odors have a projection onto magnitude axis.")
(disjointWith Odor Texture)
(disjointWith ObjectTypeByTaste Texture)
(disjointWith Odor ObjectTypeByTaste)
(disjointWith AccountTypeByStatus PartiallyTangibleTypeByPhysicalStructuralFeature)
(comment BedTypeBySize "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$BedTypeBySize is a collection of
instances of #$Bed-PieceOfFurniture each classified a certain size.  Instances include: 
#$KingSizedBed, #$QueenSizedBed, #$FullSizedBed, and #$TwinSizedBed.  One can specify the size of a
particular bed with the predicate #$sizeOfBed.")
(comment AssociateLevel "The educational attribute for anyone who has an #$AssociatesDegree but no Bachelor's degree")
(comment CoalitionClassificationUS-TopSecretInformation "An attribute, an instance of #$AttributeValue. This attribute is our representation of the US government
classification 'top secret' that applies to information that is accessible only to members of a coalition with the US that
have top secret security clearance.  See #$SecurityClearance for details.")
(comment Hi-Fi "An attribute of audio or audio-visual components or systems. A hi-fi component or system is capable of recording, processing, or reproducing sound signals that are relatively close to the original recorded sound, compared to its non-hi-fi counterparts.")
(comment FixedTimeLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$FixedTimeLoan if it is made with a fixed #$agreementMaturity.  (#$loanTermOptions LOAN FixedTimeLoan) means that LOAN will become due and payable at a time specified in the original terms.")
(comment Inflamed "inflamed body parts are hotter than normal and may appear red.")
(comment Classic "The attribute that describes those things that have a formal, traditional, balanced style; which adhere to high standards and principles; which have been evaluated and are considered standard and authoritative.  Classics maintain their standing as models of their kind over time.")
(disjointWith Classic Experimental)
(comment Orangeish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$OrangeColor.")
(comment OnSale-Discounted "An attribute for #$Buying events where there is a reduction           in the cost of goods that you are buying.")
(comment GreekArchitecturalStyle "The #$GreekArchitecturalStyle is a building style developed in the #$AncientGreece, with wide use of columns and pediments.  It was later adopted and modified in the #$RomanArchitecturalStyle.  See also #$NeoclassicArchitecturalStyle and #$RomanesqueArchitecturalStyle.")
(comment YellowColor "The color yellow.")
(comment Virgo-TheSign "The sixth sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Virgo, or The Virgin. Ruled by the #$PlanetMercury. #$Persons born between August 23 and September 22 have this sign.")
(comment GassyIntestine "Discomfort caused by having too much gas in the intestine.")
(comment BaroqueArchitecturalStyle "The #$BaroqueArchitecturalStyle is an ornate style of building developed in Europe after #$TheRenaissance, that became dominant in Europe in the 17th and 18th Centuries.  It is characterized by the use of rich ornament and embellishments, broken lines, and Greek and Roman style details.")
(disjointWith NonViolentAction ViolentAction)
(comment ElegantClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present an elegant image.")
(comment RomanticMode "The collection of instances of #$CWInstantiating that present instances of #$Narratives in a romantic way or mode.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Cyberpunk "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the cyberpunk style or genre.")
(comment Taurus-TheSign "The second sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Taurus, or The Bull. Ruled by #$PlanetVenus. #$Persons born between April 20 and May 20 have this sign.")
(comment NauticalClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present a nautical image.")
(comment LowLevelProgram "The collection of low level computer programs.  Instances of this collection are usually those programs which are not used

    directly by users but are instead used by the processes which run in an automated

    fashion on a computer.")
(comment LegsStraddlingPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in the posture of having their legs spread enough such that another object may fit between two adjacent legs.  For example one has this posture when riding a horse, motorcycle, etc..  We are currently (March 97) agnostic as to whether this applies only to human's or to creatures with more than two legs as well. ")
(comment MusicalComposition-Christian "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the christian style or genre.")
(comment LyingOnBackPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in the posture of lying on their backs.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Fusion "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the fusion jazz style or genre.")
(comment CupSize-D " The collection of people with female breast cup size D.")
(comment TimeAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$TimeAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of linear time.")
(comment ClassicCarLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$ClassicCarLoan (#$loanDedication LOAN #$ClassicCarLoan) just when it is a loan for the purpose of buying a previously owned ``classic'' automobile for personal use.  The funds borrowed are supposed to be used solely for this purpose.  Such a loan is secured by the #$Automobile purchased.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Soul "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"soul\" style or genre.")
(comment Elastic "has greater than Inelastic Elasticity")
(comment Bumpy "An attribute for describing surfaces which are bumpy, not flat.")
(comment LivingThingsAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$LivingThingsAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of a physical universe pretty much like the one we know, plus the existence of living things pretty much like the ones known to have existed on Earth.")
(comment PersonalLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$PersonalLoan (#$loanDedication LOAN #$PersonalLoan) just when it is a loan of money that is supposed to be used for personal reasons other than home improvement, consolidation, automobile purchase, or student expenses.  Typical uses for such loans are for vacations or for emergencies such as medical expenses.  Such a loan is typically unsecured.")
(comment Secret-ReportClassification "A report classification used by branches of the US  government.  'Secret' reports are to be viewed by a restricted  audience.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Jazz "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the jazz style or genre.")
(comment Private-Rank "The lowest standard military rank in an Army or Marine Corps.  The rank of the basic 'troops'.  A private is not an officer.

Immediately below #$PrivateFirstClass-Rank.")
(comment MusicalComposition-PopRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the pop rock style or genre.")
(comment DragAndDrop "The ability to indicate the obvious user interface operation on an iconic object by dragging it with a mouse from one point on the screen to another and dropping it there.")
(disjointWith NonFluidlikeTangibleThing FluidTangibleThing)
(comment TimerDoneState "Timers enter this state automatically from the #$TickingState.")
(comment FirstLieutenant-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$SecondLieutenant-Rank and #$Captain-Rank in the U.S. Army and in the U.S. Marine Corps.  In the U.S. Army, a First Lieutenant typically commands an instance of #$Platoon-UnitDesignation.")
(comment MusicalComposition-BluesRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the blues rock style or genre.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-InteractiveFiction "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram where, like in Myst and Riven, a major feature of them is that the player interacts with a fictional
environment, thereby, in a sense, creating their own story.")
(comment Stretched "The collection of instances of #$PartiallyTangible that have been stretched.  Something has been stretched if it has been pulled out of shape by some force.  This force has caused the thing to lengthen or widen in some way.  Often, things in this condition are structurally damaged in some way.")
(comment RestrictedInformation "#$RestrictedInformation is information that is the legal or assumed property of an individual or group and whose unauthorized distribution or availability is not allowed, presumably because it has value to the individual or group and its value would diminish if distributed or made available to others. Note that #$RestrictedInformation will usually also be #$ProtectedInformation, but this is not necessarily the case.  A piece of paper with highly confidential information may be left unprotected, and some irrelevant or extraneous information may be protected inadvertently, or as a consequence of protecting other restricted information in its proximity.")
(comment PlatinumColor "The color platinum")
(comment GunnerySergeant-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$StaffSergeant-Rank and 
  #$MasterSergeant-Rank in the #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps.")
(comment MusicalComposition-HeavyMetal "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the heavy metal style or genre.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-DrivingSimulation "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram in which a major feature of the game is that the player is depicted as driving
a vehicle. In part, the point of such games is that they simulate the experience
of driving in ways not typical of one's daily commute. They often also have a violent component akin to shooter games.
A prime example of both features is Carmageddon.")
(comment Pileable "The collection of #$PartiallyTangible stuff that can be formed into a pile.  If some stuff is pileable, pieces of that stuff stick to each other well enough that they don't slide or roll off of each other and can form piles.  Any liquid is not pileable. All granular substances are pileable.  Large bunches of larger objects are probably also pileable, even though single rigid objects are not.  E.g. a single potato is not pileable, but a group of potatoes usually is (unless they are extremely greasy).")
(comment Encrypted "The attribute describing the security level of objects protected by encryption, most typically instances of #$InternetCommunication.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-BasketballGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
basketball genre. #$Basketball-TheGame is prominently featured in works of this 
genre.  #$WhiteMenCantJump-TheMovie is an example of a movie in this genre.     ")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Arcade "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which are similar to or copies of arcade games.  Instances of this collection will most likely also be instances of another computer game genre.")
(comment TopSecretInformation "All #$Classification-US information that receives the classification 'TopSecret'.  The unauthorized disclosure of any instance of #$SecretInformation 'could reasonably be expected  to cause exceptionally grave damage to the national security (quote from Denning, 'Information Warfare and Security').")
(comment Amphitrichous "A collection of #$Bacterium that have 2 flagella which are on opposite sides of the cell.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-FamilyGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the
family genre.  Instances of this genre are intended to be suitable for children and adults.
<p>")
(comment NoOdor "The attribute of having no particular odor. This is useful for talking about products which are intentionally unscented.")
(comment Interrupted-SequentialStructure "A chain-like object has the attribute
#$Interrupted-SequentialStructure if there is some relevant qualitative
discontinuity in its sequential structure. The object need not be broken or
disconnected, but it must consist of identifiably heterogenous
segments. Examples: a chain some of whose links are tied up with a piece of
cord, a sequence of nucleotides of type X interspersed with nucleotides of
type Y, the entire southbound lane ribbon of interstate 71 between
Cleveland and Columbus with a road-work detour through Bellville,
OH. Opposite: #$Uninterrupted-SequentialStructure.")
(comment InjuryOutcome "Necessary constraints on the user in a situation. If such a constraint doesn't hold and the situation is one that can lead to injury, that increases the liklihood the user will be injured.")
(comment Engaged "The attribute of being engaged to be married.")
(comment InBloom "The collection of instances of #$Plant that are in one of the various stages of bearing flowers.  This includes plants that are budding.")
(comment Fantasy-PictorialGenre "A collection of conceptual works.  Each instance of this collection is a pictorial work of fantasy.  Included are the conceptual works corresponding to paintings of mythological scenes, scenes on alien planets, fantastic monsters, mythological or fictional characters and creatures, etcetera.")
(comment ClosedForBusiness "The collection of instances of #$LocalOrganization that are not open for business.  Uses of this collection should be temporally qualified.")
(comment ManMadeThing "The collection of things that are \"man-made.\"  A thing

  is an instance of #$ManMadeThing if it is an #$Artifact

   that is the #$outputsCreated of a #$CreatingAnArtifact

  event #$performedBy #$IntelligentAgents or

   #$Groups thereof.  To ascribe \"artificiality\"

   to #$Events or processes or other non-artifacts,

  use the more general #$NonNaturalThing.")
(comment CodingSequence-Interrupted "The characteristic attribute of a genetic coding sequence that is

interrupted by introns.  See #$Intron-MolecularSegment, and

#$Intron-GIS.")
(comment MultilingualAgent "A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$MultilingualAgent is an agent who is fluent in two or more #$NaturalLanguages.")
(comment CallLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$CallLoan (#$loanTermOptions LOAN #$CallLoan) just when it can be terminated at any time by the choice of lender (#$creditor) or of #$borrower.")
(comment Greenish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$GreenColor.")
(comment ForSale "An attribute for #$Buying events. (#$salesObjectStatus BUYING #$ForSale) means that the object(s) bought during BUYING were for sale at the start of BUYING.")
(comment CastleArchitecturalStyle "The #$CastleArchitecturalStyle is a style of building that became popular in Europe in #$TheMiddleAges for castles up through the early 19th Century, and which was used for some other buildings.  It is characterized by towers, turrets, crenellated stone walls and battlements, drawbridges, moats, loopholes and armorial decor.")
(comment Injured "Having some unspecified injury.  This makes it easier to state that someone is injured without saying '#$thereExists some instance of Injury which they have', which will bogusly skolemize a injury.")
(comment Aquarius-TheSign "The eleventh sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Aquarius, or The Water-Bearer. Ruled by the #$PlanetUranus. #$Persons born between January 20 and February 19 have this sign.")
(comment GothicArchitecturalStyle "The #$GothicArchitecturalStyle is a building style developed in Europe in the #$TheMiddleAges that became dominant after the heyday of the #$RomanesqueArchitecturalStyle.  It is characterized by the use of pointed arches (unlike the rounded arches of the #$RomanesqueArchitecturalStyle), rose windows, flying buttresses, etc.  The most famous examples are European cathedrals.")
(comment OrangeColor "The color orange")
(comment ChicClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present a chic image.  Since what is \"chic\" changes through time, assertions employing this constant should be temporally qualified.")
(comment Mirrorlike "Like the way a mirror or flat water at certain angles will reflect.")
(comment MelodramaticMode "The collection of instances of #$CWInstantiating that present or instantiate instances of #$Narratives in a way or mode that involves exaggerated shows of emotion.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Experimental "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the experimental style or genre.")
(comment Murky "Substances which are Murky can just barely be seen through.")
(comment SystemProgramTypeByFeature "Instances are collections of software tools used to facilitate the development or execution of other programs.")
(comment DualIndexedLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$DualIndexedLoan (#$loanInterestOptions LOAN #$DualIndexedLoan) just when the #$interestRate for the loan varies with shifts in the market interest rate, and the #$borrower's monthly payment fluctuates with wage changes.")
(comment KneelingPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in a kneeling posture.")
(comment MusicalComposition-AlternativeCountry "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the alternative country style or genre.  An example of a performer that plays this genre of music is #$KDLang.")
(disjointWith WhiteCollarWorker BlueCollarWorker)
(comment RecliningPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in a reclining posture.")
(comment Imprinted "This is the attribute things have when letters or other

patterns have been formed

on their surface by some device which makes indentations or, in the

case of paper items, canal-like deformations.")
(comment Opaque "#$Opaque is a #$PhysicalAttribute representing a specific degree of #$Transparency.  #$Opaque objects do not transmit light.  See also #$transparencyOfObject.")
(disjointWith Murky Opaque)
(comment ModernDevelopedRegionEverydayLifeAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$ModernDevelopedRegionEverydayLifeAnthropacity) means that MT assumes human history has unfolded as we know it, and that people are `around', sane, , and engaged in behavior that is ordinary -- `typical everyday life' -- for a more technologically developed region of the present (#$Now, close of the 20th century) world.")
(comment NewCarLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$NewCarLoan (#$loanDedication LOAN #$NewCarLoan) just when it is a loan for the purpose of buying a new automobile for personal use.  The funds borrowed are supposed to be used solely for this purpose.  Such a loan is secured by the automobile purchased.")
(comment MusicalComposition-NewAge "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the new age style or genre.")
(comment SimilarPhysicalLawsAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$SimilarPhysicalLawsAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of a physical universe in which the physical categories and forces we take for granted (i.e., 4-dimensional space-time, gravity, inertia, etc.) seem to hold.")
(comment Smooth "Smooth.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Techno "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"techno\" style or genre.")
(comment WindowsEnhancedMode "The enhanced operating mode of microsoft windows")
(comment Hollow "The volume of the main cavity in a hollow thing is so big that it takes up a significant portion of the volume of the hollow object when that object is viewed from the outside.  An unopened eggshell is considered to be #$Hollow in spite being full of its contents.  A sponge would not be considered #$Hollow because no invidual cavity is of significant size relative to that of the sponge.")
(comment LieutenantGeneral-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$MajorGeneral-Rank and

#$General-Rank in the U.S. Army and in the U.S. Marine Corps.  In the U.S. Army, a Lieutenant General typically

commands an instance of #$Corps-UnitDesignation.")
(comment MusicalComposition-AlternativeRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the alternative rock style or genre.")
(comment MultimediaAudio "The ability of the software to play multimedia music")
(comment TrapTripped "The trap has been sprung or tripped.  While in this state it may not be tripped again.")
(disjointWith Raw FermentedStuff)
(comment ChiefWarrantOfficer-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$WarrantOfficer-Rank and 
  #$SecondLieutenant-Rank in the #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps.")
(comment MusicalComposition-BritishFolkRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the British folk rock style or genre.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Basketball "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which simulate basketball.")
(comment Deformable "Things which are #$Deformable will give way to some degree when force is applied.  They may or may not spring back to shape after the pressure is released. ")
(comment GoldColor "The color gold")
(comment AuthenticationProtected "A specialization of #$LimitedAccess, #$AuthenticationProtected is the instance of #$IndividualTypeBySecurityStatus that applies to individuals which, to access, one must participate in an instance of            #$AuthenticationEvent.")
(comment Sergeant-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$Corporal-Rank and

#$SergeantFirstClass-Rank in the U.S. Army, and between #$Corporal-Rank and #$StaffSergeant-Rank in the U.S. Marine Corps.  A U.S. Army Sergeant may command an instance of either #$Squad-UnitDesignation or #$Platoon-UnitDesignation.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-HistoricalFictionGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
historical fiction genre.  Works that are set in the past which utilize many actual 
historical facts to build a framework for a fictional story.        ")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-BoardGame "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which are based upon board games such as #$Risk-TheBoardGame. The computer
versions of board games often allow a single player to play against computer
generated opponents or against online opponents.")
(comment BulkState "The physical state of being in bulk, as, for example, a bunch of sand in a sandbox or a liquid pooled in a container")
(comment ProtectedBySecrecy "The attribute describing the security level of those objects protected by virtue of their being secret.  Includes secret messages, secret meetings, secret passageways, secret transfers, etc.  Generally, to people who should have access, these objects are not secret, while to others, they are.  Thus #$ProtectedBySecrecy is a kind of #$LimitedAccess.")
(disjointWith AnaerobicBLO AerobicBLO)
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-SatireGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the
satire genre.  A genre in which the intent is to use irony or sarcasm to make fun of certain
aspects of people, places or things often with the understanding that these things are silly
in some way.
<p>")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Family "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram  designed
to be played by a whole family or by all but infant members of a family.")
(disjointWith DirectIllumination IndirectIllumination)
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-EroticDramaGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
erotic drama genre.        ")
(comment BodyOdor "The foul odor that sweating or otherwise dirty human bodies give off.")
(comment MillenniumBug "The collection of instances of #$ComputerProgramCopy that possess the millennium bug.  The millennium bug is the much hyped software-bug which many people thought would cause computer systems running old (mainly #$Cobol) #$ComputerProgram-CWs to fail once internal computer clocks rolled over into the new millenium at midnight, December 31st 1999. Also known as the Year-2000 (or Y2K) problem. The bug arises from an early programming need to conserve memory resources by allocating only two characters to the year components of date strings (e.g., '76'), meaning that such systems will perceive the year AD 2000 to be the year AD 00.")
(comment Documentary-Genre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork 
that document, usually as they occur, real life events.                          ")
(comment RepetitionBySubsectionSchema "A page numbering schema for instances of
#$SpecifiedInformationBearingThingType that are specifications of
#$TextualMaterial.  A text that has the #$RepetitionBySubsectionSchema
is divided into several formally delineated subsections, such as
chapters or subsections, with each subsection exhibiting its own
internal sequential numbering scheme.")
(disjointWith Threatened-OrganismTypeByExistentialThreatLevel NonEndangered-OrganismTypeByExistentialThreatLevel)
(comment ImprovedSurface-PathArtifact "#$ImprovedSurface-PathArtifact is the collection of instances of #$PathArtifact with an existing but relatively elementary construction. The

surface is made of stabilized or oiled-surface gravel or stone, graded and

drained gravel or stone, or graded and drained soil. For instance, in the

United States, #$ImprovedSurface-PathArtifact will apply to hard-surface roads

less  than two lanes wide, or private roads which normally are not used for

the rerouting of traffic in emergencies. Source: US Army Field Manual, FM

21-31.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-ShortGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
\"short\" genre.  The term \"short\" is usually applied to films that are significantly shorter than the average feature length film.        ")
(disjointWith UndergraduateLevel BachelorOfArtsLevel)
(disjointWith GraduateLevel BachelorOfArtsLevel)
(comment Nauseated "The queasy feeling in the abdomen and throat which precedes vomiting.  See also #$Nausea.")
(comment Whiteish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$WhiteColor.")
(comment Incapacitated "Severely injured to the point of losing most capabilities.")
(comment Purplish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$PurpleColor.")
(comment SaleReturned "The items sold were returned")
(comment PostmodernArchitecturalStyle "The #$PostmodernArchitecturalStyle is a style of building design that became popular in the late 20th Century.  It is characterized by the addition of simple visible features (to what would otherwise be in the #$ModernMinimalistArchitecturalStyle), often self-conciously parodying or referring to classic or other motifs (like pediments) without any effort to reproduce them faithfully.  Simplicity of geometric appearance, or color,  may be sacrificed somewhat in order to make some 'statement'.  It began to supplant the #$ModernMinimalistArchitecturalStyle before the turn of the millennium.")
(comment FullFigured "An attribute appropriate for clothing specifically sized for full-figured women.")
(comment Chronic "The state of being chronically afflicted by some type of ailment.")
(comment Sagittarius-TheSign "The ninth sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Sagittarius, or The Archer. Ruled by the #$PlanetJupiter. #$Persons born between November 23 and December 21 have this sign.")
(comment CurrentlyIncapableOfGoalSuccess "(#$goalSituationStatus GOAL-SITUATION
#$CurrentlyIncapableOfGoalSuccess) means that the state of affairs in
the current microtheory is such that what is desired in GOAL-SITUATION
is not currently capable of being realized.  \"Currently\" is defined
as the #$mtTime of the current mt.  For example, if Eric wishes to
ride on a train and is currently riding in an airplane, then
(#$goalSituationStatus `EricsWishForTrainRide'
#$CurrentlyIncapableOfGoalSuccess) is true.")
(comment FashionableStyle "A collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that are considered fashionable.  A item of clothing has this style if it is currently in style (in that context).   Because clothes go in and out of style, assertions employing this constant should be temporally qualified.")
(comment ComicalMode "The collection of instances of #$CWInstantiating that are instantiations of instances of #$Narrative in a way or mode intended to be comical.")
(comment Unvaccinated-Generic "The attribute of NOT having been vaccinated against some

particular #$AilmentCondition.  This is an unusual attribute--even

more unusual than its negation attribute, #$Vaccinated-AttributeValue.

 (#$hasAttributes ?ORG #$Unvaccinated-AttributeValue) means that the

 organism ?ORG is NOT vaccinated against some particular disease or

 virus.  Such formulas should only be created in specific contexts,

 where the #$AilmentCondition which the organism is not vaccinated

 against is identified.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Religious "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the religious style or genre.")
(comment Destroyed-ExistentialStatus "This instance of #$ConstructionArtifactTypeByExistentialStatus describes

instances of #$ConstructionArtifact that are destroyed, i.e., that cannot

fulfill its intended use due to constructive damage. To restore an instance

of #$ConstructionArtifact that has been destroyed would require not routine

maintenance but reconstruction.")
(comment AtSea "If an object or event is on a boat which is sailing on the open ocean, it is considered #$AtSea.  If a boat is on the open ocean, it is at sea.  If a sea battle is on the open ocean, it is at sea. Exemplar cases which may or may not be considered 'at sea' include boats on large lakes, people swimming in the ocean, submersed (see #$Underwater) submarines in the open ocean and the objects inside of them.  Perhaps these are exemplars of being 'at sea' to varying lower degrees of certainty compared with the positive exemplar cases.  See also #$Afloat.")
(comment FixedRateLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$FixedRateLoan (#$loanInterestOptions LOAN #$FixedRateLoan) just when the interest rate on it is fixed in the original terms.")
(comment StandingOnOneLegPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in the posture of being supported in an upright position by only (or mainly) one leg, as applies to many wading birds.")
(comment MusicalComposition-TraditionalPop "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the traditional pop style or genre.  These compositions are often instantiated by traditional pop music singers such as #$FrankSinatra.")
(comment Device-Overheated "The state of being at a much higher temperature than the device normally should be.")
(comment HomeLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$HomeLoan (#$loanDedication LOAN #$HomeLoan) when it is a loan for the purpose of purchasing a #$ModernHumanResidence.  Such loans are securitized by the residence that is purchased.")
(disjointWith LyingOnBackPosture PronePosture)
(comment MusicalComposition-Trance "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"trance\" style or genre.")
(comment Paved "Covered with solid concrete or asphalt.  Paved surfaces are hard and smooth.")
(comment HumanHistoryAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$HumanHistoryAnthropacity) means that MT assumes human history from human origins until the #$mtTime of the microtheory has unfolded as we know it.")
(comment AthleticPhysicalBuild "Good muscle tone and muscle definition, not fat, not skinny or emaciated.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Lounge "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"lounge\" style or genre.")
(comment RegisterArchitecture "The attribute which indicates that the computer's architecture is register based.")
(disjointWith LongThinObject SheetShapedObject)
(disjointWith LongThinObject Ball)
(comment FMState "The state that a radio is in while it is recieving signals on the FM dial.")
(disjointWith FMState AutoAMRadio)
(comment MathematicalUniverseAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$MathematicalUniverseAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of mathematical concepts such as #$Set-Mathematical, #$Collection, #$FunctionTheMathematicalType, #$Predicate, and #$ScalarInterval.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Electronic "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the electronic style or genre.")
(comment WindowsRealMode "The real operating mode of microsoft windows")
(comment Dull-NotShiny "#$Dull-NotShiny")
(comment PureBlackColor "The color that is void of any hue or chroma and has a brightness of 0.  Please note that this is different from #$BlackColor, in that #$PureBlackColor does not contain ANY hue or chroma, while #$BlackColor and its spec attribute may have some tinge of some hue.  Hence, #$PureBlackColor cannot have any spec attributes, while #$BlackColor can.")
(comment BrigadierGeneral-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$Colonel-Rank and

#$MajorGeneral-Rank in the U.S. Army and in the U.S. Marine Corps.  In the U.S. Army, a Brigadier General may command

an instance of #$Brigade-UnitDesignation or #$Regiment-UnitDesignation.")
(comment MusicalComposition-GrungeRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the grunge rock style or genre.")
(comment Straight "Not curved.")
(comment TrapArmed "The trap is armed and ready to be activated.")
(comment FleshWhiteColor "The color fleshWhite")
(comment Steamed "The attribute #$Steamed is a specialized form of #$Cooked.  Food that is #$Steamed has been prepared in an event of #$SteamingFood.")
(comment WeaklyEncrypted "The attribute of containing information that has been encrypted by means of some #$EncryptionAlgorithm which is not terribly difficult to break.  Something that is #$WeaklyEncrypted could be unencrypted (and the information therein contained thereby made accessible) within, say, a day.")
(comment MusicalComposition-ProgressiveRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the progressive rock style or genre.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Hockey "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which simulate hockey.")
(comment Puffy "having bulging, billowing, fluffy, inflated..the way cumulous clouds are.  This units definition could be improved upon.  As long the current assertions remain kosher, add your two cents.")
(comment BronzeColor "The color bronze")
(comment Classified "#$Classified is attribute anything has if it is classified. This attribute is normally associated with documents, computers, or physical facilities.")
(comment LanceCorporal-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$PrivateFirstClass-Rank and 
  #$Corporal-Rank in the #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-RomanceGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
romance genre.        ")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-FlyingSimulation "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram whose main feature is that
the player is depicted as flying some aircraft or spacecraft. What is simulated
is the experience of flying the craft.")
(comment Deceased "#$Deceased is a specialization of  #$AgentNowTerminated.  Each instance of #$Deceased is a dead, formerly living organism.  If the sentence, \"(#$isa ORG #$Deceased)\" is true, it means that ORG was once an organism (see #$Organism-Whole) which was alive and that ORG underwent death (see #$Dying) at some time prior to the time at which \"(#$isa ORG #$Deceased)\" is true.")
(comment NumberedList-ArabicNumerals "The collection of all instances of #$List-Information whose component list items (see #$itemInList-Information) are numerically and sequentially ordered, beginning at the number 1.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-ParodyGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
parody genre.  Works that make fun of or ridicule other works or things for the purposes
of satire or comedy appeal are often instances of this genre.  Harvard Lampoon's 
\"Bored Of The Rings\" is an example of a book in this genre.     ")
(comment DividedPath "#$DividedPath is the collection of roads that have a

median, i.e. with divided roadbeds.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-ClassicGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
classic genre.  This is not a \"true\" genre in that it doesn't describe the style of a
work.  Works thought to be classics (i.e. works that have stood the test of time) are 
instances of this genre.     ")
(comment FoulOdor "an odor which humans find unpleasant to smell")
(disjointWith NoOdor FoulOdor)
(comment MediumEarthOrbit "Satellites in #$MediumEarthOrbit (MEO) orbit the earth at a height of 5,000

to 10,000 miles.  MEO satellites cover much more of the earth's surface than

satellites in #$LowEarthOrbit, but not as much of the Earths's surface

as satellites in #$GeosynchronousEarthOrbit, and are often used in

conjunction with satellites in #$GeosynchronousEarthOrbit. Because they 

orbit the Earth in a lower orbit than GEO satellites, MEO satellites

offer clearer transmission and shorter transmission delay.")
(disjointWith ThreeLanesOrWiderRoadway TwoLaneRoadway)
(comment ArmExtendedPosition "The attribute of a body in which an arm is extended perpendicular to the torso.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-ComedyGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
comedy genre.  A very popular genre for movies.  Instances of this genre are designed to
be funny or humorous.       ")
(comment XRating "The #$MotionPictureAssociationOfAmerica has replaced

#$XRating with #$NC-17Rating. #$XRating is still applied to

#$Movie-CWs by parties other than the

#$MotionPictureAssociationOfAmerica because it helps sell #$Movie-CWs

with pornographic content. See the comment on #$NC-17Rating. #$XRating

only applies to #$Movie-CWs. If you're looking for a similar concept that

applies more generally, see #$PornographicMaterial.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-FantasyGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
fantasy genre.  #$RobertEHoward's stories about Conan the barbarian are examples of this
genre.          ")
(comment Bald "An attribute for describing the head's level of hair coverage: in this case.")
(disjointWith HumanWithMulletHairstyle Bald)
(comment LineOfCredit "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$LineOfCredit (#$loanTermOptions LOAN #$LineOfCredit) just when it is a loan that is valid for any amount up to a specified amount.  Money may be withdrawn on multiple occasions from the account at any time up to the due date.")
(comment Reddish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$RedColor.")
(comment SpanishColonialArchitecturalStyle "The #$SpanishColonialArchitecturalStyle is a building style first developed in the New World colonies of #$Spain, such as #$Mexico.   It uses stucco surfaces, arches and curved skylines, with small areas of ornament.  It is a popular style in the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.")
(disjointWith LitTooDarkForSeeing LitBrightEnoughForSeeing)
(comment PurpleColor "The color purple")
(comment Paraplegic "the attribute of being paralyzed in the lower half of one's body.")
(comment SaleItemsOnOrder "The items sold are on order")
(comment GettingWell "The state of getting well.")
(comment SexyClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present a sexy image.")
(disjointWith NotHungry ModeratelyFull)
(comment UsedArtifact "Used as an attribute of a product to indicate it's used. ")
(comment Healthy "The state of being healthy, i.e., not suffering from any type of
#$AilmentCondition.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Gothic "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the gothic style or genre.")
(disjointWith Destroyed-ExistentialStatus Operational-ExistentialStatus)
(comment Operational-ExistentialStatus "This instance of #$ConstructionArtifactTypeByExistentialStatus describes

instances of #$ConstructionArtifact that are operational, i.e., such that

they can fulfill their intended use.")
(disjointWith Afloat-OnWater Airborne)
(disjointWith LyingOnBackPosture LyingOnSidePosture)
(disjointWith PronePosture LyingOnSidePosture)
(comment MusicalComposition-Funk "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the funk music style or genre.")
(comment Fractured "The collection of instances of #$PartiallyTangible that have been cracked but not completely shattered.  Something that has been fractured or has a fractured part will often retain physical integrity, but will usually have its functionality impaired in some way.")
(disjointWith Device-Off Device-On)
(comment VioletColor "The color violet, also known as blue-purple or puple-blue.")
(comment CupSize-A "  The collection of people with female breast cup size A.")
(comment LexicalAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$LexicalAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of lexical concepts, but does not require

a physical or mathematical universe.")
(comment MarginAccount "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$MarginAccount (#$loanDedication LOAN #$MarginAccount) just when it is a loan for the purpose of buying a #$TradeableSecurity.  The funds borrowed are used soley for this purpose.  Such a loan is secured by the securities purchased.")
(disjointWith RecliningPosture UprightPosture)
(disjointWith KneelingPosture UprightPosture)
(comment MusicalComposition-Romantic "The collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition created during the \"romantic\" era.  This era is usually construed as the period of history from 1825-1900.")
(disjointWith Crushed-Fragmented GaseousTangibleThing)
(disjointWith Crushed-Fragmented LiquidTangibleThing)
(comment Dusty "if a surface is dusty, it is covered with dust.")
(comment HumanExistenceAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$HumanExistenceAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of a physical universe pretty much like the one we know, peopled by humans pretty much like the ones existing now.")
(disjointWith NotHungry Starving)
(comment Unclassified-ReportClassification "A report classification used by branches of the US  government.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Blues "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the blues style or genre.")
(disjointWith ExecutingStatus CancelledStatus)
(disjointWith Skirt-AnkleLength Skirt-KneeLength)
(comment Document-InPrint "Indicates that new copies of a given document are still being printed.")
(comment ContainsIncomparableScenes "This is the collection of #$ScriptedEventTypes that contain

(at least) two subevent types SCENE1 and SCENE2 such that none of the

relations expressed by the #$QuasiTemporalScriptPredicates holds between them.

This will be the true just in case the ScriptedEventType #$HasDisjunctiveBranching

or #$HasConjunctiveBranching.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Rock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the rock style or genre.")
(comment Multimedia "The ability of the software to take advantage of multimedia capabilities, namely animation, digital sound, and music")
(comment Auburn-HairColor "The human hair color auburn, a kind of reddish-brown.")
(disjointWith NeverSchooled ElementaryLevel)
(disjointWith KindergartenLevel ElementaryLevel)
(comment LieutenantColonel-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$Major-Rank and #$Colonel-Rank in the U.S. Army and in the U.S. Marine Corps.  In the U.S. Army, a Lieutenant Colonel usually commands an instance of #$Battalion-UnitDesignation.")
(comment MusicalComposition-RockAndRoll "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the rock and roll style or genre.")
(comment CopyProtectedProgram "please add more flesh to me.")
(disjointWith Foldable Shoe)
(disjointWith Foldable Helmet)
(disjointWith Foldable Crown)
(disjointWith BulkState Foldable)
(disjointWith Foldable StructuralSupportStuff)
(comment FleshRedColor "The color fleshRed")
(comment SergeantMajor-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$FirstSergeant-Rank and 
  #$MasterGunnerySergeant-Rank in the #$UnitedStatesMarineCorps.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Punk "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the punk rock style or genre.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Wrestling "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which simulate wrestling.")
(comment SensitiveButUnclassified "#$SensitiveButUnclassified is the security level of many military individuals, including military instances of #$LocalAreaNetwork.")
(comment Admiral-Rank "Supreme commander of a fleet.  Rank intermediate between vice-admiral and fleet admiral.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-HorrorGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the  horror genre.  #$JohnCarpenter-MovieDirector's #$Halloween-TheMovie is an example of a movie  in this genre.       ")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Golf "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which simulate golf.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-EducationalGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
educational genre.   #$SesameStreet-TheTVShow is a prime example of a T.V. show in 
this genre.    ")
(comment Ancient "This is the attribute of being from ancient times.")
(comment FairWeather-PathArtifact "#$FairWeather-PathArtifact is the collection of instances of 

#$PathForWheeledVehicles that become impassable for a potential user in

adverse weather and cannot be kept open by maintenace short of major

construction. Also called \"dry weather\".")
(comment RowID "A datatype internal to Oracle.")
(comment VegetativeCellState "A collection of bacteria in their
normal growing form that exhibit all of life's
characteristics, such as metabolism and reproduction. The only time the
#$Bacterium would not have the #$VegetativeCellState is when the cell
has become an instance of #$EndosporeState or has been killed.")
(comment GeosynchronousEarthOrbit "Most satellites orbit the earth in what is known as Geosynchronous Earth Orbit (GEO).
(Geosynchronous Earth Orbit is also known as the 'Clarke orbit', after author 
#$ArthurCClarke, who first proposed it in 1945.)  Satellites in 
#$GeosynchronousEarthOrbit maintain a period of rotation around the #$PlanetEarth
equal to the period of the rotation of the Earth itself, approximately 24 hours. 
Thus, satellites in #$GeosynchronousEarthOrbit remain stationary with respect to 
the surface of the Earth. All satellites in Clarke orbit are 
22,238 miles from the equator.")
(disjointWith JuniorHighLevel HighSchoolLevel)
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-AdultGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
adult genre.  Works in this genre are aimed at an audience of adults.       ")
(comment GoodMailingStatus "If an address has

          #$GoodMailingStatus, letters and packages posted to this address are

          not being returned. Note that this does not necessarily mean they

          are received by their intended recipients.")
(comment OnGroundSurface-PathArtifact "#$OnGroundSurface-PathArtifact is the collection of paths that are

located on ground, i.e. they are on the surrounding surface.")
(comment CoalitionClassificationUS-SecretInformation "An attribute, an instance of #$AttributeValue.  This attribute is our representation of the US government
classification 'secret' that applies to information that is accessible to members of a coalition with the US that 
have secret security clearance.  See #$SecurityClearance for details.")
(comment LitAtIndoorIlluminationLevel "The typical illumination level range of spaces inside houses.")
(comment Monophonic "An attribute of audio or audio-visual components or systems. Any #$Monophonic component or system is only capable of outputting a single channel of sound no matter how many #$AudioSpeakers it is attached to.")
(comment LegallyBankrupt "The state of being legally bankrupt (in the US, under Chapter 11 or Chapter 7 protection, etc.).")
(disjointWith FixedTimeLoan DemandLoan)
(comment DemandLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$DemandLoan (#$loanTermOptions LOAN #$DemandLoan) just when the loan is repayable on demand by the #$creditor (without prior notice) rather than on a specific date.  No #$DemandLoan is properly called a #$CallLoan, because that would imply that either the #$borrower or the #$creditor can end it.  Only a #$creditor can end a #$DemandLoan.")
(disjointWith FertileAnimal Infertile)
(comment Infertile "#$SexuallyMature, but inable to reproduce.")
(comment Yellowish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$YellowColor.")
(comment EgyptianArchitecturalStyle "The #$EgyptianArchitecturalStyle is a  style of building design developed in #$AncientEgypt for thousands of years, and it was ocassionally revived in the 17th through 20th Centuries in locations around the world. It is characterized by the use of carvings or other decorations with Egyptian-style motifs, symmetric arrangements of lotus-columns, massive forms with slanted walls or pyramids, or obelisks, and a lack of pediments or other Greek and Roman style details.")
(disjointWith CanopyClosure-Dense CanopyClosure-Congested)
(comment Scorpio-TheSign "The eighth sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Scorpio, or The Scorpion. Ruled by the #$PlanetPluto. #$Persons born between October 23 and November 22 have this sign.")
(comment RococoArchitecturalStyle "The #$RococoArchitecturalStyle is an ornate style of building and furniture design developed in Europe in the late 17th and 18th Centuries, at the end of the heyday of the #$BaroqueArchitecturalStyle.  It is characterized by the use of asymmetric airy embellishments, broken lines, and Greek and Roman style details, with light curvaceous flourishes.")
(comment NonModernClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that are considered old-fashioned.")
(comment Sick "The state of being sick.  This attribute is meant to refer to
actively experiencing the symptoms of a type of #$AilmentCondition.
Thus, someone who has a condition #$InRemission is not also #$Sick
with respect to that condition.  Someone who has a #$Chronic condition
may or may not be #$Sick.")
(comment Victorian "The collection of instances of #$ConstructionArtifact that are in the
victorian style.                            ")
(comment Gemini-TheSign "The third sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Gemini, or The Twins. Ruled by #$PlanetMercury. #$Persons born between May 21 and June 20 have this sign.")
(comment VictorianClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present a Victorian image.")
(disjointWith PartialCoverage NoCoverage)
(comment BootleggedArtifact "Something that is illegally made or illegally copied has this attribute.  Common bootlegged items are tapes, CD's, and liquor. Note that not all goods that have attribute bootleg are instances of #$Contraband since #$BootleggedArtifact tells us only about the legal status of the making or copying of a thing and nothing about where and how it can be legally distributed.")
(disjointWith SinglePaymentLoan InstallmentLoan)
(comment HandstandPosture "The posture in which an animal, usually a person,

supports himself with his hands on the ground.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Choral "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the choral music style or genre.")
(comment SingleStranded-MolecularStructure "#$SingleStranded-MolecularStructure is the structural attribute held by single stranded molecules.")
(comment LegallyDrunk "An instance of #$PersonTypeByPhysiologicalConditionType and a specialization of #$Drunk.  Each instance of #$LegallyDrunk is an instance of #$HomoSapiens who satisfies some applicable jurisdiction's legal definition of drunkenness.  Often this means exceeding some #$LegalBloodAlcoholLimit.")
(comment LimeGreenColor "The color lime green, also known as yellow-green or green-yellow.")
(disjointWith Airborne InOuterSpace)
(disjointWith Afloat-OnWater InOuterSpace)
(disjointWith AtSea InOuterSpace)
(comment CrouchingPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in a posture of stooping low to the ground with the knees bent.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Ska "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the ska style or genre.")
(comment PointyEnded "Includes anything which tapers to a pointy or sharp end--pens, pencils, needles, pins, nails, and wood-screws, but not wires (no taper).  Most knives would be included, but only because they have pointy ends.  A knife with a rounded ended and a sharp blade would not be included.")
(comment MagentaColor "The color magenta, also known as red-purple or purple-red.")
(disjointWith CupSize-D CupSize-C)
(comment CupSize-C " The collection of people with female breast cup size C.")
(comment IndustrialArtifactAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$IndustrialArtifactAnthropacity) means that MT assumes that various types of (normally human) artifacts -- whether or not produced by industrial processes -- exist.  The existance of #$Persons need not be recognized in the #$Microtheory.")
(comment MusicalComposition-RhythmAndBlues "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the rhythm and blues style or genre.")
(comment Wrinkled "Surfaces which are #$covers-Generic ridges, creases, folds.  Caused by aging, crumpling, folding, contraction or the like.")
(disjointWith Smooth Wrinkled)
(comment IntelligentAgentsAnthropacity "(#$mtAnthropacity MT #$IntelligentAgentsAnthropacity) means that MT assumes the existence of a physical universe pretty much like the one we know, plus the existence of intelligent agents (not necessarily living things).")
(comment CarLoan "A #$LoanAgreement is a #$CarLoan (#$loanDedication LOAN #$CarLoan) just when it is a loan for the purpose of buying an automobile for personal use.  The funds borrowed are supposed to be used soley for this purpose.  Such a loan is secured by the automobile purchased.")
(comment SCI-ReportClassification "A report classification used by branches of the US  government.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Rap "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the rap style or genre.")
(comment RISCArchitecture "A CPU architecture in which the number

 of instructions the processer can execute is minimized to improve processing 

speed (RISC stands for \"Reduced Instruction Set\"). It is contrasted with 

#$CISCArchitectures (or \"Complex Instruction Set\" architectures), of which 

Intel architectures are the most notable. RISC architectures include Sun's SPARC 

(#$SPARCProcessorArchitecture), Compaq's Alpha (#$AlphaProcessorArchitecture) 

and IBM's PowerPC (#$PowerPCArchitecture).")
(disjointWith Raw Pickled)
(disjointWith Skirt-MiniLength Skirt-KneeLength)
(disjointWith KindergartenLevel PreschoolLevel)
(disjointWith ElementaryLevel PreschoolLevel)
(comment RootPrivilege "An attribute of a #$ComputerProgramCopy running on a

#$ComputationalSystem with a #$Unix operating system. A program has

#$RootPrivilege if it is able to modify, create, or delete those files on the system that it is configured to access. For example, yppasswd will have #$RootPrivilege on the instance of #$PasswordFile in a #$Unix system. #$RootPrivilege allows a normal user to execute a program that requires root access.")
(comment MusicalComposition-SouthernRock "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the southern rock style or genre.")
(comment BatchProgram "A program which does not accept new inputs once execution begins, and for which intermediate states are unimportant.  Rather, only the input/output function computed is relevant.  Opposite of real-time.")
(comment Woven "Woven like fabric.")
(disjointWith CellularTangibleThing Woven)
(disjointWith BulkState Woven)
(comment Blonde-HairColor "The human hair color blonde, a kind of yellow or gold or very light brown.")
(disjointWith Grilled LiquidTangibleThing)
(comment Captain-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$FirstLieutenant-Rank and

#$Major-Rank in the U.S. Army and in the U.S. Marine Corps.  In the U.S.Army, a Captain usually commands an instance of #$Company-UnitDesignation or an instance of #$Battery-UnitDesignation with artillery.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Psychedelic "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the psychedelic music style or genre.")
(comment DevisedPracticeOrWork-RolePlayingGenre "The collection of instances of #$Game or #$ComputerGamePrograms which are role-playing games. In such games (#$DungeonsAndDragons is an example), the players assume roles and interact with other players as these characters.")
(comment Flaky-GranularPhysicalStructure "Objects having this physical structure consisting of a mob of separate flakes (particles having one dimension much smaller than the others) like snow or breakfast cereal.  To describe coherent objects which have a tendency to come apart into flakes when perturbed, see #$Flaky-CoherentPhysicalStructure.")
(disjointWith WellDrainedRegion SomewhatExcessivelyDrainedRegion)
(disjointWith ExcessivelyDrainedRegion SomewhatExcessivelyDrainedRegion)
(comment Olive-FleshColor "Skin color of medium lightness with yellow-green undertones.")
(disjointWith HighlyConfidential Secret)
(comment ProtectedInformation "#$ProtectedInformation is information that is protected from unauthorized use by active security measures, whether by physical containers (fences and safes), electronic security systems, or anything else that could reasonably constitute a form of security against unauthorized acquisition or access.")
(comment MusicalComposition-PostPunk "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the post punk music style or genre.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-Fighting "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram wherein the major (simulated) activity is fighting. All shooter games
are in this category, but many of these games involve (simulated) hand-to-hand
combat or combat with hand-held weapons other than guns. Non-shooter examples 
include games in the Virtua Fighter and Tekken series.")
(comment Curved "not #$Straight.")
(disjointWith Straight Curved)
(comment GeneralRating "The designation of an instance of IBO as being appropriate for a general audience (i.e. persons of all ages).")
(disjointWith ParentalGuidanceRating GeneralRating)
(comment PasswordProtected "The attribute describing the security level of objects protected by a password system.  These can range from computer passwords to passwords needed to enter a secret fortress.")
(comment SergeantFirstClass-Rank "Rank intermediate between #$Sergeant-Rank and
#$MasterSergeant-Rank in the U.S. Army.")
(comment ComputerGameProgram-CardGame "The collection of instances of #$ComputerGameProgram which are based on card games. Often the computer versions allow a single
player to play against one or more computer-generated opponents or to play against
online opponents.")
(comment PartDirectPartIndirect "(#$lightSource ?obj ?source #$PartDirectPartIndirect) means that one part of ?obj is illuminated by direct light from ?source, and another part of ?obj is illuminated by indirect light from ?source.  The two parts are not the same part.")
(disjointWith DirectIllumination PartDirectPartIndirect)
(disjointWith IndirectIllumination PartDirectPartIndirect)
(comment SecretInformation "All #$Classification-US information that receives the classification 'Secret'.  The unauthorized disclosure of any instance of #$SecretInformation 'could reasonably be expected to cause serious damage to the national security' (quote from Denning, 'Information Warfare and Security').")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-GangsterGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
gangster genre.  #$TheGodfather-TheMovie is a good example of a movie in this genre. ")
(comment PleasantOdor "An odor which humans find it pleasant to smell.")
(disjointWith NoOdor PleasantOdor)
(disjointWith FoulOdor PleasantOdor)
(disjointWith Interrupted-SequentialStructure Uninterrupted-SequentialStructure)
(comment Uninterrupted-SequentialStructure "The opposite of #$Interrupted-SequentialStructure (q.v.).")
(comment Prehistoric "This is the attribute of being prehistoric. Roughly, this means having existed during

the period before any sort of human records were kept. ")
(disjointWith MediumDuty-PathArtifact LightDuty-PathArtifact)
(comment LightDuty-PathArtifact "This is the collection of instances of #$PathForWheeledVehicles

that can accomodate light loads in all weather. Paths in this collection are

likely to be unpaved but improved [see #$ImprovedSurface-PathArtifact.].")
(comment InvolvedInRelationship "Every unit should have an english.")
(comment BearingFruit "The collection of instances of #$Plant that are in one of the various stages of bearing fruit.  The fruit is not necessarily ripe.")
(comment DamageOutcome "Necessary constraints on the input of a situation.  If such a constraint doesn't hold, that increases the liklihood the device will be damaged.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-WarGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
war genre.  #$SavingPrivateRyan-TheMovie and #$ApocalypseNow-TheMovie are examples of
movies in this genre.    ")
(comment CodingSequence-Uninterrupted "The characteristic attribute of a genetic coding sequence

that is not interrupted by any introns.  See #$Intron-MolecularSegment and

#$Intron-GIS. ")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-CultGenre "The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the 
cult genre.  Works of this genre usually have an obsessive appeal to some sub-group of the fans of a particular genre.  There is usually some element of \"camp\" in instances of this 
genre.  #$EvilDead-TheMovie is a good example of a movie in this genre.  This is not a 
\"true\" genre in that it doesn't actually describe the style of the work.   
In many cases the creator of the work had no intent to make something of this
genre.  #$EdWood-MovieDirector is a good example of someone with no such intent.  
His movies are considered, by most film critics, to be universally bad.  Yet, in spite of 
this (or more likely, because of this) his movies are considered by certain people 
to be very fun to watch and have attracted a large \"cult\" following.          ")
(comment GullibleAgent "The collection of instances of #$IntelligentAgent that are gullible.   Agents who are instances of #$GullibleAgent are easily deceived or tricked.  In particular, they are often too willing to believe what others say.")
(comment Stereophonic "An attribute of audio or audio-visual components or systems. Any stereo component or system is capable of processing 2-channel sound signals intended for left and right speakers.")
(disjointWith Monophonic Stereophonic)
(comment IISNotStandalone "The collection of instances of #$NTMachine that have some version of Microsoft IIS (#$MSInternetInformationServer) installed, along with at least one other major application or server program. This information is primarily intended for inferences concerning computer security, since isolating IIS on its own computer is generally recommended by security experts to avoid compromise of other, sensitive, programs. Important specializations include #$IISAndPDCTogether, and #$IISAndSQLTogether.")
(comment Brownish "The attribute of containing at least a tinge of #$BrownColor.")
(comment ForSaleByOwner "If a thing has this attribute, it is being offered for sale by its owner as a private individual (not by some commercial enterprise).")
(comment RomanArchitecturalStyle "The #$RomanArchitecturalStyle is a building style developed in the #$RomanEmpire.  It is like the #$GreekArchitecturalStyle in the wide use of Greek-style columns, but it is also characterized by the use of arches, barrel vaults and structural concrete.  See also #$NeoclassicArchitecturalStyle and #$RomanesqueArchitecturalStyle.")
(disjointWith CanopyClosure-Dense CanopyClosure-Moderate)
(comment Pisces-TheSign "The twelfth sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Pisces, or The Fish. Ruled by the #$PlanetNeptune. #$Persons born between February 20 and March 20 have this sign.")
(comment RomanesqueArchitecturalStyle "The #$RomanesqueArchitecturalStyle is a style developed in Europe after the fall of the #$RomanEmpire in the early part of #$TheMiddleAges that was dominant before the heyday of the #$GothicArchitecturalStyle.  It is characterized by the use of rounded arches (unlike the pointed arches of the #$GothicArchitecturalStyle), heavy decorated columns, and Roman-style details.")
(comment HorrorMode "The collection of instance of #$CWInstantiating that present instances of #$Narratives in a way or mode intended to instill horror.")
(comment PreppyClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present a preppy image.")
(comment MusicalComposition-WorldFusion "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the world fusion style or genre.")
(comment Aries-TheSign "The first sign of the zodiac. Named after the #$Constellation Aries, or The Ram. Ruled by #$PlanetMars. #$Persons born between March 21 and April 19 have this sign.")
(comment FrillyClothingStyle "The collection of instances of #$SomethingToWear that present a frilly image.")
(disjointWith FixedRateLoan AdjustableRateLoan)
(comment HangingFromArmsPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in a posture of hanging from their arms.")
(comment MusicalComposition-Ambient "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"ambient music\" style or genre.")
(comment OliveColor "The color olive, which is generally a mixutre of green and brown.")
(comment FlexibleWorkingHours "The attribute of the type of profession in which the worker (to some extent) determines for themselves what times to work, rather than being externally assigned.")
(disjointWith Indoors-IsolatedFromOutside Outdoors-ExposedToWeather)
(disjointWith InOuterSpace Outdoors-ExposedToWeather)
(comment LeaningOnKnucklesPosture "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances are in a posture of leaning on their knuckles, as in a #$KnuckleWalking event.")
(comment MusicalComposition-House "The Collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"house music\" style or genre.")
(comment ScaledAnimal "The subcollection of #$Animal whose instances have a skin-like covering consisting #$Scale-AnimalBodyParts.  Some specializations are #$Fish and #$Reptile.")
(disjointWith ScaledAnimal Primate)
(disjointWith CupSize-D CupSize-DD)
(comment CupSize-DD " The collection of people with female breast cup size DD.")
(disjointWith ThreeLanesOrWiderRoadway OneLaneRoadway)
(disjointWith FourLanesOrWiderRoadway OneLaneRoadway)
