(comment MLSLabel "A datatype internal to Oracle.")
(disjointWith VegetativeCellState EndosporeState)
(comment EndosporeState "This collection represents the property of a bacterium being a #$Spore-DormantBacterium.  This occurs when it is experiencing
a hostile environment that would otherwise kill it. The bacterium forms an
endospore, which is a nongrowing heat-resistant structure within the
cell. When the environment is no longer threatening, the #$Bacterium can
revert back to its normal #$VegetativeCell state.")
(comment FailureOutcome "Constraints necessary for success of a situation.")
(comment InternalUseInformation "Information that is authorized for access and use only by the employees of the company or corporation that is the proprieter of the information.")
(comment Utility-RoadClassification "This is the collection of those roads that are used to access

certain areas in order to perform maintenance tasks.")
(disjointWith WaterTransportationDeviceTypeByDesign OrganismPartType)
(comment successor "A binary functional predicate that corresponds to the function #$SuccessorFn (q.v.). (#$successor N M) means that #$Integer M is #$Integer N's successor.  In other words, (#$successor N M) means that M is the least integer greater than N.  Thus, (#$successor 42 43) holds, as does (#$successor -42 -41). ")
(comment toAccount "'(#$toAccount TRANS ACCT)' means that the money transferred in TRANS ends up in ACCT.")
(comment relationExists "(#$relationExists PRED COL) means that for some instance INST of COL, (PRED INST) holds. Thus  (#$relationExists PRED COL) is equivalent to the more complex form: (#$thereExists ?INST (#$isa ?INST COL) (PRED ?INST)).  A ground atomic formula (or \"GAF\") built upon #$relationExists is in effect shorthand (i.e. a \"macro\") for an equivalent assertion with the more complex quantified form.  See the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.")
(comment doOutputProgramFunctionHeader "A ternary complex action
predicate. (#$doOutputProgramFunctionHeader PROGRAMMER FILE ALGORITHM)
means that PROGRAMMER appends the header of a program function declaration
that implements ALGORITHM to the computer source code file FILE. The header
typically includes the program function's name and its parameter list.")
(comment doProgramCondition "A ternary complex action predicate.
(#$doProgramCondition PROGRAMMER FILE CONDITION) means that PROGRAMMER
appends code to the end of FILE which evaluates CONDITION and maps into
the boolean space of the contextual programming language. For example,
the conditional expressions of the \"if\" or \"while\" statements of many languages.")
(comment doProgramStep "A ternary complex action predicate. (#$doProgramStep PROGRAMMER FILE STEP) means that PROGRAMMER appends code to the end of FILE that
implements STEP, in the programming language determined by the current context.")
(comment doDefineProgramFunctionFromAlgorithm "A ternary complex action
predicate. (#$doDefineProgramFunctionFromAlgorithm PROGRAMMER FILE ALGORITHM)
means that PROGRAMMER appends a declaration of a program function that 
implements ALGORITHM to the computer source code file FILE.")
(comment doOutputProgramExpression "A ternary complex action predicate. 
(#$doOutputProgramExpression PROGRAMMER FILE EXPRESSION)
means that PROGRAMMER appends EXPRESSION to the end of computer
file FILE.")
(comment programFunctionOperator "A binary predicate. (#$programFunctionOperator RELATION OPERATOR) means that the semantics of RELATION are instantiated in the
programming language operator OPERATOR.")
(comment doOutputProgramOperator "A ternary complex action predicate.
(#$doOutputProgramOperator HACKER FILE OPERATOR) means that HACKER appends
code signifying OPERATOR to the end of source code file FILE. This is usually
done when operator is part of a #$ProgramExpression.")
(comment Currency-SocialPracticeFn "A unary function, which takes as its argument a function which is an instance of #$UnitOfMoney, and returns an instance of #$DevisedPracticeOrWork (more specifically, it returns an instance of #$Money-DevisedPractice), which is the currency concerned qua social institution -- the many and varied practices which underpin the use of that particular currency in particular contexts, and which came into being at a particular time and place. (Contrast this with instances of #$MonetaryValue which, as #$ScalarIntervals, are timeless, abstract entities.) Thus (#$Currency-SocialPracticeFn #$Dollar-Australia) is the Australian dollar as a devised social institution: created by Australians, sustained by them (as well as, increasingly, by international investors) insofar as they use it in a variety of particular contexts, and whose future is unknown.")
(disjointWith Money-DevisedPractice ConceptualWork)
(comment WhileStatement-Start "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. #$WhileStatement-Start denotes for a contextually determined programming language
the sequence of tokens that would signal the start of a while statement.
For example, in #$Pascal-ProgrammingLanguage, #$WhileStatement-Start would
denote the token \"while\".")
(comment WhileStatement-Do "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. #$WhileStatement-Do denotes for a contextually determined programming language
the sequence of tokens that would signal the start of a while statement.
For example, in #$Pascal-ProgrammingLanguage, #$WhileStatement-Do would
denote the token \"do\".")
(comment WhileStatement-End "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. #$WhileStatement-End denotes for a contextually determined programming language
the sequence of tokens that would signal the start of a while statement.
For example, in #$VisualBasic, #$WhileStatement-End would
denote the token \"Wend\".")
(comment monetaryValueOfThing "(#$monetaryValueOfThing THING VALUE) means that #$TemporalThing THING (which may be goods or services) is in some sense worth #$MonetaryValue VALUE. This may be due to its appraised value (for which see #$appraisedValue), due to its having been bought for the price VALUE some time in the past (for which see #$bookValue), due to its being currently for sale at the price VALUE (for which see #$basicPrice), due to its being a piece of #$Currency that has been manufactured to stand for the amount of money VALUE (for which see #$tenderValue), or for other reasons. This predicate is thus very much an umbrella term. See also the ternary predicate #$monetaryValueOfThingTo.")
(comment increasesSitSlot-Subjective "(#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT THING SLOT) expresses the fact
that, everything else being equal, in the situation SIT the value of the
#$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING would
be higher than some default or presupposed base value. The `everything else
being equal' clause indicates that the value of SLOT for THING might
change, even *de*crease, were more information to become available about
SIT. This predicate captures an important aspect of probability
assignments: they tend to be subjective, i.e., based on the amount of
information available to an evaluator, and they are not *persistent* with
respect to situations.  Consider an example: A situation in which I board a
plane from Austin to Chicago at 7am on a given day may *or may not* be one
in which it is more likely that I will be in Chicago on time for a 3pm meeting
than a situation in which I drive a car, because other undetermined factors
might play a negative role. For example, that particular situation might
also be a situation in which fierce storms shut down air traffic into
Chicago for 2 days. However, barring additional factors and constraints
(`everything else being equal'), if all I know about a situation is that in
it I take a plane to Chicago at 7am, I would be a fool not to assign a
higher probability to my being on time for my meeting in such a
situation. The relation expressed by #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective is not 
(necessarily) causal. Also, note that (#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT
THING SLOT) doesn't entail (#$increases-Generic SIT THING SLOT) since the
latter implies that the value of SLOT for THING *is actually* higher after
the beginning of SIT. Nor does (#$increases-Generic SIT THING SLOT) entail 
(#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT THING SLOT). Finally, the meaning of
this predicate is not inherently temporal, unlike #$increases-Generic.")
(comment increasesSitTypeSlot-Subjective "This predicate is a type-level cognate of
#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective (q.v.). (#$increasesSitTypeSlot-Subjective
TYPE THING SLOT) expresses the fact that, everything else being equal, in
situations that are instances of TYPE the value of the #$QuantitySlot SLOT
(i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING would be higher than some
default or presupposed base value. Note that the `everything else being equal' clause
indicates that the value of SLOT for THING might change, even *de*crease,
were more information to become available about TYPE: this means that this
predicate is *not* inverse-transitive w.r.t. the #$genls relation in the
first argument. See also the documentation available for
#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective.")
(comment decreasesPropSlot-Subjective "(#$decreasesPropSlot-Subjective SENT THING SLOT) expresses the fact that, everything else being equal, if SENT is true (asserted) the value of the #$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING is lower than some default or presupposed base  value. The `everything else being equal' clause indicates that the value of  SLOT for THING might change, even increase, were more information to  become available -- that is, the SLOT-value assignment to THING might  change, even holding SENT true, if additional sentences were known to be  true. This is the propositional version of #$decreasesSitSlot-Subjective. See #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective for more extensive documentation.")
(comment decreasesSitSlot-Subjective "(#$decreasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT THING SLOT) expresses the fact
that, everything else being equal, in the situation SIT the value of the
#$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e., the arg2 of SLOT) attributed to THING would be
lower than some default or presupposed base value. This predicate is
especially useful for reasoning about probability assignments. See the documentation
for #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective for examples, and for comparing this
predicates to similar ones like #$increases-Generic and
#$decreases-Generic.")
(comment increasesPropSlot-Subjective "(#$increasesPropSlot-Subjective SENT THING SLOT) expresses the fact that, everything else being equal, if SENT is true (asserted) the value of the #$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING is higher than some default or presupposed base  value. The `everything else being equal' clause indicates that the value of  SLOT for THING might change, even *de*crease, were more information to  become available -- that is, the SLOT-value assignment to THING might  change, even holding SENT true, if additional sentences were known to be  true. This is the propositional version of #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective. See that predicate for more extensive documentation.")
(comment doOutputTypeofVariable "A ternary complex action predicate.
(#$doOutputTypeofVariable PROGRAMMER FILE VARIABLE) means that PROGRAMMER
appends a string (or strings) to the end of FILE which signifies the
data type of VARIABLE. This activity will usually only occur when programming
in typed languages like #$JavaProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment doOutputBlockClose "A binary complex action predicate. 
(#$doOutputBlockClose PROGRAMMER FILE) means that PROGRAMMER appends
a symbol or symbols to the end of FILE, which signals the closing of the
current code block.")
(comment TimeAssessment-METT-TC "This is an indexical concept which corresponds to the shared (common ground) knowledge about time constraints which are relevant in some military discoruse context.")
(comment TroopAssessment-METT-TC "This is an indexical concept which corresponds to the shared (common ground) knowledge about friendly troop dispositions which are under discussion in some military discoruse context.")
(comment MissionAssessment-METT-TC "This is an indexical concept corresponding to shared (common ground) knowledge about the misson under discussion in a military discourse context.")
(comment FunctionalAlgorithm "A specialization of #$Algorithm. Each instance of #$FunctionalAlgorithm is an
algorithm with only one defined output. Note that instances of this collection
may make other computations as a side effect, but there is only one \"official\" 
result. Such algorithms are well suited to being abstracted out as independent
instances of #$ProgramFunction in computer programs.")
(comment AlgorithmFunctionFn "An individual denoting function. (#$AlgorithmFunctionFn ALGORITHM NAME) denotes an

instance of #$ProgramFunction identified by NAME which instantiates ALGORITHM with the context of

a single #$ComputerCode-Source.")
(comment FunctionCallFn "An individual denoting function. (#$FunctionCallFn FUNCTION . ARGS) denotes the program expression

in which FUNCTION is called with arguments ARGS).")
(comment softwareParameterSetInProcess "(#$softwareParameterSetInProcess SOFTWARE-PARAMETER COMPUTER-PROCESS-RUNNING) means that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER [see #$softwareParameterValueInProgram] is set at some point during COMPUTER-PROCESS-RUNNING, an instance of #$ComputerProcessRunning.  This normally means that the value changes, but it is possible that the value it is set to is its prior value.")
(comment softwareParameterSetToValueInProcess "(#$softwareParameterSetToValueInProcess SOFTWARE-PARAMETER VALUE COMPUTER-PROCESS-RUNNING) means that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER is set to the value VALUE at some point during COMPUTER-PROCESS-RUNNING, an instance of #$ComputerProcessRunning.  By the end of COMPUTER-PROCESS-RUNNING, (#$softwareParameterValueInProgram SOFTWARE-PARAMETER VALUE COMPUTER-PROCESS-RUNNING) holds.")
(comment softwareParameterSetInSoftwareObject "(#$softwareParameterSetInSoftwareObject SOFTWARE-PARAMETER SOFTWARE-OBJECT) means that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER [see #$softwareParameterValueInProgram] is set at some point during any execution of SOFTWARE-OBJECT (an instance of #$SoftwareObject).  This normally means that the value changes, but it is possible that the value it is set to is its prior value.")
(comment softwareParameterSetToValueInSoftwareObject "(#$softwareParameterSetToValueInSoftwareObject SOFTWARE-PARAMETER VALUE SOFTWARE-OBJECT) means that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER [see #$softwareParameterValue] is set to the value VALUE at some point during any execution of SOFTWARE-OBJECT (an instance of #$SoftwareObject).  This normally means that the value changes, but it is possible that the value it is set to is its prior value.")
(comment softwareParameterSetInSpecification "(#$softwareParameterSetInSpecification SOFTWARE-PARAMETER PROGRAM-SPEC) means that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER [see #$softwareParameterValue] is set at some point during any execution of a program implementing PROGRAM-SPEC (an instance of #$ProgramSpecification).  This normally means that the value changes, but it is possible that the value it is set to is its prior value.")
(comment softwareParameterSetToValueInSpecification "(#$softwareParameterSetToValueInSpecification SOFTWARE-PARAMETER VALUE PROGRAM-SPEC) mea ns that the value of SOFTWARE-PARAMETER [see #$softwareParameterValue] is set to the value VALUE at some point during any execution of PROGRAM-SPEC (an instance of #$ProgramSpecification).  This normally means that the value changes, but it is possible that the value it is set to is its prior value.")
(comment doOutputFunctionArgs "A variable arity complex action predicate. (#$doOutputFunctionArgs PROGRAMMER FILE . ARGS) means that PROGRAMMER appends
to the end of FILE code for declaring the arguments ARGS of a function call.")
(comment totalCharge-Event "The predicate #$totalCharge-Event is used to relate a buying event (that is, an instance of #$Buying) to the total amount of money charged to the buyer in that event. (#$totalCharge BUYING MONEY) means that the sum total charge for all goods, services, #$salesTax and other taxes, shipping and handling, delivery charges, discounts, etc., in the #$Buying event BUYING is the #$MonetaryValue MONEY. This predicate is not to be confused with the predicate #$totalCharge which relates _a bought thing_ (i.e. an instance of #$TemporalThing) to the total amount paid for it.")
(comment costTo "This is a ternary version of the binary predicate #$cost which allows cost assertions regarding a given thing to be relativised to those who bear the cost in question. (#$costTo OBJECT MONEY AGENT) means that the #$TemporalThing OBJECT has a cost in #$MonetaryValue of MONEY to the #$SocialBeing AGENT. See also the ternary predicates #$monetaryValueOfThingTo, #$willingToPay.")
(comment StandingOnOnesHead "The collection of events in which one is balanced on one's
head in an introverted #$StandingStill position.")
(comment hasHeadquartersInRegion "The predicate #$hasHeadquartersInRegion identifies a region in which a particular organization has one of its headquarters.")
(comment ELInferenceBinding "Each instance of #$ELInferenceBinding represents a CycL binding pair of variable and value which can be substituted for that variable in a CycL formula.  Such binding pairs are constructed for representation in the Cyc KB by using the function #$ELInferenceBindingFn.")
(comment ELInferenceBindingSet "Each instance of #$ELInferenceBindingSet represents one complete answer to a CycL Query. An #$ELInferenceBindingSet is a set of #$ELInferenceBindings, with one #$ELInferenceBinding for each free variable present in the query formula.  Such binding sets are constructed for representation in the KB by using the function #$TheSet.")
(comment ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets "Each instance of #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets is a set of answers to a CycL Query.  Because each answer will include bindings for each free variable in the query formula, the elements of an #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets will each be instances of #$ELInferenceBindingSet.")
(comment ELInferenceBindingFn "(#$ELInferenceBindingFn VAR TERM) denotes an instance of #$ELInferenceBinding which states that TERM can be substituted for VAR.  Bindings are produced as part of the result of a CycL query, when it is found that the query formula can be proven from the KB by substituting TERM for VAR.  The entire query result consists of a #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets, which includes zero or more #$ELInferenceBindingSets, each of which contains one #$ELInferenceBinding for each free variable present in the query formula.")
(comment InferenceBindingSetFn "(#$InferenceBindingSetFn BINDING-1 ... BINDING-N) denotes one #$InferenceBindingSet, which is one complete answer to a CycL query.  Each of BINDING-1 ... BINDING-N is an #$InferenceBinding, stating that some variable can be replaced with some Cyc term to prove the query formula true.  The result of a CycL query includes zero or more #$InferenceBindingSet instances, grouped together as a #$SetOfInferenceBindingSets.")
(comment SetOfInferenceBindingSetsFn "(#$SetOfInferenceBindingSetsFn BINDINGSET-1 ... BINDINGSET-N) denotes one #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets, which is one entire result of a CycL query.  Each of BINDINGSET-1 ... BINDINGSET-N is an #$ELInferenceBindingSet, stating that a group of variables can be replaced with a group of Cyc terms to prove the query formula true.  The result of a CycL query includes zero or more #$ELInferenceBindingSet instances, grouped together as a #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets.")
(comment ProgramStepSpecification "The collection of specifications that deal not with how an entire program should behave, but with how a part of a program ought to behave.")
(comment CycLQuerySpecification "Each instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification gives a description of how a particular query should be run by the #$CycInferenceEngine.  At minimum, this description includes the CycL formula that expresses the query, but can also include a number of other parameters that control inference, such as what microtheory to do the query in, how much time to allow, etc.")
(comment softwareParameterValueInSpecification "(#$softwareParameterValueInSpecification PARAM VAL SPEC) states that, according to the #$ProgramSpecification SPEC, the #$SoftwareParameter PARAM has the value VAL.")
(comment defaultSoftwareParameterValueInSpecification "(#$defaultSoftwareParameterValueInSpecification PARAM VAL SPEC) states that when a program runs according to SPEC, if the value of PARAM has not been specified, it will default to VAL.")
(comment defaultSoftwareParameterValueInSoftwareObject "(#$defaultSoftwareParameterValueInSoftwareObject PARAM VAL SOFTWARE) states that, if the value of PARAM is not otherwise specified, it will default to VAL in SOFTWARE.")
(comment fixedSoftwareParameterValueInSoftwareObject "(#$fixedSoftwareParameterValueInSoftwareObject PARAM VAL SOFTWARE) states that in the #$SoftwareObject SOFTWARE, the value of PARAM will be VAL, and SOFTWARE does not provide a mechanism for changing it from that value.")
(comment SoftwareTestSpecification "The collection of specifications for a software test.  Each instance of #$SoftwareTestSpecification should include a specification for producing a result, and a description of what result is expected (so that the result and expectation can be compared.)")
(disjointWith SoftwareTestSpecification ProgramFault)
(comment TestResultNotificationType "Each instance of #$TestResultNotificationType is a different type of notification style that may be used to communicate test results to a responsible party.")
(comment TestResultNotification "The collection of events in which a #$SoftwareAgent which is running a testing program notifies the person responsible about the results of a test.")
(comment TestResultNotification-EmailVerbose "The collection of events in which #$SoftwareAgent which is running a testing program communicates complete test results by email to the person responsible.")
(comment TestResultNotification-EmailBrief "The collection of events in which #$SoftwareAgent which is running a testing program communicates a brief synopsis of test results by email to the person responsible.")
(comment ComputerBinaryFile "The collection of #$ComputerFile-AISes encoded in binary format.    All executable programs are stored as binary files and so are most numeric data files.    A binary file is only computer-readable. In contrast, text files are stored in a form    (usually #$ASCII) that is human-readable.")
(disjointWith ComputerBinaryFile ComputerTextFile)
(comment FileHashTable-File "A specialization of #$ComputerBinaryFile whose instances are also            #$StructuredKnowledgeSources, where information is stored           in an organized fashion for ease of access.           See also #$FileHashTable-DataStructure.")
(comment FileHashTable-DataStructure "A specialization of both #$ComputerDataStructure and #$StructuredKnowledgeSource.          Each instance of FileHashTable-DataStructure is a data structure           that contains a stream (in a way, a pointer) to a #$FileHashTable-File,          as well as information about what type of encoding/decoding will need          to be used while writing/reading to/from the #$FileHashTable-File           at hand. A #$FileHashTable-DataStructure is a gateway to the          information stored in a #$FileHashTable-File. It has a functional          interface that is very similar to that of a #$HashTable-DataStructure,          in that they both support functions that look up values for given keys          or assign values to given keys. Only, in one case (#$HashTable-DataStructure)          the key/value pairs are stored in memory while in the other case           (#$FileHashTable-DataStructure) they are stored on disk (that is, in a           #$FileHashTable-File).")
(comment filenameForSKS "(#$filenameForSKS SKS FILENAME) denotes that FILENAME is the path name for the            structured knowledge source SKS. This predicate is used in determining access paths            for knowledge sources like file hash table files.")
(comment keyTestFunctionForSKS "(#$keyTestFunctionForSKS SKS TEST-FN) denotes that TEST-FN is the function used           to compare the keys in the structured knowledge source SKS.            This is used in determining what test function should be used in accessing            certain indexed structured knowledge sources such as hash tables and            file hash tables.")
(comment valueTestFunctionForSKS "(#$valueTestFunctionForSKS SKS TEST-FN) denotes that TEST-FN is the function used           to compare the values in the structured knowledge source SKS.            This is used in determining what test function should be used while           comparing the results gathered from a knowledge source against the fully-bound           arguments in a query.")
(comment serializationTypeForSKS "This predicate is used to express what type of encoding/decoding need          to be used while writing/reading to/from a given structured knowledge          source of type #$FileHashTable-File.")
(comment initialParameterValue "A specialization of the binary predicate #$softwareParameterValue. (#$initialParameterValue PARAM VALUE) means that

PARAM begins execution of a #$ComputerProcessRunning with the value VALUE in the current context.")
(comment StorageInContainerFn "(#$StorageInContainerFn OBJ-TYPE) returns the collection of situations in which instances of OBJ-TYPE are stored in containers.")
(comment sendEMailFromTo "(#$sendEMailFromTo FROM-ADDRESS TO-ADDRESS TEXT-STRING SUBJECT-STRING) is a #$notAssertible predicate which, when asked in a query returns true when totally bound, but as a side effect causes an email message to be sent from the FROM-ADDRESS to the TO-ADDRESS with the subject line SUBJECT-STRING and the text body TEXT-STRING.")
(comment atLeastAsCloseTo "'(#$atLeastAsCloseTo THING1 THING2 THING3)' means that THING1 is at least as close in space to THING2 as it is to THING3, and THING1 may be closer to THING2 than to THING3.  See #$closerTo.")
(comment programStrings "(#$programStrings OBJECT STRING) means that when
OBJECT is referenced via STRING in a computer program in the 
current context. For example, 
(#$programStrings #$AlgorithmInput1 \"x\") means that we
call the first input parameter \"x\" in the current computational
context.")
(comment ThaiCuisine "The collection of instances of what many Americans tend to think of as Thai food")
(comment servesCuisine "(#$servesCuisine FOOD-OUTLET CUISINE) means that the organization FOOD-OUTLET serves food of the #$Cuisine, CUISINE")
(comment ArrayReferenceFn "(#$ArrayReferenceFn ARRAY N) denotes the real world object that is represented at position N of ARRAY,

in the context of the current state of the contextually determined #$ComputerProcessRunning.  If ARRAY has elements have a real numbered-valued data type, then (#$ArrayReferenceFn ARRAY N) will denote some instance of #$RealNumber.  This mapping of a parameter to its real world value is defined by the intentions of the program itself. ")
(comment BBQCuisine "Barbecue food")
(comment ProgramConditionFn "A unary denotational
function. (#$ProgramConditionFn PROP) denotes a program condition
testing whether or not PROP is true in the current contextual #$ComputerProcessRunning.")
(comment SubjectiveSlotTrendPredicate "A specialization of #$SlotTrendPredicate.  Instances are predicates used to represent ceteris paribus expectations of net increases and decreases of quantities over time.  For any instance PRED of #$SubjectiveSlotTrendPredicate, (PRED SIT THING SLOT) will indicate that given the #$Situation (or situation-type) SIT, the value of the

#$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING would

be higher or lower than some default or presupposed base value. The `everything else

being equal' clause indicates that the value of SLOT for THING might

change, even in the opposite direction as that indicated by the assertion, were more information to become available about

SIT. This class of predicates captures the subjective dimension of assignments of probability -- that is, an assignment of probability is always based on the amount of information available to an evaluator.  See the #$comment on #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective for more information.")
(comment classificationOfInformation "(#$classificationOfInformation INFO USCLASS) assigns a

US classification USCLASS to the information INFO.")
(comment classificationOfInformation "(#$classificationOfInformation INFO USCLASS) assigns a US classification USCLASS to the information INFO.")
(comment designationOfInformation "(#$designationOfInformation INFO CLASS) assigns a

classification CLASS to the information INFO.")
(comment designationOfThing "(#$designationOfThing THING DESIGNATION) assigns a designation, or classification, to something.  As an example, consider a meeting that is designated 'restricted access' or 'secret'.")
(comment movieAdvisoryRating "(#$movieAdvisoryRating MOVIE-CW RATING) states that the movie MOVIE-CW has been given the #$MPAAAdvisoryClassification-Film rating RATING. For example, (#$movieAdvisoryRating #$Clueless-TheMovie #$ParentalGuidance13Rating).")
(comment hasSituationTypeStructuralProperty "A specialization of #$isa.  This predicate relates a specialization of #$Situation to a #$ScriptTypeByStructuralOrderFeature it has.  (#$hasSituationTypeStructuralProperty SITTYPE PROPERTY) holds just in case SITTYPE is an instance of PROPERTY.  That holds just in case SITTYPE has the property represented by the collection PROPERTY.")
(comment nucleicAcidSequenceConnectivity "This relation is used to indicate whether a particular nucleotide sequence (see #$nucleotideSequenceOf, (#$ListOfTypeFn #$Nucleotide-GIS)) has the attribute of being 'interrupted' or not.  Examples of molecules with uninterrupted sequences (see #$CodingSequence-Uninterrupted) include instances of #$MessengerRNA and bacterial DNA.  Examples of molecules with interrupted sequence include most eucaryotic DNA molecules and instances of #$PrimaryTranscript-RNAMolecule.")
(comment illuminationLevel "(#$illuminationLevel OBJ LIGHT-LEVEL) indicates how brightly illuminated the object OBJ is.  Higher values for LIGHT-LEVEL mean greater light intensity.")
(comment transparencyOfObject "(#$transparencyOfObject OBJ DEGREE) means that the tangible object OBJ has an intrinsic #$Transparency to light of the specified DEGREE.  Higher values indicate that more light will pass through the substance and with less distortion.")
(comment resistanceToBioDeterioration "(#$resistanceToBioDeterioration OBJ DEGREE) means that the tangible object OBJ has the indicated DEGREE of resistance to biological deterioration.")
(comment impactAbsorptionOfObject "U has the impact absorption ability v.  Impact absorption as an attribute can be decomposed (though perhaps not unambiguously) into two other attributes: (a) plasticity, and (b) the amount of force required to deform an object")
(comment colorHasHue "The formula (#$colorHasHue ?COLOR ?HUE) states that the #$Color ?COL has the #$MunsellHue ?HUE.  For example,(#$colorHasHue #$VividRed-Color (#$MunsellRedFn 50) states that the color #$VividRed-Color) has a Munsell red hue of 50.  Please see #$SharedNoteOnMunsellSystemOfColorClassification and the comment on #$MunsellHue for further explanation.")
(comment colorHasBrightness "The formula (#$colorHasBrightness ?COLOR ?BRIGHT) states that the #$Color ?COLOR has the #$MunsellBrightness ?BRIGHT.  Each instance of #$MunsellBrightness is represented by a NAT formed with #$MunsellBrightnessFn.  The brightness of a color is the attribute that makes a color light or dark.  A brightness value of 0 results in #$PureBlackColor.  A value of 100, when no hue is present, results in #$PureWhiteColor. (Please see also the comment on #$MunsellChroma.)  All instances of #$Color have a brightness value somewhere between 0 and 100.  Please see also #$SharedNoteOnMunsellSystemOfColorClassification and the constant #$MunsellBrightness for further explanation.")
(comment colorHasChroma "The formula (#$colorHasChroma ?COLOR ?CHROMA) states that the #$Color ?COLOR has the #$MunsellChroma ?CHROMA.  See #$SharedNoteOnMunsellSystemOfColorClassification and the comment on #$MunsellChroma for further explanation.")
(comment hasOrbitalPathClass "(#$hasOrbitalPathClass SATELLITE PATHTYPE) means that a particular instance of 
#$Satellite-Device,  orbitting the Earth, orbits in that  
#$OrbitalPathClass. #$OrbitalPathClasses are determined by a satellite's
distance (altitude) from the surface of the Earth. ")
(comment complexityOfConfiguration "This predicate relates a configuration to its complexity.")
(comment uniformColorOfObject "The formula (#$uniformColorOfObject OBJ COL) means that all of the visible parts of the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ have the color the #$Color COL.  Hence, this predicate only applies to objects that are entirely one color. For example, the assertion (#$relationAllInstance #$uniformColorOfObject (#$LiquidFn Gold) #$GoldColor) means that all #$visibleParts of all instances of (#$LiquidFn #$Gold) are #$GoldColor.  This predicate should be used carefully, as it has far more strength than the other color predicates.  See also #$mainColorOfObject and #$objectHasColor.")
(comment mainColorOfObject "The formula (#$mainColorOfObject ?OBJ ?COL) means that the #$PartiallyTangible ?OBJ is almost entirely colored with the #$Color ?COL.  For example, a #$BrownBear may have green eyes and white claws, but we would still say that the bear is brown.  Therefore, (#$mainColorOfObject #$BrownBear #$BrownColor) would be true.  The green and white would be referenced with the predicate, #$objectHasColor.  An example where it is NOT  appropriate to use this predicate is to describe a #$Zebra.  Since a #$Zebra is roughly equally black and white, we would not say that it is primarily either of those colors.  In this case, it would be better to use #$significantColorOfObject.  One should think of the expressions of the form 'The OBJECT is COLOR' when using this predicate.  For example, even though an apple may have a brown stem and a green leaf, we would still say 'The apple is red'.  Contrarily, we would NOT say 'That zebra is black'.  Likewise, if we had a shirt that was 49% red and 51% green, we would NOT say that the color of that shirt is green.  If we had a shirt that was green with a small, white insignia on the pocket, we WOULD, in this case, say that the #$mainColorOfObject, this shirt, is green.  See also #$mainColorOfObject, #$objectHasColor and #$uniformColorOfObject.")
(comment mainColorOfObject "The formula (#$mainColorOfObject ?OBJ ?COL) means that the #$PartiallyTangible ?OBJ is almost entirely colored with the #$Color ?COL.  For example, a #$BrownBear may have green eyes and white claws, but we would still say that the bear is brown.  Therefore, (#$mainColorOfObject #$BrownBear #$BrownColor) would be true.  An example where it is NOT  appropriate to use this predicate is to describe a #$Zebra.  Since a #$Zebra is roughly equally black and white, we would not say that it is primarily either of those colors.  In this case, it would be better to use #$objectHasColor.  One should think of the expressions of the form 'The OBJECT is COLOR' when using this predicate.  For example, even though an apple may have a brown stem and a green leaf, we would still say 'The apple is red'.  Contrarily, we would NOT say 'That zebra is black'.  Likewise, if we had a shirt that was 49% red and 51% green, we would NOT say that the color of that shirt is green.  If we had a shirt that was green with a small, white insignia on the pocket, we WOULD, in this case, say that the #$mainColorOfObject, this shirt, is green.  See also #$objectHasColor and #$uniformColorOfObject.")
(comment hasMentalFeatures "The predicate #$hasMentalFeatures is used to indicate some mental state or capacity of a particular person.  (#$hasMentalFeatures PERSON FEAT) means that the sentient-animal person PERSON is characterized by (i.e. is an instance of) the #$PersonTypeByMentalFeature FEAT.  For example, #$BobbyFischer #$hasMentalFeatures #$HighIntelligence.")
(comment rightsGranted "This predicate is used to indicate what instances of #$SituationTypeByUserRights some #$ChangeInUserRights is related to.  If (#$rightsGranted EVENT SIT-TYPE) holds, then in the #$ChangeInUserRights EVENT, the #$SituationTypeByUserRights SIT-TYPE is granted (to some agent).  What this means is that EVENT causes a particular user right situation to come about and that situation is an instance of SIT-TYPE.  Note: nothing is entailed about whether any user rights are currently enjoyed by any actors in EVENT.  #$rightsGranted simply states a causal connection between an event and a situation type.  It simply states that as a result of EVENT a certain instance of SIT-TYPE obtained.  Nothing further is inferrable about whether that instance of SIT-TYPE currently obtains.  For example, the Louisiana purchase resulted in the existence of a particular user right situation (the situation corresponding to the USA's having exclusive use rights to the territory purchased).  And this situation is an instance of #$ExclusiveUserRights (the collection of all situations in which an agent has exclusive user rights over something).  But, (#$rightsGranted LouisiannaPurchase #$ExclusiveUserRights) would still be true now even if we had

sold the territory back to France 20 years later.  The predicate is used to state a bald fact that a certain instance of argument 2 obtained at least for a short time following the temporal extent of argument 1.")
(comment eventFormality "The level of formality of an action.")
(comment locationState "An instance of #$BinaryPredicate.  (#$locationState OBJ LOC-TYPE) means that the #$SpatialThing-Localized OBJ is an instance of the #$SpatialThingTypeByGenericLocation LOC-TYPE.  For example, (#$locationState #$ChannelTunnel #$Underground).")
(comment activityCriticality "The importance to human life or property of performing this activity correctly.")
(comment levelOfEncryption "(#$levelOfEncryption AIS LEVEL) means that AIS is encrypted to degree LEVEL.")
(comment levelOfSecurity "(#$levelOfSecurity OBJ LEV) means that the instance of #$IndividualTypeBySecurityStatus, LEV, describes the security level of #$Individual OBJ.  Thus (#$levelOfSecurity MESSAGE-1 #$Encrypted) means that MESSAGE-1 is encrypted.")
(comment hasNumberingSchema "(#$hasNumberingSchema TEXT SCHEMA) means that physical

instances embodying the informational content of the

#$PublishedEdition-PIT TEXT ordinarily exhibit the page numbering

schema SCHEMA.")
(comment clothingStyle "The style of the clothing.")
(comment constructionExistentialState "This predicate is used to indicate the existential state of an

instance of #$ConstructionArtifact. (#$constructionExistentialState CONS

STATE) means that CONS is in the state describe by STATE, an instance of

#$ConstructionArtifactTypeByExistentialStatus.")
(comment datingStatus "Expands on #$maritalStatus.  Indicates a person's availability for romantic attachment (ooh!)")
(comment personalFeatures "'(#$personalFeatures PERSON FEATURE)' means that the #$Person PERSON is an instance of the collection  FEATURE, and that FEATURE represents one of the characteristics with which people are identified socially.  For instance, marital status is a personal/social feature, but blood type is not.")
(comment processorArchitectureOfComputer "(#$processorArchitectureOfComputer COMPUTER ARCHITECTURE) means that the processor(s -- all of them?) on COMPUTER \"implemement\" ARCHITECTURE.")
(comment computerArchitecture "(#$computerArchitecture COMPUTER
  ARCHITECTURE) means that COMPUTER is an instance of #$Computer and has a microprocessor with ARCHITECTURE. ARCHITECTURE must be an instance of #$ComputerTypeByArchitecture.  Some examples
  of such architectures are #$RISCArchitecture, #$SerialArchitecture and others.")
(comment hasBacterialPhysiologicalFeature "#$hasBacterialPhysiologicalFeature BAC-TYPE BPA means that BAC-TYPE has the physiological feature BPA.")
(comment tasteOfObject "(#$tasteOfObject PIECEOFFOOD TASTE) means that PIECEOFFOOD, an instance of #$PartiallyTangible, has the taste TASTE.  See also #$TasteFn.")
(comment hasPhysiologicalFeature "(#$hasPhysiologicalFeature BLO FEAT) means that the #$BiologicalLivingObject BLO has the physiological feature FEAT.  Note that many, perhaps most, assertions made with #$hasPhysiologicalFeature will be time-dependent; for example, an individual person is not #$SexuallyMature throughout his or her life, but only during a portion of it.  For this reason, many assertions made with #$#$hasPhysiologicalFeature will need to be temporally qualified.")
(comment objectEmitsOdor "A binary predicate that maps any #$PartiallyTangible to the #$Odor(s) it gives off.")
(comment placeHasOdor "Associates a place with an #$Odor.  Call Foo the free space around a #$PartiallyTangible which #$objectEmitsOdor BarOdor.  By default it is true that (#$placeHasOdor Foo BarOdor). ")
(comment computerFeatures "(#$computerFeatures COMP FEAT) means that instance of #$ComputationalSystem has the #$ComputerSoftwareOrHardwareTypeByFeature FEAT.  This predicate relates computational systems to their functional properties, i.e. the features that they have that allow them to be used or manipulated in certain kinds of computer events.  This predicate would be used to represent, for example, that computer has a certain open port or that a computer network is designed under the enterprise or workstation model.")
(comment hasSecurityClearance "gives the security clearances this agent has.  ")
(comment classificationOfReport "(#$classificationOfReport REPORT CLASSIFICATION) means that CLASSIFICATION is the security classification of REPORT.")
(comment loanDedication "An instance of #$BinaryPredicate that relates a loan (c.f., #$LoanAgreement) to a type of situation it is intended to be used to finance.  For example (#$loanDedication MyMortgage (#$BuyingFn #$HumanResidence)) says that my mortgage is intended to fund the purchase of a house.")
(comment loanInterestOptions "The predicate that relates a #$LoanAgreement to some #$LoanAgreementTypeByInterestRateOption, such as #$IndexedLoan.")
(comment hasPreparationStyle "The predicate #$hasPreparationStyle is used to indicate the way in which a particular food item was prepared.  (#$hasPreparationStyle EDIBLE PREP) means the #$EdibleStuff EDIBLE has been processed so that it has the style (see #$EdibleStuffTypeByPreparationStyle) PREP (e.g., #$Cooked, #$Roasted, #$Chilled -- or #$Raw).  For example, a piece of #$Toast #$hasPreparationStyle #$Toasted; any quantity of #$Beer that is ready to drink #$hasPreparationStyle #$Fermented.")
(comment styleOfMusicPerformer "(#$styleOfMusicPerformer MUSICIAN STYLE) means that the musician MUSICIAN composes and/or performs the type of music characterized by STYLE an instance of #$MusicalGenreType.")
(comment localOrganizationOpennessState "Used to indicate whether a #$LocalOrganization is open for business or not.")
(comment hasZodiacSign "(#$hasZodiacSign PERSON SIGN) means that the #$Person PERSON has the zodiac sign SIGN. What zodiac sign a #$Person has is determined by their birthday (cf. #$birthDay). For example, someone born between July 23 and August 22 has the sign #$Leo-TheSign. See the instances of #$PersonTypeByEuropeanZodiacSign for the dates of each sign.")
(comment softwareIsOfType "ComputerSoftwareAttribute will include instances like RealTime, BatchProgram, BackgroundProgram (like a disk controller), and other things. We might want a spec of this SoftwareInterfaceAttributes where we dump interface related attributes.  We are still a bit fuzzy on a precise meaning for this. 18 oct 90")
(comment bodyHairLevel "The level of body hair that this body part has")
(comment postureOfAnimal "(#$postureOfAnimal AN POS) means that the #$Animal AN is an instance of the #$AnimalTypeByPosture POS.")
(comment sequentialStructuralFeature "(#$sequentialStructuralFeature SEQ FEAT) means that the object

SEQ has the #$ObjectTypeBySequentialStructuralFeature FEAT. In the creator's

intention, the arg1 constraint would properly be (#$CollectionUnionFn

(#$TheSet (#$Path-Spatial #$PhysicalSeries))), but the use of collection

NATs in arg constraints is discouraged -- hence the choice of a

common generalization of the relevant collections.")
(comment terrainType "The predicate #$terrainType is used to describe the type of terrain in a particular geographical region. (#$terrainType REGION TYPE) means that #$GeographicalRegion REGION is an instance of the #$RegionTypeByTerrain TYPE.  For example, (#$terrainType #$Matterhorn-Mount #$RuggedTerrain) means that the Matterhorn has a rugged terrain.")
(comment rank-Military "(#$rank-Military PERSON RANK) means that the military person PERSON has the rank RANK in some branch of the military service of a country.  (Usually a person has only one rank, but certain persons may have different ranks in different services, or even in different countries, as when a US Army captain is also a colonel in the Soviet KGB.)  See also #$militaryRank-CountryAndBranchOfService.")
(comment verdict "The result of a trial.  If it's a criminal trial, the result is either #$GuiltyVerdict or #$NotGuiltyVerdict.")
(comment actionHasLegalityStatus "(#$actionHasLegalityStatus ACT STATUS) means that in the surrounding culture, the action ACT has been judged by the established court system to have the legality status STATUS.")
(comment computerPutToUse "The predicate #$computerPutToUse relates a type  of computer to a collection of computers representing a type of use that a computer is put to. It is used to indicate a function a type of  computer is meant to serve.  (#$computerPutToUse COMP USE-COLL) means that computers of the type COMP typically serve the function USE-COLL.")
(comment protectsFromActionBy "(#$protectsFromActionBy TANG PROTECTED ACTIONTYPE DOER) says that the #$PartiallyTangible TANG protects the #$PartiallyTangible PROTECTED from any instance ACTION of ACTIONTYPE where (#$doneBy ACTION DOER).")
(comment EffectiveForPurpose "An instance of #$COAEvaluationEffectivenessPredicate.. In its intended use, it
characterizes a type of action, event or state of affairs as being conducive to an
agent's goals. This sort of relation can be expressed by a clause of the
form (#$effectiveForPurpose ACT-TYPE PURPOSE-SENT) in the
`first-person's' or agent's perspective.")
(comment neutralForPurpose "An instance of #$EvaluativeEffectivenessPredicate. It can be used to characterize
actions, events or states of affairs that, considered as components of a
plan (e.g., a #$COASpecificationMicrotheory) neither negatively nor
positively affect the relevant's agent pursuit of the plan's
purpose.")
(comment IneffectiveForPurpose "An instance of #$COAEvaluationEffectivenessPredicate. In its intended use, it
characterizes a type of action, event or state of affairs that is seen as impeding
an agent's goals: this sort of relation can be expressed by a clause of the
form (#$ineffectiveForPurpose ACT-TYPE PURPOSE-SENT). Clearly, in a planning context we would expect
parts of plan not to be #$ineffectiveForPurpose, except perhaps if balanced
by other #$effectiveForPurpose parts of the same plan.")
(comment ProtectionSituation "A specialization of #$Situation.  Each instance of #$ProtectionSituation is a situation in which an instance of #$SomethingExisting is protected from some action that would otherwise damage  or compromise it.  Examples include intentional acts of protecting something (instances of #$ProtectingSomething), such as a military unit protecting some other unit from enemy attacks, as well as situations in which an unconscious person is protected from a small landside by virtue of being located behind a large rock.")
(comment protector-Generic "(#$protector-Generic PROTECT-SIT THING) says that THING is, intentionally or not, protecting, or keeping something safe during PROTECT-ACT. Depending on the nature of PROTECT-SIT, THING could be a bomb-shelter keeping its occupants safe, a turtle's shell keeping a turtle safe, or a person taking steps to keep someone out of harm's way.  If THING is an agent acting intentionally, then (#$protector-Agentive PROTECT-SIT THING).")
(comment protector-Agentive "(#$protector-Agentive PROTECT-ACT AGENT) says that AGENT is actively (intentionally) protecting something during PROTECT-ACT.")
(comment programAlgorithmInternals "(#$programAlgorithmInternals ALGORITHM . INTERNALS) relates ALGORITHM

to the list INTERNALS of the relevant instances of 

#$SoftwareParameter that are internal parameters to the algorithmic

process.")
(comment thingProtected-Generic "(#$thingProtected-Generic PROTECT-SIT OBJECT) means that during PROTECT-SIT OBJECT is protected.  Where PROTECT-SIT is an intentional act (i.e., an instance of #$ProtectingSomething), use the more specific #$objectProtected.")
(comment ToyDog "A specialization of #$Dog.  Each instance of #$ToyDog is a member of a small breed of dogs or of a variety smaller than the standard variety of its breed.  Examples include instances of #$ShihTzu and #$Chihuahua-Dog. As a rule, #$ToyDog types were developed as companion animals, rather than hunting or sporting dogs.")
(comment BrusselsGriffon "A specialization of #$ToyDog.  Members of this breed are descended from terriers.")
(comment Havanese-TheDog "A specialization of #$ToyDog.  The Havanese is the the national dog of #$Cuba.")
(comment Weekday "The collection of days that office workers and factory workers normally go to work and students (when school is in session) go to school.  In modern Western societies this includes only #$Mondays through #$Fridays.  In Islamic societies it would include #$Sundays but not #$Fridays.  In cultures in which there is only one day of rest, six days of the week would be #$Weekdays.")
(disjointWith Weekday CalendarWeekend)
(comment doOpenLocalVariablesScope "A variable arity complex action predicate. (#$doOpenLocalVariablesScope PROGRAMMER FILE . VARIABLES)
means that PROGRAMMER appends code to the end of computer file FILE 
that creates a scope in which VARIABLES are available.")
(comment programComment "A binary predicate. (#$programComment THING STRING) means that
any instance of #$ComputerCode-Source defining THING should also include
STRING as partial documentation.")
(comment doInitializeLocalVariables "A variable arity complex action predicate. (#$doInitializeLocalVariables PROGRAMMER FILE . VARIABLES)
means that PROGRAMMER appends code that declares and initializes VARIABLES to the end of computer file FILE.")
(comment doDeclareVariable "A ternary complex action predicate. 
(#$doDeclareVariable PROGRAMMER FILE VARIABLE) means that PROGRAMMER appends
code declaring VARIABLE (an instance of #$SoftwareParameter) to the end of the source code file FILE.")
(comment AlgorithmSequenceFn "An individual denoting function. 
(#$AlgorithmSequenceFn ALGORITHM ID) denotes the #$ProgramStepSequence of ALGORITHM that is identified by ID. For example, (#$AlgorithmSequenceFn #$AbsoluteValueComputation 2)
would denote Step Sequence 2 of the algorithm #$AbsoluteValueComputation.")
(comment doProgramStepSequence "A variable arity complex action predicate. (#$doProgramStepSequence PROGRAMMER FILE . STEPS) means that PROGRAMMER appends code to the end of FILE that implements STEPS, in the programming language determined by the current context. Each element of STEP is implemented in sequence.")
(comment MarbleCake "A specialization of #$Cake. Each instance of #$MarbleCake has a streaked apperance because it is the reuslt of baking mixed light and dark batter.")
(comment SodaCan "A specialization of #$DrinkingCan.  Each instance of #$SodaCan is a can in which soda is contained and sometimes drunk from.")
(comment BeerCan "A specialization of #$DrinkingCan.  Each instance of #$SodaCan is a can in which beer is contained and sometimes drunk from.")
(comment RockCandy "A specialization of #$Candy. Each instance of #$RockCandy is crystaline candy made by cooling a concentrated sugar syrup.")
(comment BirthdayPresent "A specialization of #$Gift.  Each instance of #$BirthdayPresent is a gift presented to a person to honor the recipient's birthday.")
(comment NoseRing "A specialization of #$Jewelry.  Each instance of #$NoseRing is jewelry intended to be worn on or through a nose.")
(comment CornSyrup "Corn syrup is a sweet sticky liquid made from #$CornPlant.")
(comment HotSpring "A specialization of #$Spring-WaterSource.  Each instance of #$HotSpring is a place from which a small stream of fresh water, warmed geothermally underground, flows.")
(comment ColdSpring "A specialization of #$Spring-WaterSource.  Each instance of #$ColdSpring is a place from which a small stream of cold fresh water flows.")
(comment memberData "A ternary predicate. (#$memberData STRUCT FIELDTYPE FIELDNAME)  means that the composite type STRUCT has a field FIELDNAME with type FIELDTYPE.")
(comment CFileObject "A specialization of #$DataStream. This is the FILE structure           used in POSIX compliant libraries like the GNU C Library.")
(comment STDOUT "An instance of #$CFileObject. This is the standard output           stream of a C program written with the standard libraries.")
(comment STDIN "An instance of #$CFileObject. This is the standard input           stream of a C program written with the standard libraries.")
(comment STDERR "An instance of #$CFileObject. This is the standard error           stream of a C program written with the standard libraries.")
(comment CHeaderFile "A specialization of #$ComputerProgramModule-CW. Each member of           this collection is a header file that can be included in a           C program with an #include directive.")
(comment fileTypicalPathname "A binary predicate that relates an instance of #$ComputerFile-Abstract to an instance of #$Pathname. (#$fileTypicalPathname PROGRAM PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a string that indicates a typical path for the standard installation of FILE. For example, (#$fileTypicalPathname #$StdIOHeaderFile \"stdio.h\") means that \"stdio.h\" is the typical pathname for the Standard C IO library header file.")
(comment moduleExportsProgramFunction "A binary predicate relating an instance of #$ComputerProgramModule-CW with an instance of #$ProgramStep. (#$moduleExportsProgramFunction MODULE FUNCTION) means that importing MODULE into a computer program allows that program to access FUNCTION as a program step.")
(comment ProgramStepArgFn "A binary denotational function. (#$ProgramStepArgFn STEP ID) denotes
the indexical formal parameter of STEP with identifier ID.")
(comment Solstice "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfYear.   Each instance of #$Solstice is an event during which the sun is at its greatest distance north or south of the celestial equator.   Each instance of #$Solstice occurs during an instance of #$SolsticeDay (q.v.).  One solstice occurs annually about June 21 (see #$JuneSolstice), and another occurs annually about December 21 (see #$DecemberSolstice).")
(comment DecemberSolstice "A specialization of #$Solstice.  Each instance of #$DecemberSolstice is an annual event during which the the sun is directly above the furthest point south of the celestial equator that it reaches during any given year.  Each instance of #$DecemberSolstice occurs annually about December 21, during some instance of #$DecemberSolsticeDay (q.v.).")
(comment JuneSolstice "A specialization of #$Solstice.  Each instance of #$JuneSolstice is an annual event during which the the sun is directly above the furthest point north of the celestial equator that it reaches during any given year.  Each instance of #$JuneSolstice occurs annually about June 21, during some instance of #$JuneSolsticeDay (q.v.)")
(comment AutumnalEquinox "A specialization of #$Equinox.   Each instance of #$AutumnalEquinox is an event during which the sun is directly above the celestial equator.   Each instance of #$AutumnalEquinox occurs annually about September 21, during an instance of #$AutumnalEquinoxDay (q.v.).")
(comment Equinox "A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfYear.   Each instance of #$Equinox is an event during which the sun is directly above the celestial equator.   Each instance of #$Equinox occurs during an instance of #$EquinoxDay (q.v.).  One equinox occurs annually about March 21 (see #$VernalEquinox), and another occurs annually about September 21 (see #$AutumnalEquinox).")
(comment VernalEquinox "A specialization of #$Equinox.   Each instance of #$VernalEquinox is an event during which the sun is directly above the celestial equator.   Each instance of #$Equinox occurs annually about March 21, during an instance of #$VernalEquinoxDay (q.v.).")
(comment Egg-Foodstuff "The stuff of which a bird's egg consists, whether cooked or not.")
(disjointWith Egg-Foodstuff Eggshell-Stuff)
(comment situationTypeIsSuchThat "This is a binary predicate used to relate a spec of #$Situation to the sentences which are characteristically true of this type of situation. (Also see #$SituationTypeSuchThatFn for more on individuating situation types in terms of the propositions which are true in them.)  This predicate and #$situationIsSuchThat are #$typeLevelCognates.")
(comment restaurantsMenuItem "(#$restaurantsMenuItem RESTAURANT FOOD) means that the Restaurant-Organization, RESTAURANT, offers FOOD as a type of food that patrons can obtain (generally by purchase) from it.  See also, #$restaurantsMenuItemPrice")
(comment restaurantsMenuItemPrice "(#$restaurantsMenuItemPrice RESTAURANT FOOD PRICE) means that the Restaurant-Organization, RESTAURANT, offers to sell instances of FOOD at the price PRICE (an instance of #$MonetaryValue).")
(comment CourseOfAction "This is a collection of situation types. An instance of this collection is a  particular military course of action, as might be specified (e.g.) in a #$COASpecificationMicrotheory (q.v.). In principle, there is a one-to-one correspondence between possible instances of #$CourseOfAction and possible instances of #$COASpecificationMicrotheory : the asserted contents of a given COASpecificationMicrotheory can be used to delimit a particular #$CourseOfAction (e.g. via a #$SituationTypeSuchThatFn NAT); and, for a given #$CourseOfAction, whatever propositions are characteristic of this situation type, these can be used to spell out the contents of a particular COASpecificationMicrotheory.")
(disjointWith CourseOfAction TimeOfDayType)
(comment assertedArg1BinaryPreds "(#$assertedArg1BinaryPreds COL BIN-PRED) means that for at least one instance INS of COL and one VALUE, it has been asserted that (BIN-PRED INS VALUE). Note that this predicate is itself #$notAssertible (q.v.), since it must be computed from the state of the knowledge base.")
(comment TheCOASpecification "This is an indexical concept which corresponds to the shared (common ground) knowledge about what actions are to be taken in the COA under discussion in a military discoruse context.")
(comment ingredients-Constituent "(#$ingredients-Constituent ARTIFACT INGREDIENT) means that INGREDIENT is one of the #$ingredients in ARTIFACT and permeates [see #$constituents] ARTIFACT.  This is the relationship, for example, between the dye in paint and the paint itself.")
(comment ingredients-Separable "The predicate #$ingredients-Separable is used to indicate a particular input used to make a particular artifact.  (#$ingredients-Separable ART INGR) means that the #$PartiallyTangible thing INGR was one of the #$inputs to the creation of the #$Artifact ART, and INGR is one of ART's #$physicalParts.  Note that #$ingredients-Separable applies only to those inputs which retain their identity in the creation process and which are incorporated into the resulting #$Artifact.  For example, a meatball can be considered an ingredient of a plate of spaghetti, and a portion of ground beef can be considered a separable ingredient of the meatball.  On the other hand, we would not say that an egg is a separable ingredient, but an #$ingredients-Destroyed, of the meatball, even though it was an input to the creation process, because its identity was not preserved.")
(comment ingredients-Destroyed "(#$ingredients-Destroyed ARTIFACT INGREDIENT) means that INGREDIENT was one of the #$inputsDestroyed in the creation of ARTIFACT and the matter that was in INGREDIENT remained part of ARTIFACT.")
(comment NovelSeries "A specialization of #$ConceptualWorkSeries.  Each instance of #$NovelSeries is a sequence of novels, each of which contributes to a larger story. Prominant examples include #$LordOfTheRings-Trilogy and #$HarryPotterNovelSeries.")
(comment Trilogy-NovelSeries "A specialization of #$NovelSeries.  Each instance of #$Trilogy-NovelSeries is a sequence of exactly 3 novels, each of which contributes to a larger story.")
(comment MovieScript "A narrative structured around scenes, actors speaking parts, direction, scene descriptions etc., to be acted out and recorded on film.")
(comment assertedArg1BinaryPreds-Count "(#$assertedArg1BinaryPreds-Count COL BIN-PRED N) means that there are N instances INS of COL for which it has been asserted for some VALUE (not necessarily the same for each) that (BIN-PRED INS VALUE). Note that this predicate is itself #$notAssertible (q.v.), since it must be computed from the state of the knowledge base.")
(comment startMeetsEndOf "(#$startMeetsEndOf POST ANTE) means that the endpoint of #$TemporalThing ANTE is simultaneous with the starting point of the #$TemporalThing POST.  ANTE and POST thus have one time point in common, which constitutes the main difference between this relation and #$contiguousAfter.")
(comment assertedArg1BinaryPreds-CountMin "(#$assertedArg1BinaryPreds-CountMin COL BIN-PRED N) means that there are at least N instances INS of COL for which it has been asserted for some VALUE (not necessarily the same for each) that (BIN-PRED INS VALUE). Note that this predicate is itself #$notAssertible (q.v.), since it must be computed from the state of the knowledge base.")
(comment assertedArg1BinaryPreds-PercentMin "(#$assertedArg1BinaryPreds-PercentMin COL BIN-PRED P) means that for at least P percent of the instances INS of COL it has been asserted for some VALUE (not necessarily the same for each) that (BIN-PRED INS VALUE). Note that this predicate is itself #$notAssertible (q.v.), since it must be computed from the state of the knowledge base.")
(comment CWInstantiating "A specialization of #$IBTGeneration.  Each instance of #$CWInstantiating is an event which instantiates some #$InformationBearingThing from some #$ConceptualWork.  See #$ibtGenerated, #$cWInstantiated, and #$instantiationOfWork.")
(comment cWInstantiated "(#$cWInstantiated CREATING CW) means that in CREATING, an instance of #$CWInstantiating, CW is the #$ConceptualWork for which an instantiation is created.  See #$ibtGenerated and #$instantiationOfWork.")
(comment keInducedStrongSuggestionPreds "(#$keStrongSuggestionPreds COL BIN-PRED) means that for every instance INS of COL, there is strongly suggested that there exist some VALUE such that (BIN-PRED INS VALUE) is known in the KB.")
(comment ProgramStepResultFn "A binary denotational function. (#$ProgramStepResultFn STEP N) denotes the indexical the value of which is the Nth returned value of STEP. For languages which do not allow multiple return values, only the value of (#$ProgramStepResultFn STEP 0) is returned.")
(comment Septum "A specialization of #$OrganismPart.  Each instance of #$Septum is a normally occurring thin partition or membrane that divides a cavity or mass of tissue in an organism.")
(disjointWith Septum Tumor)
(comment Lepton "A specialization of #$SubAtomicParticle.  Each instance of #$Lepton is a particle that does not interact with other particles in terms of the \"strong\" nuclear force, but does participate with other particles in terms of mass, electromagnetism, and the \"weak\" nuclear force.  Important subcollections of #$Lepton include #$Electron, #$Muon, #$Tau, and #$Neutrino.")
(disjointWith Lepton Nucleon)
(disjointWith Muon Neutrino)
(comment Muon "A specialization of #$Lepton.  Each instance of #$Muon is a lepton that is heavier than an electron, but not as heavy as a tau particle (see the collection #$Tau). Muon particles are unstable and exist in nature for a very short time.")
(disjointWith Muon Electron)
(disjointWith Tau Neutrino)
(comment Tau "A specialization of #$Lepton.  Each instance of #$Tau is a lepton that is heavier than either an electron or a muon (see the collection #$Muon).  Tau particles are unstable and exist in nature for a very short time.")
(disjointWith Tau Electron)
(disjointWith Muon Tau)
(comment MuonNeutrino "A specialization of #$Neutrino.  Along with #$TauNeutrino and #$ElectronNeutrino, one of the three specializations of #$Neutrino.")
(comment TauNeutrino "A specialization of #$Neutrino.  Along with #$MuonNeutrino and #$ElectronNeutrino, one of the three specializations of #$Neutrino.")
(disjointWith MuonNeutrino TauNeutrino)
(comment ElectronNeutrino "A specialization of #$Neutrino.  Along with #$MuonNeutrino and #$TauNeutrino, one of the three specializations of #$Neutrino.")
(disjointWith TauNeutrino ElectronNeutrino)
(disjointWith MuonNeutrino ElectronNeutrino)
(comment ElectronMicroscope "A specialization of #$ScientificInstrument.  Each instance of #$ElectronMicroscope uses a beam of electrons (see #$Electron) to examine objects on a very small scale.")
(comment CycHLSupportDatastructure "A specialization of both #$CycHLDatastructure and
#$CycSupportDatastructure (qq.v.) instances of which
are Cyc HL datastructures that can be used as supports
within a #$CycDeductionDatastructure (q.v.).")
(comment MonoatomicIon "A specialization of #$Atom and #$Ion.  Each instance of #$MonoatomicIon is an atom with a net positive or negative electrical charge, as a consequense of having an imbalance of #$Protons and #$Electrons.")
(comment Comprehending "A specialization of #$Learning.  Each instance of #$Comprehending is an event in which an agent understands the nature and significance of a concept.  Typically the agent who comes to comprend a concept in an instance of #$Comprehending will thereafter continue to comprehend the concept understood.")
(disjointWith Comprehending BodyMovementEvent)
(disjointWith Comprehending Conveying-Generic)
(comment thingComprehended "This predicate relates an event in which something is understood to the thing that is therein understood.  (#$thingComprehended COMPREHENSION CONCEPT) means CONCEPT (an instance of #$Thing) is understood by the performer of COMPREHENSION (an instance of #$Comprehending).")
(comment constantGUID "A strictly functional slot used to associate CycL constants with the GUID strings they are assigned. <code>(#$constantGUID CONSTANT GUID-STR)</code> means that the #$CycLConstant <code>CONSTANT</code> is associated with the #$GUIDString <code>GUID-STR</code>.  The arg1 position of this predicate is \"quoted\", so, for example, (#$constantGUID #$Dog  \"bd58daa0-9c29-11b1-9dad-c379636f7270\") means that the constant \"#$Dog\" itself, not the collection of dogs, is associated with the #$GUIDString \"bd58daa0-9c29-11b1-9dad-c379636f7270\".")
(disjointWith MeetingWithOrganizationalOutsiders StaffMeeting)
(comment MeetingWithOrganizationalOutsiders "The collection of all business meetings held on behalf of an organization by members of that organization with people who are not members of the organization.")
(comment damsStream "This predicate relates a dam to the river or stream on which it is found.  (#$damsStream DAM STREAM) means that the #$Dam DAM dams the #$Stream STREAM.")
(comment meetingAnnouncementText "(#$meetingAnnouncementText MEETING ANNOUNCEMENT_TEXT_STRING) means that ANNOUNCEMENT_TEXT_STRING is a 
text string that announces and describes the specified meeting.  IBTs with the announcement text are distributed
before the meeting.")
(comment DisplayingALink "The collection of all events in which a computer program
displays a #$ComputerDisplayLink. This link communicates an available
option to the user, and when activated by the user performs some task.
For example, when a user activates a #$ComputerDisplayContinueLink,
the program continues a temporarily suspended action.")
(disjointWith OrganizationWithClients OrganizationWithoutClients)
(comment OrganizationWithClients "The collection of #$Organization which regularly provide goods or services to non-members, as opposed to organizations that do not provide goods or services to non-members [see #$clients,  #$OrganizationWithoutClients].  Important specializations are #$OrganizationWithIndividualClients and #$OrganizationWithBusinessClients.")
(comment OrganizationWithDeliveryService "The collection of organizations that provide at least some of their #$clients with goods off site, via one or more instances of #$ShippingOrganization, which may or may not be their own #$subOrganizations.")
(comment HylobatidaeFamily "A specialization of #$Primate.  The collection #$HylobatidaeFamily is the family to which Gibbons belong; it is closely related to the #$HominidaeFamily (to which the other #$Apes and humans belong).")
(comment SCIInformation "All #$Classification-US information that receives the classification 'SCI'.")
(comment WrittenReportOnSituation-CW "The collection of the semantic contents of all 'prose' conceptual works that are intended to be factual reports describing situations that obtain or have obtained at the time of the report, and which are intended primarily to inform a target audience of the existence and nature of said situation.")
(comment intendedVictim "(#$intendedVictim ACTION AGENT) means that ACTION is a #$PurposefulAction such that its performer #$intends that (#$victim ACTION AGENT).")
(comment hitMiddleman "'(#$hitMiddleman HIT PERSON)' means that PERSON is a mediator in HIT.")
(comment ownsObjectType "'(#$ownsObjectType AGENT OBJ-TYPE)' means that AGENT owns an instance of OBJ-TYPE.")
(comment PlanningAMurder "This is the collection of acts in which a murder is planned.  It is more general than #$PlanningTheKilling, which is the planning performed by the hitman.")
(comment CriminalHarassment "The collection of criminal acts in which someone is harassed in such a way or to such an extent that it makes the perpetrator of the act criminally liable.")
(comment HospitalIndustry "The collection of all #$Hospital industries.")
(comment TruckDealership "The collection of all stores that sell #$Trucks to consumers.")
(disjointWith HotelOrganization BedAndBreakfast)
(comment HotelOrganization "The collection of organizations which run a #$HotelBuilding.")
(comment AluminumIndustry "The collection of all instances of #$Industry-Localized whose primary function is the production of #$Aluminum.")
(comment ContractKilling-Threat "This is the collection of contract killings in which a threat is killed.")
(comment ContractKilling-Obstacle "This is the collection of contract killings in which an obstacle to power is killed.")
(comment ContractKilling-Rival "This is the collection of contract killings in which a mafia rival is killed.")
(comment Counting "A specialization of #$Evaluating. Each instance of this collection is an event in which at least one agent evaluates the numeric quantity of some (reasonably discrete) group of things by assigning numbers to each thing in turn. The items counted may not necessarily be tangible objects (e.g, \"close your eyes and count to 10\", a mathematician counting the number of primes between 1 and 100). See also #$CountingDevice.")
(comment DevisingAPracticeOrWork "A specialization of #$CreationEvent. Each instance of this collection is an event in which at least one instance of #$DevisedPracticeOrWork is brought into existence. Examples include the invention of the game chess, the invention of the sport basketball, and the cultural event in which monetary exchange originated.  Cf. the more specialized collection, #$CWCreation.")
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork Landing)
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork BiologicalEvent)
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork PhysiologicalProcess)
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork ChemicalReaction)
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion)
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork Cleaning)
(disjointWith DevisingAPracticeOrWork OutOfControlCrowdEvent)
(comment nthInOrdering "A predicate used to identify an object's placement in a mathematical ordering.  (#$nthInOrdering ORDERING THING N) says that THING is the Nth object in the ordering ORDERING.  THING must be an element of the #$baseSet for ORDERING.")
(comment OrderingsOfTypeFn "An instance of #$PartialFunction.  (#$OrderingsOfTypeFn TYPE) denotes the collection of all orderings of the elements of TYPE.")
(comment PartialOrderingAlongClassificationFn "(#$PartialOrderingAlongClassificationFn SETORCOL PRED) denotes the ordering ORDERING of all of the elements of SETORCOL such that (#$orderingRelations ORDERING PRED).")
(comment PartialOrderingAlongFn "A binary function that returns an ordering relation.  (#$PartialOrderingAlongFn POP KEY) denotes a predicate that represents an ordering on the element of a set generated by applying the KEY to the elements of a set.  So where (#$baseSet ORDERING (#$TheSet #$JonC #$Kahlert #$Goolsbey)), (#$orderingRelations ORDERING (#$PartialOrderingAlongFn #$lessThan #$zipCode)) says that the #$PartialOrdering ORDERING is ordered by the zip codes of Jon, Robert, and Keith, where the smallest zip code is first, the next smallest second, and largest last.")
(comment resultSetCardinality "This predicate is used by the SKSI code to estimate the cost of querying the fields of a physical schema.  More precisely, let SCHEMA denote a physical schema, let PF-1, PF-2, ..., PF-N, denote the physical fields of SCHEMA, and let SELECT_SET and WHERE_SET denote two disjoint subsets of (#$TheSet PF-1 PF-2 ... PF-N). Then (#$resultSetCardinality SCHEMA SELECT_SET WHERE_SET NUM) means that NUM is the average cardinality of the result set returned from a query in which the fields in SELECT_SET left unbound and the fields of WHERE_SET are bound.")
(comment OpenCyc-CurrentVersion "The singleton collection consisting of the current version of OpenCyc as present in this instance of Cyc.")
(comment CourseOfAction-Offensive "The collection of all situation types that are offensive military courses of action, as might be specified (e.g.) in a #$COASpecificationMicrotheory.")
(comment CourseOfAction-Defensive "The collection of all situation types that are defensive military courses of action, as might be specified (e.g.) in a #$COASpecificationMicrotheory.")
(disjointWith CourseOfAction-Offensive CourseOfAction-Defensive)
(comment Morale "The collection of attributes expressing the morale level of
a group of agents or an individual.  Morale is the mental condition which is
characterized by enthusiasm and a willingness to endure hardships for
some higher goal.")
(comment levelOfMorale "(#$levelOfMorale ?AGT ?LEVEL) relates an #$IntelligentAgent to
the level of morale the agent is operating under in a given context.")
(comment ForestTypeByDensity "A collection of collections that classify #$Forests by the
density of tree growth.")
(comment LightForest "The collection of all #$Forests whose tree growth may be
said to be light. This means there is a greater amount of space in
between the trees as compared to other forests, see also
#$HeavyForest.")
(comment HeavyForest "The collection of all #$Forests whose tree growth may be
said to be heavy or dense as compared to other forests.")
(comment deprecatedSalientDescriptorRule "(#$deprecatedSalientDescriptorRule RULE) means that RULE should not be used to generate Salient Descriptor interview questions.")
(comment deprecatedSalientDescriptorRuleAssertion "(#$deprecatedSalientDescriptorRule RULE) means that RULE should not be used to generate Salient Descriptor interview questions.")
(comment CityTypeByCountry "A collection of collections.  Instances of #$CityTypeByCountry are collections of cities.  Each such collection COL is associated with a particular instance of  #$IndependentCountry IND, such that for all instances CITY  of COL, (#$countryOfCity IND COL) holds.  See  #$CityInCountryFn.")
(comment BloodDrive "The collection of all blood drives.  This refers to the event at which a number of people donate blood, not to the advertising campaign to urge them to donate.")
(comment keIrrelevantRedundantNART "(#$keIrrelevantRedundantNART NART)
 means that because there is an atomic term FORT such that 
 (#$rewriteOf FORT NART), printed representations of NART should not show up in any RKF-based interface.")
(comment WindowOffice "The collection of #$OfficeRooms which have a window to space the outside of the building in which they are situated, including windows that face across an open courtyard to another part of the same building.")
(comment Cubicle-Office "The collection of all partitioned-off workspaces in an #$OfficeSpace with walls that do not reach the ceiling and without doors.")
(comment OpenCycRelease "A specialization of #$CycTheCollection.  #$OpenCycRelease is the collection of all copies of released versions of the #$OpenCyc program.")
(comment USArmyDivision "#$USArmyDivision is the collection of all divisions within the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment temporallyRelated "This is the most general temporal predicate. <code>(#$temporallyRelated THING-1 THING-2)</code> means that the #$TemporalThing <code>THING-1</code> and the #$TemporalThing <code>THING-2</code> are somehow temporally related, without specifying a more exact relationship.  <code>THING-1</code> may be after, before, simultaneous with, temporally disjoint with, or temporally overlapping with <code>THING-2</code>.  Generally, <code>(#$temporallyRelated THING-1 THING-2)</code> implies that some more substantial instance of #$TemporalPredicate (q.v.) holds between <code>THING-1</code> and <code>THING-2</code>.")
(comment OrganizedCrimeOrganization "The collection of all #$CriminalOrganizations that are characterized by rigid and extensive hierarchical structure and perpetuation of activities traditionally associated with 'organized crime', e.g. #$MoneyLaundering, #$TaxEvasion, #$LoanSharking, #$MurderForHire, #$Smuggling, #$Prostitution, etc.. Many, but not all of these organizations have 'transnational' scope; neither are all #$TransnationalCriminalOrganizations #$OrganizedCrimeOrganizations in the traditional sense.")
(comment MafiaOrganization-ItalianAmerican "The collection of all 'traditional' Mafia organizations that
currently have their #$residenceOfOrganization within the geographical
boundaries of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.")
(comment MafiaOrganization-SicilianOrigin "The collection of all organizations to which the name 'Mafia' traditionally applies: i.e., transnational criminal organizations based in Europe and the United States that can trace their lineage back to the Sicilian criminal organizations of the nineteenth century.  Ethnic Italians or Italian-Americans usually comprise a large proportion of the membership of such organizations.")
(comment MafiaOrganization-Italian "The collection of all 'traditional' Mafia organizations that currently have their #$residenceOfOrganization within the geographical boundaries of #$Italy.")
(comment USArmyBrigade "#$USArmyBrigade is the collection of all brigades within the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment Icepick "The collection of handtools with a sharp, pointed pick used to chip off pieces from a large block of ice.")
(comment Glyph "The collection of all instances of 
#$CharacterToken, each instance of which is the 
#$tokensCharacter for a unique instance of #$Glyph-Abstract.")
(comment NewspaperArticle "The collection of articles published in a #$NewspaperIssue.")
(comment deviceTypeUsed "'(#$deviceTypeUsed ACT TYPE)' means that a #$PhysicalDevice of type TYPE is used in the #$Event ACT.  This predicate is a type-level version of #$deviceUsed in its second argument.")
(comment VeryLowToLowAmountFn "A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$VeryLowToLowAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>very-low-to-low amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.
<p>
For example, (#$VeryLowToLowAmountFn #$Happiness) is the range of happiness from very low to low happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$NoAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness).
<p>
See also the corresponding predicate #$veryLowToLowAmountOf.")
(comment USArmyBattalion "#$USArmyBattalion is the collection of all battalions within the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment USArmyPlatoon "The collection of all platoons within the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment USArmyCompany "The collection of all companies within the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment USArmySquadron "The collection of all squads within the #$UnitedStatesArmy.")
(comment ArmoredCavalrySquadron "The collection of all #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon level
#$ArmoredCavalryUnit-MilitarySpecialty.")
(comment AviationAttackBattalion "The collection of #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon level
#$AviationUnit-MilitarySpecialty intended for use in attck
operations.")
(comment GeneralSupportArtilleryBattalion "The collection of all #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon level
#$ArtilleryUnit-MilitarySpecialty tasked with
#$ProvidingGeneralSupport.")
(comment ArmoredBattalion "The collection of all #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon level
#$ArmoredUnit-MilitarySpecialty.")
(comment DirectSupportArtilleryBattalion "The collection of all #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon level #$ArtilleryUnit-MilitarySpecialty tasked with
#$ProvidingDirectSupport.")
(comment MechanizedInfantryCompany "The collection of #$Company-MilitaryEchelon that are also
MechanizedInfantryUnit-MilitarySpecialty.")
(comment CommittingTheMurder "This is the killing scene in the script for #$PremeditatedMurder.")
(comment PremeditatingTheMurder "This is the premeditation scene in the script for #$PremeditatedMurder.")
(comment premeditatedMurderer "'(#$premeditatedMurderer MURDER KILLER)' means that KILLER is the #$perpetrator of the #$PremeditatedMurder MURDER.")
(comment murderVictim-Premeditated "'(#$murderVictim-Premeditated MURDER VICTIM)' means that VICTIM is the intended victim of the #$PremeditatedMurder MURDER.")
(comment DeathOfTheVictim "This is the dying scene in the script for #$PremeditatedMurder.")
(comment attackedGang "'(#$attackedGang ATTACK GANG)' means that GANG is the criminal organization whose member is killed in the contract killing ATTACK.")
(comment GangWarAttack "The first scene in a #$GangWarCycle in which one of the gangs kills one of the other gang's members, which constitutes an attack on the other gang.")
(comment attackingGang "'(#$attackingGang ATTACK GANG)' means that GANG is the criminal organization which directs the contract killing ATTACK.")
(comment GangWarRetaliation "The last scene in a #$GangWarCycle in which the gang attacked in the #$GangWarAttack retaliates by killing one of the members of the attacking gang.")
(comment GangWarCycle "This is the script for the iterated cycle of attack and retaliation which makes up a #$GangWar.")
(comment iterationOfEventType "'(#$iterationOfEventType TYPE1 TYPE2)' means that TYPE1 is a type of event whose instances consist of iterations of events of TYPE2.  For example, '(#$iterationOfEventType #$Walking-Generic #$TakingAStep)' says that a walk is a series of steps.")
(comment hitGang "'(#$hitGang HIT GANG)' means that GANG is the criminal organization which is retaliated against for a previous #$GangWarAttack in the contract killing HIT.")
(comment retaliatingGang "'(#$retaliatingGang RETALIATE GANG)' means that GANG is the criminal organization which retaliates against a previous #$GangWarAttack by the contract killing RETALIATE.")
(comment Petnapping "This is the collection of acts in which someone's pet is stolen.")
(comment Stilt-Shaft "The collection of all long poles with footrests used for walking with the body significantly higher than it would be if the stilts were not used.  The stilts extend above the footrests and may either be bound to the legs or held onto with the hands.")
(comment programCompilesLanguage "#$programCompilesLanguage is a binary predicate that relates a computer program to a computer language.  (#$programCompilesLanguage PROG LANG) means that PROG, an instance of #$Compiler, interprets programs written in LANG, an instance of #$AbstractProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment IterativeSituationType "This is the collection of all specializations of #$Situation which contain an iterated sub-situation types.")
(comment iteratedProperSubSituationTypes "A specialization of #$properSubSituationTypes. (#$iteratedProperSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE) means that SUBSIT-TYPE is an iterated sub-situation type in SIT-TYPE.  The iteration termination condition can be described using #$iterationTerminalEventType (q.v.).")
(comment agentCaptured "'(#$agentCaptured CAPTURE AGENT)' means that AGENT is captured in the #$CapturingSomething CAPTURE.")
(comment CommentPrefix-SingleLine "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. This token is used, in
programming languages that support it, to signal that everything between it and the next newline should be ignored during processing.")
(comment CommentPrefix-MultipleLine "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. This token is used, in
programming languages that support it, to signal that everything between it and the next occurance of #$CommentSuffix-MultipleLine should be ignored during processing.")
(comment CommentSuffix-MultipleLine "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. This token is used, in
programming languages that support it, to signal that everything between the
last occurance of #$CommentPrefix-MultipleLine and itself should be ignored 
during processing.")
(comment tupleMemberIndex "A predicate that relates a given tuple -- which is essentially a set whose members are indexed in some way -- and one of its members (see #$memberOfTuple) to the member's associated index (see #$tupleIndexSet). (#$tupleMemberIndex TUPLE MEMBER INDEX) means that INDEX serves to index or represent the position of MEMBER in TUPLE.  Each element of TUPLE is associated with at least one element of the index set, and no element of the index set is associated with more than one member of TUPLE.  Note that MEMBER might be indexed by more than one element of the index set, as a given member can have more than one \"appearance\" within a tuple.  The complete predicate extent of #$tupleMemberIndex with respect to TUPLE in effect represents a function (TUPLE's \"indexing function\") from the index set onto the set of TUPLE's members.")
(comment BlockCloseToken "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. This token is used to signal a close of the current block of program steps.")
(comment BlockOpenToken "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. This token is used to signal the openning of a block of program steps.")
(comment FunctionDefinition-Start "An instance of #$ProgramSyntaxObject. #$FunctionDefinition-Start denotes for a contextually determined programming language the sequence of tokens that would signal the start of a function definition. For example, in #$CommonLisp, #$FunctionDefinition-Start would
denote the token \"defun\".")
(comment immigrant "(#$immigrant COUNTRY PERSON) says that PERSON is an immigrant to COUNTRY.  This entails that PERSON has played the #$performedBy Role in an instance of #$Immigration where COUNTRY is the #$toLocation.")
(comment hasOffice "(#$hasOffice PERSON OFFICE) means that PERSON regularly uses OFFICE, an instance of #$OfficeRoom as his or her office.")
(comment frequentlyFoundInLocation "(#$frequentlyFoundInLocation OBJECT LOC) means that the #$PartiallyTangible OBJECT often is located in [#$objectFoundInLocation] the #$EnduringThing-Localized LOC.")
(comment memberOfList "A binary predicate that relates each one of the things in a given #$List to the list itself.  #$memberOfList is thus analogous to #$elementOf: whereas the latter relates a set to its members, this predicate relates a list to its members.  (#$memberOfList MEMBER LIST) means that MEMBER appears at least once (perhaps multiple times) in LIST.   For example, (#$TheList A B C B) is related by #$memberOfList to each of its three members: A, B, and C.  See also #$SetOfListMembersFn.")
(comment ArmyDivision "The collection of all instances of #$Division-MilitaryEchelon that are divisions in an instance of #$Army-MilitaryEchelon.")
(comment DivisionTypeByServiceBranch "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$DivisionTypeByServiceBranch is a specialization of #$Division-MilitaryEchelon whose instances are associated to a particular instance of #$BranchOfMilitaryService.")
(comment componentOfPathSystem "This is the most general binary predicate that relates instances of #$PathSystem to their \"components\".   <code>(#$componentOfPathSystem COMPONENT SYSTEM)</code> means that <code>COMPONENT</code> is either a point on a path in <code>SYSTEM</code>, or a node, a cycle, a sub-system, or a path in <code>SYSTEM</code>.  Each path system has at least one component, since each path system has at least one point in it (see #$pointInSystem).")
(comment doOutputBlockOpen "A binary complex action predicate. 
(#$doOutputBlockOpen PROGRAMMER FILE) means that PROGRAMMER appends
a symbol or symbols to the end of FILE, which signals the beginning of a 
program code block.")
(comment roomFaces "(#$roomFaces MY_BEDROOM THE_STAIRS) means that the doorway to the room, MY_BEDROOM, faces, across another part of the building THE_STAIRS, which is another part of the same structure.")
(comment ForEachInListDoInParallelFn "A ternary denotational function. (#$ForEachInListDoInParallelFn PARAM SET STEP) denotes a #$ProgramStep
in which STEP is executed once for every element of LIST, with PARAM dynamically bound to each
element. There is *no* assumed ordering of how the elements of LIST are processed through STEP.")
(comment ForEachInListDoSeriallyFn "A ternary denotational function. (#$ForEachInListDoSeriallyFn PARAM SET STEP) denotes a #$ProgramStep
in which STEP is executed once for every element of LIST, with PARAM dynamically bound to each
element. It is assumed that each element LIST is processed in order, starting from the first 
(head) element.")
(comment ForEachInSetDoFn "A ternary denotational function. (#$ForEachInSetDoFn PARAM SET STEP) denotes a #$ProgramStep
in which STEP is executed once for every element of SET, with PARAM dynamically bound to each
element. There is no assumed ordering of how the elements of SET are processed through STEP.")
(comment Intranet "A kind of #$LocalAreaNetwork which makes use of internet technology such as TCP/IP, web servers etc. Often set up and administered within a company (see the more specialized #$InternalBusinessNetwork).")
(comment SpecialShellParameter "A specialization of #$SoftwareParameter. #$SpecialShellParameter is the collection of all parameters that
are treated exceptionally by programs interpreting scripts. Positive
examples would be the \"$$\" variable in shell scripting, which always
denotes the current process ID, and the \"$_\" variable in Perl, which
refers to the default input, output and pattern matching space.")
(comment IndexicalCloneFn "(#$IndexicalCloneFn THING) denotes a contextually unique entity that is an instance of every collection that THING is an instance of.")
(comment importedSketch "A specialization of #$genlMt, used to relate an interaction context (See #$RKFInteractionContextMicrotheory) with a nuSketch-created microtheory (see #$QualitativeState).")
(comment AssistantsContactLocation "A specialization of #$ContactLocation.   Each instance of #$AssistantsContactLocation is a location at which some particular person may be contacted by any of several means, through contact with that person's assistant(s).  The assistant screens calls, emails, faxes, or other communications and relays information to the to the boss.")
(comment VoiceMailDevice "The collection of all voice mail devices.  They are normally (2001) part of a telephone system and handle multiple telephone lines for telephones in different locations.  They are distinct from telephone #$AnsweringMachines which are associated with a single telephone line.")
(comment AllowsNullSessions "A specialization of #$AllowsAnonymousLogin. A program on a computer has this attribute if it is a #$WindowsMachine which permits so-called \"null sessions\" (designed for use by the SYSTEM account to obtain system information from another computer but, unfortunately, able to be logged into by hackers as well.)")
(comment ArmoredBrigade "The collection of all #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon level
#$ArmoredUnit-MilitarySpecialty.")
(comment MechanizedInfantryBrigade "The collection of all #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon level
#$MechanizedInfantryUnit-MilitarySpecialty.")
(comment ArmoredDivision "The collection of all echelons at the division level that
include armorded units within their ranks.")
(comment Keratin "The collection of all keratin, a type of fibrous protein that form the structure of horny epidermal tissues such as hair and nails.  Keratin contains sulfur.")
(disjointWith Keratin Collagen)
(disjointWith Keratin Gelatin)
(disjointWith Keratin Cytokine)
(comment Hair-Stuff "The collection of the material of which #$Hair-Strands are composed.")
(comment Melanin "The collection of all melanin, a pigment found in skin, eyes, and hair of #$Vertebrates.  #$Melanin protects skin from ultraviolet light, and naturally occurs in higher concentrations in ethnic groups native to the tropics than in those native to higher latitudes.  The lack of melanin in the body causes albinism.")
(comment Win2000Machine "A specialization of #$NTMachine. Each instance of this collection is a #$Computer that runs (#$runsOS) some version of #$MSWindows2000.")
(comment Panting "The collection of all events in which a mammal, such as a dog, cools itself by rapid breathing in and out with the tongue sticking out, allowing increased evaporation from the tongue and inner mouth surfaces.")
(comment OutsideDog "The collection of all pet #$Dogs who live a significant fraction of their lives outdoors.")
(comment BakingChocolate-Unsweetened "The collection of all unsweetened baking chocolate that is used for making chocolate-flavored foods.")
(comment VulnerableBecauseNoActivityLogging "A computer has this vulnerability if it has a #$ComputerAuditLogFile which, for some reason, is not being used (to log at least some of the events it should log). Thus no records, or faulty records, are kept of activities performed on that computer, including those performed by attackers. Where this occurs because the computer is running software with an #$ActivityLogBypassFault, see the more specialized #$VulnerableToActivityLogBypass.")
(comment programRunAtStartup "(#$programRunAtStartup PROG COMP) means that a copy of PROG (see #$ComputerProgramCopy) is stored on COMP, and this copy will be run whenever COMP is first switched on. A prime example of such programs is instances of #$OperatingSystem (but also instances of #$TrojanHorseComputerProgram). See also #$programCopyRunAtStartup, which relates a computer to a specific program copy, rather than a program type.")
(comment XPMachine "The collection of all #$Computers that run (#$runsOS) some version of #$MSWindowsXP. See also #$Win2000Machine, #$NTMachine and #$WindowsMachine.")
(comment programCopyRunAtStartup "(#$programCopyRunAtStartup COPY COMPUTER) means that COPY is stored on COMPUTER (e.g., on disk or in memory) and is run whenever COMPUTER is first switched on. A prime example of such programs is instances of #$OperatingSystem (but also instances of #$TrojanHorseComputerProgram). See also #$programRunAtStartup.")
(comment PartiallyCommutativePredicate "The intersection of #$Predicate and #$PartiallyCommutativeRelation.  Each instance of #$PartiallyCommutativePredicate is a predicate which is commutative with respect to some, but not all, of its arguments.  Instances of #$PartiallyCommutativePredicate must therefore have an #$arity
of at least 3.  See also #$CommutativePredicate.")
(comment CommutativePredicate "The intersection of #$Predicate and #$CommutativeRelation.  Each instance of #$CommutativePredicate is a predicate, binary or of greater arity, which is commutative in all of its argument-places.  See also #$PartiallyCommutativePredicate.")
(disjointWith SubLNonVariableNonKeywordSymbol SubLKeyword)
(comment SubLNonVariableNonKeywordSymbol "The collection of all #$SubLSymbols except #$SubLVariables (q.v.) and #$SubLKeywords (q.v.); a subcollection of #$CycLClosedAtomicTerm.  Note that `symbol' has a very specific, technical meaning in SubL; #$SubLNonVariableNonKeywordSymbols are rarely used in CycL assertions, except within those built with certain #$CycInferenceDescriptorPredicates like #$defnIff. Examples of SubL non-variable non-keyword symbols include the symbols `GENLS' and `CYC-SYSTEM-NON-VARIABLE-NON-KEYWORD-SYMBOL-P'.  Note that this collection, like most instances of #$CycLExpressionType, is \"quoted\" (see #$quotedCollection).")
(comment muchCloserToThan "(#$muchCloserToThan THING CLOSER FARTHER) says that the distance between CLOSER and THING is much less than the distance between FARTHER and THING.")
(comment functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate "A #$MetaRelation that is used to state that the referenced predicate relates the referent of the referenced function applied to a list of arguments and those arguments, themselves, with the argument order for the predicate being described by a list of the order in which the corresponding arguments appear in the function (with \"0\" denoting the functional result.  For example, (#$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate #$natArgument #$FormulaArgFn (#$TheList 2 1 0)) means that means that (#$natArgument NAT N (#$FormulaArgFn N NAT)).
<p>
The argument and result restrictions on FUNC must be equal to or more specific than the corresponding argument restrictions on PRED.
<p>
If PRED is functional in the argument which corresponds to the function denotation, use the more specific predicate, #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate-Symmetric.")
(comment functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate-Symmetric "A #$MetaRelation that is used to state that the referenced predicate relates the referent of the referenced function applied to a list of arguments and those arguments, themselves, with the argument order for the predicate being described by a list of the order in which the corresponding arguments appear in the function (with \"0\" denoting the functional result).
<p>
The argument and result restrictions on FUNC must be the same as the corresponding argument restrictions on PRED.  The predicate must be functional in the argument place corresponding to the functional result, allowing the value of the function for a list of arguments to be derived from an assertion based on the predicate.
<p>
For example, (#$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate #$PerFn #$multiplicationUnits (#$TheList 0 2 1)) means that means that (#$multiplicationUnits (#$PerFn METERS SECONDS) SECONDS METERS) for SECONDS and METERS being any instance of #$UnitOfMeasure.")
(comment VulnerableBecauseNoActivityLogging-NTScheduling "A specialization of  #$VulnerableBecauseNoActivityLogging. A computer has this vulnerability if its #$ComputerAuditLogFile does not record uses of the Scheduling command.")
(comment functionCorrespondsToPredicate "A #$MetaRelation and specialization of #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic that is used to state that the relationship of the given function to its arguments is expressed by the given predicate.
<p>
(#$functionCorrespondsToPredicate FUNC PRED N) means that FUNC maps a given argument-sequence ARGS (say of length M) to the value VAL only if PRED holds of the (M+1 length) sequence ARGS-N-VAL, where ARGS-N-VAL is just like ARGS except for having VAL as its Nth member and having ARGS's Nth to Mth members as its (N+1)th to (M+1)th members.  If PRED is a #$FunctionalPredicate, with its Nth argument-place being a \"functional\" one (see #$functionalInArgs), then the more specific relationship, #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical (q.v.) applies.
<p>
For example, (#$functionCorrespondsToPredicate #$MovieNamedFn #$properNameStrings 1) implies that (#$properNameStrings (#$MovieNamedFn \"Gandhi\") \"Gandhi\"), but (#$properNameStrings #$MahatmaGandhi \"Gandhi\") does not mean that #$MahatmaGandhi and (#$MovieNamedFn \"Gandhi\") represent the same thing.  See also #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical, #$functionCorrespondingPredicate, #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate, and #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate-Symmetric.")
(comment enemies "(#$enemies AGENT1 AGENT2) means that AGENT1 and AGENT2 have a hostle regard towards each other and are likely to take actions against one another given the opportunity.")
(comment regionalTelephoneAreaCode "(#$regionalTelephoneAreaCode PLACE CODE) means that CODE is a telephone area (or city) code number for the #$GeopoliticalEntity or #$GeographicalRegion PLACE.  Most city/area codes are specific to specific country subsidaries, but some small island countries share a country code with a larger nearby country and have a unique city/area code for the whole country.  Most area/city codes are unique for their region of coverage, but the United States has started having multiple area codes covering the same regions.  Area/city codes cover both telephone and fax numbers, at least into the year 2002.")
(comment MetaFunction "A subcollection of #$MetaRelation (q.v.).  Instances of #$MetaFunction are used to make definitional (e.g. argument-type constraint or generalization) assertions about functions (and only functions).  As such, instances of #$MetaFunction have (at least) one of their own argument-places (usually, but not always, the first or \"arg1\") constrained to be instances of #$Function-Denotational (or some subcollection thereof).")
(comment ApacheDirective "A specialization of #$SoftwareParameter.  Instances of this collection are options that can be set in #$ApacheWebServerConfigurationFiles.")
(comment CycImage "The collection of all computer processes [#$ComputerProcess-Generic] which are instantiations of Cyc.  ")
(comment VulnerableBecauseProxyDisabled "A computer has this vulnerability if it is meant to use a proxy server in at least some contexts and this functionality has been disabled (for instance through the registry, if it is a Windows machine).")
(comment ComputerCodeTypeByExecutability "A collection of collections. Each instance is a spec of #$ComputerCode whose instances are all of the same executability.")
(comment MouseDriver "A piece of software which acts as an interface between a mouse and an operating system.")
(comment MalformedInputFn "(#$MalformedInputFn PROGRAM) denotes the collection of all instances of #$CharacterString which would count as malformed input to the computer program PROGRAM in some context (i.e. causing the program to break down or to behave in a way it was not designed to).  A common source of malformed input is the so-called \"shell metacharacters\" (e.g. ']', '{', '$', '\\n'). Sending carefully crafted malformed input to selected programs is a common  hacker strategy.")
(comment Null-TimeParameter "When a formula has #$Null-TimeParameter as its time parameter, no matter what time index T the formula has (be it a century or a second), we cannot infer the formula with any time index (except T itself) temporally subsumed by T.")
(comment MicrotheoryIndex "This is the collection of all microtheory indices, which are used in inference.  Sub-collections of this collection include #$ModalIndex, #$TimeInterval, #$History-ModalIndex and #$ModalReasoningMicrotheory etc.")
(disjointWith MicrotheoryIndex Situation)
(comment History-ModalIndex "This is a sub-collection of #$ModalIndex, the collection of all `history/world' modal indices, which are used in some modal inference.  Instances of this collection should be used to form `new' microtheories together with other modal indices such as those in #$TimeInterval and #$ReasoningWithMultiFutureMicrotheory.")
(comment CalendarWorkWeek "Instances of #$CalendarWorkWeek are #$Dates that are exactly composed of a five successive instances of #$CalendarDay, starting from an instance of #$Monday to one of #$Friday.  Note that this collection of time intervals is NOT a #$CalendarCoveringType : although the instances of #$CalendarWorkWeek are mutually disjoint (one of the two requirements of #$CalendarCoveringType), there are many #$TimePoints which are not in any #$CalendarWorkWeek (and so it violates the requirement that its instances completely cover all of time).")
(disjointWith CalendarWorkWeek CalendarSeason)
(comment TimeParameter "This is a sub-collection of #$ModalIndex.  Each instance of #$TimeParameter can be used as a `parameter of time index' of formulas. When a formula PROP is asserted, by hand or inferred, with time index (#$TimeInterval) t and time parameter (#$TimeParameter) p (and other indices), PROP is taken to be true at t as well as every time interval t0 that is properly subsumed by t such that (#$temporallySubsumes-InsType t0 p) holds (other indices fixed).  For example, suppose that a formula PROP is asserted with (in addition to indices i_0, ..., i_k) the time index 05/01/02 and the time parameter #$CalendarSecond.  This means that PROP is taken to be true not only at 05/01/02, but also at each calendar hour and each calendar second temporally subsumed by 05/01/02 (with all other indices i_0, ..., i_k fixed).  There are two kinds of time parameters, one kind is #$SameLengthTimeIntervalType, and each time parameter of this kind contains time intervals of the same length (the so-called `sliding' time parameters); and the other kind includes the rest of #$TimeParameter, and each time parameter of that kind contains time intervals, often disjoint, which are fixed on the time line.  Among the second type of time parameters, there is an important class, #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries, which is used for reasoning with metric tenses.")
(comment ModalIndex "This is the collection of all modal indices, a sub-collection of #$MicrotheoryIndex, which are used in some modal inference.  Sub-collections of this collection include #$TimeInterval, #$History-ModalIndex and #$ModalReasoningMicrotheory.")
(comment mtTimeIndexWithParameter "(#$mtTimeIndexWithParameter MT INTERVAL PARA) means that the instance MT of #$TemporalMicrotheory has INTERVAL as its value of the dimension `time index' and PARA as `time parameter'.  See also #$mtTimeIndex and #$mtTimeParameter.")
(comment mtTimeIndex "(#$mtTimeIndex MT INTERVAL) means that the instance MT of #$TemporalMicrotheory has INTERVAL as its value of the dimension `time index' and, if there is no corresponding #$mtTimeParameter assertion about MT, #$Null-TimeParameter as `time parameter', i.e., it means the same as (#$mtTimeIndexWithParameter MT INTERVAL #$Null-TimeParameter).")
(comment InsecureMailStorageFault-Local "A program has an #$InsecureMailStorageFault-Local if it allows unauthorized access to #$EMailFiles stored on the computer it is running on. See for instance the specialization  #$InsecureMailEncryptionFault.")
(comment vulnerableTo-RoleInType "A ternary #$VulnerabilityPredicate. (#$vulnerableTo-RoleInType OBJ TYPE ROLE) says that OBJ is vulnerable to playing ROLE in some instance of TYPE.  The notion of 'vulnerability' here is to be cashed out in terms of an agent's interests or desires being thwarted if some instance INS of TYPE in which (ROLE INS TYPE) were to become true.  That agent need not be identical to OBJ, though often will be.")
(disjointWith ComputerIBT GeographicalThing)
(disjointWith ComputerIBT FluidTangibleThing)
(disjointWith ComputerIBT ConstructionArtifact)
(comment ComputerIBT "The collection of all #$InformationBearingThings instantiated on or for instances of #$ComputationalSystem, whether as fixed stored data (see #$ComputerDataArtifact), processes executing computer code (see #$ComputerProcessRunning), or as #$SoftwareAgents.")
(disjointWith Married ComputerIBT)
(comment ComputerCodeCopy "The collection of all instantiations of computer code instantiated on a computer.  This is a general concept, including processes (#$ComputerProcessRunning) as well as static instantiations on mass storage (see #$ComputerDataArtifact) and #$SoftwareAgents.  If the code instantiates a complete #$ComputerProgram-CW, use the more specific, #$ComputerProgramCopy.")
(comment ComputerProgramCopy-Data "The collection of all instantiations of computer programs (#$ComputerProgram-CW) stored in hardcopy form or in computer memory.")
(comment softwareCode "(#$softwareCode SOFTWARE CODE) means that the #$ComputerCode CODE is source or executable code for the #$SoftwareObject-Individual SOFTWARE.  If SOFTWARE is an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW instead of a subroutine, subroutine package, etc., use the more specific predicate #$programCode.")
(comment VulnerableBecauseAdministratorCalledAdministrator "A specialization of #$VulnerableBecauseOfInsecureUsernames. A computer has this vulnerability if its #$AdministratorAccount is named 'Administrator', which is well-known to be the default username for this (privileged) account, and can easily be changed.")
(comment CompactDisc-GenericTypeByWritability "A collection of collections. Each instance is a spec of #$CompactDisc-Generic whose instances are all of the same writability.")
(comment gameOfProgram "(#$gameOfProgram PROG GAME) means that GAME is the structured and rule-governed activity in which one engages when one plays with a running copy of PROG.  For example, (#$gameOfProgram #$CompleteChess-TheGame #$Chess) means that one plays chess when one plays with a running copy of Complete Chess.  Note that a single program may be associated with multiple games via the relation #$gameOfProgram, and that a single game is often associated with multiple programs.")
(comment ChessProgram "A specialization of #$ComputerGameProgram.  Each instance of #$ChessProgram is a program that can be used by someone to play #$Chess.")
(comment keInteractionRequirement "(#$keInteractionRequirement TERM RULE) states that whenever the interaction is in state represented by TERM, the rule RULE should fire. Example: (#$keInteractionRequirement #$UIA-GeneralMilitary-DemoEnvironmentMt RULE).")
(comment CycCopy "The collection of all instantiations of Cyc (#$CycProgram) which are in long- or short-term computer memory.  A specializatio
n of #$ComputerProgramCopy-Data.")
(comment ComputerLogFile "A specialization of #$ComputerFile-AIS.  Each instance of #$ComputerLogFile is an abstract file meant to store data collected by a running program.  Often programs can be configured to store certain kinds of data in such files, and often also they can be configured to write to such a file in a particular specified location.")
(comment programWritesToLogFileType "(#$programWritesToLogFileType PROG FILE-TYPE) means that #$ComputerProgram-CW PROG writes to the specialization of #$ComputerLogFile, FILE-TYPE.")
(comment scheduledAttendee "(#$scheduledAttendee EVENT PERSON) means that the #$Agent-Generic PERSON is scheduled to be a participant in (#$deliberateActors) the referred to event.  A default conclusion is (#$deliberateActors EVENT PERSON), but there are many exceptions for which a scheduled participant misses participation in the event.")
(comment TelnetProgram "#$TelnetProgram is the collection of programs that implement the #$TelnetProtocol, including #$TelnetServerPrograms and #$TelnetClientPrograms. Individual telnet programs typically should be asserted to be instances of one or both of those more specialized collections.")
(comment scheduledEventTypeWhereWhen "(#$scheduledEventTypeWhereWhen EVENT_TYPE PLACE DATE) means that an event of type EVENT_TYPE is scheduled to occur at PLACE at the time DATE.  Cyc can conclude that such an event actually occurs at that time and place unless there are other reasons for concluding otherwise.")
(comment COADescriptionMicrotheory "The collection of all microtheories that contain pump-priming assertions relating COAs to their background scenarios.")
(comment initialLocationInformation "A predicate for identifying, via nuSketch, the initial, relative directional information about moving objects.")
(comment NLParagraph "The collection of all strings of words, in any natural language,
which in the context of a given #$TextualMaterial form paragraphs.")
(comment DocumentLocator "Instances of #$DocumentLocator are abstractions that are used
to locate a document (#$TextualMaterial) within a collection. The abstraction
is required because different location systems may use different resources
(#$Integer, #$CharacterString, ...) to perform this task.")
(comment SubDocumentLocator "Instances of #$SubDocumentLocator are abstractions that are used to
locate a sub-document unit (#$NLParagraph, #$NLSentence) within a document
(#$TextualMaterial). The abstraction is required because different location
systems may use different resources (#$Integer, #$CharacterString, ...) to
perform this task.")
(comment ParagraphLocator "Instances of #$ParagraphLocator are abstractions that are used to
locate an $NLParagraph within a document (#$TextualMaterial). The abstraction
is required because different location systems may use different resources
(#$Integer, #$CharacterString, ...) to perform this task.")
(comment SentenceLocator "Instances of #$SentenceLocator are abstractions that are used to
locate an $NLSentence within a document (#$TextualMaterial) or #$NLParagraph.
The abstraction is required because different location systems may use
different resources (#$Integer, #$CharacterString, ...) to perform this
task.")
(comment SentenceNumberFn "A function to define instances of
#$SentenceLocator in instances of #$NLDocument or #$NLParagraph that
identify sentences by numbers.")
(comment ParagraphNumberFn "A function to define instances of
#$ParagraphLocator in instances of #$NLDocument that identify paragraphs
by numbers.")
(comment DocumentNumberFn "A function to define instances of #$DocumentLocator in instances of
#$Corpus or other collections that identify documents by numbers.")
(comment ParagraphSentenceFn "#$ParagraphSentenceFn can be used to denote instances of
#$NLSentence by reference to the #$NLParagraph they belong to and their
location in the #$NLParagraph. For example (#$ParagraphSentenceFn PARAGRAPH
(#$SentenceNumberFn 17)) denotes the 17th sentence of DOCUMENT.")
(comment DocumentSentenceFn "#$DocumentSentenceFn can be used to denote
instances of #$NLSentence by reference to the #$NLDocument they belong to and
their location in the document. For example (#$DocumentSentenceFn DOCUMENT
(#$SentenceNumberFn 17)) denotes the 17th sentence of DOCUMENT.")
(comment DocumentParagraphFn "#$DocumentParagraphFn can be used to denote
instances of #$NLParagraph by reference to the #$NLDocument they belong to and
their location in the document. For example (#$DocumentParagraphFn DOCUMENT
(#$ParagraphNumberFn 17)) denotes the 17th paragraph of DOCUMENT.")
(comment Corpus "A #$Corpus is an organized repository of instances of
#$ConceptualWork. Typically a #$Corpus exists in order to support some
reference or scientific endeavor. For example, Migne's Patrologia Latina is a
#$Corpus that contains an exhaustive sample of Latin works by the Fathers of
the Church and is used by theologians and historians; the CHILDES #$Corpus
contains transcriptions of spontaneous child speech and is used as a research
tool by developmental psycholinguists; etc...")
(comment corpusContainsContentType "(#$corpusContainsContentType CORPUS TYPE) is true iff at least
some of the items in CORPUS are of type TYPE. This predicate can be used to
described corpora that contain heterogeneous materials such as #$Map and
#$LegalDocument. Compare to #$corpusContainsExclusivelyType.")
(comment corpusContainsExclusivelyType "(#$corpusContainsExclusivelyType CORPUS TYPE) is true iff every item
in CORPUS is guaranteed to be of type TYPE. Compare with
#$corpusContainsContentType")
(comment CorpusDocumentFn "#$CorpusDocumentFn can be used to denote
instances of #$NLDocument by reference to a #$Corpus and a #$Corpus location.
For example, (#$CorpusDocumentFn #$TREC-Corpus (#$DocumentNumberFn 666)) denotes the
#$NLDocument DOC-AIS such that (#$infoStructureOfCW DOC-CW DOC-AIS) is true,
where DOC-CW is document numer 666 in the #$TREC-Corpus.")
(comment TREC-Corpus "A #$Corpus containing instances of #$NewspaperArticle from
#$WallStreetJournal-TheNewspaper and other American #$NewspaperSeries
assembled by the National Institute of Standards and Technology for the
purpose of testing and evaluating question-answering systems.")
(comment documentCorpus "(#$documentCorpus DOC-AIS CORPUS) is true iff
DOC-AIS is an #$NLDocument which corresponds, via the (#$infoStructureOfCW
DOC-CW DOC-AIS) to a #$PropositionalConceptualWork DOC-CW which is an element of the
#$Corpus CORPUS. Information about the location of DOC-CW in CORPUS is to be
obtained via the predicate #$documentCorpusID.")
(comment documentCorpusID "(#$documentCorpusID DOC-AIS LOCATOR) is true
iff LOCATOR is a #$ContentLocator which determines DOC-CW's position in the
#$Corpus where DOC-CW is the #$PropositionalConceptualWork such that (#$infoStructureOfCW
DOC-CW DOC-AIS). This can be determined via the predicate #$documentCorpus.")
(comment keInteractionStrongSuggestion "(#$keInteractionStrongSuggestion TERM SENTENCE) states that for TERM, it is strongly suggested that Cyc know whether SENTENCE obtains, and if so, with what bindings for its existentially quantified variables.")
(comment EmperorPenguin "This is the largest of the penguins.")
(comment schemaFieldNameList "(#$schemaFieldNameList SCHEMA LIST) means that (i) the
members of LIST are the field names of the physical fields of SCHEMA,
and (ii) the order in which the physical field name strings appear in
LIST determines the order in which the physical field values of SCHEMA
are returned by the in batch translations of the knowledge source
defining SCHEMA.")
(comment CycAccount "A specialization of #$ApplicationAccount.  Each instance of #$CycAccount is an account that provides a user with access to protected features of running processes of #$CycProgram.  For example, a user must log in to a running Cyc process with a valid #$CycAccount in order to edit assertions in the Cyc KB.")
(comment accountForProgram "(#$accountForProgram ACCOUNT PROGRAM) means that ACCOUNT is an account for PROGRAM; that is, it is possible to log in to some running processes of some copies of PROGRAM as ACCOUNT.  See also #$accountForProgramCopy, which relates an account to the specific program copy the account is for.")
(comment accountForProgramCopy "(#$accountForProgramCopy ACCOUNT COPY) means that ACCOUNT is an account for COPY; that is, it is possible to log in to a running process of COPY as ACCOUNT.")
(comment ApplicationAccountFn "(#$UserAccountFn APPLICATION_COPY USERNAME) denotes the #$ApplicationAccount for the #$ComputerProgramCopy APPLICATION_COPY (#$userAccountForSystem, q.v.) which has USERNAME as its login username.  For example, (#$ApplicationAccountFn #$Cyc \"Blake\") denotes my user account for #$Cyc.")
(comment hasApplicationAccount "(#$hasApplicationAccount AGENT ACCOUNT) means that AGENT has been authorized to use ACCOUNT.")
(comment IndianCuisine "The collection of instances of what many Americans tend to think of as Indian food.  Add specs as appropriate")
(comment TimeZone-IntegralOffset "The subcollection of instances of #$TimeZone which start an integer number of hours off of #$GMTTimeZone.")
(comment typeBehaviorIncapable "'(#$typeBehaviorIncapable TYPE SIT-TYPE ROLE)' means that instances of TYPE are defeasibly incapable of playing ROLE in instances of SIT-TYPE.  For instance, '(#$typeBehaviorIncapable #$FlightlessBird #$Flying-FlappingWings #$doneBy)' means that flightless birds are incapable of flying.")
(comment rowCount-SKS "(#$rowCount-SKS SOURCE COUNT) means that the
structured knowledge source SOURCE has COUNT number of raw tuples
(e.g. rows in a database or entries in a hash table). At the
procedural  level, the row count of a knowledge source determines the
cost of querying  that knowledge source with a fully unbound query.
See also #$resultSetCardinality, which is a more expressive relation
used in cost estimates.")
(comment doRedirectDNSFrom "(#$doRedirectDNSFrom HACKER MANNER NS COMP) means that, from COMP and in manner MANNER, HACKER exploits the functionality of a name server program in such a way that the program looking at the DNS table on NS directs traffic in a way other than intended by the designer of the network NS is on.  See alos #$doRedirectDNS.")
(comment SpoofableFileExtensionsFault "A program has this fault if it allows attackers to spoof file extensions.  For example, an .exe file can be made to look like a .txt (or other seemingly harmless file type) file in the attachment list, increasing the likelihood of malicious code being executed without the user's knowledge.")
(comment RequestingAnApology "This is the collection of actions in which a human agent--that is, an individual person or an organization--requests an apology from another.")
(comment Apologizing "This is the collection of actions in which one human agent--that is, an individual person or an organization--expresses regret to another for a previous wrong.")
(comment Forgiving "The collection of acts in which a human agent pardons some past perceived wrong on the part of another human agent.")
(comment Artifact-Communication "The collection of artifacts designed to facilitate    information transfer during instances of #$CommunicatingUsingADevice.  Examples include devices designed to send, receive, or route information during these events (see
#$CommunicationDevice), pathway artifacts designed to carry
information (cf., #$Path-InfoTransfer), and networks composed of the
foregoing.")
(disjointWith Artifact-Communication Water)
(disjointWith Artifact-Communication Dam)
(comment projectManagers "(#$projectManagers PROJECT MANAGER) means that MANAGER manages PROJECT, an instance of #$Project.")
(comment superPeers "(#$superPeers COMP1 COMP2) says that COMP1 and COMP2 are play the role of super-peer computers in a #$SuperPeerNetwork.  That is, COMP1 and COMP2 are the boxes running the processes of software agents that are the #$superPeerSoftwareAgents in the super-peer system.")
(comment SuperPeerNetwork "A specialization of #$ComputerNetwork.  In a super-peer network, a number of computers, \"peers\" communicate with a single computer, which communicates with a number of \"super-peer\" computers, each of which has its own set of peers that communicate with it.")
(comment superPeerNode-Computer "This predicate relates a #$SuperPeerNetwork to the boxes that act as the \"super-peer\" nodes.  More precisely, it identifies the boxes on which the \"super-peer\" processes are running.  To identify those processes (treated as software agents), use #$superPeerNode-Agent.")
(comment temporallySubsumes-InsType "A binary #$TemporalPredicate that relates a #$TemporalThing to a type of #$TimeInterval or #$QualitativeTimeOfDay (q.v.) an instance of which the thing temporally subsumes.  (#$temporallySubsumes-InsType TEMP INTERVAL-TYPE) means that TEMP #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.) some instance of INTERVAL-TYPE.
<p>
For example, '(#$temporallySubsumes-InsType FredsBoyhood #$CalendarDecade)' means that Fred's boyhood subsumed a decade.
<p>
See also #$temporallySubsumedBy-InsType and #$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType.")
(comment peerNode-Computer "Use #$peerNode-Computer to relate an instance of #$SuperPeerNetwork to any of the \"peer\" computers in the network.")
(comment peerWRTSuperPeer "(#$peerWRTSuperPeer PEER SUPER) says that PEER is one of the computers that can communicate with the superpeer SUPER.")
(comment peerComputers "(#$peerComputers COMP1 COMP2) says that the #$Computers COMP1 and COMP2 are peers -- i.e., they are both #$peerWRTSuperPeer with respect to the same super-peer computer in an instance of #$SuperPeerNetwork.  COMP1 and COMP2 can communicate with the super-peer, but not with each other.")
(comment peerNode-Agent "Use #$peerNode-Agent to relate a network to any of the \"peer\" processes (software agents) in a #$SuperPeerNetwork.")
(comment superPeerNode-Agent "Use #$superPeerNode-Agent to relate a network to any of the \"super-peer\" processes (software agents) in a #$SuperPeerNetwork.  For any such network, all super-peer nodes can communicate.")
(comment softwareAgentsConnected "(#$softwareAgentsConnected AGT1 AGT2) says that the #$SoftwareAgents AGT1 and AGT2 are such that 
 #$softwareAgentsConnected-FromTo holds between them, in one direction or the other (or both).")
(comment softwareAgentsConnected-FromTo "(#$softwareAgentsConnected-FromTo FROM-AGENT TO-AGENT) says that the #$SoftwareAgents FROM-AGENT and TO-AGENT are connected such that TO-AGENT can receive information from FROM-AGENT.")
(comment softwareAgentsInterconnected "(#$softwareAgentsInterconnected AGENT1 AGENT2) says that the #$SoftwareAgents AGENT1 and AGENT2 are connected such that AGENT1 can receive information from AGENT2 and vice versa.")
(comment peerWRTSuperPeer-Agents "(#$peerWRTSuperPeer-Agents PEER SUPER) says that PEER is one of the #$SoftwareAgents that can communicate with the superpeer SUPER (but with nothing else).")
(comment peerSoftwareAgents "(#$peerSoftwareAgents AGT1 AGT2) says that the #$SoftwareAgents AGT1 and AGT2 are peers -- i.e., they are both #$peerWRTSuperPeer-Agents with respect to some super-peer in an instance of #$SuperPeerNetwork.  AGT1 and AGT2 communicate with the super-peer, but not with each other.")
(comment superPeerSoftwareAgents "(#$superPeerSoftwareAgents AGT1 AGT2) says that AGT1 and AGT2 are #$SoftwareAgents that play the role of super-peers in a #$SuperPeerNetwork.  That is, AGT1 and AGT2 can communicate with each other; moreover, AGT1 and AGT2 can each communicate with a unique set of \"peer\" agents, none of which can communicate with one another.")
(comment softwareAgentSupportsService "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate (#$softwareAgentSupportsService AGT  TYPE) says that the #$SoftwareAgent AGT is capable of performing a #$ProgramActions of type TYPE.")
(comment softwareAgentSupportsServiceIterations "(#$softwareAgentSupportsServiceIterations AGT TYPE N) says that the #$SoftwareAgent AGT can, at most, support N #$ProgramActions of type TYPE simultaneously.")
(comment WestNileEncephalitis "A viral infection of the brain caused by #$WestNileEncephalitis-Virus. West Nile encephalitis had not been documented in the Western Hemisphere until 1999.")
(comment WestNileEncephalitis-Virus "West Nile virus is the flavivirus that causes #$WestNileEncephalitis.  It is closely related to #$StLouisEncephalitis-Virus.")
(comment Mission-AssignedActionType "An instance of this collection is a specialization of #$Situation representing a mission in the sense commonly intended in the context of planning, in particular military planning. In such contexts, a `mission' is typically a normative specification of an action or complex action to be performed by a designated agent. Accordingly, the two key aspects of a mission are (i) the goal of the mission, that is, the end state the designated agent ought to bring about by executing the mission, and (ii) a specification, at a very general level, of the type of action that is expected to fulfill the mission. A typical instance of mission in a military planning context would be something like 'secure area Z by blocking all land access corridors', which can be taken as indicating both the type of state that must obtain at the end of a successful execution and the general type of operation that must be executed. In our representation, such a mission would be an instance of #$Mission-AssignedActionType, that is: a specialization of #$Situation in which (cf. #$SituationTypeSuchThatFn and #$situationTypeIsSuchThat) area Z has been secured by blocking  actions. Note that because situation types can in principle be described by propositions, there is a one-one correspondence between #$Mission-AssignedActionType and #$MissionSpecificationMicrotheory -- much like in the case of the more specific #$CourseOfAction and #$COASpecificationMicrotheory (q.v.).")
(comment missionDescribingSpecification "(#$missionDescribingSpecification MISSION MT) is true iff MT is
the instance of #$MissionSpecificationMicrotheory that describes the facts
obtaining in the situation type MISSION.")
(comment mainActionTypeForMission "This predicate is used to characterize the most general subtype of
#$PurposefulAction which is explicitly or implicitly assigned as part of
an instance of #$Mission-AssignedActionType (q.v.).
(#$mainActionTypeForMission ACT_TYPE MISSION) is true if the  
execution of MISSION, taken as a whole, exemplifies the type of action
ACT_TYPE.")
(comment nodeInNetwork "(#$nodeInNetwork NODE NETWORK) means that NODE is a computer on the #$ComputerNetwork, NETWORK.")
(comment MissionSpecificationMtFn "(#$MissionSpecificationMtFn MISSION) denotes the instance MT of
#$MissionSpecificationMicrotheory that satisfies
(#$missionDescribingSpecification MISSION MT), for a given instance
MISSION of #$Mission-AssignedActionType. Cf. #$MissionSpecificationMtFn-Inverse.")
(comment MissionSpecificationMtFn-Inverse "(#$MissionSpecificationMtFn-Inverse MT) denotes the instance MISSION of
#$Mission-AssignedActionType that satisfies
(#$missionDescribingSpecification MISSION MT), for a given instance 
MT of #$MissionSpecificationMicrotheory. This is the inverse of
#$MissionSpecificationMtFn.")
(comment doGetAccessToAccountOnFrom "(#$doGetAccessToAccountOnFrom AGENT ACCOUNT COMPUTER1 COMPUTER2) means that, from COMPUTER2,  AGENT gets access to ACCOUNT on COMPUTER1, which is a node in ACCOUNT's #$AccountSystem. See also #$doGetAccessToAccountOn.")
(comment SameLengthTimeIntervalType "Each instance of #$SameLengthTimeIntervalType is a collection which, for a fixed length/duration, contains all and only the time intervals of that length.  For example, (#$SameLengthTimeIntervalTypeFn (#$MinutesDuration 3)) is an instance of this collection, which contains all and only time intervals that are three minute long, such as (#$TimeIntervalInclusiveFn (#$MinuteFn 1 (#$HourFn 3 (#$DayFn 5 (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 2002))))) (#$MinuteFn 3 (#$HourFn 3 (#$DayFn 5 (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 2002)))))).  Note that the `same length' is used in its strict sense: a collection of intervals whose lengths all fall into a range specified by different lengths, such as all intervals of lengths between 3 and 5 seconds, is not an instance of this collection.")
(disjointWith SameLengthTimeIntervalType TemporallyDisjointTemporalObjectType)
(comment SameLengthTimeIntervalTypeFn "For each instance QUAN of #$Time-Quantity, (#$SameLengthTimeIntervalTypeFn QUAN) is the collection of all (solid) time intervals whose lengths are QUAN.  For example, (#$SameLengthTimeIntervalTypeFn (#$HoursDuration 2)) is the collection of all time intervals that are 2 hours long.  Note that because of the #$resultIsa constraint, one should not use expressions like (#$SameLengthTimeIntervalTypeFn (#$HoursDuration MIN MAX)).")
(comment evaluatableAlongSubDimensions "A binary predicate used to assert dependency relationships among instances of #$EvaluationDimension. (#$evaluatableAlongSubDimensions DEP IND) says that the value of ?DEPVAL in (DEP ARG1 ?DEPVAL) is dependent to some degree on (IND ARG1 INDVAL).  So, if (evaluatableAlongSubDimension DEP SUB1) and (evaluatableAlongSubDimension DEP SUB2), where SUB1 and SUB2 are the only known subdimensions for DEP, then if (SUB1 PLAN VAL) and (SUB2 PLAN VAL), where VAL is the #$EvaluativeAttribute for the plan PLAN along SUB1 and SUB2 it will follow that (DEP PLAN VAL).")
(comment EvaluationDimension "A specialization of #$BinaryPredicate.  Instances are predicates corresponding to key
evaluative measures of the soundness and effectiveness of a plan. See also #$PlanSpecificationMicrotheory and #$PlanSpecification.")
(comment NLDocument "The collection of all strings of words, in any natural language,
which in the context of a given #$TextualMaterial form documents.")
(comment TextStringPropositionalExtractionMtFn "Several instances of #$CycRelatedPrograms, for example instances of
#$CycNLParser, have the ability to map instances of #$TextString into (sets
of) #$CycLPropositionalSentence. For example a #$CycNLParser might map the
#$TextString \"Bill Clinton lives in NY State\" to the
#$CycLPropositionalSentence (#$residesInRegion #$BillClinton #$NewYork-State).
Given this ability, it is often useful for applications to know that certain
assertions which were used in reasoning a) have not been hand crafted or
logically concluded to, but are the output of some program; b) are (part of)
the CycL interpretation of some known #$TextString. The function
#$TextStringPropositionalExtractionMtFn can be used to specify a microtheory
for all these assertions that compositionally records all this information:
for example, the NAT (#$TextStringPropositionalExtractionMtFn
#$DependencyParser #$DependencyParserDefaultSetting \"Bill Clinton lives in New
York State\") denotes the microtheory whose assertions are all and only the
instances of #$CycLPropositionalSentence that are the output of the
#$CycRelatedProgram #$DependencyParser when it parses \"Bill Clinton lives in
New York State\" with its parameters set as detailed by the
#$DependencyParserDefaultSetting. Note that the third argument need not be an
actual #$TextString but could be a #$TextString compositionally specified by a
NAT such as (#$DocumentSentenceFn DOCUMENT SENTENCE-LOCATOR).")
(comment MapFunctionOverSet "This function applies a unary (evaluatable) function to all elements in a given set and
returns the resulting set of values. For example, asking
(#$evaluate ?VAR (#$MapFunctionOverSet #$SquaredFn (#$TheSet 1 2)))
will return the binding (#$TheSet 1 4) for ?VAR.")
(comment MapFunctionOverList "This function applies a unary (evaluatable) function to all elements in a given list and
returns the resulting list of values. For example, asking
(#$evaluate ?VAR (#$MapFunctionOverList #$SquaredFn (#$TheList 1 2)))
will return the binding (#$TheList 1 4) for ?VAR.")
(comment doGetAccessToElectronicMailboxOf "(doGetAccessToElectronicMailboxOf AGENT ACCOUNT) means that AGENT gets access to an #$EMailFile which stores the electronic mail of the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT (on whichever computer serves as the mail server for ACCOUNT).")
(comment doGetAccessToLocalEMailOf "(doGetAccessToLocalEMailOf AGENT ACCOUNT) means that AGENT gets access to an #$EMailFile which stores electronic mail belonging to the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT which has been \"pulled down\" from the mail server and stored locally (usually on the account-owner's personal computer).")
(comment shortestTrafficableRouteToFor "(#$shortestTrafficableRouteToFor PATH OBJ AGT) means that PATH is the shortest trafficable route for AGT, between OBJ and AGT.  For example, in the context of a COA if (#$shortestTrafficableRouteToFor I-35 AustinTexas TankUnit001) is true, then I-35 is the most direct route between TankUnit001 and AustinTexas, and I-35 is a trafficable route for TankUnit001.")
(comment doGetAccessToAccountOfType "(#$doGetAccessToAccountOfType AGENT ACCT_TYPE) means that AGENT takes steps toward the goal of controlling an account of type ACCT_TYPE on some machine.")
(comment Critique-CW "The collection of all critiques.  Instances are #$PropositionalConceptualWorks that are critical assessments of some #$Thing.")
(comment doDenialOfServiceAttackOfTypeFrom "(#$doDenialOfServiceAttackOfType AGENT MANNER DENIED ATTACK-TYPE COMPUTER) means, from COMPUTER, that AGENT performs in MANNER a denial of service attack of ATTACK-TYPE to render DENIED incapable of performing basic services, such as responding to network requests.")
(comment thingAnalyzed "(#$thingAnalyzed ANALYSIS THING) says that THING is being studied or analyzed during the #$AnalyzingSomething ANALYSIS.")
(comment mailboxFileForAccount "(#$mailboxFileForAccount FILE ACCOUNT) means that the #$EMailFile FILE is the mail-server repository for the electronic mail of the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT. See also #$localEMailFileForAccount.")
(comment localEMailFileForAccount "(#$localEMailFileForAccount FILE ACCOUNT) means that the #$EMailFile FILE is a repository for the electronic mail of the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT which has been \"pulled down\" from the mail server and stored on some user's machine. For email which is still on the server, see #$mailboxFileForAccount.")
(comment SmartCardServerProgram "The collection of all #$ComputerProgram-CWs designed to read or otherwise deal with 'smart cards' (used as virtual purses, in mobile phones etc.).")
(comment PrimitiveEventType "??")
(comment primitiveProperSubSituationTypes "(#$primitiveProperSubSituationTypes SUBSITTYPE SITTYPE) means that SUBSITTYPE is not further decomposed when considering SITTYPE, i.e. SUBSITTYPE is itself not known to have important or interesting sub-situation types.")
(comment compositeProperSubSituationTypes "(#$compositeProperSubSituationTypes SUBSITTYPE SITTYPE) means that SUBSITTYPE is further decomposed into sub-situation types when considering SITTYPE, i.e. it is treated as a composite situation type type in this context.")
(comment requiredTypeForSituationType-Count "(#$requiredTypeForSituationType-Count TYPE SCRIPT N) means that N objects of TYPE are constituents in every instance of SCRIPT.")
(comment requiredTypeForSubSituationType-Count "(#$requiredTypeForSubSituationType-Count TYPE SCRIPT SCENE N)  means that N instances of TYPE are constituents (in the sense of #$situationConstituents, q.v.) in every instance of SCENE in SCRIPT. When SCRIPT is a scene in other scripts, the same number of things are constituents.")
(comment requiredTypeForSituationType-Many "(#$requiredTypeForSituationType-Many TYPE SCRIPT) means that many instances of TYPE are constituents in every instance of SCRIPT, in a sense similar to the more precise #$requiredTypeForSituationType-Count.")
(comment requiredTypeForSubSituationType-Many "(#$requiredTypeForSubSituationType-Many TYPE SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE) means that many instances of TYPE are constituents  (cf. #$situationConstituents) in every instance of SUBSITTYPE in any instance of SITTYPE.")
(comment inheritRolePlayersOfType-SitTypeToSubSitType "(#$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SitTypeToSubSitType SIT-TYPE  ROLE1 SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE2 TYPE) means any instance of TYPE playing ROLE1 in an instance of SIT-TYPE plays ROLE2 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE that is a subsituation of SIT-TYPE (cf. also #$onlyTypePlaysRoleInSubSituationType). This predicate allows us to track the identity of script participants by role into the scenes of a given script. For a similar relation tracking identity of participants from scenes to scripts, cf. #$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSitType.")
(comment inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSitType "(#$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSitType SIT-TYPE ROLE1 SUB-SIT-TYPE ROLE2 TYPE) means that any instance of TYPE playing ROLE2 in an instance of SUB-SIT-TYPE that is a subsituation of an instance of SIT-TYPE plays ROLE1 in that instance of SIT-TYPE. This predicate allows us to track the identity of participants in a script by the role they play in scenes of the same script. For a dual version of the relation, cf. #$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SitTypeToSubSitType.")
(comment inheritSomeRolePlayersOfType-SitTypeToSubSitType "(#$inheritSomeRolePlayersOfType-SitTypeToSubSitType SIT-TYPE ROLE1 SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE2 TYPE) means that some instance(s) of TYPE playing ROLE1 in instances of SITTYPE play ROLE2 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE that is a sub-situation of  SIT-TYPE.")
(comment inheritSomeRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType "(#$inheritSomeRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE1 ROLE1 SUBSITTYPE2 ROLE2 TYPE) means that some instance(s) of TYPE playing ROLE1 in SUBSITTYPE1 play ROLE2 in SUBSITTYPE2 of SITTYPE. Note: this is a weaker form of #$inheritRolePlayersOfType-SubSitTypeToSubSitType.")
(comment ShutDownProgram "A specialization of #$UtilityProgram. Each member of this collection is a computer program whose function is to shut down other programs.")
(comment MathematicalPuzzle "Mathematical puzzles are mentally challenging problems and games that are intended to excercise mathematical reasoning.  An example is given in the accompanying #$cyclistNotes.")
(comment termInGlossary "(termInGlossary TERM GLOSSARY) means that TERM is an element of the items contained in GLOSSARY.")
(comment noteOnMilitaryDoctrine "This predicate is used to preserve background information about military doctrine, particularly as it relates to the decompositon of action types which are typically assigned to military organizations (cf. #$TaskedMilitaryActionType).")
(comment forceRatioFavorsInType "(#$forceRatioFavors TYPE AGENT) means that the #$combatForceRatioInEvent of any instance of TYPE favors AGENT in the planning context.")
(comment DefenseAttorney "The collection of all defense attorneys:  i.e., #$Lawyers who, by profession, are employed in courtroom defense of individuals who have been charged with criminal acts.")
(comment LawEnforcementOrgTypeByJurisdiction "This type-level collection is intended to taxonomize the collection #$PoliceOrganization according to an important typology, namely the jurisdiction within which the organization is authorized to operate.  Each instance of #$LawEnforcementOrgTypeByJurisdiction is a collection of #$PoliceOrganizations characterized by the fact that all instances operate within the same jurisdiction within some federal hierarchy:  city, county, borough, state, national, etc.")
(comment HaveOccupationTypeInIndustryTypeFn "Where IND_TYPE is an instance of #$IndustryOrEconomicSectorType and OCCUPATION is a #$PersonTypeByOccupation, (#$HaveOccupationTypeInIndustryTypeFn IND_TYPE OCCUPATION) returns the collection of all instances of OCCUPATION who hold that possition within a business that is a member of an #$Industry-Localized that in turn is an instance of the industry- or economic sector-type IND_TYPE.")
(comment Prosecutor "The collection of all prosecutors:  i.e., the collection of all lawers employed by the state for the purpose of prosecuting criminal cases within a defined jurisdiction.")
(comment CustomsAgent "The collection of all persons whose principle job is inspect goods and produce being brought into a country by travelers and immigrants, with the purpose of intercepting potentially dangerous items.")
(comment BorderSecurityAgent "The collection of all non-military personnel whose job is to insure the security of a national border, either by preventing ingress of dangerous materials, or by preventing illegal immigration, or by acting to prevent or discourage ingress by enemy forces or interests.")
(comment ContractKiller "The collection of all professional 'hit' persons, often members of some #$OrganizedCrimeOrganization who are regularly employed in instances of #$MurderForHire.")
(comment BorderGuard "The collection of all persons whose job is to guard the borders of some country with the purpose of preventing ingress of illegal or enemy aliens.")
(comment IntelligenceOperative "The collection of all #$IntelligenceAgents who are employed as operatives in the field for purposes of intelligence gathering by the #$IntelligenceOrganization of one country against another country.")
(comment PoliceOfficer-Municipal "The collection of all police officers in the sense of #$LawEnforcementOfficers who serve municipalities:  cities, towns, villages, and the like.  This collection is probably what the average person thinks of first on hearing the word 'police':  however, users should bear in mind that the term 'police' or 'policeman' has a broader scope than just #$PoliceOfficer-Municipal.  The determining feature of this collection is the jurisdiction within which authority is exercised:  if it is at the municipal level, then the individual is a #$PoliceOfficer-Municipal, otherwise, not.  Examples would include an English 'bobby', a highway patrolman who is a member of the police department of a modern American city, and the town constable in a turn-of-the-century American town.  Negative examples would include a military policeman, a member of the state highway patrol, or an agent of the FBI.")
(comment LocalLawEnforcementOfficer "The collection of all #$LawEnforcementOfficers who serve in #$LocalPoliceOrganizations:  that is, who serve in law enforcement organizations whose jurisdiction is at the city, borough, or county level, or at a comparable level within an analogous federalized law enforcement system.")
(comment IntelligenceOfficial "The collection of all 'official' members of an #$GovernmentIntelligenceOrganization:  i.e., #$IntelligenceAgents who are also #$PublicOfficials in the government of which their #$GovernmentIntelligenceOrganization is a part.")
(comment IntelligenceOfficer-Occupation "The collection of all #$IntelligenceAgents who are employed in the job of managing field operatives (see #$IntelligenceOperative) or analysts (#$IntelligenceAnalyst).")
(comment haveOccupationTypeInIndustryType "The corresponding predicate of HaveOccupationTypeInIndustryTypeFn.")
(comment NuclearEnergyIndustry "The collection of all instances of #$Industry-Localized that are comprised of businesses whose concern is the generation and transmission of electricity generated by means of #$NuclearReactors.")
(comment CanalizingMovement "An instance of #$CanalizingMovement is an event in which something's movement is directed along a preferred channel or path. This includes both cases in which movement physically constrained to follow a particular path (e.g. fluid flow along a #$FlowPath) as well as cases in which movement is (intentionally) guided or influenced by an agent acting remotely (e.g.  a police officer directing traffic),")
(comment AccessingCDROMData "An #$EmployeeComputerActivityType and a specialization of #$DataSending.  This is the collection of #$Events in which data is accessed from a CD through a #$CD-ROMDriver and sent to some output device.")
(comment RippingACompactDisk "A collection of actions done by computers on files. In each instance of #$RippingACompactDisk, a #$CDRipper program converts digital audio from a CompactDisc-Audio into computer files (usually WAVFiles).")
(comment usedInBattleMonitorCode "A predicate to tag constants used in the battle monitor code.")
(comment Encoding-MP3 "The collection of events where some data in an #$InformationBearingThing are tranformed from a more 'raw' or natural format to a more compressed/complex mp3 format.")
(comment Decoding-MP3 "The collection of events where some data in an #$InformationBearingThing are transformed from a MP3 format to a more usable or natural format.")
(comment AccessingData-MP3 "A specialization of #$DataSending. Each instance of this collection is an #$Event in which data is accessed from a #$MP3FileCopy and sent to some output device.")
(comment AccessingData-DVD "A specialization of #$DataSending. Each instance of this collection is an #$Event in which data is accessed from a #$DVD-Disk, perhaps also interacting with a #$DVDPlayer device, and sent to some output device.")
(comment accessToTypeNotAmenable "(#$accessToTypeNotAmenable AGT TYPE) means that it is expected that the individual AGT would not have access (see #$hasAccess) to any individuals that are instances of the collection TYPE.")
(comment accessToTypeExpected "(#$accessToTypeExpected AGT TYPE) means that it is expected that the individual AGT would have access (see #$hasAccess) to any individuals that are instances of the collection TYPE.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenComputerDeviceAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenComputerDeviceAndOS, a #$DeviceDriverProgram interfaces between some piece of computer hardware and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment eMailReceived "(#$eMailReceived RECIPIENT_ADDRESS FROM_ADDRESS TEXT SUBJECT) means that an email was received at RECIPIENT_ADDRESS addressed from FROM_ADDRESS with the subject line SUBJECT and with TEXT as the message.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenAudioCardAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenAudioCardAndOS, a #$AudioDriver interfaces between a sound card and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenCDROMDriveAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenCDROMDriveAndOS, a #$CD-ROMDriver interfaces between a #$CD-ROMDrive and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment accessToExpected "(#$accessToExpected AGT IND) means that it is expected that the individual AGT would have access (see #$hasAccess) to the individual IND.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenModemAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenModemAndOS, a #$ModemDriver interfaces between a #$Modem and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment accessToNotAmenable "(#$accessToNotAmenable AGT IND) means that it is expected that the individual AGT would not have access (see #$hasAccess) to the individual IND.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenMouseAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenMouseAndOS, a #$MouseDriver interfaces between a #$ComputerMouse and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenPrinterAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenPrinterAndOS, a #$PrinterDriver interfaces between a #$ComputerPrinter and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment InterfacingBetweenVideoCardAndOS "A collection of actions performed by computers. In each instance of #$InterfacingBetweenVideoCardAndOS, a #$VideoDriver interfaces between a video card and the operating system; permitting the latter to manipulate the former in a standard way.")
(comment worksInDepartments "(#$worksInDepartments PERSON DEPT) means that PERSON works in the department DEPT of some company.  For example, (#$worksInDepartments #$Klein #$CycorpOE-Department) means that #$Klein works in the OE department at Cycorp.  Note that a particular person may work in more than one department.")
(comment violatesPolicyBecause "(#$violatesPolicyBecause IND SENT) means that IND violates a policy because SENT is true. For example, (#$violatesPolicyBecause Joe (#$thereExists ?X (#$steals Joe ?X))) means that some policy is violated by Joe because he steals something.")
(comment LoginProgram "A specialization of #$ServerProgram. Each instance of this collection is a computer program which somehow facilitates users logging in to a computational system. An important specialization is #$RemoteLoginProgram.")
(comment NetworkPacketFiltering "The collection of #$Events in which information packets from a network are filtered; often for security purposes.")
(comment SynthesizerProgram "A specialization of #$AudioProgram. Each instance of this collection is a computer program designed to emulate an #$ElectronicSynthesizer.")
(comment NetworkPacketRouting "The collection of #$Events in which information packets from a network are received and forwarded toward their  proper destination.")
(comment ProgramInterfacing "In each instance of #$ProgramInterfacing, a #$InterfaceProgram is used to interface with another program; permitting a user to manipulate the latter in a standard way.")
(comment PreprocessorProgram "A specialization of #$Compiler. Each instance of #$PreprocessorProgram is a program that performs various code-cleanup tasks preliminary to the main compile.")
(comment Plan-ExpectedSituationType "An instance of this collection is the type of #$Situations
representing the complex of states of affairs that would constitute the
realization or fulfillment of a given plan. This is not the only
ontologically relevant aspect of `plans', but it is central: a plan
can typically be seen as the description of a complex of states of affairs
that is expected to be brought about by someone's actions. Particular
situations, therefore, are `realizations' of a plan insofar as they satisfy
the plan's specified constraints -- thus a plan specifies a type of
situations. Relevant specializations are (mostly in the context of military
planning) #$Mission-AssignedActionType and #$CourseOfAction.")
(disjointWith SameLengthTimeIntervalType ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries)
(comment ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries "A specialization of #$ConventionalClassificationType and #$TimeParameter.  Each member TYPE of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries is a conventionally named 'series' of 'solid' time intervals in the sense that, for each instance t of TYPE, one can meaningfully speak of 'the one before t' or 'the one after t' in the sequence (or speak of 'the last one' or 'the next one' etc. in the sequence if we are to temporally locate ourselves in t). Examples of instances of this collection are #$Friday and #$CalendarYear etc.  Specializations of this collection include #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-Disjoint, #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-DisjointWithGaps, #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-Covering and #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint.")
(comment ServiceStation-Facility "The collection of all facilities devoted exclusively to housing #$ServiceStations.")
(comment DirectedPath-Generic "The collection of all instances of #$Path-Simple with an implicit or explicit direction. This includes actual routes that can be followed in a certain direction (that is, #$DirectedCustomaryPath), but also abstract paths that are regarded as having an orientation: an upward trajectory, or a westward #$AxisOfAdvance are examples.")
(comment orientationOfDirectedPath "This predicate relates an instance of #$DirectedPath-Generic to its intrinsic orientation, expressed as a #$TerrestrialDirection. Note that this is a binary relation, in constrast with the ternary #$directionsOfPath, which doesn't require that a path have any inherent directionality.")
(comment ReactionToSituationFn "#$ReactionToSituationFn returns a collection of actions when given a situation as an argument.  (#$ReactionToSituationFn SIT) denotes the specialization of #$Action whose instances are all and only the actions deliberately performed in reaction to (see #$inReactionTo) #$Situation SIT.")
(comment organismKilled "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$inputsDestroyed and #$maleficiary.  <code>(#$organismKilled EVT ORG)</code> means that the #$Organism-Whole <code>ORG</code> is killed in the #$PhysicalDestructionEvent <code>EVT</code>.")
(comment functionPreservesConstraints "A predicate that denotes a relation which may hold between predicate denoting functions and the constraints on the predicates they take as arguments.  (#$functionPreservesConstraints FUNC PRED) means that the constraints placed by PRED on any predicate REL given to FUNC as an argument are also placed on the denotatum of (FUNC REL).  For example, (#$functionPreservesConstraints #$ComplexActionFn #$argIsa) means that if (#$and (#$natFunction ?NAT #$ComplexActionFn) (#$natArgument ?NAT 1 ?PRED) (#$argIsa ?PRED ?NUM ?THING)), then (#$argIsa ?NAT ?NUM ?THING).")
(comment ComplexActionFn "#$ComplexActionFn is a unary reifiable function that returns an instance of #$ComplexActionPredicate when given an instance of #$NonDavidsonianPredicate as an argument.  Thus (#$ComplexActionFn PRED) denotes a relation that is identical in every way to PRED, save that it is a candidate for decomposition in a planning context, whereas PRED is not such a candidate.")
(comment ActorPlaysRoleFn "#$ActorPlaysRoleFn is a binary  function that returns a collection of events when given a temporal thing and a role as arguments.  (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn ACTOR ROLE) denotes the specialization of #$Situation which consists of all and only the events in which ACTOR plays ROLE.  For example, (#$ActorPlaysRoleFn #$GeorgeWBush #$objectActedOn) denotes the collection of situations in which Bush is the object acted on.  See also #$SituationTypeTernaryRolePlayedInFn.")
(comment TruncateFn "(#$TruncateFn QUANTITY) converts QUANTITY into one where the maximum and minimum ranges have been rounded towards zero to integer values. For example, (#$TruncateFn (#$Meter 3.7)) denotes (#$Meter 3) and (#$TruncateFn (#$DegreeCelsius -273.1)) denotes (#$DegreeCelsius -273).")
(comment ECommerceProgram "A specialization of #$BusinessComputerProgram. This is the collection of programs used to make commercial transactions over the #$Internet. This usually consists in banking or shopping.")
(comment OntologicalEngineering "A specialization of #$DevisingAPracticeOrWork.  Ontological engineering (\"OE\") is the activity of representing the structure of individuals and collections in such a way that a computer can reason effectively about them. Common OE tasks include creating optimally designed datastructures to represent specific entities, and writing rules or axioms that describe features of entities at the right level of generality.")
(comment OntologicalEngineer "A specialization of #$IntelligentAgent.  Each instance of #$OntologicalEngineer is an agent (usually a #$Person) who represents the structure of beings in such a way that a computer can reason effectively about it.")
(comment QuestionAnsweringSystem "The collection of #$ComputerProgram-CW that answer user questions,
often drawing on heterogeneous collection of data such as instances of
#$Corpus, #$Database-AbstractContent and web services.")
(comment AnimalSpecies "An instance of #$BiologicalTaxonType. This is the collection of all instances of #$BiologicalSpecies which are specializations of #$Animal.  The collection is defined by a rule that uniquely determines whether a type of animals is an instance of an instance of #$AnimalSpecies or not at any given point in time.  This rule is that:
1.) there are instances of the type that can breed.
2.) instances of the type that breed with each other can produce viable offspring which can also breed.
3.) such offspring are members of the same species as their parents.
4.) if a member of one animal species successfully breeds with an animal which is not a member of the species, no offspring that result would be fertile.
<p>
Thus, whether a type of organisms is an instance of #$AnimalSpecies or not depends on what other collections of animals exist at the same time.
<p>
Historical cut-offs of the starting point of a species are conventional.  The earliest member(s) of an animal species would be able to breed with some of their ancestors and their ancestors' kin.")
(comment StrictlyFunctionalRelation "A specialization of #$FunctionalRelation (q.v.) each instance of which is either a function (see #$Function-Denotational) or a predicate that is strictly \"functional\" in at least one argument-place (see #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate and #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs).  Examples include #$IntervalMaxFn, #$BorderBetweenFn, and #$biologicalMother.")
(comment licenseNumber "The license number -- in some Mts, this will make sense for the device (e.g., automobile; in others, for the license itself.")
(comment EvaluativeEffectivenessPredicate "A collection of predicates, a specialization of #$EvaluativePredicate.  Its instances attribute and reflect value judgements, as to the effectiveness of plans, actions, and processes.")
(comment LookingForObjectOfTypeFn "'(#$LookingForObjectOfTypeFn OBJ-TYPE)' picks out the subcollection of #$LookingForSomething in which the #$objectSought is an object of type OBJ-TYPE.")
(comment inheritAllRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType "(#$inheritAllRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType SIT-TYPE ROLE) means that if INST is an instantiation of SIT-TYP, then whatever plays ROLE in INST also plays ROLE in sub-situations of INST.  For instance, in the toothbrushing script, the performer of the instantiation performs every sub-situation, that is, (#$inheritAllRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType #$BrushingTeeth #$performedBy).")
(comment RogueNation "A specialization of #$IndependentCountry.  Each instance of #$RogueNation is a country that shows disregard for international law, has a tendency to be hostile, is a threat to its region, and/or scoffs at international consensus.  Contrast with #$LawAbidingNation.")
(comment LawAbidingNation "A specialization of #$IndependentCountry.  Each instance of #$LawAbidingNation is a country that shows regard for international law and consensus. Contrast with #$RogueNation.")
(disjointWith RogueNation LawAbidingNation)
(comment AssignedTaskType "Instances of this collection are specializations of #$PurposefulAction which are assigned tasks in some planning context.")
(disjointWith AssignedTaskType TimeOfDayType)
(comment criticalToPlan-Generic "(#$criticalToPlan-Generic PLAN THG) means that THG is
somehow critical to the success of ?PLAN.  If ?THG is an event and does
not occur, or if ?THG is an object and does not play its role in
?PLAN, then ?PLAN has a greatly reduced chance of succeeding.")
(comment SituationTypeTernaryRolePlayedInFn "#$SituationTypeTernaryRolePlayedInFn is a ternary function that returns a collection of events when given two temporal things and a ternary role as arguments.  (#$SituationTypeTernaryRolePlayedInFn ACTOR1 ACTOR2 ROLE) denotes the specialization of #$Situation which consists of all and only the events in which ACTOR1 plays ROLE with ACTOR1.  For example, (#$SituationTypeTernaryRolePlayedInFn #$GeorgeWBush #$GeorgeHWBush #$objectsInContact) denotes the collection of situations in which George W. Bush is in physical contact with George H. W. Bush.")
(comment SpindleMicrotheory "The collection of all microtheories which are the spindle elements.  A spindle microtheory system is one in which sibling spindle microtheories each have #$genlMt links to a superior #$CollectorMicrotheory, which has its own links to relevant contexts.  Additionally there exists a subordinate #$CollectorMicrotheory (inference microtheory) that has #$genlMt links to each of the spindle microtheories.  The name spindle derives from the visual metaphor of a spinning wheel spindle placed on its side in which the spokes represent the #$genlMt links.  Spindle microtheory systems are useful for organizing a set of contexts having shared assumptions, and requiring an inference context from which all are visible.")
(disjointWith Odor VisibleFeatureType)
(disjointWith ObjectTypeByTaste VisibleFeatureType)
(disjointWith VisibleFeatureType Texture)
(disjointWith VisibleThing Sound)
(disjointWith VisibleThing StrictlyMentalEvent)
(comment VisibleThing "Each instance of #$VisibleThing is something for which one or more visible features can be discerned, either by the naked eye or with the aid of an optical device such as binoculars or a telescope. An important specialization of #$VisibleThing is #$ColoredThing.")
(comment visibleFeatureOfObject "A specialized form of the predicate #$isa. (visibleFeatureOfObject OBJECT FEATURE) means that OBJECT shares the visible feature that all instances of FEATURE share. For example, all instances of #$Red are red in color, so (visibleFeatureOfObject #$JohnsCar #$Red) means that John's car is mainly or entirely red. This is the most general of the visible feature predicates; for more specialized predicates see e.g. #$mainColorOfObject and #$uniformColorOfObject.")
(comment predHoldsBetweenRolePlayers "(#$predHoldsBetweenRolePlayers EVENT-TYPE ROLE1 ROLE2 BIN-REL) means that in any instance of EVENT-TYPE, the binary relation BIN-REL holds between any player of ROLE1 and any player of ROLE2.")
(comment requiredRolesForSubSituationType "(#$requiredRolesForSubSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE) means that if an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE is a #$subSituations of an instantiation of SITTYPE, there must be some #$SomethingExisting that plays the role ROLE in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE.")
(comment predHoldsBetweenRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType "(#$predHoldsBetweenRolePlayers-SitTypeToSubSitType SITTYPE SITTYPE_ROLE SUBSITTYPE SUBSITTYPE_ROLE BIN_PRED) means that in an instance SIT of SITTYPE an agent playing the role SITTYPE_ROLE will be related to an agent playing the role SUBSITTYPE_ROLE in the instance of the SUBSITTYPE that is a subsituation of SIT by the relation BIN_PRED.")
(comment predHoldsBetweenRolePlayers-SubSitTypeToSitType "(#$predHoldsBetweenRolePlayers-SubSitTypeToSitType SCRIPT SCRIPT_ROLE SCENE SCENE_ROLE BIN_PRED) means that in an instantiation of SCRIPT the agent playing the role SCENE_ROLE in the instantiation of the SCENE scene will be related to the agent playing the role SCRIPT_ROLE in the overall script-instantiation by the relation BIN_PRED.")
(comment colorHasRGBBlueValue "The RGB blue value of a color expresses the amount of blue light the color contains.")
(comment colorHasRGBGreenValue "The RGB green value of a color expresses the amount of green light the color contains.")
(comment colorHasRGBRedValue "The RGB red value of a color expresses the amount of red light the color contains.")
(comment mainColorsOfObject "The formula (#$mainColorsOfObject OBJ COLOR-SCHEME) means that the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ is almost entirely colored with the #$Colors from the set of colors in COLOR-SCHEME.  For example, since a #$Zebra is roughly equally black and white, we would not say that it is primarily either of those colors. So neither (#$mainColorOfObject MyZebra #$BlackColor) nor (#$mainColorOfObject MyZebra #$WhiteColor) would be true.  And the assertion (#$objectHasColor MyZebra #$WhiteColor) would be too weak for this case where #$WhiteColor is one of the Zebra's main colors. COLOR-SCHEME could either be a set of colors formed using #$TheSet or it could be an instance of #$ColorScheme, which is itself a specialization of (#$SetOfTypeFn #$Color).")
(comment ExploitableFromSystemFn "(#$hasVulnerability COMPUTER (#$ExploitableFromSystemFn VULNERABILITY SYSTEM)) means that COMPUTER has the #$ComputerVulnerabilityAttribute VULNERABILITY and, furthermore, this vulnerability may be exploited by anyone logged in to the #$AccountSystem SYSTEM.   For example, (#$hasVulnerability #$Crystal-Cyc-Com (#$ExploitableFromSystemFn #$VulnerableBecauseOfReadableMail #$CycorpLANAccountSystem-Windows)) means that email stored on #$Crystal-Cyc-Com may be subject to unauthorized access from anywhere in the Cycorp Windows #$AccountSystem.")
(comment readableByExpected "(#$readableByExpected ACCT FILE) means that it is expected that the file FILE is readable by (see #$readableBy) the account ACCT. See also #$readableByNotAmenable.")
(comment readableByNotAmenable "(#$readableByNotAmenable FILE ACCOUNT) means that it is expected that the file FILE is not readable by (see #$readableBy) the account ACCOUNT.  See also #$readableByExpected.")
(comment readableByNotAmenable-Type "(#$readableByNotAmenable-Type FILE-TYPE ACCOUNT) means that it is expected that the account ACCOUNT would not be able to read (see #$readableBy) files of the type FILE-TYPE.")
(comment readableByExpected-Type "(#$readableByExpected-Type FILE-TYPE ACCOUNT) means that it is expected that the account ACCOUNT would be able to read (see #$readableBy) some files of the type FILE-TYPE.  See also #$readableByNotAmenable-Type.")
(comment departmentalTypeTaskType "(#$departmentalTypeTaskType DEPT-TYPE TASK-TYPE ROLE) means that instances of DEPT-TYPE are typically tasked with performing (or having their members perform) ROLE in instances of TASK-TYPE.  For example, some people in accounting departments are typically tasked with preparing financial records, and thus (#$departmentalTypeTaskType #$AccountingDepartment #$PreparingFinancialRecords #$performedBy) holds.")
(comment userOfAccountInDepartment "(#$userOfAccountInDepartment ACCOUNT DEPT) means that the authorized user of ACCOUNT is a member of the department DEPT.")
(comment executableByNotAmenable-Type "(#$executableByNotAmenable-Type FILE-TYPE ACCOUNT) means that it is expected that the account ACCOUNT would not be able to execute (see #$executableBy) any files of the type FILE-TYPE.")
(comment executableByExpected-Type "(#$executableByExpected-Type FILE-TYPE ACCOUNT) means that it is expected that the account ACCOUNT would be able to execute (see #$executableBy) some files of the type FILE-TYPE.  See also #$executableByNotAmenable-Type.")
(comment executableByExpected "(#$executableByExpected ACCT FILE) means that it is expected that the file FILE is executablable by (see #$executableBy) the account ACCT. See also #$executableByNotAmenable.")
(comment Reflectivity "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$Reflectivity is a collection of visible objects that are the same with respect to having a common reflectivity. Indicate a particular object's #$Reflectivity with one of the following predicates:  #$mainReflectivityOfObject or #$objectHasReflectivity.")
(comment ColorPattern "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ColorPattern is a collection of visible objects that are the same with respect to having a common color pattern. Indicate a particular object's #$ColorPattern with one of the following predicates:  #$mainColorPatternOfObject or #$objectHasColorPattern.")
(comment OrganizationFoundingFn "#$OrganizationFoundingFn is a convenience
#$IndividualDenotingFunction for denoting compositionally events in which
organizations are founded, as opposed to creating constants such as
#$FoundingOfAlQaeda or #$USFormsDesertShieldCoalition where the relationship
between the #$FoundingAnOrganization event and the #$Organization founded is
opaquely encoded in the name of the constant itself.")
(comment foundingDate "(#$foundingDate ORG DATE) is true iff the #$Organization ORG was founded in the #$TimeInterval DATE. It is a convenience predicate for relating the date of foundation to the organization founded without referring to the relevant #$FoundingAnOrganization event.")
(comment awardBestowed "(#$awardBestowed CEREMONY AWARD) is true iff AWARD is an #$AwardPractice which is bestowed on a recipient in #$AwardsCeremony
CEREMONY. This kind of bestowing is really quite different from the
#$GeneralizedTransfer events for which the #$transferredThing slot would be
appropriate.")
(comment AdministrativeDepartment "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is a group of employees that perform some kind of management role for an organization.")
(comment SystemsAdministrationDepartment "A specialization of #$ITDepartment. Each instance of this collection is a group of employees who set up, maintain, administer and ensure the security of an organization's computer systems.")
(comment GuardingSomething "A specialization of #$ActionOnObject.  Instances are events in which something (the #$objectGuarded) is watched over, or guarded, in order to maintain control over the thing (as when a prison guard guards a prisoner to prevent escape), or to maintain control over the safety of the thing (as when a bodyguard protects his/her charge).")
(comment COABackgroundScenarioMicrotheory "A collection of instances of #$Microtheory.  Each instance of #$COABackgroundScenarioMicrotheory is an MT for assertions that make up the background scenario information used in constructing a #$CourseOfAction.")
(comment actsInCapacity-DeniedFunction "(#$actsInCapacity-DeniedFunction AGENT SLOT EVENT-TYPE) holds just in case agent is not permitted to act in the way indicated by SLOT in events of EVENT-TYPE.  For instance, if Bob is not permitted to be the #$buyer in instances of #$StockTrading, we can assert (#$actsInCapacity-DeniedFunction Bob #$buyer #$StockTrading) which means Bob is not allowed to buy stocks. ")
(comment pragmaticallyNormal "A #$MetaKnowledgePredicate that is used to express that certain pragmatic preconditions on a given rule are satisfied.  The predicate takes as arguments a list of CycL variables and an asserted rule (see #$CycLRuleAssertion) in which those variables occur \"free\".  At the EL (\"epistemological level\"), #$pragmaticallyNormal sentences are not asserted as GAFs, but appear instead as the antecedents of system-generated #$implies rules.  For a given sequence of values for the respective variables VAR1, ..., VARn,    (#$pragmaticallyNormal (#$TheList VAR1 ... VARn) RULE) means that any pragmatic preconditions placed on RULE's being used by the Cyc inference engine (see #$pragmaticRequirement) are satisfied with respect to those values.
<p>
Let's make this more precise and explicit.  A pragmatic precondition for a rule is written like so:
<p>
(#$pragmaticRequirement CONDITION RULE)
<p>
and gets canonicalized into a rule involving #$pragmaticallyNormal like so:
<p>
(#$implies
(#$pragmaticallyNormal (#$TheList VAR1 ... VARn) RULE)
CONDITION),
<p>
where VAR1, ..., VARn are all and only the variables that occur free in RULE and thus include all (and possibly only) the variables that occur free in CONDITION (i.e. as RULE and CONDITION explicitly appear in the KB browser, without consideration of their having \"implicit\" initial universal quantifiers).
<p>
The above #$pragmaticallyNormal rule is used in inference as though it were a #$constraint rule.  If CONDITION does not hold for a given sequence of values <VAL1, ..., VALn> for its variables <VAR1, ..., VARn>, then those values are not \"pragmatically normal\" for RULE; and hence inference will not proceed using <VAL1, ..., VALn> with RULE.
<p>
The effect of the foregoing is that any asserted rule RULE is implicitly treated by the Cyc inference engine as if it had the form
<p>
(#$implies
(#$pragmaticallyNormal (#$TheList VAR1 ... VARn) RULE)
RULE)
<p>
(with VAR1, ..., VARn as described above).
<p>
This convention allows the logical content of a rule to be expressed independently of any pragmatic preconditions for its being used in inference.
<p>
Also see #$abnormal, which is syntactically similar and has a related  interpretation that makes it roughly the complement of #$pragmaticallyNormal.")
(comment RelationTypeByLogicalFeature "A collection of collections 
and a specialization of #$RelationshipType.  Each instance of 
#$RelationTypeByLogicalFeature is a collection of #$Relations (q.v.) 
whose instances all share some significant logical feature, such as 
commutativity or associativity.  Instances include 
#$CommutativeRelation and #$StrictlyFunctionalRelation.  An 
important specialization is #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature.")
(comment NISPlusServer-Network "A subcollection of #$Computer whose instances function as NIS+ servers for #$NISPlusDomains. It is a newer, more fully-featured version of #$NISServer-Network.")
(comment NISPlusDomain "#$NISPlusDomain is a specialization of #$ComputationalSystem.  Every #$NISPlusDomain is the group of hosts that participate in the centralized management of configuration and login information run by one #$NISPlusMasterServer-Network.")
(comment NISPlusSlaveServer-Network "A subcollection of #$NISPlusServer-Network whose instances function as slave servers for #$NISPlusDomains.")
(comment NISPlusMasterServer-Network "A subcollection of #$NISPlusServer-Network whose instances function as NIS+ master servers for #$NISPlusDomains.")
(comment importedInteractionContext "A specialization-predicate of #$genlMt.  #$importedInteractionContext is used to express visibility relationships among instances of #$RKFInteractionContextMicrotheory.")
(comment relationExpected "(#$relationExpected REL THING-1 THING-2) means that it is expected that the relation REL holds between THING-1 and THING-2; that is, it is expected that (REL THING-1 THING-2) holds.")
(comment relationNotAmenable "(#$relationNotAmenable REL THING-1 THING-2) means that it is expected that the relation REL does not hold between THING-1 and THING-2; that is, it is expected that (#$not (REL THING-1 THING-2)) holds.  See also #$relationExpected.")
(comment URLFn "An instance of both IndividualDenotingFunction and ReifiableFunction. Given a URL string as its single argument, URLFn returns the corresponding instance of #$UniformResourceLocator.")
(comment CellularTelephoneSystem "The collection of all #$TelephoneSystem that allow #$MakingACellularCall to or from #$CellularTelephone.")
(comment GSMNetwork "The collection of all #$CellularTelephoneSystem that are based on the GSM standard.")
(comment GSM900Network "The collection of all #$GSMNetwork that operate at the 900 MHz range.")
(comment GSM1800Network "The collection of all #$GSMNetwork that operate at the 1.8 GHz range.")
(comment MakingACellularCall "The collection of events in which a #$CellularTelephone is used to make a #$MakingAPhoneCall.")
(comment CellularTelephoneCompany "The collection of #$TelephoneCompany that provide services delivered over #$CellularTelephone.")
(comment cellularCompanyCoversRegion "(#$cellularCompanyCoversRegion COMPANY REGION) is true iff someone that has a valid contract with the #$CellularTelephoneCompany would be able to #$MakingACellularCall using a functioning instance of #$CellularTelephone anywhere within REGION.")
(comment cellularNetworkCoversRegion "(cellularNetworkCoversRegion NETWORK REGION) is true iff someone that has a valid contract with the #$CellularTelephoneCompany that #$hasRightsOver NETWORK would be able to #$MakingACellularCall using a functioning instance of #$CellularTelephone.")
(comment XMLFile "The subcollection of #$ComputerFile-AIS written in the language #$XML-MarkupLanguage.")
(comment keIrrelevantPrecisionSuggestion-TermGenl "(#$keIrrelevantPrecisionSuggestion-TermGenl TERM) says
the precision suggestor should never offer to generalize over TERM.  So for example, if a SME asserts, (#$genls TERM COLL) for some #$Collection COLL, the precision suggestor should not offer to replace TERM with any of its specs.
 Likewise, if (#$keIrrelevantPrecisionSuggestion-TermGenl PRED), and a SME asserts (#$relationAllExists PRED COL1 COL2), the precision suggestor should not
 offer to replace PRED with any of its #$genlPreds.")
(comment DigitalTelephoneSubscriberLine "The collection of all #$TelephoneSubscriberLines, whether land lines or cellular, which operate using digital, as opposed to analog signals.  See #$AnalogTelephoneSubscriberLine")
(comment TelephoneSubscriberLine "The collection of all telephone lines rented, purchased, or otherwise subscribed to by organizations or people.  The only communications along an instance of #$TelephoneSubscriberLine are to or from a telephone with a telephone number associated with that line.  Often, the line is a physical #$TelephoneWire between the residence or place of business (see #$TelephoneLandLine).  However, a physical wire might be multiplexed (or used in some other way) to carry several different #$TelephoneSubscriberLines.  A fiber-optic cable might carry a #$TelephoneSubscriberLine for your house as well as television signals.  This collection also includes subscriber lines for cell phones.")
(comment AnalogTelephoneSubscriberLine "The collection of all #$TelephoneSubscriberLines, whether land lines or cellular, which operate using analog, as opposed to digital signals.  See #$DigitalTelephoneSubscriberLine")
(comment TelephoneLandLine "The collection of all instances of #$TelephoneSubscriberLine which are physical wires (#$TelephoneWire) from a telephone company installation to a residence or place of business.  This does not include instances of #$FiberOpticCable which carry telephone and other signals.")
(comment TelephoneBooth "The collection of all, at least mostly, enclosed telephone booths, whether outdoors or in, freestanding or attached to walls.  This collection does not include spaces in front of banks of #$PublicTelephones at airports or telephones mounted on walls of buildings.")
(comment PublicTelephone "The collection of all telephones situated to allow use by the general public for placing telephone calls.  Some public telephones can receive calls, while others cannot.  In the vast majority of the cases, the user must pay for placing a call.  A user does not have to pay for receiving a call on a public telephone (as of fall 2002).  Public telephones, often called ``pay phones'', normally have specific hardware for collecting money and as a result have structural features to prevent such money (and the phones themselves) from being stolen.  Some newer pay phones accept electronic money (e.g. using credit/debit cards, phone cards) as well, or instead of, accepting cash.")
(disjointWith WaitingList CharacterStringToken-Serial)
(disjointWith WaitingList PartiallyTangible)
(comment WaitingList "The collection of all waiting lists.  People, cars to be serviced, and jobs to be done can all be arranged in [be #$seriesMembers of] a waiting list.")
(comment WaitingList "The collection of all waiting lists.  People, cars to be serviced, and jobs to be done can all be arranged in [be #$seriesMembers of] a waiting list.  Items on the waiting list in order to participate in some type of activity in the future which it cannot participate in at the present time due to other items of a similar time occupying limited resources.  On most waiting lists there is an assurance that the item on the list will participate in an instance of the activity type it is awaiting if it is left on the list long enough.  However, some waiting lists are for event types in which an absolute limit on the number of participants means that those waiting will only participate if others, further along in the process, drop out.  A waiting list changes over time with new things being placed on the list and old things being taken off.
<p>
Some instances of waiting list have criteria for the next type of item to be removed from the list to be serviced, which may mean that the next party that fits the criteria will be taken instead instead, for example, the next table to open for seating in a restaurant may not seat everyone who is in the next party on the restaurant's waiting list.")
(comment HeadquartersCompany-MechInf "The collection of military units at the company echelon that are tasked with the operation of a headquarters as part of a Mechanized Infantry unit.")
(comment HeadquartersCompany-Armd "The collection of military units at the company echelon that
are tasked with serving as the a headquarters as part of an
Armored unit.")
(comment DSArtilleryBattery "The collection of direct support artillery units at the
battery level.")
(comment waitingListFor "(#$waitingListFor WAIT_LIST PRED EVENT_TYPE) means that the instance of #$WaitingList, WAIT_LIST, is a list of items waiting to play the role PRED in an event of type EVENT_TYPE.  Normally an agent on a waiting list wishes to play the role PRED in an event of type EVENT_TYPE and is intentionally on the waiting list.  This is not always the case: consider a waiting list to be a maleficiary of a firing squad.")
(comment AQUAINT-Corpus "A #$Corpus containing instances of #$NewspaperArticle from
#$TheNewYorkTimes-TheNewspaper and other #$NewspaperSeries
assembled by the National Institute of Standards and Technology for the
purpose of testing and evaluating question-answering systems.")
(comment AviationCompany "The collection of #$AviationUnit-MilitarySpecialty at the
Company echelon.")
(comment TheSalientFn "Similar to #$The, (#$TheSalientFn X (SENTENCE)) refers to the X that meets the constraints of SENTENCE that is most salient in the context, where context refers to the discourse context (i.e. the conversation) in which (#$TheSalientFn X (SENTENCE)) is used.  For example, in a conversation about the black dog Fido and the white dog Rover, (#$TheSalientFn ?X (#$and (#$isa ?X #$Dog) (#$colorOfObject ?X #$BlackColor))) would refer to Fido.")
(comment TheSalientInstance "Similar to #$The, (#$TheSalientInstance THING) refers to the instance of THING that is most salient in the context, where context refers to the discourse context (i.e. the conversation) in which (#$TheSalientInstance THING) is used.")
(comment ArmoredCavalryTroop "The collection of cavalry units that have a troop
echelon.  The troop echelon in the cavalry is equivalent to the
company desgination in the rest of the US Army.")
(comment enables-SitTypeProp "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.
 (#$enables-SitTypeProp SITTYPE PROP) says that any intance SIT of SITTYPE
 will enable PROP to be true; i.e., (#$enables-SitProp SIT PROP).")
(comment prevents-SitTypeProp "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.
 (#$prevents-SitTypeProp SITTYPE PROP) says that for any instance SIT of SITYPTE, (#$prevents-SitProp SIT PROP).")
(comment PHPWebEditor "A specialization of #$HTMLEditor-Dynamic. A #$PHPWebEditor is a #$ScriptingProgram that allows web developers to create dynamically-generated web pages using #$PHP-ProgrammingLanguage.")
(comment desiredEndStateOfPlan "(#$desiredEndStateOfPlan PLAN PROP) says that if an instantiation EXECUTION of the #$Plan-ExpectedSituationType PLAN is successful (see #$successfulForAgents), then PROP will be true at the end of EXECUTION.")
(comment executionOfPlanEST "A specialization of #$isa.  
(#$executionOfPlanEST EXECUTE PLAN) says that EXECUTE is an 
 execution (carrying out of) of the #$Plan-ExpectedSituationType PLAN.
 The execution of a plan can be represented as a description of
 events unfolding in a #$PlanSpecificationMicrotheory (see #$executionOfPlan).")
(comment valence-Possible "(#$valence-Possible CHEM_STUFF_TYPE N) means that individual atoms, ions, or radicals [#$MolecularRadical] of instances of CHEM_STUFF_TYPE have a possible valence of N (an integer between -7 and +7).  The predicate, #$valence is used to specify the valence of an individual #$ChemicalObject.")
(comment kappaCardinality "This is a stub created to be used in determining cost
estimates for SKSI removal modules.")
(comment valence "(#$valence CHEM_OBJ N) means that the atom, ion, or radical [#$MolecularRadical], CHEM_OBJ, has the valence N in the current context.  If N is positive, CHEM_OBJ has N electrons outside its largest complete shell that it can donate to other chemical objects with nearly complete outer shells.  If N is negative, its outer shell would be complete if it gained (the absolute value of) N electrons.")
(comment objectStored "(#$objectStored STOR OBJ) means that in the #$StorageOfSomething situation STOR, #$PartiallyTangible OBJ is an object that is stored.")
(comment ChemicalObjectGroupingFn "(#$ChemicalObjectGroupingFn CHEM_TYPE N) is the collection of all groups of N chemical objects of type CHEM_TYPE, when CHEM_TYPE is an instance of #$ChemicalObjectTypeByChemicalSpecies.")
(disjointWith SewerLine Aqueduct)
(comment Aqueduct "A specialization of #$FluidConduit.  Each instance of #$Aqueduct is a conduit used to transport water for public use.  #$Aqueduct is disjoint with #$SewerLine.")
(comment MuscootRiver "A #$River in #$NewYork-State.")
(comment AppointedOffice "The collection of all public offices normally filled by appointment.  For example, #$USStateSecretaryOfState.  A normally elected or hereditary office filled in an exceptional circumstance by appointment does not become an instance of #$AppointedOffice as a result.")
(comment HereditaryOffice "The collection of all public offices normally filled by a relative of the previous office holder according to some set of rules.  Monarchies, for example, are normally hereditary.  There are many examples of hereditary offices being seized by persons not in hereditary line of succession.  Such seizures of office lead to the cessation of hereditary dynasties if such has been established.")
(disjointWith AppointedOffice HereditaryOffice)
(disjointWith USStateSecretaryOfState ElectedOfficial)
(comment ElectedOfficial "The collection of all public officals [#$PublicOfficial] in positions normally filled by election.  Occasionaly such offices can be filled by appointment or succession (e.g., when the previous office holder retires or dies) or by ``stealing the election''.  Such office holders are still consdered instances of this collection.  [This condition allows collections such as #$USSenator or #$UnitedStatesPresident to be specs of #$ElectedOfficial.]")
(comment stuffExhaled "(stuffExhaled EXHALE STUFF) means that during EXHALE, an instance of #$Exhaling, STUFF, an instance of #$PartiallyTangible, is the stuff which leaves the lungs.")
(comment ExhalingFn "This is a collection-forming function which takes a type of stuff and returns the class of events in which that stuff is exhaled.  Thus, '(#$ExhalingFn STUFF)' denotes the collection of exhalings of stuff.")
(comment ElectricalGenerator-Nuclear "A specialization of #$ElectricalGenerator (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ElectricalGenerator-Nuclear is a generator powered, at least indirectly, by energy released during nuclear fission (typically this enery is first used to power some other event, such as the production of steam, which in turn powers the generator).  The nuclear fission is effected by the component of the generator which is an instance of #$NuclearReactor (q.v).  A nuclear powered generator is is found in each instance of #$NuclearPowerPlantBuilding (q.v.).")
(comment ElectricalGenerator-Hydroelectric "A specialization of #$ElectricalGenerator.  Each instance of #$ElectricalGenerator generates electricity by harnessing the energy of moving water.  Typically, #$ElectricalGenerator-Hydroelectric are found in #$Dams (see #$HydroelectricDam).")
(disjointWith LandTransportationDevice ElectricalGenerator-Hydroelectric)
(comment ElectricalGenerator-Solar "A specialization of #$ElectricalGenerator.")
(comment ElectricalGenerator-Geothermal "A specialization of #$ElectricalGenerator.")
(comment HydroelectricDam "A specialization of #$Dam.  Each instance of #$HydroelectricDam is a #$Dam that contains at least one #$ElectricalGenerator-Hydroelectric (q.v.).")
(comment subKS "This parthood relation holds between two instances of
#$StructuredKnowledgeSource.  That is, (#$subKS WHOLE
PART) means that the knowledge source PART is conceptually part of
WHOLE.  For example, the individual tables in a database are the
#$subKSs of the database.  See the specialization #$subKS-Direct, which is used most often in practice.")
(comment SKSIContentMicrotheorySpindle "An instance of #$SKSIContentMicrotheorySpindle is a
#$MicrotheorySpindle that collects the content microtheories of the
individual parts of a complex knowledge source.  That is, the
#$mtSpindleMembers of an instance of this collection are the
#$contentMts of the #$subKSs of some instance of
#$StructuredKnowledgeSource-Complex.")
(comment contentMtSpindle "(#$contentMtSpindle COMPLEX-SOURCE SPINDLE) means that
SPINDLE is the #$SKSIContentMicrotheorySpindle that collects all of
the microtheories that are associated via #$contentMt with all of the
direct proper parts of COMPLEX-SOURCE (related via #$subKS-Direct).")
(comment ContentMtSpindleFn "(#$ContentMtSpindleFn COMPLEX-SOURCE) denotes the instance
of #$SKSIContentMicrotheorySpindle that collects all of the
microtheories that are associated via #$contentMt with all of the
proper parts of COMPLEX-SOURCE (related via #$subKS-Direct).  See the
corresponding predicate, #$contentMtSpindle.")
(comment contentMtHead "(#$contentMtHead SOURCE HEAD) means that the head of the
#$SKSIContentMicrotheorySpindle associated with SOURCE via
#$contentMtSpindle is HEAD.")
(comment ContentMtHeadFn "(#$ContentMtHeadFn SOURCE) denotes the head microtheory in
the #$SKSIContentMicrotheorySpindle associated with SOURCE via
#$contentMtSpindle.  See also the corresponding predicate
#$contentMtHead.")
(comment deceivedAgent "(#$deceivedAgent DECEPTION AGENT) says that AGENT was/is deceived in the #$Deceiving event DECEPTION.")
(comment enables-SitTypeSitType "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate. (#$enables-SitTypeSitType SITTYPE1 SITTYPE2) says that for (#$relationAllExists #$enables-SitSit SITTYPE1 SITTYPE2).")
(comment logicalPhysicalSchemaMap "Let LS denote an instance of #$LogicalSchema and let PS denote an instance
 of #$PhysicalSchema.  Then (#$logicalPhysicalSchemaMap LS PS) means that
the logical fields of LS encode the logical content of the physical fields of
PS.")
(comment PowerGenerationComplex-Nuclear "A specialization of #$PowerGenerationComplex.  Each instance of #$PowerGenerationComplex-Nuclear is a complex of buildings that serve to house devices that produce electricity by exploiting the energy released by a #$NuclearReactor.")
(comment NuclearReactorCoolingTower "A specialization of #$NuclearPowerPlantBuilding.  Each instance of #$NuclearReactorCoolingTower is a building that houses condenser cooling water used to cool the turbine-turning steam in an #$ElectricalGenerator-Nuclear.")
(comment NuclearReactorContainmentBuilding "A specialization of #$NuclearPowerPlantBuilding.  Each instance of #$NuclearReactorContainmentBuilding is a building that houses a #$NuclearReactor that is a component of some #$ElectricalGenerator-Nuclear.")
(comment StateOfUnconsciousness "This is the collection of all unconscious mental states of animals.")
(comment ConsciousActivity "This is the collection of all activities which are performed in a state of consciousness, that is, the animal performing the action is not sleeping, in a coma, or otherwise unconscious.")
(disjointWith ChemicalReaction ConsciousActivity)
(disjointWith BiologicalProcess-CellularLevel ConsciousActivity)
(disjointWith ConsciousActivity StateOfUnconsciousness)
(comment trafficableForVehicle "(#$trafficableForVehicle PATH VEHICLE)
says that PATH will support movement by VEHICLE.")
(comment trafficableForVehicleType "(#$trafficableForVehicleType PATH TYPE)
says that typical instances of TYPE are such that PATH will support their movement [see #$trafficableForVehicle].")
(comment TransformerSubstation "A specialization of #$ConstructionArtifact.  Each instance of #$TransformerSubstation is a complex in which large transformers convert high voltage to low voltage, or vice versa.  Transformer substations between instances of #$PowerGenerationComplex and #$ElectricPowerGrid (qq.v.) convert voltage to extremely high voltages for long-distance transmission. Transformer substations farther from generation facilities step voltage down for safe distribution to consumers.")
(comment toTheRightOf "(#$toTheRightOf OBJ1 OBJ2) says that OBJ1 is located to OBJ2's right.  Thus the arg2 of #$toTheRightOf is restricted to instances of #$LeftAndRightSidedObject. The arg1 can be any instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  Because of this asymmetry in argument restrictions, #$toTheRightOf is not sufficient for representing all cases in which one object is to the left of another (see #$toTheLeftOf).  Note that this predicate cannot be used to state that an object is to the right of another, from the perspective of a third party, except as an argument in a #$perceivesThat assertion.")
(comment ActorPlaysRoleInSituationTypeFn "#$ActorPlaysRoleInEventTypeFn is a ternary function that returns a collection of events when given a temporal thing and a role and a type of event as arguments.  (#$ActorPlaysRoleInEventTypeFn  ACTOR ROLE SITUATION-TYPE) denotes the collection of instances of SITUATION-TYPE in which ACTOR plays ROLE.  For example, (#$ActorPlaysRoleInEventTypeFn  #$GeorgeWBush #$toPossessor #$GiftGiving) denotes the collection of gift giving situations in which Bush is the recipient of the gift given.  See also #$ActorPlaysRoleFn.")
(comment toTheLeftOf "(#$toTheLeftOf OBJ1 OBJ2) says that OBJ1 is located to OBJ2's left.  Thus the arg2 of #$toTheLeftOf is restricted to instances of #$LeftAndRightSidedObject. The arg1 can be any instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  Because of this asymmetry in argument restrictions, #$toTheRightOf is not sufficient for representing all cases in which one object is to the right of another (see #$toTheRightOf).  Note that this predicate cannot be used to state that an object is to the left of another, from the perspective of a third party, except as an argument in a #$perceivesThat assertion.")
(comment pathFollowsPath "(#$pathFollowsPath P1 P2) means that the path P1 follows essentially the same course as the path P2 over some apprecialble extent of its length. This predicate may be used to describe situations in which P1 and P2 are partially overlapping (cf. #$overlappingPaths, #$subPaths-Spatial), as well as cases in which one path merely parallels the other for some distance (cf. #$alignedAlongLength).")
(comment parallelPaths "(#$parallelPaths P1 P2) means that the path P1 follows a course which parallels that of the path P2 over some appreciable extent of its length. To indicate that one path parallels another over its entire length, see the predicate #$alignedAlongLength.")
(comment overlappingPaths "(overlappingPaths PATH1 PATH2) means that PATH1 and PATH2 overlap over some appreciable extent of their length. In particular, it means that there are #$subPaths-Spatial of each which are such that one #$spatiallySubsumes the other.")
(comment expectedResponseToEventType "(#$expectedResponseToEventType AGT TYPE RESPONSE-TYPE ROLE) says that in response to an instance of TYPE, AGT is expected to play the role ROLE in an instance of RESPONSE-TYPE.  More formally,  (#$expected-ToBe (#$thereExists ?RESPONSE-INS (#$and (#$isa ?RESPONSE-INS RESPONSE-TYPE) (ROLE ?RESPONSE-INS AGT)))).  This predicate is particularly useful in representing expectations to responses to executions of plans (See #$Plan-ExpectedSituationType).")
(comment AroundAreaFn "(#$AroundAreaFn GEOTHG) takes an instance of
#$GeographicalThing and returns an instance of the area that could be
said to be around GEOTHG.  This function is useful when the exact
location of an object is not known, but there is some need to specify
where about the object is.")
(comment sitTypeEnablesAgentTo "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate. 
 (#$sitTypeEnablesAgentTo SITTYPE AGT EVENTTYPE) says that for every instance SIT of SITTYPE, (#$sitEnablesAgentTo SIT AGT EVENTTYPE).")
(comment ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies is a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible whose instances are defined _only_ by their chemical composition -- not by their physical state or any other property. Instances of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies are collections whose instances are completely uniform with each other in terms of chemical structure, e.g., #$Water, #$Caffeine, and #$IronOxide.  This collection does not include the chemical elements -- such as #$Carbon and #$Oxygen, since there can be multiple types that molecules can be formed out of a single element, e.g. O2 and #$Ozone.  Use the broader collection, #$ChemicalSubstanceType, for substances which have a general chemical specification, that is, whose instances do not have exactly the same chemical composition but fall within certain specifications, e.g., #$DNAStuff.")
(comment perCapita "(#$perCapita PRED GEO_THING MEAN) means that for every resident of (the territory of) GEO_THING, the mean val
ue of (PRED RESIDENT VALUE) is MEAN).")
(comment genlMtForApplication-Lexical "A specialization of #$genlMt.
 Use this predicate to relate special lexical microtheories to be used
 with a given #$ApplicationContext.")
(comment ApacheModule "A specialization of #$ComputerProgramModule-CW. Each instance of this collection is a module of the #$ApacheWebServer.")
(comment sourceAssumptions "This predicate relates a #$StructuredKnowledgeSource to the
#$Microtheorys that contain the concepts needed to interpret the
source.  That is, (#$sourceAssumptions SOURCE MT) means that MT
contains meta information that is required to interpret the contents
of SOURCE in CycL.  If CONTENT is the #$contentMt of SOURCE, then it
should always be provable that (#$genlMt CONTENT MT).")
(comment groupAssessedQuantity "(#$groupAssessedQuantity GROUP QUANT) means that the group
of #$BiologicalLivingObjects GROUP is assessed to have the level of
the mental feature QUANT.  This means that a significant proportion 
of the GROUP #$hasMentalQuantity QUANT, enough to warrant generalizing 
over the group, for purposes of inferring group needs or predicting
group actions.")
(comment xmlNameSpace "A predicate relating an imported XML namespace string with its source Uniform Resource Identifier.")
(comment InfrastructureOfFn "An instance of both CollectionDenotingFunction and ReifiableFunction. (#$InfrastructureOfFn COLLECTION) results in the collection of elements denoting the physical infrastructure aspect of the elements of the argument COLLECTION.")
(comment csModelOfNetwork "A predicate that relates a network model to a network.  The network changes over time as computers are added or removed, software changes,.  A model is a 'snapshot' of a network at a certain time.  (csModelOfNetwork MODEL NETWORK) means that MODEL is a snapshot of the network NETWORK.")
(comment csModelContentMt "(csModelContentMt MODEL MT) means that MODEL stores its physical description of the network in microtheory MT.")
(comment csModelPeopleMt "(csModelPeopleMt MODEL MT) means that MODEL stores the information relating to the people typically using the machines in microtheory MT.")
(comment csModelFeaturesMt "(csModelFeaturesMt MODEL MT) means that MODEL stores the information anout the general features of its LAN in mt MT.")
(comment csModelPlanningDomainMt "(csModelPlanningDomainMt MODEL MT) means that planning work for MODEL should be done with mt MT.")
(comment csModelLastModificationDate "(csModelLastModificationDate MODEL TIMEINTERVAL) means that MODEL was last modified at TIMEPOINT.")
(comment csModelCreationDate "(csModelCreationDate MODEL TIMEPOINT) means that MODEL was created at TIMEPOINT.")
(comment ManyYearsDuration "The #$Time-Quantity of many years duration.")
(comment worldProductionAmountDuring "(#$worldProductionAmountDuring PRODUCT-TYPE THING AMOUNT) says that the #$NonNegativeScalarInterval AMOUNT is a measure of the total amount of instances of the #$ProductType PRODUCT-TYPE produced anywhere in the world during the temporal extent of the #$TemporalThing THING.")
(comment explains-SitProp "(#$explains-SitProp SIT PROP) says that the #$Situation SIT explains why  PROP is the case.")
(comment typePlaysRoleInRegion "(#$typePlaysRoleInRegion TYPE SIT-TYPE ROLE REGION) says that in REGION,
 it is common-place for instances of TYPE to play ROLE in instances of SIT-TYPE.  So, for example, (#$typePlaysRoleInRegion #$Opium #$MoneyTransfer #$objectTendered #$Afghanistan) says that in Afghanistan, Opium is frequently
 used like money.")
(comment waitingListToObtainObjectType "(#$waitingListToObtainObjectType LIST OBJ_TYPE) means that LIST is a waiting list [#$WaitingList] for people to obtain [see #$toPossessor] instances of OBJ_TYPE, such as tickets, telephone lines, or the latest Christmas toy fad item.  Use #$waitingListFor if the waiting list is for particpation in some type of event.")
(comment UserAccountGroup "A specialization of #$Group. Each instance of this collection is a 'group' as defined on a computer network as a named set of #$UserAccounts which share a given set of access permissions to certain network resources (see, for example #$groupAccountForFile). Groups may be defined along functional, departmental, or other lines, and users may belong to a number of groups. However, each user account will be a member of just one primary group, which will be associated by default with the files that that user creates. A group is related to its user accounts by the predicate #$groupMembers (q.v.). Note that instances of #$UserAccountGroup, being #$Groups, are not #$Collections.")
(comment groupHasSingers "(#$groupHasSingers GROUP SINGER) says that the musical group GROUP has, among its singers, SINGER.")
(comment accountName "A predicate that relates computer accounts to their name-strings (as listed in the 'etc/passwd' file). (#$accountName ACCOUNT NAME) means that the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT's name-string is NAME. Note that very occasionally it can be sensible to have more than one name-string for a given account (i.e. an account with a given UID). For instance, in cases such as UUCP, the superuser account, one sometimes wants to give multiple people access to the same account, but to track their activities via the audit trail. See also #$groupName, #$accountFullName.")
(comment ReligiousEdict "A specialization of #$Announcement.  Instances of #$ReligiousEdict are proclamations by relgious leaders that have the
 force of law, and so, in effect, are modifications of some body of laws that
 guide some religion or religious organization.")
(comment Retrovirus "The collection of all viruses that have their genome expressed in RNA instead of DNA and use the enzyme, reverse transcriptase, to copy its genome into the DNA of host cells.")
(comment ProteaseInhibitor "The collection of all types of drugs that block retroviral protease from working.  This blocks a step in viral reproduction.")
(comment groupName "A predicate that relates computer user account groups to their name-strings (as listed in the file '/etc/groups'). (#$groupName GROUP NAME) means that the #$UserAccountGroup GROUP's name-string is NAME. See also #$accountName.")
(comment groupForAccountSystem "A predicate that relates computer user account groups to the #$AccountSystem which administers them. (#$groupForAccountSystem GROUP SYSTEM) means that the #$UserAccountGroup GROUP's #$AccountSystem is SYSTEM.")
(comment softwareHasFault "A simplification predicate. (softwareHasFault FAULT     PROGRAM-OR-SERIES) holds just in case there is some DATABASE and ID-STRING such that (faultOfSoftwareObject FAULT  DATABASE ID-STRING PROGRAM-OR-SERIES).")
(comment DomainAdministratorAccountFn "(#$DomainAdministratorAccountFn DOMAIN) denotes the #$AdministratorAccount on the #$NTDomain DOMAIN.  That is, (#$administratorAccountForSystem (#$DomainAdministratorAccountFn DOMAIN) DOMAIN) holds. This type of account is different than a \"Local Administrator Account\" (cf. #$LocalAdministratorAccountFn) which governs a single machine.")
(comment FarmersInRegionFn "(#$FarmersInRegionFn REG) denotes the collection of all farmers
of a particular region.  This function allows inferencing on the
activities and such of a regions farmers, i.e. French farmers on
strike, farmers of South Carolina experienceing a drought, Afghan
farmers planting opium seeds.")
(comment areaOfPlantBLOPlantedByType "(#$areaOfPlantBLOPlantedByType SEED-TYPE FARMER-TYPE AREA) relates
the area of land AREA the collection of #$Persons FARMER-TYPE planted
of a seed type SEED-TYPE.  This predicate can be useful to make
inferences on the amount of seed planted versus crops harvested,
etc.")
(comment drugCocktailIngredient "(#$drugCocktailIngredient COCKTAIL DRUG_TYPE) means that any instance of COCKTAIL, an instance o
f #$DrugCocktail, includes doses of the specified type of drug.")
(comment AIDSDrugCocktail "The collection of all #$DrugCocktails designed for the treatment of HIV/AIDS patients.")
(comment DrugCocktail "A second-order collection.  Each instance of #$DrugCocktail is a collection of groups of drug do
sages (using a specific set of drug types) which are designed to be taken to counteract some disease.  The
term ``drug cocktail'' is normally used for HIV patients, but the collection is appropriate for other dis
eases as well.")
(comment agentSupportsAgent-Generic "(#$agentSupportsAgent-Generic AGT1 AGT2) means that AGT1
supports in some manner AGT2.")
(comment orgAllocatesAmountFor "(#$orgAllocatesAmountFor ORG MONEY SIT-TYPE) means the
instance ORG has allocated the amount MONEY for instances of
SIT-TYPE.")
(comment orgAllocatesAmountForIn "(#$orgAllocatesAmountForIn ORG MONEY SITTYPE DATE) means
that in DATE, the #$Organization ORG allocates the funds MONEY for instances of
SITTYPE.  This is a broad predicate meant to cover a variety of
organizations like the US government that sets aside money for the
drug war, or the Gates Foundation that allocates money for AIDS
research.")
(comment JapaneseCompany "The collection of #$CommercialOrganization that are based in
#$Japan. Sufficient conditions include being in the #$hasHeadquartersInRegion
with #$Japan. Necessary conditions are hard to specify.")
(comment ventureCompany "(#$ventureCompany VENTURE COMPANY) is true iff COMPANY is the #$CommercialOrganization that is the focus of the #$JointVentureActivity VENTURE. For example if #$IBMInc and #$HitachiLtd participated in a #$JointVentureActivity IBM-HITACHI-VENTURE resulting in the company IBM-HIT, then (#$ventureCompany IBM-HITACHI-VENTURE IBM-HIT) would be true.")
(comment ventureMember "(#$ventureMember VENTURE PARTNER) is true iff PARTNER is an
instance of #$CommercialOrganization that is one of the partners in
#$JointVentureActivity VENTURE.")
(comment interestInVenture "(#$interestInVenture PARTNER VENTURE INTEREST) is true iff PARTNER is an
instance of #$CommercialOrganization that is one of the partners in
#$JointVentureActivity VENTURE and has INTEREST -- e.g. 15% -- interest in VENTURE.")
(comment ChineseCompany "The collection of #$CommercialOrganization that are based in
#$Japan. Sufficient conditions include being in the #$hasHeadquartersInRegion
with #$China-PeoplesRepublic. Necessary conditions are hard to specify.")
(comment venturePartner "(#$venturePartner COMPANY1 COMPANY2) is true iff there is a
#$JointVentureActivity such that both COMPANY1 and COMPANY2 have an interest
in it (see #$interestInVenture).")
(comment ThaiCompany "The collection of #$CommercialOrganization that are based in
#$Thailand. Sufficient conditions include being in the #$hasHeadquartersInRegion
with #$Thailand. Necessary conditions are hard to specify.")
(comment JointVenturesFn "The NAT (#$JointVenturesFn SET) denotes the collection of all
#$JointVentureActivity in which all companies that are #$elementOf SET have or have
had an interest. For example, (#$JointVenturesFn (#$TheSet #$IBMInc #$HitachiLtd))
denotes the set of all #$JointVentureActivity in which both #$IBMInc and #$HitachiLtd
have or have had an interest (see #$interestInVenture).")
(comment IndianCompany "The collection of #$CommercialOrganization that are based in
#$India. Sufficient conditions include being in the #$headquartersInRegion
with #$India. Necessary conditions are hard to specify.")
(comment BallBearing "The collection of little sphere shaped objects that are used in 
#$MechanicalDevices in order to reduce friction.")
(comment sourceOfFor-TypeLevel "(#$sourceOfFor-TypeLevel ?SOURCE-TYPE ?OF ?FOR) means that ?SOURCE-TYPE is a source of ?OF for ?FOR, e.g. plants are a source of food for many mammals or electricity is a source of energy for computers.")
(comment sourceOfForInstance-TypeLevel "(#$sourceOfForInstance-TypeLevel ?SOURCE-TYPE ?OF ?IND) means that
?SOURCE-TYPE is a source of ?OF for the instance ?IND of
#$Individual, e.g. plants are a source of food for any
particular human being.  See #$sourceOfFor-TypeLevel.")
(comment MSOperatingSystem "A specialization of #$OperatingSystem.  It is the collection of all #$MicrosoftInc
operating systems.  More general than #$MSWindowsOperatingSystem it includes all Windows and
Dos operating systems.")
(comment patchFixesSoftwareFaultOf "a simplification predicate.  (#$patchFixesSoftwareFaultOf PATCH FAULT  SOFTWARE-OBJECT) holds just in case there exists a DATABASE and ID-STRING such  that (#$patchFixesFaultOf PATCH FAULT DATABASE ID-STRING SOFTWARE-OBJECT).")
(comment productUnderPatentInCountry "(#$productUnderPatentInCountry PRODUCT COUNTRY) means that PRODUCT, an instance of #$ProductType, is held under patent according to the laws of COUNTRY, an instance of #$Country.  This meansthat instances of PRODUCT may not be produced or marketed within COUNTRY's territorial jurisdiction without approval from (and normally payment to) the patent holder.")
(comment countryVisited "<code>(#$countryVisited PERSON COUNTRY)</code> means that <code>PERSON</code> has visited <code>COUNTRY</code> at some point in time.")
(comment nodeOfTypeInAccountSystem "(#$nodeOfTypeInAccountSystem COMPUTER COMPUTERTYPE ACCOUNT-SYSTEM) means that COMPUTER is one of the #$Computers that play the #$ComputerServerTypeByFunction role of COMPUTERTYPE in the #$AccountSystem ACCOUNT-SYSTEM. For instance, (#$nodeOfTypeInAccountSystem Betty #$MailServer-Network #$CycorpLANAccountSystem-Windows) means that Betty is an email server on the Cycorp Windows account system. See also #$nodeOfTypeInLAN.")
(comment infectionRateOfIn "(#$infectionRateOfIn DISEASE PLACE NUMBER) means that the number of people in (the territory of) PLACE who are infected with DISEASE is NUMBER (a fraction between 0 and 1).")
(comment RoboticArm "The collection of mechanical devices that can manipulate objects and/or materials automatically.")
(comment homeDirectoryLocation "A binary predicate that relates #$LoginAccounts to their home directory #$Pathnames. (#$homeDirectoryLocation ACCOUNT PATHNAME) means that PATHNAME is a string that indicates the location of the home directory for ACCOUNT. (This usually pertains to Unix systems). For instance, (#$homeDirectoryLocation (#$UserAccountFn #$CycorpLANAccountSystem-Unix \"bfraser\") \"/home/bfraser\").")
(comment MoneyPerQuantityRate "How much something costs per given quantity.")
(comment DollarsPerUnit "The cost (in U.S.Dollars) per unit.")
(comment animalHasBloodType "(#$animalHasBloodType ANIMAL BLOOD_TYPE) means that the specified animal has blood of the #$BloodType BLOOD_TYPE).  See also #$hasBloodType for describing blood.")
(comment SambaServer-Network "A specialization of #$ComputerServer. Each instance of this collection is a computer which runs #$Samba-TheProgram (a filesharing package which provides interoperability between Unix and Windows systems).")
(comment Agrochemical-Molecule "The subcollection of #$Molecules developed and used for agricultural purposes,
e.g. herbicides or insecticides.")
(comment Radionuclide "The collection of radioactive nuclides.")
(comment MovingAllLegs "This is the collection of all #$LegMovement events in which all legs are moved.")
(comment OneAtATimeProcessType "The collection of types of process which occupy the doer to such an extent that two such processes can be done simultaneously by the same doer only if both are common #$subEvents of a process of the same type.  Cf. #$OneAtATimeActionType.")
(comment MovingBothArms "This is the collection of all #$ArmMovement events in which both arms are moved.")
(comment OneAtATimeActionType "The collection of types of action which occupy the doer to such an extent that two such actions cannot be done simultaneously by the same doer.")
(comment SQLServer-Network "A specialization of #$DatabaseServer-Network. Each instance of this collection is a computer which provides database services to a network by running some instance of #$SQLServerProgram.")
(comment DatabaseServer-Network "A specialization of #$ComputerServer. Each instance of this collection is a computer which provides database services to a network by running some instance of #$DatabaseServerProgram. An inmportant specialization of this collection is #$SQLServerProgram.")
(comment faultAllowsExploit "A binary predicate that relates a computer bug or fault with the hack that 
 exploits it.  (#$faultAllowsExploit FAULT MALWARE) holds just in case FAULT is a fault 
in some program that MALWARE exploits.")
(comment shellForAccount "A binary predicate that relates Unix #$LoginAccounts to the program which each account runs upon logging in. This program will usually be a #$UnixShell (e.g. #$Bash-TheProgram, #$Csh-TheProgram), but need not necessarily be (e.g. #$Sync-TheProgram).")
(comment primaryGroupForAccount "A predicate that relates instances of #$ComputerAccount to their primary #$UserAccountGroups (the #$groupName of which will be listed alongside their #$accountName in the 'etc/passwd' file). Note that accounts may belong to other #$UserAccountGroups as well as their primary group. (#$primaryGroupForAccount ACCOUNT GROUP) means that the primary group of ACCOUNT is GROUP.")
(comment UNIXMachine "The collection of all #$Computers that run some version of #$UnixOS (e.g. #$Linux, Solaris...).")
(comment productionRateDuring "An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.
 #$productionRateDuring contains an argument place for a #$TemporalThing, so that one can make the equivalent of #$productionRate assertions wrapped in #$holdsIn.")
(comment BayesVariable "The collection of all Bayesian Network Variables intended for probability reasoning.  #$BayesVariables are random variables that each typically represent the likelihood of a proposition being true (expressed as a real number between zero and one, where zero means certainly false and one means certainly true).  See #$BayesNet.")
(comment BayesDiscreteOutcome "The type collection of all Bayesian Network variable Outcomes intended for probability reasoning.  Instances of #$BayesDiscreteOutcomes are collections that characterize a possible outcome state of a #$BayesVariable.")
(comment PotentialResourceForFn "A unary function that returns an instance of #$FirstOrderCollection when given a specialization of #$Situation as an argument. (#$PotentialResourceForFn SIT-TYPE) denotes the collection of individuals that can play roles required to bring about an instance of SIT-TYPE.")
(comment bayesVariableOutcomes "A predicate used for constructing bayesian networks.  See #$BayesNet. (#$bayesVariableOutcomes BAYES-VARIABLE LIST) means that the list contains instances of #$BayesDiscreteOutcome, and the BAYES-VARIABLE is an (uncertain) instance of one of these outcomes.")
(comment bayesVariableNet "A predicate used for constructing bayesian networks.  See #$BayesNet. (#$bayesVariableNet BAYES-VARIABLE BAYES-NET) means that the BAYES-VARIABLE is a node of the BAYES-NET")
(comment Packet-SYN "#$Packet-SYN is a #$Packet-Internet.  When a computer wishes to establish a network connection to another computer, according to the #$InternetProtocol, a #$Packet-SYN (synchronization request packet) is sent from the originating computer to a specific port that is in a \"listen\" state on another computer.  The listening computer sends a SYN/ACK (synchronize/acknowledge) packet back to the first computer, which completes the handshake by sending an #$Packet-ACK (acknowledge packet).")
(comment Packet-ACK "#$Packet-ACK is a #$Packet-Internet, and is the last part of the threeway handshake that occurs when a computer wishes to establish a network connection to another computer.  According to the #$InternetProtocol, a SYN (synchronization request) packet is sent from the originating computer to a specific port that is in a \"listen\" state on another computer.  The listening computer sends a SYN/ACK (synchronize/acknowledge) packet back to the first computer, which completes the handshake by sending an #$Packet-ACK (acknowledge packet).")
(comment RemoteAdministrationProgram-Malicious "#$RemoteAdministrationProgram-Malicious is a specialization of #$RemoteAdministrationProgram and #$MaliciousComputerProgram.  It is the collection of malicious programs used to administer computers remotely.")
(comment WindowsService "A specialization of #$ServiceProgram. Windows services are highly specialized applications designed to run for extended periods in their own Windows sessions, usually with no user interface. (They perform a similar function to daemons on Unix.) Windows services exist for network connection management, disk access monitoring, and certain security tasks. Windows always has a variety of services running, most of which the user is never directly aware of. In #$MSWindows2000 the list of available services may be viewed by opening Administrative Tools from the Control Panel, then opening Computer Management and selecting Services on the navigation tree.")
(comment USSecretaryOfDefense "The federal Secretary of Defense heads the  efforts of the Department of Defense.")
(comment bayesVariableHorizontalGraphPosition "A #$BinaryPredicate which associates a #$BayesVariable node with its horizontal graph position in the JavaBayes editor panel.  The graph position is measured in #$Pixels.  See #$bayesVariableVerticalGraphPosition")
(comment bayesVariableVerticalGraphPosition "A #$BinaryPredicate which associates a #$BayesVariable node with its vertical graph position in the JavaBayes editor panel.  The graph position is measured in #$Pixels.  See #$bayesVariableHorizontalGraphPosition.")
(comment VulnerableToUnauthorizedDomainAdministratorAccess "A computer has this vulnerability if it allows an unauthorized user to access a domain administrator account.  When remotely exploitable, it is a consequence of an #$ExternalAccessToAccountsFault-RemoteAdministrator  When locally exploitable, it is typically a consequence of an #$ElevatedPrivilegeFault-Administrator.")
(comment CreationTypeByMethod "Each instance of #$CreationTypeByMethod is a spec of PhysicalCreationEvent which is characterized by a particular method for the creation of the output.  Examples include #$Assembling, #$Mixing and #$Extruding.  Instances of CreationTypeByMethod are generally agnostic as to the type of thing created and the materials used, as much as they can be within the constraints of the creation method -- e.g., you can't really assemble liquids or extrude powder.")
(comment CreationByMethodFn "(#$CreationByMethodFn EX-TYPE METHOD) denotes the collection of events in which instances of EX-TYPE are made using the process METHOD.  For example, (#$CreationByMethodFn #$Table-PieceOfFurniture #$Assembling) is the collection of events in which instances of #$Table-PieceOfFurniture are created by #$Assembling.")
(comment creationByMethod "(#$creationByMethod EX-TYPE METHOD CREATE-TYPE) means that CREATE-TYPE is the collection of creation events in which things of type EX-TYPE are created using the method METHOD.  For example, (#$creationByMethod #$Table-PieceOfFurniture #$Assembling FOO) means that FOO is is the collection of events in which instances of #$Table-PieceOfFurniture are created by #$Assembling.")
(comment VulnerableToUnauthorizedLocalAdministratorAccess "A computer has this vulnerability if it allows an unauthorized user to access its local administrator account.  When remotely exploitable, it is a consequence of an #$ExternalAccessToAccountsFault-RemoteAdministrator  When locally exploitable, it is typically a consequence of an #$ElevatedPrivilegeFault-LocalAdministrator.")
(comment doListAction "A ternary complex action predicate. (#$doListAction ACTION-TEMPLATE LIST N) means that ACTION-TEMPLATE is executed once wherein each element of LIST is substituted for the Nth arg position of ACTION-TEMPLATE.")
(comment AccessingAComputerAccount "A specialization of #$ComputerActivity.  Each instance of #$AccessingAComputerAccount is an event in which an agent accesses a computer account, usually by getting a process to run with the privileges of the account.  Typically, this is done by logging in to an account by entering a username and password when prompted (see, e.g.,  #$LoggingInOnAComputer), but it can be done in less forthright ways via security holes.")
(comment accountAccessed "(#$accountAccessed ACCESS ACCOUNT) means that in the #$AccessingAComputerAccount event ACCESS, the #$ComputerAccount accessed is ACCOUNT.")
(comment accountSystemAccessed "(#$accountSystemAccessed ASSESS SYSTEM) means that in the #$AccessingAComputerAccount event ACCESS, the #$AccountSystem on which an account is accessed is SYSTEM.")
(comment biologicalDaughters "A specialization of #$biologicalOffspring (q.v.)  that relates a given person or other animal to each of its female biological offspring.  (#$biologicalDaughters PARENT DAUGHTER) means that PARENT is a biological parent of the female DAUGHTER.")
(comment GreatestHitsAlbum-CW "The collection of all #$Album-CWs that contain HitSong-CWs by a particular musician, composer or musical group.")
(comment LiveAlbum-CW "The collection of all #$Album-CWs that are reproductions of live performances.")
(comment DoubleAlbum-CW "The collection of all #$Album-CWs whose intantiations contain two records or CDs.")
(comment AggregateSituationTypeFn "(#$AggregateSituationTypeFn SIT-TYPE) denotes the collection of situations whose instances are composed (a la #$subSituations) of multiple instances of SIT-TYPE.  The denoted collection is agnostic as to whether its instances are composed of serially-occurring instances of SIT-TYPE, instances which occur in parallel, or some mix thereof.  The genls of the denoted collection is determined by the value for AG-TYPE in (#$typeAggregatesIntoType SIT-TYPE AG-SIT-TYPE).")
(comment aggregateSituationType "(#$aggregateSituationType SIT-TYPE AG-TYPE) means that AG-TYPE is the collection of situations whose instances are composed (a la #$subSituations) of multiple instances of SIT-TYPE.  AG-TYPE is agnostic as to whether its instances are composed of serially-occurring instances of SIT-TYPE, instances which occur in parallel, or some mix thereof.  The genls of AG-TYPE is determined by the value for AG-SIT-TYPE in (#$typeAggregatesIntoType SIT-TYPE AG-SIT-TYPE).")
(comment typeAggregatesIntoType "(#$typeAggregatesIntoType ?SIT-TYPE1 ?SIT-TYPE2) means that any situation which is an aggregate of ?SIT-TYPE1 -- i.e., it is composed (a la #$subSituations) of some number of instances of ?SIT-TYPE1, but not of other different types of situations -- will be an instance of ?SIT-TYPE2.  For example, (#$typeAggregatesIntoType #$Buying #$Buying) means that a buying event which is composed of multiple buyings (e.g., when several objects are bought) will still be an instance of #$Buying.")
(comment AggregateSituationTypeByRoleFn "(#$AggregateSituationTypeByRoleFn SIT-TYPE ROLE) denotes a collection of situations whose instances are aggregates of two or more instances of SIT-TYPE, with different objects filling ROLE.  For example, the purchase of a dozen eggs can be viewed as an aggregate of 12 purchasings, in each of which one egg is purchased, and would be an instance of (#$AggregateSituationTypeByRoleFn #$Buying #$objectPaidFor).")
(comment aggregateSituationTypeByRole "(#$aggregateSituationTypeByRole SIT-TYPE ROLE AG-TYPE) means that AG-TYPE is a collection of situations, each instance of which is an aggregate of 2 or more instances of SIT-TYPE in which different objects play ROLE.")
(comment aggregateRole "(#$aggregateRole ROLE AG-ROLE) means that AG-ROLE is an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate which is related to ROLE in that whenever both (ROLE ?SITUATION ?OBJ) and (AG-ROLE ?SITUATION ?GROUP) hold, ?OBJ is a member of ?GROUP.  In other words, AG-ROLE is a #$BinaryRolePredicate that relates a situation to the group consisting of all the situation participants which relate to it via ROLE.  For example, consider a dishwashing event Dishwashing001 in which many dishes are washed.  Each dish that is washed is an #$objectActedOn in Dishwashing001.  If (#$aggregateRole #$objectActedOn AG-ROLE), then each washed dish is also a member of a group, call it DishGroup001, such that (AG-ROLE Dishwashing001 DishGroup001).")
(comment AggregateRoleFn "(#$AggregateRoleFn ROLE) denotes an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate which is related to ROLE in that whenever both (ROLE ?SITUATION ?OBJ) and ((#$AggregateRoleFn ROLE) ?SITUATION ?GROUP) hold, ?OBJ is a member of ?GROUP.  In other words, (#$AggregateRoleFn ROLE) is a #$BinaryRolePredicate that relates a situation to the group consisting of all the situation participants which relate to it via ROLE.  For example, consider a dishwashing event Dishwashing001 in which many dishes are washed.  Each dish that is washed is an #$objectActedOn in Dishwashing001.  Each washed dish is also a member of a group, call it DishGroup001, such that ((#$AggregateRoleFn ROLE) Dishwashing001 DishGroup001).")
(comment Duo-MusicalPerformanceGroup "A group of 2 musicians that perform together.")
(comment uniquePartOfType "'(#$uniquePartOfType WHOLE PART-TYPE PART)' means that PART is the one and only part of WHOLE of type PART-TYPE.")
(comment MtSpace "(#$MtSpace MT-1 ... MT-N) denotes the microtheory whose set of domain assumptions is the union of the assertions true in each of MT-1 ... MT-N. Consequently, (#$MtSpace MT-1 ... MT-N) has each of MT-1 ... MT-N as one of its #$genlMts. Typically, but not always, there will be assertions true in (#$MtSpace MT-1 ... MT-N) that are not true in any of its #$genlMts. For example,
<p>
(#$MtSpace #$WorldHistoryMt (#$MtDim #$mtTimeIndex (#$YearFn 1790)))
<p>
denotes the microtheory whose domain assumption are precisely those of the #$WorldHistoryMt (whose basic assumption is that we are in the context of actual world history) and those of the microtheory (#$MtDim #$mtTimeIndex (#$YearFn 1790)) (whose basic assumption is that 1790 is the present year -- see #$MtDim). The conjunction of these domain assumptions implies everything that is true in real world history in the year 1790. For example, these domain assumptions imply that (#$president #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$GeorgeWashington) is true. Consequently, this assertion is true in (#$MtSpace #$WorldHistoryMt (#$MtDim #$mtTimeIndex (#$YearFn 1790))).")
(comment MtDim "(#$MtDim MT-DIM-PRED VALUE) denotes a microtheory whose
sole assumptions (beyond the #$UniversalVocabularyMt) are that VALUE
is its value for the property MT-DIM-PRED.  For example,
<p>
(#$MtDim #$mtTimeIndex (#$YearFn 1776))
<p>
denotes a microtheory whose only assumptions are that the
#$mtTimeIndex is the year 1776.  This is most useful in conjunction
with #$MtSpace.")
(comment MicrotheoryDimensionPredicate "The collection of binary predicates that constitute the
currently supported dimensions of microtheory space.")
(comment SingleFixedDimensionMicrotheory "The collection of all instances of #$Microtheory which have
a specified value for exactly one #$MicrotheoryDimensionPredicate and
no other assumptions beyond that of the #$UniversalVocabularyMt.  If
the #$UniversalVocabularyMt can be thought of as the entire
N-dimensional microtheory space, then a
#$SingleFixedDimensionMicrotheory is an N-1 subspace of that space.
These are usually denoted by uses of #$MtDim. ")
(comment sentenceTruth "A #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) that is used to state the truth of a given CycL sentence. (#$sentenceTruth SENT TRUTH) means that the CycL sentence SENT has the truth value TRUTH, which is either #$True or #$False.")
(comment NLGenerationFunction "The collection of all #$NLFunctions that denote non-atomic terms used for generating natural language.")
(comment networkImplementsProtocol "(#$networkImplementsProtocol NETWORK PROT) means that communication is conducted over the #$ComputerNetwork NETWORK according to the #$NetworkProtocol PROT.")
(comment BinaryRoleTypePredicate "A specialization of #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate.  Each instance of #$BinaryRoleTypePredicate is a predicate that relates instances of #$Situation (q.v.) to types of things that are \"involved\" in them in some way.  Cf. #$BinaryRolePredicate.")
(comment BinaryRoleTypePredFn "#$BinaryRoleTypePredFn is a function that returns an instance of #$BinaryRoleTypePredicate when given an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate.  (#$BinaryRoleTypePredFn BIN-ROLE-PRED) denotes the instance of #$BinaryRoleTypePredicate that has the same argument constraints as BIN-ROLE-PRED in the first argument position, and has as an #$arg2Genl what BIN-ROLE-PRED has as an #$arg2Isa.")
(comment binaryRoleTypePred "(#$binaryRoleTypePred BIN-ROLE-PRED BIN-ROLE-TYPE-PRED) means that BIN-ROLE-PRED is like BIN-ROLE-TYPE-PRED in terms of argument constraints except that BIN-ROLE-TYPE-PRED has as an #$arg2Genl what BIN-ROLE-PRED has as an arg2Isa.  #$binaryRoleTypePred is the predicate that corresponds to the function #$BinaryRoleTypePredFn (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical).")
(comment CombinationReactionType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$CombinationReactionType is a type of chemical reaction (i.e., a specialization of #$ChemicalReaction) in which two or more substances (instances of #$ChemicalObject) react to form a single product (also an instance of #$ChemicalObject).")
(comment IonicCompoundType "A specialization of #$IonicDecomposableCompoundType and transitively, of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.  Each instance of #$IonicCompoundType is a type of compound whose instances contain both positively and negatively charged ions and form three-dimensional structures of varying extent instead of molecules.  Instances of #$IonicCompoundType generally consist of metal cations and non-metallic anions.")
(comment NonMetal "An instance of #$TangibleStuffCompositionType and a specialization of #$ElementStuff.  Each instance of #$ElementStuff is a piece of tangible stuff, composed of a quantity of atoms, all of which are of the same non-metallic element.")
(disjointWith Metal NonMetal)
(comment Cation "A specialization of #$Ion.  Each instance of #$Cation is an ion with a positive charge.")
(comment Anion "A specialization of #$Ion.  Each instance of #$Anion is an ion with a negative charge.")
(disjointWith Cation Anion)
(comment ElectronicCharge "An instance of #$UnitOfCharge. When applied to a number, #$ElectronicCharge returns an instance of #$ElectricalCharge as its value.  For example (#$ElectronicCharge 3) is an electrical charge of +3.  Note that (#$ElectronicCharge 1) is equal to 1.602 x 10^-19 Coulombs.")
(comment cationTypeOfIonicCompoundType "A binary predicate that relates a type of ionic compound to the type of cation that occurs in that compound. (#$cationTypeOfIonicCompoundType COMPOUND-TYPE CATION-TYPE) means that CATION-TYPE is the type of cation that occurs in ionic compounds of the type COMPOUND-TYPE.")
(comment anionTypeOfIonicCompoundType "A binary predicate that relates a type of ionic compound to the type of anion that occurs in that compound. (#$anionTypeOfIonicCompoundType COMPOUND-TYPE ANION-TYPE) means that ANION-TYPE is the type of anion that occurs in ionic compounds of the type COMPOUND-TYPE.")
(comment CombustionReaction "A specialization of both #$ChemicalReaction and #$CombustionProcess.  Each instance of #$CombustionReaction is a rapid chemical reaction that produces a flame.  Many combustion reactions involve oxygen from the air as a reactant.")
(comment HydrocarbonMolecule "An instance of #$MoleculeTypeByChemicalSpecies.  Each instance of
#$HydrocarbonMolecule is a molecule consisting wholly of a combination of
hydrogen and carbon atoms.")
(comment CombustionOfHydrocarbonInAir "A specialization of #$CombustionReaction.  Each instance of
#$CombustionOfHydrocarbonInAir is a combustion reaction in which a
hydrocarbon combines with oxygen in the air to form carbon dioxide and
water vapor.")
(comment objectTypeAvailableToAgentType "(#$objectTypeAvailableToAgentType OBJ-TYPE AGENT-TYPE) means that typically some instance of OBJ-TYPE is available for agents of type AGENT-TYPE.  If an instance OBJ of OBJ-TYPE is available to an instance AGENT of type AGENT-TYPE, typically nothing prevents AGENT from choosing to use OBJ to accomplish a task. Compare this predicate to #$availableForEvent, #$availableToAgentTypeForEvent, and #$availableToAgentTypeForEventType.")
(comment Log-10Fn "The Base-10 log function.  (#$Log-10Fn NUM) returns the exponent that 10 is raised to to get NUM.  For example, (Log-10Fn 100) = 2; 10^2 is 100.")
(comment InvLog-10Fn "The inverse of the Base-10 Log function.  (#$InvLog-10Fn NUM) returns the number  that is the result of raising 10 to the exponent NUM. For example, (#$InvLog-10Fn 2) = 100; 100 is the result of raising 10 to the exponent 2.")
(comment NamesForTermFn "(#$NamesForTermFn TERM) returns a list of strings each of which is known to be a name for TERM (via #$termStrings or one of its spec preds).")
(comment RemoveLeadingDefiniteArticleFromStringFn "(#$RemoveLeadingDefiniteArticleFromStringFn STRING) returns the substring of STRING with no leading definite article ``the'' (STRING itself if it doesn't start with ``the'').")
(comment ELListToSubLListFn "(#$ELListToSubLListFn LIST) returns a SubL list corresponding to LIST.")
(comment proximityConstraints-Type "(#$proximityConstraints-Type SIT-TYPE ROLE1 ROLE2 RANGE) means that RANGE is the range of distance that object OBJ can be from the location in which something plays ROLE2 in an instance SIT of #$Situation SIT-TYPE in order for OBJ to play the role ROLE1 in SIT.  For example, (#$proximityConstraints-Type (#$KillingByMeansOfFn #$Hatchet) #$performedBy #$organismKilled (#$Meter .25 30) means that whoever kills with a hatchet must be between a quarter meter and 30 meters from the organism killed; (#$proximityConstraints-Type (#$KillingByMeansOfFn #$Hatchet) #$deviceUsed #$organismKilled #$ActuallyTouching) means that a hatchet must actually touch any organism it kills.  Compare this predicate to the instance-level predicate #$proximityConstraints.")
(comment massNumberOfAtom "(#$massNumberOfAtom ATOM NUM) is true iff ATOM is an instance
of #$Atom with a mass number of NUM. The mass number of an
atom is the sum of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom of
X. All atoms of a particular instance of
#$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons have the same number of protons in the
nucleus   (expressed as the #$atomicNumber of the element). Isotopes of an
element  (cf. #$IsotopeFn) have a different number of neutrons. Thus the
number of neutrons in an atom of X (where X an element or isotope) equals: the
mass number of the atom minus the atomic number of X. Isotopes are identified by
the mass number of their atoms: e.g. (#$IsotopeFn Carbon 14).")
(comment LogarithmFn "A #$BinaryFunction which returns the result of computing the logarithm of a specified number to a specified base. (#$LogarithmFn NUMBER BASE) returns log_BASE(NUMBER), i.e. the logarithm of NUMBER in base BASE, where NUMBER is an instance of #$PositiveNumber and BASE is an instance of #$NonNegativeNumber. It returns instances of #$RealNumber.
<p>
Note that (#$ExponentFn BASE (#$LogarithmFn NUMBER BASE)) = NUMBER.
<p>
See also #$LogFn, a unary function which returns the logarithm of a number in base #$E-LogarithmBase.")
(comment PhysicalDestructionByMeansOfFn "#$PhysicalDestructionByMeansOfFn is a function that returns a collection that is a specialization of both #$PurposefulAction and #$DestructionEvent when given a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  (#$PhysicalDestructionByMeansOfFn OBJ-TYPE) denotes the specialization of #$DestructionEvent in which an instance of OBJ-TYPE plays an instrumental role (i.e., in which an instance of OBJ-TYPE plays the role of #$instrument-Generic).")
(comment BattleAxe "A specialization of #$CloseQuarterCombatWeapon.  Each instance of #$BattleAxe is a large axe designed to be used as a weapon in hand-to-hand fighting.")
(comment completeAtomicComposition-List "A ternary predicate that relates a type of chemical substance to the types of atoms and  the number of atoms of each type that make up its instances. (#$completeAtomicComposition-List SUBST ELEMENT-LIST SUBSCRIPT-LIST), where ELEMENT-LIST and SUBSCRIPT-LIST are lists of the same length, means that instances of SUBST are composed of the types of atom listed in ELEMENT-LIST, in the    respective proportions corresponding to the numbers in SUBSCRIPT-LIST. ELEMENT-LIST is of the form (#$TheList ATOM-TYPE-1 ... ATOM-TYPE-N), where  each ATOM-TYPE-K (for K >= 1 and <= N) is an instance of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons and each NUMBER-K is a positive  integer. As an example, (#$completeAtomicComposition-List #$CarbonDioxide (#$TheList #$Carbon #$Oxygen) (#$TheList 1 2)) is true, and entails (#$atomicComposition (q.v.) #$CarbonDioxide #$Oxygen 2).")
(comment authenticationDeviceUsed "An instance of #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$deviceUsed, #$authenticationDeviceUsed is used to state that  a particular authentication device is used in a particular authentication event.  <code>(#$authenticationDeviceUsed AUTH-EVT DEV)</code> means that <code>DEV</code> is an authentication device used in #$AuthenticationEvent <code>AUTH-EVT</code>.")
(comment BiometricAuthenticationViaFn "#$BiometricAuthenticationViaFn is a function that returns a specialization of #$BiometricAuthenticationEvent when given a specialization of #$BiometricAuthenticationDevice as an argument.  (#$BiometricAuthenticationViaFn DEV) denotes the collection of events in which instances of DEV are used as authentication devices.")
(comment explosiveDeviceUsed "(#$explosiveDeviceUsed EXPL-EVT DEV) means that DEV is an explosive device used the explosion EXPL-EVT.")
(comment PhysicalDestructionOfByMeansOfFn "#$PhysicalDestructionOfByMeansOfFn is a function that returns a specialization of #$PhysicalDestructionEvent when given two specializations of #$PartiallyTangible.  (#$PhysicalDestructionOfByMeansOfFn TARGET-TYPE DESTROYER-TYPE) denotes the specialization of #$DestructionEvent in instances of which an instance of DESTROYER-TYPE plays an instrumental role in destroying an instance of TARGET-TYPE (i.e., in which an instance of DESTROYER-TYPE plays the role of #$instrument-Generic, and an instance of TARGET-TYPE plays the role #$inputsDestroyed).")
(comment AlkaliMetalElementType "A specialization of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Each instance of #$AlkaliMetalElementType is a type of #$ElementStuff and is a soft metallic element. They comprised the leftmost column of metals in the #$PeriodicTable and possess many similarities. They have high thermal and electrical conductivities. For each row of the periodic table, the #$AlkaliMetalElementType has the lowest first ionization energy, which means they are highly reactive.")
(comment GramsPerCubicCentimeter "(#$GramsPerCubicCentimeter NUM) is a function that takes a
number and returns a quantity of mass in a unit volume of a
substance, typically a solid, in grams per cubic centimeter.")
(comment ChalcogenElementType "A specialization of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Instances make up the third row from the right in the #$PeriodicTable. Moving down the row, there is a change from nonmetals to metallic elements. #$Oxygen is a colorless gas at room temperature, all the others are solids.")
(comment AlkalineEarthMetalElementType "A specialization of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Instances comprised of the second column of metals to the left in the #$PeriodicTable. These elements are also metallic solids, like instances of #$AlkaliMetalElementType, but this collection tends to be harder and more dense. These are less reactive than #$AlkaliMetalElementType.")
(comment GramsPerLiter "(GramsPerLiter NUM) is a function that takes a number and returns
a concentration of mass in a unit volume of a substance, typically a gas,
in grams per liter.")
(comment VulnerableBecauseOfInsecureJavaCode "A computer has this vulnerability if it has a program on it
with a #$InsecureJavaCodeFault.")
(comment Alkane "The most basic class of Hydrocarbons.  Each subcollection of #$Alkane is a type of stuff 
whose carbon atoms are bonded to four other atoms.  Subcollections include #$Methane and 
#$Propane.")
(comment Propanol-1 "C3H8O; an alcohol.  A molecule of propanol-1 contains 3 carbon 
atoms with an outer carbon atom bonded with an OH functional group.")
(disjointWith IsopropylAlcohol Propanol-1)
(comment Butane "C4H10. An alkane with 4 carbon atoms.")
(comment Pentane "C5H12. An alkane with 5 carbon atoms.")
(comment Octane "C8H18. An alkane with 8 carbon atoms.  A primary component in gasoline.")
(comment Ethane "C2H6.  An alkane whose structure is 2 carbon bonded carbon atoms surrounded
by 6 hydrogen atoms.")
(comment Ethene "C2H4.  Ethene is an unsaturated hydrocarbon.  A molecule of ethene consists of two carbon atoms double bonded with two hydrogen atoms bonded to each carbon atom.  The common name for this compound is 'ethylene")
(comment Hydrocarbon-Unsaturated "A specialization of #$Hydrocarbon.  A hydrocarbon is unsaturated just in case it 
has a carbon-carbon double bond.  In general, this makes them more reactive than a similar 
compound with only single bonds between carbon atoms.")
(comment Polyethylene "A specialization of #$Alkane.  Polyethylene is an alkane with tens of thousands of 
carbon atoms.  A solid compound that forms the basis of many familiar plastics.")
(comment Acetone "C3H6O. #$Acetone is a ketone.  It is also known as 'propanone' and it is a common household solvent.")
(comment Ketone "Ketones are organic molecules consisting of a carbonyl radical (C = O) bonded to two other carbon radicals, wherein the two available carbon bonds of the carbonyl are used to attach the carbon atoms of the other radicals. One good example is acetone (a.k.a. dimethyl ketone), in which the carbon atom of the carbonyl is attached to the carbon atoms of two methyl radicals CH3- . The resulting formula is CO(CH3)2.")
(comment Trinitrotoluene "A specialization of #$HighExplosive. Each instance of #$Trinitrotoluene is a yellow crystalline compound that is a flammable toxic derivative of #$Toluene, and is typically referred to as \"TNT\".  TNT is often  used as a high explosive, and it is a constituent of many explosives, such as #$Amatol, pentolite, tetrytol, torpex, #$Tritonal, picratol, ednatol, and #$CompositionB.  In a refined form, TNT is a very stable high explosive with a long shelf life.")
(comment HighExplosive "A specialization of #$ExplosiveMaterial (q.v.).  Each instance of #$HighExplosive is an explosive that releases its energy fast enough to sustain a detonation wave (see #$DetonationWave). High explosives decompose almost instantaneously (i.e., at rate of over 1000 meters per second), either in a manner similar to a rapid combustion or with rupture and rearrangement of the molecules that comprise the explosive.  High explosives are conventionally subdivided into two categories that are differentiated by sensitivity: primary explosives, which are extremely sensitive to shock, friction, and heat (see #$PrimaryDetonatorExplosiveType); secondary explosives, which are relatively insensitive to shock, friction, and heat (see #$SecondaryDetonatorExplosiveType). See also #$LowExplosive.  Note that high explosives detonate (see #$Detonation), whereas low explosives deflagrate (see #$Deflagration).")
(disjointWith HighExplosive LowExplosive)
(comment LowExplosive "A specialization of #$ExplosiveMaterial.  Each instace of #$LowExplosive is an explosive that releases its energy too slowly to support a detonation.   Low explosives undergo autocombustion at rates that vary from a few centimeters per second to approximately 400 meters per second. Low explosives are  normally employed as propellants and they also include smokeless powders and pyrotechnics such as flares and illumination devices. See also #$HighExplosive.  Note that high explosives detonate (see #$Detonation), whereas low explosives deflagrate (see #$Deflagration).")
(comment RDXExplosive "A specialization of #$HighExplosive.  RDX is a powerful explosive also known as \"cyclonite\" or \"hexogen\". \"RDX\" itself stands for \"Royal Demolition eXplosive\" and comes from Great Britain.  RDX is important as a military explosive which has wide uses in detonators and fuses.  Upon detonation, RDX explodes in all directions, unlike instances of #$HMXExplosive, which explode perpendicular to the force of gravity.  It is used as a constituent of other explosives, such as #$CompositionA and #$CompositionB.")
(comment HMXExplosive "A specialization of #$HighExplosive.  HMX is a powerful explosive also known as \"octogen\". \"HMX\" is itself an acronym for \"High velocity Military eXplosive\". HMX is used as an explosive charge when desensitized, as a booster charge in mixtures with TNT (octols), and as an oxidizer in solid rocket and gun propellants.  Upon detonation, HMX explodes perpendicular to the force of gravity, unlike instances of #$RDXExplosive, which explode in all directions.")
(comment CompositionB "A specialization of #$HighExplosive.   Composition B explosives contain various mixtures of #$RDXExplosive and #$Trinitrotoluene, in proportions of approximately 60% RDX and 40% TNT.")
(comment CompositionA "A specialization of #$HighExplosive.   Composition A explosives consist of a series of formulations of #$RDXExplosive and a desensitizer, such as #$Wax.")
(comment PlasticBondedExplosive "A specialization of #$HighExplosive. PBX's contain a high percentage of basic explosives such as #$RDXExplosive, #$HMXExplosive, #$HNSExplosive, or #$PETN in a mixture with a polymeric binder.")
(comment AmmoniumNitrate "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.  Ammonium Nitrate is marketed as a #$Fertilizer, and is the main ingredient in the #$HighExplosive, #$ANFO.")
(comment VulnerableToJavascriptFileCommandExecution "A computer has this vulnerability if it has a program with a #$JavascriptFileExecutionFault on it.")
(comment tNTEquivalent-Type "(#$tNTEquivalent-Type EXPL-DEV-TYPE INT) means that a single instance of EXPL-DEV-TYPE has the same explosive force as the amount of #$Trinitrotoluene (\"TNT\") specified by INT.  For example, (#$tNTEquivalent-Type #$AerialBomb-MK84 (#$Pound-UnitOfForce 1000)) means that each instance of #$AerialBomb-MK84 has explosive equivalence to 1000 pounds of TNT.")
(comment mainConstituentAmount-Type "(#$mainConstituentAmount-Type OBJ-TYPE1 OBJ-TYPE2 AMOUNT) means that instances of OBJ-TYPE1 have amount AMOUNT of OBJ-TYPE2 as a main constituent.")
(comment ObjectWithAmountOfMainConstituentFn "A function that returns a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible when given a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible and an instance of #$ScalarInterval as arguments. For example, (#$ObjectWithAmountOfMainConstituentFn  #$Trinitrotoluene (#$Pound-UnitOfForce 1)) denotes the collection of explosive devices whose main explosive constituent is one pound of TNT.")
(comment AlkaneMolecule "The collection of molecules of alkanes; a subcollection of #$HydrocarbonMolecule.")
(comment HydrocarbonMolecule-Unsaturated " A specialization of #$HydrocarbonMolecule.  A hydrocarbon molecule is unsaturated 
just in case it has a carbon-carbon double bond.")
(comment PolyethyleneMolecule "A specialization of #$AlkaneMolecule.  It is an alkane molecule with a chain of tens
of thousands of carbon atoms.")
(comment ShellProgram "A specialization of #$CommandInterpreter. Each instance of this collection is a command interpreter that takes textual commands from a user or program and executes them.")
(comment chemicalFormulaString "(#$chemicalFormulaString CPD STING) means that STRING is the standard chemical formula abbreviation for CPD. The generation code handles subscripts and superscripts, so in these assertions, numbers and signs indicating electronic charge should be put in parentheses. Examples: H(-) and ASO4(3-).")
(comment lifeOf "'(#$lifeOf LIVING BLO)' means that LIVING is the life, considered as a single event of type #$Living, of BLO.")
(comment goodChoiceAmongSentences "A binary predicate intended for posing multiple-choice questions to Cyc.  (#$goodChoiceAmongSentences CHOICE (#$TheList SENTENCE-1 SENTENCE-2 ... SENTENCE-n)) means that, among the sentences SENTENCE-k for 1 <= k <= n, CHOICE would be a good choice.  Note that the #$arg1Format is #$SetTheFormat, so there may be more than one good choice.")
(comment (Milli Mole-ChemicalCountUnit) "The unit of measure of one milli-mole")
(comment (PerFn (Milli Mole-ChemicalCountUnit) Liter) "The unit of measure of one milli-mole per liter")
(comment Mole-ChemicalCountUnit "A #$UnitOfMeasure used to measure amounts of matter in terms of the number of atoms present.  One mole of a given substance is a portion of the substance consisting of as many atoms as the number of atoms in twelve grams of carbon-12.  This number, known as #$AvogadrosNumber, is equal to 6.0221421 x 10^23.
<p>
Molar concentration, or molarity (typically abbreviated as 'M'), is stated in terms of (#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter), or moles per liter.")
(comment molarityOfSubstanceInSample "A ternary predicate designed to express the concentration of a substance in moles per unit of volume considered to be in a sample of something -- typically a solution.  If the substance is an ionic compound, such as hydrogen chloride, it may be totally disassociated in the solution and so is not a chemical ``species'' in the solution at all.  The molar concentration is stated in terms of (#$molarityOfSubstanceInSample SAMPLE CHEMICAL ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) NUM)) which is moles per liter of CHEMICAL in SAMPLE.
Use #$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample for concentration of a chemical object type (e.g. #$ChlorideIon or #$O2Molecule) instead of a chemical substance (e.g. #$Sucrose).  #$molarityOfSubstanceInSample is not used for specifying the concentration of ions.")
(comment HydroniumIon "The collection of ions that consist of hydrogen protons attached to molecules of H2O forming H3O.  The molar concentration of  hydronium ions in an aqueous solution can be expressed in terms of pH; the lower the pH the higher the concentration.")
(comment AqueousSolution "A specialization of #$Solution.  Each instance of #$AqueousSolution is a solution in which the dissolving medium (see #$solvent) is an instance of #$Water.")
(comment pHOfSample "pH is used to express the molar concentration of hydronium ions in an aqueous  solution.  (#$pHOfSample SAMPLE NUM) holds, if and only if NUM = - (#$Log-10Fn H-NUM)  where H-NUM is the number of hydronium ions expressed in moles.")
(comment interArgResultGenl-Asserted "#$interArgResultGenl-Asserted is a quaternary instance of #$MetaRelation.  It is used to state that a certain complex and partly #$genls -based relationship holds between a given argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned when the function is applied to the argument.                                                                                       (#$interArgResultGenl-Asserted FUNC N INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL                                                                                             DEPENDENT-ARG-COL) means that, when a term that is asserted to be                                                                                    a specialization of INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL appears as the Nth argument to FUNC,                                                                              then (regardless of what the other arguments to FUNC might be) the value                                                                             (if any) of that function for those arguments is a specialization of                                                                                      DEPENDENT-ARG-COL. That is: if INST is asserted to be a specialization of                                                                                 INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL and (FUNC ARG(1) ... ARG(N-1) INST ...) = VALUE, then                                                                            VALUE is a specialization of DEPENDENT-ARG-COL.  Note that, unlike                                                                                        #$interArgResultGenl assertions, #$interArgResultGenl-Asserted assertions                                                                              will not support assertions of the form (#$genls VALUE DEPENDENT-ARG-COL)                                                                                unless it is asserted in the Cyc KB that (#$genls COL INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL).")
(comment AreaUniformlyDistantFromFn "A function that returns an instance of #$SpatialThing when given instances of #$SpatialThing and #$Distance as arguments. (#$AreaUniformlyDistantFromFn SP-THING DIST) denotes a spatial thing which is comprised of sub-regions that each are #$Distance DIST away from some sub-region of the surface (see #$ConvexHullFn) of #$SpatialThing SP-THING at a certain time.  For example, (#$AreaUniformlyDistantFromFn #$WashingtonMonumentInWashingtonDC (#$Meter 1)) denotes the location that surrounds the Washington Monument at a distance of one meter.")
(comment areaUniformlyDistantFromSurface "(#$areaUniformlyDistantFromSurface SP-THING1 DIST SP-THING2) means that #$SpatialThing SP-THING2 is comprised of su-regions, each of which is #$Distance DIST away from some sub-region of the surface (see #$ConvexHullFn) of #$SpatialThing SP-THING1.")
(disjointWith AlkaliMetalElementType MetalloidElementType)
(disjointWith AlkalineEarthMetalElementType MetalloidElementType)
(comment MetalloidElementType "A specialization of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Each instance of #$MetalloidElementType is a collection of #$ElementStuff whose instances exhibit some characteristics of instances #$NonMetallicElementType and some characteristics of instances of #$MetallicElementType.")
(comment NonMetallicElementType "A specialization of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Instances are collections of #$ElementStuff that appear on the right side of the #$PeriodicTable (except for hydrogen).  At room temperature the elements can be solids, liquids, or gases.  These elements exhibit a number of different physical and chemical characteristics from metals.")
(comment MetallicElementType "A specialization of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Each instance of #$MetallicElementType is a specialization of #$UnalloyedMetal.  Most of #$ElementStuff is an instance of this collection.")
(disjointWith NonMetallicElementType MetallicElementType)
(disjointWith AerialBomb Bomb-ProximityTriggered)
(comment Bomb-ProximityTriggered "A specialization of #$Bomb and #$ProximityTriggeredWeapon .  Each instance of #$Bomb-ProximityTriggered is a bomb designed to explode when triggered by movement in its environment.  Important specializatins include #$Mine-Weapon and #$CarBomb-BoobyTrap.")
(disjointWith CarBomb-VehicleDelivered CarBomb-BoobyTrap)
(disjointWith ConventionalWeapon CarBomb-BoobyTrap)
(comment maximumDurationOfType "'(#$maximumDurationOfType TYPE MAX)' means that things of type TYPE typically do not endure longer than MAX.")
(comment averageDurationForType-Mean "'(#$averageDurationForType-Mean TYPE DURATION)' means that the mean average duration of things of type TYPE is DURATION.")
(comment HouseFly "A medium-sized fly often found in houses, and prevalent in most areas of the world.  The most familiar fly.")
(comment HouseMouse "A common mouse, prevalent throughout the world, and often found in or near human habitations.  The most familiar mouse.")
(comment GoldenHamster "The most familiar type of hamster, native to Asia Minor, which is used as a pet and a laboratory animal.")
(comment Budgerigar "A parakeet originating in Australia, usually with green, yellow, and black plumage.")
(comment Cockatoo "A type of parrot originally from the Australasian area which is frequently kept as a pet.")
(comment CommonBoxTortoise "A box tortoise found chiefly in eastern North America.  Box turtles have a hinged shell that allows them to withdraw their bodies entirely and close the shell tight to protect against predators.")
(comment chemicalEquation-Unbalanced "(#$chemicalEquation-Unbalanced REACTION-TYPE REACTANT-LIST PRODUCT-LIST) means that in #$ChemicalReactions of type REACTION-TYPE instances of the substances included in REACTANT-LIST react to form instances of the substances included in PRODUCT-LIST. This predicate expresses the unbalanced chemical equation corresponding to a given type of chemical reaction. By the meaning of this predicate one should not assume that the equilibrium of REACTION-TYPE lies entirely to the right of the equation.")
(comment ChemicalReactionTypeByEquationFormula "An instance of this collection is a specialization of
#$ChemicalReaction identified by a corresponding equation formula: that is,
for every instance R-TYPE of #$ChemicalReactionTypeByEquationFormula, there
are lists of, resp., reactants and products LIST1 and LIST2 such that
(#$chemicalEquation-Unbalanced R-TYPE LIST1 LIST2) is true.")
(comment chemicalEquation-Balanced "A quintary predicate used to describe the balanced chemical equation for a given type of #$ChemicalReaction. In a gaf of the form (#$chemicalEquation-Balanced R-TYPE LIST1 LIST2 LIST3  LIST4), LIST1 and LIST3 are lists of chemical substances, and LIST2 and LIST4 are list of numbers each of which is the balancing coefficient of the corresponding element in, respectively, LIST1 and LIST3. Thus, (#$chemicalEquation-Balanced R-TYPE (#$TheList COMPOUND-1 COMPOUND-2) (#$TheList 2 3) (#$TheList PRODUCT-1 PRODUCT-2) (#$TheList 1 4)) means that in the type of chemical reaction denoted by R-TYPE 2 units of COMPOUND-1 and 3 units of COMPOUND-2 result in 1 unit of PRODUCT-1 and 4 units of PRODUCT-2. Just as in the case of #$chemicalEquation-Balanced, one should not assume that the equilibrium of REACTION-TYPE lies entirely to the right of the equation.")
(comment MethaneCombustion-Reaction "A type of #$ChemicalReaction in which methane combines with
oxygen to produce water and carbon dioxide.")
(comment elementTypesInReaction-List "(#$elementTypesInReaction-List REACTION-TYPE LIST) means that
LIST is a complete list of the types of elements involved in a chemical
reaction of type REACTION-TYPE.")
(comment matrixOfReactionType "A binary predicate that relates a specialization of #$ChemicalReaction to a (#$ListOfTypeFn #$List).  (#$matrixOfReactionType REACTION-TYPE MATRIX) means that MATRIX (represented as a list of lists, where each of the lists corresponds to a row in MATRIX) is the matrix associated with a chemical reaction of type REACTION-TYPE.")
(comment ChromiumOxidation "The type of #$ChemicalReaction in which #$Chromium combines with #$Oxygen to form #$ChromiumIII-Oxide : Cr + O_2 ==> Cr_2O_3. Check the #$coefficientOfSubstanceInReaction-Interim to see how we derive the balancing coeeficients (cf. #$chemicalEquation-Balanced).")
(comment ChromiumIII-Oxide "Chromium (III) oxide, aka chromic oxide: Cr_2O_3.")
(disjointWith CovalentBond IonicBond)
(comment IonicBond "The collection of all configurations in which two ions are bonded by electrical attraction due to the opposite charge on each.")
(disjointWith CovalentBond MetallicBond)
(comment MetallicBond "The collection of all configurations in which metallic atoms are bound to each other due to their being adjacent within a larger metal to which both provide electrons.")
(disjointWith IonicBond MetallicBond)
(comment IonicBonding "The collection of all instances of #$ChemicalBonding that establish an #$IonicBond between atoms.")
(comment ionizationEnergy "(#$ionizationEnergy ELEMENT N KJ_PER_MOLE) means that the ionization energy for removing the Nth electron from an atom of ELEMENT is the specified amount (normally expressed in kiloJoules per mole).")
(comment NonpolarCovalentBond "The collection of all covalent bonds [#$CovalentBond] in which both atoms have the same attraction to the shared electrons.  This occurs when the atoms have the same electronegativities [#$electronegativity].")
(comment PolarCovalentBond "The collection of all covalent bonds [#$CovalentBond] in which one atom has more attraction to the shared electrons, resulting in different charges on the different molecular components.  This occurs when the atoms have different electronegativities [#$electronegativity].")
(disjointWith PolarCovalentBond NonpolarCovalentBond)
(comment DipoleMoment "A specialization of #$PhysicalQuantity.  Each instance of #$DipoleMoment is a dipole moment (positive or negative) possessed by a particular instance of #$Dipole.  Instances of #$DipoleMoment may be either a fixed interval, such as the dipole of one hydrogen iodide molecule, or a range; see ScalarInterval. Also see UnitOfDipoleMoment for the units used by Cyc to measure dipoles.")
(comment UnitOfDipoleMoment "The collection of functions which return elements of #$DipoleMoment.  E.g., (#$Debye 4.8 denotes a dipole moment of 4.8 debyes.")
(comment dipoleMomentOf "(#$dipoleMomentOf DIPOLE MOMENT) means that the dipole moment of a dipole, DIPOLE, is MOMENT.")
(comment Debye "A unit of #$DipoleMoment equal to 3.34E-30 coloumb-meters, used for specifying te dipole moment of instances of #$ChemicalObject.")
(comment bondLengthBetweenAtoms "(#$bondLengthBetweenAtoms ATOM1 ATOM2 DISTANCE) means that a chemical bond [#$ChemicalBond] exists between the two specified atoms and that the distance between their centers is DISTANCE.")
(comment bondLength "(#$bondLength BOND DISTANCE) means that the distance between the centers of the two atoms forming a chemical bond, BOND, is DISTANCE.")
(comment bondLengthOfBinaryMolecule "(#$bondLength MOLECULE DISTANCE) means that the distance between the centers of the two atoms a molecule, MOLECULE, is DISTANCE.")
(comment Question-IBT "The collection of all instances of #$InformationRequest-IBT which take the form of a question.  This collection includes yes/no questions, questions that ask for bindings (e.g. beginning with 'Who', 'What' or 'When') and questions that ask for descriptions or explanations (e.g. 'How' or 'Why').")
(comment dead-BLO "'(#$dead-BLO BLO)' means that BLO is no longer living but did live once.")
(comment alive-BLO "'(#$alive-BLO BLO)' means that BLO is living.")
(comment UserIDSpoofingFault "A specialization of #$ExternalAccessToAccountsFault-User. A program has this fault if it allows a remote user to imitate another (authenticated) user's credentials.")
(comment Islamist "A specialization of #$Person.  Each instance of #$Islamist is a person who subscribes to #$IslamicSocietyIdeology.")
(comment TerroristGroup-Islamist "A specialization of #$TerroristGroup.  Each instance of #$TerroristGroup-Islamist is a terrorist group that is motivated largely by Islamism, the Islamic revivalist movement characterized by the attempt to implement Islamic values in all spheres of life (see #$IslamicSocietyIdeology).")
(comment NDActorFn "A binary evaluatable function. (#$NDActorFn SENTENCE SLOT) denotes the actor playing the role in SENTENCE corresponding to SLOT.
It is assumed that the operator of SENTENCE is an instance of #$NonDavidsonianPredicate. SENTENCE should also be closed.")
(comment NDEventFn "A unary evaluatable function. (#$NDEventFn SENTENCE) denotes the event type corresponding to action sentence SENTENCE.
It is assumed that the operator of SENTENCE is an instance of #$NonDavidsonianPredicate. Furthermore, SENTENCE should also be closed.")
(comment HostAndUsernameSpoofingFault "A specialization of #$UserIDSpoofingFault and #$SpoofableProxyFault, this is a fault which pertains to filesharing programs, whereby remote users can gain access to the system by crafting a fake header containing a valid hostname and username.")
(comment Nonelectrolyte "The collection of all chunks of substances that although soluble in (#$LiquidFn #$Water) do not decompose into ions when dissolved.  An instance of #$Nonelectrolyte may be a mixture of chemical compounds.  Instances of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies that are specializations of this collection cannot be also instances of #$IonicCompoundType.")
(comment StrongElectrolyte "The collection of all polyatomic chemical stuffs that decompose into ions when disolved in water.  An instance of #$StrongElectrolyte may be a mixture of chemical compounds.  It need not be an instance of any instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.")
(comment EMailRelayFault "A specialization of #$SpoofableMailServerFault. A program has this fault if it is susceptible to being used as an entirely 'open relay' for spam or other unsolicited email (i.e. if it will forward any message to any destination, without checking for spoofed headers, etc.).")
(comment HTTP-RequestMethod "Protocols for issuing HTTP requests, one of 
#$HTTP-GET-RequestMethod or #$HTTP-POST-RequestMethod")
(comment HTTP-GET-RequestMethod "The HTTP GET request protocol. See also #$HTTP-POST-RequestMethod")
(comment HTTP-POST-RequestMethod "The HTTP POST request protocol. See also #$HTTP-GET-RequestMethod")
(comment httpdRequestMethodForSKS "(#$httpdRequestMethodForSKS WEB-PAGE REQUEST-METHOD) states that
the web service WEB-PAGE uses REQUEST-METHOD as its HTTP request method.")
(comment httpdRequestURLForSKS "(#$httpdRequestURLForSKS WEB-SOURCE URL-STRING) states that 
the web service WEB-SOURCE has URL-STRING as the 'url' portion of an http url.
For example, in the url http://us.imdb.com/Name?Coen,+Joel the 'Name' portion.")
(comment RandomIntegerFn "(#$RandomIntegerFn INTEGER) returns a random #$NonNegativeInteger between 0 and INTEGER minus one, inclusive.")
(comment UMLStateMachine "#$UMLStateMachine is the collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage state machines. Cyc can use them to design, model and implement its own behavior.")
(comment UMLElement "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Element objects.")
(comment UMLModelElement "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage ModelElement objects.")
(comment SituationPredicate "#$SituationPredicate is a collection of predicates that are used to make assertions about situations (dynamic or static occurrences, e.g. \"walking\", or \"containing something\") without reifying the situation.  The representation of situations in terms of #$SituationPredicates is an alternative to the Davidsonian-style representation of situations in which the \"participants\" in a situation are related to the reified individual situation (an instance of #$Situation) in which they participate (for representational approach, see #$Role).  The arguments to an instance of #$SituationPredicate will be the participants in the situation, and the actual occurrence is not explicitly mentioned.  For situations which are dynamic (involving a change in the world, even if only temporary), see the more specific #$EventPredicate, or in the case where there is an agentive force acting intentionally, #$ActionPredicate.")
(comment UMLNamespace "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Namespace objects.")
(comment UMLFeature "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Feature objects.")
(comment UMLBehavioralFeature "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage BehavioralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLOperation "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Operation objects.")
(comment UMLParameter "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Parameter objects.")
(comment UMLProcedure "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Procedure objects.")
(comment DecomposableSituationPredicate "A specialization of #$SituationPredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$DecomposableSituationPredicate is such that a task specification formed with it may decompose into further situations, in contrast to task specifications formed with instances of #$SimpleSituationPredicate (q.v.).  Note that whether a predicate is an instance of #$SimpleSituationPredicate or #$DecomposableSituationPredicate depends on context.")
(comment UMLExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Expression objects.")
(comment UMLArgListsExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage ArgListsExpression objects.")
(comment SimpleSituationPredicate "A specialization of #$SituationPredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$SimpleSituationPredicate is such that a task specification formed with it does not decompose into further situations, in contrast to specifications formed with instances of #$DecomposableSituationPredicate (q.v.).  Note that whether a predicate is an instance of #$SimpleSituationPredicate or #$DecomposableSituationPredicate depends on context.")
(comment UMLBooleanExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage BooleanExpression objects.")
(comment UMLMappingExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage MappingExpression objects.")
(comment DecomposableSitPredFn "#$DecomposableSitPredFn is a unary reifiable function that returns an instance of #$DecomposableSituationPredicate when given an instance of #$SituationPredicate as an argument.  Thus (#$DecomposableSitPredFn PRED) denotes a relation that is identical in every way to PRED, save that it is a candidate for decomposition in a planning context, whereas PRED is not such a candidate.")
(comment UMLProcedureExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage ProcedureExpression objects.")
(comment UMLTimeExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage TimeExpression objects.")
(comment UMLTypeExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage TypeExpression objects.")
(comment UMLSignal "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Signal objects.")
(comment UMLPin "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Pin objects.")
(comment UMLInputPin "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage InputPin objects.")
(comment UMLOutputPin "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage OutputPin objects.")
(comment UMLEvent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Event objects.")
(comment UMLCallEvent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage CallEvent objects.")
(comment UMLChangeEvent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage ChangeEvent objects.")
(comment UMLCompletionEvent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage CompletionEvent objects.")
(comment UMLSignalEvent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage SignalEvent objects.")
(comment UMLTimeEvent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage TimeEvent objects.")
(comment UMLStateVertex "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage StateVertex objects.")
(comment UMLState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage State objects.")
(comment UMLPseudoState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects.")
(comment UMLCompositeState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage CompositeState objects.")
(comment PolyCompoundChemicalType "The collection of all instances of #$ChemicalSubstanceType whose instances contain molecules of different instances of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.")
(comment Protocol "A spec of #$Instructions. A #$Protocol specifies how one or more agents should behave, in order to be considered to form part of, or to be participating in, some larger system.
<p>
Examples: there are certain rules about how a surrender should be negotiated in war. There are rules about a way a person should behave at a rave, or in the presence of the Queen of England.
There are rules about how one should behave in order to participate in a committee.
<p>
There are also rules that specify how a device should behave electrically in order to be considered a node on a 10 base-T Ethernet network, and there are rules about what types of messages to send to interact with a SOAP-based service on a network.
<p>
Protocol is currently partitioned into #$HumanProtocol and #$MachineProtocol. Specializations of #$HumanProtocol include #$DiplomaticProtocol and #$SocialProtocol.
<p>
Instances of #$MachineProtocol include #$EthernetProtocol, #$IDEProtocol, #$SOAPProtocol.
<p>
Since the #$Protocol subcollections are called XProtocol (for some X), individual #$Protocols should be called Y-TheProtocol.")
(comment MachineProtocol "A specialization of #$Protocol and #$Specification. The collection of all protocols that specify how machines should behave in order to participate in some larger system or to be considered to be of a particular type. To be considered a #$Protocol, this interaction of system with system must involve communication, as opposed to direct action as gears perform on each other in a gearbox.
<p>
Examples: There are rules that specify how a device should behave electrically in order to be considered a node on a 10 base-T Ethernet network, and there are rules about what types of messages to send to interact with a SOAP-based service on a network.")
(disjointWith MachineProtocol HumanProtocol)
(comment HumanProtocol "The collection of protocols that are meant to be followed by humans.
<p>
A specialization of #$Protocol, and disjoint with #$MachineProtocol.
<p>
Almost certainly less precise than a #$MachineProtocol.")
(comment BusProtocol "The collection of #$MachineProtocols used on a #$CommunicationBus.")
(comment StreamBasedProtocol "The specialization of #$MachineProtocol whose instances transmit information in a continuous stream. Disjoint with #$PacketBasedProtocol.")
(comment PacketBasedProtocol "The specialization of #$MachineProtocol whose instances transmit information in packets. This means that the information transmitted via such a protocol is sent in discrete chunks, rather than as a continuous stream. Disjoint with #$ConnectionBasedProtocol.")
(disjointWith StreamBasedProtocol PacketBasedProtocol)
(comment DatabaseProtocol "The collection of #$Protocols for communicating with relational databases.")
(comment StructuredKnowledgeSourceProtocol "The collection of #$MachineProtocols for interacting with #$StructuredKnowledgeSources.")
(comment ProtocolParticipant "A person or a machine employing or filling a role in a #$Protocol.")
(comment SignalTransportMediumType "The collection of types of media by which a signal can be carried. eg wire, fiber optic cable, space (for electromagnetic waves, sound, etc).")
(comment SKSConnectionSpecification "A specialization of #$CommunicationWithServiceSpecifications. Each instance of #$SKSConnectionSpecification is a #$Specification that contains all of the information needed to communicate with an external #$StructuredKnowledgeSource.")
(comment ODBCConnectionSpecification "The collection of #$CommunicationWithServiceSpecifications suitable for communicating with Open Data Base Connectivity data sources.")
(comment protocolTransportMediumIs "A predicate asserting that a #$CommunicationWithServiceSpecification is via a #$ProtocolTransportMediumType.")
(comment connectionProtocolIs "A #$BinaryPredicate asserting that a #$CommunicationWithServiceSpecification uses a #$MachineProtocol.")
(comment TCPServiceSpecification "A specialization of #$CommunicationWithServiceSpecification, instances of which are carried over a TCP/IP network (ie a network using the same underlying protocols as the internet).")
(comment UMLFinalState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage FinalState objects.")
(comment UMLStubState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage StubState objects.")
(comment UMLSubmachineState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage SubmachineState objects.")
(comment UMLSynchState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage SynchState objects.")
(comment UMLGuard "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Guard objects.")
(comment UMLSimpleState "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage SimpleState objects.")
(comment UMLTransition "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Transition objects.")
(comment umlNamespaceLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlNamespaceLink links an instance #$UMLModelElement with an instance of #$UMLNamespace.")
(comment UMLFeature-Instance "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLFeature objects which appear in each instance of the owning #$UMLClassifier.")
(comment UMLFeature-Classifier "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLFeature objects which appear once for all instances of the owning #$UMLClassifier.")
(comment UMLClassifier "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Classifier objects.")
(comment umlOwner "The #$BinaryPredicate umlOwner links an instance #$UMLFeature with an instance of #$UMLClassifier.")
(comment UMLBehavioralFeature-IsQuery "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLBehavioralFeature objects which have isQuery true.")
(comment UMLBehavioralFeature-IsNotQuery "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLBehavioralFeature objects which have isQuery false.")
(comment umlParameterLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlParameter links an instance #$UMLBehavioralFeature or an instance of #$UMLEvent with an instance of #$UMLParameter which is a parameter for the first argument.")
(comment UMLOperation-Sequential "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which operate sequentially.")
(comment UMLOperation-Guarded "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which operate in a concurrent environment and are guarded.")
(comment UMLOperation-Concurrent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which operate concurrently.")
(comment UMLOperation-IsRoot "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which must not inherit a declaration of the same operation.")
(comment UMLOperation-IsNotRoot "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which may inherit a declaration of the same operation.")
(comment UMLOperation-IsLeaf "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects whose operation definition must not be overriden by descendant class declaration of the same operation.")
(comment UMLOperation-IsNotLeaf "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects whose operation definition may be overriden by descendant class declaration of the same operation.")
(comment UMLOperation-IsAbstract "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which are abstract and cannot be instantiated.")
(comment UMLOperation-IsNotAbstract "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage #$UMLOperation objects which are concrete and can be instantiated.")
(comment umlSpecification "The #$BinaryPredicate umlSpecification provides a #$CharacterString specification for the #$UMLOperation.")
(comment umlDefaultParameter "The #$BinaryPredicate umlDefaultParameter provides a default #$UMLExpression for the #$UMLParameter.")
(comment UMLParameter-InDirection "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Parameter objects which are input parameters.")
(comment UMLParameter-InOutDirection "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Parameter objects which are are both input and output parameters.")
(comment UMLParameter-OutDirection "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Parameter objects which are are output parameters.")
(comment UMLParameter-ReturnDirection "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Parameter objects which are are return parameters.")
(comment umlBehavioralFeatureLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlBehavioralFeature links an instance #$UMLParameter with the instance of #$UMLBehavioralFeature for which it is a parameter.")
(comment umlType "The #$BinaryPredicate umlType links an instance of #$UMLPin or an instance of #$UMLParameter with the instance of #$UMLClassifier that is its type.")
(comment UMLInteger "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Integer type objects.")
(comment UMLLong "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Long type objects.")
(comment UMLBoolean "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Boolean type objects.")
(comment UMLCharacter "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Character type objects.")
(comment UMLString "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage String type objects.")
(comment UMLFloat "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Float type objects.")
(comment UMLDouble "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Double type objects.")
(comment UMLPrimitiveBoolean "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage primitive boolean type objects.")
(comment UMLPrimitiveChar "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage primitive char type objects.")
(comment UMLPrimitiveDouble "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage primitive double type objects.")
(comment UMLPrimitiveFloat "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage primitive float type objects.")
(comment UMLPrimitiveInt "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage primitive int type objects.")
(comment UMLPrimitiveLong "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage primitive long type objects.")
(comment PyrotechnicMaterial "A specialization of #$LowExplosive.  Each instace of #$PyrotechnicMaterial is a low explosive that deflagrates with a certain luminous intensity (candlepower), burning rate, color, color value, and efficiency of light production.  #$PyrotechnicMaterial is the main ingredient in colorful fireworks.")
(comment Firework-PyrotechnicDevice "A specialization of #$PyrotechnicDevice.  Each instance of #$Firework-PyrotechnicDevice is a pyrotechnic device that is used to create an instance of #$Firework-Display (q.v.).")
(comment umlMultiplicity "The #$BinaryPredicate umlMultiplicity links an instance of #$UMLPin with the number of values used by the UML Pin")
(comment UMLPin-Ordered "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Pin objects whose values are ordered.")
(comment UMLPin-Unordered "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Pin objects whose values are unordered.")
(comment umlProcedureLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlProcedureLink links an instance #$UMLInputPin or an instance of #$UMLOutputPin with an instance of #$UMLProcedure which is the procedure that owns the pin.")
(comment umlLanguage "The #$BinaryPredicate umlLanguage links an instance #$UMLExpression with an instance of #$CharacterString which names the programming language in which the body of the expression is written.")
(comment umlBody "The #$BinaryPredicate umlBody links an instance #$UMLExpression with an instance of #$Thing which consists of programming language source statements which implement the expression.  The argument is typically a #$CharacterString or #$CycLExpression.")
(comment umlOperationLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlOperationLink links an instance #$UMLCallEvent with an instance of #$UMLOperation which is the operation that is called.")
(comment umlChangeExpression "The #$BinaryPredicate umlChangeExpression links an instance #$UMLChangeEvent with an instance of #$UMLBooleanExpression which is the boolean expression that when true, triggers the change event.")
(comment umlStateLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlStateLink links an instance #$UMLCompletionEvent with an instance of #$UMLState which is the state whose completion created the completion event.")
(comment umlSignalLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlSignalLink links an instance #$UMLSignalEvent with an instance of #$UMLSignal which is the signal that created the signal event.")
(comment umlWhen "The #$BinaryPredicate umlWhen links an instance #$UMLTimeEvent with an instance of #$UMLTimeExpression which is the time expression that when becomming true, created the time event.")
(comment umlContainer "The #$BinaryPredicate umlContainer links an instance #$UMLStateVertex with an instance of #$UMLCompositeState which is the composite state which contains the state vertex.")
(comment umlIncoming "The #$BinaryPredicate umlIncoming links an instance  #$UMLStateVertex with instances of #$UMLTransitions which are the transitions coming into the state vertex.")
(comment umlOutgoing "The #$BinaryPredicate umlOutgoing links an instance  #$UMLStateVertex with instances of #$UMLTransitions which are the transitions going out of the state vertex.")
(comment umlEntry "The #$BinaryPredicate umlEntry links an instance of #$UMLState with an instance of #$UMLProcedure which is the procedure to perform when entering the state.")
(comment umlExit "The #$BinaryPredicate umlExit links an instance of #$UMLState with an instance of #$UMLProcedure which is the procedure to perform when exiting the state.")
(comment umlDeferableEvent "The #$BinaryPredicate umlDeferableEvent links an instance of #$UMLState with an instance of #$UMLEvent which is a deferable event in the state.")
(comment AcidType-Arrhenius "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of
#$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$AcidType-Arrhenius is a type of
substance instances of which, when dissolved in water, increase the
concentration of #$HydroniumIons in those samples.")
(comment BaseType-Arrhenius "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$BaseType-Arrhenius is a type of substance instances of which, when dissolved in water, increase the concentration of #$HydroxideIons in those samples.")
(disjointWith AcidType-Arrhenius BaseType-Arrhenius)
(comment AcidType-Bronsted-Lowry "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of
#$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$AcidType-Bronsted-Lowry is a
type of substance, instances of which can donate a proton to another
substance in a chemical reaction.")
(comment AcidType-Lewis "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$AcidType-Lewis is a type of substance whose instances are electron-pair acceptors.")
(comment BaseType-Lewis "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$BaseType-Lewis is a type of substance whose instances are electron-pair donors.")
(comment BaseType-Bronsted-Lowry "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of
#$ChemicalSubstanceType.  Each instance of #$BaseType-Bronsted-Lowry is a
type of substance whose instances are proton acceptors.")
(comment HydrogenChloride "The collection of samples of hydrogen chloride.  Hydrogen chloride (HCl) is a strong acid.  It is highly soluble in water.  Its aqueous solution is hydrochloric acid.  In its concentrated form, hydrochloric acid is roughly 37% HCl by mass and is 12 moles per liter in HCl.")
(comment SodiumHydroxide "The collection of all samples of Sodium hydroxide (NaOH).  Instances of #$SodiumHydroxide are bases and ionic compounds.  When dissolved in water they release sodium ions(+) and hydroxide ions(-).")
(comment BasicSolution-Liquid "A specialization of #$ChemicallyBasicSubstance. Each instance of #$BasicSolution-Liquid is a basic substance that is in solution.  If SAMPLE is an instance of #$BasicSolution-Liquid, then, for any NUM  if NUM is greater than 7, (#$pHOfSample SAMPLE NUM) holds.")
(comment NeutralSolution-Liquid "The subcollection of #$LiquidSolution whose instances all have a pH of exactly 7 at 25 degrees C.")
(comment umlDoActivity "The #$BinaryPredicate umlDoActivity links an instance of #$UMLState with an instance of #$UMLProcedure which is the activity to be performed while the state is active.")
(comment umlInternalTransition "The #$BinaryPredicate umlInternalTransition links an instance of #$UMLState with an instance of #$UMLTransition which is a transition whose both source and target states are the state instance.")
(comment umlStateMachineLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlStateMachine either links an instance of #$UMLState with an instance of #$UMLStateMachine which is the state machine containing the state, or links an instance of #$UMLTransition with an instance of #$UMLStateMachine which is the state machine containing the transition.")
(comment UMLCompositeState-IsConcurrent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage CompositeState objects which have concurrent regions.")
(comment UMLCompositeState-IsNotConcurrent "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage CompositeState objects which do have have concurrent regions.")
(comment umlSubVertex "The #$BinaryPredicate umlSubVertex links an instance of #$UMLCompositeState with an instance of #$UMLStateVertex which is a state vertex contained within the composite state.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-Choice "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are execution choice points.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-DeepHistory "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are deep history entry points into a nested state hierarchy.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-Fork "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are execution fork points.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-Initial "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are initial execution points.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-Join "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are execution join points.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-Junction "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are execution junction points.")
(comment UMLPseudoState-ShallowHistory "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage PseudoState objects which are shallow history entry points into a nested state hierarchy.")
(comment umlReferenceState "The #$BinaryPredicate umlReferenceState links an instance of #$UMLStubState with an instance of #$UMLState which is the state to which this stub state refers.")
(comment umlBound "The #$BinaryPredicate umlReferenceState links an instance of #$UMLSynchState with an instance of #$Integer which is the bound of the synch state.")
(comment umlExpressionLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlExpressionLink links an instance #$UMLGuard with an instance of #$UMLBooleanExpression which is the guard expression for some #$UMLTransition.")
(comment umlTransitionLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlTransitionLink either links an instance #$UMLGuard with an instance of #$UMLTransition for which it is the guard expression, or links an instance of #$UMLStateMachine with its transitions.")
(comment CalciumMonoxide "A specialization of #$CalciumMonoxide and #$InorganicCompound.  Instances of
#$CalciumMonoxide are composed of one atom of #$Calcium and one atom
of #$Oxygen.")
(comment SodiumAzide "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Instances of #$SodiumAzide are composed of one atom of #$Sodium and three atoms of #$Nitrogen.")
(comment insolubleIn "(#$insolubleIn THING LIQUID) means that THING is not soluble in LIQUID.  In events of #$ImmersingInLiquid in which some solid made of THING #$entersFluid LIQUID, THING will be an #$unchangedActors.")
(comment BariumCarbonate "A specialization #$OrganicCompound.  Instances of
#$BariumCarbonate are composed of one atom of #$Barium, one atom of
#$Carbon and three atoms of #$Oxygen.")
(comment LeadNitrate "Pb(NO3)2, a specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Instances are
composed of one atom of #$Lead and two molecules of nitrogen trioxide (NO3).")
(comment PotassiumIodide "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Instances are composed of one atom of #$Potassium and one atom of #$Iodine (KI).")
(comment PotassiumNitrate "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Instances of
#$PotassiumNitrate are composed of one atom of #$Potassium, one atom
of #$Nitrogen, and three atoms of #$Oxygen.")
(comment LeadIodide "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Instances of
#$LeadIodide are composed of one atom of #$Lead and two atoms of
#$Iodine.")
(comment IronSulfate "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Instances of
#$IronSulfate are composed of one atom of #$Iron, one atom of
#$Sulfur, and four atoms of #$Oxygen.")
(comment umlContext "The #$BinaryPredicate umlContext links an instance #$UMLStateMachine with an #$UMLClassifier which is the context object for the state machine.")
(comment umlTop "The #$BinaryPredicate umlTop links an instance #$UMLStateMachine with its top #$UMLState which usually is a #$UMLCompositeState.")
(comment umlSubmachineStateLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlStateMachineLink links an instance #$UMLStateMachine with an instance of #$UMLSubmachineState which is its submachine state.")
(comment umlGuardLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlGuardLink links an instance #$UMLTransition with an instance of #$UMLGuard which is its guard expression.")
(comment umlEffect "The #$BinaryPredicate umlEffect links an instance #$UMLTransition with an instance of #$UMLOperation which is its effect.")
(comment umlTrigger "The #$BinaryPredicate umlTrigger links an instance #$UMLTransition with an instance of #$UMLEvent which is its triggering event.")
(comment umlSource "The #$BinaryPredicate umlSource links an instance #$UMLTransition with an instance of #$UMLStateVertex which is the source of the transition.")
(comment httpdRequestQueryTemplateForSKS "(#$httpdRequestQueryTemplateForSKS WEB-PAGE PHYSICAL-SCHEMA EXPRESSION) states that
the web service WEB-PAGE uses EXPRESSION as the query template involving the values 
for physical fields from PHYSICAL-SCHEMA.")
(comment httpdRequestSubLParsingProgramForSKS "(#$httpdRequestSubLParsingProgramForSKS WEB-PAGE SUBL) states that
the web service WEB-PAGE can be parsed into a result set by applying
the SubL program SUBL to the result of tokenizing the resulting XML 
stream provided by connecting to the service.  This differs from a parsing program declared using #$httpdRequestUnboundOnlySubLParsingProgramForSKS in that the latter is expected to return only values for unbound logical fields in the query sentence, while the former returns values for all logical fields.")
(comment umlTarget "The #$BinaryPredicate umlSource links an instance #$UMLTransition with an instance of #$UMLStateVertex which is the target of the transition.")
(comment LithiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation. Instances of
#$LithiumIon are composed of a single #$Lithium atom that has lost an
electron and now has a +1 electric charge.")
(comment SodiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of
#$SodiumIon  are  composed of a single #$Sodium atom that has lost an
electron and now has a +1 charge.")
(comment PotassiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of
#$PotassiumIon are composed of a single #$Potassium atom that has lost
an electron and now has a +1 charge.")
(comment CesiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of #$CesiumIon are composed of a single #$Cesium atoms that has lost an electron and now has a +1 charge.")
(comment SilverIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of #$SilverIon are composed of a single #$Silver atom that has lost an electron and now has a +1 charge.")
(comment MagnesiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of #$MagnesiumIon are composed of a single #$Magnesium atom that has lost two electrons and now has a +2 charge.")
(comment CalciumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of #$CalciumIon
are composed of a single #$Calcium atom that has lost two electrons and now has a
+2 charge.")
(comment StrontiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of #$StrontiumIon are composed of a single #$Strontium atom that has lost two electrons and now has a +2 charge.")
(comment BariumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of #$BariumIon are composed of a single #$Barium atom that has lost two electrons and now has a +2 charge.")
(comment ZincIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of
#$ZincIon are composed of a single #$Zinc atom that has lost two electrons
and now has a +2 charge.")
(comment CadmiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation.  Instances of
#$CadmiumIon are composed of a single (#$AtomFn #$Cadmium) that has lost two electrons
and now has a +2 charge.")
(comment AluminumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation. Instances of
#$AluminumIon are composed of a single (#$AtomFn #$Aluminum) that has
lost three electrons and now has a +3 charge.")
(comment VariableChargeCation "A specialization of #$Cation.  Each instance of #$VariableChargeCation is a cation of an element that may form differently charged ions.  For example, #$Iron may form either a 2+ or 3+ charged cation.  The positive charge is given by a roman numeral in paraentheses following the name of the metal: iron(II) ion and iron(III) ion.")
(comment PolyatomicCation "A specialization of #$Cation.  Instances of #$PolyatomicCation are composed of two or more atoms that have lost one or more electrons.")
(comment CuprousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$CuprousIon are single atoms of #$Copper that have lost an electron.")
(comment CobaltousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$CobaltousIon are single atoms of #$Cobalt with an electrical charge of
+2, which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment CupricIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$CupricIon are single atoms of #$Copper with an #$ElectronicCharge of +2,
which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment FerrousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$FerrousIon are single atoms of #$Iron  with an #$ElectronicCharge of +2,
which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment ManganousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$ManganousIon are single atoms of #$Manganese with an #$ElectronicCharge of
+2, which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment MercurousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$MercurousIon are single atoms of two #$Mercury atoms with an
#$ElectronicCharge of +2, which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment MercuricIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$MercuricIon are single #$Mercury atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of
+2, which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment NickelousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$NickelousIon are single #$Nickel atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of
+2, which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment PlumbousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$PlumbousIon are single #$Lead atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of +2,
which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment StannousIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$StannousIon are single #$Tin atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of +2,
which means it has lost 2 electrons.")
(comment ChromicIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
$#ChromicIon are single #$Chromium atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of
+3, which means it has lost 3 electrons.")
(comment FerricIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$FerricIon are single #$Iron atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of +3,
which means it has lost 3 electrons.")
(comment BromideIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$BromideIon are single #$Bromine atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of
-1, which means it has gained an electron.")
(comment IodideIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$IodideIon are single #$Iodine atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of -1,
which means it has gained an electron.")
(comment CyanideIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of #$CyanideIon are composed of #$Carbon and #$Nitrogen atoms and have an #$ElectronicCharge of -1.")
(comment PeroxideIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$PeroxideIon are composed of two #$Oxygen atoms and have an
ElectronicCharge of -2.")
(comment SulfideIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$SulfideIon are single #$Sulfur atoms with an ElectronicCharge of -2,
which means the atom has gained 2 electrons.")
(comment NitrideIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$NitrideIon are single #$Nitrogen atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of
-3, which means it has gains three electrons.")
(comment ChlorateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$ChlorateIon are composed of one #$Chlorine and three #$Oxygen atoms
with an #$ElectronicCharge of -1.")
(comment PerchlorateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of #$PerchlorateIon are composed of one #$Chlorine and four #$Oxygen atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of -1.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn NitrateIon) "The collection of samples of (NO3-); The nitrate ion.")
(comment NitrateIon "The collection of NO3- ions and a specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$NitrateIon are composed of one #$Nitrogen and three #$Oxygen atoms
with an #$ElectronicCharge of -1. Cf. also (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$NitrateIon).")
(comment PermanganateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$PermanganateIon are composed of one #$Manganese and four #$Oxygen
atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of -1.")
(comment ChromateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$ChromateIon are composed of one #$Chromium and four #$Oxygen atoms
with an ElectronicCharge of -2.")
(comment DichromateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Instances of
#$DichromateIon are composed of two #$Chromium and seven #$Oxygen
atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of -2.")
(comment HydroxideIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion, corresponding to OH-.  Instances of #$HydroxideIon are polyatomic anions consisting of one #$Hydrogen atom and one #$Oxygen atom with an #$ElectronicCharge of -1.")
(comment ExploitableAcrossAccountSystem "A specialization of #$ProgramFault. Each instance of this collection is a fault which exposes the computer running the faulty software to exploitation from across a local #$AccountSystem. This kind of fault often afflicts #$ServerPrograms - for instance if an #$SQLServerProgram has a #$SQLQueryModificationFault, it will be exploitable from any of the client machines which access that SQL server. See also #$ExploitableFromSystemFn.")
(comment FissionableMaterial "A specialization of #$NuclearFuel.  Each instance of #$FissionableMaterial is a portion of material that can undergo nuclear fission after capturing a neutron with an energy above some threshold value. See also the important specialization of this collection, #$FissileMaterial.")
(comment FissileMaterial "A specialization of #$FissionableMaterial.  Each instance of #$FissileMaterial is a portion of material that can undergo nuclear fission after capturing a neutron of any energy.  They are to be contrasted with \"fissionable isotopes\" (see #$FissionableMaterial), which fission only after the capture of neutrons with energies above some threshold value.  Many heavy isotopes are fissionable, but many fewer of them are also fissile, and almost all of these are isotopes of uranium or plutonium.  All fissile materials and some fissionable materials are usable in weapons.  It is the odd-numbered isotopes of uranium and plutonium that are fissile: U-233, U-235 and Pu-239, Pu-241. U-235 and Pu-239 are the most common and are the best weapon materials.")
(comment hasAcidStrengthGreaterThan "(#$hasAcidStrengthGreaterThan ACID1 ACID2) holds just in case ACID1 is a stronger acid than ACID2.  For instance, (#$hasAcidStrengthGreaterThan #$HydrogenChloride  #$AceticAcid) holds because hydrogen chloride is a stronger acid than acetic acid.")
(comment hasBaseStrengthGreaterThan "(#$hasBaseStrengthGreaterThan BASE1 BASE2) holds just in case BASE1 is a stronger Base than BASE2.  For instance, (#$hasBaseStrengthGreaterThan #$SodiumHydroxide  (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$HydroxideIon)) holds because sodium hydroxide is a stronger base than the hydroxide ion.")
(comment conjugateAcid-BasePair "(#$conjugateAcid-BasePair ACIDTYPE BASETYPE) holds just in case ACIDTYPE and  BASETYPE are conjugate acid-base pairs of each other; that is, just in case they differ  only by the presence or absence of a proton.  For instance, HNO2 and NO2- are conjugate  acid-base pairs of each other.")
(comment ConjugateBaseOfAcidFn "A function that returns the conjugate base of the acid type used as argument.  For instance, (#$ConjugateBaseOfAcidFn (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$SulfateIon)) returns (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$SulfateIon).")
(comment ConjugateAcidOfBaseFn "A function that returns the conjugate acid of the base type used as argument.  For 
instance, (#$ConjugateAcidOfBaseFn #$SulfuricAcid) returns 
(#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$BisulfateIon).")
(comment NitricAcid "The collection of all samples of nitric acid.  Nitric Acid is a strong acid that can be made by dissolving sodium nitrate in sulfuric acid.  Its chemical formula is HNO3.")
(comment IonOfChargeFn "(#$IonOfChargeFn CHEM_TYPE CHARGE) denotes the collection of all ions of the chemical species CHEM_TYPE which are ionized to have a charge, CHARGE, which is a near-zero positive or negative integer")
(disjointWith Atom IonicCompound)
(disjointWith MolecularComponent IonicCompound)
(comment IonicCompound "The collection of all chemically bonded ionic compounds, which may be macroscopic crystals.  No distinct molecules appear in such a substance.  An ionic compound may dissolve in a polar liquid forming ions, not molecules.")
(disjointWith Nonelectrolyte IonicCompound)
(comment AcidType-Strong "The collection of strong acid types and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType. The instances of the instances of this collection completly transfer their hydrogen protons to water and leave no undissociated molecules in solution.")
(disjointWith AcidType-Strong BaseType-Lewis)
(disjointWith AcidType-Strong IonicCompoundType)
(comment AcidType-Weak "The collection of weak acid types and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.
The instances of the instances of this collection only partly dissociate in water.")
(disjointWith AcidType-Strong AcidType-Weak)
(disjointWith AcidType-Weak IonicCompoundType)
(comment AcidType-Negligible "A specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  The instances of this collection are neither strong nor weak acids.")
(disjointWith AcidType-Weak AcidType-Negligible)
(disjointWith AcidType-Strong AcidType-Negligible)
(comment BaseType-Strong "The collection of all strong base types.  The instances of the instances of this collection have a strong tendency to abstract hydronium ions from water to form OH- ions.")
(disjointWith BaseType-Strong AcidType-Lewis)
(comment BaseType-Weak "The collection of all weak base types.  The instances of the instances of this collection have a moderate tendency to abstract hydronium ions from water to form OH- ions.")
(disjointWith IonicCompoundType BaseType-Weak)
(disjointWith BaseType-Strong BaseType-Weak)
(comment BaseType-Negligible "The collection of compound types That are neither strong nor weak bases.")
(disjointWith BaseType-Weak BaseType-Negligible)
(disjointWith BaseType-Strong BaseType-Negligible)
(comment HydrogenFluoride "The collection of samples of hydrogen fluoride.  Its instances are moderately strong acids.  Its formula is HF.")
(comment CarbonicAcid "The collection of samples of carbonic acid.  Its instances are moderately weak acids.  Its formula is H2CO3.")
(comment HydrogenSulfide "The collection of samples of hydrogen sulfide, a weak acid.  H2S has medium acid strength.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn DihydrogenPhosphateIon) "The collection of samples of H2PO4-; the ion that is the result of a phosphoric acid atom giving up a hydrogen proton.  Instances of this collection can  act as a  moderately weak acid and moderately weak base .")
(comment DihydrogenPhosphateIon "The collection of the ion H2PO4-.  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$DihydrogenPhosphateIon).")
(comment AmmoniumIon "The collection of ammonium ions.  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$AmmoniumIon).")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn AmmoniumIon) "The collection of samples of ammonium ions.  Its instances are moderately weak acids.  Its formula is NH4+.")
(comment HydrogenCarbonateIon "The collection of HCO3- ions.  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$HydrogenCarbonateIon). ")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HydrogenCarbonateIon) "The collection of samples of HCO3-.  Instances can act as a moderately weak acid and as a moderately weak base.")
(comment HydrogenPhosphateIon "The collection of HPO4(-2) ions.  Instances can act as a weak acid and a weak base.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HydrogenPhosphateIon) "The collection of samples of HPO4(-2).  Instances can act as a weak acid and a weak base.")
(comment ChlorideIon "The collection of chloride ions (Cl-).  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$ChlorideIon). ")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn ChlorideIon) "The collection of samples of the chloride ion (Cl-).")
(comment SulfateIon "The collection of (SO4(-2)); The sulfate ion.  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn 
#$SulfateIon).")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn SulfateIon) "The collection of samples of (SO4(-2)); The sulfate ion.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn FluorideIon) "The collection of samples of (F-); The fluoride ion.")
(comment FluorideIon "The collection of (F-) ions; the fluoride ion.  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn 
#$FluorideIon). ")
(comment AcetateIon "The collection of ions that derive from the ionization of molecules of acetic acid.  Their composition is C2H3O2(-).")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn AcetateIon) "The collection of samples of the conjugate base of acetic acid.  Its formula is C2H3O2(-).")
(comment HydrogenSulfideIon "The collection of hydrogen sulfide ions (HS-).")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HydrogenSulfideIon) "The collection of samples of hydrogen sulfide ions (HS-).")
(comment CarbonateIon "The collection of carbonate (CO3(-2)) ions.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn CarbonateIon) "The collection of samples of carbonate (CO3(-2)).")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn PhosphateIon) " The collection of samples of phosphate ions (PO4(-3)).")
(comment OxideIon "The collection of O(2-) ions.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn OxideIon) "The collection of samples of O(2-).")
(comment HydrideIon "The collection of (H-) ions.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HydrideIon) "The collection of samples of (H-).")
(comment MethaneIon "The collection of (CH3-) ions.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn MethaneIon) "The collection of samples of (CH3-).  Instances are methane minus one hydrogen proton.")
(comment UMLFORT "The KB subset collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage terms.")
(comment preconditionForMethod "A binary predicate that relates instances of #$ELTemplate to instances of #$ELTemplate. #$preconditionForMethod is used to state that one formula expresses a necessary precondition for the truth of another sentence that expresses a way to do something.  (#$preconditionForMethod COND METHOD) means that METHOD expresses a suitable decomposition for an action whenever COND is true.")
(comment Client-ServerProtocol "The collection of asymmetric 2-way protocols with different roles, one designated 'client' and the other 'server'.
<p>
Note that it is not necessarily obvious which is which. An X11 server, for example, is the program that displays the screen content on a Unix-like system. Programs that are displaying their contents are the clients in this case.")
(comment protocolServerIs "A predicate relating a protocol to the collection of its servers.")
(comment protocolClientIs "A predicate relating a protocol to the collection of its servers.")
(comment protocolClientIs "A predicate relating a #$Client-ServerProtocol to the collection of its clients.")
(comment subProtocol "A predicate relating two protocols, when the first is part of the second.")
(comment umlAssociation "A predicate which relates objects from the #$UnifiedModelingLanguage.")
(comment UMLSyntaxObject "The collection of syntax objects which are elements of the #$UnifiedModelingLanguage (UML).")
(comment TestStateMachine "This is a test individual #$UMLStateMachine.")
(comment TestStateMachineNamespace "A test instance of #$UMLNamespace for use as the namespace of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-TopState "This is a test individual #$UMLCompositeState and is the top state of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-InitialState "This is a test individual #$UMLPseudoState and is the initial state of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-CounterState "This is a test individual #$UMLSimpleState and is the counter state of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-FinalState "This is a test individual #$UMLFinalState and is the final state of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-InitializeTransition "This is a test individual #$UMLTransition and is the tranistion from the initial state of the test state machine.")
(comment UMLModelElement-HasStateMachineLink "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage model elements which may have an associated state machine.")
(comment mappingMt "This predicate relates a structured knowledge source with its mapping microtheory.  (#$mappingMt SKS MT) means that MT contains the interpretations of the schemata of the SKS.")
(comment TestStateMachine-CountingTransition "This is a test individual #$UMLTransition and is the self transition out of and into the counting state of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-TerminateTransition "This is a test individual #$UMLTransition and is the loop termination tranistion out of the counting state of the test state machine.")
(comment UMLProcedure-InitializeNumberToZero "This is the #$UMLProcedure which intializes its output pin to zero.")
(comment UMLProcedure-IsList "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Procedure objects which have a single list request object and single response list object.")
(comment UMLProcedure-IsNotList "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Procedure objects which have zero, or one or more request objects and zero or one or more response list objects.")
(comment UMLModelElement-HasLanguage "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage model elements which have an associated programming language.")
(comment UMLModelElement-HasBody "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage model elements which have a body written in an associated programming language.")
(comment umlPartialAssociation "A predicate which relates objects from the #$UnifiedModelingLanguage to some other thing.")
(comment UMLProcedure-IncrementNumber "This is the #$UMLProcedure which increments the given input pin and sets the output pin to the result.")
(comment UMLProcedure-InitializeNumberToZero-OutputPin-X "This is the output pin for the procedure which initializes to zero.")
(comment UMLModelElement-HasType "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage model elements which have an associated Classifier type.")
(comment umlName "The #$BinaryPredicate umlLanguage links an instance #$UMLModelElement with an instance of #$CharacterString which is its name in those cases where the constant's name is not appropriate.")
(comment UMLProcedure-IncrementNumber-InputPin-X "This is the input pin for the procedure which increments by 1.")
(comment UMLProcedure-IncrementNumber-OutputPin-X "This is the output variable for the procedure which increments by 1.")
(comment TerrainFlight "The collection of all instances of 
(#$TransportViaFn #$AirTransportationDevice) where the flight path is relatively low to the ground, typically for the purpose of avoiding detection.")
(comment WebBrowsing "A specialization of #$ComputerActivity.  Each instance of #$WebBrowsing is an event in which an agent uses a #$WorldWideWebClientProgram to browse (or 'surf') the #$WorldWideWeb-DynamicIndexedInfoSource.")
(comment TestStateMachine-BooleanExpression1 "This is a test individual #$UMLBooleanExpression and is the guard expression for transition2 of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-Guard1 "This is a test individual #$UMLGuard and is the guard for transition2 of the test state machine.")
(comment UMLModelElement-HasTransition "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage model elements which have an associated transition.")
(comment TestStateMachine-BooleanExpression2 "This is a test individual #$UMLBooleanExpression and is the guard expression for transition3 of the test state machine.")
(comment TestStateMachine-Guard2 "This is a test individual #$UMLGuard and is the guard for transition3 of the test state machine.")
(comment actsInCapacity-RequiredFunction "a predicate which characterize the capacity in which some agent does a kind of action.  (#$actsInCapacity-RequiredFunction AGENT SLOT EVENT-TYPE) indicates that an agent does a certain type of action as a required function (rather than, say, as an optional function).")
(comment SKSISupportedStructuredKnowledgeSourceType "The collection of all specs of #$StructuredKnowledgeSource that are currently supported by SKSI.")
(comment SKSISupportedDatabaseServerProgram "The collection of all #$DatabaseServerPrograms that are currently supported by SKSI.")
(comment IonTypeByChemicalSpecies "A type-level collection. Each instance is a collection of #$Ions characterized by the fact that all of its instances are ions with exactly the same chemical formula, structure, and ionization level.  If two ions have the same chemical formula, structure, and ionization level they will be instances of the same instance of #$IonTypeByChemicalSpecies regardless of whether they are bound in an #$IonicCompound, are disolved in a fluid, or are otherwise part of a fluid or gas (e.g. liquid or gaseous NaCl).")
(comment AcidType-Diprotic "The collection of diprotic acids and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  
The instance of this collection are all types of acids whose instances contain a pair of hydrogen atoms per molecule that are susceptible to dissociation in aqueous solution.")
(comment PhenolAcid "The collection of samples of PhenolAcid; HC6H5O.")
(comment acidDissociationConstant "(#$acidDissociationConstant ACIDTYPE NUM) holds just in case NUM is the acid-dissociation constant for ACIDTYPE.  For instance, (#$acidDissociationConstant #$AceticAcid (#$TimesFn 1.8 (#$ExponentFn 10 -5))) holds according to (#$ChapterOfBook-OrdinalFn  #$ChemistryTheCentralScience-Textbook 16).  If an acid is an instance of  #$AcidType-Strong it dissociates completely in aqueous solution and hence  has no dissociation value associated with it.")
(comment AcidType-Monoprotic "The collection of monoprotic acids and a specialization of #$AcidType-Lewis.  The instances of this collection are all types of acids whose instances contain a single hydrogen atom per molecule that is susceptible to dissociation in aqueous solution.")
(comment ArsenicAcid " H3AsO4.  The collection of samples or quantities of arsenic acid.")
(comment BenzoicAcid "The collection of samples of benzoic acid; HC7H5O2.")
(comment NitrousAcid "The collection of samples of HNO2; nitrous acid")
(comment pOHOfSample "#$pOHOfSample is used to express the molar concentration of hydroxide ions 
in an aqueous solution.  (pOHOfSample SAMPLE NUM) holds just in case 
NUM = 14 - (the number, NUM2, such that  (#$pHOfSample ?SAMPLE NUM2)).")
(comment HydrogenPerchlorate "HClO4.  The collection of all samples of hydrogen perchlorate; a strong acid.")
(comment Kw-IonProductConstant " '#$Kw-IonProductConstant' denotes the real number: 1.0 x 10^-14.  This is the 
ion-product constant for #$Water at 25 degrees C.  If refers to the product of the
molarity of #$HydroniumIons and #$HydroxideIons in water at 25 degrees C. ")
(comment HydrogenChlorate "HClO3.  The collection of all samples of hydrogen chlorate; a strong acid.")
(disjointWith ChemicallyAcidicSubstance ChemicallyNeutralSubstance)
(disjointWith ChemicallyBasicSubstance ChemicallyNeutralSubstance)
(comment ChemicallyNeutralSubstance "The collection of chemically neutral substances.  Every instance of ChemicallyNeutralSubstance is a quantity of stuff with a pH of 7 .")
(comment HydrogenIodide "HI.  The collection of all samples of hydrogen iodide; a strong acid.")
(comment HydrogenBromide "HBr.  The collection of all samples of hydrogen bromide; a strong acid.")
(comment MunsellRedFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellRedHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellRedHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellYellowRedFn "This function takes as an argument one #$SubLInteger, 
from 0 to 100, and returns the collection of colors that have that value as their #$MunsellHue (See the predicate #$colorHasHue).  Each value for this function is an instance of #$MunsellYellowRedHue.  For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellYellowRedHue.")
(comment MunsellYellowFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellYellowHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellYellowHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellGreenFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellGreenHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellGreenHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellGreenYellowFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellGreenYellowHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellGreenYellowHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellBlueGreenFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellBlueGreenHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellBlueGreenHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellBlueFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellBlueHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellBlueHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellPurpleBlueFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellPurpleBlueHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellPurpleBlueHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellPurpleFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellPurpleHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellPurpleHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellRedPurpleFn "This function takes as an argument either one or two #$SubLIntegers, 
from 0 to 100, and returns either a value or an interval of values in the range 
of hues belonging to #$MunsellRedPurpleHue. For more detail on the hues denoted 
in this range, see #$MunsellRedPurpleHue and #$colorHasHue.")
(comment MunsellBrightnessFn "This function takes as an argument one or two #$SubLIntegers, from 0 to 100, and returns a #$MunsellBrightness as its value. For example, (MunsellBrightnessFn 75) is the #$MunsellBrightness of degree 75, which would be a fairly bright color. (#$MunsellBrightnessFn 25), on the other hand, would be the brightness of a dark color. Every color has a degree of brightness. Please see #$MunsellBrightness and #$colorHasBrightness for further explanation.")
(comment MunsellChromaFn "This function takes as an argument one or two #$SubLIntegers and returns a 
#$MunsellChroma as its value. For example, (MunsellChromaFn 100) is the 
#$MunsellChroma of degree 100. Since the majority of colors (though not all) 
have chromas ranging between 0 and 150, this is a relatively high chroma, and will 
most likely apply to a moderately or highly saturated color. Also note that unlike 
other functions that express values related to the Munsell system of color 
classification, there is no known upper limit on the domain of MunsellChromaFn. 
Some colors, such as flourescent colors, can have chromas of 300 or higher. Please 
see #$MunsellChroma and #$colorHasChroma for further explanation.")
(comment OdorFn "#$OdorFn takes a type of partially tangible object TYPE as its argument and returns the collection of objects that have the characteristic smell or odor of instances of TYPE")
(comment ContentManagementProgram "A specialization of #$HTMLEditor-Dynamic. Each instance of this collection is a program which facilitates easy posting of news and other rapidly-changing information on web pages accessible across some enterprise or the Internet more generally.")
(comment WeakElectrolyte "The collection of all polyatomic chemical stuffs that partially decompose into ions when disolved in water.  An instance of #$WeakElectrolyte may be a mixture of chemical compounds.  It need not be an instance of an instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.")
(disjointWith StrongElectrolyte WeakElectrolyte)
(comment AccountSystemFn "(#$AccountSystemFn SYSTEM-TYPE STRING) denotes an #$AccountSystem of type SYSTEM-TYPE which is hosted on a domain uniquely identified by STRING. For example, (#$AccountSystemFn #$NISDomain \"hello.world.net\") denotes the #$NetworkInformationSystem account system on hello.world.net.")
(disjointWith Propanol Food-ReadyToEat)
(comment AccountSystemTypeByImplementationMeans "A collection of types of #$AccountSystem, divided by their means of implementation. The two most notable instances are #$NISDomain and #$NTDomain.")
(disjointWith AcidType-Monoprotic AcidType-Polyprotic)
(comment AcidType-Polyprotic "The collection of polyprotic acids and a specialization of #$ChemicalSubstanceType. The instances of this collection are all types of acids whose instances contain at least 2  hydrogen atoms per molecule that are susceptible to dissociation in aqueous solution.")
(comment percentOfIonization "When ACID-OR-BASE is a substance that ionizes in water -- either an acid or a base, (#$percentOfIonization SAMPLE ACID-OR-BASE PERCENT) holds just in case PERCENT is the percentage of ACID-OR-BASE that is ionized in SAMPLE.")
(comment ArsenousAcid "The collection of samples of arsenous acid; H3AsO3.")
(comment BoricAcid "The collection of samples of boric acid; H3BO3.")
(comment ButanoicAcid "The collection of samples of butanoic acid; HC4H7O2.")
(comment ChloroaceticAcid "The collection of samples of chloroacetic acid; HC2H2O2Cl.")
(comment ChlorousAcid "The collection of samples of chlorous acid; HClO2.")
(comment CitricAcid "The collection of samples of citric acid; H3C6H5O7.")
(comment CyanicAcid "The collection of samples of cyanic acid; HCNO.")
(comment FormicAcid "The collection of samples of formic acid; HCHO2.")
(comment HydroazoicAcid "The collection of samples of hydroazoic acid; HN3.")
(comment HydrogenChromateIon "The collection of hydrogen chromate ions; HCrO4(-1).")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HydrogenChromateIon) "The collection of samples of hydrogen chromate ions; HCrO4(-1).")
(comment HydrogenSelenateIon "The collection of hydrogen selenate ions; HSeO4.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HydrogenSelenateIon) "The collection of samples of hydrogen selenate ions; HSeO4.")
(comment HypoBromousAcid "The collection of samples of hypobromous acid; HBrO.")
(comment HypoiodousAcid "The collection of samples of hypoiodoius acid; HIO.")
(comment IodicAcid "The collection of samples of iodic acid; HIO3.")
(comment LacticAcid "The collection of samples of lactic acid; HC3H5O3.")
(comment MalonicAcid "The collection of samples of malonic acid; H2C3H2O4.")
(comment OxalicAcid "The collection of samples of oxalic acid; H2C2O4.")
(comment ParaperiodicAcid "The collection of samples of paraperiodic acid; H5IO6.")
(comment PropanoicAcid "The collection of samples of propionic acid; HC3H5O2.")
(comment PyrophosphoricAcid "The collection of samples of pyrophosphoric acid; H4P2O7.")
(comment SelenousAcid "The collection of samples of selenous acid; H2SeO3.")
(comment SulfurousAcid "The collection of samples of sulfurous acid; H2SO3.")
(comment TartaricAcid "The collection of samples of tartaric acid; H2C4H4O6.")
(comment baseDissociationConstant "(#$baseDissociationConstant BASETYPE NUM) holds just in case NUM is the  base-dissociation constant for BASETYPE. The dissociation constant is a measure of the tendency of a base to ionize in aqueous solutions. Note that only compounds that are instances of #$BaseType-Weak have a dissociation constant: if a base is an instance of #$BaseType-Strong, it dissociates completely into ions in aqueous solution and hence has no corresponding dissociation value.")
(comment PotassiumHydroxide "The collection of samples of potassium hydroxide; a strong base.  Its' chemical
formula is KOH.")
(comment Aniline "The collection of samples of aniline; C6H5NH2.")
(comment Dimethylamine "The collection of samples of dimethylamine; (CH3)2NH.")
(comment CalciumHydroxide "The collection of samples of calcium hydroxide; Ca(OH)2.")
(comment Ethylamine "The collection of samples of ethylamine; C2H5NH2.")
(comment Hydrazine "the collection of samples of hydrazine; H2NNH2.")
(comment Trimethylamine "The collection of samples of trimethylamine; (CH3)3N.")
(comment Hydroxylamine "The collection of samples of hydroxylamine; H2NOH.")
(comment Methylamine "The collection of samples of methylamine; CH3NH2.")
(comment HypochloriteIon "The collection of ClO(-1) ions.")
(comment (ChemicalSubstanceFn HypochloriteIon) "The collection of samples of ClO(-1).")
(comment clinicalSyndrome "(clinicalSyndrome COND AILMENT) means that the #$PhysiologicalCondition COND is part of the clinical syndrome used to diagnose
the #$AilmentCondition AILMENT.")
(comment PotassiumCarbonate "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies and a specialization of #$Salt-ChemicalSubstance. Each instance of #$PotassiumCarbonate is a sample of the compound K2CO3.")
(comment AmmoniumBromide "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies and a specialization of #$Salt-ChemicalSubstance. Each instance of #$AmmoniumBromide is a sample of the compound NH4Br.")
(comment SodiumAcetate "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies and a specialization of #$Salt-ChemicalSubstance. Each instance of #$SodiumAcetate is a sample of the compound CH3COONa.")
(comment CopperSulfate "An instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$CopperSulfate is a sample of the compound CuSO4.")
(comment BariumChloride "A instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$Salt-ChemicalSubstance.  Each instance of #$BariumChloride is a sample of the compound BaCl2.")
(comment GameDesigner "A #$Person who habitually engages in #$DesigningAGame activities, whether or not it's as a #$ProfessionalFn. Also covers #$Persons who design pieces of games, such as #$MUDRealms.")
(comment AccountGroupFn "(#$AccountGroupFn ACCOUNT_SYSTEM ID) denotes the #$UserAccountGroup on ACCOUNT_SYSTEM (#$groupForAccountSystem, q.v.) which is uniquely identified by the number ID.  For example, (#$AccountGroupFn #$CycorpLANAccountSystem-Unix \"3\") denotes group no. 3 on the Cycorp UNIX LAN.")
(comment CognitiveCycFORT "The KB subset collection of terms related to Cyc's active cognitive behavior.")
(comment ChatterBotStateMachine "This is the #$UMLStateMachine used by #$Cyc to implement #$ChatterBot behavior.")
(comment CelestialObject "A specialization of #$AstronomicalObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CelestialObject is an astronomical object that is visible in the 
heavens from the surface of the Earth, without the aid of artificial 
optical instruments.  A #$CelestialObject might be either an individual 
heavenly body (see the specialization #$CelestialBody) or a group or 
system made up of such bodies (e.g. a #$Constellation).")
(comment keStrongSuggestionPreds-SpecsOfType "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
(#$keStrongSuggestionPreds-SpecsOfType COL BINPRED TYPE) means that for every known (i.e.
Cyc-reified) instance INST of COL, it is strongly suggested that there be 
some reified specialization SPEC of TYPE such that (BINPRED INST SPEC) is known in the Knowledge 
Base.")
(comment keStrongSuggestionPreds-InsOfType "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
(#$keStrongSuggestionPreds-InsOfType COL BINPRED TYPE) means that for every known (i.e.
Cyc-reified) instance INST of COL, it is strongly suggested that there be 
some reified instance INS-TYPE of TYPE such that (BINPRED INST INS-TYPE) is known in the Knowledge 
Base.")
(comment keStrongSuggestionPreds-Ins "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
(#$keStrongSuggestionPreds-Ins COL BINPRED THING) means that for every known (i.e.
Cyc-reified) instance INST of COL, it is strongly suggested that (BINPRED INST THING) is known in the Knowledge 
Base.")
(comment keStrongSuggestionPredsInverse-InsOfType "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
(#$keStrongSuggestionPredsInverse-InsOfType COL BINPRED TYPE) means that for every known (i.e.
Cyc-reified) instance INST of COL, it is strongly suggested that there be 
some reified instance INS-TYPE of TYPE such that (BINPRED INS-TYPE INST) is known in the Knowledge 
Base.")
(comment keStrongSuggestionPredsInverse-SpecsOfType "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
(#$keStrongSuggestionPredsInverse-SpecsOfType COL BINPRED TYPE) means that for every known (i.e.
Cyc-reified) instance INST of COL, it is strongly suggested that there be 
some reified specialization SPEC of TYPE such that (BINPRED SPEC INST) is known in the Knowledge 
Base.")
(comment keStrongSuggestionPredsInverse-Ins "A #$KEFacilitationPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.
(#$keStrongSuggestionPredsInverse-Ins COL BINPRED THING) means that for every known (i.e.
Cyc-reified) instance INST of COL, it is strongly suggested that (BINPRED THING INST) is known in the Knowledge 
Base.")
(comment ChatterBot "The collection of all #$SoftwareAgent that exhibit a conversational behavior via natural langage.")
(comment UMLStateMachineInterpreter "The collection of all interpreters for #$UMLStateMachines.")
(comment softwareAgentImplementedBy "A binary predicate that relates an instance of #$SoftwareAgent to the instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW which implements its behavior.")
(comment keStrongSuggestionHigherArityPreds "(#$keStrongSuggestionHigherArityPreds COL PRED) says that for any instance INS of COL, it is strongly suggested that Cyc know (#$thereExists ?ARG2 ... (#$thereExists ?ARG-N (PRED INS ?ARG2 ... ?ARG-N)) ...), where N is the #$arity of PRED.  So, for example, (#$keStrongSuggestionHigherArityPreds #$PhysicalContactSituation #$objectsInContact) says that Cyc should know (#$thereExists ?ARG2 (#$thereExists ?ARG3 (#$objectsInContact INS ?ARG2 ?ARG3))), where INS is any instance of #$PhysicalContactSituation.")
(comment UMLProcedure-Write "This is the #$UMLProcedure which writes the given string to the given stream.")
(comment UMLProcedure-ParseChat "This #$UMLProcedure parses natural language chat input.")
(comment UMLProcedure-PerformCycL "This is the #$UMLProcedure that performs the given CycL action.")
(comment UMLProcedure-ParseChat-InputPin-ChatText "This instance of #$UMLInputPin is the input variable for the procedure which parses the chat request.")
(comment keGenlsStrongSuggestionPreds-RelationAllExists "An instance of #$KEFacilitationPredicate.  (#$keGenlsStrongSuggestionPreds-RelationAllExists COL PRED) says that, for any specialization SPEC of COL, it is strongly suggested that Cyc know that there exists ?TYPE such that (#$relationAllExists PRED SPEC ?TYPE).")
(comment accountUserID "A predicate that relates computer accounts to their uniquely identifying ID numbers (as listed in the 'etc/passwd' file). (#$accountUserID ACCOUNT ID) means that the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT's user ID ('UID') is ID. See also #$groupID.")
(comment UMLProcedure-PerformCycL-InputPin-CycLRequest "This instance of #$UMLInputPin is the input variable for the procedure which performs the CycL request.")
(comment UMLProcedure-ParseChat-OutputPin-ChatCycL "This instance of #$UMLOutputPin is the output parsed CycL for the chat parsing procedure.")
(comment UMLProcedure-PerformCycL-OutputPin-Response "This instance of #$UMLOutputPin is the output variable for the procedure which performs the given CycL performative.")
(comment keGenlsStrongSuggestionPreds-RelationAllInstance "An instance of #$KEFacilitationPredicate.  (#$keGenlsStrongSuggestionPreds-RelationAllExists COL PRED) says that, for any specialization SPEC of COL, it is strongly suggested that Cyc know that there exists ?THING such that (#$relationAllInstance PRED SPEC ?THING).")
(comment groupID "A predicate that relates computer account groups to their uniquely identifying ID numbers (as listed in the 'etc/groups' file). (#$groupID GROUP ID) means that the #$UserAccountGroup GROUP's ID ('GID') is ID. See also #$accountUserID.")
(comment accountFullName "A predicate that relates computer accounts to the full names listed for them in the 'etc/passwd' file. (#$accountFullName ACCOUNT FULL-NAME) means that the #$ComputerAccount ACCOUNT's full name is FULL-NAME. For the account's 'username', see #$accountName.")
(comment FileSystemFn "(#$AccountSystemFn SYSTEM-TYPE STRING) denotes an #$AccountSystem of type SYSTEM-TYPE which is hosted on a domain uniquely identified by STRING. For example, (#$AccountSystemFn #$NISDomain \"hello.world.net\") denotes the #$NetworkInformationSystem account system on hello.world.net.")
(comment FileSystemTypeByImplementation "A collection of types of #$Filesystem, divided by their means of implementation. One widely deployed instance is #$NetworkFileSystem.")
(comment solubleIn-TypeType "(#$solubleIn-TypeType TANGIBLE-TYPE LIQUID-TYPE) means that
all instances of TANGIBLE-TYPE are soluble in every instance of
FLUID-TYPE.  We use the following operational definition, from
#$ChemistryTheCentralScience-Textbook : a substance A is soluble in another
substance B if a quantity of 0.01 moles of A would dissolve in a 1-liter
quantity of B at room temperature. For an instance version of this
predicate, see #$solubleIn.")
(comment partiallySolubleIn-TypeType "(#$partiallySolubleIn-TypeType TANGIBLE-TYPE LIQUID-TYPE)
means instances of TANGIBLE-TYPE are partially soluble in instances of
#$LIQUID-TYPE.  Partial Solublility is defined here as a substance
that dissolves to the extent of 0.1 to 10 grams per liter at room
temperature.")
(comment partiallySolubleIn "(#$partiallySolubleIn SEMISOL SOLVENT) means that SEMISOL is
only partially soluble in SOLVENT.  This means that SEMISOL will
dissolve at an extent between 0.1 to 10 grams per liter at room
temperature.")
(comment insolubleIn-TypeType "(#$insolubleIn-TypeType TANGIBLE-TYPE LIQUID-TYPE) means that all
instances of TANGIBLE-TYPE are insoluble in instances of LIQUID-TYPE, by
some quantitative measure of solubility. Different criteria can be
used. At the moment we define  `Insolubility' as a substance's dissolving to the
extent of less than 0.01 moles per liter at room temperature.")
(comment Electrolyte "The collection of all polyatomic chemical stuffs that decompose into ions when disolved in water. An instance of #$Electrolyte may be a mixture of chemical compounds. It need not be an instance of any instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.")
(disjointWith Nonelectrolyte Electrolyte)
(disjointWith Alcohol-Compound Electrolyte)
(disjointWith Liquor-Drink Electrolyte)
(comment AmphotericSubstanceType "A specification of #$ChemicalSubstanceType.  The collection of all instances of #$ChemicalSubstanceType that are instances of both #$AcidType-Lewis and #$BaseType-Lewis.")
(comment ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW "A specialization of #$ReportOnSituation-CW. Each instance of this collection is an official report of some #$ProgramFault or #$ComputerVulnerability, intended to alert IT personnel to the need to secure their computer systems.")
(comment ComputerVulnerabilityReportFn "A #$BinaryFunction which takes as arguments an instance of #$ComputerVulnerabilityDatabase (for example, the #$BugtraqFaultDatabase), and a report number which is uniquely identifying for that database, and returns the report (#$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW) which is so indexed.")
(comment faultOfSoftwareObject-ReportIndexed "(#$faultOfSoftwareObject-ReportIndexed FAULT REPORT LIST), where LIST is the list (PROGRAM-OR-SERIES-1 ... PROGRAM-OR-SERIES-N), means that, according to the computer vulnerability report REPORT, versions (see #$subSoftwareObjects) of PROGRAM-OR-SERIES-N in concert with versions of PROGRAMS-OR-SERIES-Nminus1 ... in concert with versions of PROGRAM-OR-SERIES-1 manifest FAULT.")
(comment ionicDecomposition "(#$ionicDecomposition IONIC_COMPOUND CATION N ANION M) means that instances of the #$IonicCompound, IONIC_COMPOUND, have a N cations of type CATION for every M anions of type ANION.")
(comment ComputerHardwareTypeByBrandVersion "A specialization of #$ComputerHardwareTypeByBrand and #$ProductTypeByBrandVersion. Each instance of this (second-order) collection is
a spec of #$ComputerHardwareItem which is a version of a type of brand-name computer (for example, #$AppleiMacComputer) or a version of a type of brand-name computer hardware item (for
example, #$Motorola68C000Processor).")
(comment HydrogenPlusIon "The collection of all isolated protons in a chemical milieu.  They are normally boundwithin an ionic compound [#$IonicCompound] in a solid or freely moving in a gas.  In aqueous solution they bind to water molecules to form #$HydroniumIons.")
(comment patchFixesFaultOf-ReportIndexed "(#$patchFixesFaultOf-ReportIndexed PATCH  FAULT  FAULT-REPORT LIST), where LIST is (#$TheList SOFTWARE-OBJECT ... SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N), means that PATCH fixes the fault FAULT of the version of SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N that is packaged with SOFTWARE-OBJECT N-1 that is packaged with ... SOFTWARE-OBJECT-1.  (That is, SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N (the last member of LIST) is the \"sub-program\" and SOFTWARE-OBJECT-1 the \"main program\" of which SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N is a part.  DATABASE and ID-STRING indicate the security database (and its reference no.) where the fault-information is recorded.  For example: (#$patchFixesFaultOf-ReportIndexed #$Q252795-W2K-SP3-X86-En-TheSoftwarePatch #$BufferOverflowFault-CommandExecution (#$ComputerVulnerabilityReportFn #$BugtraqFaultDatabase \"3215\") #$MSWindows2000) means that the buffer overflow fault with MS Windows 2000 reported in Bugtraq entry number 3215 will not render a computer vulnerable if patch Q252795 has been applied to the computer in question. Sometimes the \"patch\" is actually an updated version of the program being patched.")
(comment AqueousBufferSolution "A specialization of #$AqueousSolution.  Each instance of #$AqueousBufferSolution is a solution containing both a weak acid and its conjugate base (or a weak base and its conjugate acid), whose pH changes only slightly upon addition of small amounts of acid or alkali. Usually  constructed from a weak acid and a salt that has an ion that is the same as the conjugate base of the acid.")
(comment Khat "A specialization of #$DrugProduct and #$PsychoactiveAgent.  Each instance of #$Khat is a portion of harvested leaves of the plant #$CathaEdulis. #$Khat is a recreational drug that is especially popular in Ethiopia, Somalia and Yemen.")
(comment CathaEdulis "A specialization of #$Plant.  each instance of #$CathaEdulis is a plant kinown best as the source of #$Khat.  Catha Edulis is a seedles plant whose wild grtowth is limited to the Yemens and nearby Ethiopia and East Africa.  It grows best at 3000 to 6000 feet above sea level and reaches a height of 20 feet.")
(comment DesignDepartment "#$DesignDepartment is a specialization of #$Department.  Each instance of #$DesignDepartment is responsible for being the creative force behind the development of a design that will best fit a product and/or a corporate image.  They will often work closely with the #$MarketingDepartment.")
(comment AfricanOilPalm "A specialization of #$PalmTree.  Each instance of #$AfricanOilPalm is a palm tree which can produce the red fruit from which palm oil is extracted.")
(comment PalmOil "A specialization of #$EdibleOil.  Palm oil is oil pressed from the dried fruit of the #$AfricanOilPalm.")
(comment completeChemicalComposition-List "(#$completeChemicalComposition-List CHEM_SUBSTANCE_TYPE LIST_OF_GROUPS) means that molecules of CHEM_SUBSTANCE_TYPE, an instance of #$ChemicalSubstanceTypes, has a chemical formula expressed by the following list.  Elements of the list are collections of groups of a single cardinality of chemical objects of the same species [#$GroupOfCardinalityOfChemObjectTypeBySpecies], normally expressed using either #$AtomGroupingFn or #$ChemicalObjectGroupingFn.  The order of groups in the list is conventional and gives an idea of the structure of instances of the molecule type.
<p>
For example:
<p>
(#$completeChemicalComposition-List #$AceticAcid
(#$TheList
(#$AtomGroupingFn #$Carbon 1)
(#$AtomGroupingFn #$Hydrogen 3)
(#$AtomGroupingFn #$Carbon 1)
(#$AtomGroupingFn #$Oxygen 1)
(#$ChemicalObjectGroupingFn #$HydroxideRadical 1)))
<p>
means what the chemical formula for #$AceticAcid being CH3COOH expresses.")
(comment EngineeringDepartment "#$EngineeringDepartment is a specialization of #$Department.  Each instance of #$EngineeringDepartment consists of employees who are responsible for engineering-related tasks within an organization.")
(comment MarketResearchDepartment "#$MarketResearchDepartment is a specialization of #$MarketingDepartment.  Each instance of #$MarketResearchDepartment consists of employees who study and analyze consumers and the market in order to gain a competitive advantage for the organization.")
(comment keIrrelevantTerm-AttributeCleanupVocabulary "(#$keIrrelevantTerm-AttributeCleanupVocabulary TERM) should be used to hide all experimental terms that were created in the attribute cleanup.  These terms generally end in -Coll.  These new attribute terms should lose their #$keIrrelevantTerm-AttributeCleanupVocabulary status once they are ready for general use.")
(comment keIrrelevantTerm-ExperimentalVocabulary "(#$keIrrelevantTerm-ExperimentalVocabulary TERM) should be used for any term that should not be seen in an RKF-based interface because it is still an experimental term.")
(comment keIrrelevantCPOFTerm "(#$keIrrelevantTermCPOFTerm TERM) should be used for all terms created for CPOF that are generally not meant to be seen in a UIA interaction.  This includes terms created only for specific demos and deliverables such as BPVI.")
(comment keIrrelevantTerm-InternalCycTerm "(#$keIrrelevantTerm-InternalCycTerm TERM) should be used for all Cyc terms that are considered 'internal' to Cyc and not for a SME or other non-Cyclist to see in any RKF-based interface.")
(comment keIrrelevantTerm-UpperOntologyConcept "(#$keIrrelevantTerm-UpperOntologyConcept TERM) should be used for all Cyc terms that are considered too high in the ontology for a SME or other non-Cyclist to see in any RKF-based interface.")
(comment Macaw "A specialization of #$Parrot.   There are 16 different species of parrots classified as Macaws.  Macaws are generally brightly collored, and have tails that are usually as long as their bodies or longer.")
(comment ScarletMacaw "A specialization of #$Macaw.  Each instance of #$ScarletMacaw is a red, blue and yellow macaw.")
(comment ResearchAndDevelopmentDepartment "#$ResearchAndDevelopmentDepartment is a specialization of #$Department.  Each instance of #$ResearchAndDevelopmentDepartment consists of employees who are involved in some type of specialized research activity which contributes to the development of a product to be used or marketed by an organization.")
(comment WhiteFacedCapuchin "#$WhiteFacedCapuchin is a specialization of #$Capuchin. Native to Central America, they are well known throughout the world as the \"organ grinder's monkey\".")
(comment Capuchin "A specialization of #$Monkey.  The Capuchin monkey belongs to the family Cebidae. There are five different species of Capuchin monkeys.")
(comment HowlerMonkey "A specialization of #$Monkey.  The howler monkey is the loudest land animal -- males produce calls that can be heard at a distance of 3 miles.")
(comment SquirrelMonkey "A specialization of #$Monkey.  The squirrel monkey is native to Costa Rica to Panama and north and central South America.")
(comment UMLProcedure-ReadLine "This #$UMLProcedure reads a line of text from the given input stream.")
(comment UMLProcedure-ReadLine-OutputPin-TextLine "This instance of #$UMLOutputPin is the output text variable for the procedure which reads the next newline-terminated string from the given input stream.")
(comment Agouti "A specialization of #$Rodent.  Agoutis are among the largest rodents, and are native to Central and South American tropics.")
(comment Sloth "A specialization of #$Edentata.  Sloths are slow-moving tree-dwelling tropical mammals.")
(comment NonZeroNumber "A specialization of #$RealNumber, and the binary union of
#$PositiveNumber and #$NegativeNumber. It is disjoint with the singleton
set of 0.")
(comment QuadraticSolution-PositiveFn "(#$QuadraticSolution-PositiveFn a b c) returns the positive-form solution of the quadratic equation the coefficients of which are, respectively, a b and c: thus the value returned is the quotient of (-b + (SqrtFn (b^2 - 4ac))) and 2a. Because of limitations in handling of #$ComplexNumber, this function is restricted to #$RealNumber solutions, i.e. it returns a value only if (b^2 - 4ac) is greater than or equal to 0.")
(comment QuadraticSolution-NegativeFn "(#$QuadraticSolution-NegativeFn a b c) returns the negative-form
solution of the quadratic equation the coefficients of which are,
respectively, a b and c: thus the value returned is the quotient of (-b -
(SqrtFn (b^2 - 4ac))) and 2a. Because of limitations in handling of #$ComplexNumber,
this function is restricted to #$RealNumber solutions, i.e. it returns a
value only if (b^2 - 4ac) is greater than or equal to 0.")
(comment MonadicMicrotheory "A #$MonadicMicrotheory is a temporally and otherwise unqualified #$Microtheory; it is indivisible along all currently defined microtheory dimensions (see #$MtSpace and #$MtDim).  For example, #$PeopleDataMt is a #$MonadicMicrotheory, but (#$MtSpace #$PeopleDataMt) is not; nor is (#$MtSpace #$PeopleDataMt (#$MtDim #$mtTimeIndex (#$YearFn 1955))).  Note, however, that a #$MonadicMicrotheory need not be atomic: (#$MtOfBeliefSystemFn #$Buddhism) is a #$MonadicMicrotheory.  Together with #$PolyadicMicrotheory, this partitions #$Microtheory.")
(comment PolyadicMicrotheory "A #$PolyadicMicrotheory is a #$Microtheory which is deconstructable along some currently defined microtheory dimension.  For example, (#$MtSpace #$PeopleDataMt (#$MtDim #$mtTimeIndex (#$YearFn 1955))) is a #$PolyadicMicrotheory, as is (#$MtSpace #$PeopleDataMt); however, #$PeopleDataMt is not. Together with #$MonadicMicrotheory, this partitions #$Microtheory.")
(disjointWith MonadicMicrotheory PolyadicMicrotheory)
(comment doConnectRemotelyToProgramOnSystem "(#$doConnectRemotelyToProgramOnSystem AGENT PROGRAM SYSTEM) means that AGENT sets up a remote connection to the #$ServerProgram PROGRAM on the #$AccountSystem SYSTEM (by sending packets to whatever port the program is listening on). See also #$doConnectRemotelyToProgramOnComputer.")
(comment doConnectRemotelyToProgramOnComputer "(#$doConnectRemotelyToProgramOnComputer AGENT PROGRAM COMPUTER) means that AGENT sets up a remote connection to the #$ServerProgram PROGRAM on the #$Computer COMPUTER (by sending packets to whatever port the program is listening on). See also #$doConnectRemotelyToProgramOnSystem.")
(comment SKSIMappingMicrotheory "This is the collection of all microtheories that contain
part, or all of the assertions required to register the SKSI removal
modules for a knowledge source.  See also its specializations
#$SKSISchemaDescriptionMicrotheory, 
#$SKSISourceDescriptionMicrotheory, and #$SKSISchemaTranslationMicrotheory.")
(comment HTTP-URL-EncodeFn "(#$HTTP-URL-EncodeFn STRING) evaluates to a version of STRING encoded in the style used for HTTP URLs.  In particular, spaces are converted to '+' characters, and characters that are not alphanumeric ASCII characters are converted to a hexadecimal encoding sequence of the form %HH where HH is the character code of the character expressed in hexadecimal.")
(comment NationalCulture "This is the collection of #$HumanCultures which are defined as the cultures of nations.  For example, #$FrenchCulture is the #$NationalCulture of #$France.  This collection is not a specialization of #$RegionalCulture because some #$NationalCultures have influence beyond a specific region -- for instance, #$FrenchCulture has influence beyond #$France -- and some #$NationalCultures are uprooted and dispersed.  #$NationalCultures typically include a national language, cuisine, music, and other distinctive cultural practices.")
(comment NationalCultureFn "(#$NationalCultureFn NATION) is the #$NationalCulture of NATION.  This is a partial function because not all nations have a specific culture, but most do.  Some young nations may have yet to develop a distinctive culture, whereas others may include more than one national culture within their political boundaries.")
(comment cultureOfNation "'(#$cultureOfNation CULTURE NATION)' means that CULTURE is the #$NationalCulture of NATION.")
(comment occupationDominatedByType "This predicate relates occupations to the types of people it almost always employs. That is, if (isa PERS FIELD) holds, and (occupationDominatedByType FIELD COL) holds, then (#$expected-ToBe (isa PERS COL)) holds.")
(comment incubationPeriodForType "(#$incubationPeriodForType INFECTION-TYPE ORG-TYPE TIME-QUANTITY)
means that the duration of the time period between the
initial onset of an infection of type INFECTION-TYPE and the point at
which the infection becomes clinically symptomatic for an instance of ORG-TYPE is TIME-QUANTITY.  Compare with #$averageIncubationPeriod.")
(comment versionOfProductBrand "A predicate used to relate an instance of #$ProductTypeByBrandVersion to the appropriate instance of #$ProductTypeByBrand of which it is a version. (#$versionOfProductBrand VER BRND) means that VER is a version of BRND. (e.g. The collection #$DietCoke is a version of the product brand #$CocaCola).")
(comment AppleComputer "The collection of all personal computers made by #$AppleInc with the Apple brand name.")
(comment InhalationalBotulism "A form of #$Botulism that does not exist naturally.  Cases #$InhalationalBotulism (would) occur as a result of the inhalation of (#$BacterialSporeFn #$ClostridiumBotulinum-Species).")
(comment formOfCondition "(#$formOfCondition SPEC-COND GENL-COND) says that the instance of the #$PhysiologicalConditionType SPEC-COND is a form of the instance of #$PhysiologicalConditionType GEN-COND.  For example, (#$formOfCondition (#$CancerFn #$Lung) #$Cancer) says that lung cancer is a form of cancer.")
(comment FoodborneBotulism "A form of food poisoning that is potentially fatal if untreated.  It can be contracted by eating food (usually from cans) that has been contaminated by the #$BotulinumToxin.")
(comment InfantBotulism "A form of #$Botulism, common among infants, caused by ingesting botulinum spores, which then grow in the intestines and release #$BotulinumToxin.")
(comment LockingComputerDisplay "#$LockingComputerDisplay is a specialization of #$ComputerActivity.  Each instance involves either a person-iniated or an automatic computer process that locks the computer when it is not used for a period of time. A person is generally required to enter a password to unlock the computer.  The computer is generally running a screen saver while it is locked.")
(comment WoundBotulism "A form of #$Botulism, increasingly common among intraveneous drug users, caused by open-wound exposure to botulinum, which then grow and release #$BotulinumToxin.")
(comment InhalationalInfectionByBotulinum "A specialization of 
  (#$InfectionFn #$RespiratorySystem).  Instances are infections caused by inhaling spores of botulinum.")
(comment createNewConceptInCyc "a instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$createNewConceptInCyc AGT STRING) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT creates the #$Cyc term named by the #$SubLString STRING.")
(comment assertAnAssertionInCyc "a instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$assertAnAssertionInCyc AGT SENTENCE) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT asserts the #$Cyc assertion having EL form SENTENCE.")
(comment unassertAnAssertionInCyc "a instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$unassertAnAssertionInCyc AGT SENTENCE) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT unasserts the #$Cyc assertion having EL form SENTENCE.")
(comment RenamingAConstant "The collection of events in which an #$IntelligentAgent renames a Cyc constant.")
(comment renameConstantInCyc "a instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$renameConstantInCyc AGT CONSTANT STRING) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT renames the #$Cyc CONSTANT to the new name STRING.")
(comment KillingAConstant "The collection of events in which an #$IntelligentAgent kills a Cyc constant.")
(comment killConceptInCyc "a instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$killConceptInCyc AGT TERM) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT kills the #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm TERM.")
(comment internallyQueryCycKB "a instance of #$ActionPredicate.  (#$internallyQueryCycKB AGT QUERY) means that the #$IntelligentAgent AGT asks the #$Cyc QUERY.")
(comment EnteringInfoIntoDatabase "A specialization of #$ComputerActivity. Each instance of this collection is an event in which someone enters information  into some #$Database-Physical by means of some #$DatabaseProgram.")
(disjointWith EnteringInfoIntoDatabase CommunicationAct-Single)
(disjointWith EnteringInfoIntoDatabase RemovingSomething)
(disjointWith EnteringInfoIntoDatabase Conveying-Generic)
(comment greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar "(#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar IND1 IND2 PRED) means that PRED relates IND1 to some value that is greater than or equal to the value to which PRED relates IND2.")
(comment formulaArity "(#$formulaArity FORMULA INTEGER) means that the CycL formula FORMULA contains INTEGER arguments. See also #$FormulaArityFn.")
(comment MovementThroughAir "The collection of all instances of #$Movement-TranslationEvent that follow a complete trajectory through the air. [See #$TrajectoryThroughAir, #$trajectory-Complete.]")
(disjointWith Locomotion-SolidSurface MovementThroughAir)
(comment BurningACompactDisk "A collection of actions done by computers on files and a specialization of #$CopyingAComputerFile and #$InformationRecordingProcess. In each instance of #$BurningACompactDisk, a #$CDCreatingProgram transfers computer files to a compact disc.")
(comment PlayingAComputerGame "The process of playing a computer game")
(disjointWith PlayingAComputerGame BodyMovementEvent)
(disjointWith PlayingAComputerGame AthleticActivity)
(disjointWith PlayingAComputerGame (CollectionDifferenceFn PlayingABoardGame PlayingAComputerGame))
(comment FoodProductTypeByBrand "The collection of all brand name food products. Instances of #$FoodProductTypeByBrand are collections of food products with a specific brand name (e.g. Quaker Oats Oatmeal or Yoplait Yogurt).")
(comment LowCalorieDessertFood "A spec of #$DessertFood, #$LowCalorieDessertFood is the collection of dessert foods that have a few or no calories.")
(comment AccessingReferenceResources "The collection of all events in which a person accesses reference resources, such as a dictionary, thesaurus, atlas, encyclopedia, or etc.")
(comment ComputerNetworkTrafficMonitoring "A specialization of #$ProgramAction -- the collection of all events in which computer network traffic is monitored by some program designed for that purpose.")
(comment PacketSniffing "#$PacketSniffing is the collection of all events where packets, which are sent over the Internet, are examined and information is extracted from them.")
(comment DetectingNetworkIntrusion "A specialization of #$GuardingSomething, #$BecomingAwareOfAnEvent and #$ComputerActivity. Each instance of this collection is an event that involves scanning computer networks and finding signs of unauthorised intrusion. These days, this is often done with the help of an #$IntrusionDetectionProgram.")
(comment DiagnosingComputerHardwareOrSoftware "A specialization of #$Diagnosing and #$ComputerActivity. Each instance of this collection is an action of diagnosing (identifying problems which afflict) computer hardware (cf. #$ComputerHardwareItem) or software (cf. #$SoftwareObject-Individual).")
(comment durationOfType "(#$durationOfType TIME-TYPE DURATION) means that the duration for any instance of TIME-TYPE is DURATION.")
(comment periodOfTimeIntervalSeries "(periodOfTimeIntervalSeries TIME DURATION) means that the minimal duration 
from the beginning of one instance of TIME to the next instance of TIME is 
DURATION.")
(comment prodromeOfAilment "(#$prodromeOfAilment COND AILMENT) means that the #$PhysiologicalCondition COND is part of the prodrome normally associated with the #$AilmentCondition AILMENT.")
(comment AccessingPersonalInformation "The collection of all events in which a person accesses personal information, such as an address book, calendar, memos, or etc.")
(comment MappingMtFn "This unary and reifiable function takes a structured knowledge source (#$StructuredKnowledgeSource) as its sole argument and denotes a mapping microtheory (#$SKSIMappingMicrotheory). It is the function corresponding to the predicate #$mappingMt with the canonical argument order.  See #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical for details.")
(comment oxidationNumber "(#$oxidationNumber ATOM-OR-ION NUM) holds just in case NUM is the number of electrons that would be gained or lost by ATOM in oxidation or reduction reactions.")
(comment moreEasilyOxidizedThan "(#$moreEasilyOxidizedThan ELEMENT1 ELEMENT2) holds just in case
ELEMENT1 is more readily susceptible to oxidation (i.e. more susceptible to a loss
of electrons) than ELEMENT2. This ordering corresponds to the `activity series' of metallic elements.")
(comment CommunicatingByTelnet "#$Communicating that occurs through using the #$TelnetProtocol.")
(comment UserModelingConstant "A collection of Cyc terms, each instance of which is useful in modeling software users, so that Cyc can take steps to improve user experience and its own performance.")
(comment LoggingInRemotely "A specialization of #$ConnectingToAComputerNetwork. #$LoggingInRemotely is the collection of events where a person logs in to a #$ComputerAccount from a remote location.")
(comment PartitioningADisk "A specialization of #$ComputerActivity. In each instance of this collection a hard drive disk is partitioned: divided into sections that may implement different file formats (cf. #$ComputerFileTypeByFormat).")
(comment TableInWorkNumbered-PCW "The collection of all numbered (information) tables occuring in #$TextualPCWs")
(comment TableInWork-OrdinalFn "A term of the form (#$TableInChapter-OrdinalFn (#$ChapterOfBook-OrdinalFn BOOK N) M)
denotes the table in BOOK in chapter N that is numbered M.  If a chapter of BOOK has no tables
or no numbered tables the function is undefined for that chapter.")
(comment doUninstallProgram "(#$doInstallProgram AGENT PROGRAM COMPUTER) means that AGENT takes steps toward the goal of having PROGRAM uninstalled on COMPUTER's hard drive.")
(comment SodiumDihydrogenPhosphate "The collection of samples of sodium dihydrogen phosphate; its chemical formula is NaH2PO3.")
(comment BisulfateIon "The collection of (HSO4-); The bisulfate, aka hydrogen sulfate, ion.")
(comment ElementalSubstanceType "A collection of instances of #$ChemicalSubstanceType. Each
instance of #$ElementalSubstanceType is a non-molecular substance composed
exclusively of single atoms of some instance of
#$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. The instances of #$MetallicElementType are
typically of this sort: their elemental form is non-molecular. #$Oxygen, on
the other hand, is an element which occurs in molecular form, e.g. the
allotropes O2 or O3 (cf. #$Ozone).")
(comment ElementalSubstanceFn "A term of the form (#$ElementalSubstanceFn ELEMENT) denotes the elemental substance composed of chemically pure ELEMENT, where ELEMENT is an instance of #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons. Note that (#$ElementalSubstanceFn ELEMENT), when defined, is a specialization of ELEMENT. This function is only partially defined over #$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons since not every element occurs in elemental-substance, non-molecular form.")
(comment lewisAcidStrength "(#$lewisAcidStrength CHEM_OBJ_TYPE STRENGTH) means that the strength of the given type of chemical object [#$ChemicalObjectTypeByChemicalSpecies] when considered as a LewisAcid is STRENGTH.  If the type is not a Lewis Acid, STRENGTH is 0. At least initially, this predicate is used for comparing relative strengths, not for assigning absolute strengths to Lewis acids.")
(comment ElectronPairAcceptorType "The collection of all species of chemical objects [#$ChemicalObjectTypeByChemicalSpecies] which can act as electron pair acceptors, and thus are Lewis acids.")
(comment AmmoniumCarbonate "An instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$InorganicCompound. Each instance of #$AmmoniumCarbonate is a sample of the compound (NH4)2CO3.")
(comment AmmoniumSulfate "An instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$InorganicCompound. Each instance of #$AmmoniumSulfate is a sample of the compound (NH4)2SO4.")
(comment StrontiumNitrate "An instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$InorganicCompound. Each instance of #$StrontiumNitrate is a sample of the compound Sr(NO3)2.")
(comment AvogadrosNumber "The number of atoms, molecules, etc. in a gram mole of any chemical substance.")
(comment HydrogenBorateIon "An instance of #$IonTypeByChemicalSpecies and a specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Each instance of #$HydrogenBorateIon is an anion with chemical formula HBO3(2-).")
(comment DihydrogenBorateIon "An instance of #$IonTypeByChemicalSpecies and a specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Each instance of #$DihydrogenBorateIon is an anion with chemical formula H2BO3(-).")
(comment temporalBoundsIntersectInSituationType "<code>(#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> are not temporally disjoint. (see also #$subSitTypesTemporallyDisjointInSitType).
<p>
Note that <code>(#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment OxalateIon "The collection of C2O4(2-) ions.  See (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$OxalateIon). ")
(comment UMLStateMachine-Functional "#$UMLStateMachine-Functional is the collection of #$UMLStateMachines which model the behavior of a function.")
(comment 4-MethylSulfonylBenzenePropanoicAcid "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies, and a specialization of #$OrganicCompound.  Each instance of #$4-MethylSulfonylBenzenePropanoicAcid is a sample of C10H12O4S.")
(comment SulfurDioxide "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies, and a specialization of #$Oxide.  Each instance of #$SulfurDioxide is a sample of SO2.")
(comment cotemporalSubSituationTypes "<code>(#$cotemporalSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> are cotemporal.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$cotemporalSubSituationTypes SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment equilibriumConstantOfReaction "(#$equilibriumConstantOfReaction R-TYPE VALUE) means that VALUE is the equilibrium constant for R-TYPE, a specialization of #$ChemicalReaction individuated by a particular reaction equation. When reactants and products are all in solution, this constant is the result of dividing the product of the concentrations of the compounds produced in R-TYPE by the product of the concentrations of the compounds reacting in R-TYPE, where each of these concentrations is raised to a power equal to the respective coefficient in the balanced equation.")
(comment OxalicAcidDissociation-Diprotic "The type of #$ChemicalReaction in which #$OxalicAcid dissociates in
#$HydroniumIon and #$OxalateIon.")
(comment BariumSulfate "An instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$BariumSulfate is a sample of the compound BaSO4.")
(comment SilverChloride "A instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of #$SilverChloride is a sample of the compound AgCl.")
(comment ZincHydroxide "An instance of #$IonicCompoundType and a specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$ZincHydroxide is a sample of Zn(OH)2.")
(comment IonicDecompositionFn "(#$IonicDecompositionFn CAT-TYPE CN AN-TYPE AN) a function
that returns the #$IonicCompoundType composed of CN number of
CAT-TYPE, a specialization of #$Cation, and AN number of AN-TYPE, a
specialization of #$Anion.")
(comment IonicDecomposableCompoundType "A specialization of ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.  Each instance of #$IonicDecomposableCompoundType is a type of compound whose instances decomposes under certain conditions in positively and negatively charged ions.  #$IonicDecomposableCompoundType is a generalization of #$IonicCompoundType (those compounds of ions that form in three-dimensional blocks) and of compounds formed from a hydrogen ion [#$HydrogenPlusIon] and an anion, including #$AcidType-Strong. Note that strong acids can exist in molecular form (e.g., as gases) even though they dissolve into ions in aqueous solution.")
(comment overlapsAfterStartInSituationType "<code>(#$overlapsAfterStartInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then (#$overlapsStart <code>SIT1 SIT2)</code>.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$overlapsAfterStartInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment CalciumHypochlorite "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of
#$CalciumHypochlorite is a sample of the compound Ca(OCl)2.")
(comment NonPoliticallyCorrectSpeech "Certain word senses of words or phrases which are deemed to be not politically correct, i.e. may be rude and offensive. A term may be in common use, then at some point be deemed politically incorrect.")
(comment PoliticallyCorrectSpeech "Certain word senses of words or phrases which are deemed to be politically correct, i.e. not rude and non-offensive. Politically correct terms are often wordy and euphemistic, compared to the terms they replace.")
(comment PersonalDigitalAssistantProgram "The collection of all instances of #$ComputerProgram-CW that are designed primarily to interact with a user and assist him or her with organizing and performing everyday tasks.")
(comment IntelligentPersonalDigitalAssistantProgram "A specialization of #$PersonalDigitalAssistantProgram.  Instances are #$IntelligentComputerPrograms.")
(comment ElectronPairDonorType " A specialization of #$ChemicalObjectTypeByChemicalSpecies.
Each instance is a collection of #$ChemicalObjects that donate a pair
of electrons to form a bond.")
(comment programPersonalizedFor "(#$programPersonalizedFor PROGRAM USER) says that the #$ComputerProgram-CW PROGRAM has been customized so as to optimize for the needs of USER.")
(comment IndexicalReferentFn "An instance of #$EvaluatableFunction.  (#$IndexicalReferentFn TERM) evaluates to REFERENT, where (#$indexicalReferent TERM REFERENT).")
(comment myToDoList "(#$myToDoList AGENT LIST) identifies LIST as a #$ToDoList-CW (list of tasks) assigned to AGENT.")
(comment ConstructingAToDoList "A specialization of #$CreatingAnArtifact.  Each instance is an event in which someone constructs a list of action items to be followed.")
(comment ConstructingAToDoListForFn "(#$ConstructingAToDoListForFn AGENT) denotes the collection of all instances of #$ConstructingAToDoList where AGENT is responsible for the #$ToDoList that is the #$outputs (see #$myToDoList).")
(comment List-CW "The collection of all instances of #$TextualPCW that are in list form -- i.e., have the information structure of a #$List-Information.")
(comment RelationInstanceExistsFn "(#$relationInstanceExists PRED THING COLL) says that THING stands in the relation PRED to some (not necessarily known) instance of the #$Collection COLL. (#$RelationInstanceExistsFn PRED THING COLL) denotes this instance of COLL.")
(comment ToDoList-CW "The collection of all instances of #$List-CW that are lists of tasks for someone (see #$myToDoList) to perform.")
(comment partialMathematicalStructureOfCW "#$partialMathematicalStructureOfCW is used to relate an instance of #$ConceptualWork to a #$StructuredInformationSource that is the mathematical structure of that work, or of some part of that work.  c.f. #$mathematicalStructureOfCW.")
(comment mathematicalStructureOfCW "#$mathematicalStructureOfCW relates a #$StructurallySpecificWork to the unique #$StructuredInformationSource that is its mathematical structure.  This is a different notion from the information structure (c.f. #$infoStructureOfCW) of a work; a particular #$List-CW, for example, might have an info-structure that consists of the complete sequence of characters that were used in composing it.  That list's *mathematical* structure, on the other hand, is a mathematical #$Tuple (indeed, a #$List) that consists in the ordering of items referred to by the string-sequences that make up the info-structure of that list.  Though a given work might undergo change, and so have different a different mathematical structure at different times, it cannot have more than one structure at any given point.")
(comment toDoListItem "(#$toDoListItem LISTCW ITEM) says that ITEM is one of the tasks on the list LISTCW of things to do.")
(comment ComputationalLinguist "The collection of all #$ComputerScientists who specialize in the #$Linguistics branch of #$ArtificialIntelligence.")
(comment MountaineeringEvent "An event that involves climbing a mountain.")
(comment Philanthropist "The collection of all people who donate large amounts of money or other items to charities or people in need.")
(comment AcademicYear-American "The collection of #$AcademicYears as recognized by American academic institutions.")
(comment ScientificConference "The collection of all #$Conferences dealing specifically with some scientific subject.")
(comment ComputerScienceConference "The collection of all #$ScientificConference dealing specifically with computer science.")
(comment doesTypeWithFrequency "(#$doesTypeWithFrequency AGENT TYPE FREQUENCY) says that AGENT plays the role of #$doneBy in instances of TYPE with FREQUENCY.")
(comment RelationExistsInstanceFn "(#$relationExistsInstance PRED COLL THING) says that some (not necessarily known) instance INS of the #$Collection COLL stands in the relation PRED to THING. (#$RelationExistsInstanceFn PRED COLL THING) denotes INS.")
(comment softwareOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed "(#$softwareOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed SOFT COMP FAULT REP) means that the software SOFT, as it is installed and configured on COMP, manifests the FAULT reported in the #$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW REP.")
(comment softwareInstalledOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed "(#$softwareInstalledOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed SOFT COMP FAULT REP) means that the software SOFT, as it is installed and configured on COMP, manifests the FAULT reported in the #$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW REP.")
(comment programRunningOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed "(#$programRunningOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed SOFT COMP FAULT REP) means that the software SOFT, as it is installed and configured on COMP, manifests the FAULT reported in the #$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW REP.")
(comment operatingSystemOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed "(#$operatingSystemOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed OS COMP FAULT REP) means that the software SOFT, as it is installed and configured on COMP, manifests the FAULT reported in the #$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW REP.")
(comment programListeningOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed "(#$programListeningOnComputerHasFault-ReportIndexed OS COMP FAULT REP) means that the software SOFT, as it is installed and configured on COMP, manifests the FAULT reported in the #$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW REP.")
(comment ChargingABattery "The collection of all events in which a #$Battery is charged.")
(disjointWith TransportationEvent ChargingABattery)
(disjointWith BiologicalProcess ChargingABattery)
(comment RechargeableBattery "Batteries which may be recharged (cf. #$ChargingABattery).")
(comment TransportationDeviceType "An instance of #$SecondOrderCollection and a specialization of #$ProductType.  Instances are specs of #$TransportationDevice, the main function of which is to play the purpose of a vehicle, i.e., to play the role of #$vehicle in transportation events.")
(disjointWith PersonTypeByCulture TransportationDeviceType)
(disjointWith OrganismClassificationType TransportationDeviceType)
(comment PrimaryResidenceOfFn "(#$PrimaryResidenceOfFn PERSON) denotes the primary residence of the #$Person, PERSON. (See #$primaryResidence).")
(comment keTestQuestionForTerm "(#$keTestQuestionForTerm TERM FORMULA QUERY-SPEC) says that for TERM, the #$CycLFormula FORMULA is an interesting question to ask, where the relevant query parameters are determined by the #$CycLQuerySpecification QUERY-SPEC.")
(comment NthCommonQueryOfTypeFn "(#$NthCommonQueryOfTypeFn TEST INT) denotes the INTth #$CycLQuerySpecification of type TEST.")
(comment UsingInstanceFn "(UsingInstanceFn THING) denotes the collection of instances of UsingAnObject in which the instrument-Generic is THING.")
(comment protectiveEquipmentUsed "(#$protectiveEquipmentUsed ACT EQUIP) says that during ACT, EQUIP is used as protective equipment (c.f. #$ProtectiveEquipment-Human).")
(comment ThankingSomeone "The collection of instances of #$CommunicationAct-Single, in which one agent thanks another.")
(comment exploitFaultOfProgramOnSystem-ReportIndexed "(#$exploitFaultOfProgramOnSystem AGENT FAULT PROGRAM SYSTEM REP) means that AGENT exploits the FAULT of PROGRAM reported in REP when PROGRAM is running on SYSTEM.")
(comment exploitFaultOfProgramOnSystemWithInstrument-ReportIndexed "An #$ActionPredicate relating instances of #$IntelligentAgent with instances of  #$ProgramFaultAttribute, #$ComputerProgram-CW, and #$ComputationalSystem.  (#$exploitFaultOfProgramOnSystemWithInstrument-ReportIndexed AGENT FAULT PROGRAM SYSTEM THING REP) means that AGENT uses THING to exploit the FAULT of PROGRAM described in REP when PROGRAM is running on SYSTEM.")
(comment actAppreciated "(#$actAppreciated THANKS ACT) identifies ACT as the #$PurposefulAction that prompted the #$ThankingSomeone THANKS.")
(comment recipientOfThankYou "(#$recipientOfThankYou THANKS AGENT) identifies AGENT as one of the individuals thanked in the #$ThankingSomeone THANKS.")
(comment ThankingForFn "(#$ThankingForFn AGENT ACT) denotes the collection of all instances of #$ThankingSomeone in which AGENT is thanked for performing ACT.")
(comment giftOccasion "(#$giftOccasion GIFT OCCASION) says that OCCASION is the occasion for which the giving of the #$Gift GIFT is appropriate.")
(comment IPDALexicalMicrotheoryForFn "(#$IPDALexicalMicrotheoryForFn IPDAMT) denotes the instance of #$LexicalMicrotheory that contains lexical data intended for use by the intelligent personal assistant program for the agent whose #$IPDAMicrotheory is IPDAMT.")
(disjointWith ContainerArtifact Segway-ISeries)
(comment doFixFaultOfSoftwareOnComputer "(#$doFixFaultOfSoftwareOnComputer AGENT SOFTWARE COMPUTER FAULT REP) means that AGENT fixes the FAULT of SOFTWARE reported in REP that is on COMPUTER.")
(comment storagePlace "(#$storagePlace STORAGE PLACE) says that PLACE is the storage place for the #$StorageOfSomething situation STORAGE.")
(comment storagePlaceFor "(#$storagePlaceFor PLACE OBJ) says that PLACE is the storage place for OBJ. See also #$storagePlace and #$objectStored.")
(comment minimumDuration "This predicate relates an instance of #$TemporalThing to its expected minimum duration. For instance, (#$minimumDuration 2003StateOfTheUnionAddress (HoursDuration 1)). Note that the #$minimumDuration is modal, not factual: it is consistent that both (#$minimumDuration 2003StateOfTheUnionAddress (HoursDuration 1)) and (#$duration 2003StateOfTheUnionAddress (HoursDuration .5)).")
(comment SelfPoweredProjectile "The collection of all #$SelfPoweredDevices that are also #$Projectile-Devices.")
(comment PickingUpSomeone "A specialization of #$EncounteringSomething and
Translation-LocationChange. Each instance of #$PickingUpSomeone is the
event in which one agent travels to a place and picks up another agent
and takes the picked up person to another location. This is a broad
collection, intended to encompass a father picking up his son from
soccer, the bus picking up people at a bus stop, picking a friend up
at the airport, etc.")
(comment TransportingMultiplePeople "A specialization of #$TransportingPeople.  Each instance of
#$TransportingMultiplePeople is a transportation event in which more
than one person is transported somewhere.")
(comment MultiPassengerTransportationDevice "A specialization of #$TransportationDevice. Each instance of
#$MultiPassengerTransportationDevice is a vehicle designed to carry
more than one passenger, driver inclusive. Positive examples of this
collection include passenger airplanes, mini-vans, etc.")
(disjointWith MultiPassengerTransportationDevice SinglePassengerTransportationDevice)
(comment SinglePassengerTransportationDevice "A specialization of #$TransportationDevice. Each instance of #$SinglePassengerTransportationDevice is a vehicle *designed* to carry only one passenger, typically only the driver. Positive examples of this collection will include the unicycle, most bicycles, and some kinds of airplanes.")
(comment UserManual "A spec of #$InstructionalWork that contains text and/or diagrams to show how a particular #$PhysicalDevice should be used. For example, a new car will typically come with a #$UserManual that describes appropriate care, maintenance, and how-to's. To relate a #$UserManual and a #$PhysicalDevice, see #$userManualForType.")
(comment ReadingFn "(#$ReadingFn TEXT) denotes the collection of all instances of #$Reading in which TEXT is read.")
(comment userManualForType "(userManualForType MANUAL DEVICE-TYPE) relates a particular instance of #$UserManual to the type of #$PhysicalDevice it describes.")
(comment associatedComputerVulnerabilityReport "(#$associatedComputerVulnerabilityReport REPORT1 REPORT2) means that one #$ComputerVulnerabilityReport-CW is associated with another in the sense of containing at least some of the same information; often it is the same report, but from a different #$ComputerVulnerabilityDatabase.")
(comment GroceryShopping "The collection of grocery shopping activities.  An activity is an instance of this collection only if it includes the purchase of at least one shopping bag full of groceries. Trips to the supermarket in which less than that amount of groceries are purchased fail to qualify as bona fide instances of #$GroceryShopping.")
(comment ReplicatedChromosomeCondensation "Occuring before the M phase of the cell cycle, the cells chromosomes are condensed and are therefore 'less likely to get entangled and therefore physically easier to spearate during mitosis.' (Essential Cell Biology, pg. 550)")
(comment InboundLegTypeOfRoundTripTypeFn "A function used to reason about the inbound legs of round trips of a given type (usually of a type that occurs regularly.  (#$InboundLegTypeOfRoundTripTypeFn ROUNDTRIP) denotes the collection of all instances of #$Translation-LocationChange that are the #$inboundLegOfRoundTrip for any instance of ROUNDTRIP, a specialization of #$Translation-RoundTrip.")
(comment SnowTire "The collection of all tires made for use in the snow. For snow tires made specifically for automobiles, see the spec #$AutomobileSnowTire.")
(comment CarryingGroceries "The collection of events in which someone transports bags, boxes, heaps of groceries from the store to the place of storage or consumption. A subevent type of #$GroceryShopping. In #$MainstreamAmericanCultureMt, this is almost always a type of (#$TransportViaFn #$RoadVehicle). ")
(comment ExteriorLightingDevice "The collection of all #$LightingDevices mounted on the exterior of an object. For example, many buildings have lights that illuminate the approach.  Vehicles often have exterior lights to help the driver see, and to help others see the vehicle.")
(comment PickingSomeoneUpFn "(#$PickingSomeoneUpFn AGENT) denotes the subcollection of #$PickingUpSomeone in which AGENT is a #$recipientOfService.")
(comment hasReadWork "A predicate used to identify the instances of #$TextualPCW that an individual has read. (#$hasReadWork AGT WORK) says that AGT has read WORK.")
(comment bugtraqDocumentationString "#$bugtraqDocumentationString relates instances of #$CycSecureFORT to snippets of text from the Bugtraq fault reports.")
(comment hasLiabilityInsuranceToDriveVehicle "(hasLiabilityInsuranceToDriveVehicle AGENT VEHICLE) means that AGENT is covered by liability insurance when driving VEHICLE.")
(comment usedToTravelTerrainType "This relates a #$LandTransportationDevice to the kind of terrain it's used for.  This can affect how the vehicle needs to be equipped, how long it lasts, etc.")
(comment ResonanceStructureMoleculeType "A specialization of #$ExistingObjectType.   Each instance of
#$ResonanceStructureMoleculeType is a collection of #$ChemicalObjects
whose known arrangement of #$Atoms are not completely described by a
single Lewis structure.  Examples include (#$MoleculeFn Ozone) and
#$NitrateIon.")
(comment TheUninstallScriptFn "(#$TheInstallationScriptFn PROGRAM) denotes an archetypical code script that, when executed on a machine, installs PROGRAM on that machine.")
(comment TheUninstallScriptFn "(#$TheUninstallScriptFn PROGRAM) denotes an archetypical code script that, when executed on a machine, uninstalls PROGRAM on that machine.")
(comment typeAcceptsSubatomicType "(#$typeAcceptsSubatomicType CHEMTYPE PARTICLETYPE) means
that instances of CHEMTYPE are capable of accepting instances of
PARTICLETYPE when reacting chemically with substances that donate (cf. #$typeDonatesSubatomicType)
instances of PARTICLETYPE.")
(comment TheAssertionSentence "#$TheAssertionSentence denotes the EL sentence of the `current assertion' being referred to in the context of a particular tool that is manipulating #$CycLAssertions.")
(comment Starfish "A specialization of #$Echinodermata.  Starfish are the most familiar of the echinoderms.")
(comment SeaUrchin "A specialization of #$Echinodermata.  Sea urchins are common echinoderms.")
(comment SeaCucumber "A specialization of #$Echinodermata.  Sea cucumbers are common echinoderms.")
(comment SandDollar "A specialization of #$Echinodermata.  Sand dollars are common echinoderms.")
(comment ColumbusDay-US "The instance of #$Holiday honoring #$ChristopherColumbus-HistoricPerson.")
(disjointWith VeteransDay-US ReligiousHoliday)
(comment VeteransDay-US "The instance of #$Holiday honoring American veterans.")
(disjointWith FlagDay-US ReligiousHoliday)
(comment FlagDay-US "The instance of #$Holiday honoring the American flag.")
(comment RoshHashana "The Jewish New Year, celebrated on the first (and sometimes second) day of the month Tishri.")
(comment actsAsBaseInReactionType "(#$actsAsBaseInReactionType REACTION-TYPE BASE) holds just in case in instances of REACTION-TYPE some instance of an  instance of #$AcidType-Bronsted-Lowry acts as a proton donor giving up protons  [#$HydrogenPlusIon] to instances of BASE.")
(comment actsAsAcidInReactionType "(#$actsAsAcidInReactionType REACTION-TYPE ACID) holds just in case in instances of REACTION-TYPE instances of ACID donate at least some of their protons [#$HydrogenPlusIon] to bases involved in the reaction.  
See #$actsAsBaseInReactionType.")
(comment typeDonatesSubatomicType "(#$typeDonatesSubatomicType CHEMTYPE PARTICLETYPE) means that instances of CHEMTYPE are capable of donating instances of PARTICLETYPE when reacting chemically with substances that accept instances of PARTICLETYPE.  See #$typeAcceptsSubatomicType.")
(comment pcwRead "(#$pcwRead READING TEXT) relates a particular instance of                                  #$Reading to the #$TextualPCW that is being read.")
(comment doExploitFaultOfProgramOnSystem-ReportIndexed "(#$doExploitFaultOfProgramOnSystem-ReportIndexed AGENT FAULT PROGRAM SYSTEM REPORT) means that AGENT exploits the FAULT of PROGRAM (reported in REPORT) when PROGRAM is running on SYSTEM.  Cf. the simple action predicate, #$exploitFaultOfProgramOnSystem-ReportIndexed.")
(comment (EmergencyProcedureForFn (SkinContactWithFn ChemicallyAcidicSubstance)) "The collection of all activities in which a person is given emergency treatment after his skin has been exposed to an acidic substance.")
(comment RinsingWithFn "(#$RinsingWithFn ?FLUID) returns the collection of events in which an object is rinsed with the fluid ?FLUID.")
(disjointWith Drink (SolutionFn (LiquidFn Water) Ammonia))
(comment SolutionFn "(#$SolutionFn SOLVENT SOLUTE) returns the collection of solutions in which SOLVENT is the #$solvent and SOLUTE is ae #$solute. If the strength of the solution is known, use #$SolutionOfStrengthFn. Note that #$SolutionFn does not assume that SOLUTE is the only #$solute present. Therefore, (#$SolutionFn (#$LiquidFn #$Water) #$HydrogenChloride) and (#$SolutionFn (#$LiquidFn #$Water) #$SodiumAcetate) need not be disjoint, in that particular solutions that contain both would be instances of both.")
(comment FlyingViaAirTransportationDevice "The collection of instances of (#$TransportViaFn #$AirTransportationDevice) in which the device is actually #$Flying.  Many #$AirTransportationDevices are capable of land or water travel, and often engage in transporting events while in such land or water travel modes (e.g., a plane driving passengers to their gate from the runway, after landing.)")
(comment solubilityValueOfSubstanceType "(#$solubilityValueOfSubstanceType
SOLUTE SOLVENT VALUE) means that VALUE is the solubility constant of the
substance type SOLUTE in the substance type SOLVENT at 25C temperature. The
solubility constant of a substance type SOLUTE is the maximum amount of an
instance of SOLUTE that can be dissolved in a given quantity of the type
SOLVENT. This constant can be expressed in a variety of inter-reducible
ways: here we use mass fraction of solute, a percentage measure: ie.,
VALUE = m2 / (m1 + m2), where m1 is the mass of solvent and m2 is the mass
of solute in a saturated solution. An example:
(#$solubilityValueOfSubstanceType #$Salt-NaCl (#$LiquidFn Water) 0.265), meaning that a saturated solution of NaCl at 25C will contain 26.5 grams per 100 grams of water.")
(comment chemicalLaw "This predicate can be used to characterize those rules and #$CycLAssertions that represent in Cyc fundamental laws of chemistry. Additionally, it is a #$CycInferenceHeuristicPredicate : rules corresponding to fundamental laws of chemistry are to be preferred in inference [this feature should be considered 'experimental' at this time].")
(comment emergencyProcedureForType "(#$emergencyProcedureForType SIT-TYPE PROCEDURE-TYPE) indicates that, when an instance of SIT-TYPE occurs, the emergency procedure PROCEDURE-TYPE should be carried out. See also #$EmergencyProcedureForFn. ")
(comment RestaurantSeatingArea "A specialization of  #$HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject.  Instances of this collection are the individual seating areas (e.g. tables and booths) which are typically found in resturants.")
(comment Booth-SeatingArea "A specialization of  #$HumanlyOccupiedSpatialObject.  Instances of this collection are seating areas (e.g. as found in a restaurant) which comprised of a table and bench seating (#$BenchWithBack).")
(comment Booth-OccupationConstruct "A specialization of #$HumanOccupationConstruct. Notable specializationa of this collection include #$TollBooth and #$TelephoneBooth.")
(comment DisplayStand-PieceOfFurniture "A piece of furniture used to facilitate the display of artistic or decorative objects.")
(comment Sideline "A specialization of #$LineOfDemarcationForSportsArea.  Instances of this collection mark the lateral boundaries to some #$AthleticField.")
(disjointWith Point-Empirical LinearObject)
(disjointWith GeoculturalRegion LineOfDemarcationForSportsArea)
(comment rider "(rider TRANS AGT) means that AGT, a member of #$Agent-Generic, is a rider in the #$TransportationEvent TRANS.")
(comment GettingAHitInBaseball "Getting a hit in baseball.")
(comment Synagogue-LocalCongregation "The collection of local synagogues.")
(comment TreasuryDepartment "#$TreasuryDepartment is a specialization of #$ExecutiveGovernmentalOrganization.  Instances of #$TreasuryDepartment are organizations which typically have responsibility over activities like the minting of currency and management of public revenues on behalf of some government.")
(disjointWith SheetOfSomeStuff Block)
(disjointWith Beam Block)
(comment Block "A specialization of both #$SolidTangibleThing and #$RectangularParallelepiped.  This is the collection of solid tangible block-shaped object.  Specializations include #$Brick, #$IceCube, and #$ToyBlock.")
(disjointWith SheetShapedObject Block)
(comment Church-LocalCongregation "The collection of local church congregations.")
(comment MakingSomethingMoreCompact "The collection of events in which something becomes more compact, e.g. by #$Folding or #$Compressing.")
(disjointWith GovernmentAgency LegislativeOrganization)
(comment PassingALaw "The collection of all events in which a governing body with legislative powers passes some #$Law.")
(comment Senate "#$Senate is a specialization of #$LegalGovernmentOrganization and #$LegislativeOrganization.  Each instance of #$Senate is an organization which has the main responsibility (in part or in whole) of conducting legislative activities on behalf of some government.")
(disjointWith ChangeOfStatusEvent Collapsing-Generic)
(disjointWith Holiday Collapsing-Generic)
(disjointWith AnnualClimateCycle Collapsing-Generic)
(comment Collapsing-Generic "A specialization of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent and #$PhysicalEvent. In events of this sort, an entity collapses either because of a loss of structural integrity or, in the case of agents, self-control.")
(disjointWith Table-SeatingArea Booth-SeatingArea)
(comment Table-SeatingArea "A specialization of  #$RestaurantSeatingArea.  Instances of this collection are seating areas which are comprised of a table and several chairs.")
(comment Provisions "A collection which includes food, water clothing, and other items needed for survival.")
(comment objectReserved "(#$objectReserved RESERVATION OBJECT) means that OBJECT is the thing being reserved in RESERVATION.")
(comment genlPredsWRTTypes-Ternary "(#$genlPredsWRTTypes TERNPRED1 TERNPRED2 COL1 COL2 COL3) means that if (TERNPRED1 INS1 INS2 INS3) where INS1 is an instance of COL1, INS2 is an instance of COL2, and INS3 is an instance of COL3, then (TERNPRED2 INS1 INS2 INS3).  This allows us to specify a pseudo-genlPreds relationship between two #$TernaryPredicates when their argument constraints are not in the appropriate #$genls relationship.  See also #$genlPredsWRTTypes, which applies to #$BinaryPredicates.")
(comment BaseballPitch "The motion of a #$Baseball-Ball as it travels from the #$BaseballPitcher to (and possibly past) #$HomePlate following a #$BaseballDelivery. The #$Baseball-Ball almost always undergoes some #$NonConstrainedRotation.")
(comment SodiumIodide "A specialization of #$IonicCompound, Salt-ChemicalSubstance,
and InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$SodiumIodide is a sample
of the compound NaI.")
(comment SodiumIodide "The chemical compound formed from #$Sodium and #$Iodine ions.")
(comment Glycerol "A specialization of #$Alcohol-Compound.  Each instance of
#$Glycerol is a sample of C3H8O3.")
(comment NetworkPacketTransferFn "(#$NetworkPacketTransferFn PROT NUM) denotes the collection of network packet transfers in which data is transfered via PROT on NUM.")
(comment sharesInformation "(#$sharesInformation INFO1 INFO2) means that the #$AspatialInformationStores INFO1 and INFO2 share a significant amount of information. For the case where INFO1 contains all the information in INFO2, and more besides, see #$subInformation.")
(comment LeadIICarbonate "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of
#$LeadIICarbonate is a sample of the substance PbCO3.")
(comment DepartmentTypeByOrganization "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$DepartmentTypeByOrganization is a collection of #$Departments whose instances are distinguished by the fact that they all belong to a given #$Organization. See, for instance, #$CycorpDepartment. See also #$DepartmentTypeByFunction.")
(comment oxidationNumberInForm "(#$oxidationNumberInForm ELEMENT NUMBER SUBSTANCE) holds just in case NUMBER is
the oxidation number of ELEMENT when it is found in the atomic composition of SUBSTANCE.  For example, #$Zinc has an oxidation number of 0 in (#$ElementalSubstanceFn #$Zinc), and an oxidation number of +2 in #$ZincHydroxide or in (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$ZincIon).
<p>
<p>")
(comment disproportionationReactionFor "(#$disproportionationReactionFor ELEMENT REACTION-TYPE) holds just in case instances
of ELEMENT involved in reactions of type REACTION-TYPE are both oxidized (loses electrons)
and reduced (gains electrons).  For example, in the following unbalanced equation
(MnO4(2-) --> MnO4- + MnO2) Mn's oxidation number changes from 6 to 7 in the first product
and from 6 to 4 in the second product.")
(comment WritingAReport "A specialization of #$Writing, each instance of which is intended to produce an instance of #$ReportOnSituation-CW.")
(disjointWith SportsAction WritingAReport)
(comment FillingOutAFormByHand "The collection of instances of #$FillingOutAForm that are also instances of #$WritingByHand.")
(comment DepartmentTypeByFunction "A collection of collections and a specialization of #$OrganizationType.  Each instance of
#$DepartmentTypeByFunction is a collection of #$Departments 
(q.v.) whose instances are distinguished by the fact that 
they perform a certain function for an organization (e.g. 
#$BudgetDepartment and #$ShippingAndReceivingDepartment).  
See also #$DepartmentTypeByOrganization.")
(comment ReservationAtARestaurant "A specialization of #$Reservation.  Instances are agreements on the part of a #$Restaurant-Organization to hold a #$RestaurantSeatingArea for a patron.")
(disjointWith TravelReservation ReservationAtARestaurant)
(comment startsFirstTypeInInterval "(#$startsFirstTypeInInterval INT1 TYPE INT2) means that INT1 is the first occuring #$TimeInterval within INT2, which cooriginates with some instance of the #$TimeParameter TYPE, also contained in INT2.")
(comment SodiumMonoxide "A specialization of #$Oxide and #$InorganicCompound.  Each
instance of #$SodiumMonoxide is some amount of Na2O.")
(comment BariumNitrate "A specialization of both #$IonicCompound and #$InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$BariumNitrate is a sample of the compound Ba(NO3)2.")
(comment SodiumIodideSolution "A specialization of #$AqueousSolution.  Each instance of
#$SodiumIodideSolution is an aqueous solution whose solute is some
amount of #$SodiumIodide.")
(comment PotassiumChromate "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instacne of
#$PotassiumChromate is a sample of the compound K2CrO4.")
(comment ClassicsDepartment "A specialization of #$AcademicDepartment, instances of which conduct teaching and research in classicical languages, literature, philosophy etc.")
(comment PsychologyDepartment "A specialization of #$AcademicDepartment, instances of which conduct teaching and research in #$Psychology.")
(comment EconomicsDepartment "A specialization of #$AcademicDepartment, instances of which conduct teaching and research in #$Economics.")
(comment AcademicDepartmentTypeBySubject "A collection of collections. Each instance of #$AcademicDepartmentTypeBySubject is a collection of #$AcademicDepartments whose instances are distinguished by the fact that they all teach a similar subject matter (for instance, #$MathematicsDepartment, #$PhilosophyDepartment). This collection differs from #$DepartmentTypeByFunction in that being an #$AcademicDepartment is assumed to be a single 'function', and that faceting by subjects taught/researched is therefore a further issue.")
(comment objectHasColor "(#$objectHasColor OBJECT COLOR-COLL) means that some visible part of OBJECT is 
colored COLOR-COLL. This is the most general of the color predicates; for more specialized 
predicates see #$significantColorOfObject, #$mainColorOfObject, and 
#$uniformColorOfObject. Note that, unlike these latter three predicates, 
#$objectHasColor is not a specialization of #$isa. This is because an object's having 
some part that's a certain color does not entail that the object itself, as a whole, is 
characterized by that color in the sense of belonging to that color collection.")
(comment UniversityDepartment "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of #$UniversityDepartment is a department or suborganization of a kind specific to institutions of higher education (universities, community colleges, technical institutes etc). Notable specializations include #$OfficeOfStudentAffairs, #$BursarsOffice, and, of course, #$AcademicDepartment.")
(comment ComputerProcessNamedFn "(#$ComputerProcessNamedFn STRING) denotes the #$ComputerProcessRunning named STRING.")
(comment TestStateMachine-X "An internal counter parameter of the #$TestStateMachine.")
(comment htmlStringVersionOfCharacter "(#$htmlStringVersionOfCharacter CHARACTER STRING) means that STRING is the #$SubLString from an HTML rendering system would be expected to produce CHARACTER.")
(comment htmlGlyphCode "(#$htmlGlyphCode CHARACTER STRING) means that STRING is the HTML code - starting with an ampersand and ending with a semicolon - from which an HTML rendering system would be expected to produce CHARACTER.")
(comment quantityOfSubstanceInSample "A ternary predicate intended to express the quantity of a #$ChemicalSubstanceType in a sample of something, (possibly a sample of the same substance) when the quantity is measured in terms of number of basic parts or units. A standard unit used in chemistry is #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit. Thus, for instance, (#$quantityOfSubstanceInSample SAMPLE #$Salt-NaCl (#$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit 0.5)) says that SAMPLE is a partially tangible thing containing 0.5 moles of sodium chloride. Cf. #$quantityOfChemicalObjectInSample.")
(comment HydroselenicAcid "The collection of samples of hydroselenic acid; H2Se.")
(comment MusicalComposition-GangstaRap "the collection of instances of #$MusicalComposition that are in the \"gangsta rap\" style or genre.")
(comment TemporalMicrotheory "Each instance MT of #$TemporalMicrotheory is a microtheory that has the following characteristics. (i) MT has a time interval TIME as the value of `time index' (see #$MtTimeDimFn and #$mtTimeIndex), be it a time point, or an hour, or a day, or a stage in a plan or in a scenerio, or any other amount except the empty interval and `gappy' intervals.  (ii) MT has a time interval type PARA (an instance of #$TimeParameter) as the value of its `time parameter' (see #$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn and #$mtTimeIndexWithParameter), be it #$CalendarDay, (#$SameLengthTimeIntervalTypeFn (#$DaysDuration 1)) or #$Null-TimeParameter (also see #$TimeInterval, #$TimeParameter and #$MtSpace). (iii) Each statement PROP in MT (either asserted or inferred) is understood in the following way: (1) PROP is true _at_ TIME, and (2) for each time interval T subsumed by TIME, if (#$temporallySubsumes-InsType T PARA) holds, PROP is true at T, and (3) other conditions specified in terms of the values of MT's other dimensions.  Note that if MT-1 and MT-2 are instances of #$TemporalMicrotheory such that MT-1 has `time index' T-1 and `time parameter' P-1 and such that MT-2 has `time index' T-2 and `time parameter' P-2 (and the rest of them are the same), then the following are (jointly) sufficient and necessary conditions for (#$genlMt MT-2 MT-1) to hold: (a) (#$temporallySubsumes T-1 T-2), (b) (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType P-2 P-1) and (c) (#$temporallySubsumes-InsType T-2 P-1).")
(disjointWith AtemporalMicrotheory TemporalMicrotheory)
(comment AtemporalMicrotheory "This collection is the complement of #$TemporalMicrotheory relative to #$Microtheory.")
(comment DiOxygenFluoride "The collection of samples of fluorine peroxide; O2F2.")
(comment SeleniumFluoride "The collection of samples of selenium fluoride; SeF6.")
(comment oxidationStatesOfElement "(#$oxidationStatesOfElement ELEMENT NUM) holds just in case NUM is a possible 
oxidation state (cf. #$oxidationNumber) for atoms of ELEMENT.  For example, the following both hold 
(#$oxidationStatesOfElement #$Carbon 4) and (#$oxidationStatesOfElement #$Carbon 2).")
(comment DecaffeinatedTea-Beverage "The collection of all #$Tea-Beverage that is decaffeinated.")
(comment DrinkingDecaf "The collection of events in which some #$DecaffeinatedCoffee-Beverage is the #$thingsConsumed.")
(comment DecaffeinatedCoffee-Beverage "A specialization of #$Coffee-Beverage.  Each instance of this collection has been processed to have significantly less caffeine than regular coffee.")
(comment PrecipitationReaction "The collection of #$ChemicalReactions in which an insoluble product is formed. Precipitation reactions occur when certain pairs of oppositely-charged ions attract each other so strongly that they form an insoluble ionic solid.")
(comment IonicCompoundFn "(#$IonicCompoundFn ?CATION-TYPE ?ANION-TYPE) represents the collection of ionic compounds formed from the ions ?CATION-TYPE and ?ANION-TYPE. If the relative numbers of cations and anions are known, #$IonicDecompositionFn can be used instead.")
(comment ITDepartment "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is a group of employees who set up, maintain, administer and ensure the security of an organization's information systems -- including computer networks, telephones, projectors etc. An important specialization of this collection is #$SystemsAdministrationDepartment.")
(comment CalciumHydride "The collection of samples of calcium hydride; CaH2.")
(comment QualityAssuranceDepartment "A specialization of #$Department.  Each instance of #$QualityAssuranceDepartment consists of employees who are responsible for ensuring that an organization's products are of high standard (by testing them and putting in place processes which ensure quality production in the first place).")
(comment MetallicHydrideType "The collection of instances of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies formed when #$Hydrogen reacts with any of the transition metals.")
(comment MolecularHydrideType "The collection of instances of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies formed when #$Hydrogen reacts with a
 nonmetal or semimetal element.")
(comment BinaryHydrogenCompoundType "The collection of all instances of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies that are the result of #$Hydrogen reacting with another element.  This collection is partitioned into three subcollections: #$IonicHydrideType, #$MetallicHydrideType, and #$MolecularHydrideType.")
(comment SignificantDigitsFn "(#$SignificantDigitsFn DIGITS QUANTITY) is a binary, evaluatable relation that converts the quantity QUANTITY to the closest quantity of the same type that has only DIGITS number of significant decimal digits.  For example, (#$SignificantDigitsFn 2 (#$Meter 212.0d0)) returns (#$Meter 210).")
(comment SulfiteIon "The collection of ions with chemical structure SO3(-2)).  See also (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$SulfiteIon).")
(comment ChemicalSpeciesType "The #$collectionUnion of #$ChemicalObjectTypeByChemicalSpecies and #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.  Instances are either specific types of molecule, atom, or ion, or ``mobs'' of instances of one of these.")
(comment Appendix-PCW "The collection of all textual subdivisions that are appendices to larger, structured, typically book-length #$PropositionalConceptualWorks.  An example would be Appendix D of #$ChemistryTheCentralScience-Textbook.")
(comment DirectorsOffice "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit which performs a high-level management function for that organization. Its employees will include some instance of  #$DirectorOfOrganization, and staff who support him or her. For the very top management level, see also #$PresidentsOffice.")
(comment PresidentsOffice "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit which performs the very highest management function for that organization. Its employees will include some instance of  #$PresidentOfOrganization, and staff who support him or her. See also #$DirectorsOffice.")
(comment AppendixOfWork-LetterFn "(#$AppendixOfWork-LetterFn
(#$ChapterOfBook-OrdinalFn BOOK N) LETT) denotes appendix LETT in the instance BOOK of #$Book-CW, where LETT is an order-designating #$RomanLetter.")
(comment SoftwareParameterFromSyntaxFn "#$SoftwareParameterFromSyntaxFn is a unary function which returns the #$SoftwareParameter corresponding to the given #$ProgramSyntaxObject.")
(comment IodineMolecule "I2. The collection of all samples of bi-atomic Iodine.  A gas under standard temperature and pressure.")
(comment MagnesiumSulfate "MnSO4. The collection of all samples of magnesium sulfate.")
(comment PotassiumSulfate "K2SO4. The collection of all samples of magnesium sulfate.")
(comment AmmoniumHydroxide "The chemical compound created in #$Solutions of #$Ammonia and #$Water.")
(comment MultiplicationSign-TheSymbol "A sign which looks like an x.")
(comment ionicEquation-Complete "A ternary predicate used to describe the complete ionic equation for a given type of #$ChemicalReaction.   In a gaf of the form (#$ionicEquation-Complete  R-TYPE REACTANT-SET PRODUCT-SET), REACTANT-SET and PRODUCT-SET are sets of two-member lists, where the first member of each of the lists is a #$ChemicalSubstanceType, and the second member is the corresponding  balancing coefficient in a reaction of R-TYPE.  See also #$chemicalEquation-Balanced.")
(comment LeadIodidePrecipitation-Reaction "A type of chemical reaction in which a solution containing lead nitrate and a solution containing potassium iodide combine to precipitate out lead iodide.")
(comment ionicEquation-Net "A ternary predicate used to describe the net ionic equation for a given type of #$ChemicalReaction.   In a gaf of the form (#$ionicEquation-Net  R-TYPE REACTANT-SET PRODUCT-SET), REACTANT-SET and PRODUCT-SET are sets of two member lists, where the first member of each of the lists is a #$ChemicalSubstanceType, and the second member is the corresponding  balancing coefficient in a reaction of R-TYPE.  See also #$chemicalEquation-Balanced and #$ionicEquation-Complete.")
(comment PeroxideType "The collection of compounds called 'peroxides'.  Each instance of #$PeroxideType
is a sample of a compound (but not necessarily an ionic compound) containing the 
[#$PeroxideIon] O2(2-).  An example is (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$HydrogenPeroxideMolecule).")
(comment Airfield "An airplane landing strip with no facilities for the
commercial handling of cargo or passengers")
(comment AlluvialFan "A fan-shaped wedge of coarse alluvium with apex merging with
a mountain stream bed and the fan spreading out at a low angle slope
onto an adjacent plain.")
(comment Alluvium "Clay or slit or gravel carried by rushing streams and
deposited where the stream slows down.")
(comment Amphitheater "An oval or circular structure with rising tiers of seats
about a stage or open space.")
(comment Arch-Topographical "A natural arch-like opening in a rock mass.  Also called a
natural bridge.")
(comment ArchaeologicalArtifact "The objects of study of #$Archaeology.")
(comment AstronomicalStation "A place on the earth whose position has been determined by
observations of celestial bodies.")
(comment Bight "An open body of water forming a slight recession in a coastline")
(comment Blowout-Topographical "A small depression in sandy terrain, caused by wind erosion.")
(comment Blowhole-Topographical "A hole in coastal rock through which sea water is forced by
a rising tide or waves and spurted through an outlet into the air.")
(comment Borderlands-Undersea "A region adjacent to a continent, normally occupied by or
bordering a shelf, that is highly irregular with depths well in excess
of those typical of a shelf.")
(comment BoundaryMarker "A fixture marking a point along a boundary.")
(comment BreakwaterStructure "A structure erected to break the force of waves at the
entrance to a harbor or port.")
(comment BurialCave "A cave used for human burials.")
(comment CivilAdministrativeRegion "Geographical artifacts or regions administrated by civil
government authorities, often for specific land-use purposes.
Examples include civil government facilities, national parks, and .
Also includes complex entities such as townships, municipalities,
counties, cities, etc.  It does not include military facilities, nor
law enforcement facilities.")
(disjointWith MilitaryAdministrativeRegion CivilAdministrativeRegion)
(disjointWith CivilAdministrativeRegion (SubcollectionOfWithRelationFromTypeFn GroundsOfOrganization physicalQuarters LawEnforcementOrganization))
(disjointWith (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet Park Forest CivilAdministrativeRegion)) (CollectionDifferenceFn GeographicalRegion (CollectionUnionFn (TheSet Park Forest CivilAdministrativeRegion))))
(comment Cleft "A deep narrow slot, notch, or groove in a coastal cliff.")
(comment CommercialFarmFacility "Farms which raise either livestock or crops for profit.")
(comment CommercialFarmOrganization "Farms which raise either livestock or crops for profit.")
(comment CommunityCenter "A facility for community recreation and other activities.")
(disjointWith JunctionOfPipes ConfluenceOfStreams)
(comment ConcessionArea "A lease of land by a government for economic development, eg
mining, forestry.")
(comment Convent-Residence "A building where nuns live.")
(comment Cordillera "An entire mountain system including all subordinate ranges,
interior plateaus, and basins.  Every instance of #$MountainRange is a
#$geographicalSubRegions of some instance of #$Cordillera, while every
instance of #$Cordillera has at least one #$MountainRange as a
#$geographicalSubRegions of it.  However, no instance of #$Cordillera
can be a #$geographicalSubRegions of a larger #$MountainRange.  In
particular, no two distinct instances of #$Cordillera may bear the
relation #$geographicalSubRegions with each other.")
(comment DrainageBasin "An area drained by a stream or river system.")
(comment DryDock "A #$Dock providing support for a vessel, and means for
removing the water so that the bottom of the vessel can be exposed.")
(comment Flat-Topographical "A relatively level area within a region of greater relief.
Examples include forest clearings, mountain terraces, playas, and
mesas.")
(comment IrrigationCanal "A #$Canal which serves as a main conduit for irrigation water.")
(comment LaborCamp "A camp used by migrant or temporary laborers.")
(comment LavaField "Land formations resulting from the consolidation of molten
rock on the surface of the Earth.")
(comment Locale "A place at which there is or once was human activity; it
does not include populated places, mines, and dams.")
(comment LoggingCamp "A camp used by loggers.")
(comment Miner "A person who works in a mine.")
(comment MiningCamp "A camp used by miners.")
(disjointWith IrrigationCanal NavigationCanal)
(disjointWith DrainageCanal NavigationCanal)
(comment NuclearResearchFacility "A facility where nuclear research is conducted, either for
acedemic, commerical, or military weapons research.  Nuclear research for
military purposes is denoted by the specialization
#$NuclearWeaponResearchFacility.")
(comment OilFieldWorker "A person who works in an oil field.")
(comment OilCamp "A camp used by oil field workers.")
(disjointWith Locale PopulatedPlace)
(comment PopulatedPlace "A #$Place or area with clustered or scattered buildings and
a permanent human population, including cities, settlements, towns,
and villages.  It does not include #$Locales.")
(comment PsychiatricHospital "A #$Hospital that specializes in the long term care of the
mentally disabled.")
(disjointWith FlatRegion Slope-Topographical)
(comment KrispyKreme-Donut "The collection of all #$Doughnuts made by #$KrispyKremeCorporation")
(comment UnfilledDonut "The collection of all #$Doughnuts with no fillings.")
(comment FilledDonut "The collection of all #$Doughnuts with fillings.")
(comment EEOOffice "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit which oversees \"equal employment opportunity\" for an organization. ")
(comment TrainingDepartment "A specialization of #$HumanResourcesDepartment. Each instance of this collection is a group of employees who train other employees in work-related skills.")
(comment OfficeOfHealthAndSafety "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit which ensures that an organization adheres to guidelines regarding the health and safety of its workers.")
(comment EthicsCounsel "A specialization of #$Department. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit which provides some kind of ethical guidance to an organization.")
(comment OfficeOfChiefFinancialOfficer "A specialization of #$FinanceDepartment. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit whose employees include an organization's #$ChiefFinancialOfficer, and staff who support him or her.")
(comment NeutralizationReactionType "A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$NeutralizationReactionType is a type of chemical reaction (i.e., a specialization of #$ChemicalReaction) in which an acid solution and a base solution are mixed. Salts are usually products of neutralization reactions.")
(comment Hydrolysis-ChemicalReaction "A specialization of #$ChemicalReaction. Each instance of #$Hydrolysis-ChemicalReaction is a reaction between a substance and water in which two or more new substances are formed. This process includes the ionization of the water molecules involved in the reaction as well as the decomposition of the compound in the reaction.  One major specialization of this collection occurs when ions react with water to create H+ or OH-.")
(comment solutionOf "(#$solutionOf ?SOLTYPE ?SOLVENTTYPE ?SOLUTETYPE) means that solutions of type ?SOLTYPE are created by combining instances of the #$solvent ?SOLVENTTYPE with instances of the #$solute ?SOLUTETYPE.  This predicate does not assume ?SOLVENTTYPE and ?SOLUTETYPE are the only substances present, see also #$totalSolutesInAqueousSolution.")
(comment F2Molecule "A specialization of #$Molecule.  Each instance of
#$F2Molecule is a binary #$Fluorine molecule.")
(comment AmideIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Each instance of
#$AmideIon is a sample of NH2(-).")
(comment PotassiumAmide "A specialization of #$IonicCompound and #$InorganicCompound. Each instance of #$PotassiumAmide is a sample of KNH2.")
(comment substanceFormOfElement "(#$substanceFormOfElement SUBSTANCE-TYPE ELEMENT) says that the #$ElementalSubstanceType SUBSTANCE-TYPE is the collection of all samples of stuff-like material composed entirely of the element ELEMENT.  So, for example (#$substanceFormOfElement (#$ElementalSubstanceFn #$Calcium) #$Calcium) says that (#$ElementalSubstanceFn #$Calcium) is the collection of all elemental substance composed of the element #$Calcium.")
(comment TransitionMetalElementType "A specialization of #$MetallicElementType.  Instances are such that their valence electrons have more than one shell.")
(comment EpsomSalt "An instance of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies and a specialization of #$Salt-ChemicalSubstance.  Each instance of #$EpsomSalt is a sample of the compound MgSO4*7H2O.")
(comment Library-Department "A specialization of #$Library-Organization. Each instance of this collection is a library which stands in the #$subOrganizations relation to some larger entity (such as a University).")
(comment PlanningAndStrategicInitiativesDepartment "A specialization of #$AdministrativeDepartment. Each instance of this collection consists of employees of an organization who are responsible for high-level functions such as: integrating work performed across the organization, gathering data to be used in developing new policy for the organization, helping draft such new policies, and evaluating the results of past policy-choices.")
(comment OfficeOfInspectorGeneral "A specialization of #$FinanceDepartment. Each instance of this collection is an organizational unit whose job it is to monitor an organization for fraud, waste, misuse of the organization's resources, etc..")
(comment InspectorGeneral "A specialization of #$Inspector-Professional. Each instance of this collection is a high-level official who performs independent audits, investigations, and evaluations for a particular governmental organization, attempts to eliminate fraud and waste and abuse, reviews pending legislation, and keep the agency head and the government informed about the above issues. Their expertise thus spans accounting, auditing, law, management and public administration (among other areas).")
(comment ZincChloride "A specialization of #$IonicCompound and #$InorganicCompound.
Each instance of #$ZincChloride is a sample of ZnCl2.")
(comment TimeInterval-Incl-Excl-Fn "Provided T0 ends before T1 starts, (#$TimeInterval-Incl-Excl-Fn T0 T1) denotes the maximal contiguous time interval that contains no time points before T0 and subsumes no time point in the temporal extent of T1.")
(comment TimeInterval-Excl-Incl-Fn "Provided T0 ends before T1 starts, (#$TimeInterval-Excl-Incl-Fn T0 T1) denotes the maximal contiguous time interval that contains no time points after T1 and subsumes no time point in the temporal extent of T0.")
(comment granuleOfChemicalSubstanceType "(#$granuleOfChemicalSubstanceType STUFFTYPE OBJTYPE) says  that the stuff-like #$ChemicalSubstanceType STUFFTYPE has as its spatial granules (or granularity level) instances of the object-like collection OBJTYPE. Typically, OBJTYPE will be a specialization of #$Molecule or #$Ion.")
(comment sksRepresentationComplete "(#$sksRepresentationComplete SKS) means that the KB representation of the structured knowledge source SKS is complete, meaning that all necessary information required to register the SKSI removal modules for SKS has been asserted in the KB. The extent of this predicate is used by the SKSI user interfaces that enable users to register/deregister SKSI inference support for structured knowledge sources.")
(comment BoronTrichloride "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$BoronTrichloride is a sample of BCl3.")
(comment BoronTriflouride "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound.  Each instance of #$BoronTriflouride is a sample of BFl3.")
(comment PropanoateIon "The collection of propanoate ions; C3H5O2-.")
(comment SodiumPropanoate "The collection of samples of sodium propanoate; NaC3H5O2.")
(comment ZincSulfate "A specialization of #$IonicCompound and #$InorganicCompound.
Each instance of #$ZincSulfate is a sample of the compound ZnSO4.")
(comment SodiumPhosphate "A specialization of #$InorganicCompound and #$IonicCompound.
Each instance of #$SodiumPhosphate is a sample of the compound
NA3PO4.")
(comment SilverNitrate "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of
#$SilverNitrate is a sample of the compound AgNO3.")
(comment elementTypesInFormula-List "(#$elementTypesInFormula-List TYPE ELEMENTS) holds just in case ELEMENTS is the complete list, without repetition, of elements in the chemical formula of the #$ChemicalSubstanceType TYPE. In other words, for each #$memberOfList INST in ELEMENTS, it is the case that (#$atomicComposition TYPE INST N), for some N greater than 0.")
(comment LithiumBromide "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of #$LithiumBromide is a sample of the compound LiBr.")
(comment PotassiumSulfite "A specialization of #$IonicCompund.  Each instance of
#$PotassiumSulfite is a sample of the compound K2SO3.")
(comment TinIIChloride "A specialization of #$IonicCompund.  Each instance of
#$TinIIChloride is a sample of the compound SnCl2.")
(comment EmployeeComputerActivityType "A specialization of #$ComputerActivityType. Each instance of this (second-order) collection is a subcollection of #$ComputerActivity all of whose instances are a particular and distinct type of computer-involving activity which is typically performed by employees as part of their work duties. Examples include #$EMailCommunication and #$PreparingPresentationSlides. Examples of #$ComputerActivityType which are not instances of #$EmployeeComputerActivityType include #$InterfacingBetweenComputerDeviceAndOS and #$HomecamBroadcasting. Activities such as #$ModifyingAFile, though often performed by employees, are assumed to be too fine-grained and not distinct enough to warrant inclusion in this collection.")
(comment PotassiumPermanganate "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of
#$PotassiumPermanganate is a sample of the compound KMnO4.")
(comment ThiocyanateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  each instance of
#$ThiocyanateIon is a sample of the ion SCN(-).")
(comment AmmoniumThiocyanate "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of
#$AmmoniumThiocyanate is a sample of the compound NH4SCN")
(comment FerricChloride "A specialization of #$IonicCompound.  Each instance of
#$FerricChloride is a sample of the compound FeCl3.")
(comment ClassifyingReactions-APChem-WebPage "The webpage for Classifying Reactions to help with
Predicting Reactions, based on a worksheet from Mr. Grove's AP
Chemistry class.")
(comment startsAfterStartOfInSituationType "<code>(#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts after <code>SIT2</code> starts.
<p>
Note that <code>(#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).")
(comment MetathesisReaction "The collection of all instances of #$ChemicalReaction in which positive ions and negative ions swap 'partners,' and so conform to a general pattern: AX + BY -> AY + BX.")
(comment spectatorIonInReactionType "(#$spectatorIonInReactionType R-TYPE ION)
says that ION, a specialization of #$Ion, occurs on both sides of the complete ionic equation (and thus does not occur at all in the net ionic equation) for R-TYPE, a specialization of #$ChemicalReaction. See also #$ionicEquation-Complete and #$ionicEquation-Net.")
(comment intervalRange "A ternary predicate that relates two comparable scalar intervals (see #$comparableScalars) to the (usually proper) interval between and including them.  <code>(#$intervalRange RANGE SCALAR1 SCALAR2)</code> means that <code>RANGE</code> is the interval that ranges from <code>SCALAR1</code> to <code>SCALAR2</code> inclusive.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$intervalRange #$CoolToCold #$Cold #$Cool)</code> means that #$CoolToCold is the #$Temperature ranging from #$Cold to #$Cool.  Also, <code>(#$intervalRange (#$Unity 1.0 10.0) 1.0 10.0)</code> holds.
<p>
For more details, see the corresponding function #$IntervalRangeFn.")
(comment MP3FileCopy "A specialization of #$ComputerFileCopy whose instances contain #$MP3Files as the encoded data.")
(comment totalChemicalReactants "A predicate used to enumerate all the chemical reactants of a given #$ChemicalReactionTypeByEquationFormula, by identifying the set of those reactants. (#$totalChemicalProducts REACTION-TYPE SET) says that SET is the set of all chemical substance types such that  (#$chemicalReactants-TypeType REACTION-TYPE SUBSTANCE-TYPE) holds.")
(comment totalChemicalProducts "A predicate used to enumerate all the chemical product-types of a given #$ChemicalReactionTypeByEquationFormula, by identifying the set of those product-types. (#$totalChemicalProducts REACTION-TYPE SET) says that SET is the set of all chemical substance types such that  (#$chemicalProducts-TypeType REACTION-TYPE SUBSTANCE-TYPE) holds.")
(comment EnteringAnHOC "This is the general event-type of entering a building, room, or vehicle, that is, moving from outside to inside by passing through a portal.")
(comment LeavingAnHOC "This is the general event-type for leaving a building, room, or vehicle, that is, moving from inside to outside by passing through a portal.")
(comment ionTypeOfIonicCompoundType "A binary predicate that relates a type of ionic compound to the types #$Ions that occurs in that compound. (#$ionTypeOfIonicCompoundType COMPOUND-TYPE ION-TYPE) means that ION-TYPE is a type of ion that occurs in ionic compounds of the type COMPOUND-TYPE.  Note that #$ionTypeOfIonicCompoundType is partitioned into #$cationTypeOfIonicCompoundType and #$anionTypeOfIonicCompoundType.")
(comment precipitate "(#$precipitate REACTION SOLID) says that SOLID is an insoluble substance that is the #$chemicalProducts-Macroscopic of the chemical reaction REACTION.")
(comment MethylAmmoniumNitrate "The collection of samples of methylammonium nitrate; CH3NH3NO3.")
(comment instantiatesReactionType "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot.  Use this predicate to relate an individual #$ChemicalReaction with the unique #$ChemicalReactionTypeByEquationFormula that it instantiates.  (#$instantiatesReactionType REACTION TYPE) says that REACTION is an instance of this unique TYPE.")
(comment instantiatesCompoundType "An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot.  Use this predicate to relate an instance of #$PartiallyTangible with the unique #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies that it instantiates (if any).  (#$instantiatesCompoundType TANGIBLE TYPE) says that TANGIBLE is an instance of this unique TYPE.")
(comment Bathroom "Any room which is primarily intended for bathing, showering, or washing.")
(comment MatrixSolutionFn "#$MatrixSolutionFn is an #$EvaluatableFunction that calculates the solution to a set of linear equations.  (#$MatrixSolutionFn MATRIX) denotes the solution (should one exist) to the integer matrix MATRIX.  By 'integer matrix', we mean a #$List of #$Lists (of the same length) of #$Integers.  By 'the solution to the matrix', we mean the #$List of #$Integers that is the lowest whole-number solution to the set of linear equations that the matrix represents; each row of the matrix (#$TheList <a1> <a2> ... <an>) being interpreted as the linear equation <a1>*x1 + <a2>*x2 + ... + <an-1>*xn-1 = <an>*xn.  In other words, if (#$MatrixSolutionFn MATRIX) evaluates to (#$TheList <b1> <b2> ... <bn>), that means that <b1>, <b2>, ... , and <bn> are values of the variables x1, x2, ... , and xn, respectively, for which all of the equations represented by rows of the matrix are true.  If there is no solution to the matrix, or if there are infinitely many lowest whole-number solutions, then (#$MatrixSolutionFn MATRIX) denotes nothing.")
(comment availableReactantTypeInReactionType "(#$availableReactantTypeInReactionType REACTTYPE SUBSTTYPE) means that in the #$ChemicalReaction REACTTYPE, SUBSTTYPE is an available substance for reacting with another substance.  This does not mean that substance *will* react with another, as this depends on the other substances present for REACTTYPE.  See also #$spectatorIonInReactionType and #$chemicalProducts-TypeType.")
(comment Reduction-ChemicalReaction "A specialization of #$ChemicalReaction. Each instance of
#$Reduction-ChemicalReaction is a reaction in which an instance of
#$ChemicalObject either accepts one or more electrons from another,
oxygen is removed from the instance, or hydrogen is added to the
instance.  #$Reduction-ChemicalReaction and #$OxidationProcess typically
occur together, see also #$RedoxReaction.")
(comment LeastCommonMultipleFn "(#$LeastCommonMultipleFn INT_1 ... INT_N) returns the least common multiple of the #$ScalarIntegralValues INT_1, ..., INT_N.")
(comment HelpDeskProgram "A specialization of #$HelpProgram. Each instance of this collection is software designed to support a #$HelpDesk-Organization by providing online help to users.")
(comment commonIonTypeInCompoundTypes "(#$commonIonTypeInCompoundTypes COMPOUND1 COMPOUND2 ION) holds if ION is the common ion of the decomposable compounds COMPOUND1 and COMPOUND2, in the sense of the `common ion effect.' This means either that both contain ION as
part of their #$ionicDecomposition, or that ION is the common product of their dissociation.  For example,
#$AceticAcid and #$SodiumAcetate have a common ion:
(#$commonIonTypeInCompoundTypes #$AceticAcid #$SodiumAcetate
#$AcetateIon), but so do #$Ammonia and #$AmmoniumChloride.")
(comment HydrogenOxalateIon "The collection of HC2O4(1-) ions.  See also (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn #$HydrogenOxalateIon). ")
(comment Chlorofluorocarbon "A specialization of #$OrganicCompound.  Each instance of #$Chlorofluorocarbon is a sample of a compound comprised of carbon, chlorine, and fluorine, such as #$Freon.")
(comment ruleTrivialForJustificationParaphrase "(#$ruleTrivialForJustificationParaphrase RULE) means that when
RULE is used in a transformation proof, a paraphrase of that proof need
not explicitly mention RULE, as a human can be expected to consider that
rule obvious.")
(comment StannicIon "A specialization of #$VariableChargeCation.  Instances of
#$StannicIon are single #$Tin atoms with an #$ElectronicCharge of +4,
which means it has lost 4 electrons.")
(comment StuffIShouldKnowForAPChemTest-Halo-WebPage "An instance of #$WorldWideWebPage-AIS.  This webpage offers
information on chemical substances students preparing for hte AP
Chemistry exam would find useful.")
(comment ionicEquation-Net-Unbalanced "This predicate represents the net ionic equation listing the reactants and products of a type of chemical
reaction. (#$ionicEquation-Net-Unbalanced TYPE I-REACTANTS I-PRODUCTS)
expresses the equation of a reaction in which each member of I-REACTANTS or
I-PRODUCTS is an instance of #$ChemicalSubstanceType, and no instance of
#$ChemicalSubstanceType of the form (#$ChemicalSubstanceFn ION-TYPE) (where ION-TYPE is an instance of #$IonTypeByChemicalSpecies) occurs as a member of both I-REACTANTS and I-PRODUCTS.")
(comment programDataTypeNameString "Relates a computer program and a data type to the name of that data type in that program.")
(comment deprecatedProgramDataTypeNameString "Relates a computer program to a data type (by name in that program) that is deprecated. Such types should be recognised but never generated.")
(comment BitStringDatatypeType "The collection of all data types stored, sent or received as a single string of bits. Examples include anything that might be stored as a field in a database (eg string, number, date, BLOB), and any data \"flattened\" or \"pickled\" (ie reduced to a single string). Note that the flat string of bits may *represent* a complex data structure; the only constraint is on the way it is stored or transmitted.")
(comment 4ByteIntegerDatatype "The collection of all computer data types that employ exactly 4 bytes to represent some subset of the integers or values related to represnting integer values such as NAN. Note that this does not specify, for example, whether the data type represents positive integers only, or is in two's complement representation.")
(comment 2ByteIntegerDatatype "The collection of all computer data types that employ exactly 2 bytes to represent some subset of the integers or values related to represnting integer values such as NAN. Note that this does not specify, for example, whether the data type represents positive integers only, or is in two's complement representation.")
(comment ApproximateBinaryRealNumberTypeFn "(#$ApproximateBinaryRealNumberTypeFn MANTISSA EXPONENT) denotes the collection of all approximate binary real numbers represented as MANTISSA binary digits of mantissa, and EXPONENT binary digits of exponent, assuming standard 2's complement representation of these two components.")
(comment IP4Address "A specialization of UniqueID and IDString. Each instance of IP4NetworkAddress is a string that is a unique address of a network or a computer that is used in communications over the Internet that conform to InternetProtocol V4. An IP4NetworkAddress is equivalent to an IPAddress along with a subnet mask. Note that the #$IPAddress, being the address of a single computer, is just a IP4NetworkAddress with a subnet mask of 255.255.255.255.")
(comment ratioOfTo "The predicate corresponding to #$QuotientFn.  (#$ratioOfTo NUM-1 NUM-2 NUM-3) holds just in case the ratio of NUM-1 to NUM-2 is NUM-3; that is, just in case  (#$QuotientFn NUM-1 NUM-2) = NUM-3.  For example, (#$ratioOfTo 1 2 0.5) is true because 1 divided by 2 is 0.5.")
(comment MelonPlant "A specialization of #$Plant-NonWoody.  Each instance of #$MelonPlant is a vine that bears melons as fruit (see (#$FruitFn #$MelonPlant)).")
(comment ELFFORT "The KB subset collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage terms.")
(comment SportsCommissioner "The collection of persons who are the #$departmentHeads of the #$SportsOrganization #$Departments which have ultimate control over the league in question.")
(comment CommissionersOffice-SportsOrganization "The collection of #$AdministrativeDepartments which have control over #$SportsOrganizations. Such offices will typically perform such actions as: maintaining the league's #$CodeOfConduct; approving (or, rarely, rejecting) player transactions within the league; carrying out $#FinePenalizing, suspensions, and other penalties; and so on.")
(comment Metaville "Metaville is a fictional city very much like a typical American city circa 2003. Used for testing and demonstrating Cyc KB content with persistent hypothetical instances. See also #$MetavilleMt and #$MetaWorldMt.")
(comment StringOfMaximumLengthFn "An instance of (#$StringOfMaximumLengthFn N) is a string with at most N characters.")
(comment StringOfMinimumLengthFn "An instance of (#$StringOfMinimumLengthFn N) is a string with at least N characters.")
(comment ListOfMaximumLengthFn "An instance of (#$ListOfMaximumLengthFn N) is a list with at most N elements.")
(comment ListOfMinimumLengthFn "An instance of (#$ListOfMinimumLengthFn N) is a list with at least N elements.")
(comment ModerateBreezeArea "An instance of #$OutdoorLocationByWeatherType.  It is characterized by wind speeds of 15-25 mph.")
(comment umlProcedureEvaluationContext "The #$BinaryPredicate umlProcedureEvaluationContext links an instance #$UMLProcedure with an instance of #$UMLProcedureEvaluationMicrotheory which contains #$softwareParameterValue assertions representing the procedural state.")
(comment contributesToSpeciesInSample "(#$contributesToSpeciesInSample SAMPLE SUBSTANCE ION-TYPE INTERVAL) means that a certain (unspecified) amount of #$ChemicalSpeciesType SUBSTANCE contributes #$ScalarInterval INTERVAL amount of #$IonTypeByChemicalSpecies ION-TYPE to the #$PartiallyTangible SAMPLE.  (#$contributesToSpeciesInSample SAMPLE SUBSTANCE ION-TYPE INTERVAL) implies that SUBSTANCE is \"in\" SAMPLE (for the relevant sense of \"in\", see #$molarityOfSubstanceInSample). Also, if it is true that (#$contributesToSpeciesInSample SAMPLE SUBSTANCE ION-TYPE INTERVAL), it is possible for INTERVAL to be \"zero\" -- i.e, it is possible that SUBSTANCE contributes no amount of ION-TYPE to SAMPLE.  However, it is not possible for SUBSTANCE to contribute a \"negative amount\" of ION-TYPE to SAMPLE.")
(comment speciesAddedToResultsIn "(#$speciesAddedToResultsIn CHEM-TYPE SAMPLE1 SAMPLE2) means that adding a certain (unspecified) amount of the #$ChemicalSubstanceType CHEM-TYPE to the #$PartiallyTangible SAMPLE1 results in #$PartiallyTangible SAMPLE2.  Note that #$speciesAddedToResultsIn is an instance of #$FunctionalPredicate -- there is a unique SAMPLE2 that results from adding the  relevant amount of CHEM-TYPE to SAMPLE1.")
(comment UMLProcedureEvaluationMicrotheory "A microtheory to contain state variables for the evaluation of a #$UMLProcedure.")
(comment UMLStateMachine-StateMicrotheory "A microtheory to contain state variables for the evaluation of a #$UMLStateMachine.")
(comment umlStateMachineInterpretationContext "The #$BinaryPredicate umlProcedureEvaluationContext links an instance #$UMLStateMachine with an instance of #$UMLStateMachine-StateMicrotheory which contains #$softwareParameterValue assertions representing the state.")
(comment UMLStateMachineDefinitionMicrotheory "A microtheory to contain the defining assertions for a #$UMLStateMachine.")
(comment BariumHydroxide "The collection of samples of barium hydroxide; a strong base whose chemical formula is Ba(OH)2.")
(comment StrontiumHydroxide "The collection of samples of strontium hydroxide; a strong base whose chemical formula is Sr(OH)2.")
(comment CesiumHydroxide "The collection of samples of cesium hydroxide; a strong base whose chemical formula is CsOH.")
(comment RubidiumHydroxide "The collection of samples of rubidium hydroxide; a strong base whose chemical formula is LiOH.")
(comment RubidiumIon "A specialization of #$MonoatomicCation. Instances of #$RubidiumIon are single #$Rubidium atoms that have lost an electron and now has a +1 charge.")
(comment LithiumHydroxide "The collection of samples of lithium hydroxide; a strong base whose chemical formula is LiOH.")
(comment StateForCityFn "(#$StateForCityFn CITY) returns the US state of which CITY is a #$geopoliticalSubdivision. Example, (#$StateForCityFn (#$CityNamedFn \"Medford\" #$Oregon-State) will return #$Oregon-State.")
(comment ProvinceForCityFn "(#$ProvinceForCityFn CITY) returns the Canadian province of which CITY is a #$geopoliticalSubdivision. Example, (#$ProvinceForCityFn #$CityOfTorontoOntario) will return #$Ontario-CanadianProvince.")
(comment umlStateMachineInterpetationContextStackParent "The #$BinaryPredicate umlProcedureEvaluationContext links two evaluation context frames in a #$UMLStateMachine interpreter thread.")
(comment UMLStateMachineContextFrame "A microtheory to contain state variables for the evaluation of a #$UMLProcedure.")
(comment UMLStateMachineContextFrame "A microtheory to contain state variables for the evaluation of a #$UMLProcedure or #$UMLState.  Each microtheory of this sort is part of a program stack.")
(comment UMLStateMachineContextFrame "A microtheory which contains state variables for the evaluation of a #$UMLProcedure or #$UMLState.  Each microtheory of this sort is part of a program stack.")
(comment geopoliticalEntityCodeDigraph "(#$geopoliticalEntityCodeDigraph PLACE CODE) means that CODE is an internationally recognized two-letter abbreviation for the name of PLACE.")
(comment Advertising-Business "A specialization of #$Advertising and #$MarketingActivity, and an instance of #$BusinessTransactionType. Each instance of this collection consists of some communication to potential customers of a #$Business, the desire to do business with them. (The desire may be either to \"do business\" in general, or to sell them something specific.) Successful instances of #$Advertising-Business often result in instances of #$Buying.")
(comment Advertising-Political "A specialization of #$Advertising.  Each instance of #$Advertising-Political is an instance of #$Communicating in which some #$PoliticalOrganization attempts to influence voters to support one of that organization's causes or candidates.  An example would be the #$DemocraticParty-US running a #$TVCommercial-IBT attempting to persuade people to vote for #$JohnKerry in 2004.")
(comment MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn "An unreifiable #$BinaryFunction that takes a #$TimeInterval and a #$TimeParameter and returns a #$TemporalMicrotheory.  <code>(#$ist (#$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn INTERVAL PAR) ASSERTION)</code> holds just in case <code>ASSERTION</code> holds at <code>INTERVAL</code> to granularity <code>PAR</code>.  See also #$MtTimeDimFn.")
(comment IntegerComputerNumber "The collection of all computer data types that are capable (only) of representing some subset of the integers or values related to representing integer values such as NAN.")
(comment BugReport "The collection of all #$PropositionalConceptualWorks that contain information about a particular software bug.")
(disjointWith CommunicationConvention BugReport)
(disjointWith VisualWork BugReport)
(comment BugzillaBugReport "The collection of database records created for bug tracking purposes using #$Bugzilla-TheProgram.  Each record is meant to represent a bug or other problem or enhancement in a particular piece of software.")
(comment USSecretaryOfHomelandSecurity "The U.S. federal Secretary of Homeland Security heads the efforts of the U.S. Department of Homeland Security.")
(comment UMLStateMachineProcedureDefinitionMicrotheory "A microtheory to contain the defining assertions for a #$UMLProcedure.")
(comment MtTimeDimFn "An unreifiable #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$TimeInterval and returns a #$TemporalMicrotheory.  <code>(#$ist (#$MtTimeDimFn INTERVAL) ASSERTION)</code> holds just in case <code>ASSERTION</code> holds at <code>INTERVAL</code> to granularity #$Null-TimeParameter.  See also #$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn.")
(comment umlProcedureDefinition "The #$BinaryPredicate umlProcedureDefinition links an instance #$UMLProcedure with the microtheory which contains its UML definitional assertions.")
(comment NitriteIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Each instance of
#$NitriteIon is a sample of NO2(-).  This ion may coordinate through
either the nitrogen atom (then called \"nitro\") or through one of the
oxygen atoms (in which case it is referred to as \"nitrito\").")
(comment NitriteIon "The collection of nitrite ions. Their chemical formula is NO2-.")
(comment ThiosulfateIon "A specialization of #$PolyatomicAnion.  Each instance of
#$ThiosulfateIon is a sample of S2O3(-2).")
(comment UMLProcedure-ReadLine-InputPin-InputStream "This instance of #$UMLInputPin is the variable giving the input stream for the procedure which reads the next newline-terminated string.")
(comment UMLProcedure-InitializeChatterBot "This is the chatterbot state user input procedure.")
(comment UMLCycLExpression "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage objects which are typed as #$CycLExpressions.")
(comment UMLProcedure-InitializeChatterBot-OutputPin-InputStream "This is the output initialized input stream variable.")
(comment UMLProcedure-InitializeChatterBot-OutputPin-OutputStream "This is the output initialized output stream variable.")
(comment rentingOf "A predicate which relates a type of thing typically rented out to the collection of #$Renting events in which that type of thing is rented. (#$rentingOf OBJECT-TYPE EVENT-COL) means that all events in EVENT-COL involve the renting of a thing of type OBJECT-TYPE (such as #$Automobile, #$CellularTelephone, etc). See also #$rents.")
(comment reactionTypeInMixture "(#$reactionTypeInMixture MIXTURE REACTIONTYPE) relates an
instance of #$Mixture MIXTURE to the type of #$ChemicalReaction
REACTIONTYPE that takes place in MIXTURE.")
(comment totalSubstancesInMixture "(#$totalSubstancesInMixture MIXTURE SET-OF-SUBST) means that
each instance of #$ChemicalSubstanceType contained in SET-OF-SUBST is
present in MIXTURE.  In this manner, all components of a mixture may
be enumerated.  #$ChemicalReactions may or may not occur as a result
of the different substances present, but all of the substances present
are available for reaction.  See also
#$availableReactantTypeInReactionType.")
(comment salientURLRootForType "An instance of #$BinaryPredicate, used to identify a salient URL for a class of entities, which serves as a \"root\" for salient URLs for instances of that class.  (#$salientURLRootForType TYPE PATHNAME) says that there are notable instances of TYPE that have #$salientURLs with the root URL of PATHNAME.")
(comment Slake-ChemicalReaction "A specialization of #$ExothermicReaction and
#$CombinationReaction. Each instance of #$Slake-ChemicalReaction is a
chemical reaction between water and #$CalciumMonoxide. This reaction
results in the creation of #$CalciumHydroxide and produces heat.")
(comment UMLProcedure-Write-InputPin-Text "This instance of #$UMLInputPin is the input text variable for the procedure which writes text to the given output stream.")
(comment UMLProcedure-Write-InputPin-OutputStream "This instance of #$UMLInputPin is the input output-stream variable for the procedure which writes the given text.")
(comment ProgramStepFromSyntaxFn "#$SoftwareParameterFromSyntaxFn is a unary function which returns the #$ProgramStep corresponding to the given #$ProgramSyntaxObject.")
(comment LactateIon "The collection of lactate ions; C3H5O3 (-).")
(comment SodiumLactate "The collection of samples of sodium lactate; NaC3H5O3.")
(comment slotValueToSignificantDigits "(#$slotValueToSignificantDigits SLOT THING SIG-DIG VALUE) means that THING's SLOT value to SIG-DIG significant digits is VALUE.")
(comment UMLProcedure-PerformCycL-OutputPin-ErrorMessage "This instance of #$UMLOutputPin is the error message variable for the procedure which performs the given CycL performative.")
(disjointWith ZeroDimensionalThing PositiveDimensionalThing)
(disjointWith Event PositiveDimensionalThing)
(comment knowledgeDomainOwner "(#$knowledgeDomainOwner MT CYCLIST) means that CYCLIST is primarily responsible for the knowledge in MT.")
(comment ClothTowel "The subcollection of #$Towels made out of #$Cloth.")
(comment AClassificationOfAPChemistryReactions-Webpage "A webpage for classifying reactions on the AP chemistry exam.")
(comment ChloriteIon "The collection of chlorite ions. Their chemical formula is ClO2-.")
(comment uniqueFieldList "(#$uniqueFieldList PS LIST) means that (i) every member of LIST is a physical 
field of PS, (ii) no two tuples from a knowledge source associated with PS via 
#$physicalSchemaSourceMap can have the same values for all physical fields in 
LIST. (Note that null values may not be considered  equal in this consideration. 
That means, even in the presence of a unique constraint, it may be possible to 
store an unlimited number of rows that contain a null value in at least one of the 
constrained columns. This behavior conforms to the SQL standard, but some SQL 
databases may not follow this rule.)")
(comment defaultFieldValue "(#$defaultFieldValue PS PF VAL) means that (i) PF is a physical
field of PS, and (ii) VAL is the default value of PF in PS. Note that
VAL must be an instance of the #$fieldDataType corresponding to PF.")
(comment schemaConstraints "This predicate relates a physical schema with an EL sentence
that describes a 'check' constraint on one or more of its physical
fields.")
(comment SKSIPhysicalSchemaConstraintPredicate "#$SKSIPhysicalSchemaConstraintPredicate is the collection of
all predicates used by SKSI to describe constraints on a physical
schema.")
(comment SideEffectPredicate "The collection of all predicates whose evaluation during inference results in a side-effect outside returning a value, such as sending an email, writing to a file, or changing the KB.")
(comment LigandType "A specialization of #$ChemicalCompoundTypeByChemicalSpecies.
Instances of #$LigandType are collections whose instances are substances capable of surrounding and bonding to a central metal ion, forming
a metal complex (or simply complex). In forming a complex, the ligands
are considered to coordinate to the metal.")
(comment ComplexIonFormation "A specialization of #$ComplexIonFormation.  Each instance of
#$ComplexIonFormation is a chemical reaction in which a transition
metal is surrounded by molecules or ions to create a single complex.")
(comment umlStateMachineDefinition "The #$BinaryPredicate umlProcedureDefinition links an instance #$UMLStateMachine with the microtheory which contains its UML definitional assertions.")
(comment placeName-WithRegionAbbreviation "(#$placeName-WithRegionAbbreviation PLACE STRING) means that
STRING is the name for PLACE including the abbreviation of the
GeopoliticalEntity that PLACE is a part of. Example: (#$placeName-WithRegionAbbreviation #$CityOfAustinTX \"Austin, TX\")")
(comment TimeInPosition-Obligation "The collection of #$Obligations which specify the time interval during which someone who becomes a #$PersonTypeByPositionInOrg is expected/allowed to retain that position. While the length of the #$Obligation is determined by some #$Agreement, there are many contexts in which the #$Person is not an #$agreeingAgents; for example, the US president's term is goverend by the #$USConstitution, but that's an #$Agreement between the US government and #$ThePublic (or something), not with (say) #$BillClinton. Hence, (#$genls #$TimeInPosition-Obligation #$Agreement) is often false. If someone agrees to accept an #$AppointedOffice, that obligation might or might not genls to #$Agreement, depending on how the term of office is determined.")
(comment obligationsCreated "An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$outputsCreated.  #$obligationsCreated relates an instance of #$Event to an instance of #$Obligation.  <code>(#$obligationsCreated EVENT OBLIGATION)</code> means that <code>OBLIGATION</code> was created in <code>EVENT</code>.  See also #$permissionsGranted.")
(comment obligationPeriod "(#$obligationPeriod OBLIGATION TIMEINTERVAL) is used to indicate the period of time during which a particular #$Obligation is in force. (#$StartFn #$TimeInterval) may occur after or (more rarely) before the creation of the #$Obligation itself.  If the #$Obligation is part of an #$Agreement, its #$obligationPeriod is not required to be the same as its #$agreementPeriod.")
(comment resultingTermInOffice "(#$resultingTermInOffice ELECTION TERM) holds if the #$TimeInPosition-Obligation describes the period during which the #$winner-First of that #$ElectionForOffice is expected to be in the relevant #$PersonTypeByPositionInOrg, for the TERM's #$obligationPeriod.")
(comment Commissioner-Rank "The collection of individuals who hold the title and rank of Commissioner within some #$Organization, often (but not necessarily) a #$Commission-Organization. In some cases they are the #$organizationHead of that organization, and in others they are simply high-ranking #$hasMembers who, as a result, are #$authorityCapableOf various actions.")
(comment UMLStateMachineStateVariable "The collection of all variables used to model state in a #$UMLStateMachine.")
(comment InputDataStream "This generic data type encapsluates the concept of input streams in programming languages.")
(comment OutputDataStream "This generic data type encapsluates the concept of output streams in programming languages.")
(comment BidirectionalDataStream "This generic data type encapsluates the concept of bidirectional streams (allowing both input and output) in programming languages.")
(comment ConsoleInputDataStream "This is the console input data stream.")
(comment ConsoleOutputDataStream "This is the console output data stream.")
(comment (ApplyingForFn LoanAgreement) "The collection of all events in each of which a person (or married couple) makes an application for a loan (a #$LoanAgreement) from some #$Organization.  Each such event is a complex script involving various standard subevents, although practices differ from lender to lender and from locale to locale.  This does not include the actions of the organization in response to the application.")
(comment (ApplyingStuffFn Makeup) "A specialization of #$HumanActivity in each instance of which cosmetics are applied in order to improve a person's appearance.")
(comment (ApplyingStuffFn Deodorant) "A specialization of #$ApplyingStuffToSurface, the application of #$Deodorant to one's underarm.")
(disjointWith TeethCleaning (ApplyingStuffFn Makeup))
(disjointWith GoingToTheBathroom (ApplyingStuffFn Makeup))
(disjointWith Gargling (ApplyingStuffFn Makeup))
(disjointWith (ApplyingStuffFn Makeup) (ApplyingStuffFn Deodorant))
(disjointWith Gargling (ApplyingStuffFn Deodorant))
(disjointWith GoingToTheBathroom (ApplyingStuffFn Deodorant))
(comment (ApplyingStuffFn SunTanLotion) "The application of suntan lotion to the #$Skin.")
(comment (ApplyingStuffFn Shampoo) "The act of applying shampoo to one's hair.")
(comment Voter "The collection of #$HumanAdults who regularly participate in #$Voting in #$Elections run by their #$GovernmentFns. It omits those who are eligible to vote, but who don't bother to exercise that #$CivilRights-COC.")
(comment Focus-Geometric "From allmath.com: imaginary point seen in parabolas, hyperbolas, and #$Ellipses.")
(comment Debut "A #$Debut is the first instance of something, often a performance or role or commercial availability of an object.")
(comment Ballot-CW "A list of candidates or propositions for an election.  A voter is meant to choose from this list.")
(comment Ballot-PaperCopy "The collection of physical copies of #$Ballot-CWs.")
(comment Allegation "An #$Allegation is a negative, but unproven statement about someone, often stated in an #$Accusing event.")
(comment Mansion "A #$Mansion is a very large and expensive house.")
(comment PropositionalConceptualWork-TragedyGenre " The collection of instances of #$PropositionalConceptualWork that are of the
tragedy genre.  Usually something bad happens to someone in these works.   ")
(comment TragicMode "#$The collection of instances of #$CWInstantiating that present instances of #$Narrative in a way or mode intended to be tragic.")
(comment Opener-Device "The collection of all devices used to open things, especially containers.  Specs include #$ManualCanOpener, #$ElectricCanOpener and #$LetterOpener.")
(comment SocialReformEvent "The collection of events in which some element of society is changed for the better whether by law or by collective action.")
(comment Plea-Legal "A #$Plea-Legal involves someone accused of a crime declaring whether or not they are guilty.  Other possible variations include insanity pleas and no contest pleas.")
(comment GuiltyPlea "A #$GuiltyPlea is a confirmation that an person has committed a crime.")
(comment NotGuiltyPlea "A #$NotGuiltyPlea is a denial that an person has committed a crime.")
(disjointWith GuiltyPlea NotGuiltyPlea)
(comment Backyard "A #$Backyard is a #$Yard-BuildingGrounds that is specifically behind a home or building")
(comment MythologicalPCW "The collection of all #$PropositionalConceptualWorks that are specifically concerned with the telling of myths or legends.  This is a more specialized type of #$FictionalPCW that deals primarily with stories of Gods, mythic creatures, magic, and the mystical origins and prophecies of societies.")
(comment Warrior "The collection of all #$Professional-Adults who specialize in fighting and combat agagainst some enemy forces.  This includes #$Soldiers and other combat members of armies, but does not include #$MartialArtists who practice only for athletic purposes.")
(comment PrivacyLevel "Instances of #$PrivacyLevel correspond to assessments of the relative privacy level of a particular thing.  The level of privacy reflects how much or how little something is exposed to the public or to some non-owner of the thing in question.")
(comment BootSpur "#$BootSpurs connect to boots and used in #$HorsebackRiding to encourage the horses movement.")
(comment SportsPostSeason "The #$SportsPostSeason is the #$ChampionshipCompetition held every year after the regular season.  A #$SportsPostSeason usually consists of more than one game and is often comprised of a series of elimination games or elimination series.")
(comment Shutout "A #$Shutout occurs in team sports when the losing team does not score at all during a game.")
(comment Nightmare "A #$Nightmare is a scary or otherwise negative #$Dream")
(comment Welfare-GovernmentService "#$Welfare-GovernmentService is a #$GovernmentPaidService that provides monetary support to people who fall below a certain income or poverty level or qualify in some similar way.")
(comment TechniqueType "#$TechniqueType is the the collection of types of practical actions that require specific skills and are used for specific purposes or tasks.  Although #$TechniqueType is a specialization of #$SkilledActivityType, techniques are usually not main, general or primary activities, but rather sub-activities that accomplish more specific elements of more general #$SkilledActivityTypes.  For example, #$DoingMath would not be a instance of #$TechniqueType, but a spec of #$SolvingAMathematicalEquation could be as long as it represents a particular approach towards solving a math problem.")
(comment umlStateVariable "The #$BinaryPredicate umlBody links an instance #$UMLExpression with an instance of #$Thing which consists of programming language source statements which implement the expression.  The argument is typically a #$CharacterString or #$CycLExpression.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement-IsRoot "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects which have no ancestors.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement-IsNotRoot "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects which have ancestors.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement-IsLeaf "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects which have no descendents.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement-IsNotLeaf "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects which have descendents.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement-IsAbstract "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects which are abstract.")
(comment UMLGeneralizableElement-IsNotAbstract "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage GeneralizableElement objects which are not abstract and therefore can be instantiated.")
(comment umlGeneralization "The #$BinaryPredicate umlGeneralization links an instance #$UMLGeneralizableElement with another instance of #$UMLGeneralizableElement such that the second argument is taken to be a generalization of the first argument.")
(comment umlFeatureLink "The #$BinaryPredicate umlFeature links an instance #$UMLClassifier with one of its #$UMLFeature objects, such as an attribute.")
(comment UMLClass "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Class objects.")
(comment UMLClass-IsActive "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Class objects which have an active thread.")
(comment UMLClass-IsNotActive "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Class objects which do not have an active thread.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-Changeable "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage changeable StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-Frozen "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage frozen StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-AddOnly "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage add-only StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-Ordered "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage ordered StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-Unordered "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage ordered StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-Instance "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage instance scoped StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLStructuralFeature-Classifier "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage classifier scoped StructuralFeature objects.")
(comment UMLAttribute "The collection of #$UnifiedModelingLanguage Attribute objects.")
